/* Psychiatry for Residents: the method for each challenge. Original content. */
window.TPR_METH={"c-0-1-1":{"name":"Talking Jordan down: safe scene, one voice, real choices","why":"Brings Jordan's arousal down without force by making the room safe, hearing him out and offering choices he can accept.","frame":["Scene safety","Engage and let him vent","Name the need and the feeling","Calm boundaries","Real choices","Fallback plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Arrive armed with facts, not bravado","say":["Priya, I've got a working alarm. What's happened, who knows him best, and who's leading?","Has he had any PRN today, and when? Anything on leave, drink or drugs?","Can your colleague take the other patients to the lounge, and can the response team wait out of sight?"],"hear":"\"Leave was refused at teatime and he's been building since. Lorazepam 2 mg at two o'clock.\"","mark":"Trigger and PRN history identified"},{"h":"Position yourself and open gently","say":["Jordan, I'm Dr Ade, one of the doctors. I'm going to stay right here, not come any closer.","I can see you're really angry. Tell me what's gone on tonight.","Take your time, I'm listening."],"hear":"\"You lot locked me up for nothing and now you won't even let me out for an hour!\"","mark":"Room cleared, single lead voice set"},{"h":"Let him vent, then name the need","say":["So the leave being cancelled felt like the last straw. Have I got that right?","What's the thing you need most right now?","Is part of this that you feel everyone's watching you?"],"hear":"\"I've got deals to close. And they're all staring, like they're going to jump me.\"","mark":"Need identified: to feel safe and to be heard about his business"},{"h":"Agree where you can, set one clear limit","say":["Losing leave is frustrating, and anyone would want to sort out their work.","I can't let anyone get hurt, and that includes you. Could you put the chair down for me?","It's hard for me to help while there's shouting. Can we talk a bit more quietly?"],"hear":"\"Fine. But I'm not being jumped.\" (puts the chair down)","mark":"Boundary accepted"},{"h":"Screen for a physical driver","say":["You said your head's buzzing. How have you been sleeping?","Any coffee, alcohol or anything else today?","Since the new tablets, do your legs feel restless, like you can't keep still?"],"hear":"\"About four strong coffees. And yeah, my legs won't stop since the new pills.\"","mark":"Possible akathisia and caffeine identified"},{"h":"Offer real choices, tablets before anything else","say":["Two options I can do right now: a tablet to take the edge off, or quiet time in your room with a nurse close by.","I can also get your phone so you can ring your brother.","I'll make sure your consultant hears tomorrow that you settled."],"hear":"\"I'll take a tablet if it helps me sleep. And I want to ring my brother.\"","mark":"Oral PRN accepted"},{"h":"Agree the fallback with the nurse and close","say":["Priya, oral lorazepam 1 mg now keeps him within the 24-hour limit. I'll write an IM plan so we're ready if he escalates.","If he becomes violent again, the response team comes in and I'll come back to review.","I'll write this up, flag the restless legs for the team, and ask the RC to review leave and his medication in the morning."],"hear":"\"That works. I'll do the incident report and keep an eye on him.\"","mark":"Plan agreed and recorded"}],"table":{"cols":["De-escalation step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Scene safety","Four patients watching, two nurses crowding him","An audience and crowding drive arousal; clear the room first"],["Trigger","Leave refused at teatime","A 'no' with no reason feels like punishment in mania"],["Feeling and need","Feels watched and about to be jumped; worried about his business","Fear underneath the anger; give space and acknowledge his worries"],["Physical driver","Four coffees, restless legs since new tablets","Caffeine and possible akathisia feeding agitation; review with the team"],["Choices","Accepts a tablet and a call to his brother","Agreement restored without force"]]},"key":"Good doctors calm the patient. Excellent ones also look for what is fuelling the agitation (caffeine, akathisia, a refused request) and agree a written fallback before leaving.","objmap":[[2],[6],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-0-1-2":{"name":"Leanne at 02:00: is IM needed, which drug, and under what law","why":"Gives rapid tranquillisation only when it is truly needed, with the safest drug for Leanne, a clear legal basis and proper monitoring.","frame":["Safety of the hold","Oral first","Legal authority","Drug choice and limits","Monitoring","Aftercare and debrief"],"steps":[{"h":"Make the hold safe before anything else","say":["Sam, is she on her side, and is anyone's weight on her chest or back?","Who is watching her airway and breathing right now?","Is Rachel hurt? Has someone taken over from her?"],"hear":"\"She's on her side on the mattress. Rachel's shaken and has a sore jaw.\"","mark":"Hold is safe; injured nurse supported"},{"h":"Check what she has had and her history","say":["What has she had in the last 24 hours, regular and PRN?","Is there an ECG on file, and any history of reactions to antipsychotics?","Any drink or drugs, or any medical problems we know about?"],"hear":"\"Aripiprazole this morning, lorazepam 1 mg at four. No ECG, she refused it. Dystonia on haloperidol years ago.\"","mark":"No ECG and past dystonia identified"},{"h":"Confirm the legal basis","say":["What section is she on, and since when?","Section 2, admitted Monday: she's well within the first three months, so Section 63 covers treatment for her mental disorder.","We still try for her agreement first."],"hear":"\"Section 2, day three.\"","mark":"Legal authority confirmed: Section 63"},{"h":"Offer oral once more","say":["Leanne, I'm Dr Shah. Nobody wants to hurt you. We want to help you feel calmer.","Would you take a tablet now? If you do, the staff can ease off.","If you can't, I'll need to give you an injection to keep you and everyone safe."],"hear":"\"You're poisoning me. Get off!\"","mark":"Oral refused, risk ongoing"},{"h":"Choose the drug and dose","say":["With no ECG and a past dystonia, IM lorazepam alone is the safest choice.","Lorazepam 2 mg IM now. That keeps her within the BNF daily maximum with the 1 mg she had earlier.","No haloperidol tonight. If she needs more, I'll come back and review rather than leave a second drug as PRN."],"hear":"\"OK. The day team usually like haloperidol, but I see why.\"","mark":"IM lorazepam chosen"},{"h":"Set up monitoring","say":["Pulse, blood pressure, breathing, temperature, oxygen and level of alertness every 15 minutes for the first hour, then hourly.","If she refuses observations, watch her breathing and responsiveness and write it down.","If her breathing drops below 10 a minute, call me and the emergency team; flumazenil is in the bag."],"hear":"\"Got it. Emergency bag and oxygen are by the office.\"","mark":"Monitoring plan in place"},{"h":"Aftercare, records and debrief","say":["I'll check the mark on her forehead once she's settled, and arrange an ECG when she'll allow it.","Please record the restraint and the injection on the Use of Force record and Datix.","Let's debrief the team, and offer Leanne a chance to talk about it tomorrow."],"hear":"\"I'll do the forms. Thanks for explaining.\"","mark":"Aftercare agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["RT element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Oral first","Refused lorazepam at 01:50 and again now","Oral was genuinely tried; IM is now justified by ongoing risk"],["Legal authority","Section 2, day three","Section 63 covers it; no T2/T3 needed yet"],["Drug choice","No ECG, past acute dystonia on haloperidol","IM lorazepam alone; avoid haloperidol tonight"],["Dose limits","Lorazepam 1 mg at 16:00","Add all doses before prescribing; stay under the daily ceiling"],["Monitoring","Emergency bag, oxygen and flumazenil on the ward","15-minute checks for the first hour; flumazenil if breathing is depressed"]]},"key":"Good doctors pick a drug. Excellent ones pick the drug for this patient: no ECG plus past dystonia rules out haloperidol, and they total every dose given in 24 hours first.","objmap":[[3],[5],[6],[5,6],[1]]},"c-0-1-3":{"name":"After the hold: is Kyle physically safe?","why":"Finds the life-threatening complications of restraint early in a hot, breathless young man and gets the right tests and reviews.","frame":["Airway and breathing","Positional asphyxia","Muscle breakdown and heat","Heart rhythm","Injuries","Drugs on board"],"steps":[{"h":"Handover from the nurse","say":["Dawn, how long was he held, and in what position?","Was anyone's weight on his back or chest?","What has he had: any IM, PRN or drugs on leave?"],"hear":"\"Twelve minutes, about four of them face down with hands on his back. IM lorazepam 2 mg at four.\"","mark":"Prone restraint with pressure identified"},{"h":"Engage him and get consent","say":["Kyle, I'm Dr Patel. I'm not here about what happened. I'm here to check your body is OK.","Being held like that can affect your breathing and muscles. Can I check you over?","You can tell me to stop at any time."],"hear":"\"Nobody's touching me after what they did. I couldn't breathe.\"","mark":"Breathing difficulty during the hold reported"},{"h":"Ask about danger symptoms","say":["Do you feel short of breath now? Any chest pain or tight chest?","Are your muscles aching? Is your pee dark?","Do you feel hot or confused?"],"hear":"\"I'm boiling and everything aches. I just want to sleep.\"","mark":"Heat and muscle pain identified"},{"h":"Check drugs on board","say":["Did you smoke or take anything on leave yesterday?","Did anyone give you a pill? Do you know what it was?","When did you last drink anything?"],"hear":"\"Some weed. Someone gave me a pill. Nothing to drink since lunch.\"","mark":"Unknown drug and dehydration identified"},{"h":"Observe and assess","say":["Can I just do your pulse, breathing, temperature and oxygen? Then I'll leave you to rest.","Let me look at your elbow and check your neck and chest for marks.","If you'd rather I didn't touch you, I'll just watch from here."],"hear":"\"Just the clip on my finger then.\"","mark":"NEWS2 obtained"},{"h":"Explain the tests","say":["I'd like a blood test for a muscle enzyme called CK, your kidneys and sugar, and a heart tracing.","Muscles damaged by struggling can harm the kidneys, and that pill could affect your heart.","It will take ten minutes, and I'll stay while it's done."],"hear":"\"Fine, if it's quick.\"","mark":"Consent to CK, U&E, glucose and ECG"},{"h":"Plan, escalate and close","say":["Dawn, NEWS2 every 15 minutes for the first hour. Oral fluids, cool room. Call me if anything changes.","If his temperature keeps rising, he becomes confused or his CK is very high, he goes to A&E.","Please complete the Use of Force record and Datix, and let's arrange a debrief with Kyle and the team."],"hear":"\"Will they hold me like that again?\"","mark":"Plan and escalation thresholds agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Physical risk","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Positional asphyxia","Face down four minutes, hands on back, 'couldn't breathe'","High-risk hold; check breathing and oxygen now"],["Muscle breakdown","Muscles ache, hot, nothing drunk since lunch","Check CK and U&E; give fluids"],["Heat and drugs","Cannabis and an unknown pill","Unknown drug raises the risk of overheating and rhythm problems; watch temperature closely"],["Heart rhythm","IM lorazepam plus unknown drug","ECG now"],["Injury","Hit elbow, not his head","Check the elbow for swelling or fracture; record it"]]},"key":"A sweaty, breathless, hot patient after prone restraint is a medical emergency until proven otherwise. Set clear thresholds for A&E, not just 'routine obs'.","objmap":[[1],[5],[6],[1,7],[2]]},"c-0-1-4":{"name":"The first seclusion review: still needed, and is Darren safe?","why":"Checks whether seclusion must continue and spots the physical danger of a dehydrated man on lithium.","frame":["Why seclusion started","Is it still needed","Hydration","Lithium toxicity","Mental state","Review and exit plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Handover from the nurse","say":["Tom, why did seclusion start, and what was tried first?","When did he last drink or pass urine?","Any medication or rapid tranquillisation given?"],"hear":"\"He punched the window at two. Refused oral PRN. No drink since about seven last night.\"","mark":"Dehydration identified"},{"h":"See him face to face","say":["Darren, I'm Dr Lee, the on-call doctor. I'm here to see how you are and whether you can come out.","I'm listening. What happened tonight?"],"hear":"\"Let me out of this box now! The nurse wound me up.\"","mark":"Engagement established"},{"h":"Ask about his body","say":["When did you last have a drink?","Have you been to the toilet?","Your hands look shaky. Is that new? Any sickness or wobbliness?"],"hear":"\"Nothing since tea. Haven't been. Hands won't stop shaking and I feel wobbly.\"","mark":"Coarse tremor and unsteadiness: possible lithium toxicity"},{"h":"Check his mental state and the risk now","say":["How are you feeling in yourself right now?","Are you hearing anything others don't?","If I open the door, what would you do?"],"hear":"\"No voices. Noise just wound me up. I'd want a drink and my bed.\"","mark":"Risk to others reducing"},{"h":"Fluids and observations now","say":["Tom, can we pass him water now and get a set of observations through the hatch or with staff?","I'll take an urgent lithium level, U&E and creatinine.","Let's look at his right knuckles too."],"hear":"\"He'll take water. He'll let me do his obs.\"","mark":"Fluids and urgent bloods started"},{"h":"Decide on seclusion","say":["He's calmer and talking. Can we end seclusion now with a nurse close by?","If not, we review again within four hours, nursing reviews every two hours, and 15-minute records.","If it goes past eight hours, we need an independent MDT review."],"hear":"\"Let's try him in the low-stimulus room with one-to-one.\"","mark":"Seclusion ended"},{"h":"Explain, record and hand over","say":["Darren, you're coming out. You need water and a blood test because the shaking could be your lithium.","We'll hold tonight's lithium until the result is back.","I'll document the review, and the day team will look at your lithium in the morning."],"hear":"\"Will they stop my lithium?\"","mark":"Plan documented and handed over"}],"table":{"cols":["Review item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Still needed?","Calmer, no voices, wants a drink and bed","Seclusion may no longer be justified; end it"],["Hydration","No drink since 19:00, no urine","Dehydration; give fluids now"],["Lithium","Took lithium 22:00; shaking, wobbly","Possible toxicity; urgent level, U&E; hold next dose"],["Injury","Sore right knuckles","Check for fracture"],["Review schedule","First review at 03:00","2-hourly nursing, 4-hourly medical; independent MDT at 8 hours"]]},"key":"The review is not just 'still dangerous?'. A dry, shaky man on lithium may be heading for toxicity. Treat the body and end seclusion at the earliest safe point.","objmap":[[2,6],[3],[5],[6],[2]]},"c-0-1-5":{"name":"Chloe after the ligature: first five minutes, then the ward","why":"Keeps Chloe alive in the first minutes, gets her to A&E for the right reasons, and makes the ward safer afterwards.","frame":["Airway and breathing","Neck injury","Hidden overdose","A&E decision","Observation and safety","Review and learning"],"steps":[{"h":"Call for help and do ABC","say":["Chloe, can you hear me? Can you tell me your name?","Jess, emergency bag and oxygen please, and call the emergency team.","Let me look at your neck and listen to your breathing."],"hear":"\"Chloe. I'm fine. It was nothing.\" (hoarse)","mark":"Airway patent; hoarse voice identified"},{"h":"Look for neck injury","say":["Does your throat hurt? Is it hard to swallow?","Do you remember the nurse coming in?","Any pain in the back of your neck?"],"hear":"\"It hurts to swallow. I don't remember her coming in.\"","mark":"Laryngeal injury and loss of consciousness identified"},{"h":"Ask about a hidden overdose","say":["Chloe, have you taken anything tonight? Any tablets?","What, how many, and what time?"],"hear":"\"About ten paracetamol at nine. I saved them from home.\"","mark":"Paracetamol overdose identified"},{"h":"Decide on A&E and explain","say":["Your voice change, trouble swallowing, losing consciousness and the paracetamol all need hospital tonight.","Jess, please call 999. She needs a nurse escort.","I'll ring A&E to hand over: time of tablets and the ligature."],"hear":"\"I don't need hospital. I've caused enough trouble.\"","mark":"A&E transfer arranged"},{"h":"Capacity and her worries","say":["You haven't caused trouble. We want you safe.","Can you tell me in your words why the hospital needs to check you?","If you want to leave A&E, staff there will talk with us first."],"hear":"\"Because the tablets could hurt my liver. Will you tell my mum?\"","mark":"Capacity to accept transfer assessed"},{"h":"Make the ward safe","say":["The charger cable needs removing. Let's search her room with her.","Until she returns, and when she does, she needs increased observation agreed by the MDT.","Let's agree what helps her: her friend Amy, music, cold water."],"hear":"\"I'll get the room checked.\"","mark":"Room safety and observation level agreed"},{"h":"Family, records and learning","say":["Chloe, I'd like to tell your mum you're safe. Is that OK?","Please complete Datix. The trust will decide the learning response.","We'll do a full psychosocial assessment when she's back, not a risk score."],"hear":"\"Just say I'm safe. Don't tell her everything.\"","mark":"Consent to share agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Emergency item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Airway","Conscious, hoarse","Airway open but at risk; oxygen, watch closely"],["Neck injury","Painful swallow, can't recall being found","Possible laryngeal injury and loss of consciousness; A&E"],["Hidden overdose","Ten paracetamol at 21:00","Needs a paracetamol level and treatment in A&E"],["Ward safety","Phone charger cable from her bag","Search belongings; remove ligature points and items"],["What helps","Friend Amy, music, cold water","Build into a safety plan"]]},"key":"Always ask 'Have you taken anything?' after any self-harm. The hidden paracetamol, not the ligature, may be what decides her care tonight.","objmap":[[1],[4,7],[6],[5],[5,6]]},"c-0-1-6":{"name":"Neil is AWOL: grade the risk, tell the right people","why":"Treats a missing detained patient with a threat history as high risk, starts the AWOL process and keeps his sister informed and involved.","frame":["Leave terms","Risk grade","Police report","Where he may be","Who to tell","Return and records"],"steps":[{"h":"Check the leave and timeline","say":["Mo, what exactly did the Section 17 form allow?","When was he due back, and who last saw him?","What did his last relapse look like?"],"hear":"\"One hour unescorted from six. Last relapse he threatened his neighbour.\"","mark":"Absent without leave; threat history identified"},{"h":"Grade the risk","say":["He's on Section 3, overdue more than two hours, not answering, with past threats to a named person.","That makes him high risk. We call the police now.","Is the RC or on-call consultant aware?"],"hear":"\"I'll ring the police and the bleep holder.\"","mark":"High risk graded"},{"h":"Give the police clear information","say":["He is detained under Section 3 and absent without leave. Section 18 lets us return him.","He has schizophrenia and threatened his neighbour during his last relapse.","Here's his description, photo and likely places."],"hear":"\"Can you give the neighbour's address?\"","mark":"Police informed"},{"h":"Call his sister","say":["Ms Dunn, it's the on-call doctor. Neil hasn't come back from his leave, and we're worried.","Has he been in touch with you today?","Where do you think he might go?"],"hear":"\"He rang about half seven. He said the neighbour's spying on him again.\"","mark":"Paranoid content about the neighbour identified"},{"h":"Hear her worries","say":["I understand you're angry he was out alone. His leave was planned, but I hear you.","Is there anywhere he goes, like a pub or a friend's?","Do you have a key to his flat?"],"hear":"\"The Red Lion near his flat. I've got a key, but I'm forty minutes away.\"","mark":"Likely locations identified"},{"h":"Explain what happens next","say":["The police will look for him. He isn't arrested; he's being brought back for care.","If he's in his flat and won't come out, a warrant may be needed.","If he contacts you, please call us or 999 if anyone is in danger."],"hear":"\"What if he hurts the neighbour?\"","mark":"Sister engaged in the plan"},{"h":"Inform and record","say":["I'll update the police with the Red Lion and his comments about the neighbour.","Mo, please tell the MHA office and complete Datix.","On return: physical check, mental state, leave reviewed by the RC."],"hear":"\"Thank you for telling me.\"","mark":"Process completed"}],"table":{"cols":["AWOL element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Leave terms","One hour unescorted from 18:00","Overdue: absent without leave under Section 18"],["Risk grade","Past threats to his neighbour","High risk; police now"],["New information","Says the neighbour is 'spying on him again'","Relapse signs aimed at the same person; tell the police"],["Where he may be","Red Lion pub; sister has a key","Share with the police"],["Who to tell","Sister is nearest relative","Inform her, the RC, MHA office"]]},"key":"The risk grade comes from who he is and what he said, not how late he is. Paranoia about a neighbour he threatened before makes this urgent.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[2,7],[1,2],[7]]},"c-0-2-1":{"name":"Hannah at the door: talk first, then 5(2) if needed","why":"Decides quickly and fairly whether Hannah can go home safely or must be held, within the legal time limits.","frame":["The 5(4) clock","Talk and listen","Suicide risk","Protective items","Section 5(2) criteria","Next steps"],"steps":[{"h":"Check the clock","say":["Ruth, when did the 5(4) start?","Why are you worried about her?"],"hear":"\"Nine-fifty. She told me she'd take tablets tonight.\"","mark":"Section 5(4) at 21:50; up to 6 hours"},{"h":"Engage her","say":["Hannah, I'm Dr Ali. I can see you want to go, and that you feel let down. Can we talk somewhere quieter?","What's made you want to leave tonight?"],"hear":"\"You said I could leave whenever. That was a lie. It's too loud here.\"","mark":"Engagement established"},{"h":"Assess her risk","say":["How's your mood been today?","Are you having thoughts about ending your life? How often?","Have you made a plan?"],"hear":"\"Black all day. I think about it most of the time. The tablets in my kitchen drawer.\"","mark":"Plan and means identified"},{"h":"Explore the details","say":["How many tablets do you have?","Would anyone be with you tonight?","What's kept you going so far?"],"hear":"\"About a month of amitriptyline. I live alone. My dog's at a neighbour's.\"","mark":"High lethality and isolation identified"},{"h":"Test safety","say":["Do you think going home tonight could be dangerous?","Could you stay tonight and talk to the consultant in the morning?","Is there anything that would make staying more bearable?"],"hear":"\"I know it's dangerous. I don't care. I'm leaving.\"","mark":"Unwilling to stay, high risk"},{"h":"Use Section 5(2) and explain","say":["Hannah, I'm worried you won't be safe tonight. I'm using a holding power, Section 5(2), which keeps you here for up to 72 hours while you're properly assessed.","You'll get your rights in writing, and you can speak to an advocate.","Let's find you a quieter room."],"hear":"\"Am I sectioned now? How long?\"","mark":"Section 5(2) completed"},{"h":"Handover and next steps","say":["Ruth, the 5(4) ends now. The form goes to you as the managers' representative.","I'll tell the on-call consultant now and request an MHA assessment with the AMHP as early as possible.","Observation level set; her dog's care noted."],"hear":"\"I'll get the rights read and contact the MHA office.\"","mark":"Escalation completed"}],"table":{"cols":["Decision point","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["5(4) clock","Started 21:50","Up to 6 hours, ends when you arrive and decide"],["Suicidal intent","Thoughts most of the day, plan tonight","High risk"],["Means","A month of amitriptyline at home","Highly lethal in overdose"],["Protection","Lives alone; dog with neighbour","Little to stop her tonight"],["Section 5(2)","Will not stay, 'doesn't care'","Application ought to be made; hold and arrange assessment"]]},"key":"A holding power buys time for a proper assessment; it is not the decision. Tell the consultant and request the AMHP straight away.","objmap":[[5],[3],[3],[7],[7]]},"c-0-2-2":{"name":"Can we give Daniel the IM? The three-month rule and the certificate","why":"Works out whether medicine can lawfully be given against Daniel's wishes tonight, and keeps him safe and heard if it cannot.","frame":["Three-month clock","Certificate (T2 or T3)","Capacity and consent","Emergency (Section 62)","Choice and side effects","SOAD and records"],"steps":[{"h":"Triage the call","say":["Grace, is he a danger to anyone right now, or is he just refusing?","Has anything been given tonight, PRN or otherwise?"],"hear":"\"He's calm. Just adamant. No PRN.\"","mark":"No emergency identified"},{"h":"Work out the clock","say":["When was his first dose of medication for mental disorder in this period of detention?","He came in on a Section 2, then a Section 3. The clock started then, not at the Section 3.","Ninety-four days: past three months, so Section 58 applies."],"hear":"\"Ninety-four days ago.\"","mark":"Three-month rule applies"},{"h":"Read the certificate","say":["What does the T2 or T3 say: which drugs, which routes, what doses?","If it lists oral antipsychotics only, an IM is not covered."],"hear":"\"T3. Oral antipsychotics within BNF limits only.\"","mark":"IM not authorised"},{"h":"Talk with Daniel","say":["Daniel, I'm Dr Shah. I hear you're worried about the tablets. Tell me about it.","What do you think the tablets are for?","Have they caused you any problems?"],"hear":"\"You're poisoning me. And since I started them I can't sit still, and I've put on two stone.\"","mark":"Akathisia and weight gain identified"},{"h":"Offer choices","say":["Would you take it as a melt from a sealed pack you open yourself?","We take those side effects seriously. Your consultant can look at changing the dose or drug.","Would you like an advocate to help you?"],"hear":"\"I'd take the melt if I open it.\"","mark":"Oral dose accepted"},{"h":"Explain the law to the nurse","say":["Grace, there's no emergency, so Section 62 doesn't apply. We can't force an IM tonight.","If he refuses, we record it and keep offering. If he becomes violent, Section 62 might be considered and I'll come and review.","No T2 is possible while he doesn't accept he's ill and refuses."],"hear":"\"OK, so no IM. What do I write?\"","mark":"Unlawful IM avoided"},{"h":"Records and handover","say":["Record his refusal, the reasons, the side effects and what was offered.","Ask the RC tomorrow to request a SOAD if the T3 needs to cover IM, and to review the akathisia.","Let the advocate know he'd like help."],"hear":"\"I'll put it in the notes.\"","mark":"Plan documented"}],"table":{"cols":["Consent-to-treatment step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Three-month clock","First dose 94 days ago","Section 58 now applies"],["Certificate","T3: oral antipsychotics only","IM not covered"],["Emergency","Calm, no violence","No Section 62 basis"],["Capacity and consent","Believes he's being poisoned","No valid T2 consent; T3 needed for any change"],["Side effects","Can't sit still, two stone gained","Akathisia and weight gain; reasons for refusal to address"]]},"key":"Section 3 is not a blank cheque. After three months, the certificate decides what can be given, by which route. Read it before you write anything.","objmap":[[3],[1,6],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-0-2-3":{"name":"Mrs Doyle at three months: capacity for medicine, capacity for ECT","why":"Assesses Mrs Doyle's capacity separately for medication and ECT, so the right certificates, and any advance refusal, are handled correctly.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate","Advance refusal","Physical risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and explain","say":["Good morning Mrs Doyle, I'm Dr Shah. Can we talk about your treatment for a few minutes?","We need to know what you think about your tablets, and about ECT, which the team has suggested."],"hear":"\"I don't want anything. Give it to someone who deserves it.\"","mark":"Guilt and hopelessness identified"},{"h":"Understand: the tablets","say":["What are your tablets for?","What might happen if you stopped them?","Do you know any side effects?"],"hear":"\"For depression and the voices. They make me dizzy and dry.\"","mark":"Understands medication"},{"h":"Understand and retain: ECT","say":["What do you know about ECT?","It's a short treatment under anaesthetic, usually twice a week. It can cause memory problems for a time.","Can you tell me back what I said?"],"hear":"\"It's shocks under anaesthetic. My memory might go. It fries the brain.\"","mark":"Understands and retains, with a fixed fear"},{"h":"Use and weigh","say":["Do you think treatment could help you?","When you think about the risks and benefits, how do you decide?","Do you believe you deserve to get better?"],"hear":"\"I don't deserve to get better. I should just die.\"","mark":"Weighing impaired by depressive guilt"},{"h":"Look for an advance refusal","say":["Have you ever written down that you didn't want ECT?","Does anyone hold decisions for you, a lasting power of attorney?"],"hear":"\"I wrote it years ago after my mother had it. Claire might have it in a folder.\"","mark":"Possible advance refusal of ECT identified"},{"h":"Check the physical risk","say":["How much are you drinking each day?","Do you feel dizzy when you stand? Are you passing urine?","Have you thought of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"A few sips. Dizzy, yes. I'd just stop eating.\"","mark":"Dehydration identified"},{"h":"Summarise to the consultant","say":["Dr Patel, I think she lacks capacity for both. She understands and retains, but her guilt stops her weighing.","So a SOAD for medication on a T3, and for ECT on a T6.","Claire needs to find the advance refusal; if valid, a T6 can't authorise ECT. Meanwhile, urgent fluids and bloods."],"hear":"\"Agreed. I'll request the SOAD today.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Capacity item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Knows tablets are for depression and voices; describes ECT","Present"],["Retain","Repeats back side effects","Present"],["Use and weigh","'I don't deserve to get better'","Impaired by depressive guilt: lacks capacity"],["Advance refusal","Wrote one years ago; daughter may have it","Find it; if valid and applicable, a T6 is blocked"],["Physical risk","Sips only, dizzy, little urine","Urgent medical care alongside"]]},"key":"Understanding is not the same as capacity. She can repeat every risk but cannot weigh them while she believes she deserves to die.","objmap":[[7],[5,7],[1,3],[7],[7]]},"c-0-2-4":{"name":"Liam back late and intoxicated: his leave, his safety, his mother","why":"Keeps Liam safe tonight, makes a fair leave decision within the law, and supports his mother without breaking confidentiality.","frame":["Physical safety","Leave breach","Risk","Leave decision","Mother's position","Confidentiality"],"steps":[{"h":"Welcome back and check his body","say":["Liam, I'm Dr Shah, the doctor on call. I'm glad you're back. Can we chat in here?","You look unsteady. Did you fall or bang your head?"],"hear":"\"Tripped on a kerb. Bumped the back of my head. Bit of a headache.\"","mark":"Head injury identified"},{"h":"Ask about substances","say":["What did you drink, and how much?","You mentioned weed. Anything else?","Would you give a urine sample?"],"hear":"\"Three or four cans and some weed at a mate's. Yeah, I'll do a sample.\"","mark":"Intoxication identified"},{"h":"Explore the breach and risk","say":["You were due back three hours ago. What happened?","Any trouble while you were out?"],"hear":"\"Had a row with a bouncer and walked off. No police.\"","mark":"Conflict while on leave identified"},{"h":"Explain tonight's plan","say":["You're not being punished. Tonight you stay on the ward with head injury observations.","Your consultant decides about leave. I'll pass on that you came back and what you want.","Overnight leave tomorrow won't happen until they review."],"hear":"\"So am I being punished?\"","mark":"Leave suspended pending RC review"},{"h":"Call his mother: listen","say":["Mrs Carter, it's Dr Shah. Thank you for ringing. I'd like to hear your concerns.","What happened last time he was home?"],"hear":"\"He smashed the TV and scared his sister. I won't have him home.\"","mark":"Risk at home identified"},{"h":"Respect confidentiality, offer support","say":["I can't share details without his permission, but I can hear everything you tell me.","Your concerns will go to his consultant. Leave home will not happen without it being discussed with you.","Have you been offered a carer's assessment?"],"hear":"\"Will you tell him it was me? No, I've never been offered support.\"","mark":"Carer's needs identified"},{"h":"Record and handover","say":["I'll write a note on the leave breach, alcohol, cannabis and head injury.","Ask Liam whether he consents to sharing information with his mum.","Handover to the RC for leave review, and refer his mother for a carer's assessment."],"hear":"\"Thank you for listening.\"","mark":"Plan completed"}],"table":{"cols":["Leave issue","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Physical safety","Head bump, headache, intoxicated","Head injury observations; GCS checks"],["Leave breach","Three hours late","Record; RC reviews all leave"],["Substances","Three to four cans and cannabis","Urine screen; relapse risk"],["Home situation","Mother refuses; past violence","Overnight leave home not safe; plan with family"],["Carer","Never offered support","Carer's assessment referral"]]},"key":"The on-call doctor does not grant or cancel leave as a sanction; keep him safe tonight, document, and hand the leave decision to the RC.","objmap":[[4],[6],[6],[6],[]]},"c-0-2-5":{"name":"Mark on a 136 in A&E: clock, body, then mind","why":"Keeps Mark's Section 136 lawful and safe, treats his physical risks first, and plans the joint assessment within 24 hours.","frame":["The 24-hour clock","Physical risks","Fit for interview","Roles: AMHP, Section 12 doctor, police","Extension rules","Safety while waiting"],"steps":[{"h":"Start the clock","say":["Officer, what time did you arrive here with him?","Did you consult a health professional before using the 136?"],"hear":"\"About 01:20. We rang the mental health line. The suite's full.\"","mark":"Clock started 01:20"},{"h":"Agree roles with the police","say":["The clock runs until 01:20 tomorrow. A&E is now his place of safety.","Can you stay until A&E staff can keep him safe? Then we can agree a handover.","Is he carrying anything risky?"],"hear":"\"No weapons. We need to get back out.\"","mark":"Police role agreed"},{"h":"Check his physical state","say":["Mark, I'm Dr Shah. You're safe here. How did you cut your head?","Have you taken any tablets tonight?"],"hear":"\"Fell over. I took two packets of paracetamol about nine.\"","mark":"Paracetamol overdose identified"},{"h":"Get medical treatment going","say":["A&E team, he took two packets of paracetamol at 21:00. He needs a level now and treatment.","His head wound needs checking because he's drunk.","Once he's sober enough, we'll assess his mental health."],"hear":"\"Will that take long?\"","mark":"Medical care prioritised"},{"h":"Brief risk screen","say":["What was happening near the road?","What's been going on recently?"],"hear":"\"I wanted to die. Wife left in March. Lost my job.\"","mark":"High suicide risk identified"},{"h":"Explain his position","say":["You're not under arrest. You're here for your safety for up to 24 hours.","When you're sober, a psychiatrist and an AMHP will see you together.","Is there someone we can call?"],"hear":"\"My brother. Not my wife.\"","mark":"Support identified"},{"h":"Plan and record","say":["Contact the AMHP now to book a joint assessment once he is sober.","An extension of up to 12 hours is only for medical reasons, not for waiting.","Close observation in A&E; record times and roles."],"hear":"\"Thank you. We'll head off once your staff are ready.\"","mark":"Plan completed"}],"table":{"cols":["136 element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Clock","Arrived 01:20","24 hours from arrival"],["Physical risk","Paracetamol at 21:00, head cut, drunk","Medical treatment first"],["Suicide risk","Wanted to die near a road; losses","High risk; close observation"],["Roles","Police want to leave","Agree a safe handover with A&E"],["Extension","Assessment delayed by intoxication","Only extend for clinical reasons"]]},"key":"Intoxication delays the interview, not the clock. Treat the overdose and head injury now, and book the AMHP early.","objmap":[[1,6],[4],[6],[2],[7]]},"c-0-2-6":{"name":"Ryan's tribunal tomorrow: honest evidence on the four tests","why":"Makes sure the tribunal gets accurate, current, first-hand evidence on each legal test, from the right clinician.","frame":["Nature","Degree","Necessity (health, safety, others)","Appropriate treatment available","Aftercare (Section 117)"],"steps":[{"h":"Escalate first","say":["Dr Patel, Dr Rao is off sick. Ideally an approved clinician who knows Ryan should give evidence.","I've known him six weeks. I can help, but I'll stay within what I've seen."],"hear":"\"Go on. What's out of date?\"","mark":"Escalated to covering consultant"},{"h":"Update the report","say":["His aripiprazole went from 10 to 15 mg ten days ago.","He has had three escorted ground leaves without problems."],"hear":"\"Good. Put that in an addendum.\"","mark":"Report updated"},{"h":"Nature and degree","say":["Nature: first-episode psychosis with fixed beliefs about his neighbours.","Degree: he still believes they watch him through the walls."],"hear":"\"Is he dangerous?\"","mark":"Nature and degree established"},{"h":"Necessity","say":["He has confronted the neighbours angrily before.","He only takes tablets because he has to and feels slowed down.","Without detention he'd likely stop and the risk to the neighbours would return.","Appropriate treatment is available here: the dose increase is recent and his leave is going well."],"hear":"\"Good. Stick to facts.\"","mark":"Necessity established"},{"h":"Prepare Ryan","say":["Ryan, tomorrow I'll say what I've seen: the progress and the worries.","Your solicitor arrives at nine. You can speak too."],"hear":"\"Will you say I'm mad?\"","mark":"Patient prepared"},{"h":"Answer his fears","say":["I'll describe your illness and how you've improved, not label you.","If they discharge you, we plan aftercare. If not, we keep working on leave."],"hear":"\"What happens if they let me go?\"","mark":"Fears addressed"},{"h":"Aftercare and close","say":["If discharged, Section 117 aftercare: CMHT follow-up and medication plan.","Dr Patel, can you join if possible? I'll say 'I don't know' if asked beyond my knowledge."],"hear":"\"I'll try to attend.\"","mark":"Aftercare planned"}],"table":{"cols":["Tribunal test","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Nature","First-episode psychosis","Mental disorder present"],["Degree","Still believes neighbours watch him","Active symptoms"],["Necessity","Past angry confrontations; reluctant on tablets","Risk to others if discharged now"],["Appropriate treatment","Aripiprazole up 10 days ago; ground leave going well","Treatment available and working"],["Aftercare","Wants to go home","Section 117 plan ready"]]},"key":"Give the tribunal balance: the progress as well as the risk, all from what you have seen yourself, and say 'I don't know' rather than guess.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[1],[1,2],[7]]},"c-0-2-7":{"name":"Chloe hot and stiff on haloperidol: which law lets us take bloods?","why":"Gets urgent bloods for a possible neuroleptic malignant syndrome lawfully, by persuasion first and the Mental Capacity Act if needed.","frame":["Clinical urgency","Persuasion","Which law","Capacity","Best interests and least restriction","Drug screen consent"],"steps":[{"h":"Get the obs","say":["Tom, what are her observations?","When did haloperidol start? How is her confusion?"],"hear":"\"Temp 38.2, pulse 118, BP up and down. Stiff. Started haloperidol three days ago. More muddled than this morning.\"","mark":"Possible NMS identified"},{"h":"Act on the medicine","say":["Hold the haloperidol now.","Let's get an ECG, and I'll speak to the medical team about transfer.","We need CK, U&E, full blood count and a blood culture."],"hear":"\"But she won't let us take bloods.\"","mark":"Antipsychotic stopped"},{"h":"Persuade Chloe","say":["Chloe, I'm Dr Shah. You've got a fever and stiff muscles. It could be the new medicine.","A blood test tells us if your muscles are being harmed.","What worries you about it?"],"hear":"\"You'll use my blood for experiments. I hate needles.\"","mark":"Reasons for refusal identified"},{"h":"Make it easier","say":["It's only for these tests and then disposed of.","Would it help with a nurse you trust and someone holding your hand?"],"hear":"\"Maybe with Tom and someone holding my hand.\"","mark":"Bloods accepted"},{"h":"Check capacity if she still refuses","say":["Can you tell me back what could happen if we don't test?","What makes you decide yes or no?"],"hear":"\"It could get worse, and I could get really ill.\"","mark":"Capacity assessed"},{"h":"Explain the law to the nurse","say":["Tom, the Section covers treatment for her mania, not this possible reaction. We use the MCA.","If she lacks capacity, we act in her best interests with the least restraint.","The urine drug screen needs her consent. We can't force it."],"hear":"\"Got it. No holding her under the Section.\"","mark":"Correct law applied"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["Bloods now, ECG, regular obs, and medical review.","If she worsens, she goes to the general hospital.","Document her decision, capacity and the reasons."],"hear":"\"I'll set it up.\"","mark":"Plan completed"}],"table":{"cols":["Legal or clinical step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Clinical urgency","Fever, tachycardia, labile BP, rigid, more muddled","Possible NMS; stop haloperidol, urgent bloods"],["Which law","Nurse asks to hold her under the Section","Section 63 is not for unrelated physical illness; use the MCA"],["Persuasion","Fears experiments and needles","Address the fear; trusted nurse and hand-holding"],["Capacity","Can explain the risk back","Likely has capacity; respect her decision"],["Drug screen","Refuses urine screen","Needs consent; record refusal"]]},"key":"The Section treats the mental disorder, not a suspected drug reaction. Persuade first, assess capacity for this decision, then use the MCA if needed.","objmap":[[1,2],[],[3,5],[2],[6]]},"c-0-2-8":{"name":"Andrew off his depot: does the CTO recall test apply?","why":"Helps the RC decide on recall, serve it properly, convey Andrew safely and plan the 72 hours.","frame":["Recall criteria","Serving the notice","Getting him to hospital","The 72 hours","Release or revoke","Treatment on recall"],"steps":[{"h":"Hear the facts","say":["Jo, tell me what you saw today beyond the missed depot.","Any risk to himself or others?"],"hear":"\"Windows covered in foil, very suspicious, shouted at his neighbour. No weapons or self-harm.\"","mark":"Relapse identified"},{"h":"Gather more","say":["Is he eating? Sleeping?","What does he say about the injection?"],"hear":"\"Fridge nearly empty. Says it makes him a zombie. Thinks there are cameras in the walls.\"","mark":"Self-neglect and side effects identified"},{"h":"Test the recall criteria","say":["He needs hospital treatment for his mental disorder.","Without recall, there's a risk to his health and his neighbour.","That meets the test, but it's the RC's decision."],"hear":"\"Agreed. I'll talk to Dr Patel.\"","mark":"Recall criteria met"},{"h":"Plan the notice and conveyance","say":["The RC signs a CTO3. Best served by handing it to him.","Would he come in your car?","If he won't come, he's AWOL. A Section 135(2) warrant may be needed to enter."],"hear":"\"He'll come in my car, not an ambulance.\"","mark":"Safe conveyance planned"},{"h":"Talk with Andrew","say":["Andrew, Dr Patel wants you in hospital for a few days to get you settled.","You'll be there up to 72 hours, then we decide next steps together.","We'll talk about the side effects, and whether tablets could work."],"hear":"\"How long will you keep me? Will they take my flat?\"","mark":"Patient informed"},{"h":"Release or revoke","say":["Within 72 hours, the RC either releases you on the CTO or revokes it.","Revoking needs an AMHP to agree and brings back the Section 3.","Your flat stays yours."],"hear":"\"Will they inject me again without talking?\"","mark":"Options explained"},{"h":"Treatment and records","say":["Treatment on recall follows the CTO11, or Section 62 if urgent.","Document times, notice, conveyance and risk.","Tell the MHA office."],"hear":"\"I'll sort the car.\"","mark":"Plan completed"}],"table":{"cols":["CTO step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Recall criteria","Foil windows, shouting at neighbour, refused depot","Needs hospital treatment; risk without recall"],["Notice","Home visit possible","Hand the CTO3 to him"],["Conveyance","Will go in Jo's car","Least restrictive safe transport"],["72 hours","Fears how long","Release on CTO or revoke with AMHP"],["Treatment","Prefers tablets; hates depot effects","Discuss options; CTO11 covers treatment on recall"]]},"key":"Missing a depot alone is not grounds for recall. The test is need for hospital treatment plus risk without it, and the 72 hours ends in release or revocation.","objmap":[[1,3],[6],[4],[5,6],[2,5]]},"c-0-2-9":{"name":"Can she leave tonight? Delirium first, then capacity, then the right law","why":"Finds the cause of her confusion, tests capacity for one decision, and keeps her on the ward lawfully and gently under the MCA.","frame":["Delirium (4AT)","Capacity for this decision","Proportionate restraint (MCA s5-6)","Urgent DoLS authorisation","Family, LPA and IMCA"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle her before anything else","say":["Mrs Patel, I'm one of the doctors on call tonight. You look worried. Shall we sit here for a minute?","Tell me about Ramesh while we sit. I'll ask the nurse for two cups of tea."],"hear":"\"He'll be waiting for his tea. I have to catch the bus before it stops.\"","mark":"Distress settled without force"},{"h":"Get the facts from the nurse","say":["When was she last her usual self?","What has she had today: codeine, sedatives, anything new?","Her observations, temperature, bowels and fluid balance?"],"hear":"Nurse: \"Clear as a bell at six. Codeine this afternoon, bowels not open, temp 37.8, NEWS2 2.\"","mark":"Acute onset and fluctuation identified"},{"h":"4AT at the bedside","say":["Can you tell me how old you are, and your date of birth?","Where are we just now? And what year is it?","Could you list the months backwards from December for me, starting at December?"],"hear":"\"Eighty-four... This is the community centre, isn't it? 1998. December, November, October... I've lost it.\"","mark":"4AT 4 or more: delirium likely"},{"h":"Capacity for one decision: leaving tonight","say":["You have a water infection and you're very dry. The drip and antibiotics are treating it. What might happen if you went home now?","Can you tell me back what I just said about the infection?","How do you weigh going home tonight against staying until it settles?"],"hear":"\"I'm not poorly. What would I want a drip for? I just need to get home to Ramesh.\"","mark":"Cannot retain or weigh: lacks capacity for this decision, tonight"},{"h":"Name the law for the team","say":["Her confusion comes from a physical illness, and she lacks capacity to decide to leave, so the Mental Capacity Act covers keeping her here tonight, not the Mental Health Act.","Talking, redirecting and walking with her are fine. Holding her is lawful only to prevent harm, using the least force that works.","Please give an urgent DoLS authorisation tonight and send the standard request; the urgent one lasts up to 7 days."],"hear":"Registrar: \"So you're not sectioning her?\"","mark":"MCA and DoLS chosen over the MHA, with reasons"},{"h":"Least restrictive care and the cause","say":["Can she have one nurse with her tonight, low lights, her glasses on and a clock she can see?","Let's stop the codeine, treat the constipation, offer drinks often and chart her urine.","Sedation is a last resort, not a first step. If she becomes very distressed despite all this, a small dose of haloperidol for a short time, unless she has Parkinson's or Lewy body dementia."],"hear":"Nurse: \"We can do one-to-one. Her daughter is the next of kin.\"","mark":"Least restrictive measures agreed"},{"h":"Daughter, LPA and review","say":["Anjali, I'm the doctor at the hospital. Your mum is confused because of an infection; this usually improves as it is treated.","Has your mum ever made a lasting power of attorney that covers her health and care?","Could you come in tomorrow? A familiar face helps more than anything we can give her.","I'll write up the capacity assessment, why it failed, and when to reassess once she is brighter."],"hear":"\"Only for her money, not health. She was just a bit forgetful with names before. I'll come first thing.\"","mark":"No welfare LPA: best-interests decision with Anjali's views"}],"table":{"cols":["Legal and clinical step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Delirium (4AT)","Clear at six; wrong place and year; months stop at October","Acute confusion: chase the infection, constipation and codeine"],["Capacity","Cannot recall the infection; insists she is well","Lacks capacity to leave tonight only; reassess when brighter"],["Legal route","Physical illness causing the confusion","MCA and DoLS, not the MHA"],["Restraint","Settles with tea and talk; pushes when argued with","Redirection and one-to-one; force only in proportion to harm"],["Family and LPA","Daughter holds a finance LPA only","Best interests with Anjali's views; DoLS request to the local authority"]]},"key":"Capacity is judged for this decision tonight, not for her as a person. Confusion from a physical illness makes the MCA and DoLS the lawful route, not a section.","objmap":[[3],[4,7],[5],[5],[7]]},"c-0-2-10":{"name":"Refusing transfer for cellulitis: the four parts of capacity","why":"Tests capacity for one treatment decision, separates Section 3 from physical treatment, and finds the least restrictive way to get antibiotics in.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate a choice","Legal basis: s63 or MCA"],"steps":[{"h":"Open without arguing","say":["Mr Mensah, I'm one of the ward doctors. Could I look at your leg and talk it through with you?","How has the leg been, from your side?"],"hear":"\"It's fine. I told them already. I'm not going to that hospital.\""},{"h":"Find out why he refuses","say":["What worries you about the general hospital?","What do you think would happen to you there?","Is there anyone here you trust to help with this?"],"hear":"\"They put things in the medicine there. Joe's all right. Joe would open my tablets.\"","mark":"Refusal linked to a fixed belief about poison"},{"h":"Explain, then check understanding and recall","say":["Your leg has an infection of the skin called cellulitis. The heat, the swelling and your temperature all point to it.","Antibiotics through a drip work fastest. Untreated, it can spread into the blood and become life-threatening.","Could you put what I've just explained into your own words?"],"hear":"\"Skin infection, drip antibiotics, it could go into my blood.\"","mark":"Understands and retains"},{"h":"Use and weigh","say":["How does that fit with how your leg feels to you?","If the infection did spread, what would that mean for you?","What are the pros and cons of going, compared with staying?"],"hear":"\"There's no risk. Their antibiotics are poison, so nothing bad happens if I stay here.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh, because of the delusion: lacks capacity for this decision"},{"h":"Tell the nurse the legal position","say":["His section lets us treat his mental disorder. Section 63 does not cover an infection in his leg.","He can't weigh the risk because of his belief about poison, so for this decision he lacks capacity, and we act in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act.","Schizophrenia alone would not prove that; the link to the belief is what I'm writing down."],"hear":"Nurse: \"So the section doesn't let us just send him?\"","mark":"s63 limit stated; MCA applies"},{"h":"Least restrictive plan with the medical team","say":["Could the medical registrar advise whether oral antibiotics here are safe for now, in sealed packs Joe opens in front of him?","Draw round the redness, NEWS2 four-hourly and a medical review this evening.","If NEWS2 rises or the redness spreads, he transfers in his best interests, with Joe escorting and section 17 leave from his consultant."],"hear":"\"If Joe opens them in front of me, I'll take them.\"","mark":"Least restrictive option agreed, with an escalation trigger"},{"h":"Family, record and reassess","say":["Would you be happy for me to tell your brother and hear what he thinks?","I'll record which part of the decision you found hard and why, and check again tomorrow.","If you change your mind about the hospital, tell Joe or any nurse at any time."],"hear":"\"Tell my brother. He'll say take them.\"","mark":"Family views recorded; capacity review set"}],"table":{"cols":["Part of capacity","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Repeats: skin infection, drip, spread to the blood","Intact"],["Retain","Still recalls it minutes later","Intact"],["Use and weigh","'No risk, their antibiotics are poison'","Fails because of the delusion: lacks capacity for this decision"],["Communicate","Clear, steady refusal of transfer; accepts sealed tablets from Joe","Intact; his wishes shape the plan"],["Legal basis","Cellulitis unrelated to his psychosis","Not s63: MCA best interests, least restrictive"]]},"key":"A section is not a licence to treat everything, and an unwise refusal is not incapacity. Record the exact link between his belief and his failure to weigh the risk.","objmap":[[3,5],[7],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-0-3-1":{"name":"Clerking a first psychosis: the body before the first dose","why":"Looks for a physical cause of psychosis and sets safe baselines before any antipsychotic is prescribed.","frame":["Consent and chaperone","Heart and lungs","Abdomen and thyroid","Nerves and fundi","Baseline tests"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and chaperone","say":["Tom, I'm one of the ward doctors. I'd like to check your physical health tonight, because the body can sometimes cause symptoms like yours.","That means listening to your heart and chest, feeling your tummy and neck, testing your nerves and looking in your eyes. Is that OK?","Would you like a nurse with us? Nurse Smith can stay."],"hear":"\"Yeah, fine. Be quick though, I'm knackered.\"","mark":"Consent and chaperone recorded"},{"h":"Focused physical history","say":["Any headaches, fits, blackouts or head injuries?","What do you use: cannabis, spice, alcohol, anything else?","Anyone in the family with thyroid trouble, or who died suddenly young?"],"hear":"\"Headaches at the end of the day. I smoke weed every day, more lately to sleep. My uncle's got thyroid.\"","mark":"Daily cannabis and headaches identified"},{"h":"Heart and lungs","say":["I'll check your pulse and blood pressure, lying and then standing.","Does your heart ever race or pound?","Deep breaths in and out through your mouth while I listen."],"hear":"\"Sometimes it pounds, yeah.\"","mark":"Palpitations: think thyroid, cannabis or anxiety"},{"h":"Abdomen and thyroid","say":["Lie back for me. Tell me if anywhere hurts as I press.","I'll stand behind you and feel your neck; take a sip of water and swallow when I ask.","Hold your arms out straight, fingers spread."],"hear":"\"I've lost a bit of weight. I'm not eating much.\"","mark":"Weight loss: check thyroid and nutrition"},{"h":"Nerves and fundi","say":["Follow my finger with your eyes, keeping your head still.","Push against my hands, now pull. Then I'll check your reflexes and watch you walk.","I'll dim the lights and shine a light in each eye to see the back of them."],"hear":"\"That light's bright. Is that it?\"","mark":"Neurological and fundal findings recorded, normal or not"},{"h":"Baseline tests","say":["Tonight I'd like bloods: blood count, kidney, liver and thyroid tests, sugar, cholesterol, calcium and prolactin.","A heart tracing, and a urine sample to check for drugs, which helps us choose treatment.","And your weight, waist and blood pressure written down as a starting point."],"hear":"\"Do I have to do the wee test?\"","mark":"Baselines requested: bloods, ECG, urine drug screen"},{"h":"Hand over to the night team","say":["Anything he declines, I'll write what was offered, why he said no, and when we'll try again tomorrow.","Night team: ECG before any antipsychotic, and please chase the drug screen and thyroid result.","I looked for fever, confusion, focal signs, papilloedema and fits. If any appear, call me."],"hear":"Nurse: \"Got it. The ECG goes top of the list.\"","mark":"Outstanding parts dated; owners named"}],"table":{"cols":["System","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["Consent","Agrees; chaperone present","Record both; date any refusal and re-offer"],["Heart","Pounding heart at times","Pulse, BP and ECG; consider thyroid and cannabis"],["Abdomen and thyroid","Weight loss; uncle with thyroid disease","TFTs before calling it a primary psychosis"],["Nerves and fundi","Headaches at the end of the day","Fundi and focal signs to exclude raised pressure"],["Substances","Daily cannabis, more lately","Urine screen; may be driving the psychosis"]]},"key":"A psychiatric admission is no reason to skip the body. Fundi and neurology are the parts most often missed, and an undated 'declined' is not a plan.","objmap":[[1],[3,5],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-0-3-2":{"name":"QTc 510 on haloperidol: hold, check, present, escalate","why":"Makes the heart safe today, reads the ECG properly, and keeps a plan for her mania.","frame":["Rate and rhythm","Intervals: QT by hand","ST segments and T waves","Causes: drugs and electrolytes","STEMI recognition"],"steps":[{"h":"Hold the noon dose","say":["Please hold the noon haloperidol and write the reason on the drug chart.","Can I see the full 12-lead trace, not just the printed summary?"],"hear":"Nurse Kim: \"Holding it. She's only had lorazepam PRN, nothing else new.\"","mark":"Dose held, reason documented"},{"h":"Read the ECG in order","say":["This is Grace Bennett's 12-lead ECG from this morning, at standard speed and calibration.","Sinus rhythm at about 100. Normal axis. PR and QRS within normal limits.","I measured QT by hand in lead II across three beats and corrected it with Fridericia, as her rate is fast. It is still over 500."],"hear":"Nurse Kim: \"So the machine was right?\"","mark":"QTc above 500 confirmed by hand"},{"h":"ST segments and T waves","say":["No ST elevation or depression in any lead.","T waves upright where expected, no U waves, so nothing yet to suggest low potassium."],"hear":"Nurse Kim: \"Good. So it's the drug?\"","mark":"No ischaemia on this trace"},{"h":"Ask Grace","say":["Grace, your heart tracing shows a change we need to look into. Have you felt dizzy, faint, or your heart racing?","Any chest pain or breathlessness?","Has anyone in your family collapsed suddenly or had heart trouble?"],"hear":"\"A bit dizzy standing up. Dad had a pacemaker and collapsed at home. But my heart's magnificent!\"","mark":"Dizziness and family history of collapse identified"},{"h":"Look for the causes","say":["Let's send potassium, magnesium, calcium and kidney tests now.","Check the chart for anything else that lengthens QT: antiemetics, antibiotics, methadone, other antipsychotics.","She's eating little and drinking tea all day, so her salts may be low."],"hear":"Nurse Kim: \"Last bloods were Friday. Nothing else on the chart.\"","mark":"Electrolytes requested; other QT drugs excluded"},{"h":"Present to the consultant","say":["Situation: Grace Bennett, Section 3 for mania, QTc over 500 on day three of haloperidol, confirmed by hand.","Background: dizzy on standing, father had a pacemaker after a collapse, no other QT drugs.","Assessment: real risk of torsades. Recommendation: haloperidol stopped, electrolytes today, repeat ECG, medical advice, and a heart-friendlier option for her mania."],"hear":"Consultant: \"Agreed. Get medical advice and we'll choose the switch at ward round.\"","mark":"SBAR delivered with a clear request"},{"h":"Explain to Grace","say":["Your heart is taking a little longer than it should to reset between beats, so we've paused one medicine to keep you safe.","We'll still treat your mood with something kinder to the heart, and repeat the tracing.","If you feel faint, your heart flutters or you black out, tell a nurse straight away."],"hear":"\"Fine. But nothing that slows me down.\"","mark":"Plan for the mania kept"},{"h":"Know a heart attack when you see one","say":["New ST elevation in two neighbouring leads with chest pain is a heart attack.","That means 999 for primary PCI and aspirin 300 mg if safe, not a referral letter."],"hear":"Nurse Kim: \"So with that one, we don't wait for the medics.\"","mark":"STEMI pathway named"}],"table":{"cols":["ECG step","What you found or heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Rate and rhythm","Sinus, about 100","Bazett overcorrects at fast rates: use Fridericia"],["QT by hand","Still over 500 in lead II","Real torsades risk: haloperidol stays held"],["ST and T waves","No ST change, no U waves","No ischaemia; electrolytes still needed"],["History","Dizzy standing; father collapsed and had a pacemaker","Possible inherited risk: get medical or cardiology advice"],["Causes","No other QT drugs; poor intake","Correct potassium and magnesium before any restart"]]},"key":"Never act on the machine's QTc alone: measure it and correct for rate. The job has two halves, protect her heart today and still treat her mania.","objmap":[[2],[1],[5],[6],[8]]},"c-0-3-3":{"name":"Chest pain at 04:00 on a stand-alone unit: 999 first","why":"Recognises a heart attack quickly, treats it, and gets a detained patient to a heart centre lawfully and without delay.","frame":["A–E assessment and NEWS2","12-lead ECG","999 and immediate treatment","MHA: s17 leave, escort, papers","SBAR handover"],"steps":[{"h":"A to E","say":["Gary, I'm the doctor. I'll check you over quickly. Show me where the pain is.","Ade, sats, pulse, BP in both arms and a blood sugar, please, and bring the emergency bag and defibrillator.","Are you able to talk in full sentences? Any breathlessness?"],"hear":"\"It's crushing. Right in the middle, down my left arm. Since half three.\"","mark":"Cardiac-sounding pain with NEWS2 7: emergency"},{"h":"12-lead ECG","say":["Let's get a 12-lead ECG on now.","ST elevation in II, III and aVF: this looks like an inferior heart attack."],"hear":"Ade: \"Sats 94, BP 100/60, pulse 104, sugar 11.\"","mark":"Suspected STEMI"},{"h":"999 before paperwork","say":["Ring 999 now: suspected STEMI, 58-year-old man, pain since 03:30. The paperwork can follow.","Gary, it's likely your heart. An ambulance is coming to take you to a heart unit."],"hear":"\"My heart? Am I dying?\"","mark":"999 called before any MHA paperwork"},{"h":"Treat while you wait","say":["Any allergy to aspirin, stomach ulcers or black stools?","Please chew this 300 mg aspirin. Ade, write the time down.","No GTN while his pressure is this low. Oxygen only if his sats drop below 94."],"hear":"\"No allergies. Never had ulcers.\"","mark":"Aspirin given and timed"},{"h":"The MHA side","say":["I'm ringing the on-call consultant for section 17 escorted leave to the heart centre.","Copies of his section papers and drug chart go with him, with a nurse escort.","Nothing holds up the ambulance. If I can't reach the consultant in time, I'll record why and keep trying."],"hear":"Consultant: \"Granted. Escorted leave to the cardiac centre; send Ade with him.\"","mark":"s17 leave and escort arranged"},{"h":"SBAR to the paramedics","say":["Situation: Gary Collins, 58, suspected inferior STEMI, pain since 03:30, NEWS2 7.","Background: diabetes, smokes 20 a day, detained under Section 2 with escorted leave.","Assessment and request: aspirin 300 mg given at 04:06, no GTN because BP is low; please take him to a primary PCI centre."],"hear":"Paramedic: \"Thanks. Straight to the cath lab.\"","mark":"SBAR handover given in under a minute"},{"h":"Make the ward safe and write it down","say":["Can the nurse in charge cover the ward while Ade escorts him?","I'll write a timed note: bleep, ECG, aspirin, 999 call, leave granted, departure.","Gary, would you like us to let your daughter know?"],"hear":"\"Yeah. Tell her. We've not spoken in months.\"","mark":"Times documented; next of kin considered"}],"table":{"cols":["Step","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["A–E and NEWS2","Sweaty, BP 100/60, sats 94, NEWS2 7","Emergency response and continuous monitoring"],["ECG","ST elevation in II, III, aVF","Inferior STEMI: primary PCI pathway"],["Treatment","No aspirin allergy or bleeding","Aspirin 300 mg; avoid GTN with low BP"],["Legal","Section 2 on a stand-alone unit","s17 escorted leave and papers, never delaying 999"],["Handover","Pain began at 03:30","Onset time decides PCI eligibility: say it first"]]},"key":"With no medical team on site, the ambulance is the treatment. Call 999 the moment you suspect a heart attack; section 17 leave and papers follow, never lead.","objmap":[[2,7],[3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-0-3-4":{"name":"Fever, stiffness and confusion after a dose rise: NMS","why":"Recognises neuroleptic malignant syndrome, stops the cause, and gets him to medical care with a safe handover.","frame":["Fever","Rigidity","Altered consciousness","Autonomic instability","CK and kidneys"],"steps":[{"h":"A to E at the bedside","say":["Liam, I'm the doctor. Squeeze my hand. Can you tell me where you are?","A full set of observations and a blood sugar now, please.","Can you take a sip of water for me?"],"hear":"\"Hot... hospital? My arms won't... move right.\"","mark":"Altered consciousness identified"},{"h":"Look for the core features","say":["I'm going to bend your arm at the elbow. Tell me if it hurts.","What was his blood pressure an hour ago, and how is his urine?","When exactly was the dose increased, and when was the IM given?"],"hear":"Nurse: \"168/95, it was 110/70. Urine dark and scanty. Raised two days ago, IM yesterday.\"","mark":"Lead-pipe rigidity and autonomic instability: NMS likely"},{"h":"Stop the cause","say":["Stop all antipsychotics now, including the PRN, and write the time on the chart.","Cool him, get IV access, and start fluids if the ward can.","Bloods: CK, U&E, LFT, FBC, CRP, glucose and blood cultures."],"hear":"Nurse: \"Stopped. Cannula's in.\"","mark":"Antipsychotics stopped and timed"},{"h":"Think of the differentials","say":["This could be sepsis, serotonin syndrome or lethal catatonia.","NMS fits best: it built up over two days after a dose rise and an IM, with lead-pipe rigidity, and no clonus or diarrhoea.","Whichever it is, he needs a medical bed tonight."],"hear":"Consultant on the phone: \"Agreed. Get him to A&E.\"","mark":"Differentials weighed"},{"h":"999 and the legal side","say":["Ring 999: suspected neuroleptic malignant syndrome, temperature 39, pulse 120, BP swinging.","Please authorise section 17 escorted leave; his papers and drug chart travel with him.","If he becomes agitated, use lorazepam, never more haloperidol."],"hear":"Paramedic: \"We're ten minutes away.\"","mark":"Transfer arranged with s17 leave"},{"h":"SBAR to the receiving team","say":["Situation: Liam Turner, 34, suspected NMS: fever 39, rigidity, confusion, swinging BP.","Background: schizophrenia on Section 3; haloperidol raised two days ago, IM dose yesterday; dark scanty urine.","Request: urgent CK and kidney function, IV fluids, consider critical care; no antipsychotics."],"hear":"Paramedic: \"Got it. Any allergies?\"","mark":"SBAR with the dose timeline"},{"h":"Tell his mother, with his consent","say":["Liam, can I talk to your mum about what's happening?","Mrs Turner, Liam has had a rare but serious reaction to one of his medicines. We've stopped it and he's going to the general hospital.","It usually improves once the drug is stopped. Here is the ward number; I'm your contact tonight."],"hear":"\"He was fine on Thursday. Nobody told me anything. Is he going to be OK?\"","mark":"Family updated honestly, with consent"},{"h":"Report and plan ahead","say":["I'll complete an incident report and a Yellow Card to the MHRA.","Any future antipsychotic waits at least two weeks after recovery, starts low with a different drug, and has CK and temperature checks."],"hear":"Nurse: \"I'll add an alert to his record.\"","mark":"Reporting and safe rechallenge planned"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["Fever","39.0 and sweating","Core feature; still screen for sepsis"],["Rigidity","Stiff arms, lead-pipe tone","Points to NMS rather than serotonin syndrome"],["Consciousness","Confused, slow to answer","Altered mental state, not agitation to sedate"],["Autonomic","Pulse 120, BP 110/70 to 168/95","Instability: needs medical monitoring"],["CK and kidneys","Dark, scanty urine","Possible rhabdomyolysis: CK, U&E, IV fluids"]]},"key":"Confusion and agitation after an antipsychotic rise are a reason to stop the drug, not to give more. Feel for rigidity and send a CK before any IM.","objmap":[[2,3],[3],[4],[6],[7]]},"c-0-3-5":{"name":"Not anxiety: serotonin syndrome and the Hunter criteria","why":"Spots serotonin toxicity from the drug list and clonus, stops the culprits, and sets up safe follow-up.","frame":["Serotonergic drugs","Clonus: spontaneous, inducible, ocular","Autonomic signs","Neuromuscular signs","Differentials: NMS, anticholinergic"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and timeline","say":["Mrs Price, I'm from the liaison psychiatry team. You look really uncomfortable; please don't apologise.","When did this start, and what changed in the days before?"],"hear":"\"Saturday night. The legs since this morning. The GP gave me tramadol on Friday.\"","mark":"New serotonergic drug identified"},{"h":"The full drug list","say":["How much tramadol have you been taking, and any extra?","Anything else: cough or cold remedies, herbal products like St John's wort, or recreational drugs?","And your sertraline: what dose, and for how long?"],"hear":"\"Sertraline 100 for two years. One or two extra tramadol on Sunday. A cough syrup from the chemist.\"","mark":"Possible dextromethorphan: another serotonergic drug"},{"h":"Look for clonus","say":["I'm going to push your foot up sharply and see whether it keeps jerking.","Follow my finger slowly from side to side.","Let me check your reflexes and your temperature."],"hear":"A&E doctor: \"Sustained ankle clonus both sides, brisk knees, temp 37.9.\"","mark":"Hunter criteria met"},{"h":"Explain the diagnosis to A&E","say":["This is serotonin syndrome, not anxiety: a serotonergic drug plus inducible clonus with agitation and sweating meets Hunter.","NMS builds over days with lead-pipe rigidity and slowness; this came on within hours, with clonus and diarrhoea.","Anticholinergic toxicity gives hot, dry skin and a dry mouth; she's sweating."],"hear":"A&E doctor: \"Right. What do we do?\"","mark":"Distinguished from NMS and anticholinergic toxicity"},{"h":"Stop and support","say":["Stop the sertraline, the tramadol and the cough syrup.","Admit under the medical team with cardiac monitoring, CK, U&E and fluids, and check TOXBASE.","Benzodiazepines for agitation; if she gets hotter or stiffer, critical care and cyproheptadine on specialist advice."],"hear":"A&E doctor: \"I'll call the medics.\"","mark":"Causative drugs stopped; admission agreed"},{"h":"Ask about intent and mood","say":["Mrs Price, I need to ask: were the extra tablets for pain, or were you trying to hurt yourself?","How's your mood been lately?"],"hear":"\"For the pain, honestly. My mood's fine. I've got grandchildren.\"","mark":"Accidental; no suicidal intent"},{"h":"Explain and plan follow-up","say":["Two of your medicines together pushed a brain chemical called serotonin too high. It usually settles within a day or two of stopping them.","Please don't restart the sertraline until we've reviewed you; I'll see you on the ward in a day or two.","I'll write to your GP: no tramadol with this antidepressant, another painkiller, and the plan for your mood."],"hear":"\"So the tablets did this together? I'll be careful what I buy.\"","mark":"GP informed; liaison follow-up booked"}],"table":{"cols":["Hunter item","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["Serotonergic drugs","Sertraline, tramadol, cough syrup","Two or three serotonergic agents"],["Clonus","Sustained ankle clonus","Core Hunter feature: diagnosis made"],["Autonomic","Sweating, diarrhoea, 37.9","Moderate toxicity; watch the temperature"],["Neuromuscular","Brisk reflexes, tremor, jerking legs","Serotonin excess, not NMS rigidity"],["Mental state","Agitated and restless","Toxicity, not anxiety; benzodiazepine if needed"]]},"key":"Check the legs before calling it anxiety. Clonus makes the diagnosis, and the drug history must include over-the-counter and recreational products.","objmap":[[4],[2],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-0-3-6":{"name":"Lithium 1.6 and vomiting: treat the patient, not the number","why":"Stops further lithium, confirms toxicity at the bedside, finds the cause and gets her to the medical team when needed.","frame":["Level and sample timing","Signs of toxicity","Causes: NSAID and dehydration","Kidney tests and ECG","Medical referral"],"steps":[{"h":"Hold the dose","say":["Please hold the 18:00 lithium and the ibuprofen, and write why on the chart.","Can you make sure the night team hears it at handover too?"],"hear":"Nurse: \"Done. I'll flag it at handover.\"","mark":"Next dose and NSAID held"},{"h":"Check the timing","say":["Mrs Whitfield, I'm the ward doctor. When did you last have your lithium?","Have you taken any extra tablets?","What time was your blood taken this morning?"],"hear":"\"Ten last night, from the nurse. Nothing extra. Blood was about half nine.\"","mark":"Roughly 12-hour level: the 1.6 is valid"},{"h":"Look for toxicity","say":["How many times have you been sick, and can you keep drinks down?","Are your hands shakier than usual? Any unsteadiness, or trouble getting words out?","Hold your hands out, touch my finger then your nose, and walk to the door for me."],"hear":"\"I spilt my tea, I'm that shaky. I went dizzy standing and swayed on the way to the loo.\"","mark":"Coarse tremor and unsteadiness: clinical toxicity"},{"h":"Find the cause","say":["You started ibuprofen on Monday? Any water tablets or blood pressure tablets?","Ibuprofen slows how fast your kidneys clear lithium, and vomiting dries you out, so the level climbs."],"hear":"\"I asked for the ibuprofen. No other tablets.\"","mark":"NSAID interaction and dehydration identified"},{"h":"Tests with an owner","say":["Bloods now: U&E, creatinine, eGFR, calcium, thyroid and a repeat lithium level.","An ECG, and NEWS2 with lying and standing blood pressure.","I'll chase the results myself and hand them to the night doctor."],"hear":"Nurse: \"The bloods are going now.\"","mark":"Investigations ordered with a named owner"},{"h":"Refer to the medical team","say":["She has toxic signs with a level of 1.6 and can't keep fluids down, so she needs the acute medical team for IV fluids and monitoring.","I'd refer at once for any of: confusion, worsening tremor or gait, a level above 2, rising creatinine, ECG changes or a fit."],"hear":"\"Do I have to go over to the other hospital?\"","mark":"Medical referral criteria met"},{"h":"Explain, check and record","say":["Your lithium is too high, partly from the ibuprofen and the sickness. We've stopped both, and the medical team will give you fluids.","Once it's back in range we'll restart lithium, probably at a reviewed dose, and avoid anti-inflammatory painkillers.","Can you tell me in your words what's happening and why?","I'll write it up and complete an incident report."],"hear":"\"No ibuprofen, fluids, and you'll restart the lithium later. Can I have paracetamol for my back?\"","mark":"Understanding checked; incident reported"}],"table":{"cols":["Item","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["Level and timing","1.6, about 12 hours after the dose","Valid result, above the usual 0.6–1.0 range"],["Toxic signs","Coarse tremor, swaying, vomiting three times","Clinical toxicity, not just a high number"],["Causes","Ibuprofen since Monday; few fluids kept down","Reduced clearance: stop the NSAID, rehydrate"],["Kidneys and ECG","Results pending","Named owner; AKI or ECG change escalates"],["Referral","Cannot keep fluids down; neurological signs","Acute medical team now"]]},"key":"Treat the patient, not the number. A 'normal' level with coarse tremor and ataxia is toxicity, and any level needs its sample time checked before you act on it.","objmap":[[1],[3,6],[2],[5],[6]]},"c-0-3-7":{"name":"Clozapine after three missed days: restart low, screen first","why":"Prevents a dangerous full-dose restart and screens for the side effects that kill: neutropenia, myocarditis and gut slowing.","frame":["Missed doses: the 48-hour rule","Neutropenia","Myocarditis","Gut hypomotility","Hypersalivation","Consent form (T2 or T3)"],"steps":[{"h":"Answer the nurse","say":["Please don't give 400 mg. After more than 48 hours off clozapine, a full dose can cause collapse, fits or heart problems.","We'll restart from a low dose with pharmacy once his bloods are back."],"hear":"Nurse: \"Oh. He's been off it since Wednesday.\"","mark":"Full-dose restart refused"},{"h":"Why he stopped","say":["Daniel, I'm glad you want to restart. What made you stop?","Tell me about the drooling. How bad is it?"],"hear":"\"The pillow's soaked. People notice. It's embarrassing.\"","mark":"Hypersalivation drove the refusal"},{"h":"Neutropenia screen","say":["How sore is your throat? Any ulcers in your mouth?","Any shivers or sweats?","Let me look in your mouth and feel your neck."],"hear":"\"Hurts to swallow. There's a sore bit on my gum. Hot and cold last night.\"","mark":"Possible neutropenia: urgent FBC"},{"h":"Myocarditis screen","say":["Any chest pain, tightness or breathlessness?","Does your heart race when you're lying still?","We'll do an ECG now, and bloods for troponin and CRP."],"hear":"\"Chest's a bit tight, and my heart pounds when I lie down.\"","mark":"Possible myocarditis: troponin, CRP, ECG"},{"h":"Bowels","say":["When did you last open your bowels? Any bloating, pain or vomiting?","Let me feel and listen to your tummy.","We'll start a softener and a stimulant laxative today and check every day."],"hear":"\"Five days. I'm bloated. Not been sick.\"","mark":"Constipation: active treatment started"},{"h":"Bloods, monitoring service and pharmacy","say":["Send FBC with neutrophils, CRP and troponin urgently.","Report the FBC to the clozapine monitoring service; a red result means stop and seek haematology advice.","Ask the on-call pharmacist for a written re-titration plan from 12.5 mg."],"hear":"Pharmacist: \"Start 12.5 mg, go up slowly, and watch BP and pulse.\"","mark":"Monitoring service and pharmacy involved"},{"h":"Legal authority and smoking","say":["Is clozapine listed on his T2 or T3? No dose until it is.","He stopped smoking on admission, so the same dose now gives higher blood levels; the target may be lower, with a level checked."],"hear":"Nurse: \"It's on his T3.\"","mark":"Consent form checked"},{"h":"Agree the plan with Daniel","say":["The drooling is a known side effect and we can treat it, though some remedies worsen constipation, so we'll choose carefully.","We'll restart slowly over the coming days so you feel steady and your blood tests stay safe."],"hear":"\"If the drooling stops, I'll take it.\"","mark":"Shared plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Missed doses","Off since Wednesday, over 48 hours","Re-titrate from 12.5 mg"],["Neutropenia","Sore throat, gum sore, temperature 38.2","Urgent FBC; act on the traffic-light result"],["Myocarditis","Tight chest, pounding heart, pulse 118","Troponin, CRP and ECG today"],["Gut","Bowels not open for five days, bloated","Laxatives now; daily review; ileus risk"],["Hypersalivation","Soaked pillow, embarrassed","Treat it: it is why he stopped"],["Consent","Section 3","Clozapine must be on the T2 or T3"]]},"key":"Agreeing to restart is good news, but after 48 hours the dose resets. Fever, a fast pulse and constipation are the same drug's dangers: screen before the first tablet.","objmap":[[1],[6],[3,4],[6],[7]]},"c-0-3-8":{"name":"Found on the floor at night: injury, anticoagulant, delirium","why":"Checks for injury before she is moved, gets a timely CT for a head injury on apixaban, and looks for the delirium behind the fall.","frame":["Check before moving","Head injury on an anticoagulant","Neurological observations","Delirium screen (4AT)","Transfer, s17 leave and family"],"steps":[{"h":"Check before anyone lifts her","say":["Please leave her where she is for a moment while I check her.","Mrs Marsh, I'm the doctor. You've had a fall. Does your neck hurt? Can you move your arms and legs?","I'll press gently on your hips. Tell me if it hurts."],"hear":"\"My neck's all right. Ooh, the left hip aches a bit.\"","mark":"Hip pain: lift flat, X-ray needed"},{"h":"Head injury and GCS","say":["Eyes, words, movements: what's her GCS? Are the pupils equal and reacting?","Has she been sick? Did anyone see the fall?","How long might she have been on the floor?"],"hear":"HCA: \"Nobody saw it. Found her about half two, awake and talking. Not sick.\"","mark":"Unwitnessed head injury; GCS recorded"},{"h":"The anticoagulant","say":["She's on apixaban. Anyone on an anticoagulant with a head injury needs a CT head within 8 hours.","Any reason for a scan within the hour: GCS under 13, vomiting more than once, a fit, new weakness?"],"hear":"HCA: \"No, none of those.\"","mark":"Apixaban: CT head within 8 hours"},{"h":"4AT","say":["Mrs Marsh, how old are you? And your date of birth?","What is this place, and what year is it?","Can you say the months backwards from December?"],"hear":"\"Seventy-five. This is a hotel, isn't it? December, November, October...\"","mark":"4AT positive: delirium on top of dementia"},{"h":"Causes and what not to do","say":["She was muddled last evening, so this began before the fall.","Look for infection, constipation, urine retention, pain, dehydration and new drugs: bloods and a urine culture, not a dipstick.","No sedation tonight: it worsens confusion and falls."],"hear":"HCA: \"No PRN tonight. Usually she knows she's in hospital and chats.\"","mark":"Acute change from baseline: causes listed"},{"h":"Transfer lawfully","say":["I'm calling the on-call consultant for section 17 escorted leave to A&E.","Her section papers, drug chart and a written handover go with her, with a nurse escort.","Half-hourly neurological observations until she leaves."],"hear":"HCA: \"I'll get the papers copied.\"","mark":"s17 leave and escort arranged"},{"h":"Family, report and reassurance","say":["I'll ring her family now to explain what happened and where she's going.","I'll complete an incident report and list her falls risks: apixaban, delirium, getting up at night.","Mrs Marsh, you're safe. We're getting your head and hip checked at the hospital."],"hear":"\"Is my husband coming?\"","mark":"Family informed; incident reported"}],"table":{"cols":["Item","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["Before moving","Left hip tender; neck fine","Possible fracture: flat lift and X-ray"],["Head injury","Bruised forehead, on apixaban","CT head within 8 hours"],["One-hour triggers","No vomiting, no fit, talking","Not immediate, but neuro observations now"],["Delirium","Thinks she is 75, ward is a hotel, months stop at October","4AT positive: find the cause"],["Legal","Section 2","s17 escorted leave; papers travel with her"]]},"key":"Do not blame new confusion on dementia. Her muddle began before the fall, so search for delirium and its cause, and let the apixaban, not how well she looks, decide the scan.","objmap":[[1],[3],[6],[4,5],[6]]},"c-0-3-9":{"name":"Weight gain and a raised HbA1c: keep the treatment, change the plan","why":"Keeps a young man on treatment by acting on his weight, and turns one high HbA1c into a confirmed result and a shared plan with the GP.","frame":["Metabolic risk","Relapse risk if stopped","Options: lifestyle, switch, metformin","HbA1c interpretation","GP letter and monitoring"],"steps":[{"h":"Validate","say":["Ryan, nine kilos in twelve weeks is a lot, and you're right to be upset.","Thank you for telling me instead of just stopping."],"hear":"\"None of my clothes fit. I'm done with them.\"","mark":"Concern validated; still engaged"},{"h":"Explore","say":["What has the olanzapine done for the voices?","Tell me about your eating, activity, smoking and drinking.","Anyone in the family with diabetes or heart disease?"],"hear":"\"Voices have mostly gone. I'm starving all the time, takeaways at midnight. Dad's got type 2.\"","mark":"Metabolic risks identified"},{"h":"The risk of stopping","say":["If you stop suddenly, the voices are much more likely to come back.","So let's change what's causing the weight, without losing what's working."],"hear":"\"I don't want the voices back.\"","mark":"Agrees not to stop abruptly"},{"h":"Three options","say":["One: a healthy eating and activity programme, with a dietitian if you'd like.","Two: switch slowly to a medicine with less weight gain, such as aripiprazole.","Three: add metformin, which can limit further gain. Which feels right to you?"],"hear":"\"I'd try switching, slowly, and the gym thing.\"","mark":"Shared decision recorded"},{"h":"Monitoring","say":["We'll check your weight and waist regularly, plus blood sugar, HbA1c, cholesterol and your heart risk score.","If you cut down smoking, tell us first, because it can raise the olanzapine level."],"hear":"\"OK. I smoke about ten roll-ups. I'll tell you.\"","mark":"Physical health checks booked"},{"h":"Mrs Shaw: what 51 means","say":["Mrs Shaw, your HbA1c is 51. From 48 upwards is in the diabetes range.","Have you had thirst, passing more urine, blurred vision or repeated infections?","Without symptoms, we repeat the test to confirm before calling it diabetes."],"hear":"\"No thirst. I'm up once a night to pee.\"","mark":"HbA1c above the threshold; repeat to confirm"},{"h":"Her plan and the GP","say":["Quetiapine can raise blood sugar. Today we'll check your weight, cholesterol and blood pressure.","If it's confirmed, your GP leads the diabetes care, and we'll consider whether to change the quetiapine without unsettling your mood.","I'll write to your GP this week with the result and who does what."],"hear":"\"I don't want to lose the quetiapine. It's the first thing that's worked.\"","mark":"GP letter and shared plan"}],"table":{"cols":["Item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Metabolic risk","+9 kg in 12 weeks, constant hunger, father with type 2","Well over 7% gain: act now"],["Relapse risk","Voices gone on olanzapine","Abrupt stopping risks relapse"],["Options","Prefers a slow switch and exercise","Shared decision; record his choice"],["HbA1c","51, no symptoms","Diabetes range; confirm with a repeat"],["Monitoring","Smokes ten a day; physical checks due","Regular checks and a GP letter this week"]]},"key":"Weight gain is a reason to change the plan, not to stop treatment. One HbA1c without symptoms needs confirming, not labelling, but it still needs a letter to the GP this week.","objmap":[[3,5],[4],[6],[7],[5]]},"c-0-4-1":{"name":"Monday handover: three patients in three minutes, by risk","why":"Gives the consultant what she needs to act today, with legal status, owners and deadlines for every job.","frame":["Situation","Background","Assessment","Recommendation","Legal status and leave","Owner and deadline"],"steps":[{"h":"Headline","say":["Morning. Three patients from the weekend, highest risk first: Amy Ford's self-harm, Ben Lowe's AWOL and Colin Hart's chest pain.","Everyone else was settled. I'll finish with who does what."],"hear":"Dr Morgan: \"Go ahead. Keep it tight, we've got ward round at ten.\"","mark":"Priorities set by risk"},{"h":"Amy: self-harm","say":["Amy Ford, 23, informal. On Saturday evening she cut her left forearm; superficial, cleaned and steri-stripped.","She was on general observations. Afterwards she denied ongoing intent and agreed a safety plan; I increased her to 15-minute checks and her room was searched.","I need your risk review today and a decision on her observations and leave."],"hear":"Dr Morgan: \"Fifteen-minute obs after a cut? Why not closer?\"","mark":"Highest risk handed over with a clear request"},{"h":"Take the challenge calmly","say":["Fair question. She was calm, open and handed over what she'd used, so the nurse in charge and I judged it proportionate.","I'd value your view when you see her."],"hear":"Dr Morgan: \"Fine. I'll see her first.\"","mark":"Challenge answered without defensiveness"},{"h":"Ben: AWOL on Section 2","say":["Ben Lowe, 40, on Section 2. He went AWOL from ground leave on Sunday afternoon; police brought him back at 22:00, calm and unhurt.","I sent a urine drug screen and put his section 17 leave on hold for your review. Police were informed and there's a Datix.","I should have rung you at the time, and I'm sorry. For any detained patient who goes missing, I'll call you."],"hear":"Ward manager: \"Has his family been told?\"","mark":"Legal status and leave change stated; gap owned"},{"h":"Colin: chest pain","say":["Colin Hart, 66, informal. Central chest pain on Sunday afternoon, no cardiac history.","ECG and troponin normal, pain reproduced by pressing the chest wall, so likely musculoskeletal.","I'll repeat his NEWS2 by twelve and call the medical registrar if anything changes."],"hear":"Dr Morgan: \"Fine. What are the jobs?\"","mark":"Lower-risk item kept brief, with an owner"},{"h":"Jobs, owners, times","say":["Dr Morgan: Amy's review and Ben's leave today. Nurse in charge: Ben's mother by ten.","Me: Amy's tetanus and dressing by eleven, Colin's obs by twelve, Ben's urine result by two.","All three are in the notes and on the handover sheet."],"hear":"Nurse in charge: \"I'll ring Ben's mum by ten and write it up.\"","mark":"Every task has an owner and a time"},{"h":"Check and close","say":["Is there anything else you'd like me to follow up today?","I'll make sure tonight's doctor knows Ben's leave is on hold."],"hear":"Dr Morgan: \"No. That's clear. Good handover.\"","mark":"Understanding checked; handover recorded"}],"table":{"cols":["SBAR item","What you said","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Situation","Three patients, highest risk first","The team knows the agenda in 15 seconds"],["Background","General obs; ground leave; no cardiac history","Explains how each event happened"],["Assessment","Superficial cuts with a safety plan; returned unhurt; musculoskeletal pain","Current risk, not the story"],["Recommendation","Review today; leave on hold; repeat NEWS2","A clear request for each patient"],["Legal status","Informal, Section 2, informal","Ben's leave is his responsible clinician's decision"],["Owner and deadline","Consultant, nurse in charge, me, each with a time","Nothing is left 'pending'"]]},"key":"Order by risk, not by the clock. 'Bloods pending' is not a handover: every job leaves the room with a name and a time.","objmap":[[1],[4],[6],[6],[3]]},"c-0-4-2":{"name":"Angry mother on the phone: hear everything, share only what he allows","why":"Calms an angry carer, captures new risk information, protects her son's confidentiality and gives her real next steps.","frame":["Listen and validate","Risk information","Confidentiality: what can be shared","His consent","Carer support and next steps"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and confirm identity","say":["Hello, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors on call for the ward tonight. Can I check who I'm speaking to?","I'm sorry you've had to wait. Tell me what's been happening; I'll listen first."],"hear":"\"Janet Hughes, his mother. And about time someone picked up. Nobody tells me anything!\"","mark":"Identity confirmed"},{"h":"Listen, then reflect","say":["Go on, I'm listening.","So he came home not himself, and since then nobody has called you. That sounds frightening, and lonely."],"hear":"\"He said the telly was sending him messages. He paced all night. I just want to help him.\"","mark":"Concerns heard and summarised back"},{"h":"Ask about risk","say":["What you saw at home really matters to us. Did he say anything about harming himself or anyone else?","Was he using anything, alcohol or drugs?","How did you feel around him: scared for him, or of him?"],"hear":"\"He said he'd be better off away from everyone. Came back smelling of cannabis. I was scared for him, not of him.\"","mark":"New risk information recorded"},{"h":"Explain confidentiality kindly","say":["Liam is an adult and has asked us not to share details, so I can't tell you his diagnosis or medicines tonight without his agreement.","That doesn't stop me listening, writing down what you've told me, and acting on it tonight.","I can explain in general how leave is decided, what psychosis is, and how to get support for you."],"hear":"\"So if he tried to kill himself, you wouldn't even tell me?\"","mark":"Confidentiality explained without hiding behind it"},{"h":"The limits of confidentiality","say":["If there were a serious risk to his life, we would share what's needed to keep him safe, even without his agreement.","What you've told me goes into his notes and to the nurse in charge now, and I'll see him myself before I leave."],"hear":"\"Will you ask him if I can know more?\"","mark":"Risk exception explained"},{"h":"Next steps for her","say":["Yes. I'll ask him tonight what he's happy for you to know.","I'll ask his consultant or care coordinator to ring you within two working days.","You're caring alone and exhausted. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment, and PALS can help if you want to raise a concern."],"hear":"\"Nobody's ever offered me that.\"","mark":"Concrete next steps agreed"},{"h":"Ask Liam, then close","say":["Liam, your mum rang because she's worried about you. What, if anything, are you happy for her to know?","Would your medicines be OK to share, or nothing for now?","Mrs Hughes, Liam is happy for you to know his medicines. I'll write everything up and the team will follow up."],"hear":"Liam: \"Just my meds. Nothing else.\"","mark":"Consent sought and documented"}],"table":{"cols":["Item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Listen and validate","Nobody called after leave; she's exhausted","The anger is fear: let her finish"],["Risk","'Better off away from everyone'; cannabis on leave","Record tonight; review his risk and leave"],["Confidentiality","Wants his diagnosis and medicines","Not without his consent; general information is fine"],["Consent","Liam agrees to medicines only","Share exactly that, and ask again later"],["Carer support","Caring alone, never offered an assessment","Carer's assessment, consultant call within two working days"]]},"key":"Confidentiality limits what you tell, never what you hear. Take in her information, act on the risk, and keep asking him what he will let her know.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[3],[6]]},"c-0-4-3":{"name":"Telling Megan it is schizophrenia: ask, warn, name, pause, plan","why":"Gives a hard diagnosis honestly at her pace, answers her fears, checks her safety and leaves her with hope and a plan.","frame":["Setting and who is present","What she knows and wants to know","Warning shot and naming it","Emotion and silence","Hope, plan and safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up","say":["Hello Megan, I'm a doctor in the early intervention team. Do you still want your mum to stay?","We have time today, and we won't be interrupted."],"hear":"\"Yes. I want her here.\"","mark":"Setting prepared; mother's presence agreed"},{"h":"What she already thinks","say":["Before I answer your question, what do you make of what's been happening?","What have you wondered it might be?"],"hear":"\"Voices, people at uni watching me. I thought stress, or going mad. I scared myself online.\"","mark":"Understanding explored"},{"h":"How much she wants","say":["Some people want all the detail at once, others want the main point first. What would suit you?","And you can stop me at any time."],"hear":"\"Just tell me straight.\"","mark":"Information preference agreed"},{"h":"Warning shot, then the word","say":["I'm afraid I have some important news, and it may be hard to hear.","From everything you've told us, the team believes you have an illness called schizophrenia.","I'll stop there for a moment. What's going through your mind?"],"hear":"\"No. That's not me. That's people who are dangerous.\"","mark":"Diagnosis named; denial met with space"},{"h":"Respond to the emotion and the myths","say":["That's what films show, so it makes sense that's what you picture.","Most people with schizophrenia are never violent; they're more likely to be hurt by others.","Many people recover well and go back to study and work."],"hear":"Helen: \"Did I cause this?\"","mark":"Fears addressed"},{"h":"Hope and a clear plan","say":["Helen, nothing you did caused this. It comes from a mix of genes, stress and other things.","Megan, treatment has three parts: a medicine to quieten the voices, a talking therapy called CBT for psychosis, and family sessions.","Your care coordinator works with you for up to three years, and we'll help you stay on track with university."],"hear":"\"Will I be on tablets forever? Can I still finish uni?\"","mark":"Hope and a plan given"},{"h":"Ask about safety","say":["News like this, or the voices, can leave people feeling hopeless. Have you ever felt you'd be better off dead?","Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to harm yourself?"],"hear":"\"When the voices are bad, I've thought everyone would be better off without me. I'd never do it to Mum.\"","mark":"Passive thoughts, no plan: safety plan and crisis numbers"},{"h":"Check and close","say":["What will you take away from today?","I'll see you again within a week. Here's written information and the crisis line, day or night.","With your agreement, I'll write to your GP today."],"hear":"\"It's serious, but it's treatable, and I'm seeing you next week.\"","mark":"Understanding checked; follow-up within a week"}],"table":{"cols":["Stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her understanding","Stress or 'going mad'; frightened by the internet","Start from her fears and correct gently"],["What she wants","'Tell me straight'","Name it early and plainly"],["Naming it","Shock, then denial; the film image","Silence first, then myth-busting"],["Emotion","Tears; mother asks if she caused it","Respond to both, keep Megan central"],["Safety","Passive thoughts when the voices are bad, no plan","Safety plan, crisis numbers, review within a week"],["Plan","Tablets forever? Uni?","Hope tied to a concrete plan"]]},"key":"Say the word 'schizophrenia', then stop talking. The pause matters; rushing to reassure, or answering her mother instead of her, shuts her out.","objmap":[[3],[4],[7],[6],[8]]},"c-0-4-4":{"name":"After the ligature: safe, reported, open, written down, supported","why":"Turns a frightening overnight incident into five clear actions with owners, while keeping the record honest and yourself supported.","frame":["Safety now","Report the incident","Records and statement","Duty of candour","Support for you and the team"],"steps":[{"h":"Answer her question honestly, then listen","say":["Honestly, I feel shaken. I saw him yesterday and I keep going over it.","Before we plan, can you tell me what you have heard so far?"],"hear":"\"The night nurse found him at ten past two, tied to the wardrobe hinge. He's awake on the medical ward now.\""},{"h":"Safety now: Mr A, the room, the other patients","say":["Who is medically responsible for him on the medical ward, and is he still detained there under Section 3?","Has the hinge been made safe, or is the room still out of use?","Is anyone else on the ward struggling after last night?"],"hear":"\"Room's taped off, Estates haven't fixed the hinge. Mr K woke up and is very unsettled.\"","mark":"Ward risk identified"},{"h":"Report it this shift","say":["Has anyone put it on the incident system yet?","Then I will do it before ten: times, what was found, what was done. Facts only, no opinions.","I will copy in the patient safety team so they can decide the PSIRF response."],"hear":"\"Nobody has. The night nurse thought the day team would.\"","mark":"Unreported incident identified"},{"h":"Records: leave yesterday alone","say":["The ward manager suggested I add a line under yesterday's entry. I won't do that.","Yesterday's note stays exactly as written. If I remember anything more, it goes in a new note, dated and timed today, saying it was written after the event.","Altering it now, even with something true, would look dishonest and would not help him."],"hear":"\"Good. Why do you think that matters so much?\"","mark":"Pressure to alter notes identified"},{"h":"Duty of candour with his mother","say":["He agreed on admission that his mother can be told about his care, so we can speak to her today.","Would you like to lead, with me there? She is seeing him at nine.","We say sorry clearly, tell her what we know so far, say what we don't know yet, promise updates, and follow it up in writing."],"hear":"\"I'll lead. Come with me at half eight. Saying sorry isn't admitting fault, remember.\"","mark":"Candour duty triggered"},{"h":"The statement","say":["I will write it in the first person: my name, grade, when I wrote it and what I used to write it.","Then what I did at three yesterday, what he told me in his words, my plan and who I told. Nothing about other staff, and 'I don't recall' where that is true.","Could you read it before it goes? I will also ring my defence organisation."],"hear":"\"Yes, send it to me first. The safety team want statements within a week.\""},{"h":"Look after each other","say":["You have known him for years. This must be hard for you too.","I'm fit to work today, but I would like a debrief for the staff, and I'll talk to my educational supervisor this week."],"hear":"\"Thank you. It is hard. I'll ask the ward manager to arrange a debrief this afternoon.\"","mark":"Second-victim risk identified"},{"h":"Summarise owners and times, then close","say":["So: I report by ten, Estates today, debrief this afternoon, you lead with his mother at half eight, my statement to you by Friday.","Have I missed anything? Shall we meet again at five?"],"hear":"\"That's everything. Five o'clock is fine.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Safety now","Hinge not fixed; Mr K unsettled","Ligature point and witnesses need action before anything else"],["Report the incident","Nobody has reported it","Report it yourself this shift; anyone involved can and should"],["Records and statement","Advice to 'add a line'","Old entry untouched; new notes dated, timed, marked retrospective; first-person factual statement"],["Duty of candour","His mother rang; he consented to her being told","Apology, known facts, updates and a written follow-up, led by the consultant today"],["Support for you and the team","You are shaken; she has known him for years","Debrief, supervisor and defence organisation; nobody carries it alone"]]},"key":"Honesty protects you more than any edited note. Leave yesterday's entry untouched, report it yourself, and say sorry to the family: an apology is not an admission of liability.","objmap":[[3],[5],[4],[6],[6]]},"c-0-4-5":{"name":"Two requests, one rule: who is asking, on what basis, and what does Mr C want?","why":"Keeps Mr C's confidence while still helping the police, supporting his mother and acting on the risk she reveals.","frame":["Identity and reason","Legal basis","Consent","Risk to others","Carer support"],"steps":[{"h":"The officer: identity and reason","say":["I'm one of the ward doctors. Could I see your warrant card and take your collar number?","What is the request about, and is anyone's life or safety at risk right now?"],"hear":"\"PC Reid, 4412. He's a witness to a pub fight last Saturday. No, nobody's in danger.\"","mark":"No immediate risk identified"},{"h":"Neither confirm nor deny; ask for the legal basis","say":["I can't confirm or deny whether anyone is a patient here.","If you need information, please send a written request through your force's data protection route, and I'll pass it to our information governance team.","I'll take your card in case anyone wants to contact you."],"hear":"\"A yes or no would save me a wasted trip.\"","mark":"No court order or written request"},{"h":"Mr C: tell him and ask what he wants","say":["A police officer has asked about you as a witness. I have not told him you are here.","What, if anything, are you happy for us to share, and with whom?","Can I write down your wishes and read them back to you?"],"hear":"\"I'll talk to him on the ward if a nurse sits in. My mum can know I'm safe, nothing else.\"","mark":"Consent given, limited"},{"h":"His mother: listen first","say":["I can hear how worried you are. Tell me what you have noticed and what frightens you most.","I can't share details about him without his agreement, but I can listen to everything you tell me, and it will help."],"hear":"\"I'm his mother, I have a right to know. Is it schizophrenia?\""},{"h":"Explain the limit and what you can offer","say":["He has said I can tell you he is safe. I can't confirm or rule out any diagnosis.","What I can do is give you general information on what we see on the ward, and how carers get support.","Would it help if I asked him whether he'd meet with you and the team together?"],"hear":"\"So I just wait? How can I help him when he comes home?\""},{"h":"Ask what worries her most and act on it","say":["You said something worries you most. What is it?","Could you send us those screenshots today? I'll tell his consultant this afternoon."],"hear":"\"He messaged his ex saying 'she'll pay'. I've got screenshots.\"","mark":"Threat to a third party identified"},{"h":"Carer support, safety-net, document","say":["You can ask the council for a carer's assessment. I'll refer you and get a named person to ring you by Monday.","If you are ever worried about his safety or anyone else's, ring the ward or 999.","I'll record each request, what I decided, why, and who I spoke to."],"hear":"\"Nobody's ever offered me that. Thank you.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Principle","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Identity and reason","PC Reid, 4412; witness to a past assault","Real officer, but a witness inquiry is not urgent"],["Legal basis","No court order, no written request, no immediate danger","No basis to disclose without consent; neither confirm nor deny"],["Consent","Mr C will see the officer with a nurse; mother told 'safe' only","Share exactly what he agreed, no more"],["Risk to others","Message to ex-partner: 'she'll pay'","Possible public interest; escalate to consultant today, record the decision"],["Carer support","Mother scared, never offered support","Listen fully, give general information, offer a carer's assessment"]]},"key":"Confidentiality limits what you tell, never what you hear. Listen to his mother fully, share only what Mr C allows, and treat the new threat as a separate decision about disclosure.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[7],[2,7]]},"c-0-4-6":{"name":"Warm no, safe yes: the scarf, the number, the follow request","why":"Holds each boundary kindly, links the requests to her fear of going home, and turns that fear into a real crisis plan.","frame":["Gift","Personal contact","Social media","Fear of leaving","Safe follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Thank her and ask what it means","say":["Jade, thank you, that's really kind. Can I ask what made you want to do this?","I'm glad the time here felt useful to you."],"hear":"\"Because you actually listened. Nobody does.\""},{"h":"Gift: keep the card, return the scarf","say":["I'd love to keep the card. The scarf is too generous for me to accept; we don't take expensive gifts from anyone we look after, so care stays fair for everyone.","You're not in any trouble at all. It was a kind thought."],"hear":"\"Will I get in trouble for it?\"","mark":"Gift boundary held"},{"h":"Number and follow request: no, with the reason","say":["I can't give you my own number. If you rang at 3am I might be asleep or abroad, and you'd be left without help. The crisis team is always awake.","I won't accept the Instagram request, and my accounts stay private. That's the same for every patient.","It doesn't mean it was fake. The care was real; this is how I keep it safe."],"hear":"\"So it was all fake, then?\"","mark":"Personal contact declined"},{"h":"Name the fear behind it","say":["It sounds like you're worried about leaving. What worries you most?","When are things hardest for you?"],"hear":"\"Nights alone in the flat. That's when it gets bad.\"","mark":"Fear of abandonment identified"},{"h":"Ask directly about safety","say":["Before you came in you took all the tablets you had. Are you having thoughts of hurting yourself now?","What's in the flat now, and who has helped with that?"],"hear":"\"Not now. Sometimes at night. Kayleigh cleared the cupboard; the pharmacy gives me a week at a time.\"","mark":"Risk at night identified"},{"h":"Build the plan together","say":["Your care coordinator will see you within 72 hours; I'll get a name, day and time before you leave.","If it gets bad at night: ice cubes and paced breathing first, then Kayleigh or Mo, then the crisis team or NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123.","Can you tell me the plan back in your words?"],"hear":"\"Ice, breathing, Kayleigh or Mo, then the crisis line. And someone's coming by Monday.\""},{"h":"Be open and close warmly","say":["I'll write in your notes that you offered the gift and asked for my number, and I'll mention it to my supervisor. That's routine, not a complaint.","I hope it goes well at home. The team is still here for you."],"hear":"\"Okay. Thanks for being straight with me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Boundary","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Gift","Card and an expensive scarf","Accept the card; decline the scarf kindly with one reason that applies to everyone"],["Personal contact","'In case I need you at 3am'","A private number is not a safety plan; the crisis team is"],["Social media","Instagram follow request sent","Do not accept; keep accounts private; the same applies after discharge"],["Fear of leaving","'Nights alone are the worst'","The requests express fear of abandonment; name it with warmth"],["Safe follow-up","Past overdose; night thoughts; week's supply","72-hour contact with a named person and a written crisis plan"]]},"key":"The boundary is not the answer to her request; the plan is. A warm no only works when it comes with a named person, a day and a time.","objmap":[[3],[4,6],[6],[3],[2,7]]},"c-0-4-7":{"name":"Five bleeps: sort by risk, not by order","why":"Puts the dying patient first, keeps every caller safe while they wait, and gets senior help with one clear call.","frame":["Physical deterioration","Violence and rapid tranquillisation","Section 136 clock","Waiting risk","Routine requests","Escalation"],"steps":[{"h":"Ward 2 first: the sick man","say":["Tell me his NEWS2 and each observation now. Is his airway clear?","Ring 999 now. Start ABCDE, give oxygen, check his glucose and repeat the observations every 15 minutes.","Someone stays with him. I'm on my way, five minutes."],"hear":"\"NEWS2 is 7. Sats 91 on air, rate 26, pulse 118, temp 38.4. Airway's fine. We haven't called 999.\"","mark":"Physical emergency identified"},{"h":"PICU: safety, status, medicines","say":["That sounds frightening, especially two staff down. Is anyone hurt?","What's his legal status, and what has he had in the last 24 hours?","Keep talking him down and offer oral lorazepam first. If IM is needed, follow the chart and monitor pulse, BP, breathing, temperature and alertness at least hourly, and every 15 minutes if he's very sedated."],"hear":"\"He caught a nurse's arm. Section 3. Olanzapine 10 at six, no PRN yet. Can we just give the IM?\"","mark":"Risk to staff identified"},{"h":"The 136 suite: medical first, then the clock","say":["What time did he arrive? Any injuries, alcohol or drugs? Has the AMHP been called?","The 24 hours runs from 22:10, so it ends at 22:10 tomorrow.","He's said he wants to die: please keep him on close observation until I see him."],"hear":"\"Arrived ten past ten. No injuries, smells of drink. AMHP's on the way.\"","mark":"Detention clock started"},{"h":"A&E: is she safe while she waits?","say":["I'm sorry about the wait. Is she being observed? Has her risk changed?","She tried to leave once, so please ask the A&E team to keep her under close watch. Someone will see her tonight."],"hear":"\"She's not on constant obs. She tried to walk out once.\"","mark":"Waiting risk identified"},{"h":"Ward 4: anything new?","say":["Is anything different tonight, or is this her usual request?","I won't start a sleeping tablet without seeing her. I'll come by about midnight; bleep me if anything changes."],"hear":"\"Nothing new. That's fine.\""},{"h":"One SBAR call to the registrar","say":["Tom, it's the CT1. I have five calls and need help.","Ward 2 man, 72, NEWS2 7, probable sepsis, 999 called. PICU patient may need IM. 136 man is suicidal and intoxicated. A&E woman tried to leave.","Could you come in and take A&E? And does the PICU man have a recent ECG?"],"hear":"\"Clear. I'll be there in 30 minutes and take A&E. Check his ECG before any IM.\"","mark":"Senior help agreed"},{"h":"Read back, record, keep moving","say":["So I go to Ward 2, then PICU, then 136. You take A&E. Ward 4 at midnight.","I'm writing each call with the time and action, and I'll hand all this over in the morning."],"hear":"\"Good. Ring me if Ward 2 gets worse before the ambulance arrives.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Priority","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Physical deterioration","NEWS2 7, new confusion, fever","Probable sepsis or delirium: 999 now, ABCDE, go first"],["Violence and rapid tranquillisation","Nurse grabbed; Section 3; olanzapine at 18:00","De-escalate, oral first, IM only per chart with monitoring and ECG check"],["Section 136 clock","Arrived 22:10, intoxicated, suicidal","24 hours from arrival; medical safety and close observation first"],["Waiting risk","Not observed, tried to leave","Lower than Ward 2 but needs observation now"],["Routine requests","Usual night sedation","Lowest priority; give a time, no hypnotic unseen"],["Escalation","Registrar asks 'what do you need?'","One SBAR call with a specific request"]]},"key":"Calling for help early is the safe choice, not a weakness. Every caller also needs a clear instruction and a time, so nobody is left simply waiting.","objmap":[[1,6],[1,6],[6],[3],[7]]},"c-1-1-1":{"name":"Ryan's last good day: building the timeline from Josh","why":"Anchors a vague story to dates, picks up catatonia and drug use, and makes 'medically fit' earn its name before any plan.","frame":["Last well and first change","Intake and output","Substances","Organic red flags","Catatonia","Tonight's safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and enlist Josh","say":["I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Thank you for bringing Ryan in; you did the right thing.","Could you stay a few minutes? You can help me piece together what has happened."],"hear":"\"Is he going to be alright? He's just sitting there not talking.\""},{"h":"Anchor to the last good day, then go forward","say":["When was Ryan last completely his usual self? Was there an event that day?","What's the very first thing you noticed after that?","And then, day by day, what came next?"],"hear":"\"Ellie's birthday meal, two weeks ago tomorrow. A few days later he stopped sleeping and paced at 4am.\"","mark":"Onset dated"},{"h":"Food, drink and toilet, with times","say":["When did he last eat a meal? Why does he say he won't eat?","How much has he drunk today? When did he last go to the toilet?"],"hear":"\"He says the food isn't right. Just a few sips today. I haven't seen him use the loo since this morning.\"","mark":"Poor fluid intake identified"},{"h":"Drugs, asked without judgement","say":["Lots of students use something. I'm not here to get anyone in trouble. Has Ryan used cannabis, spice or anything else?","When did he last use?"],"hear":"\"Spice, most days since term ended. Last time was Monday.\"","mark":"Synthetic cannabinoid use identified"},{"h":"Organic red flags and catatonia","say":["Any fever, headache, fits, recent illness or knock to the head?","Has he been standing still for long periods or stopped speaking?"],"hear":"\"Just a cold a month ago. But for two days he's stood in one spot for ages and barely said anything.\"","mark":"Catatonic features identified"},{"h":"Name what is unknown and why it matters","say":["When I saw Ryan he was very still and puzzled. I'll score that on a catatonia scale. Not drinking can damage his kidneys quickly.","Before anyone calls him medically fit, I'll ask for bloods including CK, kidney function, glucose, thyroid and calcium, a drug screen and an ECG. Spice may not show on the usual urine test."],"hear":"\"Should we have brought him in sooner?\""},{"h":"Tonight's plan and the sectioning worry","say":["You brought him in at the right time. Tonight he stays in an observed area and my senior knows about him.","If he tried to leave and couldn't make that choice safely, we'd use the law that fits, the Mental Capacity Act or Mental Health Act. That's to keep him safe, not to punish him.","His mum is next of kin: I'll ask Ryan if I can ring her, and if he can't decide, I'll speak to her in his best interests."],"hear":"\"Okay. His mum's in Leeds, she's waiting to hear.\""},{"h":"Safety-net and close","say":["If you see him go stiff, get very hot, or have a fit, tell a nurse at once.","Thank you, Josh. I'll update you before you go home."],"hear":"\"Thanks. I'll tell Ellie.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Timeline item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Last well and first change","Well at the birthday meal; sleepless days later","Onset under two weeks: acute, so organic causes stay open"],["Intake and output","Food 'not right'; sips only; no urine since morning","Delusional reason for not eating, not an eating disorder; dehydration risk"],["Substances","Daily spice; last Monday","Possible drug-induced psychosis or withdrawal; standard screen may miss it"],["Organic red flags","No fever, fits or head injury reported","Reassuring but not enough without bloods and ECG"],["Catatonia","Standing in one spot, barely speaking","Treatable emergency; screen formally before any antipsychotic"]]},"key":"'Not eating' describes behaviour, not a diagnosis. Ask why, measure how much he drinks, and screen for catatonia before anyone writes 'medically fit' or starts an antipsychotic.","objmap":[[2],[6],[6],[6],[7]]},"c-1-1-2":{"name":"Denise when well, Denise now: baseline, change, reasons","why":"Separates her lifelong symptoms from the new relapse, finds why it happened, and links each reason to an action.","frame":["Baseline","Change","Medicines","Substances and stress","Risk and exploitation","Early warning signs"],"steps":[{"h":"Her view first, not the notes","say":["Hello Denise, I'm one of the ward doctors. I'd rather hear from you than read about you.","What do you think has been going on?"],"hear":"\"Not you lot again. I told the home team I was fine.\""},{"h":"Baseline: a normal day when well","say":["When you're well, what's a normal day like? Sleep, food, who you see?","And the voices when you're well: what are they like then?"],"hear":"\"I cook, day centre Tuesdays and Thursdays, my sister on Sundays. Voices every day, but quiet.\"","mark":"Baseline established"},{"h":"Change since discharge","say":["What's different now from when you left three weeks ago?","Are the voices louder, saying different things, or telling you to do things?"],"hear":"\"Loud all day. Calling me names, going on about my neighbour, saying I should sort him out.\"","mark":"Command hallucinations identified"},{"h":"The injection","say":["When did you last have your injection? Did something make it hard?","I'll check the depot record so we have the exact date."],"hear":"\"I skipped the last one, about seven weeks ago. It put a stone on me.\"","mark":"Missed depot identified"},{"h":"Sleep, drink, money","say":["How are you sleeping? Any alcohol or drugs since you went home?","How's money been?"],"hear":"\"Three or four hours. Two or three cans most nights. The neighbour took two hundred quid with my card.\"","mark":"Financial exploitation identified"},{"h":"Risk, asked directly","say":["When the voices say to sort him out, do you feel you might act on that?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"No. I wouldn't. I'm just scared of him.\""},{"h":"Two clear sentences, then actions","say":["So when you're well you hear quiet voices daily but cook, go out and sleep eight hours. Now the voices are loud and commanding, you sleep three hours and you've stopped going out. Is that right?","I think three things drove this: stopping the injection, more drinking, and the stress from your neighbour.","We'll talk about other medicines with less weight gain, report the theft today with safeguarding, and do bloods, an ECG and a medicines check within 24 hours."],"hear":"\"Who's going to stop the neighbour?\""},{"h":"Warning signs and close","say":["What are the first signs you're becoming unwell?","I'll invite your care coordinator to the first review. You're informal: you're here by choice, and we'll plan going home from day one."],"hear":"\"I stop answering the phone, stop cooking, stay up all night.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Clerking item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Baseline","Daily quiet voices; cooks, day centre, sleeps 8 hours","Persistent voices are her normal; not proof of relapse"],["Change","Loud command voices; sleep 3–4 hours; stopped going out","A clear relapse, separate from baseline"],["Medicines","Missed paliperidone 7 weeks ago due to weight gain","Main reason for relapse; review choice of medicine with her"],["Substances and stress","2–3 cans nightly; neighbour stress","Additional drivers; alcohol advice and support"],["Risk and exploitation","£200 taken; no intent to act on commands","Adult safeguarding referral; ongoing risk review"]]},"key":"Baseline is how she is when well, not how she always looks on admission. Write baseline and change as two separate sentences, and give each reason for relapse its own action.","objmap":[[2,7],[4],[1,4],[7],[8]]},"c-1-1-3":{"name":"What his wife saw: from depression to a possible bipolar picture","why":"Uses a carer's account to test the working diagnosis, spot mania or a mixed state, and get the antidepressant reviewed today.","frame":["Sleep and energy","Speech, plans and spending","Past episode","Family history","Other causes","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge her, set confidentiality","say":["Mrs Desai, thank you for coming. You're right that we need to hear from you.","I can listen to everything you tell me. What I can share back depends on what Amit has agreed, and I'll be honest about that."],"hear":"\"Nobody asked me what he's actually like. And you've put him on antidepressants.\""},{"h":"Timeline and sleep","say":["When did you first notice a change? What was the first sign?","How much is he sleeping? Does he seem tired the next day?"],"hear":"\"About three weeks ago, after a work review. Two or three hours a night and he's full of beans.\"","mark":"Reduced need for sleep identified"},{"h":"Speech, plans, money","say":["Has his talk changed: faster, jumping between ideas, big plans?","How much has he spent, and is any of it borrowed?"],"hear":"\"An app 'bigger than Uber'. £18,000 savings gone and a £10,000 loan in my name.\"","mark":"Manic symptoms identified"},{"h":"The episode at 25","say":["Tell me about when he was 25. How long did it last? Did he see anyone?","What happened afterwards?"],"hear":"\"Three weeks of no sleep and big plans, quit his job. GP said stress. Then months of being low.\"","mark":"Past hypomanic or manic episode identified"},{"h":"Family history and other causes","say":["Has anyone in the family had depression, bipolar disorder or a hospital stay?","Is he drinking, using drugs or taking steroids? Any thyroid problems?"],"hear":"\"His dad had breakdowns and took lithium for years. Four or five pints most nights.\"","mark":"Family history of bipolar disorder likely"},{"h":"Safety: him, the road, her","say":["Has he talked about not wanting to live? How is his driving?","Do you feel safe at home with him?"],"hear":"\"He said we'd be better off without him, then laughed. He drives too fast. He's never threatened me.\"","mark":"Mixed-state risk identified"},{"h":"What it means and what happens today","say":["What you've told me suggests this may be part of a mood illness with highs as well as lows. That can change his treatment.","I'll tell his consultant today, and the antidepressant will be reviewed today because it can make highs worse.","It's not your fault. People often don't see the highs as illness."],"hear":"\"So is it bipolar?\""},{"h":"Carer support and close","say":["We'll talk with Amit about what he's happy for us to share. Meanwhile, ask your bank about the loan, and Citizens Advice can help.","I'll offer you a carer's assessment. I'll ring you by five today; if he leaves or you're worried, ring the ward."],"hear":"\"Thank you. I've been carrying this alone.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sleep and energy","2–3 hours, not tired","Reduced need for sleep: points to mania, not depression"],["Speech, plans and spending","Fast talk, grand app, £28,000 spent or borrowed","Manic symptoms with serious financial harm"],["Past episode","Three weeks of elation at 25, then low","Probable earlier episode; history fits bipolar disorder"],["Family history","Father on lithium","Raises likelihood of bipolar disorder"],["Risk","Hopeless remark, fast driving","Possible mixed state; suicide and accident risk; review antidepressant today"]]},"key":"Ask 'does he feel tired?' Less sleep with full energy suggests mania, while less sleep with exhaustion suggests depression. One question can change the diagnosis and the medicine.","objmap":[[4],[5],[7],[1],[8]]},"c-1-2-1":{"name":"Mood, personality or psychosis? Four questions that separate them","why":"Uses duration, triggers, the nature of voices and life between crises to reach a provisional formulation Chloe recognises.","frame":["Duration and triggers","Elevated mood","Voices and first-rank symptoms","Between crises","Trauma","Safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge the labels","say":["Chloe, three different labels must be confusing. Today I want to understand your experience, not add another one.","Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Everyone gives me a different label. I just want help.\""},{"h":"How long, and what sets it off","say":["When your mood changes, how long does it last: minutes, hours, days or weeks?","What usually sets it off? When did it first start?"],"hear":"\"Hours. After arguments or if I feel rejected. Since I was about 14.\"","mark":"Reactive mood shifts in hours identified"},{"h":"Any sustained highs?","say":["Have you ever had at least four days of feeling high or full of energy, when you hardly needed sleep?","Did other people notice you were different then, talking faster or spending more?"],"hear":"\"No. Just good nights out.\"","mark":"No hypomanic episode"},{"h":"The voice","say":["Tell me about the voice. Does it seem to come from inside your head or outside? When does it happen?","Do you ever feel thoughts are put into or taken from your head, or that something controls you?"],"hear":"\"A man's voice in my head, critical, when I'm stressed. I know it's my own mind. Nothing like that.\"","mark":"No first-rank symptoms"},{"h":"Between the bad times","say":["When things are calmer, how is life: work, friends, relationships?","How do you see yourself?"],"hear":"\"Part-time in a café, two close friends. Relationships get intense and end badly.\""},{"h":"Trauma, without needing details","say":["Sometimes difficult things happen to people growing up. Has anything like that happened to you? You don't need to give details.","Thank you for telling me. Is anyone, including any children, at risk from him now?"],"hear":"\"My stepdad abused me. I've never told anyone. He left when I was 15.\"","mark":"Childhood trauma identified"},{"h":"Last night and safety","say":["You said last night's cut was deeper. Did you want to die? Can I look at it?","What helps you get through, and who can you contact?"],"hear":"\"After a row with my boyfriend. I didn't want to die. Ice helps, and Jess or my sister.\""},{"h":"A plain-words formulation and next steps","say":["Your feelings swing within hours when you feel rejected, which has been true since you were young. The voice comes with stress and you know it's your own mind. Painful early experiences may explain much of this. That fits less with bipolar disorder or schizophrenia.","Does that fit for you? Next: a talk with someone who knows you, a trauma assessment, and psychology. And a written safety plan today."],"hear":"\"That makes more sense than anything I've been told.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Question","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Duration and triggers","Hours, after rejection, since 14","Pattern fits emotional instability more than bipolar disorder"],["Elevated mood","No four-day highs","Argues against bipolar disorder"],["Voices and first-rank symptoms","Inner critical voice under stress, insight kept, no first-rank symptoms","Less likely a primary psychotic illness"],["Trauma","Childhood abuse by stepfather","Consider complex PTSD; trauma-informed care and safeguarding check"],["Safety","Deeper cut, no intent, coping skills and contacts","Safety plan; wound check; no risk score"]]},"key":"Time course separates them: mood shifts over hours with triggers suggest emotional instability, episodes over days to weeks suggest bipolar disorder. Voices alone do not mean psychosis.","objmap":[[2],[2,3],[6],[8],[8]]},"c-1-2-2":{"name":"Not cleared yet: persuading the medical registrar to admit Laura","why":"Puts the objective red flags in front of a busy colleague, requests named tests, and escalates calmly if he declines.","frame":["Delirium","Abnormal observations","Seizure","Prodrome and no history","Investigations","Capacity and law"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with the request","say":["I'm the psychiatry registrar, calling about Laura Kent, 29, in cubicle 9.","I'm asking you to admit her under medicine tonight. I think this is delirium, possibly encephalitis."],"hear":"\"I've got two minutes. She's been medically cleared, hasn't she?\""},{"h":"Objective findings","say":["Her 4AT is positive and her attention fluctuates; she drifts in and out while I talk to her.","Pulse 118, temperature 37.9, CRP 24.","Her partner describes a funny turn yesterday that sounds like a seizure, after ten days of anxiety and no sleep. She has no psychiatric history."],"hear":"\"Couldn't that be a pseudoseizure? It sounds psychiatric to me.\"","mark":"Delirium identified"},{"h":"Name the tests","say":["I'd like blood cultures, CK, a pregnancy test and a urine drug screen tonight.","Then an MRI, an EEG, and a lumbar puncture with HSV PCR and NMDA receptor antibodies in CSF and blood.","With fever and a probable seizure, would you start aciclovir now?"],"hear":"\"MRI won't happen before Monday. I'd start aciclovir with fever and a fit.\"","mark":"Encephalitis red flags identified"},{"h":"Acknowledge workload, hold the line","say":["I know you have eleven waiting and no beds. That's hard.","But 'medically cleared' here meant normal basic bloods. It doesn't exclude encephalitis.","If you can't admit her, I'll need to ask your consultant, Dr Moss, and mine to discuss it."],"hear":"\"Fine. I'll come and see her. But she's agitated, can you sedate her?\""},{"h":"Sedation advice","say":["I'd avoid antipsychotics; they can mask signs and may be risky here.","Calm environment and nursing first; low-dose lorazepam only if needed for safety."],"hear":"\"Okay. Who consents her for the LP? Can't you section her?\""},{"h":"Capacity and the right law","say":["The Mental Health Act doesn't authorise tests or treatment for a physical illness.","Your team assesses her capacity for the LP. If she lacks it, you decide in her best interests using the Mental Capacity Act, and consult her partner."],"hear":"\"Fair enough. That's clearer.\"","mark":"Mental Capacity Act route agreed"},{"h":"Summarise and hand over","say":["So: you review her now, admit under medicine, cultures and bloods tonight, aciclovir started, LP and imaging planned. We review daily.","Her partner knows we're worried about a physical cause; who will update him next?","I'll record our names and times. Bleep me on 4410 if anything changes."],"hear":"\"Agreed. I'll update the partner after I've seen her.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Red flag","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Delirium","Positive 4AT, fluctuating attention","Acute brain dysfunction with a cause to find"],["Abnormal observations","Pulse 118, temp 37.9, CRP 24","Possible infection or autonomic instability; not anxiety"],["Seizure","Funny turn yesterday","Encephalitis until proven otherwise; aciclovir and EEG"],["Prodrome and no history","Anxiety and insomnia then psychosis over 10 days, age 29","Fits autoimmune encephalitis; LP with NMDA receptor antibodies"],["Capacity and law","Registrar asks to 'section her' for LP","Mental Capacity Act and best interests, not the Mental Health Act"]]},"key":"'Medically cleared' usually means normal routine bloods. New psychosis with fluctuating attention, fever, tachycardia or a seizure needs a medical bed and specific tests, not an antipsychotic.","objmap":[[2,3],[3],[1,4],[6],[7]]},"c-1-2-3":{"name":"Two patients, two pairs: treat both conditions together","why":"Shows the consultant why neither condition can wait for the other, and gives each patient one plan with names and dates.","frame":["Kyle: how use drives relapse","Kyle: who leads care","Nadia: PTSD symptoms","Nadia: why depression persists","Safety","Integrated plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Kyle in one line, then the link","say":["Kyle, 30, schizophrenia, readmitted after daily cannabis and some cocaine. His relapses follow heavier use.","Every admission in two years followed heavy use. He says skunk calms the voices at night but they're worse next day. About three grams a day, roughly £30, and cocaine at weekends. He won't stop, but would cut down to save money."],"hear":"\"So it's a drugs problem. The CMHT want him with drug services. Do you agree?\"","mark":"Substance-driven relapse identified"},{"h":"Who leads","say":["I don't. NICE guidance NG58 says substance use isn't a reason to exclude someone from mental health services. The CMHT should keep leading, with drug services alongside.","Sam has known him four years. Losing that would likely mean more relapses, not fewer."],"hear":"\"Fine, but I'm tired of this revolving door.\"","mark":"Exclusion risk identified"},{"h":"Kyle's plan","say":["I understand the frustration. Plan: Sam stays and starts motivational work, the drug service joins his CPA review, his overdue depot is given, and drug use goes in his relapse plan.","Physical: ECG and blood pressure for the cocaine; he smokes 20 a day, so if he stops on the ward his olanzapine level may rise and we check it. DVLA advice too.","Could you write to the CMHT manager?"],"hear":"\"Yes, I'll write today.\""},{"h":"Nadia: the missing diagnosis","say":["Nadia, 38, depression not improving after citalopram and eight weeks of mirtazapine.","She has flashbacks most days of an assault five years ago, avoids the area, and is jumpy and sleeps badly. Her PCL-5 is 58, well above the usual cut-off."],"hear":"\"Shouldn't we just try a third antidepressant?\"","mark":"PTSD identified"},{"h":"Why the depression persists","say":["I think untreated PTSD is keeping her depression going: the nightmares wreck her sleep and avoidance shrinks her life.","NICE recommends trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Another switch won't touch the flashbacks or the avoidance."],"hear":"\"Psychology have a six-week wait.\""},{"h":"Her safety now","say":["It was her ex-partner. He still messages her and knows her address, though they haven't met for a year.","I'll do a DASH with her today, refer to the IDVA, and to MARAC if it's high risk. Leave only with a safety plan that covers him: blocked number, safe address, police contact."],"hear":"\"Is she safe to go on leave this weekend?\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"},{"h":"Summarise both plans","say":["Kyle: stays with Sam and the CMHT, drug service joins, motivational work, ECG and olanzapine level, relapse plan.","Nadia: DASH and IDVA today, safety plan before leave, ward psychologist starts stabilisation while she waits for trauma therapy, mirtazapine for now. Review both in four weeks?"],"hear":"\"Good. Write to the CMHT manager and quote NG58.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Kyle: how use drives relapse","3 g skunk daily, weekend cocaine; voices worse next day","Use and psychosis feed each other; treat both together"],["Kyle: who leads care","CMHT want to discharge","NICE NG58: mental health services keep the lead"],["Nadia: PTSD symptoms","Flashbacks, avoidance, jumpiness; PCL-5 of 58","Probable PTSD, previously missed"],["Nadia: why depression persists","Two antidepressants, no response","Untreated PTSD maintains it; trauma-focused therapy"],["Safety","Ex-partner still messages and knows her address","DASH, IDVA, MARAC if high risk; safety plan before leave"]]},"key":"Coexisting conditions are a reason for joined-up care, not a reason to discharge. When depression does not respond, ask about trauma before trying another antidepressant.","objmap":[[2],[1,3],[4,6],[7]]},"c-1-3-1":{"name":"Lisa in three minutes: one line, five Ps, a plan that follows","why":"Gives the consultant a crisp picture of why Lisa is unwell now, how risky she is, and a plan where each part answers a formulation item.","frame":["Presenting","Predisposing","Precipitating","Perpetuating","Protective"],"steps":[{"h":"One-sentence opening","say":["Lisa Ashton, 44, informal, admitted last night after a suicidal crisis following redundancy and separation last month.","I'll give the key points, a five-P formulation, risk and one question."],"hear":"\"Good. Go on, quickly.\""},{"h":"Presenting picture","say":["Low mood, poor sleep, guilt, hopelessness and suicidal thoughts since the losses. No psychotic symptoms.","Working diagnosis: a moderate to severe depressive episode; one similar episode at 30 that responded to CBT."],"hear":"\"How risky is she right now?\""},{"h":"Why her, why now","say":["Predisposing: neglect in childhood, a depressive episode at 30 and a family history of depression. So she's vulnerable to low mood.","Precipitating: redundancy and separation in the same week. Both losses of role and support at once.","Plan link: psychology for the old patterns, social work and a fit note for the job loss."],"hear":"\"So what? What does that change?\"","mark":"Losses identified as trigger"},{"h":"What keeps it going","say":["Perpetuating: a bottle of wine a night since he left, debt and withdrawing from friends.","Plan link: AUDIT and a withdrawal chart today, debt advice and Breathing Space, and her sister and daily nursing time."],"hear":"\"Is it the drink or the depression?\"","mark":"Alcohol identified as maintaining"},{"h":"What protects her","say":["Protective: her sister, her dog, and a good response to CBT before.","We'll use them: her sister is our link for leave planning and can take the dog."],"hear":"\"Fine. Risk, in plain words.\""},{"h":"Risk in words, not a score","say":["Last night she planned an overdose with saved paracetamol and wrote notes; her sister found her. Risk is high now, falling with support. Drinking, debt letters and being alone raise it.","Her sister and dog protect her. I'd keep intermittent observations and no leave until there's a written safety plan."],"hear":"\"That's the group we lose. I share your worry.\"","mark":"Current risk stated"},{"h":"Her view and the question","say":["She wants the money sorted and to sleep. She's unsure about tablets, willing to talk, and wants her sister involved.","My question: start an antidepressant now, or wait to see if alcohol is driving the low mood? Given severity and risk, I'd lean to sertraline now."],"hear":"\"Yes, if she agrees. What if she refuses tablets?\""},{"h":"Respond and confirm","say":["Then I record her reasons and offer it again at review; everything else goes ahead. I'll warn her about early nausea and restlessness, and review within a week.","I'll check the plan with her after the round and record her agreement."],"hear":"\"Good. Involve the sister in leave planning.\""}],"table":{"cols":["5P item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Presenting","Low mood, hopelessness, suicidal thoughts, planned overdose","Depressive episode; no psychosis"],["Predisposing","Childhood neglect, episode at 30, family history","Long-standing vulnerability; psychology referral"],["Precipitating","Redundancy and separation in the same week","Why now; social work and practical help"],["Perpetuating","Bottle of wine nightly, debt, isolation","What keeps it going; AUDIT, withdrawal plan, debt advice"],["Protective","Sister, dog, good past CBT response","Build them into leave and safety plans"]]},"key":"A formulation explains, a list only describes. Every 'P' should lead to an action in the plan, or the consultant will rightly ask 'so what?'","objmap":[[2],[1],[6],[1,8],[8]]},"c-1-3-2":{"name":"Depression, drink or both? A 5P formulation with alcohol at the centre","why":"Shows the team how alcohol starts and maintains his low mood, and turns that into a safe, timed plan.","frame":["Predisposing","Precipitating","Perpetuating","Protective","Alcohol-induced or independent low mood"],"steps":[{"h":"Open without judgement","say":["Mr Hollis, I'm Dr ___, one of the ward doctors. The team wants to work out how the drinking and your mood fit together, so we treat the right thing.","No lecture, I promise. I just need your help with the order things happened in."],"hear":"'Go on then. As long as nobody's going to lecture me about drink.'"},{"h":"Which came first: the timeline","say":["When did your mood start to slide: before the drinking went up to a bottle a day, or after?","Have you ever had a month or more completely dry? What was your mood like then?"],"hear":"'It went up about a year ago when work went quiet. I had four months off it in 2019 and felt the best I had in years.'","mark":"Mood lifted when dry: alcohol-related low mood likely"},{"h":"Predisposing: what made him vulnerable","say":["Has anyone in your family had trouble with drink or with their mood?","How old were you when drinking became a regular thing?"],"hear":"'My dad drank heavily. His liver went and he died at 61.'","mark":"Predisposing factor identified"},{"h":"Precipitating: why now","say":["What has changed over the last year, at work and at home?","Since your wife left three weeks ago, where have you been staying and how is money?"],"hear":"'Work dried up, we were rowing all the time, then she left. I'm on my brother's sofa.'","mark":"Precipitating factors identified"},{"h":"Perpetuating: the dependence cycle and the physical risks","say":["What does a drink do for you in the first hour? And how are you the next morning?","When you stopped before, did you get shakes, see things that weren't there, or have a fit?","Any muddled thinking or wobbly walking since you came in? We're giving you thiamine to protect your memory."],"hear":"'It switches my head off. Mornings I'm shaking till I've had one. Last time I stopped I saw spiders on the wall.'","mark":"Perpetuating cycle identified: relief drinking and withdrawal"},{"h":"Protective factors, suicide risk, children and driving","say":["Who or what keeps you going at the moment?","When thoughts about ending your life come, have you usually been drinking? Are they there now?","Do you drive? And do the children stay with you?"],"hear":"'Only late at night after a bottle, never sober, none now. The kids keep me going. They stay every other weekend. I drive the van for work.'","mark":"Protective factors and drink-linked suicide risk identified"},{"h":"Give the consultant the formulation","say":["In 5P terms: predisposed by his father's drinking; precipitated by the separation and a year of escalating drinking; kept going by dependence, morning relief drinking, poor sleep and shame; protected by his children, his job held open and a good dry spell in 2019.","My working view is low mood largely driven by alcohol, but an independent depression is not ruled out. I'd review mood after three to four weeks alcohol-free before deciding on an antidepressant.","Now: finish the detox, thiamine, bloods including LFTs, GGT and MCV, daily review of suicide risk, DVLA advice, and a safeguarding check for the children."],"hear":"'Fine. So we hold off on antidepressants and set a review date.'"},{"h":"Explain to him and agree the plan","say":["Heavy drinking can pull mood right down on its own, and losing your marriage hurts on top of that. Both are real.","Let's get you three or four weeks dry and then look at your mood again, so if tablets are needed we know they're for the right thing.","With dependence you must stop driving for now and tell the DVLA. I'll help you work out what that means for the van."],"hear":"'So no tablets yet. Alright, as long as there's a date we look again.'","mark":"Shared plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["5P item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Predisposing","Father drank heavily, died of liver disease at 61","Family loading for dependence; raises his own risk"],["Precipitating","Work slump, marital rows, wife left three weeks ago","Loss on top of escalating drinking tipped him into crisis"],["Perpetuating","Drinks to stop morning shakes; past withdrawal hallucinations","Dependence keeps mood low and needs a safe detox with thiamine"],["Protective","Children, job held open, four good dry months in 2019","Real reasons to stay well; build the plan around them"],["Alcohol-induced or independent","Mood was good when dry; suicidal thoughts only when drunk","Likely alcohol-driven; recheck mood after 3–4 weeks dry"]]},"key":"Don't force a choice between depression and alcohol. Hold both, treat what is certain now (withdrawal, thiamine, risk, driving, children) and set a date, three to four weeks dry, to decide whether a separate depression remains.","objmap":[[7],[2,7],[7],[7]]},"c-1-3-3":{"name":"Not 'non-engagement': formulating trauma, possible autism and personality together","why":"Replaces a discharge-for-missed-appointments plan with an understanding that changes how the team works with her.","frame":["Trauma","Possible autism","Possible ADHD","Personality difficulties","Service factors that keep crises going"],"steps":[{"h":"Make it safe to talk","say":["Jade, I'm Dr ___. Nobody is discharging you today. I'd like to understand what makes coming here hard so we can change what we do.","I'll ask short, specific questions. Tell me if anything is unclear."],"hear":"'It's fine. The buzzing's off. Everyone's going to discharge me anyway.'"},{"h":"Barriers to coming: the service side","say":["When a letter says 'please attend to discuss your care', what do you think it means?","What happens for you in our waiting room?"],"hear":"'I don't know what the letters mean. The lights buzz, people stare, so I walk out.'","mark":"Service barriers identified"},{"h":"Childhood, school and senses","say":["At school, how did you find assemblies, friendships and changes to the timetable?","Are there sounds, lights, crowds or clothes that you find very hard?","If someone says 'pop in any time', what do you take that to mean?"],"hear":"'Assemblies were unbearable. One friend. Timetable changes, I'd melt down. I wear the same three tops.'","mark":"Possible autism features identified"},{"h":"Trauma and dissociation","say":["What was home like when you were little? Was there enough food, and someone looking after you?","Do you get nightmares or flashbacks? Do you ever lose time when things get too much?"],"hear":"'Mum drank. Often no food. I get nightmares every week, and sometimes I go blank, lose an hour and find cuts.'","mark":"Trauma features identified: consider complex PTSD"},{"h":"Attention, impulsivity and the self-harm moment","say":["Since childhood, have you found it hard to sit still, wait your turn, or finish things?","Just before you hurt yourself, what is happening in your body and your thoughts?","Some nights do you have thoughts of not being here? Any plan?"],"hear":"'Some nights I think it, but there's no plan. When I go blank I don't know I've done it.'","mark":"Self-harm pattern and current risk identified; ADHD to screen further"},{"h":"Strengths and what helps","say":["What has helped, even a little?","Who is on your side at the moment?"],"hear":"'Drawing. My sister Leanne. My GP, Dr Patel.'","mark":"Protective factors identified"},{"h":"Share the formulation with her","say":["From what you've told me, a lot of your distress makes sense after what happened when you were little. None of the barriers here are your fault.","I also wonder if noise, lights and sudden changes are much harder for you than for most people. I'm not saying you are autistic. I'm asking if you'd like an assessment to find out.","You don't have to do groups. We can start one-to-one, with a quiet room and text reminders."],"hear":"'Are you saying I'm autistic now? I thought I had BPD. OK, if it's one-to-one.'","mark":"Shared formulation agreed"},{"h":"Change the plan with the team lead","say":["I don't think non-engagement is the right frame. The formulation points to trauma and possible autism, and our setting may be adding to her crises.","I suggest a neurodevelopmental referral, reasonable adjustments under the Equality Act, one consistent clinician and a written crisis plan shared with A&E liaison. Not for discharge."],"hear":"'Fair enough. Put the adjustments on the record and we'll keep her.'"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Trauma","Neglect, weekly nightmares, losing an hour and finding cuts","Trauma-driven dysregulation and dissociation; consider complex PTSD"],["Possible autism","Sensory overload, literal thinking, meltdowns at change","Needs a proper assessment, not a relabel today"],["Possible ADHD","No clear history yet","Screen as part of the neurodevelopmental referral"],["Personality difficulties","Fear of rejection, expects discharge","May coexist; personality-focused help still relevant later"],["Service factors","Unclear letters, noisy waiting room, four coordinators","Our setup maintains crises; adjust it"]]},"key":"Several explanations can be true at once. The excellent formulation doesn't swap one label for another; it names what the service is doing to keep crises going and changes that first.","objmap":[[8],[8],[8],[8]]},"c-1-4-1":{"name":"What changed today? Chronic versus acute risk and a safety plan made with her","why":"Finds the new crisis behind a familiar presentation and leaves her with a plan she wrote and follow-up within 24 hours.","frame":["Long-standing background","What changed today","Intent and meaning","Protective factors and means","Safety plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Start fresh, not from the notes","say":["Nicola, I'm Dr ___ from the mental health team. I've not read you a label; I'd like to hear about tonight from you.","Can you take me through today, from when you woke up?"],"hear":"'I've done this a hundred times. Just let me go home.'"},{"h":"What was different this time","say":["You've had a lot of hard nights. What was different about this one?","What's been the hardest thing in your life this week?"],"hear":"'Eviction notice came Wednesday. Nowhere I can afford takes dogs. Bella went to the RSPCA on Friday. She was why I got up.'","mark":"Acute change identified: home and Bella lost"},{"h":"Intent and meaning, not method detail","say":["That's a huge loss. I'm so sorry about Bella.","When you took the tablets, what did you hope would happen?","Did you plan it, leave a note or tell anyone? How does it feel now, knowing you're still here?"],"hear":"'I wanted everything to stop. No note. Kim turned up and rang the ambulance. Part of me's relieved she did.'","mark":"High intent at the time; now ambivalent"},{"h":"The long-standing background","say":["Over the last ten years, what usually sets the bad nights off?","Did you drink tonight? Does that change things for you?"],"hear":"'Being on my own at night. I'd had half a bottle of wine.'","mark":"Chronic pattern identified: alone, night-time, alcohol"},{"h":"Protective factors and access to means","say":["What has kept you safe on the bad nights before? Is that still there?","Are there more tablets at home?"],"hear":"'Bella was. Now it's Kim, and I want to fight the eviction. There's more in the bathroom cabinet.'","mark":"Protective factors fragile; means at home identified"},{"h":"Write the safety plan together","say":["Let's write a plan for when the feelings rise again, in your words. What are your warning signs?","What can you do on your own first, and who or where can you go to?","Would you let Kim clear the bathroom cabinet tonight? You can choose what we tell her."],"hear":"'Wine and sitting in the dark. I could ring Kim. She can take the tablets if I say what she's told.'","mark":"Safety plan co-produced"},{"h":"Follow-up, honesty about admission, and close","say":["Telling me how bad it is won't get you sectioned. It helps me get you the right support.","The crisis team will contact you within 24 hours, and I'll ask Sue and the social worker to help with housing.","Here are the numbers: the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, and Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"'Nobody rings when I'm drowning.' / 'OK. If someone actually calls tomorrow.'","mark":"Follow-up agreed within 24 hours"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Long-standing background","Ten years of self-harm, worse alone at night with alcohol","Background unchanged; tells you when risk rises"],["What changed today","Eviction notice; Bella taken by the RSPCA","New losses: acute risk is higher than her usual"],["Intent and meaning","Wanted everything to stop; now partly relieved","Serious intent; ambivalence is something to work with"],["Protective factors and means","Kim, fighting eviction; tablets at home","Fragile supports; remove means tonight"],["Safety plan","Her signs, Kim, crisis numbers, cabinet cleared","Practical plan she owns; crisis team in 24 hours"]]},"key":"Chronic risk is the background, not the answer. The question that separates good from excellent is 'what changed today?', and the response is a plan she wrote, not a risk score or a low, medium or high label.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[6],[7]]},"c-1-4-2":{"name":"A risk formulation a stranger can act on at 3am","why":"Turns 'Risk: low' into a specific, linked account of what could go wrong, why, how you'd spot it and what to do.","frame":["Specific risk and to whom","History and context","Triggers","Early warning signs","Protective factors","Actions linked to leave"],"steps":[{"h":"Open about the leave, honestly","say":["Mr Bell, I'm Dr ___, covering this weekend. Your leave tomorrow is still on; I'm updating your notes so whoever's on duty can support you.","I'll ask about what led to coming in, and what helps you stay well."],"hear":"'Is this about my leave? Nobody's cancelling it, are they?'"},{"h":"The index event and its context","say":["Can you tell me what was happening before you came into hospital?","I need to write about what happened with your neighbour in March, because it helps people support you, not to judge you."],"hear":"'I'd stopped my tablets and was smoking every day. I thought he was poisoning my food. I pushed him and hit him.'","mark":"Specific risk identified: violence to neighbour in relapse"},{"h":"Current state: is the belief still there?","say":["How have the worries about your neighbour been this week? Do you still wonder about the food?","Any voices? Are you angry with anyone at the moment?"],"hear":"'Sometimes I still wonder. But I'm not going to do anything. No voices, sleeping fine.'","mark":"Residual belief, no current intent"},{"h":"Triggers: the leave plan itself","say":["Where will you go tomorrow and who will you see?","Would you call at the flat? Do you plan to smoke anything while you're out?"],"hear":"'Leon's. I wanted to get my post from the flat. One joint wouldn't matter, would it?'","mark":"Triggers identified: cannabis and the neighbour's street"},{"h":"Early warning signs and protective factors","say":["What would tell Leon you're becoming unwell again?","Can I tell Leon your leave times and what to look out for?"],"hear":"'Not sleeping, checking my food, staying in my room, asking about the neighbour. Yeah, tell Leon.'","mark":"Warning signs and carer consent recorded"},{"h":"Check with the nurse who knows him","say":["What would worry you if he came back late?","Have you seen any food checking or questions about the neighbour this week?"],"hear":"'Nothing this week. If he's late and smells of weed, I'd be worried.'"},{"h":"Write it down in plain words","say":["Main risk: violence to his neighbour if he relapses; also self-neglect and disengagement. Last time: off medication, daily cannabis, believed he was being poisoned.","Warning signs: poor sleep, food checking, isolating, asking about the neighbour. Protective: depot for three weeks, brother Leon, no aggression on the ward.","If late or warning signs: phone him, then Leon; tell the duty doctor; follow the AWOL policy under s18."],"hear":"Nurse: 'That's clear. I'd know exactly what to do if he's late.'","mark":"Risk formulation written"},{"h":"Agree conditions with him","say":["The leave goes ahead, two hours, to Leon's and not on your old street. Leon or staff can collect your post.","Cannabis was part of what made you ill last time, so the plan is none on leave."],"hear":"'Fine, if Leon gets my post. I'll stay off my street.'","mark":"Leave conditions agreed and matched to the s17 form"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Specific risk and to whom","Hit neighbour he believed was poisoning him","Risk is to an identifiable person, during relapse"],["Triggers","Stopped tablets, daily cannabis; wants to visit his street","Leave conditions must avoid both"],["Early warning signs","Poor sleep, food checking, isolating, asking about neighbour","What any night doctor or nurse should look for"],["Protective factors","Depot three weeks, Leon, sleeping well","Risk lower than at admission; leave reasonable"],["Actions linked to leave","Two hours to Leon's, return time, s18 if late","Clear steps a stranger can follow"]]},"key":"A risk level is an opinion; a formulation is a usable story. Name the risk, to whom, under what conditions, and what to do. Then make sure the s17 form and the handover say the same thing.","objmap":[[7],[8],[7],[5,8]]},"c-1-5-1":{"name":"Why does this keep happening? Sharing a 4P formulation with her","why":"Answers her question in plain words, lets her correct it, and links it to treatment she chooses.","frame":["Her own explanation","Predisposing","Precipitating","Perpetuating","Protective"],"steps":[{"h":"Ask her view first","say":["That's a really important question. Before I answer, what do you think is going on?","Would it help if I drew how I understand it so far, and you correct me?"],"hear":"'A friend said it's a chemical imbalance. Is my brain broken?'","mark":"Her model: chemical imbalance, fear of brain damage"},{"h":"Address the fear, then the roots","say":["Your brain isn't damaged. Depression changes how the brain works for a while, and it usually lifts with the right help.","Some things make a person more likely to get depressed. You lost your mum when you were 12. Does that feel part of it?"],"hear":"'I suppose so. I've always had to hold it together.'","mark":"Predisposing factor identified"},{"h":"Why now","say":["This time, what changed in the months before it started?","Was anything else going on at home or with friends?"],"hear":"'The new job, three months ago. Long hours, I never switch off.'","mark":"Precipitating factor identified"},{"h":"What keeps it going","say":["When you're low, what do you do with friends and with your evenings?","Last time sertraline helped. What happened when you stopped it?","Does that fit, or have I got something wrong?"],"hear":"'I cancel people and work late, then crash. I stopped after three months because I felt fine. And you missed something: I'm terrified of failing.'","mark":"Perpetuating cycle identified; her correction added"},{"h":"Reframe, don't blame, and check safety","say":["Being a perfectionist isn't a fault. It's a way you learned to cope, and it works until it doesn't.","When you're this low, do thoughts of not being here come into your mind?"],"hear":"'Some nights I think everyone would cope better without me. No plans.'","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts; no plan or intent"},{"h":"Strengths","say":["What has helped before?","Who could you lean on now, even a little?"],"hear":"'My friend Maya. Running, when I was doing it.'","mark":"Protective factors identified"},{"h":"Link the picture to treatment","say":["Each part of the drawing gives us something to work on. Talking therapy can tackle the self-criticism and fear of failure.","Sertraline worked for you. If you restart, staying on it at least six months after you feel well cuts the chance of it coming back; with three episodes we'd talk about longer, often around two years.","Which one thing would you like to work on first?"],"hear":"'I don't want tablets for life. Therapy first, and I'll think about the sertraline.'","mark":"Shared decision made"},{"h":"Teach-back and close","say":["Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","I'll write to you with this diagram and copy your GP. If those night-time thoughts get stronger, call the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2."],"hear":"'It's not my brain being broken. It's old stuff, the job and how I cope. Therapy referral, and think about sertraline.'"}],"table":{"cols":["4P item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her own explanation","Chemical imbalance, fear of brain damage","Correct the fear; build on what she believes"],["Predisposing","Mother died when she was 12; always held it together","Early loss raises lifelong vulnerability"],["Precipitating","Demanding new job three months ago","The stress that started this episode"],["Perpetuating","Self-criticism, fear of failure, cancelling friends, stopping sertraline early","Targets for therapy and for longer treatment"],["Protective","Maya, running, good past response to sertraline","Real strengths to plan around"]]},"key":"A formulation she can correct is worth more than a perfect one. Ask her view first, stay tentative, and let her edit it; her correction ('fear of failing') is often the key to treatment.","objmap":[[7],[1],[7],[8]]},"c-1-5-2":{"name":"Did we cause this? Explaining first-episode psychosis to his parents within his consent","why":"Gives the parents a blame-free, hopeful understanding, uses what they know, and supports them as carers without breaking Ryan's confidence.","frame":["Consent limits","Stress-vulnerability","Cannabis","Outlook","Carer support"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the consent boundary kindly","say":["Thank you both for coming. Ryan has agreed I can talk about his diagnosis and treatment plan. There are a few personal things he's asked me to keep private.","That doesn't stop you telling me anything. We will always listen to you."],"hear":"'He's 19 and you won't tell us everything? We're his parents!'","mark":"Consent limits explained"},{"h":"Hear what they've seen","say":["What have you noticed in him over the last few months?","Has anyone in the family had anything similar?"],"hear":"'Since Christmas he stopped sleeping and seeing friends. He said the TV was sending him messages. His uncle had a breakdown in his twenties.'","mark":"Collateral and family history gathered"},{"h":"Name the guilt and explain the cause","say":["Many parents ask if they caused this. You didn't. Divorce and parenting don't cause psychosis.","Think of it as a vulnerability some people carry, partly in their genes, plus stresses on top: strong cannabis, sleep loss, leaving university. When the load is too much, psychosis can start."],"hear":"'I keep thinking the divorce did it.' / 'So it's not him being lazy?'","mark":"Guilt addressed; stress-vulnerability explained"},{"h":"Cannabis, honestly","say":["Strong cannabis does raise the risk and can make recovery harder. Stopping is one of the most helpful things he can do.","Taking his phone or arguing tends to raise tension. Calm, low-key support helps more."],"hear":"'He's smoked strong stuff since 16.'","mark":"Cannabis as a stress factor explained"},{"h":"A hopeful, balanced outlook","say":["Many young people recover well after a first episode, especially with early treatment and family support. Some go back to study.","Some have more episodes, so the team will help him spot early signs."],"hear":"'Will he ever be normal again? Go back to uni?'"},{"h":"The girlfriend question: take it seriously without sharing","say":["I can't discuss his relationship. But I hear that you're worried about someone's safety, and I'll make sure the team considers that today.","If you see or hear anything that worries you, tell us straight away."],"hear":"'OK. As long as someone's actually looking at it.'","mark":"Concern escalated without breaching confidence"},{"h":"Support them as carers","say":["How are you both coping? Who's sleeping?","The team offers family sessions: about ten meetings that help families and young people deal with stress together. You can also have a carer's assessment through the council."],"hear":"'I check on him all night. I haven't slept in weeks.'","mark":"Carer strain identified; family intervention offered"},{"h":"Check understanding and close","say":["How would you explain this to his sister?","If you're worried in a crisis, here's how to reach the team."],"hear":"'It's not our fault, he has a vulnerability, the weed makes it worse, and he can get better.'"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Consent limits","'We're his parents!'","Share only what Ryan agreed; always listen"],["Stress-vulnerability","Uncle's breakdown; divorce guilt","Vulnerability plus stress; nobody to blame"],["Cannabis","Strong cannabis since 16","A real, changeable risk factor"],["Outlook","'Will he be normal again?'","Many recover; early treatment helps"],["Carer support","Mother not sleeping; father angry","Family intervention and carer's assessment"]]},"key":"Confidentiality limits what you share, never what you hear. Listen to everything, act on any risk they raise, and offer family support even when you cannot give them details.","objmap":[[1,3],[3,4],[1],[7]]},"c-1-5-3":{"name":"The formulation in two minutes, then a plan with names and dates","why":"Gives the meeting a shared, concise understanding and leaves with a written plan that she owns.","frame":["Background","Why now","What keeps it going","Strengths","Risks","Aftercare plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Ask her first","say":["Angela, is it OK if I summarise how we understand things, and then you tell everyone what matters to you?","Please stop me if I get anything wrong."],"hear":"'Go ahead. As long as I get a say.'"},{"h":"Background and why now","say":["Angela has schizoaffective disorder. Her past relapses have followed stopping medication when she felt well, and she has had a spell of homelessness before.","This time she stopped her medication because of weight gain, then lost her office job and stopped sleeping."],"hear":"'That's right. The weight was why.'","mark":"Background and trigger stated"},{"h":"What keeps it going, strengths and risks","say":["Weight gain makes her stop medication, and she cuts herself off when unwell.","Strengths: her sister Joanne, her art group, and she wants to work. Risks when unwell are self-neglect and being taken advantage of. No violence."],"hear":"'You're all talking about me like I'm not here.'","mark":"Strengths and risks named"},{"h":"Turn to her directly","say":["You're right, Angela. What would you like everyone to know?","The new medicine has caused less weight gain so far. How do you feel about it?"],"hear":"'If the weight comes back, I'm stopping. And I want my job back.'","mark":"Her priorities: weight and work"},{"h":"Hear the team","say":["Joanne, what did you notice first last time? What can you realistically do?","Care coordinator, what is ready in the community? OT, how is she managing cooking and money?"],"hear":"'Poor sleep, no answer on the phone. I work full time, I can't ring every day, but I can have the crisis plan.'","mark":"Early warning signs and realistic carer role agreed"},{"h":"Agree the plan, with names and dates","say":["This is aftercare under section 117. Follow-up within 72 hours of discharge.","Home treatment visits for a short, fixed period, then clinic as you prefer. Weight checked at each visit, and a physical health clinic referral.","Employment referral for the job. Joanne gets a copy of the crisis plan."],"hear":"'Can I skip home treatment if I come to clinic?' / 'Two weeks, then clinic. OK.'","mark":"Section 117 plan agreed"},{"h":"Answer her worry about the flat and close","say":["If things slip, early contact protects the flat. The care coordinator can link you to housing support now.","Have I missed anything? I'll put this in writing with names and dates, and copy the GP."],"hear":"'No. That's fair.'"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Background","Relapses after stopping medication; past homelessness","Pattern to plan against"],["Why now","Weight gain, stopped tablets, lost job, poor sleep","Side effects drove this relapse"],["What keeps it going","'If the weight comes back, I'm stopping'","Monitor weight; act early on side effects"],["Strengths","Sister, art group, wants to work","Build recovery on these"],["Risks","Self-neglect, exploitation; no violence","Plan support, not restriction"],["Aftercare plan","s117, 72-hour follow-up, crisis plan to Joanne","Named people, dates, in writing"]]},"key":"Two minutes is enough only if each part links to an action. The excellent version checks with the patient before and after, and ends with names and dates rather than 'we'll arrange support'.","objmap":[[3],[3,4],[6],[5]]},"c-2-1-1":{"name":"First-episode psychosis at midnight: symptoms, drugs, organic screen, risk, early intervention","why":"Confirms psychosis, rules out a medical cause, finds the hidden risk and sends him home safely with early intervention involved.","frame":["Psychotic symptoms","Substance use","Organic screen","Mood","Risk","Early intervention plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle him and agree the room","say":["Hi Kieran, I'm Dr ___, one of the mental health doctors. Shall we turn that light off?","I'll take about half an hour. Tell me in your words what's been happening these last few weeks."],"hear":"'Can we turn that light off? I don't like it. I haven't slept for three weeks.'"},{"h":"Explore the belief with curiosity","say":["You mentioned the smoke alarm. What do you think is going on there?","How sure are you, out of ten? Could there be any other explanation?"],"hear":"'The neighbours upstairs are filming me through it. Ten out of ten.'","mark":"Persecutory delusion identified"},{"h":"Hallucinations and thought interference","say":["Do you ever hear people talking when nobody is in the room? What do they say?","Do you feel someone is putting thoughts into your head or taking them out, or that others can know them?"],"hear":"'I hear them through the ceiling: \"He's up again.\" My thoughts leak out, so they know.'","mark":"Auditory hallucinations and thought broadcasting identified"},{"h":"Cannabis and other drugs, without judgement","say":["How much cannabis, what type, and has that changed? Anything else: cocaine, spice, alcohol?","Strong weed can bring on exactly this kind of experience. That's why it matters, not to judge you."],"hear":"'Skunk, five or six joints a day since I left uni. Cocaine once a month ago. Never spice.'","mark":"Daily high-potency cannabis identified"},{"h":"Organic screen, explained test by test","say":["Any headaches, fits, head injury or fevers? What's today's date, and where we are?","I'd like some bloods: blood count, kidney, liver, thyroid, glucose, and infection tests, plus a urine drug screen. Each one rules out a physical cause. You can watch me do it.","Is there any family history of mental health problems?"],"hear":"'No headaches or anything. If you tell me what each one's for, OK. My mum's cousin had schizophrenia.'","mark":"Oriented; no delirium features; bloods agreed; family history identified"},{"h":"Mood and risk both ways","say":["How's your mood been? Have you had thoughts of wishing you weren't here?","Have you done anything to protect yourself, or thought about confronting the neighbours?"],"hear":"'Sometimes I wish I wouldn't wake up, but my mum keeps me going. I bought a knife last week for by the bed.'","mark":"Risk to others identified: knife for protection"},{"h":"Collateral and making home safe","say":["Is it OK if I speak to Danny about what he's noticed?","Would you agree to Danny taking the knife out of the flat tonight?"],"hear":"'Danny's alright. Yeah, he can have it.'","mark":"Collateral consented; means removed"},{"h":"Explain and agree the plan","say":["I think you're experiencing psychosis. Lack of sleep and strong cannabis can bring it on. It's treatable, and young people often do well with early help.","Nobody is locking you up. If the bloods are normal, you can go home with Danny. I'm referring you to the early intervention team tonight, and the crisis team will contact you within 24 hours.","If you drive, don't drive for now; the DVLA needs to know about this. Here's NHS 111 option 2 if things get worse."],"hear":"'So I'm not going crazy? OK. Home with Danny, and they'll call me.'","mark":"Early intervention referral agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Psychotic symptoms","Filmed via smoke alarm; voices through the ceiling; thoughts leak out","First-episode psychosis with first-rank features"],["Substance use","Five or six skunk joints a day; cocaine once","Cannabis-associated; primary illness still possible"],["Organic screen","Oriented, no fits, fevers or head injury","Delirium unlikely; bloods and drug screen still needed"],["Risk","Passive death wish; bought a knife for protection","Remove the knife; act on delusion is the main risk"],["Early intervention plan","Agrees home with Danny and follow-up","EIP referral tonight; crisis team within 24 hours"]]},"key":"Ask about protection, not just harm. 'Have you done anything to protect yourself?' found the knife that 'Do you want to hurt anyone?' would have missed, and it changed the safe plan.","objmap":[[2],[5],[4],[8]]},"c-2-1-2":{"name":"Describing exactly what she experiences: delusions, voices, first-rank symptoms, thought disorder","why":"Replaces 'psychotic' with precise, quoted phenomenology the consultant can use for diagnosis, risk and the section 2 paperwork.","frame":["Delusions","Hallucinations","Thought interference","Passivity","Formal thought disorder","Insight and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Rapport and normalising","say":["Ms Grant, I'm Dr ___. I know you've told the police already. I'm asking so the team understands exactly what you've been going through.","Some of my questions may sound odd. I ask them to everyone."],"hear":"'Why does everybody keep asking?'"},{"h":"Delusions: content, onset, conviction","say":["You told the police someone was controlling the cars. Can you tell me more?","How did you first know? How certain are you, out of ten? Could there be another explanation?"],"hear":"'They're being controlled. I knew it when a traffic light went red last Monday. Ten out of ten.'","mark":"Persecutory delusion and delusional perception identified"},{"h":"Hallucinations: true or pseudo, person, form","say":["Do you hear people talking when nobody is there? One voice or more? Do they speak to you, or talk about you?","Where does the sound come from: inside your head, or outside like my voice?","Do they ever comment on what you're doing?"],"hear":"'A man and a woman, from the ceiling vent. They talk about me, \"she's going out now\".'","mark":"Third-person voices discussing her and running commentary: first-rank symptoms"},{"h":"Thought interference","say":["Do you ever hear your own thoughts spoken aloud?","Do you feel that thoughts get put into your mind or taken out of it, or that others know what you're thinking?"],"hear":"'No to the first. But my thoughts go out like radio, so the cars know where I am.'","mark":"Thought broadcasting identified; no echo, insertion or withdrawal"},{"h":"Passivity","say":["Do you feel your actions, movements or feelings are controlled by an outside force?","Or that your body is being moved or made to feel things by something outside you?"],"hear":"'No. Nobody controls me. It's the cars.'","mark":"Passivity absent"},{"h":"Formal thought disorder: let her talk and quote her","say":["Tell me how the cars are controlled.","(Write her exact words; note where the links between ideas break.)"],"hear":"'The signals come from the lights and the vent is a kind of exchange, and that's why the bus, Monday is red.'","mark":"Loosening of association recorded verbatim"},{"h":"Insight and risk tied to symptoms","say":["When the voices say things, what do you do?","Do you think this could be part of an illness? If you left today, where would you go?","Any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else?"],"hear":"'The woman says she has to warn them. Yesterday I stepped out to stop a bus. I'd go back to Hill Street. I'm not ill.'","mark":"Acting on voices: risk from road traffic; no insight"},{"h":"Summarise in descriptive language","say":["Fixed persecutory delusion about cars being controlled, with a delusional perception last Monday.","Third-person auditory hallucinations discussing her, with running commentary, heard from the vent, so true hallucinations; thought broadcasting; no passivity, insertion, withdrawal or echo.","Loosening of association, quoted. No insight. She has acted on the voices by stepping into traffic."],"hear":"Consultant: 'That's what I needed. Flag the road risk to the nurse in charge.'"}],"table":{"cols":["Phenomenon","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Delusions","Cars controlled; knew when a light turned red","Persecutory delusion with delusional perception"],["Hallucinations","Man and woman from the vent discussing her","True third-person voices with running commentary"],["Thought interference","Thoughts 'go out like radio'","Thought broadcasting; no echo, insertion or withdrawal"],["Passivity","'Nobody controls me'","Absent"],["Formal thought disorder","Loosely linked sentences about the bus and Monday","Loosening of association, quoted"],["Insight and risk","Stepped in front of a bus; 'I'm not ill'","No insight; acting on voices, road risk"]]},"key":"Name the phenomenon only after you have the evidence. Ask where the voice is heard and what she does in response; that one question separates a true hallucination from a pseudo-hallucination and finds the risk.","objmap":[[3],[6],[2],[8]]},"c-2-1-3":{"name":"Collateral from an angry brother: listen to everything, share only what Liam agreed","why":"Gathers the history and risk only family can give, while keeping Liam's confidence and supporting his brother.","frame":["Premorbid personality","Timeline of change","Family history","Risk: threats, weapons, targets","Substances","Carer strain"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge the anger and set the frame","say":["Mr Walsh, I'm sorry you found out from the landlord. That shouldn't have happened.","Liam has agreed I can talk with you. There's some information I can't share, but I can listen to anything, and what you know really helps us."],"hear":"'Nobody tells us anything! We found out he was here from his landlord.'","mark":"Confidentiality limits explained without defensiveness"},{"h":"What was he like before?","say":["Tell me about Liam before all this. What was he like?","What did he enjoy, and how did he get on at work?"],"hear":"'Funny, sociable, five-a-side every week, team leader at the warehouse.'","mark":"Good premorbid functioning identified"},{"h":"Timeline of change","say":["When did you first notice something was different?","What happened next with work, friends, eating and sleep?"],"hear":"'About a year ago he quit football, said the lads were talking about him. Lost his job in March, said his boss was spying. Up all night, taped over the sockets.'","mark":"Prodrome and timeline identified"},{"h":"Family and past history","say":["Has anything like this happened before, to Liam or anyone in the family?","Has he ever seen a mental health team or been in hospital before?"],"hear":"'Our Uncle Mick had schizophrenia.'","mark":"Family history identified"},{"h":"Risk: threats, weapons, targets","say":["Has he said or done anything that frightened you, about his safety or anyone else's?","Is there anyone he's angry with? Any weapons at home?"],"hear":"'Last Thursday he said he'd stab the man next door if he kept beaming things at him. Only kitchen knives. He once said he was tired of it all.'","mark":"Threat to an identifiable person: inform the team today"},{"h":"Substances: receive, don't confirm","say":["I can't go into what Liam has told us. But anything you know about alcohol or drugs is useful to hear.","You can tell me whatever you know; I won't be able to confirm or deny it."],"hear":"'Is he on drugs? Just tell me straight. His mates say he smokes a lot of weed.'","mark":"Collateral on cannabis received; nothing disclosed"},{"h":"His own strain","say":["How are you managing? It sounds like you've been carrying a lot.","You can have a carer's assessment, and there's family support from the team."],"hear":"'I've been sleeping on his sofa for weeks to keep an eye on him.'","mark":"Carer strain identified; carer's assessment offered"},{"h":"Be honest about using the information, and close","say":["Will Liam know? I'll be careful how we use it, but I can't promise to keep a threat secret, because keeping people safe comes first.","I'll pass this to the consultant today. Here is the ward number; you can call any time."],"hear":"'Fair enough. As long as someone's actually doing something.'"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Premorbid personality","Sociable, football, team leader","Clear decline from a good baseline"],["Timeline of change","Withdrew a year ago; job lost March; sockets taped","Long prodrome into persecutory psychosis"],["Family history","Uncle had schizophrenia","Raises likelihood of a primary psychotic illness"],["Risk","Threat to stab neighbour; 'tired of it all'","Specific target: update risk plan and team today"],["Carer strain","Sleeping on Liam's sofa for weeks","Carer's assessment and family support"]]},"key":"Listening never breaches confidentiality; sharing can. Ask specifically about threats, weapons and targets, because relatives often hold the risk information the patient leaves out.","objmap":[[4],[5],[5],[7]]},"c-2-1-4":{"name":"At the door: engage, assess mental state and risk, decide what next","why":"Gets enough mental state and risk from a crack in the door to act safely and lawfully today.","frame":["Engagement and safety","Psychotic symptoms","Self-neglect","Risk to others","Risk to self","Legal options"],"steps":[{"h":"Position safely and introduce","say":["(Stand to the side of the door, Sam beside you, exit clear, office aware of the visit.)","Hello Mr Price, I'm Dr ___ from the mental health team, with Sam. We've been worried because we haven't seen you. Can we talk for a few minutes from here?"],"hear":"'Go away. I don't need you. I'm fine in here.'"},{"h":"Validate the fear, ask about the foil","say":["It sounds like things have felt frightening. We're not coming in.","I can see foil on the windows. What's that for?"],"hear":"'The neighbours beam radiation through the walls. The foil keeps it out.'","mark":"Persecutory delusions identified"},{"h":"Voices and sleep","say":["Do you hear anything, voices or whispers, when no one's around?","How are you sleeping?"],"hear":"'They whisper through the sockets. Call me dirty, say don't trust anyone. Two hours a night.'","mark":"Auditory hallucinations identified"},{"h":"Self-neglect: food, money, power","say":["When did you last have a proper meal? Have you got gas and electricity?","Is anyone else staying in the flat or using it?"],"hear":"'Not for days. No money. The meter ran out two days ago.'","mark":"Self-neglect identified"},{"h":"Risk to others, asked calmly","say":["I noticed a knife by the door. Can you tell me about that?","Is there anyone in particular you're worried about, or angry with?"],"hear":"'It's for them, if they come through.'","mark":"Weapon held against perceived persecutors"},{"h":"Risk to self","say":["With all this going on, do you ever think about ending your life?","Have you made any plans, or is anything stopping you?"],"hear":"'Sometimes I'd be better off dead. No plan. I wouldn't leave my mum.'","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts; mother protective"},{"h":"Why the depot stopped, and what would help","say":["What made it hard to come for your injection?","What would help right now? Could Sam bring some food and sort the meter today?"],"hear":"'My legs went stiff, and it poisons me. You're going to stab me with that needle again. Food would be good.'","mark":"Side effects and delusion behind non-adherence; accepts food"},{"h":"Decide and escalate","say":["Thank you for talking to us. We'll come back with food today.","(To Sam, away from the door) Relapse with a knife, self-neglect and no power. I'll ring the consultant and the AMHP now about a Mental Health Act assessment; if he won't open the door, the AMHP can apply for a section 135(1) warrant."],"hear":"Sam: 'Agreed. I'll update his mum and the duty team.'","mark":"MHA assessment requested"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Psychotic symptoms","Radiation through walls; whispers from sockets","Clear relapse of schizophrenia"],["Self-neglect","No food for days, no money, meter ran out","Urgent practical needs today"],["Risk to others","Knife by the door 'for them'","Serious risk if he feels threatened"],["Risk to self","Better off dead at times; mum protective","Passive thoughts; monitor closely"],["Legal options","Refuses hospital; won't let anyone in","MHA assessment; s135(1) warrant if entry needed"]]},"key":"Do not force your way in, and don't walk away either. Get what you can from the doorway, meet a practical need, then escalate through the right legal route, a s135(1) warrant if entry is needed.","objmap":[[5,8],[4,8],[5,8],[8]]},"c-2-1-5":{"name":"Why the relapse happened: a no-blame timeline and a plan to stop it repeating","why":"Finds every reason she relapsed, not just 'stopped tablets', and turns them into a relapse prevention plan she agrees with.","frame":["Adherence and side effects","Substance use","Life stressors","Service factors","Early warning signs","Relapse prevention plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Remove the blame first","say":["Chloe, I'm Dr ___. This isn't about fault. Most people stop or miss tablets at some point. I want to understand what happened so the next discharge works better.","Can we map out together what happened between leaving the ward and coming back?"],"hear":"'I know, I stopped the tablets. It's my fault I'm back.'"},{"h":"Adherence and side effects","say":["When did you stop the aripiprazole, and what was it like taking it?","Some people get restless on it, as if they can't sit still. Did anything like that happen?"],"hear":"'In April. I paced all night, like ants in my legs.'","mark":"Akathisia identified as the reason for stopping"},{"h":"Life stressors","say":["What else was going on around then: money, work, relationships?","Had anything ended or changed for you in the spring?"],"hear":"'My boyfriend of five years left in May.'","mark":"Major loss identified"},{"h":"Cannabis","say":["Were you using cannabis or anything else, and did that change?","I'm asking because it matters for your plan, not to tell you off."],"hear":"'Weekends before. After he left, every day.'","mark":"Escalating cannabis identified"},{"h":"Service factors","say":["Who from the team were you seeing? Was it enough?","Did you know who to call when things got harder?"],"hear":"'My care coordinator left and nobody replaced her for two months. One night in June I thought about an overdose and rang my mum instead.'","mark":"Service gap identified; acknowledge and apologise"},{"h":"Early warning signs","say":["Looking back, what did you notice first? What did your mum or friends notice?","Would you be happy for us to ask your mum what she spotted?"],"hear":"'I stopped sleeping, then thought people at work were talking about me. Mum said I went quiet. A friend got weird texts at night.'","mark":"Relapse signature identified"},{"h":"Treatment options, her choice","say":["The restlessness is a known side effect. We could change the dose, treat the restlessness, or try a different medicine.","Some people choose a monthly injection so they don't have to remember tablets. It isn't only for people who are forced. It's your choice, and you can think it over."],"hear":"'I'm not having a depot. That's for people who are forced.' / 'Maybe, if it's my choice.'","mark":"LAI offered without coercion"},{"h":"Write the relapse prevention plan","say":["Let's write your early signs, what you'll do, and who to call, and share it with your mum if you agree.","After a section 3 you're entitled to section 117 aftercare. Someone will see you within 72 hours of discharge, and a named care coordinator before you leave."],"hear":"'Will anyone actually follow me up this time?' / 'OK, if it's in writing.'","mark":"Relapse prevention plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Adherence and side effects","Stopped in April; 'ants in my legs'","Akathisia drove non-adherence; treat or switch"],["Substance use","Cannabis weekly to daily after break-up","Address cannabis in the plan"],["Life stressors","Boyfriend of five years left in May","Loss triggered the slide"],["Service factors","No care coordinator for two months","Our gap; named coordinator before discharge"],["Early warning signs","Poor sleep, suspicious at work, quiet, night texts","Her relapse signature for the plan"]]},"key":"Non-adherence is the start of the question, not the answer. Ask what made her stop; here it was akathisia, a loss, cannabis and a service gap, and each needs its own part of the plan.","objmap":[[1,6],[2],[6,8],[7,8]]},"c-2-1-6":{"name":"Telling a family it is schizophrenia: causes, genetics and outlook without blame","why":"Gives Jake and his mother an accurate, hopeful understanding they can repeat to his sister.","frame":["What schizophrenia is","Causes","Genetics","Treatment","Outlook and safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with what they know and fear","say":["Jake, Mrs Ellis, before I explain, what do you understand about what's been happening?","What worries you most about the word schizophrenia?"],"hear":"Jake: 'I looked it up. Split personality.' Mother: 'Is this our fault, and will his sister get it too?'","mark":"Ideas and fears identified: split personality, blame, inheritance"},{"h":"What it is, linked to his symptoms","say":["Schizophrenia affects how a person thinks and perceives things. For Jake, that's the voices and feeling watched since March.","It isn't a split personality. Some people also lose motivation or find concentrating harder."],"hear":"Mother: 'I thought it was just stress from his job.'","mark":"Diagnosis linked to his own symptoms"},{"h":"Causes, and naming the guilt","say":["There's no single cause. Some people carry a vulnerability, partly genetic, and stresses on top, such as heavy cannabis use or difficult life events, can bring it on.","Parenting doesn't cause schizophrenia. This is not your fault."],"hear":"Mother (tearful): 'I keep going over his childhood.'","mark":"Guilt addressed directly"},{"h":"Genetics, with numbers","say":["In the general population the lifetime risk is about 1 in 100. For a brother or sister of someone with schizophrenia it's higher, roughly 1 in 10.","That means most siblings never develop it. If his sister ever has worrying changes, she can get help early."],"hear":"Mother: 'So it's not certain she'll get it.'","mark":"Sibling risk explained"},{"h":"Treatment and what matters to Jake","say":["Treatment is medicine, talking therapy, family sessions and help getting back to work.","Jake, what matters most to you? How long you take medicine is something we'll decide together over time."],"hear":"Jake: 'Getting back to work and seeing my mates. Will I have to take tablets for life?'"},{"h":"Outlook and the fear of danger","say":["Many people recover well, and some have only one episode. Getting treatment early, as Jake is, helps.","Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; they're more likely to be harmed by others."],"hear":"Mother: 'Does this mean he's dangerous?' / Jake: 'So my life's not over.'","mark":"Stigma and safety addressed"},{"h":"Practical safety: driving and mood","say":["Jake, with this diagnosis you need to tell the DVLA; I'll explain what that involves.","You had dark thoughts a few weeks ago. If they come back, call us or NHS 111 option 2."],"hear":"Jake: 'None now. OK.'"},{"h":"Teach-back and close","say":["How would you explain this to his sister?","I'll give you written information and the team's number, and offer family sessions."],"hear":"Mother: 'It's not our fault, it's an illness of thinking, she's at a bit more risk but probably won't get it, and he can get better.'"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What schizophrenia is","'Split personality'; 'just stress'","Correct myths; link to his voices and feeling watched"],["Causes","Mother blaming his childhood","Vulnerability plus stress; parenting is not a cause"],["Genetics","'Will his sister get it?'","About 1 in 10 for a sibling; most never do"],["Treatment","Wants work and friends; fears tablets for life","Shared plan built on his goals"],["Outlook and safety","'Is he dangerous?'","Many recover; more likely victim than danger"]]},"key":"Numbers reassure only when framed both ways. Say the sibling risk is about 1 in 10 and that nine in ten never develop it, then check the family can say it back.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,8],[8]]},"c-2-1-7":{"name":"Choosing an antipsychotic together, and explaining a long-acting injection","why":"Matches the medicine to what matters to her, keeps her well, and leaves the injection decision with her.","frame":["Her priorities","Side-effect profiles","Relapse history","Long-acting injection","Pregnancy plans","Monitoring"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge the weight first","say":["Priya, I'm Dr ___. The voices have gone, which is great, but gaining 8 kilos in two months is a real problem and I take it seriously.","Let's look at the options together."],"hear":"'I hate how I look. I'm stopping it.'","mark":"Weight gain acknowledged"},{"h":"What matters to her","say":["What matters most to you in a medicine: weight, energy, periods, sex life, not having to remember tablets?","How are your periods? Are you planning a family?"],"hear":"'Weight, and not feeling like a zombie till 11. Periods are fine. We want to try for a baby next year.'","mark":"Priorities: weight, sedation, pregnancy plans"},{"h":"Relapse history","say":["What happened the times you stopped your tablets before?","How quickly did things go downhill after you stopped?"],"hear":"'I got ill again. Twice.'","mark":"Two relapses after stopping suddenly"},{"h":"Side-effect profiles in plain words","say":["Olanzapine is good for symptoms but often causes weight gain and sleepiness.","Aripiprazole usually causes less weight gain and sleepiness and doesn't usually affect periods. Some people feel restless on it, and we'd watch for that.","Please don't stop suddenly. That's when you've relapsed before. We'd switch gradually."],"hear":"'Will the weight come off if I switch?'","mark":"Switch to aripiprazole proposed"},{"h":"Explain the long-acting injection without pressure","say":["Some people choose an injection every month instead of daily tablets, so they don't have to remember. It's not a punishment or a sign of being a problem patient.","With aripiprazole you'd take tablets first to check you suit it, and keep taking them for a short time after the first injection. It's a small injection into a muscle.","It's your choice. You don't need to decide today."],"hear":"'How often is it, and does it hurt? What if I say no?'","mark":"LAI explained; choice left with her"},{"h":"Pregnancy planning","say":["Before you try for a baby, let's plan it with the specialist perinatal team so your medicine is right for you and the baby.","Keep your implant until then."],"hear":"'OK, good to know before we start trying.'","mark":"Perinatal advice offered"},{"h":"Monitoring and weight support","say":["We'll check your weight weekly for the first six weeks, and blood sugar and cholesterol at 12 weeks.","Some weight may come off; we'll add diet and exercise support too."],"hear":"'Fine.'"},{"h":"Decision, teach-back and record","say":["So the plan is?","I'll write that you understood the options and chose this yourself."],"hear":"'Switch to aripiprazole tablets slowly. Think about the injection. Weight checks.'","mark":"Shared decision and capacity recorded"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her priorities","Weight, daytime sedation","Choose a lower metabolic, less sedating option"],["Relapse history","Two relapses after stopping suddenly","Avoid abrupt stopping; consider an LAI"],["Long-acting injection","Fears being 'controlled', dislikes needles","Explain as an option; her choice"],["Pregnancy plans","Trying for a baby next year","Perinatal advice before conception"],["Monitoring","Wants the weight to come off","Weekly weight, 12-week bloods, lifestyle support"]]},"key":"Name the side effect before you name the solution. Once she feels heard about the weight, she is ready to weigh the options; pushing the injection first loses both the switch and the injection.","objmap":[[2],[4],[5],[7,8]]},"c-2-1-8":{"name":"Is it the drug? Naming akathisia, checking EPSE and explaining prolactin","why":"Recognises akathisia as a side effect rather than agitation, documents a movement baseline and agrees a safer plan Jade can accept.","frame":["Akathisia","Acute dystonia","Parkinsonism","Tardive dyskinesia","Raised prolactin","NMS red flags"],"steps":[{"h":"Meet her where she is","say":["Hi Jade, I'm one of the ward doctors. Stand or walk if that's easier, we can talk on the move.","The nurses tell me you can't keep still. Tell me what it feels like from the inside."],"hear":"\"Like I'm going to burst out of my skin. I can't sit for a minute.\""},{"h":"Akathisia: inner feeling and timing","say":["When did this restless feeling start, and had the haloperidol just begun?","Is it mostly in your legs? Is it worse when you try to sit or lie down?","Is it a feeling you have to move, rather than worries or voices pushing you?"],"hear":"\"A few days after the new tablet. My legs won't stop, sitting is the worst.\"","mark":"Akathisia identified"},{"h":"Safety linked to the restlessness","say":["When it's this bad, some people feel they'd rather be dead. Has that crossed your mind?","Any plan to act on it, or urges to lash out at someone?"],"hear":"\"Last night I thought I'd rather be dead. I wouldn't do anything. I don't want to hurt anyone.\"","mark":"Passive death wish, no plan or intent"},{"h":"Other movement effects, then look","say":["Early on, did your neck or eyes ever twist or lock?","Any shaking, stiffness, or movements of your mouth or tongue you can't control?","Could you sit for two minutes, then stand still for two while I watch? Then I'll gently move your arms and check your walk."],"hear":"\"My neck went twisted on day two, they gave me an injection. My hands shake a bit.\"","mark":"Past acute dystonia; mild tremor; no tardive signs"},{"h":"Hormones: rule out the other causes first","say":["You mentioned your periods and some milk. When was your last period? Could you be pregnant at the moment?","Any headaches or changes in your eyesight?","Any fever, sweating, confusion or aching muscles?"],"hear":"\"About seven weeks ago. I haven't had sex for months. No headaches, eyes are fine.\"","mark":"Likely raised prolactin; no pituitary or NMS flags"},{"h":"Explain both side effects plainly","say":["This restlessness has a name, akathisia. It's the medicine, not your illness getting worse, and more of the same drug won't help it.","Haloperidol also raises a hormone called prolactin. That stops periods and can cause milk. It's not dangerous, and when the level falls, periods usually return.","I'll check a blood level and a pregnancy test today, plus thyroid, kidney and muscle tests."],"hear":"\"So I'm not going mad? Will I still be able to have kids?\""},{"h":"Agree the plan and close","say":["My suggestion: lower the dose now and talk with your consultant about switching to one that's gentler on restlessness and hormones, like aripiprazole.","Propranolol can calm the restlessness meanwhile, as long as you don't have asthma.","I'll ask nurses not to give extra haloperidol for this, and to tell me if those dark thoughts come back. Does that sound fair?"],"hear":"\"Yes. As long as nobody gives me more of that stuff.\"","mark":"Shared plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Side effect","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Akathisia","Inner urge to move, worst sitting, began days after haloperidol; pacing on the ward","Drug-induced; rate on the Barnes scale and stop PRN haloperidol for 'agitation'"],["Acute dystonia","Neck twisted on day two, settled with an injection","Confirms high EPSE sensitivity to this drug"],["Parkinsonism","Mild hand tremor","Record a Simpson-Angus baseline; dose reduction should help"],["Tardive dyskinesia","No mouth, tongue or face movements","AIMS baseline for future comparison"],["Raised prolactin","Seven weeks without a period, milk leaking, no sex, no headache or visual change","Likely drug effect; confirm with prolactin and hCG, then switch to a prolactin-sparing drug"],["NMS red flags","No fever, sweating, confusion or muscle pain","Not NMS now; CK as a baseline"]]},"key":"Akathisia looks like agitation. The excellent doctor names it as the drug, stops extra antipsychotic being given for it, and asks about suicidal urges because akathisia itself drives them.","objmap":[[6],[4],[6],[7],[3,6]]},"c-2-1-9":{"name":"Clozapine: why him, what to watch, and home or ward start","why":"Gives Michael and his mother a balanced picture of clozapine and agrees a safe place to start it.","frame":["Why clozapine","Blood monitoring","Serious risks","Everyday side effects","Smoking and missed doses","Where to start"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with his story and hopes","say":["Thanks for coming, both of you. Michael, how are the voices at the moment?","What would you most like to be different if a new medicine worked?"],"hear":"\"They're there all day. I just want to sleep properly and get back to the gym.\""},{"h":"Why clozapine, and what he knows","say":["You've taken olanzapine and risperidone properly and the voices stayed. That's what we call treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","Clozapine is the one medicine shown to help when two others haven't. Many people in your position get real benefit.","What have you heard about it already?"],"hear":"\"I read it can kill you.\"","mark":"Treatment resistance confirmed; main fear named"},{"h":"Blood tests and the white cell risk","say":["A few people get a drop in the infection-fighting white cells. That's why everyone has blood tests: weekly for 18 weeks, then every two weeks up to a year, then monthly.","Results come back as green, amber or red. Red means we stop it straight away, which is how we keep it safe.","Any sore throat, fever or flu-like illness, tell us that day."],"hear":"\"Weekly needles? I suppose, if that's the reason for them.\"","mark":"Agrees to blood monitoring"},{"h":"Heart, bowel and fits","say":["In the first couple of months it can rarely inflame the heart, so we check pulse, temperature and blood markers and want to hear about chest pain or breathlessness.","It slows the gut, and severe constipation can be dangerous. How often do your bowels open now?","At higher doses it can cause fits, so we go up slowly."],"hear":"\"Every other day, usually.\"","mark":"Constipation risk identified: laxative from the start"},{"h":"Everyday side effects, smoking and missed doses","say":["Common ones are sleepiness, drooling at night, weight gain and a fast pulse. We watch weight and blood sugar.","Smoking lowers the level of clozapine. If you cut down or stop, even in hospital, tell us first so we can adjust the dose.","If you miss more than two days, don't restart on your own. Call us, as we'd need to build up again from a low dose."],"hear":"\"I smoke about twenty a day. I'm not giving up.\"","mark":"Smoking and missed-dose rules given"},{"h":"Home or ward start","say":["We start low and go up over two to three weeks, checking pulse, blood pressure and temperature every day at first.","Starting at home can work if someone is around, you can get bloods done and the team can visit daily. Angela, are you home most days?","If the visits can't be arranged, or anything worries us, a short ward stay is the safer option."],"hear":"(Mother) \"I'm home nearly every day. I can make sure he takes it.\"","mark":"Community start feasible"},{"h":"Decide together and check back","say":["So the plan: register you with the monitoring service, do bloods, an ECG and weight, then start at home with daily team visits.","Can you tell me in your words the three things you'd ring us about straight away?","I'll give you a leaflet and a number to call. Take a few days if you want to think."],"hear":"\"Sore throat or fever, chest pain, and if I can't go to the toilet.\"","mark":"Shared decision; teach-back done"}],"table":{"cols":["Clozapine area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Why clozapine","Voices all day despite two antipsychotics taken reliably","Meets treatment resistance; clozapine is the evidence-based next step"],["Blood monitoring","Willing to have weekly bloods once the reason is clear","Register with the monitoring service and book the schedule"],["Serious risks","Bowels open every other day; read it can kill","Laxative from day one; give clear red-flag symptoms for gut, heart and infection"],["Smoking and missed doses","Smokes twenty a day, no plan to stop","Levels will rise if he stops; he must tell the team; restart rule after 48 hours"],["Where to start","Mother home most days; does not want admission; bus ride to clinic","Home start with daily observations if the team can deliver it; ward if not"]]},"key":"Explaining risks is not enough. The excellent doctor turns them into the few symptoms the family must act on, and bases home versus ward on who can do the daily checks.","objmap":[[2],[4],[3],[6],[5]]},"c-2-1-10":{"name":"Not lazy, unwell: negative symptoms and what actually helps","why":"Finds and rules out secondary causes, explains negative symptoms to Ryan and his father, and starts real psychosocial treatment.","frame":["Negative symptoms","Secondary causes","CBT for psychosis","Family intervention","Return to education"],"steps":[{"h":"Engage Ryan first","say":["Ryan, I'll talk with you first and see your dad after, if that's OK with you.","Walk me through a normal day at the moment."],"hear":"\"Get up about twelve. Stay in my room. That's it really.\""},{"h":"Map the negative symptoms","say":["Is there anything you still get pleasure from, even a bit? Do you look forward to things?","When you think about doing something, what stops you getting started?","How much do you talk to people now compared with before?"],"hear":"\"Not really. Can't be bothered. Nothing feels worth it.\"","mark":"Avolition, anhedonia and social withdrawal identified"},{"h":"Rule out secondary causes","say":["How's your mood? Do you ever feel there's no point at all? Any thoughts about ending your life?","Does the aripiprazole make you sleepy, stiff or restless?","Are the voices still around, and do you avoid people because of them? Any cannabis since you left hospital?"],"hear":"\"Bit sleepy mornings. Voices are quiet. I avoid people from college because they saw me ill.\"","mark":"Some sedation and embarrassment; no depression with intent; no cannabis"},{"h":"Offer CBT for psychosis","say":["There's a talking therapy called CBT for psychosis. You'd meet a psychologist for at least 16 sessions.","It looks at things like 'what's the point' and the fear of people judging you, and helps you take small steps back.","Would you give it a try?"],"hear":"\"Maybe. If it's not just talking for nothing.\"","mark":"Open to CBTp"},{"h":"One goal, practical support","say":["You said you might think about college part-time. Our employment and education worker can help with that.","Could we pick one small thing to do each week with your care coordinator?","I'll also ask about giving your tablet at night instead, to help the morning sleepiness."],"hear":"\"Part-time might be alright.\"","mark":"Shared goal: supported return to college"},{"h":"Bring Dad in: explain without blame","say":["Mr Clarke, what we're seeing is part of the illness, not laziness. We call these negative symptoms, and they often last after the voices settle.","We'd like to offer family intervention: around ten sessions with Ryan and you, about understanding the illness and reducing stress at home.","Pushing hard usually backfires. Small, steady goals work better."],"hear":"(Father) \"So he's not just lying about in there? Nobody told us that.\""},{"h":"Support the father and close","say":["You're carrying a lot. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment, and I'll arrange it.","So: CBT for psychosis, family sessions, college support and a medicine timing review. Ryan, does that feel right?"],"hear":"(Ryan) \"Yeah. OK.\"","mark":"Plan agreed with both"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Negative symptoms","In his room all day, no drive, nothing feels worth it, barely speaks","Primary negative symptoms likely after positive symptoms settled"],["Secondary causes","Morning sleepiness, avoidance from embarrassment, 'what's the point' with no intent","Adjust dose timing, monitor mood, address social anxiety; not a reason to raise the dose"],["CBT for psychosis","Willing if it is practical","Refer; at least 16 sessions"],["Family intervention","Father angry and feels nothing is happening","Offer around ten sessions plus a carer's assessment"],["Return to education","Would consider part-time college","Supported education and employment worker; one weekly goal"]]},"key":"Before calling it negative symptoms, rule out depression, sedation, avoidance and drugs. The excellent doctor then offers named NICE therapies with a goal Ryan chose, not just reassurance.","objmap":[[2,3],[6],[4,7],[5],[7]]},"c-2-1-11":{"name":"Psychosis, trauma and cannabis: one plan, not three","why":"Finds PTSD symptoms without asking for the story, understands what cannabis does for Sophie, and builds trauma-informed care into the ward plan.","frame":["Re-experiencing","Avoidance","Hyperarousal","Cannabis use","Safety now"],"steps":[{"h":"Safe start, no story needed","say":["Sophie, you're in control here. You don't have to tell me what happened, only how it's affecting you now.","Is it OK to have the nurse with us?"],"hear":"\"It's fine with her here.\""},{"h":"Re-experiencing","say":["The nurses say you have bad dreams. How often?","During the day, do memories ever push in, so it feels like it's happening again?"],"hear":"\"Most nights. And flashbacks in the day, out of nowhere.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing identified"},{"h":"Avoidance and hyperarousal","say":["Are there places or things you avoid because of it?","Do you feel on edge, jumpy, always watching? What happens when male staff come in at night?"],"hear":"\"I avoid that part of town. When a man opens my door at night I'm terrified.\"","mark":"Avoidance and hyperarousal identified"},{"h":"Separate trauma from psychosis","say":["Are the voices the same as these memories, or different?","Do they get louder when you're scared?"],"hear":"\"Different. But they're louder when I'm frightened.\"","mark":"PTSD alongside psychosis, each feeding the other"},{"h":"Cannabis: what it does for her","say":["How much do you smoke, and what does it do for you?","What happened the last time you stopped?","Have you noticed any link between smoking and the voices coming back?"],"hear":"\"Two or three joints of skunk a day, about £60 a week. It helps me sleep. I stopped once and couldn't sleep.\"","mark":"Cannabis used to manage sleep and fear; withdrawal on stopping"},{"h":"Safety now","say":["Is the man still around? Are you safe where you live?","After the nightmares, have you thought of harming yourself?","If you ever want to report it, we'll support you. If not, that's your choice, and nothing changes in your care."],"hear":"\"He's on my estate. No threats. I've had thoughts after nightmares, never done anything. I don't want police.\"","mark":"Ongoing exposure; fleeting self-harm thoughts"},{"h":"Integrated plan and close","say":["Tonight I'll ask that female staff do your night checks and knock and say who they are.","When things are calmer, there's therapy that works for trauma: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Having psychosis or using cannabis doesn't rule you out, and we go at your pace.","Our dual diagnosis worker can help find other ways to sleep, so the cannabis isn't doing that job. And what happened was not your fault."],"hear":"\"Will therapy make me relive it?\" ... \"OK, if I can stop when I need to.\"","mark":"Integrated plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Re-experiencing","Nightmares most nights, daytime flashbacks","Core PTSD feature present"],["Avoidance","Keeps away from that part of town and going out at night","PTSD supported; check against housing and leave plans"],["Hyperarousal","Jumpy, on edge, terrified of male staff at night","Ward routines retraumatise her; change night checks now"],["Cannabis use","Skunk two to three joints daily for sleep; withdrawal when stopped","Self-treatment that worsens psychosis; motivational work and sleep plan"],["Safety now","Perpetrator on her estate; fleeting self-harm thoughts after nightmares","Safeguarding advice, reporting offered, risk plan in care notes"]]},"key":"Ask about symptoms, never the story. The excellent doctor sees cannabis as her sleep and fear treatment and replaces that job, rather than demanding she stops before trauma work.","objmap":[[3],[5],[7],[7],[6]]},"c-2-1-12":{"name":"A named person, a plan for tonight: managing risk of violence in A&E","why":"Establishes how specific and imminent the threat to the neighbour is, keeps everyone safe and secures the right legal route and disclosure.","frame":["The belief","Intent and plan","Means and past violence","Current drivers","Protecting others"],"steps":[{"h":"Safe room, calm start","say":["Mr Hughes, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. I'll sit by the door; security are nearby, as they are for everyone.","You're keen to get home. Tell me what's been going on."],"hear":"\"My neighbour's poisoning my water. I need to get back and sort it.\""},{"h":"The belief","say":["How do you know it's him? What have you noticed?","How sure are you, from nought to a hundred?","How does it make you feel when you think about it?"],"hear":"\"It tastes of metal. Hundred percent. I'm furious.\"","mark":"Fixed persecutory delusion with anger"},{"h":"Intent and plan","say":["What do you mean by 'sort it'?","When? Where would you see him?","What would you do?"],"hear":"\"Tonight, when he's back from the pub. I'll deal with him.\"","mark":"Named victim, imminent plan"},{"h":"Means and past violence","say":["Do you have anything at home you'd use, like knives?","Have you ever hurt anyone before, especially when unwell?","Does anyone else live with him?"],"hear":"\"Kitchen knives. I was done for assault years ago when I was ill. His wife lives there.\"","mark":"Access to weapons; past violence when unwell"},{"h":"Drivers, protection and self-harm","say":["When did you stop the depot? Any drink or drugs tonight?","What would stop you going through with it?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"Two months ago, it made me a zombie. Two cans. I don't want prison again. No, not myself.\"","mark":"Non-adherence and alcohol; prison a deterrent; no self-harm"},{"h":"Tell him the plan honestly","say":["I'm worried someone could get badly hurt tonight, including you. I can't agree to you going home.","I'd like you to stay here while I speak to my consultant and arrange a Mental Health Act assessment.","Because there's a risk to your neighbour, I may need to share limited information with the police. I'm telling you because I won't do it behind your back."],"hear":"\"So he gets away with it and I get locked up?\""},{"h":"Escalate and secure","say":["(To consultant) Thirty-five-year-old man, paranoid schizophrenia, off depot two months, named neighbour, plan for tonight, knives at home, past assault. Requesting an MHA assessment via the AMHP now.","Until then: one-to-one with security nearby, offer oral medication, and the police told if he leaves.","I'll record the disclosure decision and what was shared with the police."],"hear":"(Consultant) \"Agreed. Hold him safely and ring the AMHP.\"","mark":"Escalated; MHA assessment requested; disclosure planned"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The belief","Neighbour poisoning his water, total conviction, angry","Persecutory delusion with anger: a recognised path to violence"],["Intent and plan","Will 'deal with him' tonight after the pub","Specific and imminent: high risk"],["Means and past violence","Kitchen knives; assault conviction when unwell","Best predictor of future violence is past violence in the same state"],["Current drivers","Off depot two months; two cans tonight; lives alone","Relapse and disinhibition raise risk"],["Protecting others","Neighbour and his wife next door; does not want prison","MHA assessment, police disclosure in the public interest, deterrent noted"]]},"key":"A named person, a plan for tonight and access to weapons are high risk whatever the scores say. The excellent doctor tells him about disclosure honestly and remembers s5(2) does not apply in A&E.","objmap":[[7],[7],[7],[6],[7]]},"c-2-1-13":{"name":"Morbid jealousy: belief, behaviour and danger to the family","why":"Assesses Darren's jealousy without putting Kerry at risk, sees her alone, and puts safeguarding for her and the children in place the same day.","frame":["The belief","Checking and control","Violence and threats","Alcohol","Children"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, without quoting Kerry","say":["Mr Shaw, thanks for coming. I'd like to hear your side in your words.","How are things at home at the moment?"],"hear":"\"Terrible. My wife's having an affair and everyone thinks I'm the problem.\""},{"h":"The belief","say":["What makes you sure?","Might there be another explanation for what you've seen?","Who do you think the man is?"],"hear":"\"Marks on her underwear, deleted texts. There's no other explanation.\"","mark":"Fixed belief on trivial evidence: delusional jealousy"},{"h":"Checking and control","say":["What do you do to find out the truth?","How often do you check her phone or her things? Do you wait for her anywhere?"],"hear":"\"Every day. Phone, bag, washing. I wait outside her work.\"","mark":"Daily checking and following identified"},{"h":"Violence, threats, rivals","say":["When you argue, has it ever got physical?","What would you do if you found the man?","Has this happened in past relationships? Any weapons at home?"],"hear":"\"I pushed her against a wall last week. I'd batter him. My ex left because of it. No weapons.\"","mark":"Recent violence, threat to rival, previous pattern"},{"h":"Alcohol and children","say":["How much do you drink over a week?","What do the children see and hear when you argue?"],"hear":"\"Six or seven pints most nights. My son told me to stop shouting.\"","mark":"Heavy drinking; children exposed"},{"h":"See Kerry alone","say":["Kerry, I'm seeing you on your own so you can speak freely. Has he ever said anything that frightened you?","I'd like to go through some questions about how safe you are at home. Is there somewhere you and the children could stay?","None of this is your fault."],"hear":"(Kerry) \"He said 'if I can't have you, no one will'. My sister would take us.\"","mark":"High risk on DASH; safe place available"},{"h":"Safety plan and honest close","say":["Kerry, with children at home I have to refer to children's social care today. That's to support you, not to take them away. I'll also refer you to an independent domestic violence adviser and to MARAC.","Darren, I think your mind is convincing you of something that isn't true. I'd like you to try an antipsychotic and get help with the drinking.","And yes, I am involving social services, because the children need to be safe. I'd rather tell you than have you hear it elsewhere."],"hear":"(Darren) \"I don't need tablets, she needs to tell the truth.\"","mark":"Safeguarding, MARAC and treatment plan in place"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The belief","Sure of an affair from underwear marks and deleted texts","Delusional jealousy; no amount of reassurance will shift it"],["Checking and control","Daily phone, bag and washing checks; waits outside her work","Escalating surveillance often comes before serious assault"],["Violence and threats","Pushed her last week; 'if I can't have you no one will'; would batter the rival; ex left","High risk of serious harm or homicide; MARAC threshold"],["Alcohol","Six or seven pints most nights","Major added risk; offer alcohol treatment"],["Children","Aged 9 and 12, hear arguments","Same-day referral to children's social care"]]},"key":"Never put Kerry's account to Darren: it can trigger violence. The excellent doctor sees her alone, uses DASH, and treats the children's exposure as a safeguarding matter today.","objmap":[[1],[6],[7],[4],[7]]},"c-2-2-1":{"name":"Depression, risk and a worried partner: what you can hear and share","why":"Takes a depression history and MSE, finds the true level of risk, and uses the partner's account within the limits of confidentiality.","frame":["Core symptoms","Biological symptoms","Thoughts about self and future","Suicide risk","Collateral and confidentiality"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with her","say":["Ms Patel, I'd like to talk to you on your own first, then your partner can join if you're happy.","How have things been for you lately?"],"hear":"\"Fine. I'm just tired. Work's mad. He's overreacting.\""},{"h":"Core symptoms","say":["How's your mood been, day to day, over the last few months?","Are there things you used to enjoy that you've stopped?"],"hear":"\"Flat since the summer. I've stopped running, and I don't go to the kids' swimming.\"","mark":"Low mood and loss of interest identified"},{"h":"Biological symptoms and screens","say":["How are you sleeping and eating? Any weight change?","Have you ever had a spell where you felt very high? Any experiences like hearing voices?","How much do you drink?"],"hear":"\"Awake at four. Lost half a stone. Off work two weeks. Never high. Glass of wine some nights.\"","mark":"Biological features; bipolar and psychosis screens negative"},{"h":"Self, future and suicide","say":["How do you see yourself at the moment? And the future?","Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or thoughts of ending it?","Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to prepare?"],"hear":"\"I'm a terrible mother. They'd be better off without me. I've had thoughts. No plans.\"","mark":"Guilt, hopelessness, suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Agree what can be shared","say":["Your partner would like a word. I'll listen to whatever he says. What would you be happy for me to share with him?","If I thought you were at serious risk, I might need to share that to keep you safe."],"hear":"\"You can tell him I'm depressed. Not everything.\""},{"h":"Collateral from Tom","say":["Mr Reid, thank you for waiting. What worries you most?","Have you noticed anything that looks like she's getting ready, like letters, giving things away or tablets?"],"hear":"(Tom) \"I found letters to the kids, and paracetamol in her bag.\"","mark":"Preparation identified from collateral"},{"h":"Go back to her, act and close","say":["Nisha, Tom is worried and found letters and paracetamol. Can we talk about that honestly?","I'm worried about your safety. I'd like you to be seen by the crisis team today, the tablets removed, and a written safety plan with you.","For the depression, you can choose talking therapy, medicine or both. We'll review in days, not weeks. Your children are 8 and 11; who's with them if things get worse?"],"hear":"\"I did write them. I'm not sure I would have used them.\"","mark":"Risk revised to high; same-day plan"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Core symptoms","Flat since summer; stopped running and swimming trips","Depressive episode"],["Biological symptoms","Early waking at 4 a.m., half a stone lost, off work","At least moderate severity"],["Thoughts about self and future","Terrible mother; family better off without her","Guilt and hopelessness drive risk"],["Suicide risk","Denied plans; partner found letters and paracetamol","Preparation present: high risk despite her denial"],["Collateral and confidentiality","Agreed he can know she is depressed","Listen fully; share only what she agreed, or more if serious risk"]]},"key":"Consent limits what you share, never what you hear. The excellent doctor takes the partner's account of letters and tablets back to her and revises the risk.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5],[5,7],[7]]},"c-2-2-2":{"name":"Psychotic depression: how ill, how urgent, what tonight","why":"Confirms severe depression with psychotic features, measures the physical danger of fluid refusal and agrees an emergency plan with the nurse in charge.","frame":["Depressive syndrome","Mood-congruent delusions","Hallucinations","Food and fluid refusal","Suicide risk","Other causes"],"steps":[{"h":"Slow, quiet start","say":["Mrs Doyle, I'm one of the ward doctors. I'll sit here with you. Take your time.","How have you been feeling in yourself?"],"hear":"(quietly) \"There's no point you sitting there.\""},{"h":"Depressive syndrome","say":["How long have you felt like this?","How are you sleeping? Is there anything you still get pleasure from?"],"hear":"\"Black inside since the summer. I wake at four and just lie there.\"","mark":"Severe depressive episode"},{"h":"Beliefs, without arguing","say":["You told the nurse your insides have rotted. Can you tell me about that?","Do you feel you've done something wrong, or deserve to be punished?"],"hear":"\"There's nothing left in there to feed. I'm wicked. I deserve it.\"","mark":"Nihilistic and guilt delusions identified"},{"h":"Voices","say":["Sometimes when people feel this low they hear voices. Has that happened?","What do they say? Do they tell you to do anything?"],"hear":"\"A woman at night. She says I'm dirty and don't deserve to drink.\"","mark":"Second-person derogatory voice linked to fluid refusal"},{"h":"Fluids and physical danger","say":["What have you had to drink today? When did you last pass water?","If you keep not drinking, where do you see that leading?"],"hear":"\"A sip with my tablet this morning. Not since then. Then it'll be over.\"","mark":"Fluid refusal with intent: physical emergency"},{"h":"Suicide, history and other causes","say":["Have you thought of ending your life? Have you done anything towards it?","Have you been like this before? Ever very high? Any alcohol or other medicines?"],"hear":"\"I thought about walking into the river before I came. One bad time in my forties. Never high. Just amlodipine.\"","mark":"High suicide risk; unipolar history"},{"h":"Tell her, then plan with the nurse","say":["Margaret, you're very unwell with depression, and it's making your mind tell you things that aren't true. It's treatable, and we're going to look after you tonight.","(To Sam) She needs NEWS2, a fluid chart, urgent U&E, bloods including phosphate, and a medical review tonight. Within eyesight observations.","I'll assess her capacity to refuse fluids and call the consultant now: antidepressant plus antipsychotic, an urgent ECT discussion, and an MHA assessment if she refuses care or tries to leave."],"hear":"(Sam) \"I'll start the chart and move her nearer the office.\"","mark":"Emergency plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Depressive syndrome","Black since summer, early waking, no pleasure","Severe depressive episode"],["Mood-congruent delusions","Insides rotted, wicked, deserves punishment","Psychotic depression: nihilistic and guilt delusions"],["Hallucinations","Woman's voice calls her dirty, says she doesn't deserve to drink","Voice directly drives the fluid refusal"],["Food and fluid refusal","One sip today, no urine since morning, sees it as a way out","Risk of AKI and death in days; treat as an emergency"],["Suicide risk","Thought of walking into the river","High risk; close observation"],["Other causes","One past depression, never high, amlodipine only, no alcohol","Unipolar likely; still check bloods for organic causes"]]},"key":"This is a medical emergency, not a ward-round item. The excellent doctor counts fluids and urine, involves the consultant tonight, and raises ECT early because it is fastest when life is at risk.","objmap":[[3],[7],[7],[7],[7]]},"c-2-2-3":{"name":"Why no tablets? Reasons, capacity and a safer plan","why":"Finds the real reason Gareth refuses sertraline, tests his capacity fairly, and agrees a plan that keeps him safe without threats.","frame":["Reasons for refusal","Understand and retain","Weigh and communicate","Suicide risk","Alternatives"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge his position","say":["Mr Lewis, I'm one of the ward doctors. I'm not here to force anything.","I'd like to understand why you've said no."],"hear":"\"If you're here about the tablets, I've already said no.\""},{"h":"Reasons for refusal","say":["What do you think the tablets would do to you?","Have you taken one before? What was it like?"],"hear":"\"Fluoxetine two years ago. Like being wrapped in cotton wool. I stopped it myself.\"","mark":"Emotional blunting from past SSRI identified"},{"h":"Understand and retain","say":["Can you tell me in your words why the team suggested sertraline?","What might happen if you don't take anything for the depression?"],"hear":"\"Because I'm depressed. It might stay like this, I know.\"","mark":"Understands and retains"},{"h":"Weigh and communicate","say":["How do you weigh the chance of feeling better against feeling flat again?","Does any part of you feel you don't deserve to get better, or that it can't work for you?"],"hear":"\"No, I just don't want to feel numb. I'm not bad, I just lost my job.\"","mark":"Weighs using his own experience: capacity present"},{"h":"Suicide risk","say":["How often do you think you'd be better off dead?","Any plans here on the ward? What keeps you going?","Have you thought about walking off the ward?"],"hear":"\"Most days. I thought about an overdose before I came in. My kids keep me going. I've thought of going for air, that's all.\"","mark":"Ongoing suicidal thoughts; protective factors"},{"h":"Real alternatives","say":["There are other antidepressants that work differently, and some people find they don't feel flattened on them. We could start low and review it with you.","We can also offer practical talking therapy, and help with work and money.","You're an informal patient, and you have the right to say no. I won't use the law as a threat."],"hear":"\"If it doesn't flatten me, I'd think about it. Can I go home this weekend if I take one?\""},{"h":"Honest answer and close","say":["Leave depends on how safe things are, not on a tablet. That's a decision with you and your consultant.","Can I ask you to tell a nurse if you feel like leaving or the thoughts get stronger?","I'll record your capacity, your choice and the plan, and the nurses will check on you more often."],"hear":"\"Fair enough. I'll talk to the consultant about a different one.\"","mark":"Plan agreed; observations reviewed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Reasons for refusal","Felt like cotton wool on fluoxetine; stopped it himself","Emotional blunting is a real, treatable concern"],["Understand and retain","Knows why it was offered and that he may stay unwell","Two capacity steps met"],["Weigh and communicate","Balances benefit against numbness; no guilt delusions","Has capacity; refusal is valid"],["Suicide risk","Daily thoughts, prior overdose idea, thoughts of leaving; children protective","Raised risk; set observations and triggers"],["Alternatives","Open to a drug that doesn't flatten him and practical therapy","Offer a different antidepressant and psychological support"]]},"key":"Disagreeing is not lacking capacity. The excellent doctor tests whether hopelessness distorts his weighing, respects a capacitous refusal, and never uses the Mental Health Act as a bargaining tool.","objmap":[[2],[4],[6],[5],[6]]},"c-2-2-4":{"name":"Not today: side effects, discontinuation and a safe way off paroxetine","why":"Takes her sexual side effects seriously, explains why stopping suddenly is risky, and agrees a written plan she can control.","frame":["Side effects","Discontinuation symptoms","Relapse risk","Options","Interactions"],"steps":[{"h":"Hear what she wants","say":["Ms Bennett, thanks for being straight with me. I'd like to help you get this right.","What's made you decide to stop now?"],"hear":"\"I'll be honest, I want to stop it. Today.\""},{"h":"Side effects, sensitively","say":["Some people get sexual side effects on this. Is that part of it?","How is it affecting you and your relationship?"],"hear":"\"Low desire and I can't reach orgasm. It's straining things with Dan.\"","mark":"Sexual dysfunction identified"},{"h":"Discontinuation symptoms","say":["What happened when you missed those two days?","Did it settle when you restarted?"],"hear":"\"Dizzy, tearful, electric shocks in my head. Gone within a day of restarting.\"","mark":"Discontinuation symptoms identified"},{"h":"Explain: not addiction, but it needs care","say":["That's discontinuation. Your brain had adjusted to the drug. It's not addiction: no craving, no needing more.","Paroxetine leaves the body fast, so it's one of the hardest to stop. Stopping today could bring those zaps back for weeks.","We reduce slowly, in steps you can pause if you need to."],"hear":"\"So I'm not addicted? That's a relief.\""},{"h":"Relapse risk and other medicines","say":["How long have you felt well? What are your early warning signs?","Any other tablets or remedies? Are you on contraception?"],"hear":"\"Well about five months. I was going to try St John's wort. I'm on the pill.\"","mark":"Under six months well; St John's wort risk"},{"h":"Options","say":["St John's wort can make the pill less reliable, and with paroxetine can cause serotonin toxicity, so please don't.","You've been well under six months, so we'd usually keep you on an antidepressant a bit longer. You could switch to one with fewer sexual side effects, like mirtazapine, though it can bring sleepiness and appetite gain.","Or we taper paroxetine slowly over weeks to months and stop, with a plan if mood dips."],"hear":"\"Will mirtazapine make me put on weight?\""},{"h":"Decide and put it in writing","say":["It can for some people; we'd weigh you and review. Which option feels right?","I'll write the dose steps down, send them to your GP this week, and we'll review in two to four weeks.","If the zaps or low mood come on strongly, go back to the last dose and call us."],"hear":"\"Let's try the switch. Slowly.\"","mark":"Written switch plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Side effects","Low desire, anorgasmia, strain with Dan","Valid reason to change; take it seriously"],["Discontinuation symptoms","Dizzy, tearful, 'electric shocks' after two missed days, settled on restart","Classic paroxetine discontinuation; abrupt stop not safe"],["Relapse risk","Well about five months; clear early warning signs","Short of six months in remission; relapse plan needed"],["Options","Wants off paroxetine; worried about weight","Cross-taper to an alternative or slow taper to stop"],["Interactions","Planned St John's wort; on the combined pill; occasional ibuprofen","Advise against St John's wort; note SSRI plus NSAID bleeding risk"]]},"key":"Discontinuation is not addiction, and it is not relapse. The excellent doctor explains the difference, finds the St John's wort plan, and gives a written taper she can slow herself.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5],[7],[7]]},"c-2-2-5":{"name":"Why hasn't it worked? Treatment-resistant depression and lithium","why":"Checks the past treatment was adequate, sets out the options, and explains lithium safely, including the medicines that interact.","frame":["Adequate trials","Diagnosis review","Options","Lithium monitoring","Interactions and toxicity"],"steps":[{"h":"His question first","say":["Mr Carter, your consultant mentioned lithium. Have you had a chance to look into it?","What did you find, and what worries you?"],"hear":"\"Isn't that for bipolar?\""},{"h":"Adequate trials","say":["Tell me about the sertraline: what dose, and for how long?","And the venlafaxine, did you manage to take it every day?"],"hear":"\"Sertraline 150 for ten weeks. Venlafaxine made me sick at first, so I missed doses, then 225 for three months.\"","mark":"Two adequate trials confirmed"},{"h":"Diagnosis review","say":["Have you ever had times of feeling high, or needing little sleep?","How much do you drink? Any stresses keeping it going?","Have you had thoughts of not wanting to wake up?"],"hear":"\"Never high. Four pints at weekends. Off work, my daughter's moved. Sometimes I'd rather not wake up, no plans.\"","mark":"Unipolar; stressors and passive death wish noted"},{"h":"Options","say":["You're not bipolar. Lithium is used here to boost an antidepressant, which is one of the best-studied options.","Others include adding an antipsychotic like aripiprazole or quetiapine, combining with mirtazapine, more therapy, or ECT if it became severe."],"hear":"\"So why lithium over the others?\""},{"h":"Lithium monitoring","say":["It works well, and lowers suicide risk, but the right level matters. Before starting we check kidneys, thyroid, calcium, weight and an ECG.","Then a blood level 12 hours after the dose, weekly until steady, then every three months; kidneys and thyroid every six months."],"hear":"\"That's a lot of blood tests.\"","mark":"Monitoring explained"},{"h":"Interactions and toxicity","say":["Ibuprofen and ramipril both push lithium levels up. We'd want you off regular ibuprofen and your GP involved about the ramipril.","Signs of too much: bad shaking, vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, confusion. If so, stop it and get help the same day.","If you get dehydrated, say with a stomach bug, pause it and call us."],"hear":"\"I need something for my back though.\"","mark":"Key interactions found"},{"h":"Shared decision and close","say":["Paracetamol or physio for your back is safer with lithium. If you'd rather avoid the extra tests, an antipsychotic add-on is a fair choice too.","Which feels right? I'll arrange baseline bloods and ECG, and a shared care letter to your GP."],"hear":"\"Let's try the lithium. I'll swap the ibuprofen.\"","mark":"Decision made; baseline tests booked"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Adequate trials","Sertraline 150 mg ten weeks; venlafaxine 225 mg three months after a poor start","Two adequate trials: treatment-resistant"],["Diagnosis review","Never high; four pints weekends; job and family losses","Unipolar; address alcohol and stressors too"],["Options","Thought lithium meant bipolar","Augmentation, combination, therapy or ECT, each explained"],["Lithium monitoring","Worried about blood tests","Baseline renal, thyroid, calcium, weight, ECG; 12-hour levels"],["Interactions and toxicity","Ramipril and daily ibuprofen","Both raise levels: stop NSAID, GP review, close monitoring"]]},"key":"Before calling it resistance, check dose, duration and adherence. The excellent doctor spots the ramipril and ibuprofen, which change whether lithium is safe for this man.","objmap":[[2,5],[3],[4],[5,6],[7]]},"c-2-2-6":{"name":"ECT or the magnet? An honest comparison she can consent to","why":"Starts from Eileen's fears, explains ECT and rTMS fairly, and checks she understands enough to make her own choice.","frame":["Her beliefs and fears","Why now","What ECT involves","Side effects","rTMS compared","Consent"],"steps":[{"h":"Her beliefs first","say":["Mrs Marsh, before I explain anything, what have you heard about ECT?","What worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Electric shocks. I've seen what that does in films.\"","mark":"Fears from films identified"},{"h":"Why now","say":["Two tablets haven't helped, and you've hardly eaten for two weeks. That's why the team suggests something faster.","Have you had thoughts of not wanting to wake up?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes. I wouldn't do anything. I'm too tired for all this.\"","mark":"Passive death wish; nursing staff to know"},{"h":"What ECT actually involves","say":["It's nothing like the films. You're fully asleep under anaesthetic, with a muscle relaxant, so your body barely moves.","A brief current triggers a short seizure in the brain, usually less than a minute. You wake up in recovery.","Usually twice a week, often 6 to 12 treatments, reviewed as we go."],"hear":"\"So I wouldn't feel it?\""},{"h":"Side effects honestly","say":["Afterwards some people have a headache, aching muscles or feel muddled for a few hours.","Memory gaps around the weeks of treatment are common. Most return, but some people lose some memories for good. I won't pretend otherwise.","We'll test your memory before and during, and can change the treatment if it's affected."],"hear":"\"I worry about forgetting my grandchildren's names.\"","mark":"Memory fear addressed honestly"},{"h":"rTMS compared fairly","say":["The magnet treatment, rTMS, is done awake, with a coil on your head, every weekday for four to six weeks.","It's gentle, but it works less well when depression is this severe and someone isn't eating, and it's hard to get on the NHS.","Another tablet is an option too, but it may take weeks."],"hear":"\"Wouldn't the magnet one be better?\""},{"h":"Fitness and practical checks","say":["You had an anaesthetic for your knee without trouble, which is reassuring.","We'd do a heart tracing and bloods, and review your night-time lorazepam, as it can make ECT less effective. Your denture comes out beforehand."],"hear":"\"Fine. What if I start and want to stop?\""},{"h":"Consent and teach-back","say":["You can stop at any time, before any treatment. Saying yes once doesn't commit you.","Could you tell me back what ECT involves and what the main risks are?","I'll give you a leaflet, and we'll meet again with Claire and visit the ECT suite."],"hear":"\"Asleep, twice a week, maybe memory problems. I can stop. I'd like Claire there.\"","mark":"Understanding confirmed; no decision rushed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her beliefs and fears","Film images of shocks; feels too old and tired","Correct the myth before giving facts"],["Why now","Two failed drugs, little food for two weeks, passive death wish","Severe depression with physical risk: ECT is the fastest option"],["Side effects","Fears losing grandchildren's names","Honest memory risk; baseline and repeat cognitive testing"],["rTMS compared","Thinks the magnet treatment sounds gentler","Less effective at this severity; limited NHS access"],["Consent","Wants to know she can stop; wants daughter present","Voluntary, can withdraw any time; second meeting with Claire"]]},"key":"Reassurance is not consent. The excellent doctor is honest that memory gaps can persist, is fair that rTMS is weaker at this severity, and checks understanding by teach-back.","objmap":[[1],[7],[5],[7],[7]]},"c-2-2-7":{"name":"Low after a heart attack: mood, causes, risk and advice to cardiology","why":"Separates depression from delirium and adjustment, finds the risk behind 'no point', and gives the cardiology team safe, specific treatment advice.","frame":["Mood and timing","Medical causes","Cardiac fear","Suicide risk","Treatment advice"],"steps":[{"h":"Accept his frame","say":["Mr Brennan, I'm from the mental health team. The cardiologists asked me to see how you're coping.","You're right, anyone would be fed up here. How has it been for you?"],"hear":"\"Another doctor. I'm not depressed.\""},{"h":"Mood and timing","say":["How was your mood before the heart attack?","Since then, do you still enjoy anything, even the football? How's your appetite?"],"hear":"\"Fine before. Low since a couple of days after. Not bothered about anything.\"","mark":"Low mood and anhedonia since the MI"},{"h":"Medical causes","say":["Have you felt muddled or confused at all, especially at night?","Can I ask a few quick memory questions?","How much did you drink and smoke before you came in?"],"hear":"\"No, my head's clear. About twenty units a week, twenty cigarettes a day.\"","mark":"No delirium on screen; alcohol and nicotine withdrawal to consider"},{"h":"Cardiac fear","say":["What worries you about cardiac rehab?","Is anything else worrying you, like money or home?"],"hear":"\"Exercise will set off another one. And I'm self-employed, I can't work.\"","mark":"Fear of exercise and money stress driving avoidance"},{"h":"Suicide risk","say":["You told a nurse there's no point anymore. What did you mean?","Have you thought of ending your life? Is there anything at home you could use?"],"hear":"\"Some nights I think it'd be easier not to wake up. No plans. There's a drawer of old co-codamol.\"","mark":"Passive death wish; access to opioids"},{"h":"Explain and agree with him","say":["Low mood after a heart attack is common, and it slows recovery. It's treatable.","Rehab is supervised and built around your heart. It also lowers the risk of another attack.","Would you let Linda clear out the co-codamol? And would you consider a tablet if this carries on?"],"hear":"\"Will antidepressants strain my heart?\" ... \"OK, if it's safe.\"","mark":"Agrees to remove means"},{"h":"Advise the cardiology team","say":["(To registrar) Depressive symptoms since the MI, passive death wish, no plan; co-codamol at home being removed.","Please check bloods including TFTs and B12, start nicotine replacement and watch for alcohol withdrawal. Refer to cardiac rehab with its psychology support.","If symptoms persist, sertraline 50 mg is the safest choice after MI. With his antiplatelets, add a PPI such as lansoprazole. Avoid tricyclics. I'll follow up and write to his GP."],"hear":"(Registrar) \"Fine, we'll add the PPI and refer to rehab.\"","mark":"Clear advice handed over"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood and timing","Low and not enjoying anything since two days after the MI","Depressive symptoms; recheck after two weeks for an episode"],["Medical causes","Clear head; 20 units a week; 20 cigarettes a day","Delirium unlikely; withdrawal and bloods to cover"],["Cardiac fear","Exercise will cause another attack; money worries","Explain rehab safety; psychology input within rehab"],["Suicide risk","Easier not to wake up; old co-codamol at home","Passive wish with access to means: remove and review"],["Treatment advice","Worried antidepressants will strain his heart","Sertraline with PPI cover; avoid tricyclics; follow-up planned"]]},"key":"Low mood after an MI is not 'understandable, so leave it'. The excellent doctor excludes delirium, finds the co-codamol, and gives cardiology a named drug with the bleeding risk covered.","objmap":[[3],[5],[5,6],[7],[7]]},"c-2-2-8":{"name":"Grief or depression? Telling them apart and planning for both","why":"Lets Dennis talk about Jean, separates normal grief from a depressive episode, assesses risk and agrees support with him and Sarah.","frame":["The loss","Grief features","Depressive features","Hearing Jean","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with Jean","say":["Mr Hughes, I'm so sorry about Jean.","Would you tell me a bit about her, and what happened?"],"hear":"\"She's gone. Forty-one years. Just collapsed at home.\"","mark":"Sudden, unexpected loss"},{"h":"Grief features","say":["How have you been coping since?","Does the sadness come in waves, with reminders, or is it there all the time?","Do you blame yourself for anything?"],"hear":"\"I cry every day. I should have noticed her headaches.\"","mark":"Guilt about the death"},{"h":"Depressive features","say":["Is the low feeling there all day, every day? Can you enjoy anything, even your grandchildren?","How are you sleeping and eating? How do you see yourself now?"],"hear":"\"All day. Nothing gives me pleasure. Awake at four. Lost a stone. I'm a burden to Sarah.\"","mark":"Depressive episode features identified"},{"h":"Hearing Jean","say":["You mentioned hearing Jean. When does that happen?","In that moment, do you think she's really there? Any other voices or strange experiences?"],"hear":"\"As I'm dropping off. I know she's not there. Nothing else.\"","mark":"Grief perception, insight kept; not psychosis"},{"h":"Risk and alcohol","say":["Do you ever feel you'd like to be with Jean? Have you thought of ending your life?","Have you done anything towards it? How much are you drinking?"],"hear":"\"I want to be with her. I've looked at her tablets. No plan. Three or four whiskies a night.\"","mark":"Suicidal wish, access to means, alcohol"},{"h":"Explain the difference","say":["Hearing Jean and crying are part of grief, and you're not going mad.","But low mood all day, waking early, losing weight and feeling a burden go beyond grief. I think you also have depression, and that is treatable.","Treating it doesn't mean forgetting Jean."],"hear":"\"I don't need pills. Grief is grief.\"","mark":"Depression alongside grief"},{"h":"Plan with him and Sarah","say":["We could start with talking therapy and bereavement support, and think about medication if things don't lift.","Can Sarah return Jean's tablets to the pharmacy? Could we cut the whisky down?","Sarah, the warning signs are talk of joining her mum, more drinking, or not eating. Here is who to call. I'll see you both again in two weeks."],"hear":"(Sarah) \"I'll take the tablets today.\"","mark":"Safety plan and follow-up agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The loss","Sudden death at home after 41 years","Sudden loss raises the risk of complicated grief"],["Grief features","Daily crying, guilt about her headaches","Expected in grief"],["Depressive features","Pervasive low mood, no pleasure, early waking, weight loss, feels a burden","Meets a depressive episode alongside grief"],["Hearing Jean","Only as he falls asleep, knows she is not there","Normal bereavement experience, not psychosis"],["Risk","Wish to join Jean, looked at her tablets, heavy whisky","Raised risk: remove means, reduce alcohol, close follow-up"]]},"key":"Grief and depression can coexist. The excellent doctor uses self-worth and constant low mood to tell them apart, keeps the hearing of Jean out of the psychosis box, and acts on the tablets.","objmap":[[1,7],[2,3],[4],[5],[7]]},"c-2-2-9":{"name":"Daniel's safety plan: from his story to a written plan he owns","why":"Turns an honest conversation about suicidal thinking into a shared formulation and a written plan matched to what Daniel needs tonight, not to a score.","frame":["Suicidal thinking, intent and plan","Means and preparation","Drivers: loss, shame, alcohol, debt","What keeps him going","Safety plan steps","Follow-up and who knows"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the room and the rules","say":["Hi Daniel, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Your GP was worried about you and asked us to see you today.","What you tell me stays within the team looking after you, unless I think you or someone else is in serious danger.","Your sister is outside. Shall we talk on our own first, and you decide later what she hears?"],"hear":"\"I suppose. I don't really know why I'm here. The GP made a big deal of it.\""},{"h":"Let him tell the story","say":["Talk me through the last couple of weeks, in your words.","Losing your job and Leah moving out, so close together. How have you been coping with that?","How are you sleeping and eating at the moment?"],"hear":"\"Eight years in that warehouse, gone. Then Leah left. I'm up till four most nights.\"","mark":"Two acute losses and insomnia identified"},{"h":"Ask about suicidal thoughts in steps","say":["When things are this bad, some people feel life isn't worth carrying on with. Has that been true for you?","Have you had thoughts about ending your life? How often do they come, and how long do they last?","Is there a time they're worst, say after a drink or late at night?"],"hear":"\"Most nights, for an hour or two. Worse after a few cans.\"","mark":"Frequent suicidal thoughts, worse with alcohol"},{"h":"Plans, preparation, past acts and means","say":["Have you thought about how you might do it, or done anything to get ready?","Have you ever acted on thoughts like this before? What happened afterwards?","Is there anything at home, such as tablets, that worries you?"],"hear":"\"I looked up paracetamol doses. There are three boxes at home. I took an overdose at nineteen.\"","mark":"Method researched, means at home, previous overdose identified"},{"h":"Drivers, protective factors and a shared formulation","say":["How much are you drinking each night? Any money or legal worries piling up?","What has kept you going so far, even on the worst nights?","So the job, Leah, the rent and the drinking all pile on at night, and Alfie, Claire and the dog pull you back. Does that fit?"],"hear":"\"Six to eight cans. I'm behind on rent. Alfie's the reason I'm still here, really.\"","mark":"Formulation agreed: loss and shame, alcohol and debt as amplifiers, nephew and sister as anchors"},{"h":"Write the safety plan together","say":["Let's write a plan for the worst hours. What tells you a bad night is starting?","What can you do on your own to get through the first hour, and who could you text or ring?","If that isn't enough: Samaritans on 116 123 any time, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or 999 if you're about to act.","Would you let Claire take the paracetamol away tonight, and could you cut the cans down while things are this raw?"],"hear":"\"I could walk the dog, text Kyle. Claire can have the tablets, I suppose.\"","mark":"Written safety plan agreed; means removal accepted"},{"h":"Agree who knows, follow-up and close","say":["What would you like Claire to know? We can keep it to warning signs and who to call.","I'd like the home treatment team to visit you within 24 hours, and I'll write to your GP today about the drinking and debt advice too.","Here's your copy of the plan. Tell me back what you'll do if tonight gets bad."],"hear":"\"OK. Just the plan, not the details. I'll keep it on my phone.\"","mark":"Consent to share agreed; HTT follow-up within 24 hours"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Suicidal thinking","Most nights, an hour or two, worse after drinking","Persistent, active thoughts that peak when he is disinhibited"],["Plan and means","Researched paracetamol doses; three boxes at home","Preparation plus access: remove the means tonight"],["History","Overdose at nineteen after a breakup, no follow-up","Same trigger pattern as now; past acts matter more than any score"],["Drivers","Job loss, Leah gone, rent arrears, six to eight cans","Shame, debt and alcohol each need a named action"],["Anchors","Alfie, Claire, the dog, Kyle","Real but can shift; build them into the plan rather than relying on them"]]},"key":"Do not grade him low, medium or high. Let the formulation decide the response: researched method, means at home and nightly drinking call for means removal and home treatment within 24 hours.","objmap":[[4],[5,6],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-2-2-10":{"name":"Chloe after her overdose: medically fit first, then intent, risk and a plan","why":"Makes sure Chloe is safe to talk to and treat, measures how serious the act was, uncovers the risk from Jake, and agrees where she goes next.","frame":["Medical fitness and capacity","Circumstances and intent of the act","Current mental state and suicidal thoughts","Past self-harm and background","Risk from others","Plan and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Check the medical picture before anything else","say":["Before I come in, can I see the drug chart and bloods? What time was the paracetamol level taken, and how long is left on the infusion?","Hi Chloe, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. I know you want to go home. Can we talk while the drip finishes?","The drip protects your liver. Stopping it early could cause real damage, even though you feel fine now."],"hear":"\"I feel fine. I just want to go home.\"","mark":"Needs full acetylcysteine course and repeat bloods before medically fit"},{"h":"Capacity if she still wants to leave","say":["Can you tell me in your words what the drip is for and what might happen if you stop it?","What makes you want to leave right now?"],"hear":"\"It's for the paracetamol. I just hate it here, and I don't want Jake turning up.\"","mark":"Understands and weighs the risk; fear of Jake is driving the wish to leave"},{"h":"Walk through the overdose and its intent","say":["Take me through the evening, from the argument to the ambulance.","What did you take, and what did you think it would do?","Had you planned it at all, written anything, or tried to stop anyone finding you?","How do you feel now about still being here?"],"hear":"\"Twenty-four paracetamol and some sertraline. I locked the bathroom, then panicked and rang Megan. I'm glad I'm here.\"","mark":"Impulsive act, no planning or note, help sought, relief at survival"},{"h":"Mood now, past self-harm and drink","say":["How's your mood been since the summer, before last night?","Have you hurt yourself before, in any way?","Any thoughts now of ending your life, or of doing this again?","How much are you drinking, and do you use anything else?"],"hear":"\"Low since the summer. I cut my arms at sixteen. Wine at weekends, a bit of weed. No, I don't want to die now.\"","mark":"Ongoing low mood, past self-harm, no current suicidal intent"},{"h":"Ask safely about Jake","say":["You said you didn't want Jake here. Can I ask how things are between you?","Has he ever hurt you, controlled your money or phone, or put his hands round your neck?","Do you feel safe going back to the flat?"],"hear":"\"He checks my phone, keeps my bank card. He pushed me. Once he grabbed my neck.\"","mark":"Domestic abuse with non-fatal strangulation identified: high-risk marker"},{"h":"Agree a plan with her","say":["Thank you for telling me. What he did to your neck is serious and against the law, and it isn't your fault.","Once the drip finishes and your bloods are clear, could you stay at your mum's rather than the flat?","I'd like to refer you to a domestic abuse adviser. With your agreement I'll share this so they can help keep you safe."],"hear":"\"I can go to Mum's. Please don't tell Jake I'm here.\"","mark":"Discharge to mum's address when medically fit; IDVA referral agreed"},{"h":"Follow-up and close","say":["We won't tell Jake anything. The ward staff will know not to let him in.","The crisis team will contact you at your mum's within a day or two, and I'll write to your GP about your mood and the sertraline.","If the thoughts come back, ring Samaritans on 116 123 or NHS 111 and choose the mental health option."],"hear":"\"OK. Thank you for not making me feel stupid.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Medical fitness","Mid-infusion, feels fine, wants to leave","Not yet fit; finish the course and recheck bloods, assess capacity if she tries to go"],["Intent","Impulsive after a row, locked door, then rang Megan","Mixed intent at the time; low current intent and relief now"],["Mood and past acts","Low since summer, cut at sixteen, on sertraline","Underlying depression and past self-harm raise future risk"],["Risk from others","Controlling, pushed, grabbed by the neck","High-risk domestic abuse; safety planning and IDVA, consider MARAC"],["Supports","Mum's house, friend Megan","A safer address makes discharge with follow-up reasonable"]]},"key":"The overdose is not the whole risk. Strangulation by her partner is one of the strongest warning signs of later homicide, so where she sleeps tonight matters as much as her suicidal intent.","objmap":[[1],[3],[3,4],[5],[6]]},"c-2-3-1":{"name":"Kayleigh's mental state on arrival: mania, item by item","why":"Gives a complete, structured mental state of suspected mania in a few minutes, and turns it into immediate risks and investigations for the nurse in charge.","frame":["Appearance, behaviour and speech","Mood and affect","Thought form and content","Perception","Cognition and insight","Risk and investigations"],"steps":[{"h":"Safe start and introduction","say":["Check with the nurse: any weapons, intoxication or aggression on the way in? Sit near the door with a colleague nearby.","Hi Kayleigh, I'm one of the doctors on the ward. I want to understand what's been going on so we can look after you.","I'll keep it short. Can we sit down for ten minutes?"],"hear":"\"Ten minutes? I've got the whole NHS to fix by Christmas, darling!\"","mark":"Overfamiliar, distractible, pressured speech noted"},{"h":"Mood, sleep and energy","say":["How would you describe your mood right now?","How much have you slept this week?","Have there been any moments when you've felt low or tearful in between?"],"hear":"\"Electric! Best I've ever felt. Two hours' sleep all week. I cried for an hour on Tuesday, but that's gone.\"","mark":"Elevated mood, reduced need for sleep, possible mixed features"},{"h":"Thought form and content","say":["You mentioned fixing the NHS. Tell me more about that.","Have you noticed signs or messages meant for you, in numbers, on TV or the radio?","Do you have a special purpose or special powers that other people don't?"],"hear":"\"I'm the healer. Bus numbers are a code, the universe is sending them, plane, rain, Spain!\"","mark":"Flight of ideas with clang associations; grandiose and referential delusions"},{"h":"Perception","say":["Do you ever hear voices when no one is around?","Where do they come from, and what do they say?"],"hear":"\"God talks to me through the radio. He calls me the healer.\"","mark":"Mood-congruent auditory hallucinations: mania with psychotic features"},{"h":"Risk, asked directly","say":["Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or felt you could do something dangerous, like fly?","Has anyone upset you enough that you've lashed out?","How much money have you spent recently? Any alcohol or drugs?","When was your last period, and could you be pregnant?"],"hear":"\"I thought I could jump off a bridge and fly, not now. I shoved a bloke. Four grand on paints and New York. Period's about six weeks late.\"","mark":"Risk by misadventure, aggression, financial, and possible pregnancy identified"},{"h":"Cognition and insight","say":["Can you tell me where you are, and today's date?","Do you think anything about you has changed, or that you might be unwell?"],"hear":"\"Some hospital. Thursday? I'm not ill, I'm the best I've ever been.\"","mark":"Broadly oriented, distractible; no insight"},{"h":"Present to the nurse in charge","say":["Kayleigh is a 29-year-old woman on Section 2 with elevated and at times tearful mood, pressured speech, flight of ideas, grandiose and referential delusions and second-person voices from God.","Risks: misadventure, she spoke of flying off a bridge; aggression, she pushed a man; financial; vulnerability; possible pregnancy. She has no insight.","Plan: increased observations, pregnancy test before any medication, urine drug screen, bloods including FBC, U&Es, LFTs, thyroid, glucose and calcium, an ECG, and collateral from her partner."],"hear":"Nurse: \"Agreed. I'll put her on enhanced obs and get a urine sample.\"","mark":"Structured presentation with risks and investigations"}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Speech and thought form","Fast, rhyming, jumping topics","Pressure of speech, flight of ideas, clang associations"],["Mood","Electric, plus an hour of crying on Tuesday","Elevated mood with possible mixed features; watch suicide risk"],["Thought content and perception","Chosen healer, bus-number code, God on the radio","Grandiose and referential delusions, mood-congruent voices: psychotic mania"],["Insight","'I'm not ill'","Absent insight; supports detention and affects consent to treatment"],["Risk","Bridge, shove, £4,000, late period","Misadventure, aggression, finance, and pregnancy test before any prescribing"]]},"key":"A good mental state lists signs; an excellent one ends with action: the late period means a pregnancy test before any mood stabiliser or antipsychotic, and the 'flying' thought sets her observation level.","objmap":[[1],[2],[7],[3,5],[7]]},"c-2-3-2":{"name":"Mark at the door: can he leave, and what about the car and the money?","why":"Decides quickly and lawfully whether an informal patient with hypomania can leave, then protects his driving licence and his savings.","frame":["Current mental state and risk","Capacity and willingness to stay","Holding power (section 5(2))","Driving and DVLA","Financial protection"],"steps":[{"h":"Meet him calmly at the door","say":["Mark, I'm one of the ward doctors. Can we sit in the side room for five minutes before you go anywhere?","You're right that you're here voluntarily. I just need to understand what's happening today."],"hear":"\"You can't stop me, I'm voluntary. I've got a deal to close today.\"","mark":"Wants to leave; agrees to a brief talk"},{"h":"Assess the plan and his mental state","say":["Tell me about the car and the business.","How much is it costing, and where is the money coming from?","How much have you slept since you came in?"],"hear":"\"Forty-two grand: savings and a loan the bank approved on my phone this morning. Slept maybe an hour a night.\"","mark":"Grandiose plan, reckless spending, severe sleep loss: hypomania not settling"},{"h":"Driving, alcohol and capacity for the decision","say":["How would you get there? Have you been driving?","Any drink today?","What could go wrong if you buy the car today, and could it wait a week?"],"hear":"\"I'll drive. Nothing's going wrong. If I wait, I lose the deal.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh the risks; intends to drive while unwell"},{"h":"Try to keep him informally first","say":["I'm worried that you're not well enough to make a £42,000 decision or to drive today.","Would you stay on the ward and let your brother Steve look at the deal with you tomorrow?","Would you leave your keys with the nurses for now?"],"hear":"\"No. I'm going now.\"","mark":"Informal options refused"},{"h":"Use the holding power and explain it","say":["Because you're unwell and at risk, I'm using a legal power to keep you on the ward for up to 72 hours while you're properly assessed under the Mental Health Act.","That means a fuller assessment, possibly under a section. You can ask for an advocate, and the nurses will give you your rights in writing.","I'll call your consultant now and arrange that assessment as soon as possible."],"hear":"\"This is outrageous. So I'm locked in?\"","mark":"Section 5(2) completed by doctor in charge or nominated deputy; MHA assessment arranged"},{"h":"Driving and the DVLA","say":["While you're this high, the law says you mustn't drive.","With bipolar disorder you need to tell the DVLA yourself. If you stay well and take treatment, licences are usually given back after a period of stability.","Can Steve hold your car keys?"],"hear":"\"Will the DVLA take my licence forever?\"","mark":"Advised not to drive; DVLA duty explained"},{"h":"Protect his money and close","say":["With your agreement, let's ring the bank: you usually have 14 days to withdraw from a new loan.","Could Steve help you look at your finances, as he usually does?","I'll document all this and tell the nurse in charge. Let's talk again after the assessment."],"hear":"\"Fine. Ring Steve. But I'm telling you, it's a good deal.\"","mark":"Financial safeguards agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mental state","An hour's sleep a night, 'millionaire' business plan","Hypomania worsening on the ward"],["Risk","£42,000 loan today, plans to drive","Serious financial and road risk if he leaves now"],["Capacity and choice","Won't wait, won't hand over keys","Informal admission has failed; he cannot safely weigh the decision"],["Law","Insists he is voluntary","Section 5(2): up to 72 hours for MHA assessment"],["Driving and money","Never told DVLA; brother helps with money","Stop driving, notify DVLA, withdraw the loan, involve Steve"]]},"key":"Section 5(2) is a holding power, not treatment. It buys time for a proper MHA assessment; while it runs, he cannot be given medication against his will under it.","objmap":[[4],[5],[5],[6],[2]]},"c-2-3-3":{"name":"Treating Priya's mania: what, why and how long, explained to Raj","why":"Gives Priya and Raj a clear, evidence-based plan for acute mania, with a realistic timeline and honest answers about valproate, weight and agitation.","frame":["Stop the antidepressant","Antipsychotic first-line","Long-term mood stabiliser","Valproate and contraception","Agitation on the ward","Family and children"],"steps":[{"h":"Introductions and agreement","say":["Mrs Shah, Mr Shah, I'm one of the doctors on the team. Priya, are you happy for us to talk about your treatment with Raj here?","Raj, what do you already know, and what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"She hasn't slept for days. The kids keep asking where Mum is. How long will this take?\"","mark":"Consent to discuss with husband; main concerns: children and timeline"},{"h":"Name the problem","say":["Priya has a manic episode of bipolar disorder. Her mood and energy are far too high, and that's why she can't sleep.","Sertraline can push mood higher in bipolar disorder, so we'll stop it."],"hear":"\"She's been on it for months from the GP.\"","mark":"Antidepressant stopped"},{"h":"First-line treatment and timeline","say":["We'll offer an antipsychotic tablet, such as olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone or haloperidol, to calm the mania.","Sleep usually improves within days; most people are much better over two to four weeks, though some take longer.","Section 2 can last up to 28 days. We'll review her every week and plan home leave as she improves."],"hear":"\"So weeks, not days?\"","mark":"Realistic timeline given"},{"h":"Weight gain","say":["You mentioned weight gain. Olanzapine causes the most, so we'd lean towards one with less effect on weight.","We'll check her weight, blood sugar and cholesterol at the start and regularly after that."],"hear":"\"She'd hate putting on weight. Thank you.\"","mark":"Side effects matched to her priorities"},{"h":"Valproate and long-term protection","say":["Valproate isn't recommended for women who could become pregnant, because it can seriously harm an unborn baby. Even with her coil, we'd use it only if nothing else works and under strict rules.","For long-term protection, lithium works best at preventing future episodes. We'll talk it through with Priya once she's calmer."],"hear":"\"Her cousin takes valproate, so I thought it might be the one.\"","mark":"Valproate risk explained; lithium flagged for maintenance"},{"h":"Agitation on the ward","say":["If she becomes very distressed, staff will try talking and a quiet space first.","If that's not enough, we'd offer a tablet to calm her. An injection is a last resort, and we'd check her physical health afterwards."],"hear":"\"I was scared they'd just hold her down.\"","mark":"De-escalation plan explained"},{"h":"Support the family and close","say":["How are you managing with the children and work?","You're able to ask for a carer's assessment, and the ward can arrange visits with the children when Priya is ready.","I'll write this plan down for you both. Any questions?"],"hear":"\"My mum's helping. It's good to know there's a plan.\"","mark":"Carer support offered; written plan"}],"table":{"cols":["Treatment step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antidepressant","On sertraline, no mood stabiliser","Stop sertraline; it may be fuelling the mania"],["Acute treatment","Raj wants to know how long","Antipsychotic first; improvement over weeks"],["Side effects","Priya hates weight gain","Choose an antipsychotic with less weight effect; monitor physical health"],["Valproate","Cousin takes it; Priya has a coil","Avoid in women who could become pregnant; lithium preferred long term"],["Family","Children 4 and 7, Raj exhausted","Carer's assessment, child-friendly visits, written plan"]]},"key":"The acute plan and the long-term plan are different. An antipsychotic settles the mania now; lithium prevents relapse later, and valproate stays off the table unless all else fails.","objmap":[[1],[4],[5,6],[3,6],[1,3]]},"c-2-3-4":{"name":"Steve's bipolar depression: check lithium first, then choose safely","why":"Finds out why Steve has relapsed on lithium, manages his risk, and agrees a treatment that will not tip him into mania.","frame":["Depressive symptoms","Exclude mixed or manic features","Risk and means","Lithium adherence, level and thyroid","Treatment choice","Driving and work"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with both of them","say":["Steve, Helen, thank you for coming. Steve, tell me how the last six weeks have been.","Is it all right if Helen adds anything she's noticed?"],"hear":"\"I'm just flat. Off work. Sleeping twelve hours and still knackered.\"","mark":"Depressive episode in bipolar I"},{"h":"Depressive symptoms in detail","say":["What do you still enjoy? How's your appetite and concentration?","Any spells of racing thoughts, irritability or feeling too high, even briefly?"],"hear":"\"Nothing. Not even the allotment. Eating biscuits. Can't follow the telly. No highs.\"","mark":"Anhedonia, hypersomnia, hyperphagia; no mixed features"},{"h":"Risk and access to lithium","say":["Some mornings, do you wish you hadn't woken up?","Have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans?","Where is your lithium kept, and is there a spare supply?"],"hear":"\"Some mornings, yes. No plans. There's a spare box in the kitchen.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts; lithium stock is a risk"},{"h":"Why now? Adherence, level and thyroid","say":["How often have you been missing doses lately?","When was your last lithium level, and have you felt cold or put on weight?"],"hear":"\"About three nights a week. The last blood test was eight months ago. I'm always cold.\"","mark":"Poor adherence, overdue level, possible hypothyroidism"},{"h":"Explain and agree treatment","say":["This is a depression within bipolar disorder. An antidepressant on its own can trigger a high, so I wouldn't start citalopram alone.","First, bloods today: lithium level, kidneys, calcium and thyroid. If your thyroid is low, treating it can lift your mood.","If you're still low once your lithium is right, we can add quetiapine or lamotrigine. Talking therapy can help too."],"hear":"\"So no citalopram?\"","mark":"Antidepressant monotherapy avoided; options explained"},{"h":"Safety: tablets, driving and work","say":["Helen, could you keep the spare lithium and give Steve a week's supply at a time?","While you're this low and struggling to concentrate, please don't drive Sophie to college. You should already have told the DVLA about your bipolar disorder.","I'll give you a fit note for work for now."],"hear":"\"I didn't realise about the driving.\"","mark":"Means restriction and DVLA advice agreed"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["I'll ring you with the blood results this week, and the care coordinator will see you weekly for now.","If the thoughts get stronger, call the crisis line or 111 and choose the mental health option. Tell me what you'll do if things get worse."],"hear":"\"Ring the crisis line. Helen will hold the tablets.\"","mark":"Written plan, follow-up and crisis contacts"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms","Anhedonia, 12 hours' sleep, biscuits, poor focus","Bipolar depression with atypical features"],["Mixed or manic features","No racing thoughts or highs","Safe to treat as depression, but no antidepressant alone"],["Risk","Wishes he hadn't woken; spare lithium at home","Passive thoughts with a lethal means; Helen holds supplies"],["Lithium","Missing three nights a week; level eight months ago","Likely subtherapeutic; check level and improve adherence first"],["Thyroid","Always cold","Possible lithium-related hypothyroidism adding to depression"]]},"key":"Before adding anything, fix what is there: a missed dose pattern, an overdue lithium level and a possible underactive thyroid may explain the relapse.","objmap":[[3,6],[2,4],[5],[5],[6]]},"c-2-3-5":{"name":"Starting Laura on lithium: why the bloods, and how to stay safe","why":"Helps Laura and Anne understand lithium, its monitoring and toxicity warning signs, so she starts it safely and stays on it.","frame":["Why lithium","Baseline tests and levels","Side effects and long-term risks","Toxicity warning signs","Interactions and dehydration","Pregnancy and stopping"],"steps":[{"h":"Start from her questions","say":["Laura, you asked why you need so many blood tests and whether lithium is dangerous. What have you heard so far?","Anne, please ask anything too."],"hear":"\"My friend said it's poison and wrecks your kidneys.\"","mark":"Main fears: kidneys and toxicity"},{"h":"Why lithium","say":["Lithium is the most effective long-term treatment for preventing further manic and depressive episodes, and it lowers the risk of suicide.","It's like a seatbelt: it works best when taken every day, even when you feel well."],"hear":"\"I never want to come back in here again.\"","mark":"Motivation linked to treatment goal"},{"h":"Blood tests and levels","say":["Before we start, we check your kidneys, thyroid, calcium, weight and blood count, and do a heart tracing if needed.","Your level is checked 12 hours after the evening dose, every week until it's steady, then three-monthly for the first year, then usually every six months.","The safe and effective range is narrow. That's why we check so often."],"hear":"\"So it's about getting the dose right?\"","mark":"Monitoring plan explained"},{"h":"Side effects and kidneys","say":["Early on, some people feel thirsty, pass more urine or notice a slight tremor.","Over years, lithium can affect the kidneys or thyroid in some people. Regular blood tests pick this up early, and we can change treatment if needed."],"hear":"\"So my kidneys won't just fail?\"","mark":"Risks put in proportion"},{"h":"Toxicity warning signs","say":["If you have sickness, diarrhoea, a shaky hand that gets worse, unsteadiness, slurred speech or confusion, stop taking the lithium and get medical help straight away.","Anne, if you notice her becoming confused or drowsy, call 111 or 999."],"hear":"Anne: \"What if she just has a stomach bug?\"","mark":"Toxicity signs and action agreed"},{"h":"Interactions, heat and exercise","say":["Ibuprofen and similar painkillers can raise your lithium level. For period pain, paracetamol is safer.","In hot weather, at the gym or when you're ill, drink plenty of fluids. If you can't keep fluids down, seek advice the same day."],"hear":"\"I take ibuprofen every month. Good to know.\"","mark":"NSAID interaction and dehydration advice"},{"h":"Pregnancy, stopping and close","say":["Your implant is reliable. If you ever plan a pregnancy, talk to us first rather than stopping suddenly.","Stopping suddenly can bring on mania, so always talk to us first.","We'll give you the lithium booklet, record card and written plan. What would you do if you had vomiting and a bad tremor?"],"hear":"\"Stop it and get help. And no ibuprofen.\"","mark":"Teach-back confirmed"}],"table":{"cols":["Topic","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Purpose","Never wants to be admitted again","Lithium is the best long-term preventive treatment"],["Monitoring","Why so many blood tests?","Narrow range: weekly levels, then three-monthly, then six-monthly"],["Long-term risk","Friend says it wrecks kidneys","Kidney and thyroid effects in some, caught early by monitoring"],["Interactions","Ibuprofen for period pain","NSAIDs raise levels; use paracetamol"],["Pregnancy","Implant; no pregnancy plans yet","Plan any pregnancy with the team; do not stop suddenly"]]},"key":"Make the risk practical. Laura needs to know what toxicity looks like, what to do, and that ibuprofen and dehydration can tip her over.","objmap":[[3],[5],[1,3],[2,3],[7]]},"c-2-4-1":{"name":"Leah in A&E: panic, agoraphobia and a plan she can follow","why":"Confirms panic disorder with agoraphobia, rules out what needs ruling out, explains the panic cycle, and sends Leah home with a route to CBT.","frame":["The attack","Unexpected attacks and fear of the next","Avoidance and safety behaviours","Physical mimics and substances","Mood and risk","Panic cycle and plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle her and set the aim","say":["Hi Leah, I'm one of the liaison psychiatry doctors. I look at how stress and the body affect each other.","Your heart tests tonight are normal. I'd like to understand the attacks properly and make a plan before you go home."],"hear":"\"I'm sure it's my heart. I'm probably wasting everyone's time.\"","mark":"Fear of heart disease; embarrassment"},{"h":"The last attack, from the first sign","say":["Take me through tonight's attack from the very first sign.","How quickly did it build, and how long until it eased?","What went through your mind at the worst point?"],"hear":"\"I was in bed. My heart pounded, my chest went tight, my hands tingled. It peaked in five minutes. I thought I was dying.\"","mark":"Panic attack identified"},{"h":"Unexpected attacks and anticipatory worry","say":["When was the first one, and where were you?","Do they come out of the blue, or only in certain places?","Between attacks, how much do you worry about the next one?"],"hear":"\"The first was in the supermarket, out of nowhere. Now I check my pulse and worry all day.\"","mark":"Panic disorder identified: unexpected attacks plus persistent worry"},{"h":"Avoidance and safety behaviours","say":["Which places do you now avoid?","Do you feel safer if someone is with you?"],"hear":"\"I can't do the supermarket or the bus alone. Sam does the school run. I feel like a useless mum.\"","mark":"Agoraphobia identified; safety behaviours: pulse-checking, company"},{"h":"Physical mimics, caffeine and alcohol","say":["Any weight loss, shakiness, heat intolerance, or a racing heart when you're calm or exercising?","How much coffee and alcohol do you have in a day?"],"hear":"\"No. Three or four coffees, and half a bottle of wine most nights to settle me.\"","mark":"No thyroid or cardiac pointers; caffeine and nightly alcohol may be maintaining attacks"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How's your mood been over these six weeks?","Have you ever felt so low you thought of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Fed up sometimes, but no, never that.\"","mark":"No suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Explain the cycle and agree a plan","say":["This is panic disorder. A small body change, like a fast heartbeat, gets read as danger, adrenaline surges, and the symptoms grow. It's frightening but not dangerous.","Checking your pulse and avoiding places keep the fear going. CBT for panic teaches you to break that cycle. You can self-refer to NHS Talking Therapies tomorrow.","I'd avoid tablets to calm you down, because they can become addictive. If you need medicine later, your GP can offer an SSRI.","Try cutting back the coffee and wine, as both can trigger attacks. I'll write to your GP today. What will you do if an attack starts tonight?"],"hear":"\"Breathe slowly and remind myself it's panic, not my heart.\"","mark":"Plan: Talking Therapies self-referral, GP letter, alcohol and caffeine advice"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Panic attacks","Peaks in five minutes, fear of dying","Typical panic attack"],["Panic disorder","First attack out of nowhere, worries all day","Recurrent unexpected attacks plus anticipatory anxiety"],["Agoraphobia","Can't do supermarket or bus alone; Sam does school run","Avoidance of places where escape or help seems hard"],["Physical and substances","Normal tests; coffee; half a bottle of wine nightly","No medical cause found; caffeine and alcohol rebound may fuel attacks"],["Maintaining factors","Pulse-checking, A&E visits, company","Safety behaviours keep the cycle going; CBT targets them"]]},"key":"Reassurance alone fuels repeat visits. Give Leah a model of what is happening, a named self-referral route and a plan for the next attack.","objmap":[[2],[5],[3,6],[7],[7]]},"c-2-4-2":{"name":"Ryan: GAD or social anxiety? Sort the worry, then the plan","why":"Separates social anxiety disorder from generalised anxiety, explains why sertraline has not yet worked, and agrees an evidence-based plan with Ryan himself.","frame":["Focus of the worry","Physical symptoms in social situations","Avoidance and safety behaviours","Onset and course","Alcohol, mood and risk","Treatment adequacy and plan"],"steps":[{"h":"See Ryan on his own","say":["Ryan, I'm one of the doctors in the team. Mrs Clarke, thank you for coming. I usually start by talking to the patient on their own. Would that be OK?","Ryan, we can invite your mum back in at the end if you'd like."],"hear":"Mrs Clarke: \"Of course.\" Ryan: \"OK.\"","mark":"Seen alone; autonomy respected"},{"h":"What the worry is about","say":["When you feel anxious, what are you worried about?","Is it lots of everyday things, or mostly about what people think of you?"],"hear":"\"It's people judging me. My face goes red and my mind goes blank.\"","mark":"Fear of negative evaluation: social anxiety disorder, not GAD"},{"h":"Avoidance and safety behaviours","say":["Which situations do you avoid?","When you can't avoid them, what do you do to get through?"],"hear":"\"I skip seminars. I rehearse what to say, sit at the back and avoid eye contact.\"","mark":"Avoidance and safety behaviours identified"},{"h":"When it started","say":["When did you first notice this?","Has it changed since you started university?"],"hear":"\"Around year eight. It's got worse at uni.\"","mark":"Adolescent onset with chronic course"},{"h":"Alcohol, mood and risk","say":["Do you ever drink to cope before going out?","How's your mood lately? Ever feel it would be easier not to be here?"],"hear":"\"Three or four cans before I go out, twice a week. Sometimes I think everyone would be better off without me, but no plans.\"","mark":"Alcohol as a safety behaviour; passive suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Explain and correct the treatment","say":["This sounds like social anxiety disorder rather than generalised anxiety. It's common and treatable.","Five weeks of sertraline 50 mg isn't a full trial. The dose can be increased, and it often takes longer to work.","The best treatment is CBT for social anxiety, usually around 14 weekly sessions. It tackles the safety behaviours step by step, at your pace."],"hear":"\"Will I have to give a speech?\"","mark":"Diagnosis explained; CBT first-line"},{"h":"His questions and close","say":["No one will throw you in at the deep end. You'll build up step by step.","Pregabalin isn't recommended for social anxiety and can be habit-forming.","Let's ask the university for support with your course. If those thoughts get worse, contact your GP or call 111 and choose the mental health option. Shall we bring your mum back in?"],"hear":"\"Yeah. That makes more sense than what I thought.\"","mark":"Shared plan; risk safety-netted"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Focus of worry","People judging me","Social anxiety disorder, not GAD"],["Physical symptoms","Blushing, mind going blank","Typical social anxiety"],["Safety behaviours","Rehearsing, sitting at the back, drinking before going out","Maintain anxiety; targets for CBT"],["Treatment so far","Sertraline 50 mg for five weeks","Not yet an adequate trial"],["Risk","Passive thoughts, alcohol","Needs a safety net and monitoring"]]},"key":"Listen for what the worry is about. Fear of being judged points to social anxiety, which needs specific CBT, not just more medicine.","objmap":[[5],[2],[3,5],[6],[7]]},"c-2-4-3":{"name":"Hannah's checking: what are the thoughts, how severe, and how ERP helps","why":"Elicits Hannah's obsessions and compulsions without judgement, separates OCD from risk to Isla, and explains exposure and response prevention.","frame":["Obsessions","Compulsions and reassurance","Avoidance","Severity and impact","Risk to Isla and mood","ERP"],"steps":[{"h":"Answer her fear first","say":["Hannah, I'm one of the perinatal doctors. You asked if we'll take Isla away. Unwanted thoughts like you've described don't mean that. We're here to help you.","Can you tell me what's been happening?"],"hear":"\"I keep checking the oven, plugs and locks. I can't bath Isla alone.\"","mark":"Fear of removal addressed"},{"h":"The obsessions","say":["When you check, what are you afraid might happen?","Do the thoughts feel like your own? Do you want to act on them?"],"hear":"\"I see Isla drowning, or the house on fire. They're mine. I hate them and push them away.\"","mark":"Ego-dystonic intrusive harm images: obsessions"},{"h":"Compulsions, reassurance and avoidance","say":["How long does checking take each day?","Does anyone check or reassure you?","What do you avoid because of the thoughts?"],"hear":"\"Three or four hours. Tom checks the oven for me. I don't bath Isla alone.\"","mark":"Checking compulsions, family accommodation, avoidance"},{"h":"Severity and impact","say":["How distressing are the thoughts, from 0 to 10?","Does checking ever delay Isla's feeds or care?"],"hear":"\"Nine. Sometimes feeds are late.\"","mark":"Severe OCD affecting infant care"},{"h":"Mood, psychosis and risk","say":["How's your mood been? Do you still enjoy time with Isla?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself? Any voices or feeling thoughts come from outside?"],"hear":"\"Low and tired, but I love being with her. No, none of that.\"","mark":"No psychosis or suicidal thoughts; no evidence of intent to harm Isla"},{"h":"Explain OCD and ERP","say":["This is OCD. The thoughts are horrible precisely because they go against what you want. Having them doesn't make you more likely to act.","ERP means facing the fear in small steps, like bathing Isla while Tom waits nearby, without checking or seeking reassurance.","We'll build a ladder together, starting with what's easiest. The anxiety rises, then falls if you stay with it."],"hear":"\"I can't just stop checking.\"","mark":"OCD and ERP explained"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["You won't stop all at once. You'll learn to tolerate a bit more each week with a therapist.","Given how severe this is, an SSRI alongside therapy may help. Sertraline is often used when breastfeeding.","Tom can help by gently not checking for you. I'll write to your GP and health visitor. What would you like to ask?"],"hear":"\"I'm relieved you don't think I'm a danger.\"","mark":"Plan: CBT with ERP, consider SSRI, partner involvement"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Obsessions","Images of drowning and fire; hates them","Ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts"],["Compulsions","Checking three to four hours a day","Severe checking"],["Reassurance and avoidance","Tom checks; won't bath Isla alone","Family accommodation; maintains OCD"],["Impact and risk","Feeds delayed; no intent; no psychosis","Severe OCD affecting care, not a risk of deliberate harm"],["Treatment","Can't stop checking","ERP in graded steps; consider SSRI"]]},"key":"Intrusive harm thoughts in OCD are unwanted and resisted. Recognise that clearly so Hannah gets treatment, not a safeguarding referral driven by fear.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[5],[6],[1,3]]},"c-2-4-4":{"name":"Jordan's nose: assess BDD without arguing, then answer the surgery request","why":"Recognises body dysmorphic disorder, measures its grip and risk, and replaces the surgery letter with a treatment Jordan can accept.","frame":["Preoccupation","Checking, comparing and camouflage","Conviction and insight","Avoidance and impact","Risk: suicide and self-surgery","Treatment instead of surgery"],"steps":[{"h":"Build trust without debating his looks","say":["Jordan, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Keep the cap and mask on if that's more comfortable.","I'm not here to argue about how your nose looks. I want to understand what this is doing to your life."],"hear":"\"Don't tell me you can't see it.\"","mark":"Engaged without arguing about appearance"},{"h":"The preoccupation","say":["What is it about your nose that bothers you?","On a typical day, how many hours do the thoughts take up?","When did it start to feel this bad?"],"hear":"\"It's crooked with a big bump. Six or seven hours a day. It got bad at college.\"","mark":"Marked preoccupation with a perceived defect"},{"h":"Checking, comparing and camouflage","say":["What do you find yourself doing when the thoughts come?","Do you compare yourself with other people?","How do you hide it when you go out?"],"hear":"\"Mirrors, car windows, my phone camera. I compare side photos online. Never out without the cap and mask.\"","mark":"Repetitive checking, comparing and camouflage identified"},{"h":"Conviction and insight","say":["How sure are you that it looks as bad to others as it does to you?","Is there any chance it looks less noticeable to other people?"],"hear":"\"A hundred per cent. Well, maybe a bit less bad to others. I don't know.\"","mark":"Poor insight with slight wavering: still BDD, not a separate psychosis"},{"h":"Impact, mood and risk","say":["What has this cost you: college, friends, going out?","Do you ever feel you'd be better off dead? Any plans?","If the NHS says no to surgery, what would you do?"],"hear":"\"Left college, see nobody. Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead, but I wouldn't do that to my mum. I've watched videos on breaking my own nose and looked at clinics in Turkey.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts, risk of self-surgery and unregulated surgery abroad"},{"h":"Explain BDD and answer the surgery request","say":["What you describe fits body dysmorphic disorder. The distress is very real; the problem is how much the brain zooms in on one feature and judges it.","Surgery rarely eases BDD. People often remain unhappy or move on to another feature, so I won't support an NHS surgery letter.","What does help is CBT designed for BDD, usually weekly for several months, with an SSRI such as fluoxetine. Together they work best when it's this severe."],"hear":"\"So you think I'm mad? Will tablets change how my nose looks to me?\"","mark":"Diagnosis explained; surgery declined with reasons; CBT plus SSRI offered"},{"h":"Safety plan and close","say":["Not mad at all. Tablets won't change your nose; they turn down the volume of the thoughts so therapy can work.","Please don't try to break or reset your nose yourself. It can cause lasting damage, and surgery abroad carries real risks.","If the thoughts of dying get stronger, call 111 and choose the mental health option, or 999. I'll refer you to the specialist BDD service and write to your GP today."],"hear":"\"OK. If it gets me back to college.\"","mark":"Self-surgery and suicide risk safety-netted; specialist referral"}],"table":{"cols":["BDD feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Preoccupation","Six to seven hours a day on his nose","Severe BDD"],["Repetitive behaviours","Mirrors, windows, phone, comparing photos","Checking and comparing keep the distress going"],["Camouflage and avoidance","Cap and mask always; left college; sees nobody","Major functional impairment"],["Insight","100% sure, wavers slightly","Poor insight; treat as BDD, not as a separate delusional illness"],["Risk","Better off dead; DIY nose videos; Turkey clinics","Suicide and self-surgery risk need a safety plan"]]},"key":"Never argue about his appearance. Ask what he would do if surgery is refused: the answer, breaking his own nose, is the highest risk in the room.","objmap":[[3],[7],[],[1,6],[6,7]]},"c-2-4-5":{"name":"Amira: a trauma-informed PTSD history through a professional interpreter","why":"Gives Amira safety and control, identifies PTSD without making her retell the trauma, and agrees treatment and practical help for her family.","frame":["Interpreter, privacy and choice","Re-experiencing","Avoidance","Sense of current threat","Mood, guilt and risk","Treatment and practical support"],"steps":[{"h":"Use the professional interpreter, see her alone","say":["Mr Hassan, thank you for offering. We always use a professional interpreter, so Amira's words reach me exactly and you don't carry this.","Could you wait outside for now? We'll invite you back at the end if Amira would like."],"hear":"Mr Hassan: \"OK, I'll wait outside.\"","mark":"Professional interpreter used; seen alone"},{"h":"Confidentiality, choice and control","say":["Amira, what you tell me is confidential health information. We don't pass it to the Home Office unless you ask us to, such as for a medical report.","You don't need to tell me what happened. I'll ask how things affect you now, and you can pause whenever you want."],"hear":"\"Will this hurt my asylum case? I don't want to talk about what happened.\"","mark":"Asylum fear addressed; control given"},{"h":"Re-experiencing","say":["Do memories come back when you don't want them, as if it's happening again?","How many nights a week do you have nightmares, and how long do you sleep?"],"hear":"\"Loud bangs bring it back. Nightmares four or five nights. I sleep about three hours.\"","mark":"Flashbacks and nightmares identified"},{"h":"Avoidance","say":["Are there places, people or things you stay away from because they remind you?","Do you try to push the memories out of your mind?"],"hear":"\"I don't watch the news. I don't go to the community centre.\"","mark":"Avoidance identified"},{"h":"Sense of current threat","say":["Do you feel on guard, or jump at sudden noises?","You mentioned anger. When does it come?"],"hear":"\"I check the door at night. I shout at the children and then feel bad.\"","mark":"Hypervigilance, startle and irritability identified"},{"h":"Mood, guilt, risk and safety at home","say":["Some people feel numb, cut off, or guilty after what they've survived. Is that true for you?","Do you ever wish you wouldn't wake up? Have you made any plans?","Do you feel safe at home? How are the children?"],"hear":"\"I feel guilty I lived when my brother died. Sometimes I wish I wouldn't wake, but my children keep me going. My husband is kind. Four of us in one hotel room; the little one wets the bed.\"","mark":"Survivor guilt, numbness, passive suicidal thoughts; no abuse; family under strain"},{"h":"Explain, plan and close","say":["These are signs of PTSD, a common reaction to terrible events, not madness. It can be treated.","The best treatment is trauma-focused therapy, such as trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, usually weekly for about three months, with the same professional interpreter where possible.","I'll write to your GP about sleep, your youngest's bedwetting and housing support. If the wish not to wake gets stronger, call 111 and choose the mental health option.","Would you like your husband to hear the plan now?"],"hear":"\"Yes. I feel better that it has a name.\"","mark":"Trauma-focused therapy, practical support and safety net agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["PTSD cluster","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Re-experiencing","Flashbacks with bangs; nightmares four or five nights","Core PTSD feature, present now"],["Avoidance","News, community centre","Core PTSD feature; isolates her further"],["Sense of threat","Checks the door; jumpy; shouts at the children","Hyperarousal driving the 'anger' the GP noted"],["Mood and risk","Survivor guilt, numbness, wishes not to wake","Depressive features and passive suicidal thoughts to monitor"],["Context","Hotel room, asylum fear, child bedwetting","Practical and family support are part of treatment"]]},"key":"Trauma-informed means giving control: a professional interpreter, privacy from her husband, and no demand for the story. The diagnosis rests on symptoms now, not on details of the trauma.","objmap":[[1],[5],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-2-4-6":{"name":"Gary's pain and fear: name health anxiety without dismissing him","why":"Agrees one consistent message with the medical team, recognises health anxiety and possible depression, and gives Gary a plan that does not depend on another scan.","frame":["Medical red flags and one message","Fear of serious illness","Checking and reassurance","Family history and trigger","Mood","Plan with GP and medical team"],"steps":[{"h":"Agree the medical position with the consultant","say":["Dr Morgan, are there any new findings or red flags that would justify repeat imaging?","Can we agree one clear message for Gary, and which symptoms would change it?"],"hear":"Dr Morgan: \"No weight loss, no bleeding, bloods normal. Another scan isn't indicated.\"","mark":"One consistent medical message agreed"},{"h":"Accept the pain, find the fear","say":["Gary, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Your pain is real, and I'm not here to say you're making it up.","What do you fear is causing it?"],"hear":"\"Bowel cancer. Every doctor tells me something different.\"","mark":"Fear of a serious illness despite normal tests"},{"h":"Checking, reassurance and avoidance","say":["When the worry starts, what do you do?","After a normal test, how long does the relief last?","Is there anything you avoid?"],"hear":"\"I search online for hours, check my stools, prod my belly, ask my wife. Relief lasts a few days. I can't watch hospital programmes.\"","mark":"Checking, reassurance-seeking and avoidance identified"},{"h":"Family history and trigger","say":["Has anyone close to you had cancer?","When did the worry take off?"],"hear":"\"My dad died of bowel cancer at fifty; they missed it at first. I was twenty. It got worse last year when a friend got ill.\"","mark":"Family history and a recent trigger: understandable vulnerability"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How are your sleep and your interest in things?","Have you had any thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"I wake at four worrying. I've lost interest. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Possible depression; no suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Explain the cycle","say":["After losing your dad the way you did, this fear makes complete sense.","Checking and reassurance bring relief for a few days, then the worry returns stronger. A scan would do the same.","Your recent colonoscopy covers your family-history risk for now; any further checks are for the bowel team to plan, not the worry."],"hear":"\"So you think it's all in my head? What if I really get cancer and you ignore me?\"","mark":"Health anxiety explained using his own cycle"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["No. We'll take the pain seriously and agree which new symptoms mean you're seen quickly: bleeding, weight loss, or a lasting change in your bowels.","CBT for health anxiety, usually weekly through NHS Talking Therapies, works well. If your mood stays low, an SSRI can help both.","I'll ask your GP for regular booked appointments with one named doctor, and I'll put the same message in your notes for the medical team."],"hear":"\"OK. I'll give it a go if someone's keeping an eye on me.\"","mark":"CBT, mood review, scheduled GP follow-up and agreed red flags"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Red flags","None; CT and colonoscopy normal last month","No current indication for repeat imaging"],["Fear","Sure it is bowel cancer","Preoccupation typical of health anxiety"],["Maintaining behaviours","Searching, stool checks, prodding, reassurance","Short relief keeps the cycle going"],["Context","Father died at fifty; friend ill","Explains the fear; validate it"],["Mood","Early waking, loss of interest","Possible depression to assess and treat"]]},"key":"Validate the pain, then give one consistent message from every doctor. Booked follow-up and agreed red flags replace scans as the source of safety.","objmap":[[1],[3],[5],[6],[1]]},"c-2-4-7":{"name":"Samuel's bar-code belief: overvalued idea, shared belief or delusion?","why":"Weighs Samuel's belief in its religious context, avoids a false psychosis label, and solves the real problem: his untaken blood pressure tablets.","frame":["Origin of the belief","Shared within his culture","Conviction and flexibility","Preoccupation and action","Psychotic features","Collateral and practical plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Respect his faith and his worry","say":["Samuel, Pastor Okoro, thank you for coming. I'm one of the psychiatry doctors.","Samuel, I'm not here to judge your faith. I'd like to understand this belief and how it affects your life."],"hear":"\"I'm worried you'll say I'm mentally ill.\"","mark":"Concern acknowledged"},{"h":"How the belief arose and who shares it","say":["How did you first come to believe this about bar codes?","Do other people in your church believe the same?"],"hear":"\"A visiting preacher spoke about it five years ago, and I read more. Many in the church believe it. Some think I take it too far.\"","mark":"Learned belief, shared within his faith group"},{"h":"Conviction and flexibility","say":["How sure are you, from nought to a hundred?","Could some bar codes be harmless?"],"hear":"\"About ninety-five. Maybe some are harmless.\"","mark":"Strongly held but open to some doubt"},{"h":"How much it drives his life","say":["How much of your day does it use up up?","What do you avoid because of it, and has it caused you any problems?"],"hear":"\"Not much. I pay cash. I haven't collected my blood pressure tablets.\"","mark":"Limited preoccupation; one important practical harm"},{"h":"Screen for psychosis and mood","say":["Do you ever hear voices, or feel that messages are meant just for you?","Do you feel anyone is against you, or interfering with your thoughts?","How are your mood and sleep?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. Mood and sleep are fine.\"","mark":"No hallucinations, passivity, persecution or mood disorder"},{"h":"Collateral from the pastor","say":["Pastor Okoro, is this belief common in your church?","Have you noticed any change in Samuel recently?"],"hear":"Pastor: \"Many share it. He's the same man he's always been.\"","mark":"Culturally shared; no change in the person"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["This is a belief shared within your faith that you hold strongly. It isn't a sign of psychosis, so you don't need medication, and there's no question of a section.","The real concern is your blood pressure. Could we ask your GP and pharmacist for a way to get your tablets that you can accept, perhaps a paper prescription with the staff handling any scanning?","I'll write to your GP today. If anything changes, like new voices or fears, they can refer you back."],"hear":"\"Thank you for respecting my faith. I'll go to the pharmacy with the pastor.\"","mark":"No psychiatric treatment; practical medication plan; GP letter"}],"table":{"cols":["Test of the belief","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Origin","From a preacher, then reading","Understandable route; not a sudden delusional insight"],["Cultural context","Many in church share it; pastor confirms","Culturally congruent, which argues against a delusion"],["Conviction","95 out of 100; some codes may be harmless","Strong but not completely fixed"],["Psychosis screen","No voices, reference, persecution or passivity","No evidence of psychotic illness"],["Impact","Avoids bar codes; BP tablets uncollected","Practical health risk to solve with GP and pharmacy"]]},"key":"A belief shared by the person's faith group is not a delusion. Judge origin, cultural fit, conviction and change in the person before calling it psychosis; then fix the harm it causes.","objmap":[[2,6],[2,3],[2,5],[6],[4,7]]},"c-2-5-1":{"name":"Third visit this week: a fresh look, not a rerun","why":"Treats tonight as a new event, finds what has changed from her usual pattern, and agrees a crisis plan she owns instead of a bed that may not help.","frame":["Tonight's trigger","Change from her baseline","Safety at home: abuse and dependants","Alcohol","What has helped before","What admission would do"],"steps":[{"h":"Start without the label","say":["Hi Kayleigh, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. I've seen the notes, but I'd like to hear tonight from you.","Is this a private enough spot, or shall we find somewhere quieter?"],"hear":"\"Whatever. You're going to send me home anyway. That's what you lot always do.\""},{"h":"What happened tonight","say":["Take me back to this evening. What was going on before you cut?","What were you feeling right at that moment, and what did cutting do for it?"],"hear":"\"Jordan said he was leaving. I locked myself in the bathroom and cut to make it stop.\"","mark":"Trigger identified: row with partner, threat of loss"},{"h":"Is tonight different?","say":["Did you take any tablets or anything else tonight, as well as the cut?","Right now, do you have thoughts about ending your life? Any plan or preparations?","Compared with the other two nights this week, is anything new or worse?"],"hear":"\"No tablets. I sometimes wish I wouldn't wake up, but I've got no plan. It's the same as always: a row with Jordan.\"","mark":"No change from baseline identified"},{"h":"Home, drink and who else is affected","say":["How are things at home with Jordan? Has he ever hurt you, or made you scared?","Are there any children living with you or that you look after?","How much have you had to drink tonight, and in a usual week?"],"hear":"\"He shouts. He doesn't hit me... not really. No kids, just the cat. Two glasses tonight; a bottle most nights.\"","mark":"Possible domestic abuse identified; alcohol a risk amplifier"},{"h":"The admission question, honestly","say":["I can hear you want somewhere safe tonight. What do you hope a night on a ward would give you?","For you, long stays tend to make the crises come more often, not less. I'd rather get you safe tonight in a way that keeps you in charge.","Is there somewhere safe you could go tonight instead of home?"],"hear":"\"I just don't want to go back to the flat. Mum's ten minutes away; she'd have me.\"","mark":"Admission purpose met by a safe alternative"},{"h":"Build the plan with her","say":["What has got you through nights like this before?","Let's write down your warning signs, what you'll do, and who you'll call, in that order.","The crisis team will ring you by midday tomorrow, and I'll let Ruth, your care coordinator, know about tonight."],"hear":"\"Ice and music help. Talking to Mum. OK, I'll go to hers.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"},{"h":"Close and hand over","say":["Can you tell me back what you'll do if the urge comes again tonight?","If you feel unsafe, call the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2, or come back here; you won't be turned away.","Would you like the number for a domestic abuse helpline, just to have?"],"hear":"\"Ice, ring Mum, ring the crisis line. Yeah, give me the number.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Assessment area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Tonight's trigger","Row with Jordan, who said he was leaving","Fear of abandonment drives her crises; this is the target for therapy"],["Change from baseline","Same pattern as the last two visits, passive wish only, no plan","Chronic risk raised but no new acute rise tonight"],["Abuse and dependants","He shouts; hesitant denial of physical harm; no children","Possible domestic abuse: ask again alone, offer helpline, consider DASH"],["Alcohol","A bottle of wine most nights","Raises impulsivity and risk; offer brief advice and follow up"],["What helps","Ice, music, Mum, care coordinator Ruth","Protective supports to build the safety plan on"],["Admission","Wants a safe night away from the flat","Her mum's house meets the need; brief admission only with a clear, agreed purpose"]]},"key":"Excellent practice assesses each attendance as new, asking what has changed tonight, rather than labelling her as 'frequent'. Declining admission is safe only when paired with a specific, timed plan she helped write.","objmap":[[3,4],[4],[1],[6],[6]]},"c-2-5-2":{"name":"Why DBT, not a tablet: the four skills modules","why":"Explains DBT in plain words around what she wants to change, answers the medication question honestly, and checks she wants the referral.","frame":["Mindfulness","Distress tolerance","Emotion regulation","Interpersonal effectiveness"],"steps":[{"h":"Who is in the room","say":["Chloe, thanks for coming. Is it all right for your mum to stay while we talk?","Is there anything you'd prefer I didn't bring up in front of her?"],"hear":"\"She can stay. Just not the stuff about my ex.\"","mark":"Consent for mother to stay recorded"},{"h":"What she knows and wants","say":["What have you heard about DBT so far?","If a tablet did exactly what you wanted, what would change for you?","Have you tried therapy or medicine before? How did that go?"],"hear":"\"Someone said it's a group thing, like AA for feelings. One text and I go from nought to a hundred. Counselling just made me cry.\"","mark":"Goal identified: feelings that rise too fast"},{"h":"The four skills, linked to her life","say":["DBT is a practical therapy for people whose feelings hit hard and fast. It teaches four sets of skills.","First, noticing what's happening inside you without being swept along: that's mindfulness. Second, getting through a crisis without making it worse: distress tolerance.","Third, turning down the volume on feelings before they peak: emotion regulation. Fourth, asking for what you need and saying no without a blow-up: interpersonal effectiveness.","Which of those sounds most useful when that text arrives?"],"hear":"\"The second one. And the last one, with my ex... sorry, not that.\"","mark":"Distress tolerance and interpersonal skills identified as priorities"},{"h":"How it actually runs","say":["You'd see your own therapist once a week, and go to a weekly skills group that works more like a class than group therapy.","Between sessions you can get short phone coaching to use a skill in the moment. You fill in a diary card each day.","It usually runs for about a year. Could you manage that alongside your week?"],"hear":"\"A year? And I don't have to talk about my cutting in the group?\""},{"h":"The tablet question","say":["I wish there were a tablet for this. There's no medicine that treats EUPD itself, and NICE advises against using medicines for it.","If you became depressed or couldn't sleep in a crisis, we'd treat that on its own, for a short time.","Many people find DBT changes how often crises happen, which tablets have not been shown to do."],"hear":"\"So the sertraline wasn't going to fix it anyway. OK.\"","mark":"Medication expectations addressed"},{"h":"Safety and the wait","say":["When did you last hurt yourself? Any thoughts about ending your life at the moment?","Let's write down what you'll do if the urges build before DBT starts.","Your care coordinator will keep weekly contact while you wait. Mum, would you like information on carers' support?"],"hear":"\"Ten days ago, my thighs. No, I don't want to die. I text Mia or go for a walk.\"","mark":"Risk reviewed; safety plan agreed"},{"h":"Her decision","say":["What would you tell a friend DBT is, in your words?","Would you like me to confirm the referral, or do you want time to think?"],"hear":"\"Skills for when it all kicks off. Yeah, go on, refer me.\"","mark":"Agrees to referral"}],"table":{"cols":["DBT module","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mindfulness","Feelings go from nought to a hundred before she notices","Starting point: spotting the rise early gives other skills a chance"],["Distress tolerance","Cuts to stop the feeling; copes by texting Mia or walking","Has some crisis skills to build on; key for reducing self-harm"],["Emotion regulation","Wants a tablet to stop the feelings","Her real goal is calmer, slower emotions; this module targets exactly that"],["Interpersonal effectiveness","One text wrecks her day; relationship topics are raw","Relationships trigger her crises; skills here reduce the triggers"]]},"key":"Good doctors list the four modules. Excellent ones attach each module to her own words, so DBT answers 'what would the tablet do for you?', and explain the structure and length before asking for commitment.","objmap":[[2],[4],[5],[1],[6]]},"c-2-5-3":{"name":"Splitting on the ward: one team, one plan","why":"Names splitting kindly, holds a shared boundary, treats her complaint as real, and gets every shift working from the same written plan.","frame":["Idealisation and devaluation","Shared confidentiality","The complaint","Consistent written plan","Staff wellbeing"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with her","say":["Hi Natalie, thanks for waiting. How has this week been for you?","I've got twenty minutes set aside, so there's no rush."],"hear":"\"Better now you're here. You're the only doctor who actually understands me.\"","mark":"Idealisation identified"},{"h":"Hear her side","say":["Tell me about the nights. What's been happening?","How do things feel different between day and night staff?"],"hear":"\"Nights hate me. They make me wait for everything. Days are fine.\"","mark":"Devaluation of night staff identified"},{"h":"Shared confidentiality, warmly","say":["I'm glad our talks help. I work as part of a team, so what you tell me gets shared with them; that's how you get the same answer on every shift.","I can't keep things from the team, but I will make sure you're heard."],"hear":"\"So you'll tell them everything? Then what's the point?\"","mark":"Boundary held"},{"h":"The complaint","say":["You've written a complaint about Gary. Can you tell me what happened?","Were you hurt? Is there any mark now?","What would you like to come out of it?"],"hear":"\"At 2am he wouldn't give me my PRN and pulled my arm off the office door. It left a red mark. It's gone now. I just want nights to be fair.\"","mark":"Complaint involves physical contact: needs formal review"},{"h":"Risk and her goals","say":["Any self-harm or thoughts about ending your life in the last few days?","What do you want from the rest of this admission?"],"hear":"\"Nothing for 48 hours. I want to sleep, get back to my flat and start therapy.\"","mark":"Goals identified: sleep, discharge, therapy"},{"h":"With the nurse in charge","say":["I think we're seeing splitting, and it's pulling the team in two. It's not her fault or ours; it's how her distress shows.","Could we meet today and agree one written plan for leave, PRN and observations that every shift uses, with a named nurse and set daily time with her?","Her complaint involves physical contact, so it needs an incident report and to go through the proper process today, with Gary supported too."],"hear":"\"Fine, but the nights feel blamed. Two of them argued in the office this morning.\"","mark":"Plan for team meeting agreed"},{"h":"Look after the team and close","say":["That argument tells us how much strain people are under. Can we book reflective practice this week with the psychologist?","Natalie, the team will agree one plan today, and your complaint is being taken forward properly."],"hear":"\"OK. As long as someone actually looks at it.\"","mark":"Staff support arranged"}],"table":{"cols":["Splitting feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Idealisation and devaluation","'Only doctor who understands me'; nights 'hate' her","Classic split; the idealised doctor must not side against colleagues"],["Shared confidentiality","Asks the doctor to keep things secret","Secrets feed the split; explain team-wide sharing kindly"],["The complaint","Arm pulled off the door at 2am; red mark","Takes it seriously regardless of diagnosis: incident report and formal review"],["Consistent plan","Different answers on different shifts","One written plan for PRN, leave and obs, a named nurse, set contact times"],["Staff wellbeing","Two nurses argued in the office","Team strain is part of the picture; reflective practice and supervision"]]},"key":"Naming splitting is not enough: a complaint from a patient with EUPD still gets a full, fair investigation. Excellent practice holds both truths and treats the team, not just the patient.","objmap":[[6],[6],[3],[2,3],[5]]},"c-2-6-1":{"name":"Not the same man: personality change after head injury","why":"Uses his wife's account to compare before and after the injury, links the change to frontal damage, and acts on risk to the family and on the road.","frame":["Who he was before","Frontal changes now","The injury and its severity","Other brain-injury effects","Risk to family, self and others","Next tests and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Speak to his wife privately","say":["Mrs Hughes? I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Daniel is with the officer next door. Could we talk here, just us?","You look shaken. Take your time."],"hear":"\"Sarah, please. Yes. I'm just shaking.\""},{"h":"Tonight and before the crash","say":["What happened tonight?","What was Daniel like before the accident?"],"hear":"\"He shouted at the neighbours and punched their door. Before the crash he was a calm, careful accountant.\"","mark":"Calm pre-injury personality identified"},{"h":"The injury itself","say":["What do you remember about the injury? Was he unconscious, and for how long was he in hospital?","Has he had any scans or brain tests since?"],"hear":"\"Knocked out at the scene, four days in intensive care, no memory for about three weeks. Only a CT. He missed the neuropsychology appointment.\"","mark":"Severe injury identified; follow-up never completed"},{"h":"What has changed","say":["Has he become more impulsive, say with money, or with things he says to people?","Any blank spells, headaches or change in how much he drinks?","How has his mood been?"],"hear":"\"He's spent about eight grand. He says sexual things to my friend. He has staring spells, daily headaches, four cans at weekends.\"","mark":"Disinhibition and possible seizures identified"},{"h":"Who is at risk","say":["Has he ever hurt you, or have you felt scared of him?","Are there children at home? How are they with him?","Has he talked about harming himself? Is he still driving?"],"hear":"\"He threw a plate and grabbed my wrist once. Our two, nine and six, hide when he shouts. He said we'd be better off without him. He drives them to school.\"","mark":"Risk to wife, children and self identified; driving concern"},{"h":"Explain the link","say":["The front of the brain controls judgement, temper and holding back. Damage there can change how a person behaves, without them choosing it.","That explains a lot, but it doesn't make you or the children less important. Your safety comes first."],"hear":"\"So it's not just him being horrible?\""},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["I'd like a brain injury team review, an MRI, and an EEG for those staring spells.","He shouldn't drive until those spells are checked, and he must tell the DVLA. We'll also talk about support for you and the children, including a safeguarding referral.","Could you keep the bank cards for now? Where will you and the children sleep tonight?"],"hear":"\"I'll keep the cards. The kids are at my mum's. I'll stay there too.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Before the injury","Calm, careful accountant","Marked change from baseline: the core of personality change due to a medical condition"],["Frontal changes","Impulsive spending, rudeness, sexual comments, temper","Disinhibition and poor judgement typical of frontal damage"],["Severity","Unconscious, four days in ICU, three weeks' amnesia","Severe injury; neuropsychology never done"],["Other effects","Staring spells, headaches, weekend drinking","Possible post-traumatic epilepsy; alcohol worsens control"],["Risk","Plate thrown, wrist grabbed, children hide, said family better off without him, still driving","Domestic abuse and child safeguarding concern, suicide risk, unsafe driving"]]},"key":"The injury explains the behaviour; it does not excuse the risk. Excellent practice gives Sarah a plain brain-behaviour explanation and still acts on safeguarding, suicidality and driving tonight.","objmap":[[2],[2,3],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-2-6-2":{"name":"Epileptic or dissociative? Reading the attack","why":"Separates dissociative from epileptic seizures using the eyewitness picture, keeps her safe from both seizures and over-sedation, and explains without blame.","frame":["Eyes and movements","Duration and pattern","Awareness and recall","Her known epilepsy","Triggers and stressors","Safety and driving"],"steps":[{"h":"Ask the people who saw it","say":["Before I see her, can you describe the attacks exactly as you saw them?","Were her eyes open or shut? How did the movements go? How long, and how did she come round?","Has anyone filmed one?"],"hear":"\"Ten to fifteen minutes each, eyes screwed shut, head going side to side. She came round quickly. Her partner Jay filmed one.\"","mark":"Features suggesting dissociative attacks identified"},{"h":"Open with her","say":["Hi Leah, I'm from the liaison psychiatry team. How are you feeling now?","My job is to work out what's causing these attacks with you, not to judge them."],"hear":"\"Wiped out. Embarrassed. They think I'm faking, don't they?\""},{"h":"Her usual seizures versus today's","say":["What are your usual seizures like, from the very first feeling?","How do today's compare? Could you hear anything during them?"],"hear":"\"Usually a rising feeling in my tummy, a burning smell, I lose a minute and smack my lips. Eyes open. Today's go on and on, and I can sometimes hear people.\"","mark":"Two different attack types identified"},{"h":"Medicines and what's changed","say":["Have you taken your lamotrigine today? Any changes to other medicines lately, including contraception?","How have sleep, alcohol and life been recently?"],"hear":"\"Took it this morning. I started the combined pill about three months ago. I'm not sleeping, I lost my job and my dad's in hospital.\"","mark":"Pill may lower lamotrigine levels; stressors identified"},{"h":"Explain without blame","say":["These attacks are real; you're not putting them on. Some may be epilepsy, and some may be your brain switching off for a while under strain. People can have both.","The best test is a recording of an attack with EEG at the same time, on video. Jay's clip will help the neurologists a lot.","Your combined pill can lower lamotrigine levels, so we'll ask neurology about that too."],"hear":"\"So I'm not mad? And it might be my pill?\""},{"h":"Driving","say":["Until we know what these are, please don't drive.","If any of them prove to be epileptic, you're legally required to tell the DVLA, and the neurologist will advise you on when you can drive again."],"hear":"\"I only just got my licence back.\"","mark":"Driving advice given"},{"h":"Agree the plan with A&E","say":["Some features look dissociative, but with known epilepsy I'd keep the seizure protocol and avoid extra benzodiazepine unless it's clearly epileptic.","Can we ask neurology about video EEG and a lamotrigine level, and get Jay's video sent over?"],"hear":"\"Fine. We'll keep her on obs and call neurology.\"","mark":"Shared plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Eyes and movements","Eyes tightly shut, side-to-side head movements","Point towards a dissociative attack; epileptic seizures usually have eyes open"],["Duration","Ten to fifteen minutes each, four today","Long, fluctuating attacks favour dissociative; also beware status epilepticus"],["Awareness","Can sometimes hear people","Retained awareness suggests dissociative"],["Her usual seizures","Rising gut feeling, burning smell, lip smacking, eyes open","Typical temporal lobe seizures; these may coexist"],["Medicines","Combined pill started three months ago","Oestrogen lowers lamotrigine levels; breakthrough seizures possible"],["Stressors","Job lost, father in hospital, poor sleep, past trauma","Plausible triggers for dissociative attacks; explore later"]]},"key":"Having dissociative attacks does not rule out epilepsy. Excellent practice spots that her new combined pill can lower lamotrigine, so some of today's events could be genuine breakthrough seizures.","objmap":[[4,5],[7],[7],[5],[5,6]]},"c-2-6-3":{"name":"Mute and still: rule out the dangerous causes first","why":"Keeps her physically safe, screens for catatonia and organic causes such as encephalitis, and agrees shared care rather than a handover.","frame":["Physical safety","Catatonia","Organic causes","Depressive or dissociative stupor","Capacity and best interests"],"steps":[{"h":"At the bedside with the registrar","say":["Thanks for the referral. Can we go through things at the bedside together?","What have the tests shown so far, and what's still outstanding?"],"hear":"\"Bloods and CT are normal. We think it's functional. We need the bed, so we're hoping you'll take over.\""},{"h":"Speak to her, then look for catatonia","say":["Hello, I'm a psychiatry doctor. You don't need to answer. I'll tell you everything I'm doing.","I'm going to gently move your arm and see how it feels, and watch your eyes and posture.","Let me run through the catatonia rating scale for these signs."],"hear":"(No response. Eyes resist opening; no posturing or waxy flexibility; normal tone.)","mark":"Catatonia screen done"},{"h":"Physical safety","say":["How is her fluid balance? Has CK been done?","Is she on VTE prophylaxis, and is someone turning her regularly?"],"hear":"\"Fluid chart is patchy and urea is a bit up. No CK. She was marked mobile on admission so no VTE prophylaxis. I'm not sure about turns.\"","mark":"Physical risks identified: dehydration, VTE, pressure areas"},{"h":"Organic causes","say":["Has anyone done an EEG, MRI or lumbar puncture?","Non-convulsive status and encephalitis can both look exactly like this."],"hear":"\"None of those yet.\"","mark":"Organic work-up incomplete"},{"h":"Her mother's account","say":["Mrs Clarke, thank you for coming. What was she like in the weeks before this?","Any illness, odd ideas, drugs, or anything like this before?"],"hear":"\"She had a flu-like illness three weeks ago, then said people were watching her. Nothing like this before. Her brother Michael died at the gym last month.\"","mark":"Prodromal illness and new paranoia: encephalitis must be excluded"},{"h":"Capacity and best interests","say":["She can't currently show us she understands or decide, so we're treating her in her best interests using the Mental Capacity Act.","That covers fluids, tests and nursing care. If she needs longer-term treatment or begins to resist strongly, we'll review whether the Mental Health Act is more fitting."],"hear":"\"Thank you. Please just help her.\"","mark":"Capacity assessed; best interests care recorded"},{"h":"Agree shared care","say":["The bereavement makes a psychological cause possible, but the flu-like illness and paranoia worry me. Could we get an EEG, MRI, an LP with NMDA receptor antibodies, and a CK?","I'd suggest a lorazepam challenge and VTE prophylaxis. She should stay under medicine, and we'll review her daily."],"hear":"\"OK. I'll request those and keep her with us.\"","mark":"Shared care agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Physical safety","Patchy fluids, raised urea, no VTE cover, turns unclear","Immediate risks of dehydration, clots and pressure sores"],["Catatonia","Mute, immobile, not eating; needs formal signs checked","Treatable; a lorazepam challenge can help diagnosis"],["Organic causes","Flu-like illness, then paranoia; no EEG, MRI or LP","Autoimmune encephalitis or non-convulsive status must be ruled out"],["Psychological stupor","Brother died suddenly last month","Dissociative or depressive stupor possible, but only after organic causes are excluded"],["Capacity","Mute, cannot weigh or communicate a decision","Best interests care under the MCA; review if MHA is needed"]]},"key":"Normal bloods and CT do not make it functional. A recent flu-like illness followed by new paranoia in a young woman points to autoimmune encephalitis until proven otherwise.","objmap":[[3],[2,7],[4,7],[6],[]]},"c-2-6-4":{"name":"Frontal lobe testing at the bedside","why":"Tests each frontal function in turn, spots perseveration and disinhibition, and turns the findings into a differential and a practical plan.","frame":["Verbal fluency","Motor sequencing","Inhibition","Abstraction","Cognitive estimates","Primitive reflexes"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and consent","say":["Hello Mr Wilson. Your GP asked us to look into some changes your wife has noticed. Is it all right that she stays?","I'd like to do some short thinking tasks. Some may seem simple; that's normal. Is that OK?"],"hear":"\"Fine by me. She's the one with the problem, if you ask me.\"","mark":"Consent obtained"},{"h":"Fluency","say":["In one minute, tell me as many words as you can starting with F. No names of people or places.","Now as many animals as you can in a minute."],"hear":"\"Fish, fast, Frank, fish... funny, fine.\" (Six with a repeat and a name; about eleven animals.)","mark":"Reduced letter fluency with rule breaks identified"},{"h":"Sequencing and inhibition","say":["Watch me: fist, edge, palm. Now you do it with me, then on your own.","When I tap once, you tap twice. When I tap twice, don't tap at all."],"hear":"(Gets stuck on fist-edge, repeating it. Taps on every tap.)","mark":"Perseveration and failed inhibition identified"},{"h":"Abstraction and estimates","say":["How are an apple and a banana alike?","What does 'People in glass houses shouldn't throw stones' mean?","How fast does a racehorse gallop?"],"hear":"\"Both in the fruit bowl. Well, the stone rolls down the hill. About 200 miles an hour.\"","mark":"Concrete thinking and poor estimates identified"},{"h":"Memory and reflexes","say":["Can you tell me those three words from earlier?","I'm going to stroke your palm gently; just relax your hand."],"hear":"(Recalls all three. Grips your fingers and won't let go.)","mark":"Memory preserved; grasp reflex present"},{"h":"Explain the findings","say":["Some tasks were harder, especially switching actions and finding words, while memory was good.","That pattern can happen when the front of the brain isn't working as well. We'd like a brain scan and some more detailed tests to find out why."],"hear":"\"I've just had a bad morning.\" (His wife looks relieved someone noticed.)"},{"h":"Practical steps","say":["Are you still driving? With changes like these, it's wise to stop until we've finished the tests, and you may need to tell the DVLA.","While things are straightforward, it's worth thinking about lasting power of attorney."],"hear":"\"I still drive. We haven't sorted any of that.\"","mark":"Driving and LPA advice given"}],"table":{"cols":["Frontal test","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Verbal fluency","Six F-words with a repeat and a proper name; about eleven animals","Poor initiation and rule-keeping"],["Motor sequencing","Stuck on fist-edge","Perseveration: poor set-shifting"],["Go/no-go","Taps on every tap","Impaired inhibition, fits his disinhibited behaviour"],["Abstraction","'Both in the fruit bowl'; literal proverb","Concrete thinking"],["Cognitive estimates","Racehorse at 200 miles an hour","Impaired judgement and reasoning"],["Primitive reflexes","Grasp reflex present","Supports frontal pathology; recall intact argues against Alzheimer's"]]},"key":"Memory intact with frontal failure points to behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia, but a frontal tumour or vascular cause must be excluded with an MRI before that label is used.","objmap":[[1,2],[2],[1,2],[6],[7]]},"c-2-6-5":{"name":"Could it be ADHD? Childhood, settings, safety","why":"Tests the ADHD picture properly, rules out look-alikes and risks, and explains why diagnosis comes before any prescription.","frame":["Onset in childhood","Impairment in more than one setting","Mood and other explanations","Substance use","Heart and physical baseline","Driving safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the agenda","say":["Hi Ryan, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. I know you've waited a long time for this.","Today I want to understand the whole picture, then I'll be straight with you about what happens next."],"hear":"\"Look, I'll be honest, I've waited months. I just want to get sorted with some tablets today.\""},{"h":"Adult life now","say":["Tell me how it affects you day to day: at work, at home, with money and relationships.","What would other people say they notice about you?"],"hear":"\"Lost three jobs for being late and missing deadlines. I lose my keys, forget orders. My girlfriend says I never listen and the bills don't get paid.\"","mark":"Impairment at work and home identified"},{"h":"Back to childhood","say":["What were you like at primary school?","Is there anyone who knew you then, or any old school reports, we could look at?"],"hear":"\"Always out of my seat, sent out of class. Reports said 'needs to concentrate'. Mum still has them.\"","mark":"Childhood onset identified; collateral available"},{"h":"Mood and other explanations","say":["How's your mood been? Ever spells of feeling high, needing little sleep?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"A bit low about the jobs. Never high like that. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Bipolar and major depression less likely"},{"h":"Drugs, alcohol and the friend's tablets","say":["What do you use, including drink, cocaine or other people's tablets?","When you took your friend's medicine, how much and how often?"],"hear":"\"Coke once or twice a month. A few pints at weekends. Took my mate's Elvanse a few times.\"","mark":"Substance use identified: affects diagnosis and prescribing"},{"h":"Heart and driving","say":["Any chest pain, palpitations or blackouts? Anyone in the family die suddenly or young from heart problems?","Have you had any near misses at work on the road?"],"hear":"\"No chest pain. An uncle had a heart attack in his sixties. I clipped a wing mirror last month looking at the satnav.\"","mark":"Driving risk identified"},{"h":"Explain and agree the next step","say":["It's reasonable to look at ADHD properly. A specialist makes the diagnosis, with your mum's account and those reports, so I can't start medication today.","Before any medicine we'd check your pulse, blood pressure and weight. Please stop taking your friend's tablets, and cutting the cocaine would make treatment safer.","While distracted driving is an issue, take extra care, and tell the DVLA if your concentration affects your driving. Can I contact your mum?"],"hear":"\"Fine. Mum'll send the reports. How long's the wait?\"","mark":"Referral and collateral agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Criterion","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Childhood onset","Out of seat, sent out, 'needs to concentrate'","Symptoms before age 12; get school reports and mum's account"],["Pervasive impairment","Jobs lost, bills unpaid, relationship strain","Impairment in more than one setting supports referral"],["Other explanations","Low mood about jobs; no high spells","Depression secondary rather than causal; bipolar unlikely"],["Substances","Cocaine monthly; friend's Elvanse","Can mimic or worsen symptoms; misuse risk with stimulants"],["Physical baseline","No cardiac symptoms; uncle's MI in his sixties","Low cardiac risk; still record pulse, BP and weight first"],["Driving","Clipped a mirror while distracted","Safety advice now; DVLA if driving affected"]]},"key":"A 'quiet head' on someone else's stimulant proves nothing; stimulants sharpen most people. Excellent practice anchors the diagnosis in childhood evidence from another source before discussing any medicine.","objmap":[[1],[2,7],[5],[3,7],[5,7]]},"c-2-7-1":{"name":"Will I get better? An honest outlook in anorexia","why":"Answers her fear with honest numbers and her own hopeful signs, links the outlook to treatment and safety, and agrees what her mother hears.","frame":["What she knows and fears","Signs that point to recovery","Signs that raise concern","Physical risk now","Treatment plan","Family involvement"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with her question","say":["Hi Chloe, I'm one of the doctors here. I'll answer that honestly, I promise.","First, what did your mum read, and what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"That people die from this. Will I actually get better?\"","mark":"Main fear identified"},{"h":"What she knows","say":["What do you understand about anorexia so far?","How has it been since you started uni?"],"hear":"\"I lost weight over nine months. I run every day even when I'm shattered. I'm scared I'll have to leave my course.\"","mark":"Over-exercise identified; no purging reported"},{"h":"Honest outlook","say":["Anorexia is a serious illness, and your mum is right that it can be dangerous. But many people recover fully.","Roughly half recover completely, about a third improve a lot, and a smaller group struggle long term. Treatment changes those odds."],"hear":"\"So it's not just a death sentence?\""},{"h":"Her own hopeful signs, and the worrying ones","say":["Several things are on your side: you're young, it's been under a year, you've no vomiting or laxatives, and you have your mum's support.","What worries me is your low weight, the daily running and your low mood. Those are things we'll work on together."],"hear":"\"I didn't think the running was a big deal.\"","mark":"Good and concerning signs explained"},{"h":"Physical safety now","say":["Do you feel dizzy standing up, faint, or have chest pain? Do you feel cold a lot?","We'll check your heart, blood pressure, blood tests and weight regularly. If you faint, get chest pain or feel very weak, call 999."],"hear":"\"I get dizzy standing quickly. Always cold. No fainting.\"","mark":"Postural symptoms identified: needs physical monitoring"},{"h":"The plan","say":["We'd offer a specialist eating disorder therapy, usually weekly for several months, with regular physical checks.","We'll talk to your university about support so you may be able to keep studying, as long as your body stays safe.","How's your mood been? Any thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Low for a couple of months, but nothing like that.\"","mark":"Mood screened; no suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Agree what Mum hears and close","say":["What would you like your mum to know?","Can you tell me in your words what the outlook is and what happens next?"],"hear":"\"She can know the diagnosis and the plan, but not my weight. Most people get better, especially if they start early.\"","mark":"Consent for limited sharing recorded"}],"table":{"cols":["Outlook area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Hopeful signs","Age 19, nine months' illness, no purging, close to mum","Early treatment with support gives her a good chance"],["Concerning signs","BMI 15.8, daily over-exercise, low mood","Higher risk; targets for treatment and monitoring"],["Physical risk now","Dizzy on standing, always cold","Needs pulse, lying and standing BP, bloods and ECG; red flags explained"],["Treatment","Worried about leaving her course","Specialist therapy plus physical monitoring; liaise with university"],["Family","Mum may know diagnosis and plan, not weight","Respect her wish while involving family; review it if risk rises"]]},"key":"Good practice gives honest figures. Excellent practice puts her own hopeful signs alongside the worrying ones, so the outlook feels personal and encourages her to engage, without making her afraid.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5,6],[1,2],[7]]},"c-2-7-2":{"name":"Feeding safely tonight: the refeeding plan","why":"Grades refeeding risk, gets the right bloods, ECG and thiamine in before food, and starts feeding promptly with clear thresholds for escalation.","frame":["Risk grading","Baseline tests","Thiamine and vitamins","Starting calories","Monitoring and escalation","Consent and law"],"steps":[{"h":"Agree the goal first","say":["Thanks for bleeping. I agree she needs food tonight; let's make sure it's safe.","Tell me about her first."],"hear":"\"Megan, 24, came in this afternoon. BMI 13.2, hardly eaten for two weeks. Dietitian's gone till Monday and there are only two of us qualified on.\"","mark":"High refeeding risk identified: BMI under 16 and little intake over 10 days"},{"h":"Observations and history","say":["What are her latest observations, lying and standing?","Any vomiting, laxatives, chest pain or swelling?"],"hear":"\"Pulse 46, BP 88/56 lying, 80/50 standing and dizzy, temp 35.9, glucose 3.8. She vomits and takes laxatives some days. Couldn't stand from squatting without her hands.\"","mark":"Purging and muscle weakness identified: higher risk"},{"h":"Tests before food","say":["I'll take bloods now: U&Es, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, liver tests, full blood count and CK.","And an ECG tonight to look at her heart rate and QTc."],"hear":"\"Nothing's been done since she arrived.\"","mark":"Baseline bloods and ECG ordered"},{"h":"Thiamine first","say":["I'm prescribing thiamine now, before her first meal, plus vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin.","Let's give her something with sugar now, as her glucose is low."],"hear":"\"Right, I'll get the thiamine in.\""},{"h":"How much food","say":["Full meals straight away risk refeeding problems, but too little food is also dangerous.","Start her on the protocol meal plan, building up over a few days, with phosphate, potassium and magnesium replaced as needed."],"hear":"\"So not nothing, just the starting plan from the protocol?\"","mark":"Feeding started at protocol level"},{"h":"Monitoring and escalation","say":["Bloods daily for the first week; obs four times a day, plus blood glucose.","Call me or the medical registrar if her pulse drops below 40, she faints, her glucose falls under 3, or phosphate drops.","I'll ring the medical registrar now about her physical risk and whether she's safe to stay here."],"hear":"\"Medical reg's happy for her to stay with those thresholds.\"","mark":"Escalation thresholds set"},{"h":"If she refuses","say":["Megan, your body needs to restart carefully. We'll check your blood every day and give vitamins so eating is safe. You won't be left without food.","If she wants to leave and is at serious risk, call me. I'll assess her capacity and the Mental Health Act; you have the nurses' holding power if needed."],"hear":"\"She's said she'll leave if made to eat a full plate.\"","mark":"Plan for refusal agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Plan element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Risk grading","BMI 13.2, two weeks of little intake, purging","High refeeding risk; medical risk also high"],["Baseline tests","Pulse 46, low BP with postural drop, glucose 3.8, nothing done yet","Bloods including phosphate and magnesium, plus ECG, tonight"],["Thiamine","Not prescribed","Give before the first meal"],["Feeding","Nurses want full meals tonight","Start promptly at protocol level, increase over days; avoid both over- and underfeeding"],["Monitoring","Two qualified nurses, no dietitian","Daily bloods, regular obs, clear thresholds, medical registrar involved"],["Consent","Will leave if made to eat a full plate","Capacity assessment; MHA if leaving at serious risk"]]},"key":"The answer to 'can she eat tonight?' is yes. Excellent practice avoids both dangers: feeding her too much too quickly, and starving her out of fear of refeeding.","objmap":[[1],[3],[4],[6],[5,6]]},"c-2-7-3":{"name":"Binge, purge, and what helps: bulimia","why":"Gets a clear picture of the binge-purge cycle and its physical risks, then explains why a focused therapy works better than a tablet.","frame":["Binges","Compensation","Shape and weight","Physical risk","Mood and alcohol"],"steps":[{"h":"Make it safe to talk","say":["Hi Jade, I'm a psychiatry doctor working with your GP. Lots of people find this hard to talk about.","What we say stays within your care team, unless I'm worried about your safety."],"hear":"\"This is so embarrassing. Is there just a tablet I can take to stop it?\""},{"h":"The binges","say":["Can you walk me through a typical binge: when, what you eat, and how long it lasts?","During it, do you feel you can stop?"],"hear":"\"Four or five evenings a week. A loaf of toast, cereal, chocolate, in about half an hour. I can't stop.\"","mark":"Binges with loss of control identified"},{"h":"What comes after","say":["What do you do afterwards to get rid of it, or to make up for it?","Any laxatives, skipping meals or exercise?"],"hear":"\"I make myself sick every time. Four senna two or three times a week. I skip breakfast and lunch on bad days.\"","mark":"Vomiting, laxatives and restriction identified"},{"h":"How she sees herself","say":["How much does how you feel about yourself depend on your shape or weight?","What do you imagine would happen if you didn't make yourself sick?"],"hear":"\"Pretty much entirely.\"","mark":"Self-worth based on shape identified"},{"h":"The body","say":["Any palpitations, dizziness, cramps or weakness? How are your teeth?","Do you have diabetes or take any insulin?"],"hear":"\"Palpitations sometimes, leg cramps. Teeth are sensitive; I brush straight after. No diabetes.\"","mark":"Physical risk identified: likely low potassium"},{"h":"Mood and alcohol","say":["How's your mood been? Any self-harm or thoughts about ending your life?","How much do you drink?"],"hear":"\"Flat. Nothing like that. I drink a lot some Saturdays.\""},{"h":"Explain and agree","say":["This sounds like bulimia. It's common and treatable, and you're not alone.","The best first step is guided self-help focused on eating, with a therapist. If that isn't enough, there's longer CBT. A tablet alone doesn't treat it.","Today, let's check your potassium and do an ECG. Rinse with water rather than brushing straight after being sick, and see your dentist. Would you like me to refer you?"],"hear":"\"OK. If the talking thing works better, I'll try it.\"","mark":"Agrees to referral and blood tests"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Binges","Large amounts in half an hour, four or five times a week, can't stop","Objective binges with loss of control"],["Compensation","Vomits each time; senna; skips meals","Recurrent purging: high electrolyte risk"],["Shape and weight","Self-worth depends on shape","Core belief that therapy targets"],["Physical risk","Palpitations, cramps, sensitive teeth","Check potassium and ECG today; dental advice"],["Mood and alcohol","Flat mood, heavy Saturday drinking, no self-harm","Monitor mood; alcohol can trigger binges"]]},"key":"Normal weight does not mean low risk. Excellent practice checks potassium and an ECG on the same day because of frequent vomiting with laxatives, even in a well-looking patient.","objmap":[[2,3],[6],[7],[7],[]]},"c-2-8-1":{"name":"Planning a baby on lithium: weighing both risks","why":"Sets the small risk to the baby against her high relapse risk, plans pregnancy monitoring, and explains valproate rules without discussing her sister's care.","frame":["Her relapse history","Risk to the baby","Options for lithium","Monitoring in pregnancy and after birth","Valproate rules"],"steps":[{"h":"Open","say":["Hi Aisha, good to see you.","What would you like to talk about today?"],"hear":"\"My partner and I want to try for a baby. Do I have to stop my lithium?\""},{"h":"What she knows and wants","say":["What have you heard about lithium and pregnancy?","What matters most to you in this?"],"hear":"\"That it causes heart defects. I want a healthy baby, and I want to stay well for it.\""},{"h":"Her history","say":["Tell me about when you've been unwell before.","Have you ever stopped lithium? What happened?"],"hear":"\"Manic with psychosis at 23, six weeks in hospital. I stopped it at 26 and was manic within three months.\"","mark":"High relapse risk identified"},{"h":"Risks both ways","say":["Lithium slightly increases the chance of a heart problem in the baby, but the overall risk stays small and most babies are born healthy.","Stopping carries a real chance of you becoming very unwell, especially in the weeks after birth.","Please don't stop it suddenly. Keep using contraception until we've made a plan together."],"hear":"\"So stopping isn't the safe choice either?\"","mark":"Balanced risk explained"},{"h":"Options and monitoring","say":["Options are staying on lithium with close monitoring, or switching slowly to another medicine. The perinatal team will help you decide.","If you stay on it: folic acid now, more frequent lithium levels, a detailed scan of the baby's heart, and a birth plan in hospital.","Sam knows your early signs. Shall we write a relapse plan with him?"],"hear":"\"I'd like to see the perinatal team. Sam spots it when I stop sleeping.\"","mark":"Perinatal referral agreed"},{"h":"Her sister","say":["I can't talk about your sister's care, as that belongs to her.","In general, valproate is not started in women who could become pregnant unless there's no other option, and only with a pregnancy prevention programme and reliable contraception.","She could ask her own doctor about that."],"hear":"\"I'll tell her to ask. I didn't know about that.\"","mark":"Valproate guidance given without breaching confidentiality"},{"h":"Close","say":["You don't have to decide today. I'll refer you to perinatal psychiatry and write to your GP.","What will you take away from today?"],"hear":"\"Don't stop suddenly, keep on the pill for now, see the perinatal team.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Relapse history","Manic with psychosis; relapsed within three months of stopping","High risk of relapse in pregnancy and after birth"],["Risk to baby","Fears heart defects","Small rise in cardiac risk; most babies healthy; detailed heart scan offered"],["Options","Stable for four years","Stay on lithium or switch slowly; never stop abruptly; perinatal team to advise"],["Monitoring","Still on the pill, wants to plan","Folic acid, more frequent levels, hospital birth, level soon after delivery"],["Valproate","Sister, 27, recently started valproate","General rules only: pregnancy prevention programme; sister to ask her own doctor"]]},"key":"The question is not 'is lithium safe?' but 'which is riskier for her: lithium or relapse?'. Her rapid relapse after stopping should lead the decision.","objmap":[[2],[4],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-2-8-2":{"name":"Methadone in pregnancy: steady is safer","why":"Explains why a stable dose protects mother and baby, prepares her honestly for withdrawal in the baby and for social care, and makes home safe.","frame":["Why the dose stays stable","Withdrawal in the baby","Social care","Safety at home","Joint care with maternity"],"steps":[{"h":"Open","say":["Hi Kayleigh, thanks for coming in. How are you doing?","How is the pregnancy going so far?"],"hear":"\"I want my methadone cut, fast. I don't want my baby born addicted, and I don't want social services taking it.\""},{"h":"Where things are","say":["Six months off heroin is a real achievement. Any heroin, crack, benzos or drink lately?","Any cravings or withdrawal on your current dose?"],"hear":"\"Nothing for six months. I had the odd drink before I knew; none since. Some cravings in the evenings.\"","mark":"Stable on methadone; evening cravings"},{"h":"Why fast cuts are risky","say":["Cutting quickly can put your baby through withdrawal in the womb and makes relapse to heroin more likely, which is far riskier.","If you want to reduce, we can go slowly while things are stable, and stop if cravings rise. Some women need a higher dose later in pregnancy."],"hear":"\"So staying on it is actually better for the baby?\"","mark":"Stable dose agreed"},{"h":"Withdrawal in the baby","say":["Your baby may have withdrawal after birth. It's common, it's watched for, and it's treatable.","Your baby will stay with you in hospital for a few days, and breastfeeding is usually encouraged and can help."],"hear":"\"Will they take it away to treat it?\""},{"h":"Social care, openly","say":["Being on treatment and stable is exactly what professionals want to see.","Because of the methadone, social care will usually do an assessment before the birth. It's to plan support, and your progress counts in your favour."],"hear":"\"So they won't just take it?\"","mark":"Safeguarding explained honestly"},{"h":"Safety at home","say":["Who lives with you? Where do you keep your methadone?","Have you got naloxone at home?"],"hear":"\"My son, he's six. It's in the kitchen cupboard. I lost my naloxone.\"","mark":"Risk to child from storage identified; no naloxone"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["Let's get you a lockable box today, and a new naloxone kit.","I'll refer you to the specialist midwife so we can plan your care together, including a birth plan."],"hear":"\"OK. I'll get the box today.\"","mark":"Joint care with maternity agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Stable dose","Wants a fast cut; cravings in the evening","Rapid reduction risks fetal withdrawal and relapse; any reduction slow and only if stable"],["Baby withdrawal","Fears baby 'born addicted'","Neonatal withdrawal is common and treatable; baby stays with her"],["Social care","Terrified the baby will be taken","Pre-birth assessment is routine; engagement and stability help her"],["Safety at home","Son aged 6; methadone in a kitchen cupboard; no naloxone","Lockable storage now; new naloxone; brief child safeguarding check"],["Joint care","Not yet seen the specialist midwife","Refer today; agree a birth plan with maternity and neonatal teams"]]},"key":"Methadone can be reduced in pregnancy, but slowly and only if she is stable. Excellent practice offers that choice instead of refusing outright, and spots her six-year-old's access to methadone.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-2-8-3":{"name":"Tired or unwell? Postnatal depression and the baby","why":"Separates blues, depression and psychosis, asks directly about harm to herself and the baby, and agrees treatment that fits breastfeeding.","frame":["Blues, depression or psychosis","Core depressive symptoms","Thoughts about the baby","Suicide risk","Bonding and support","Treatment options"],"steps":[{"h":"Open gently","say":["Hi Hannah, I'm from the crisis team. Your health visitor was worried and asked us to come. We're here to help, not judge.","Would you like to talk on your own first?"],"hear":"\"I don't really know why you're here. I'm just tired. Every mum's tired.\""},{"h":"How long and how bad","say":["When did you start feeling low? Is anything still enjoyable?","Can you sleep when Isla sleeps? How is your eating?"],"hear":"\"About five weeks. Nothing feels good. I can't sleep even when she sleeps. I barely eat.\"","mark":"Depression rather than baby blues identified"},{"h":"Bonding","say":["How do you feel when you're with Isla?","Who's helping you at home?"],"hear":"\"Like I'm looking after someone else's baby. Tom's lovely but he's back at work.\"","mark":"Bonding difficulty identified"},{"h":"Thoughts about the baby","say":["Many new mums get frightening thoughts about their baby being hurt. Have you had anything like that?","How do you feel when those thoughts come?"],"hear":"\"I see myself dropping her on the stairs. It horrifies me. I won't carry her on the stairs now.\"","mark":"Intrusive, unwanted thoughts identified, not intent"},{"h":"Her own safety and psychosis","say":["Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","Have you heard voices, felt confused, or felt something strange is going on?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think everyone would be better off without me. I'd never do anything, because of Isla and Tom. Nothing strange like that.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts; protected by Isla and Tom; no psychosis"},{"h":"Explain and offer options","say":["This sounds like postnatal depression. It's common, it's treatable, and it doesn't make you a bad mother.","Those images are a common part of anxiety and depression; they don't mean you'll act on them.","We can offer talking therapy, and an antidepressant that's safe with breastfeeding. The perinatal team can see you quickly."],"hear":"\"I don't want to stop feeding her.\"","mark":"Treatment options explained"},{"h":"Safety plan with Tom","say":["Tom, could you help with night feeds so Hannah can sleep?","Call the crisis line or 999 straight away if she becomes confused, talks about harming herself or Isla, or seems very different.","We'll visit again tomorrow and refer you to the perinatal team today."],"hear":"(Tom) \"I'll take the nights. I'll call straight away.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed with partner"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Blues, depression or psychosis","Low for five weeks; no confusion or strange beliefs","Postnatal depression; not blues, no psychotic features"],["Core symptoms","No enjoyment, can't sleep when baby sleeps, poor eating","Moderate depression needing treatment"],["Thoughts about the baby","Images of dropping Isla; horrified; avoids the stairs","Intrusive, unwanted thoughts; not intent; reassure and monitor"],["Suicide risk","Passive thoughts; protected by Isla and Tom","Needs a safety plan and close follow-up"],["Bonding and support","Feels distant from Isla; Tom back at work","Perinatal team and practical help with nights"],["Treatment","Wants to keep breastfeeding","Talking therapy; antidepressant compatible with breastfeeding if needed"]]},"key":"Thoughts of harming the baby are not all the same. Unwanted images she dreads are common and not dangerous; harm thoughts she accepts, or that come with confusion, signal psychosis and an emergency.","objmap":[[4,5],[4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-2-8-4":{"name":"Postpartum psychosis: name it, rate the risk, keep them together","why":"Recognise postpartum psychosis within minutes, rate the risk to Hannah and her son, and get specialist care tonight without separating them.","frame":["Sleep and mood","Beliefs about the baby","Voices","Risk to baby and self","Confusion and background"],"steps":[{"h":"Arrive and settle","say":["Hello Hannah, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Congratulations on your little boy. And you must be David.","The midwife says you've hardly slept. Could we sit down and talk for a few minutes?"],"hear":"\"I'm fine, honestly. I've never felt better. Who sent you?\""},{"h":"Sleep, pace and mood","say":["How much sleep have you had since Thursday?","Are your thoughts coming faster than you can keep up with?","Have you been starting lots of jobs, or feeling full of energy?"],"hear":"\"Two hours, maybe. I feel like electricity. I've sorted all his clothes by colour.\"","mark":"Manic or mixed features identified"},{"h":"Beliefs and voices","say":["You told the midwife he isn't your baby. What have you noticed?","How sure are you about that?","Have you heard anyone talking when nobody else was in the room?"],"hear":"\"They switched him in the nursery. A woman's voice by the cot says he isn't mine.\"","mark":"Delusion about the baby and hallucinations identified"},{"h":"Risk, asked plainly","say":["When you feel he isn't yours, have you had any thought of harming him, or that something has to be done to him?","Does the voice ever tell you to do things?","Have you had thoughts of not being here, or of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"I'd never hurt a baby. Once tonight I thought it'd be better if I wasn't here, but I won't do anything.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thought; no stated intent towards the baby"},{"h":"Confusion, body and family (with David)","say":["David, has she seemed muddled about the day or the time?","Any fever, headache, fits, alcohol or drugs?","Has anyone in the family had bipolar disorder, or been ill after having a baby?"],"hear":"\"She asked me twice if it was Wednesday. Her aunt has bipolar and was in hospital a few times.\"","mark":"Family history of bipolar disorder; delirium still to exclude"},{"h":"Name the illness","say":["I think this is an illness called postpartum psychosis. It can start suddenly in the first days after a birth.","It's serious, but it's treatable, and most women recover fully.","It isn't the baby blues, and nobody caused it."],"hear":"(David) \"I should have noticed. Will she go back to normal?\""},{"h":"Plan for tonight","say":["Tonight a nurse will stay with you and help care for him while you rest.","The specialist team will see you within hours, and we're asking for a Mother and Baby Unit bed so you can stay together.","I'm sending blood and urine tests to rule out infection or other physical causes."],"hear":"\"Can David stay? Will I still see him?\"","mark":"Agrees to stay with David present"},{"h":"Close","say":["David, please don't leave her alone with the baby for now, and tell the nurse straight away if anything changes.","What questions do you both have before I phone my consultant?"],"hear":"\"Just keep him safe. Thank you.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sleep and mood","Two hours' sleep since Thursday, 'like electricity', racing thoughts","Manic or mixed picture starting within days of birth"],["Beliefs about the baby","Swapped in the nursery, sends signals through his eyes","Fixed false belief centred on the infant; the main driver of risk"],["Voices","Woman's voice near the cot, no commands","Auditory hallucinations; ask about commands again at each review"],["Risk to baby and self","Denies harming him; once thought she'd be better off not here","Risk stays high despite denial: 1:1 nursing and baby supervised"],["Confusion and background","Unsure of the day; aunt with bipolar disorder","Physical screen to exclude delirium; family history makes the diagnosis more likely"]]},"key":"A calm spell or a firm 'I'd never hurt him' is not reassurance. This illness swings hour to hour, so the plan follows the diagnosis, not how she seems in the next five minutes.","objmap":[[4],[7],[3,4],[7],[3,8]]},"c-2-8-5":{"name":"Two mothers: keep Laura well, reassure Chloe safely","why":"Help Laura weigh quetiapine against diabetes in pregnancy, then recognise Chloe's harm thoughts as OCD rather than danger.","frame":["Relapse history","Quetiapine and blood sugar","Options and trade-offs","Intrusive thoughts","Checking and avoidance","Psychosis and mood screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Laura: her worry first","say":["Laura, I hear the sugar test came back this morning. What worries you most?","Did anyone mention the tablet could affect blood sugar?"],"hear":"\"Nobody warned me. Did the quetiapine cause this? I want to stop it today.\""},{"h":"Explain the link honestly","say":["Quetiapine can raise weight and blood sugar, so it may have played a part, alongside the pregnancy itself.","Diabetes in pregnancy is common and very treatable with diet, home checks and, if needed, metformin or insulin."],"hear":"\"So it is the tablet's fault.\"","mark":"Link explained honestly"},{"h":"Her relapse history","say":["Before quetiapine, what happened when you came off treatment?","How quickly did you become unwell?"],"hear":"\"I stopped lithium four years ago and was high within a week. Six weeks in hospital.\"","mark":"High relapse risk identified"},{"h":"Weigh it up and agree","say":["Stopping now would put you at real risk of relapse, and the first weeks after the birth carry the highest risk.","My advice is to stay on it while the diabetes team keeps your sugar under control.","We won't change anything without a senior review, and we'll plan the birth and feeding together."],"hear":"\"Can't I just stop until the baby's born?\"","mark":"Shared plan: continue quetiapine, link with diabetes team"},{"h":"Chloe: the thoughts","say":["Chloe, thank you for writing that down. Many new parents get frightening thoughts they don't want. What are yours like?","When the picture comes, how does it make you feel?","What do you do afterwards to feel safer?"],"hear":"\"I keep seeing myself dropping her down the stairs. It horrifies me. I check the gate again and again, and my partner does bath time.\"","mark":"Intrusive harm images with checking and avoidance identified"},{"h":"Screen out psychosis and depression","say":["Do the thoughts feel like your own, or put there from outside?","Have you ever felt you ought to act on them?","How is your mood, and have you had thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"They're mine and I hate them. I'm a bit low, feeds break my sleep, but nothing like that.\"","mark":"Unwanted, resisted thoughts: OCD pattern, no psychosis"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["These are intrusive thoughts, part of OCD after a birth. Being horrified by them is the sign you won't act on them.","The main treatment is CBT with exposure, and if needed an SSRI such as sertraline, which is suitable while breastfeeding.","The checking and avoiding keep the fear going, so therapy helps you drop them step by step."],"hear":"\"Am I a monster? Will the health visitor take her off me?\""},{"h":"Close","say":["No. You're a caring mum with a treatable illness. Telling the health visitor is about support, not removal.","I'll refer you to perinatal psychology and write to your GP today."],"hear":"\"That's a relief. Thank you.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Relapse history (Laura)","Manic within a week of stopping lithium; six-week admission","Stopping an effective drug now risks relapse, highest after birth"],["Quetiapine and blood sugar","Diabetes found at 26 weeks; nobody warned her","Plausible link; manage the diabetes rather than stop the drug"],["Intrusive thoughts (Chloe)","Pictures dropping her baby; horrified by it","Unwanted, resisted, own thoughts: OCD, not intent"],["Checking and avoidance","Checks the gate, avoids stairs and bath time","Keeps the fear alive; the target for exposure work"],["Psychosis and mood screen","No voices, thoughts her own, no suicidal thoughts","Psychosis and severe depression unlikely; keep an eye on mood"]]},"key":"Avoid the two opposite errors: stopping the drug that keeps Laura well, and reading Chloe's horror at her thoughts as risk. Unwanted, resisted harm thoughts call for therapy and reassurance, not separation.","objmap":[[2],[4],[5],[7],[3,4]]},"c-2-8-6":{"name":"Pre-birth safeguarding: weigh the combination, refer openly","why":"Judge the risk to Kayla's unborn baby from the whole picture and make an honest referral that keeps her engaged.","frame":["Substance use","Domestic abuse","Engagement with care","Her mental state","Support around her"],"steps":[{"h":"The midwife's concern","say":["Sarah, what exactly worries you: the missed visits, the cocaine, Jordan or her mood?","Has she other children, or any looked after by someone else?"],"hear":"\"Three missed appointments, cocaine recently, and Jordan has a violent past. I don't want to lose her trust.\""},{"h":"Kayla: the baby and her support","say":["Kayla, thanks for talking to me. How are you feeling about the baby coming?","Who'll be around to help after the birth?","What's made it hard to get to appointments?"],"hear":"\"I really want her, the cot's ready. Mum's nearby. Jordan takes the car, so I can't always get there.\"","mark":"Strength: her mother; barrier: partner controls transport"},{"h":"Drugs and abuse","say":["How much cocaine or alcohol are you using now?","Is Jordan there? Is it safe to talk?","Does anyone at home hurt you, frighten you or check up on you?"],"hear":"\"Just a bit at a party two weeks ago. He's at work. He gets rough when he drinks, pushed me into a wall last month, and he checks my phone.\"","mark":"Domestic abuse in pregnancy identified"},{"h":"Her own risk","say":["When things get bad, do you think about hurting yourself?","Have you ever had thoughts of harming the baby?"],"hear":"\"I think about cutting when we row, but not for months. Never the baby.\"","mark":"Self-harm urges; no thoughts of harming the baby"},{"h":"Be honest about the referral","say":["Kayla, I'm worried about what's happening with Jordan, for you and for her.","I'm going to ask children's social care for a pre-birth assessment. That means support before she's born, not taking her away.","I'd rather do this with you than behind your back."],"hear":"\"So you're reporting me? They'll take her the minute she's born.\""},{"h":"Agree who acts","say":["Mark, I'll make the referral today and copy in the midwife and her care coordinator.","Can we offer an IDVA and the 24-hour National Domestic Abuse Helpline, and agree safe contact times when Jordan is out?","Her care coordinator can visit weekly until the birth."],"hear":"(Mark) \"Agreed. Flag the abuse in the referral and check whether it meets MARAC criteria.\"","mark":"Named actions and timescales agreed"},{"h":"Close with the midwife","say":["Sarah, it isn't over the top. It's the mix of risks that needs this, and telling her openly keeps her trust.","Could you help with transport to her next appointment?"],"hear":"\"That makes sense. I'll see her on Friday.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Substance use","Cocaine at a party two weeks ago; drinks little","Risk to the pregnancy; offer drug service support and keep asking"],["Domestic abuse","Pushed into a wall, checks her phone, takes the car","Abuse often escalates in pregnancy; risk to mother and unborn baby"],["Engagement with care","Three missed appointments, partly from lack of transport","A warning sign, partly driven by his control"],["Her mental state","Urges to cut during rows, none for months; no thoughts of harming the baby","Lower direct risk from her; review often"],["Support around her","Mother nearby; trusts her care coordinator","Strengths to build the plan on"]]},"key":"Her EUPD is not the reason to refer; the combination of abuse, cocaine and missed care is. Saying so openly before you refer keeps her engaged with you and with the plan.","objmap":[[3,4],[6],[5],[3,6],[1,6]]},"c-2-8-7":{"name":"Is it the cycle? PMDD and the perimenopause","why":"Link Jess's mood to her cycle rather than bipolar disorder, and spot the perimenopause behind Helen's dip.","frame":["Timing in the cycle","Bipolar screen","Impact and risk","Menopause symptoms","Compared with past depression"],"steps":[{"h":"Jess: open","say":["Jess, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Thanks for coming in.","Your GP mentioned mood swings. Can you tell me what happens?"],"hear":"\"I go irritable, tearful, out of control, then I'm fine again.\""},{"h":"Link it to the cycle","say":["When in your month does it start, and when does it lift?","How are you in the week after your period?"],"hear":"\"About a week before my period. A day or two after I start bleeding, I'm back to normal.\"","mark":"Luteal-phase pattern identified"},{"h":"Screen for highs","say":["Have you ever had days of feeling very high, needing little sleep, ideas racing, at any time of the month?","Any voices or experiences other people didn't share? Any drugs or heavy drinking?"],"hear":"\"Never. I just want to feel normal all month.\"","mark":"No hypomania: bipolar disorder unlikely"},{"h":"Impact and risk","say":["How do those days affect work and the children?","In that week, do you have thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Two warnings at work, I shout at the kids. I think they'd be better off without me, but I've never done anything.\"","mark":"Suicidal thoughts in the premenstrual week"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["This sounds like premenstrual dysphoric disorder, not bipolar disorder. It's tied to your cycle.","Please keep a mood diary every day for two cycles to confirm it.","Treatment can be an SSRI, daily or just in the second half of the cycle, plus CBT. I'll write to your GP."],"hear":"\"So I'm not bipolar? Everyone thinks I'm making excuses.\""},{"h":"Helen: the hidden menopause","say":["Helen, have your periods changed? Any hot flushes or night sweats?","Is this low mood like your past episodes, or different?","Any thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Periods are all over the place, sweats every night, words escape me at work. Flatter than before, not as hopeless. Fleeting thoughts of death on bad nights, no plan.\"","mark":"Perimenopausal symptoms identified"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["I think the perimenopause is adding to your depression, through hormone changes and lost sleep.","At 46 with these symptoms, you don't need a blood test to confirm it.","I'll ask your GP to discuss HRT with you, and we'll review your sertraline."],"hear":"\"Is HRT safe for me?\""},{"h":"Close","say":["For most women your age the benefits outweigh the risks, and your GP will check your own history.","If those thoughts come more often or you start planning, call us or the crisis line straight away."],"hear":"\"Thank you. That makes sense.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Timing in the cycle","Worse the week before her period, well a day or two after","Luteal pattern; confirm with a two-cycle diary"],["Bipolar screen","No highs, no reduced need for sleep","Not bipolar disorder; answers the GP's question"],["Impact and risk","Work warnings, shouting; 'better off without me'","Severe enough to treat; suicidal thoughts need a safety plan"],["Menopause symptoms","Night sweats, flushes, irregular periods, word-finding trouble","Perimenopause; NICE NG23: no blood test needed over 45"],["Compared with past depression","Flatter, less hopeless; taking sertraline","Mixed picture: treat both the mood and the menopause"]]},"key":"The cycle makes the diagnosis, and it only emerges if you ask when symptoms start and stop. Running a mania checklist first, or taking the GP's 'bipolar' at face value, misses it.","objmap":[[6,8],[5],[7],[5,7],[8]]},"c-2-8-8":{"name":"Coercive control: name it, measure risk, plan safe contact","why":"Respond safely to Claire's disclosure, name the abuse, judge the risk and agree a plan she can use without alerting Martin.","frame":["Coercive control","Physical abuse and threats","Escalation","The children","Her mood and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Thank her and check time","say":["Thank you for telling me, Claire. That took courage.","How long do we have before Martin comes back?","Are you safe to go home today?"],"hear":"\"Five minutes, maybe. I think so. I just feel so small.\""},{"h":"Name it","say":["Checking your phone, controlling the money, calling you useless and stopping you seeing friends is called coercive control.","It is domestic abuse, and it is a crime, even without violence."],"hear":"\"I thought it was just how he is. He's never hit me.\"","mark":"Coercive control and financial abuse named"},{"h":"Screen the risk","say":["Has he ever pushed, hit, choked or strangled you?","Has he threatened to kill you, himself, the children or pets?","Is it getting worse or more frequent?","Does he track where you go?"],"hear":"\"No hitting, no threats. But it's worse since I got ill, and he tracks my phone.\"","mark":"Escalation and tracking; no violence or threats to kill"},{"h":"Her mood","say":["With all this going on, how's your mood been?","Have you had thoughts of self-harm or of not waking up?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I wish I wouldn't wake up. No plan. The kids keep me going.\"","mark":"Passive death wish; children protective"},{"h":"Safety plan","say":["Is there a safe number and time to contact you?","I'd like to refer you to an IDVA, an advocate who helps with safety and options. The National Domestic Abuse Helpline is a free and open 24 hours.","If he asks, you can say we talked about your tablets and sleep."],"hear":"\"Text my mobile in the mornings when he's at work. Will he find out?\"","mark":"Safe contact agreed"},{"h":"The children","say":["Are the children seeing or hearing this?","Children living with abuse are affected too, so I need to speak to our safeguarding lead. I'll tell you what I do and why."],"hear":"\"My ten-year-old covers her ears. Will social workers turn up at the house?\"","mark":"Children affected: safeguarding discussion"},{"h":"Close","say":["You don't have to decide anything about leaving today. Our job is to help you and the children stay safe while you decide.","In an emergency call 999; if you can't speak, press 55 when asked."],"hear":"\"Thank you. I didn't think anyone would believe me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Coercive control","Checks and tracks her phone; stopped her seeing friends","Domestic abuse and an offence (Serious Crime Act 2015, s76)"],["Financial abuse","Twenty pounds a week; he holds all the money","Economic abuse, part of the control"],["Physical abuse and threats","None; no strangulation, no threats to kill","Fewer DASH high-risk markers, but control still causes harm"],["Escalation","Worse since she became unwell","Raises risk; repeat the DASH over time"],["The children","Ten-year-old covers her ears; seven-year-old asks why Daddy shouts","Victims in their own right (Domestic Abuse Act 2021); safeguarding lead"],["Her mood and risk","Wishes not to wake; no plan; children keep her going","Depression worsened by abuse; review suicide risk regularly"]]},"key":"No violence does not mean low risk. Name coercive control plainly, then build safety around what she wants and how she can be reached safely, rather than pressing her to leave.","objmap":[[2,6],[3],[5],[5],[6]]},"c-2-9-1":{"name":"Respectful gender assessment: identity, mood, hormone safety, pathway","why":"Affirm Sophie's identity, treat her depression and make her self-sourced hormones safer, while explaining how NHS referral really works.","frame":["Gender identity over time","Mood and risk","Hormone safety","Support and stress","Referral pathway"],"steps":[{"h":"Name and pronouns","say":["What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","Shall I update your records and letters to Sophie?"],"hear":"\"Sophie, she/her. Please don't use my old name in letters.\"","mark":"Name and pronouns recorded with consent"},{"h":"Her story","say":["Can you tell me about your gender and how you've felt over the years?","How did it feel growing up, and around puberty?","What steps have you taken so far?"],"hear":"\"I've known I'm a girl since about six. I came out to friends last year and I'm living more as myself.\"","mark":"Persistent gender incongruence since childhood"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How's your mood been lately?","Has anything made it worse, like how people treat you?","Have you had thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Some days I can't get up. I got shouted at on the bus. Some nights I'd rather not wake up, but no plan.\"","mark":"Depression with passive suicidal thoughts; minority stress"},{"h":"Hormone safety","say":["Which hormones are you taking, how much, and where from?","Have you had any blood tests since starting?","Do you smoke? Any leg swelling, chest pain or breathlessness?"],"hear":"\"Oestradiol 4 mg a day from a website. No tests. I don't smoke.\"","mark":"Unmonitored self-sourced oestrogen"},{"h":"Explain the pathway","say":["I'm not going to tell you to stop. I want to make it safer, so I'll ask your GP to check hormone levels, liver tests and lipids.","Referral to an NHS gender dysphoria clinic goes through your GP. You don't need a psychiatrist to sign you off.","Waits are long, often years, so we'll keep supporting you meanwhile."],"hear":"\"So you won't make me stop? How long will it take?\""},{"h":"Plan","say":["We'll review your sertraline and offer talking therapy for your mood.","I'll write to your GP today about the referral and blood monitoring.","Here's the crisis line number if the nights get worse."],"hear":"\"Okay. My flatmate checks on me, and my mum's on side.\"","mark":"Shared plan agreed"},{"h":"Close","say":["Is there anything about today you'd like us to do differently?","I'll see you again in four weeks."],"hear":"\"No. Thank you for calling me Sophie.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Gender identity over time","Female since about six; now living more as herself","Persistent incongruence; ICD-11 does not class it as a mental disorder"],["Mood and risk","Can't get up some days; would rather not wake; no plan","Depression to treat; risk managed alongside"],["Hormone safety","Oestradiol 4 mg online, no blood tests","Clot and liver risks unmonitored; harm reduction through the GP"],["Support and stress","Shouted at on the bus; flatmate, friends and mother supportive","Minority stress feeds low mood; good protective network"],["Referral pathway","Wants to be 'signed off'","No psychiatric sign-off needed; GP refers; long waits"]]},"key":"Her depression needs treatment, but it is not a gate to her gender care. Treat mood and hormone safety in parallel, and never treat her identity as a symptom to explore away.","objmap":[[1],[3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-3-1-1":{"name":"ACE-III done fairly: five domains, one profile","why":"Give Frank a fair, standardised cognitive test, read the domain profile with its caveats and hand a clear summary to the consultant.","frame":["Attention","Memory","Verbal fluency","Language","Visuospatial"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and consent","say":["Frank, I'm one of the doctors. The nurse is off today, so I'll do the memory and thinking questions myself. Is that all right?","Karen, the questions only work if your dad answers alone, but I'd value your view straight afterwards."],"hear":"\"Our Karen thinks I'm going daft. Go on then.\""},{"h":"Make it fair before you start","say":["Are both hearing aids in and working? Have you got your reading glasses?","Is English your first language, and how old were you when you left school?","How have your mood and sleep been? Did this come on slowly or suddenly?"],"hear":"\"New batteries this morning. Born in Leeds, left school at fifteen. Sleep like a log. It's been slow.\"","mark":"Hearing, vision, language, schooling and mood accounted for"},{"h":"Attention and orientation","say":["Some questions are easy and some are meant to be hard. There's no pass or fail.","What's the day, the date, the month and the year?","I'll give you a name and address. Repeat it after me, and I'll ask for it again later."],"hear":"\"Tuesday, November, 2026. The date I'm not sure of. Do you think I'm stupid?\"","mark":"Date uncertain; otherwise attentive"},{"h":"Fluency and language","say":["Tell me as many words beginning with P as you can in one minute, but no names of people or places.","Now please name these pictures and read these words aloud."],"hear":"\"Pen, pot, pig, paper, post, pint... park, pudding.\"","mark":"Fluency mildly reduced; language largely intact"},{"h":"Visuospatial","say":["Please copy these two drawings as carefully as you can.","Next, a clock face: put in every number, then show ten past five with the hands."],"hear":"\"Easy enough. There you are.\"","mark":"Drawing and clock intact"},{"h":"Delayed recall and recognition","say":["Earlier I gave you a name and address. What was it?","Let me read some choices, and you pick the right ones."],"hear":"\"Harry... something Road? It's gone. I'm guessing now.\"","mark":"Delayed recall clearly impaired"},{"h":"Explain fairly","say":["How do you feel it went?","Your attention, drawing and language were good. Remembering new information after a delay was harder.","One test isn't a diagnosis. The consultant will put it with your history, bloods and scan at half eleven."],"hear":"\"So have I got dementia, then?\""},{"h":"Safety and sharing","say":["Are you still driving? Any trouble finding your way or with the cooker?","If a diagnosis affecting driving is made, you'd need to tell the DVLA, and we'd help.","Is it all right to share today's results with Karen?"],"hear":"\"Every Sunday, the church minibus. Never lost. I burnt a pan last month. Yes, tell Karen.\"","mark":"Driving and kitchen safety flagged"},{"h":"Handover to the consultant","say":["Mr Doyle, 74: ACE-III 79/100. Attention 17/18, memory 13/26, fluency 9/14, language 24/26, visuospatial 16/16.","Aids and glasses in, English first language, left school at 15, mood screen negative, gradual onset. Amnestic profile. Still drives a minibus."],"hear":"(Consultant) \"Thanks. I'll see him with Karen.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Attention","17/18; unsure of the date","Largely intact; no sign of delirium"],["Memory","13/26; address lost, guessing on recognition","Main deficit: amnestic pattern, as seen in Alzheimer's disease"],["Verbal fluency","9/14; eight P words with pauses","Mildly reduced; read in light of leaving school at 15"],["Language","24/26","Preserved"],["Visuospatial","16/16; drawings and clock correct","Intact; a posterior pattern is unlikely"]]},"key":"The total of 79 matters less than the profile. Report which domains fell and the caveats (hearing, schooling, mood), and never present one score as a diagnosis.","objmap":[[2],[6],[5,9],[7],[7,9]]},"c-3-1-2":{"name":"Parietal and temporal lobes: map the deficit, then act","why":"Test each side and lobe in turn, localise Margaret's new deficits and escalate a probable right parietal lesion the same day.","frame":["Dominant parietal","Non-dominant parietal","Either parietal lobe","Temporal lobe","Onset"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent, handedness, onset","say":["Mrs Ellis, I'm one of the doctors. I'd like to do some tests of how your brain handles space, touch, words and memory. Is that all right?","Which hand do you write with?","(To nurse) When was she last her usual self?"],"hear":"(Nurse) \"Fine on Sunday, problems since Monday. A near-fall last night. NEWS2 is 1.\"","mark":"New onset over days; right-handed"},{"h":"Dominant side","say":["Show me your right hand, then put your right hand on your left ear.","Eyes closed: which finger am I touching?","What's 7 times 6? And 100 take away 7?","Please write a sentence about the weather."],"hear":"\"Correct answers, and a neat sentence.\"","mark":"No Gerstmann features: dominant parietal spared"},{"h":"Neglect and drawing","say":["Please mark the middle of this line, then cross out every line on the page.","Copy these pentagons, then draw a clock showing ten past eleven."],"hear":"\"Why draw a clock? I'm not a child.\" (Mark well to the right; only right-hand lines crossed; numbers crowded on the right.)","mark":"Left neglect and constructional apraxia identified"},{"h":"Dressing, awareness and touch","say":["Could you put on your cardigan for me?","Is anything wrong with your left arm?","Eyes closed: am I touching one hand or both? What's in your hand? What number am I drawing?"],"hear":"\"My arm's fine.\" (Cannot dress; reports only the right hand when both are touched.)","mark":"Dressing apraxia, anosognosia and left sensory inattention"},{"h":"Temporal lobe and fields","say":["Here's a name and address to remember; I'll ask again shortly.","Cover one eye and tell me when you see my finger move.","Do you get odd smells, or sudden feelings you've lived this moment before?"],"hear":"\"No odd smells.\" (Recalls part of the address; misses movement in the lower left field.)","mark":"Partial recall; lower left field defect"},{"h":"Explain to her","say":["Margaret, the tests show the right side of your brain isn't handling the left side of space as it should.","That explains the left of your plate, the cardigan and getting lost. It isn't your mind going; it needs a scan today."],"hear":"\"Is it a stroke, like my sister's?\""},{"h":"Escalate","say":["(To medical registrar) 68-year-old right-handed woman: new left neglect, dressing and constructional apraxia, anosognosia and a lower left field defect since Monday.","I'm concerned about a right parietal stroke or mass. Can she have urgent brain imaging and a medical review today?"],"hear":"(Registrar) \"Agreed. I'll request a CT now and come up.\"","mark":"Urgent imaging and medical review requested"},{"h":"Ward plan and close","say":["(To nurse) Falls precautions please; she won't notice hazards on her left.","Keep her call bell and food where she can see them, and prompt her to look left at meals.","With her agreement, let's update her son Paul today."],"hear":"(Nurse) \"Will do. I'll ring Paul after the scan.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Lobe and side","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Dominant parietal","Right–left, finger naming, sums and writing correct","No Gerstmann syndrome; left parietal spared"],["Non-dominant parietal","Line mark far right, right-only cross-out, crowded clock, can't dress","Left neglect with constructional and dressing apraxia: right parietal"],["Awareness","Denies any problem with her left arm","Anosognosia; she won't report risks, so staff must anticipate them"],["Either parietal lobe","Reports only the right hand on double touch","Left sensory inattention, fitting a right-sided lesion"],["Temporal lobe and fields","Partial address recall; lower left field missed","Some verbal memory loss; lower quadrant defect points to parietal optic radiation"],["Onset","Well on Sunday, changed on Monday","Sudden: stroke until proven otherwise, not dementia"]]},"key":"New left neglect over days is a stroke or mass until proven otherwise, not 'confusion'. Check visual fields early, since a field cut can mimic neglect, then act today: urgent imaging and medical review.","objmap":[[1,5],[7],[2,7],[7],[8]]},"c-3-1-3":{"name":"Collateral for dementia: course, pattern, risk, carer","why":"Use Mark's account to establish the pattern of decline, the likely type and the risks that need action now.","frame":["Memory and course","Daily function","Clues to the type","Reversible contributors","Risk","Carer"],"steps":[{"h":"Open","say":["Mr Ashworth, thank you for coming. Your dad knows we're talking, and what you tell me really helps.","What made you worried enough to go to the GP?"],"hear":"\"Will Dad know I've told you all this? He asks me the same thing five times a visit.\""},{"h":"Their story","say":["When did you first notice a change, and what was the very first thing?","Did it come on slowly, suddenly, or in steps?","Does he lose things or get lost?","How does he manage bills, tablets and cooking?"],"hear":"\"About three years, slowly. He missed his grandson's birthday. He's been lost twice; the police brought him home once. I pay his bills now.\"","mark":"Gradual memory-led decline with loss of function"},{"h":"The pattern","say":["Has his personality changed, or has he become rude or flat?","Does he see things that aren't there, fall, or have good and bad days? Does he act out dreams?","How is his mood, how much does he drink, and what tablets does he take?"],"hear":"\"Much the same, a bit quieter. None of those. A couple of pints at weekends, and a bladder tablet from the GP.\"","mark":"Pattern fits Alzheimer's disease; possible anticholinergic noted"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Does he still drive? Any scrapes or near misses?","Has anyone tried to take money from him?","Any problems with the cooker, or missed tablets?"],"hear":"\"Drives every day, there's a new scrape on the door. A caller pretending to be his bank took three thousand pounds. He burnt a pan.\"","mark":"Driving, financial abuse and fire risk identified"},{"h":"The carer","say":["How are you coping with all this?","Who else helps, and is there a Lasting Power of Attorney?"],"hear":"\"I'm exhausted. It's just me. No, there's nothing like that.\"","mark":"Carer strain; no LPA"},{"h":"Summarise","say":["This sounds like a slow, memory-led decline, which most often points to Alzheimer's disease. We'll put it with his tests, bloods and scan before deciding.","Some bladder tablets affect memory, so we'll ask the GP to review it.","Please report the fraud to the bank and police, and we'll raise driving and an LPA with your dad."],"hear":"\"Is it Alzheimer's? Should I have done something sooner?\""},{"h":"Close","say":["You've done a lot already, and coming today was the right step.","I'll arrange a carer's assessment and give you the Alzheimer's Society helpline."],"hear":"\"Thanks. That helps.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Memory and course","Three years, slow; repeats questions; missed a birthday","Gradual amnestic decline"],["Daily function","Son pays the bills; lost twice","Daily life affected: dementia rather than MCI"],["Clues to the type","No hallucinations, falls, fluctuation or personality change","Lewy body and frontotemporal types less likely"],["Reversible contributors","Bladder tablet; a couple of pints at weekends","Review anticholinergic burden; alcohol modest"],["Risk","Drives daily with a new scrape; £3,000 scam; burnt pan","Driving review, adult safeguarding for financial abuse, fire safety"],["Carer","Exhausted, alone, no LPA","Carer's assessment; plan an LPA while he can decide"]]},"key":"A collateral history is not only about memory. The pattern of onset guides the diagnosis, but the scam, the car and the exhausted son are what need action this week.","objmap":[[],[2],[],[4],[5]]},"c-3-2-1":{"name":"Explaining the diagnosis: which dementia, and what it isn't","why":"Tell Patricia clearly that she has mixed Alzheimer's and vascular dementia, show how it differs from other types and MCI, and plan next steps.","frame":["Dementia or MCI","Alzheimer's disease","Vascular dementia","Lewy body dementia","Feelings and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up","say":["Mrs Wells, Claire, thank you for coming back. Is it all right to go through the results with Claire here?","Some people like detail, others the main points. What suits you?"],"hear":"\"Claire can hear everything. Just the main points, please.\""},{"h":"What they know","say":["What do you understand about why you came to the clinic?","Was there anything you were worried it might be?"],"hear":"\"I forget names and appointments. I was worried it was Alzheimer's, like Mum.\"","mark":"Fear of Alzheimer's identified"},{"h":"Give the diagnosis","say":["The tests show you have dementia. That means memory and thinking have changed enough to affect daily life, like bills and shopping.","It's a mix of two types: Alzheimer's disease, which affects the memory part of the brain, and changes from small blood vessels.","It isn't just old age, but this mix is common at your age."],"hear":"\"So it's what Mum had.\"","mark":"Mixed Alzheimer's and vascular dementia named"},{"h":"What it isn't","say":["Lewy body dementia is another type, with hallucinations, stiffness and falls, which you haven't had.","Mild cognitive impairment, like your friend's, shows on tests but leaves daily life mostly unaffected. Yours has affected bills and shopping, so it's dementia."],"hear":"(Claire) \"So that's the difference from her friend.\"","mark":"Lewy body dementia and MCI distinguished"},{"h":"Feelings","say":["How are you feeling, hearing this?","Some people worry about being a burden. Have you had thoughts like that, or of not wanting to live?"],"hear":"\"Will I stop knowing Claire? I'd rather not be a burden, but I don't want to die.\"","mark":"Burden thoughts; no wish to die"},{"h":"Treatment and support","say":["It changes slowly; most people know their family for a long time.","A tablet can help the Alzheimer's part for a while, and keeping your blood pressure controlled helps the vascular part.","You must tell the DVLA, and they'll decide about driving. It's also a good time to set up a Lasting Power of Attorney."],"hear":"\"Will I have to give up my car?\""},{"h":"Check understanding","say":["That was a lot to take in. Is there anything you'd like me to go over again?","Can you tell me in your words what we've covered?"],"hear":"\"Two kinds of dementia, a tablet might help, and I tell the DVLA.\""},{"h":"Close","say":["I'll write to you and your GP, and the memory nurse will call within two weeks.","If low thoughts get stronger, tell Claire or your GP, or ring us."],"hear":"\"Thank you for being straight with me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Type","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Dementia or MCI","Missed bills, repeats shopping; ACE-III 78","Daily life affected, so dementia rather than MCI"],["Alzheimer's disease","Memory losses; hippocampal atrophy on MRI","Alzheimer's component; cholinesterase inhibitor can be offered"],["Vascular dementia","Mild small vessel change; high blood pressure on amlodipine","Vascular component; control blood pressure and other risks"],["Lewy body dementia","No hallucinations, stiffness or falls","Not Lewy body dementia"],["Feelings and risk","'Rather not be a burden', no wish to die","Watch mood after diagnosis; ask again at follow-up"]]},"key":"Name the diagnosis early in plain words, then separate it from what it isn't. MCI differs because daily life is spared; say that directly, because families will compare.","objmap":[[1],[3],[4],[6],[]]},"c-3-2-2":{"name":"Frontotemporal dementia: behaviour before memory","why":"Draw out the behavioural features of possible frontotemporal dementia from Linda, rate the risks and support her as a carer.","frame":["Disinhibition","Apathy and loss of empathy","Rituals and eating","Executive function vs memory","Speech, motor signs and family history"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle her","say":["Mrs Marsh, I can see how upset you are. Please sit down and take your time.","You're worried this isn't depression after all?"],"hear":"\"He's a different man. Rude to the neighbours, and he laughed when our grandson was in hospital.\""},{"h":"Social behaviour and warmth","say":["Tell me some of the things that have changed.","Does he say or do things in public that embarrass you?","Does he still show warmth when someone is ill or upset?"],"hear":"\"He made a crude remark to a young girl at the till. With our grandson, nothing. He just laughed.\"","mark":"Disinhibition and loss of empathy identified"},{"h":"Drive, routines and eating","say":["How does he spend his day? Does he seem sad?","Has he become rigid about routines?","Has his eating changed?"],"hear":"\"He sits all day unless I push him, but he's not sad. Lunch at twelve exactly. Sweets constantly.\"","mark":"Apathy without low mood, rigid routine, sweet craving"},{"h":"Thinking, speech, body, family","say":["Can he still manage paperwork, and how is his memory for events?","Any change in his speech, weakness, twitching or choking?","Has anyone in his family had dementia or a change like this young?"],"hear":"\"He can't do our tax return, and he was an accountant. Memory's fair. Speech fine, no weakness. His father went odd in his fifties.\"","mark":"Executive loss with memory spared; possible family history"},{"h":"Risk","say":["How are your finances now?","Does he still drive?","Has he ever hurt you? Do you feel safe at home?","And how are you coping?"],"hear":"\"He's gambled away twenty thousand online. He drives faster and carelessly. He shouts, never hurts me. I'm at the end of my tether.\"","mark":"Financial, driving and carer risks identified"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["What you describe fits frontotemporal dementia, which affects the front of the brain. Memory often stays good early, so it's easily mistaken for depression.","I'll ask for an urgent assessment by the young-onset memory service, including a scan.","Ask the bank to block gambling payments, and he should stop driving until he's assessed."],"hear":"\"Could it still be depression? Is it my fault for shouting?\""},{"h":"Close","say":["None of this is your fault; the illness is changing how he behaves.","Some types run in families, and the specialist team can discuss genetic counselling once there's a diagnosis.","Here's the carer support number. I'll ring you after I've spoken to his GP."],"hear":"\"Thank you. I thought I was going mad.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Disinhibition","Crude remark at the till; rude to neighbours","Core behavioural feature of frontotemporal dementia"],["Apathy and loss of empathy","Sits all day; laughed at his sick grandson; not sad","Apathy without low mood argues against depression"],["Rituals and eating","Lunch at exactly 12; sweets constantly","Rigid routines and sweet craving support the diagnosis"],["Executive function vs memory","Can't do the tax return; memory fair","Frontal executive loss with memory spared"],["Speech, motor signs and family history","Speech normal; no weakness; father 'went odd' in his fifties","No language variant or MND signs yet; possible genetic form"]]},"key":"Apathy is not depression. Ask directly about sadness: an absence of feeling, not low feeling, plus early behaviour change with memory spared points to frontotemporal dementia rather than a mood disorder.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-3-2-3":{"name":"Memory medicines: honest benefit, heart first","why":"Help Arthur and Jean decide about a cholinesterase inhibitor with realistic hopes and a safe start given his faint and slow pulse.","frame":["Benefits and limits","Common side effects","Heart safety","Other medicines and cautions","Starting and review"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and their views","say":["Mr Bell, Mrs Bell, today is about memory medicines, so you can decide together.","What have you heard about them?"],"hear":"(Jean) \"That they bring his memory back.\""},{"h":"His hopes","say":["Arthur, what would you hope a medicine might do for you?","What matters most to you day to day?"],"hear":"\"Keep me doing the crossword and the allotment.\"","mark":"Goals: crossword and allotment"},{"h":"Benefits and limits","say":["These tablets don't cure Alzheimer's or bring back lost memory.","For some people they help memory and daily life for a while, and slow the decline.","Donepezil is the usual first choice at your stage."],"hear":"\"So it's not a cure, then.\""},{"h":"Side effects","say":["Common effects are feeling sick, loose stools, less appetite, vivid dreams and cramps.","They often settle after the first few weeks."],"hear":"(Jean) \"He fainted in March. Could it make that happen again?\""},{"h":"Safety checks","say":["Tell me about the faint. Was his heart checked?","These tablets can slow the heart, and so does bisoprolol. Your pulse is 58 today.","Any stomach ulcers, asthma or seizures? Can I see the full tablet list?","Oxybutynin can worsen memory and works against these tablets, so I'll ask the GP to review it."],"hear":"(Jean) \"Only the GP saw him, no heart tests. No ulcers or asthma.\"","mark":"Slow pulse and unexplained faint: heart check first"},{"h":"Agree the plan","say":["First an ECG with your GP, and heart specialist advice if it shows a problem.","If it's safe, donepezil 5 mg at night for four weeks, then 10 mg if it suits you.","Later, memantine can be added, and we won't stop just because the illness progresses."],"hear":"(Jean) \"Can't he have the strongest dose straight away?\"","mark":"Shared plan agreed"},{"h":"Check understanding","say":["Starting low lets his body adjust and cuts sickness and heart effects.","Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","Jean, could you note any side effects and how he's managing?"],"hear":"\"Heart test first, then a small tablet at night, going up after a month.\""},{"h":"Safety net and close","say":["If you faint, feel dizzy or your pulse feels very slow, stop the tablet and get help that day.","The memory nurse will see you four to six weeks after starting; then your GP takes over prescribing."],"hear":"\"That's clearer than the internet.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Benefits and limits","Jean expects memory back; Arthur wants the crossword","Reset expectations: modest benefit, may slow decline"],["Heart safety","Faint in March, pulse 58, on bisoprolol","Risk of slow heart rate and syncope: ECG and cardiac advice before starting"],["Other cautions","No ulcers, asthma or seizures","No further barrier"],["Other medicines","Oxybutynin among six tablets","Anticholinergic: worsens memory and opposes the drug; GP review"],["Starting and review","Jean fills a weekly pill box; sleeps well","Good adherence support; night dose, move to morning if dreams"]]},"key":"A faint plus a pulse of 58 on a beta-blocker is not a footnote; it changes the plan. Check the heart first, start low, and say plainly that benefit is real but modest.","objmap":[[2,7],[4],[5,8],[5],[6]]},"c-3-2-4":{"name":"Lewy body dementia at night: no haloperidol, look for delirium","why":"Steer the care home away from haloperidol, find the likely cause of Edna's new distress and keep her safe until review.","frame":["Antipsychotic sensitivity","Delirium causes","Parkinsonism","Hallucinations and dream enactment","Non-drug calming"],"steps":[{"h":"Open","say":["Sam, thanks for calling. Tell me what's happening with Edna tonight.","Is she safe right now, and is someone with her?"],"hear":"\"She's seeing children in the garden and is terrified they'll fall in the pond. We're two staff short.\""},{"h":"No haloperidol","say":["I won't approve haloperidol. In Lewy body dementia it can cause severe stiffness, falls, confusion and even death.","I'll record that and let the GP service know."],"hear":"\"Why won't you just approve it? It's what the GP said.\"","mark":"Antipsychotic sensitivity explained"},{"h":"Her symptoms","say":["Has she seen the children before, or is this new?","Does she kick or shout in her sleep?","Is she stiffer or slower than usual? Any falls?"],"hear":"\"On and off for months, but tonight she's scared. She's kicked in her sleep for years. Stiffer this week; nearly fell yesterday.\"","mark":"Known DLB features, worse this week"},{"h":"Delirium check","say":["Is she sleepier or more muddled today? Drinking less?","When did she last open her bowels? Any change in her urine?","Can you check her temperature, pulse and blood pressure now? Any new medicines?"],"hear":"\"Sleepier, drinking less. Bowels four days ago, urine smells strong. 37.8, pulse 92, BP 128/74. No new medicines.\"","mark":"Probable delirium: urine infection and constipation"},{"h":"Calm her","say":["Don't argue about the children. Say you can see she's worried and you'll keep them safe.","Lights on, curtains closed, one calm member of staff with her.","Put her radio music on; could her daughter speak to her by phone?"],"hear":"\"She did settle with the radio before.\""},{"h":"Plan for tonight","say":["Offer drinks every hour and keep a fluid chart.","I'll ask the out-of-hours GP to see her tonight for the urine and bowels.","Don't let her walk alone tonight; she's at high risk of falls.","Tomorrow the older adults team will review her stiffness, slowness and walking, and her dementia medicine."],"hear":"\"Okay. I'll call the GP back now.\"","mark":"Delirium treated first; review arranged"},{"h":"Check","say":["I know it's a hard night with two staff short.","Can you tell me back what you'll do tonight?"],"hear":"\"No haloperidol, drinks, GP for her urine and bowels, lights and music, nobody lets her walk alone.\""},{"h":"Close","say":["If she's hard to wake, breathless, falls or her temperature rises, call 999.","Ring me back at any time tonight."],"hear":"\"Thank you. I'll call if anything changes.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antipsychotic sensitivity","GP suggested haloperidol","Contraindicated in DLB: risk of severe, even fatal, reactions"],["Delirium causes","Sleepier, drinking less, bowels four days, strong urine, 37.8","Likely delirium from infection and constipation; treat the cause"],["Parkinsonism","Stiffer, slower, near-fall yesterday","Motor features worsening; falls precautions, check tone and gait at review"],["Hallucinations and dream enactment","Children for months; kicks in sleep for years","Core DLB features; frightening tonight because of delirium"],["Non-drug calming","Radio music and her daughter's voice help","First-line tonight"]]},"key":"Approving haloperidol to quieten a short-staffed night risks a fatal reaction. Edna's new distress is most likely delirium, so find and treat the cause first.","objmap":[[2],[4],[7,8],[6],[4,6]]},"c-3-3-1":{"name":"Delirium at 03:00: 4AT, cause hunt, calm the ward, haloperidol last","why":"Confirms delirium in minutes, finds what is driving it, and keeps Mr Bell safe without reaching for sedation first.","frame":["Alertness","AMT4 orientation","Attention (months backwards)","Acute change or fluctuation","Cause search","Medication decision"],"steps":[{"h":"Safety first, then meet him at eye level","say":["Grace, is anyone hurt right now? What are his latest obs, sugar and sats?","Mr Bell, I'm one of the night doctors. I'm going to sit down here so you can see my face.","You're in hospital after your hip operation. It's the middle of the night and you're safe."],"hear":"\"Then who are those men in the corner? You lot are keeping me prisoner.\"","mark":"Alertness abnormal: agitated, seeing things"},{"h":"4AT: orientation and attention","say":["How old are you, Mr Bell, and when's your birthday?","Where do you think we are just now, and what year is it?","Could you go through the months backwards for me, starting with December?"],"hear":"\"Eighty-four, May the third... This is the barracks. It's 1987. December... I don't know.\"","mark":"AMT4: two errors; months backwards not started"},{"h":"4AT: acute change, from the nurse","say":["Grace, what was he like yesterday morning?","Is this constant, or does it come and go through the day?"],"hear":"\"He was chatting away normally yesterday. Every evening he gets worse.\"","mark":"Acute change and fluctuation: 4AT well above 4, delirium likely"},{"h":"Hunt for the cause","say":["Is your hip hurting? Do you feel like you need a wee?","Grace, when did his bowels last open, and what's his fluid balance?","What's he had tonight: oxycodone, cyclizine, anything new?"],"hear":"\"He keeps wanting to go but only dribbles. Nothing opened since the op. Temp 37.9, sats 93% on air, minus 400 ml.\"","mark":"Likely causes: retention, constipation, possible infection, low oxygen, opioid and anticholinergic load"},{"h":"Rule out the traps and judge capacity","say":["Does he drink much? Any Parkinson's, or a memory diagnosis?","Mr Bell, the nurses want to keep you safe tonight. What do you think is going on?"],"hear":"\"Wife says two pints on a Sunday, nothing more. No Parkinson's.\" / \"Just let me go home to Joan.\"","mark":"Withdrawal and Lewy body risk low; lacks capacity tonight: best interests using the MCA"},{"h":"Nursing measures, and why not the MHA","say":["Can we get his glasses and hearing aids in, a lamp on, a clock in view and one nurse who stays with him?","Let's leave the cannula out for now and offer him drinks instead.","Sectioning won't help him. This is delirium, so we treat the cause and act in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act."],"hear":"\"Can't he just have some lorazepam so the bay can sleep?\"","mark":"Non-drug plan in place"},{"h":"Medication decision and handover","say":["Lorazepam usually deepens delirium and makes falls more likely, so not tonight.","If he's still a danger to himself after all this, haloperidol 0.5 mg by mouth, a 24-hour maximum written on the chart, and an ECG for his QTc.","I'll ask the FY1 for a bladder scan, sodium, calcium, CRP, cultures and a chest check, and to swap the cyclizine and trim the opioid."],"hear":"\"Okay. And if he goes for the rails again?\"","mark":"Plan agreed: treat causes, nurse him well, haloperidol only as a last resort"}],"table":{"cols":["4AT item / task","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Alertness","Restless, shouting, seeing men in the corner","Hyperactive delirium; scores on alertness"],["AMT4","Right age and birthday, wrong place and year","Disorientation; two errors"],["Attention","Cannot start the months backwards","Inattention, the core sign of delirium"],["Acute change","Normal yesterday, worse each evening","Sudden onset with fluctuation; total well above 4"],["Cause search","Dribbling urine, no bowels, 37.9, sats 93%, oxycodone and cyclizine","Several treatable drivers; the surgical team must act on them tonight"],["Medication","No Parkinson's, no heavy drinking","Haloperidol possible as a last resort; avoid lorazepam"]]},"key":"The 4AT says delirium; it doesn't say why. Excellent care hands the surgical team a named cause list, puts nursing changes first, and keeps haloperidol for failed de-escalation, never with Parkinson's or Lewy body disease.","objmap":[[2,3],[7],[6],[7],[5]]},"c-3-3-2":{"name":"Delirium or dementia? Building Margaret's timeline with Helen","why":"Uses the daughter's account to show a sudden, fluctuating change on top of a possible slow decline, and gives her an honest plan.","frame":["Onset and speed of change","Baseline function","Attention and fluctuation","Lewy body and psychotic features","Precipitants: drugs, falls, infection","Past delirium and decision-makers"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge the shock, set the purpose","say":["I'm one of the psychiatrists. I can see how frightening this morning was for you.","I'd like to hear what your mum was like before, so we can work out what's happening now. Is that alright?"],"hear":"\"She didn't even know me. Why has she suddenly gone mad?\""},{"h":"Onset and timeline","say":["When did you last speak to her when she seemed herself?","When did things change, and how fast: over hours, days or months?"],"hear":"\"Sunday she sounded normal. Tuesday a neighbour found her in the garden in her nightdress.\"","mark":"Acute onset over days identified"},{"h":"Baseline: the year before","say":["Before this, what could she do on her own: shopping, cooking, washing, driving?","Over the last year, have you noticed her repeating herself or missing bills?"],"hear":"\"She did everything herself and drove into town. But for about a year she's repeated stories and missed a gas bill.\"","mark":"Possible earlier memory decline identified"},{"h":"Fluctuation, attention and perceptions","say":["Does she change through the day: muddled at some times, clearer at others?","Has she seen things that weren't there, been very sleepy, or very restless?","Any shaking, stiffness or acting out her dreams before this?"],"hear":"\"Muddled and sleepy in the mornings, worse in the evenings. Last night she saw children in the room. No shaking.\"","mark":"Fluctuation and hallucinations identified; no Lewy body pattern before admission"},{"h":"Precipitants and past episodes","say":["Any falls, new tablets, alcohol, or changes in eating and drinking?","Has anything like this happened before, say after an operation?"],"hear":"\"She fell on Monday, no head injury. The GP started oxybutynin three weeks ago. And she was confused for three days after her hip in 2019.\"","mark":"Anticholinergic drug and previous delirium identified"},{"h":"Who decides, and safety at home","say":["Is there a lasting power of attorney, and which kind?","She lives alone; has anything worried you about money or her safety there?"],"hear":"\"Property and finance only. Nothing for health.\"","mark":"No health and welfare attorney: best interests decisions rest with the team, consulting Helen"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["This looks like delirium: a sudden confusion from the infection, and possibly the bladder tablet, which can make the brain foggy.","The year of repeated stories may mean some memory change was already there, but nobody can judge that while she's this unwell.","We'll treat the infection, ask the team to stop the oxybutynin, and check her memory again once she's recovered, with a memory clinic referral if concerns remain."],"hear":"\"So is it dementia or not? Will she end up in a home?\"","mark":"Plan shared: treat causes, reassess cognition after recovery"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Onset","Normal on Sunday, lost in the garden by Tuesday","Acute change: delirium"],["Baseline","Independent, but a year of repeated stories and a missed bill","Possible underlying cognitive decline"],["Fluctuation","Sleepy mornings, worse evenings, saw children","Fluctuating attention with hallucinations, typical of delirium"],["Precipitants","UTI, oxybutynin, a fall","Treatable causes; stop the anticholinergic"],["History","Three days' confusion after 2019 surgery","Prior delirium: high risk, and a marker for later dementia"],["Decisions","Property and finance LPA only","Health decisions are best interests decisions, with Helen consulted"]]},"key":"The skill is the baseline. Separate 'normal on Sunday' from 'a year of repeated stories': the first is delirium, the second may be early dementia, and only reassessment after recovery can tell. No placement talk until then.","objmap":[[3],[5],[5,7],[6],[7]]},"c-3-4-1":{"name":"Aggression in a care home: ABC first, tablets last","why":"Turns 'prescribe something' into a clear picture of triggers and treatable causes, with a plan that keeps Edith safe and the placement intact.","frame":["Antecedent","Behaviour","Consequence","Physical causes: pain, bowels, drugs","Senses and approach to care","Theft claim and safeguarding"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge the strain, check immediate safety","say":["Denise, thanks for ringing. It sounds like your team are having a really hard time.","Is anyone hurt now, or in danger while we talk?"],"hear":"\"Two carers have bruises. Nobody's hurt right now, but we can't go on like this.\""},{"h":"ABC: what, when, who, what next","say":["Talk me through the last few times: what time was it, who was there, and what were they doing?","What happened straight after? Does anything settle her?"],"hear":"\"Monday and Saturday about seven, when agency night staff did her wash. Thursday at bath time. She calms when Shirley sings to her.\"","mark":"Antecedent identified: early personal care by unfamiliar staff"},{"h":"Pain, bowels, drugs, infection","say":["Does she wince when you move her? When did her bowels last open?","Any new medication in the last month?","Any cough, urine smell, falls or new confusion?"],"hear":"\"She winces when we roll her, bad knee. Bowels not open for five days. GP started co-codamol three weeks ago. No cough or falls.\"","mark":"Physical drivers identified: knee pain and constipation, likely worsened by codeine"},{"h":"Senses and how care is offered","say":["Are her glasses and hearing aid working?","Could Shirley or another regular carer do her wash later in the morning, explaining each step?"],"hear":"\"Her hearing aid battery's been flat for a week, now you mention it.\"","mark":"Sensory loss identified"},{"h":"The purse: take it seriously","say":["Has the purse turned up? Has she hidden things before?","If money might be missing, please log it and tell your safeguarding lead today."],"hear":"\"She's hidden it in her pillowcase before. Maybe £40 gone. We haven't logged it.\"","mark":"Theft claim: likely misplacing, but safeguarding still to be recorded"},{"h":"Why not a tablet now","say":["An antipsychotic raises the risk of stroke and death in dementia, and it won't fix pain or a flat battery.","If she stays at serious risk after these changes, we'd consider a short course of risperidone, reviewed within weeks."],"hear":"\"So what do we do if she hurts someone before you come?\"","mark":"Medication deferred: non-drug plan first"},{"h":"Agree the plan","say":["I'll ask her GP to review the co-codamol, treat the constipation and put her on regular paracetamol.","Please change the battery, keep washes with familiar staff, start ABC charts, and step back if she becomes distressed.","I'll visit within the week. Her DoLS runs to March, so tell the supervisory body if her care plan becomes more restrictive."],"hear":"\"Okay. If it settles, I can hold off giving notice.\"","mark":"Plan agreed with the home"}],"table":{"cols":["ABC / cause","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antecedent","Washes at 07:00 by agency night staff","Fear and pain triggered by unfamiliar, early care"],["Behaviour","Hits out, refuses washing","Distress shown through behaviour, not 'aggression' for its own sake"],["Consequence","Settles when Shirley sings","Familiar, calm approach works: build it into the care plan"],["Physical","Winces on rolling; no bowels five days; new co-codamol","Pain and constipation are treatable drivers"],["Senses","Flat hearing aid battery for a week","Care feels sudden and frightening when she cannot hear"],["Theft","Purse hidden before; £40 maybe missing","Probable misplacing, but log it and follow safeguarding"]]},"key":"Behaviour in dementia is usually a message. Find the unmet need: pain, constipation, a silent hearing aid, strangers at dawn. An antipsychotic is only for severe risk or distress after that, short-term and reviewed.","objmap":[[2],[3],[6],[6],[5]]},"c-3-4-2":{"name":"Why is Frank walking? Reasons, risk, and the least restrictive plan","why":"Finds the reasons behind Frank's night walking, supports an exhausted Pat, and keeps him safe without locks or the Mental Health Act.","frame":["Purpose of the walking","Night-time and physical triggers","Risks outside","Carer strain","Capacity and least restriction"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with Frank","say":["Mr Dawson, thank you for letting us visit. Is it alright if I ask you about the other night?","Where were you heading when you went out?"],"hear":"Frank: \"The depot. Early shift. I've done it forty years.\"","mark":"Purpose identified: old work routine"},{"h":"Pattern and triggers, from Pat","say":["Pat, what time does he go, and what happens just before?","How often is he up in the night, and how does he spend the afternoon?"],"hear":"Pat: \"Between two and three. He's up two or three times to wee, and the bathroom door's right next to the front door. He dozes all afternoon.\"","mark":"Triggers identified: nocturia, door layout, daytime sleep"},{"h":"Physical causes and medicines","say":["When does he take his water tablet?","Is he in pain at night? Any new tablets?"],"hear":"Pat: \"With his tea. And his knee keeps him awake.\"","mark":"Treatable causes: evening diuretic, knee pain"},{"h":"Risks outside the home","say":["Does he still have car keys?","Has he heard of, or have you filled in, a Herbert Protocol form for the police?"],"hear":"Pat: \"I hid his keys after he tried to drive last month. I've never heard of that form.\"","mark":"Driving risk identified; DVLA and keys to address"},{"h":"Pat's own strain","say":["How are you coping, Pat? When did you last have a proper night's sleep or a break?","You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, and that can open up respite."],"hear":"Pat: \"I've no help and my back's bad. I feel terrible even thinking about a home.\"","mark":"High carer strain identified"},{"h":"Locks and sectioning: why not","say":["Mark, the Mental Health Act is for treating a mental disorder in hospital; it isn't a safety lock for dementia.","Pat, locking him in could trap him in a fire and may restrict his liberty unlawfully. Let's find safer options first."],"hear":"Mark: \"At least he'd be safe in hospital.\"","mark":"MCA and least restrictive option chosen over the MHA"},{"h":"Agree the plan","say":["I'll ask the GP to move the water tablet to the morning and treat his knee pain.","A night light and sign on the bathroom door, a door sensor that alerts Pat, more activity in the day, and a GPS device if Frank agrees.","I'll send the Herbert Protocol form, refer for a carer's assessment, and Frank shouldn't drive: we'll talk about the DVLA."],"hear":"Frank: \"I don't want a tag.\" / Pat: \"A sensor I could live with.\"","mark":"Least restrictive plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Purpose","Going to the depot for an early shift","Purposeful walking from an old routine, not random wandering"],["Triggers","Up to wee at 2–3 am; bathroom beside the front door; dozes all afternoon","Nocturia, layout and reversed sleep drive the exits"],["Physical","Evening water tablet; night knee pain","Fixable by the GP"],["Risk","Found two miles away in pyjamas; tried to drive","Getting lost, cold, roads, driving: Herbert Protocol and DVLA"],["Carer","No help, no sleep, back pain, guilt","Care at risk of breaking down: carer's assessment and respite"],["Law","Wants locks; son wants sectioning","MCA, best interests and the least restrictive option"]]},"key":"Ask why he walks before deciding how to stop him. Frank leaves at his old shift time past a bathroom door beside the front door: treat the reason, not the lock.","objmap":[[6],[1,3],[7],[5],[6]]},"c-3-4-3":{"name":"Hoarding: earn trust, find the cause, make it safe","why":"Engages Irene on her terms, looks for what lies behind the collecting, judges capacity and risk, and agrees one safe step today.","frame":["Clutter and use of rooms","Meaning of the items","Secondary causes: grief, depression, dementia, psychosis","Fire and safety risk","Capacity and self-neglect"],"steps":[{"h":"At the door: permission, not pressure","say":["Good morning, Mrs Hughes. I'm a doctor with the older people's team, and this is my colleague. Nobody's here to throw anything out.","Would you be willing to show us around, just as much as you're comfortable with?"],"hear":"\"The council sent you, didn't they? They want me out.\""},{"h":"What the things mean","say":["Tell me about your things. What makes them important?","When did collecting begin, and how does it feel to let something go?"],"hear":"\"After Ken died, three years ago. Throwing anything away feels like losing a bit of him.\"","mark":"Grief-linked hoarding identified"},{"h":"How she lives in each room","say":["Can you cook, wash and sleep the way you'd like to?","Would you mind if we rate each room with a set of pictures, so we can track it together?"],"hear":"\"I use the microwave. The hob's got boxes on it. I sleep in my armchair.\"","mark":"Rooms lost to clutter: kitchen and bed unusable"},{"h":"Mood, memory, perceptions","say":["How have your spirits been since Ken died? Any thoughts about ending your life?","Have you had trouble with memory, letters or appointments? Ever felt watched or heard voices?"],"hear":"\"Low, and I sleep badly, but I still enjoy the radio. I miss letters sometimes. No voices.\"","mark":"Low mood after bereavement; no psychosis; mild memory concern to test"},{"h":"Fire and falls","say":["I noticed the electric fire near the papers. Is the smoke alarm working?","If there was a fire tonight, how would you get out, and how long would it take?"],"hear":"\"I took the battery out because it beeped. It'd take a minute or two to get down the hall. I know that's dangerous.\"","mark":"High fire risk; she understands and weighs it"},{"h":"Capacity and her right to choose","say":["You can explain the risk clearly, and that matters. Your home is your decision.","My job is to help you be safe in it, not to clear it."],"hear":"\"Are you going to put me in a home?\"","mark":"Likely has capacity: an unwise choice is still her choice"},{"h":"Agree a first step","say":["Could we move the fire away from the papers today and clear a path to the front door?","I'd like to ask the fire service to fit a new alarm, and come back next week to check your memory and mood.","With your agreement, I'll link with your housing officer and social care so everyone works to one plan."],"hear":"\"If it's just the hallway and nothing gets thrown out without me, alright.\"","mark":"First step agreed; multi-agency plan started"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Clutter","Hob covered, bed covered, sleeps in a chair","Clutter stops normal use of rooms: meets hoarding criteria"],["Meaning","Letting go feels like losing Ken","Grief drives it; therapy should work with that"],["Secondary causes","Low since bereavement, misses letters, no voices","Screen for depression and early cognitive decline"],["Fire risk","Two-bar fire near papers; alarm battery removed; slow exit","Urgent safety step: move the heater, fit an alarm, clear the hall"],["Capacity","Can describe the fire danger herself","Likely capacitous; respect her choice and keep engaging under the Care Act"]]},"key":"Aim for safety, not a clear-out. A clear hallway and working alarm today, agreed with her, beat a forced clearance that wrecks trust and refills within months.","objmap":[[7],[5],[4],[6],[7]]},"c-3-5-1":{"name":"Late-life depression: symptoms, final acts and a real safety plan","why":"Finds a severe depression in George, uncovers his plan and final acts, and builds a needs-based safety plan with his daughter.","frame":["Core and biological symptoms","Guilt and hopelessness","Suicidal thoughts, plan and means","Final acts","Protective factors","Alcohol and physical health"],"steps":[{"h":"Open gently, give him time","say":["Mr Fenwick, thank you for coming. Your GP's worried about you. How have you been in yourself?","Is there anything you still get pleasure from, even a little?"],"hear":"\"I'm just old, that's all. I don't even bother with the garden now.\"","mark":"Anhedonia identified"},{"h":"Biological symptoms","say":["How's your sleep? Do you wake early?","Have you lost weight, and how's your energy?"],"hear":"\"Awake at four every morning. Lost about six kilos. No energy at all.\"","mark":"Biological features: early waking, weight loss, fatigue"},{"h":"Guilt and hopelessness","say":["Do you blame yourself for anything?","How do you see the future?"],"hear":"\"I should have spotted Margaret's illness sooner. There's no point now.\"","mark":"Guilt and hopelessness identified; screen for nihilistic ideas"},{"h":"Suicidal thoughts, plan and means","say":["When people feel this low, they sometimes feel life isn't worth living. Has that happened to you?","Have you thought about how you might end your life? Do you have anything at home you could use?"],"hear":"\"Every day I think I'd be better off with Margaret. Her morphine's still in the bathroom cabinet.\"","mark":"Active ideation with available means identified"},{"h":"Final acts, alcohol","say":["Your daughter says you've been giving things away. Tell me about that.","Have you written any letters or changed your will? How much are you drinking?"],"hear":"\"I gave my tools to my grandson, updated my will and wrote a letter to Susan. It's three whiskies a night now.\"","mark":"Final acts and rising alcohol identified: high concern"},{"h":"What keeps him going","say":["What has stopped you acting on these thoughts?","Who could you turn to if things got worse?"],"hear":"\"My granddaughter's wedding in May. And Bess, my dog.\"","mark":"Protective factors identified"},{"h":"Plan, and bringing Susan in","say":["I think this is a serious depression, and it's treatable at any age, cancer or not.","I'd like Susan to take the morphine to the pharmacy today. Your safety matters more than keeping this from her, so I'd like to tell her, ideally with you.","I'll ask the home treatment team to visit you in the next day, start an antidepressant with a sodium check, and we'll write a safety plan together now."],"hear":"\"Don't tell Susan.\" / Susan: \"Should I take his tablets away?\"","mark":"Needs-based safety plan agreed; family involved"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms","Low six months, no pleasure, 4 am waking, 6 kg lost","Severe depressive episode"],["Thinking","Guilt over Margaret, no point","Hopelessness, a strong risk marker"],["Ideation and means","Daily thoughts; her morphine in the cabinet","Active risk with a ready method: remove it today"],["Final acts","Tools given away, will updated, letter written","Preparation: urgent same-day response"],["Protective","Wedding in May, dog Bess","Use in the safety plan; do not let them lower the level of care"],["Context","Older widower, cancer, stopped driving, more whisky","Several static and dynamic risks combine"]]},"key":"Don't score risk; understand it. A widower with daily thoughts, his wife's morphine at hand and final acts done needs action today: means removed, Susan told, home treatment involved.","objmap":[[5],[4,7],[7],[7],[5]]},"c-3-5-2":{"name":"First mania at 71: organic until proven otherwise","why":"Confirms mania, hunts for the physical or drug cause, and puts Valerie in the right setting with the right legal footing.","frame":["Manic symptoms","Organic causes and delirium","Medication triggers","Past and family history","Risks: money, driving, exhaustion","Capacity and setting"],"steps":[{"h":"Meet her mood, then ask","say":["Mrs Moss, I'm one of the psychiatrists. You seem full of energy tonight. How have you been feeling?","How much sleep have you had these last few nights?"],"hear":"\"I've never felt so alive! I don't need sleep. I'm buying a cottage by the sea for a flower café.\"","mark":"Elevated mood, reduced need for sleep, grandiose plans"},{"h":"Orientation and attention","say":["What's today's date, and where we are?","What do you make of the staff here?"],"hear":"\"Saturday, the hospital. And those nurses are spies from the bank.\"","mark":"Oriented, so delirium less likely; persecutory ideas present"},{"h":"Medicines and physical clues, from Derek","say":["Derek, any new tablets in the last few weeks?","Any falls, headaches, weakness or changes in her speech?"],"hear":"Derek: \"The GP put her on prednisolone 40 mg two weeks ago for her chest. No falls or headaches.\"","mark":"Steroid trigger identified"},{"h":"Past and family history","say":["Has she ever had a spell of very low or very high mood?","Anyone in the family with mental illness?"],"hear":"Derek: \"After our son was born she couldn't get out of bed for months. Her mother had nerves and was in hospital once.\"","mark":"Possible past depression and family history: bipolar vulnerability"},{"h":"Risks","say":["How much has she spent, and on what?","Has she been driving?"],"hear":"Derek: \"About £8,000 online. She drove to the supermarket at three in the morning.\"","mark":"Financial and driving risk identified"},{"h":"Workup and capacity","say":["Valerie, I'd like to do blood tests and a brain scan, to be sure nothing physical is causing this.","Can you tell me what the scan is for, and what might happen if you didn't have it?"],"hear":"\"There's nothing wrong with my head. Oh, fine, bloods. A scan if I must.\"","mark":"Consents to workup; capacity for admission to be judged next"},{"h":"Plan and setting","say":["The medical team will check her bloods, thyroid and calcium, arrange a CT head, and review the steroid with her chest team rather than stop it suddenly.","Once physically clear, she needs an older adults' ward: informally if she agrees with capacity, otherwise I'll ask an AMHP for a Mental Health Act assessment.","Derek, please keep the car keys and contact her bank about spending limits with her."],"hear":"Derek: \"Is she losing her mind?\"","mark":"Organic-first plan and right setting agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mania","No sleep three nights, café plans, £8,000 spent","Manic episode with grandiosity"],["Organic","Oriented; no falls, headache or focal signs","Delirium less likely, but imaging still needed at 71"],["Drug trigger","Prednisolone 40 mg for two weeks","Probable steroid-induced mania: review with the medical team"],["History","Months in bed after childbirth; mother hospitalised","Possible bipolar vulnerability unmasked"],["Risk","Night driving, spending, paranoid about staff","Protect money, keys and physical health"],["Setting","Agrees to tests, reluctant about a ward","Older adult ward; MCA for tests, MHA if she refuses needed admission"]]},"key":"Never send a first mania after 60 straight to a psychiatric ward. The steroid is the likely trigger, but bloods and a brain scan come first, and the steroid is reviewed, not stopped abruptly.","objmap":[[4],[7],[7],[7],[1]]},"c-3-5-3":{"name":"Neighbours through the wall: late-onset psychosis, done safely","why":"Maps Edna's beliefs and perceptions without arguing, screens for organic causes, catches a real gas danger, and agrees a plan she can live with.","frame":["Delusional beliefs","Perceptions: voices, smell","Organic screen","Mood","Risk: gas, fire, neighbours","Sensory loss and isolation"],"steps":[{"h":"Adapt for hearing, let her tell it","say":["(Facing her, slowly, in good light) Mrs Whitlock, can you hear me alright like this?","Tell me what's been happening with the neighbours, from the beginning."],"hear":"\"Six months ago a new couple moved in. Since then they pump gas through my bedroom wall and come in at night to move my things.\""},{"h":"The belief in full","say":["How do you know it's them? Why would they do this?","Is there any other way of explaining it, do you think?"],"hear":"\"Who else would it be? The police did nothing. I'm not mad, I just want it to stop.\"","mark":"Fixed persecutory belief with partition features identified"},{"h":"Perceptions, sense by sense","say":["Do you ever hear them when nobody's in the room? What do they say?","Do you notice any smells or tastes that worry you? Any headaches?"],"hear":"\"At night through the wall they laugh about me. There's a sweet, sickly smell in the bedroom, and I wake with a headache.\"","mark":"Auditory hallucinations identified; smell and headaches need a physical check"},{"h":"Organic and mood screen","say":["How's your memory? Do you lose things or miss appointments?","Do you ever see things others can't, or have any shaking? How are your spirits?"],"hear":"\"I forgot one GP appointment. Nothing like that. I sleep badly but I've no wish to end things.\"","mark":"No Lewy body or depressive pattern; cognition to be tested"},{"h":"Risk: gas, fire, neighbours","say":["You've taped the vents. Do you use the gas fire with them sealed?","Have you thought of doing anything to the neighbours to make it stop?"],"hear":"\"Every evening. I've thought of giving them a piece of my mind, but I've never gone round.\"","mark":"Carbon monoxide risk identified; low risk to neighbours"},{"h":"Senses and isolation","say":["When did you last wear your hearing aids? Who do you see in a week?","If we got your hearing aids working again, would you try them?"],"hear":"\"A year ago, they whistled. My son's in Canada. I see almost nobody.\"","mark":"Sensory loss and isolation: maintaining factors identified"},{"h":"Plan she can accept","say":["I can't explain the voices the way you do, but I can see how frightening this is, and I want to help you sleep and feel safer.","Please don't use the gas fire until it's checked; I'll arrange a gas safety check and a carbon monoxide alarm today.","I'd like blood tests, a memory check, a hearing aid review, and to offer a very small dose of medicine with a heart tracing first."],"hear":"\"I'm not taking pills that turn me into a zombie.\"","mark":"Plan agreed: organic screen, safety check, sensory help, low-dose antipsychotic offered"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief","Neighbours pump gas and move her things","Systematised persecutory, partition-type delusion"],["Perceptions","Laughing through the wall at night","Auditory hallucinations: very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis (VLOSLP) likely"],["Organic","One missed appointment, no visual hallucinations or tremor","Cognitive testing and bloods still required"],["Gas and smell","Sweet smell, headaches, vents sealed, gas fire nightly","Possible real carbon monoxide exposure: urgent check"],["Senses","No hearing aids for a year; sees nobody","Treat deafness and isolation; they keep the belief going"],["Mood and risk","Poor sleep, no suicidal thoughts, no plan to confront","Low risk to self and others today"]]},"key":"Neither argue nor agree; say what you can see. And a sweet smell with morning headaches in a sealed room with a gas fire may be real carbon monoxide: make that safe before calling it delusional.","objmap":[[1],[3],[2,3],[1,5],[7]]},"c-3-5-4":{"name":"Anxious, forgetful, on diazepam: anxiety, depression or dementia?","why":"Separates late-life anxiety and low mood from early dementia, finds the diazepam and alcohol effect, and agrees a safe plan with Raymond and Pat.","frame":["Anxiety pattern","Depressive features","Memory and daily function","Diazepam and alcohol","Physical causes","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Permission and his main worry","say":["Mr Clarke, I'd like to see you on your own first, then ask Pat to join us. Is that alright?","What worries you most at the moment?"],"hear":"\"Money, Pat's health, burglars. That something bad will happen and it'll be my fault.\"","mark":"Generalised worry identified"},{"h":"Onset and checking","say":["When did the worry start, and what was going on then?","What goes through your mind when you check the doors?"],"hear":"\"Nine months ago, after a heart scare that came to nothing. I check four or five times before bed.\"","mark":"Anxiety onset after a health scare; checking driven by worry"},{"h":"Mood and suicide","say":["How's your sleep and your mood? Have you stopped doing things you used to enjoy?","Have you ever felt life isn't worth living?"],"hear":"\"Poor sleep. I've stopped bowls. Low some days, but nothing like that.\"","mark":"Depressive features present; no suicidal ideation"},{"h":"Memory: example and daily function","say":["Give me an example of something you forgot recently.","Pat, has he got lost, or had trouble with bills or cooking?"],"hear":"Pat: \"He forgets what I said minutes ago, but he's never got lost and he still does all the bills.\"","mark":"Daily function intact: dementia less likely; attention-driven forgetting"},{"h":"Diazepam and alcohol","say":["How much diazepam do you take, and how do you feel the next morning?","How much do you drink, and ever alongside the tablets?"],"hear":"\"One or two 2 mg most days, sometimes 5 mg at night. Woozy in the mornings; nearly fell on the stairs. A whisky with it some nights.\"","mark":"Benzodiazepine harm identified: sedation, falls, memory, with alcohol"},{"h":"Physical causes and driving","say":["Any palpitations, weight loss or chest pain since the scare?","Do you still drive? Even small doses of diazepam can slow your reactions."],"hear":"\"I drive to the shops and take Pat to the hospital.\"","mark":"Driving with sedation: advise not to drive if drowsy"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["I understand your father had Alzheimer's, and that's frightening. Today this looks more like anxiety with low mood, and the diazepam is likely adding to the forgetting.","We'll do a memory test and bloods now, and repeat the test in a few months once the worry and the diazepam are down.","I'd suggest talking therapy and an SSRI, and a slow reduction of diazepam agreed with you: no whisky with it meanwhile."],"hear":"\"The diazepam's the only thing that helps. You're not taking it away.\"","mark":"Plan agreed: test, treat anxiety, slow taper, retest"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Anxiety","Worry about money, health and burglars; door checking","Late-life generalised anxiety; checking is worry-driven, not OCD-first"],["Mood","Low some days, stopped bowls, poor sleep","Co-existing depressive symptoms: treat both"],["Memory","Forgets recent talk; never lost; manages bills","Function preserved: points away from dementia for now"],["Drugs","Diazepam most days, morning wooziness, near-fall, whisky","Avoidable cause of poor memory and falls: taper slowly"],["Risk","No suicidal thoughts; still driving","Advise on driving while drowsy; review falls"]]},"key":"Never label dementia while anxiety, low mood and a daily benzodiazepine cloud the picture. Test now as a baseline, treat what is treatable, and let the retest decide.","objmap":[[1,2],[5],[5,7],[7],[2,7]]},"c-3-6-1":{"name":"Two decisions, two tests: where to live and managing money","why":"Assesses Dorothy's capacity separately for residence and for finances, after removing barriers, and gives the team a lawful next step.","frame":["Impairment of mind or brain","Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up for her best","say":["Mrs Hughes, have you got your glasses and hearing aids in? Is now a good time to talk?","I'd like to talk about two separate things: where you live after hospital, and your money."],"hear":"\"I've got them in. If this is about a home, I'm going home to my cat.\"","mark":"Communicates clearly; no current delirium per ward"},{"h":"Residence: understand the options","say":["Why did you come into hospital?","There are two choices: home with carers four times a day, or a care home. What do you know about each?"],"hear":"\"I came in with a water infection. I'd have carers, I suppose. A home is where they put you to die.\"","mark":"Partial understanding of the options"},{"h":"Residence: use and weigh the risks","say":["Before you came in, what happened when you fell?","If you fell at night at home again, how would you get help?"],"hear":"\"I sat down on the floor for a few hours. I'd... I don't know. I'd manage.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh night-time fall risk: residence capacity in doubt"},{"h":"Her values","say":["What matters most to you about home?","Who would look after Smudge if you were away?"],"hear":"\"Smudge, my cat. I've lived there fifty years. My husband died there.\"","mark":"Wishes and values recorded"},{"h":"Finances: understand and weigh","say":["How much is your pension each week? What happens to your bills?","If someone asked to borrow five thousand pounds, what would you do?"],"hear":"\"About a hundred pounds? Bills go in a drawer, they make mistakes. I'd lend it to anyone who needed it.\"","mark":"Cannot understand or weigh finances: likely lacks financial capacity"},{"h":"Bank card and attorney","say":["Who else uses your bank card or knows your PIN?","Did you ever sign papers giving someone power to help with decisions?"],"hear":"\"The boy next door does my shopping with my card. I signed something at the solicitor's years ago; Michael knows.\"","mark":"Possible financial abuse; possible LPA to verify"},{"h":"Advise Sister Bell","say":["I'll document two separate assessments. On both, she can't use or weigh the key information, because of her Alzheimer's.","Please check with Michael and the Office of the Public Guardian whether an LPA is registered. If not, we hold a best interests meeting with her wishes, Michael, and an IMCA if needed.","If she moves to a care home under supervision she can't leave, we'll need a DoLS application. The bank card use needs a safeguarding concern today."],"hear":"Sister Bell: \"So she can't go home?\"","mark":"Best interests route set; DoLS and safeguarding flagged"}],"table":{"cols":["Capacity element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Impairment","Moderate Alzheimer's; no delirium today","Diagnostic part met"],["Understand","Knows why admitted; unclear on care options; guesses pension","Partial for residence, poor for money"],["Retain","Holds the conversation through the interview","Retention adequate for the decision"],["Use or weigh","Can't plan for a night-time fall; would lend £5,000","Fails on both decisions: inability linked to dementia"],["Communicate","Clear, consistent wish to go home to Smudge","Communicates; her wishes carry weight in best interests"]]},"key":"Capacity is decision-specific. Wanting to go home despite risk is not incapacity; being unable to weigh how she'd get help after a fall is. Assess residence and money separately, and write each down.","objmap":[[3],[1,2],[1,2],[1,4],[7]]},"c-3-6-2":{"name":"Not driving home today: the DVLA conversation","why":"Stops an unsafe drive, explains Frank's legal duty and the doctor's role honestly, and keeps his independence through practical alternatives.","frame":["Evidence of driving risk","DVLA rules for dementia","The patient's legal duty","The doctor's duty and confidentiality","Alternatives and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Stop the drive home, kindly","say":["Mr Dawson, before you go, can we talk about the car? It matters for your safety today.","Karen, could you drive your dad home?"],"hear":"\"I'll drive myself home. Fifty-five years and not a scratch.\""},{"h":"Gather the driving evidence","say":["Has driving felt any different lately?","Karen, have you noticed anything when he's been driving?"],"hear":"\"I went the wrong way round a roundabout. They'd changed the lines.\" / Karen: \"He got lost coming back from the garden centre.\"","mark":"Current driving errors identified"},{"h":"The DVLA rule and his duty","say":["With a diagnosis of Alzheimer's, the law says you must tell the DVLA yourself. Did you know that?","They'll gather reports and decide. Some people keep a car licence for a while, often with checks.","But given the roundabout and getting lost, my advice is to stop driving from today."],"hear":"\"I didn't know. You can't stop me; you're taking my licence away.\"","mark":"Legal duty explained; advice to stop given"},{"h":"Anger with empathy","say":["I can hear how big a loss this is. Driving's been part of who you are.","This isn't about your skill over fifty-five years; it's how the illness affects judgement on the road."],"hear":"\"What if I only drive to the shops?\"","mark":"Partial-driving bargain answered: no"},{"h":"Confidentiality and the doctor's role","say":["I'll write my advice in your notes and in a letter to you and your GP.","If you kept driving against advice, I'd have to tell the DVLA myself. I'd always tell you first."],"hear":"Karen: \"Can't you just leave it out? It'll break him.\"","mark":"GMC duty to disclose explained"},{"h":"Keeping his life going","say":["Where does the car take you each week?","Could Bill give you a lift to the club? There's also a free bus pass and local community transport."],"hear":"\"The club on Tuesdays, shopping and church. Bill lives round the corner.\"","mark":"Alternatives identified: lifts, bus pass, community transport"},{"h":"Close and follow up","say":["So: Karen drives you home, you contact the DVLA this week, and I'll see you again in clinic.","Whether you get the licence back is the DVLA's decision; they'll tell you clearly."],"hear":"\"Will I get my licence back?\"","mark":"Advice documented; follow-up booked"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Driving risk","Wrong way round a roundabout; lost and called from a lay-by","Unsafe now: advise stopping today"],["DVLA rules","Car licence only; didn't know he had to report","He must notify the DVLA; they decide on the licence"],["His duty","Plans to drive home anyway","Explain the legal duty and offer a lift home"],["Doctor's duty","Daughter asks you to leave it out","If he drives against advice, inform the DVLA after telling him (GMC)"],["Alternatives","Club, shops, church; friend Bill nearby","Keep his social life going without the car"]]},"key":"The diagnosis triggers his duty to tell the DVLA; your advice follows the evidence. Be clear he must not drive now, never collude with silence, and tell him before you ever inform the DVLA yourself.","objmap":[[1],[3,5],[5],[1,2],[6]]},"c-3-6-3":{"name":"Money, cold and fear: spotting financial abuse and acting today","why":"Hears Margaret's story alone, judges capacity for the relevant decisions, and makes a same-day safeguarding referral that respects what she wants.","frame":["Signs of abuse and neglect","Her account and fears","Capacity for the specific decisions","Her wishes","Care Act safeguarding","Interim safety"],"steps":[{"h":"See her alone","say":["Mrs Ellis, would it be alright if Jo stepped out so we can talk on our own?","It's very cold in here. How long has the heating been off?"],"hear":"\"Course, love. A week or two. Jo's a treasure.\"","mark":"Neglect signs: no heating, empty fridge"},{"h":"The money","say":["Who looks after your bank card and pension?","Is there money left at the end of the week?"],"hear":"\"Liam has my card. I wrote the PIN down for him; he needs it more than me. He says there's nothing left.\"","mark":"Possible financial abuse identified"},{"h":"Fear and behaviour at home","say":["What happens when you ask Liam about the money?","Do you ever feel frightened at home? Has he ever hurt you?"],"hear":"\"He shouts and bangs doors. He's never hit me. Sometimes I'm frightened.\"","mark":"Emotional abuse and fear identified"},{"h":"Capacity for this decision","say":["Do you know how much your pension is each week?","What could happen if someone else has your card and PIN?"],"hear":"\"I don't know what it is. He wouldn't do anything bad.\"","mark":"Likely lacks capacity to manage finances; to be formally recorded"},{"h":"What she wants","say":["What would you like to happen?","If I talk to people who can help, what would worry you most?"],"hear":"\"I want Liam to stay, but I want my heating on and my own money for the hairdresser. If he gets in trouble, I'll have nobody.\"","mark":"Wishes recorded: keep Liam, regain warmth and money"},{"h":"Explain the referral","say":["I'm worried you're cold, short of food, and not getting your money. I need to tell the council's safeguarding team today.","This is about keeping you safe and warm, not about throwing Liam out."],"hear":"\"I don't want social workers poking around. Will the police get involved?\"","mark":"Patient informed of referral"},{"h":"Phone the duty social worker, set interim safety","say":["I'm raising a safeguarding concern about Mrs Ellis, 81, vascular dementia: possible financial abuse and neglect by her grandson, who holds her card and PIN.","Today she needs heating on, food in, and her bank told to protect the account. Could you look at deputyship if needed, and decide on police involvement?","I've written down what she said in her own words."],"hear":"Social worker: \"We'll open an enquiry and visit tomorrow.\"","mark":"Same-day referral made; interim plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Signs","Cold flat, empty fridge, heating off two weeks","Neglect alongside financial loss"],["Finances","Grandson holds card and PIN; nothing left","Possible financial abuse: Care Act s42 concern"],["Fear","Shouting, banging doors, sometimes frightened","Emotional abuse: raise the risk level"],["Capacity","Doesn't know her pension; trusts Liam with everything","Probably lacks financial capacity; record decision-specific findings"],["Wishes","Wants Liam to stay, plus warmth and her own money","Safeguarding must be personal: protect without removing what she values"]]},"key":"She can want Liam to stay and still need protecting. Report the same day, tell her why, and aim at her money and warmth, not at evicting her grandson.","objmap":[[7],[3],[7],[7],[7]]},"c-3-6-4":{"name":"Attorney, best interests and planning ahead for Albert","why":"Shows Irene what her LPA lets her decide, how the care home choice is made in Albert's best interests, and starts planning for pneumonia and resuscitation.","frame":["Which LPA, and is it registered","Attorney powers and limits","Best interests and his past wishes","LPA versus advance decision","Advance care planning","Carer strain, funding and DoLS"],"steps":[{"h":"Hear her first","say":["Mrs Price, this sounds like a very hard time. Tell me how things are at home.","Which powers of attorney do you have, and were they registered?"],"hear":"\"Both, done at the solicitor's five years ago and registered. The copies are at home.\"","mark":"Both LPAs held and registered"},{"h":"What the LPA lets her do","say":["The health and welfare LPA lets you make decisions only when Albert can't make that decision himself.","We'll first check his capacity for where he lives. If he lacks it, you decide, but always in his best interests."],"hear":"\"So I can just decide he goes into a home?\"","mark":"Attorney powers and limits explained"},{"h":"His wishes and values","say":["What did Albert say about care homes when he was well?","What mattered most to him in life?"],"hear":"\"He made me promise never to put him in a home. He loved chapel, his garden and brass bands, and hated fuss.\"","mark":"Past wishes and values recorded"},{"h":"Best interests, and the promise","say":["His wishes count heavily, but they're one part. His safety and your ability to care count too.","Keeping a promise you can no longer keep safely isn't a failure. We'll hold a best interests meeting, with his brother's view heard."],"hear":"\"I promised I'd never put him in a home.\"","mark":"Best interests process explained"},{"h":"Life-sustaining treatment and advance decisions","say":["Did he ever write down treatments he'd refuse?","In the LPA there's a section about life-sustaining treatment. Do you know if it was filled in?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing written. I don't remember that section.\"","mark":"No advance decision; life-sustaining authority to check"},{"h":"Pneumonia and resuscitation","say":["If he got pneumonia, we'd think together about antibiotics, staying at the care home, or hospital, based on what he'd want. He hated hospital.","CPR is a medical decision about whether it could work. You can't demand it, but your view of his wishes shapes the plan we write down."],"hear":"\"Will they just let him die if he gets pneumonia? Can I insist he's resuscitated?\"","mark":"Advance care planning started"},{"h":"Her strain, funding and DoLS","say":["You can't lift him now, and that matters. We'll ask for a carer's assessment and a social care financial assessment, and screen for NHS continuing healthcare.","If he moves, the home may need a DoLS authorisation. I'll set up the meeting this week."],"hear":"\"Who pays for the care home?\"","mark":"Funding, DoLS and next steps agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["LPA type","Both LPAs, registered, copies at home","Valid; ask to see the health and welfare one"],["Powers","Wants to decide on a care home","She can, once he lacks capacity, in his best interests only"],["Past wishes","Promise never to go in a home; chapel, garden, bands","Weigh heavily, but not binding against his safety"],["Advance decision","None written; LPA section unclear","She cannot refuse life-sustaining treatment unless that section was signed"],["Care planning","Fears about pneumonia and CPR; hated hospital","Agree a written plan; CPR is a clinical decision"],["Strain and funding","Can't lift him; asks who pays","Carer's assessment, means test, CHC check, DoLS if he moves"]]},"key":"An attorney steps in only when he lacks capacity for that decision, and then must act in his best interests. She cannot refuse life-sustaining treatment for him unless the LPA expressly gave her that power.","objmap":[[5],[2],[3],[6],[7]]},"c-4-1-1":{"name":"Is therapy right for Leah, and which one? Five suitability checks","why":"Turns a vague request to be 'fixed' into a shared picture of her pattern and a therapy matched to it.","frame":["Focus and goal","Psychological mindedness","Motivation for change","Coping and contraindications","Therapy match"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the frame","say":["Hello Leah, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. We have about 50 minutes today.","This is an assessment, not therapy yet. By the end we'll agree together what might help.","What you say stays within the care team, unless I'm worried about your safety or someone else's."],"hear":"'Fine. I just want someone to fix me.'"},{"h":"Her goal and the pattern","say":["What made you ask for therapy now?","You said the same kind of relationship keeps happening. Tell me about the most recent one.","If therapy worked, what would be different in a year?"],"hear":"'He said I was cold and walked out in March. They always end up leaving.'","mark":"Repeating relationship pattern identified"},{"h":"Psychological mindedness","say":["When a relationship goes wrong, what do you tell yourself about why?","Does that feeling of being left remind you of anything from earlier in your life?","What part do you think you play, if any?"],"hear":"'Mum left when I was eight. I learned to keep everyone happy, and I still do it.'","mark":"Psychological mindedness identified"},{"h":"Motivation and past therapy","say":["How did the guided self-help go? What helped, and what didn't?","Therapy may ask you to look hard at your own part in this. How does that sit with you?"],"hear":"'The worksheets never touched the relationship stuff. I want to stop repeating it.'","mark":"Motivation for change identified"},{"h":"Coping, barriers and safety","say":["Therapy can feel worse before it feels better. How do you usually cope with strong feelings?","Do you drink or use drugs to cope? How much at the moment?","Have you ever heard voices or felt very high? Any thoughts about ending your life now?","Is anything going on, like work, housing or court, that could get in the way of weekly sessions?"],"hear":"'I run and ring my sister. Two glasses of wine three nights a week. After the break-up I wished I wouldn't wake up, but not now.'","mark":"No contraindication identified"},{"h":"Share a simple formulation","say":["Can I try to put this together and you tell me if I've got it wrong?","You put the other person first and let your friends drop away. Then you feel used, go quiet, and he leaves. That matches what you learned as a child.","Does that fit?"],"hear":"'That's exactly it. I've never heard it put like that.'"},{"h":"Explain options with reasons","say":["No therapy fixes someone from the outside; you'd be working on this with the therapist.","Cognitive analytic therapy looks at relationship patterns like yours, including how they show up with the therapist. It's usually about 16 weekly sessions with a trained therapist.","Psychodynamic therapy is another option. Individual CBT fits less well, as it targets symptoms more than patterns."],"hear":"'Which one is best? Just tell me and I'll do it.'"},{"h":"Shared decision and close","say":["My view is that a relationship-focused therapy fits you best. What do you think?","I'll discuss this with the team, write to your GP and copy the letter to you. There is likely to be a wait.","Until then, here are the numbers to use if things get worse: your GP, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123."],"hear":"'I suppose you'll reject me too, like everyone else.'","mark":"Shared plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Suitability point","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Focus and goal","Same relationship pattern, wants to stop repeating it","A clear relational focus suits a relationship-based therapy"],["Psychological mindedness","Links being left now with her mother leaving at eight","Can reflect on past and present; good sign"],["Motivation for change","Self-help 'never touched the relationship stuff'","Wants change, not just relief; 'fix me' is a starting point"],["Coping and contraindications","Runs, rings her sister; modest drinking; no psychosis; no current suicidal thoughts","Can tolerate the strain of therapy; no reason to wait"],["Therapy match","Agrees with the formulation","CAT or psychodynamic therapy fits better than symptom-focused CBT"]]},"key":"Suitability rests on whether she can see her pattern and wants to change it, not on how distressed she is. Treat 'fix me' as something to explore together, not a reason to turn her away.","objmap":[[2,4],[3],[2,8],[1,2],[8]]},"c-4-1-2":{"name":"The watch and the friend request: holding a boundary kindly","why":"Declines the gift and online contact without shaming Daniel, and turns his fear of the ending into a plan.","frame":["The gift","The contact request","Dependency","Risk at the ending","Supervision and record"],"steps":[{"h":"Pause and acknowledge","say":["Thank you, Daniel. I can see a lot of thought went into this.","Before I say anything about it, can we take a moment to talk about it?"],"hear":"'Please take it. I'll be really hurt if you don't.'"},{"h":"Explore what the gift means","say":["What did you hope this would say to me?","It comes as we're nearing the end of our sessions. I wonder if that's connected."],"hear":"'I want you to remember me. Everyone leaves in the end.'","mark":"Fear of loss identified"},{"h":"Decline the watch, with the reason","say":["I can't accept the watch. That isn't about you or how kind it is.","Keeping gifts out of our work keeps this space safe and about you.","What you're telling me with it really matters, and I'd rather we keep talking about that."],"hear":"'So I'm just another case to you?'","mark":"Gift declined kindly"},{"h":"Decline the friend request","say":["I don't connect with patients on social media. It protects your privacy and keeps our roles clear.","I'll decline the request, and I wanted you to hear the reason from me first."],"hear":"'Is it against the rules? Will you get in trouble?'","mark":"Contact request declined"},{"h":"Explore dependency","say":["You said I'm the only person who understands you. Who else is in your life?","What gets in the way of seeing them?"],"hear":"'My sister Claire keeps inviting me round. I tell her I'm busy.'","mark":"Dependency identified"},{"h":"Check risk","say":["When you said you don't know what you'll do when this stops, what did you mean?","Has any part of you thought about hurting yourself or ending your life?"],"hear":"'No, nothing like that. I just dread the empty evenings.'","mark":"No suicidal thoughts identified"},{"h":"Plan the ending","say":["We have four sessions left. Let's use them to prepare for finishing, rather than just counting down.","What would help after we stop: Claire, church, a group, your GP?","We'll write down what to do if things get hard, so you leave with a plan."],"hear":"'Could we use the last few to work that out?'"},{"h":"Be open about the record and supervision","say":["I'll note in your record that you offered a gift and I declined, and what we talked about.","I'll also talk it through with my supervisor. That's routine and it helps me help you."],"hear":"'You're going to write down that I tried to give you a present?'","mark":"Documentation and supervision explained"}],"table":{"cols":["Boundary issue","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The gift","Expensive watch; 'I want you to remember me'","Boundary crossing that expresses gratitude and fear of loss"],["The contact request","Friend request 'to keep in touch after'","Blurs roles and privacy; decline and explain"],["Dependency","'You're the only one who understands me'; avoids his sister","Over-reliance on the therapist; widen his support before the end"],["Risk at the ending","Dreads empty evenings; no suicidal thoughts","Low current risk, but recheck as the ending nears"],["Supervision and record","Worried it will be written down","Be open: recording and supervision protect both of you"]]},"key":"Declining is only half the job. Explore what the gift and request mean, because his fear of the ending is the real issue; accepting to spare his feelings, or refusing coldly, both miss it.","objmap":[[4],[2],[6],[7],[6,8]]},"c-4-1-3":{"name":"Fresh cuts in session: assess, then share with her, not around her","why":"Keeps Chloe safe and in therapy while being honest that her care coordinator must know.","frame":["Physical injury","Intent and substances","Trigger and safety at home","Current risk and supports","Sharing information"],"steps":[{"h":"Respond calmly and check the wounds","say":["Thank you for showing me, Chloe. That took courage.","Can I look at the cuts so I know they're healing properly?","Are they still bleeding, red, hot or very sore?"],"hear":"'They stopped bleeding last night. I cleaned them myself.'","mark":"Wounds checked"},{"h":"Understand last night","say":["Talk me through what happened last night.","When you cut, what did you want to happen?","Did you take any tablets, alcohol or drugs as well?"],"hear":"'I didn't want to die. I just wanted the noise in my head to stop. No tablets, nothing.'","mark":"Low suicidal intent identified"},{"h":"Ask about the argument and safety at home","say":["What happened in the row with Josh?","Has Josh ever hurt you, or made you feel frightened?"],"hear":"'He'd been drinking, got in my face and threw my phone at the wall. He pushed me once before.'","mark":"Possible domestic abuse identified"},{"h":"Check risk now and what keeps her going","say":["How are you feeling right now? Any thoughts of hurting yourself again or ending your life?","Where will you be tonight?","Who or what keeps you going?"],"hear":"'No, not now. Josh is at his brother's tonight. My mum, my job and Ellie keep me going.'","mark":"Reasons for living identified"},{"h":"Explore the fear of telling","say":["You asked me not to tell Priya. What worries you about her knowing?","What has happened before when people found out?"],"hear":"'She'll think I'm getting worse. Will they section me or stop my therapy?'"},{"h":"Explain what must be shared and agree how","say":["I can't keep this only between us. I work as part of your team, and Priya needs to know to help keep you safe.","This isn't about sectioning or stopping therapy. It's about support.","You can choose how: we ring her together now, or you tell her and I follow up today."],"hear":"'Could we tell her together?'","mark":"Agreed plan to share"},{"h":"Make a safety plan","say":["Let's write down your warning signs and what you can do instead of cutting.","Who can you contact: Ellie, your mum, Priya, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123?","Would you like details of domestic abuse support? I'll also need to think about safeguarding with the team."],"hear":"'I can text Ellie. I'll take the numbers.'"},{"h":"Close and follow up","say":["Keep the cuts clean; see your GP or a pharmacist if they look infected.","I'll discuss today in supervision and we'll look at whether the therapy plan needs changing.","I'll see you next week as usual."],"hear":"'So I can still come next week?'"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Physical injury","Superficial cuts, cleaned, no bleeding","No urgent treatment; advise wound care"],["Intent and substances","Wanted the noise to stop; no tablets or alcohol","Low suicidal intent; no overdose to assess"],["Trigger and safety at home","Josh drunk, threw her phone, pushed her before","Possible domestic abuse; offer support, consider safeguarding"],["Current risk and supports","No thoughts now; Josh away tonight; mum, job, Ellie","Supports in place, but past self-harm still raises future risk"],["Sharing information","Fears being judged or sectioned","Explain the limits honestly and agree how to tell Priya"]]},"key":"Confidentiality is not a promise to keep secrets. Say plainly what must be shared and why, then give her as much control as possible over how, so she stays in therapy.","objmap":[[1],[2,5],[2,3],[6],[7]]},"c-4-2-1":{"name":"CBT for Ryan in ten minutes: the hot cross bun in his own words","why":"Shows that CBT takes his losses seriously and targets the thoughts and habits that keep him stuck.","frame":["Situation","Thoughts","Feelings and body","Behaviour","Thinking errors"],"steps":[{"h":"Ask what he already thinks","say":["I know you've got a bus in ten minutes, so I'll keep this short.","What have you heard about CBT so far?"],"hear":"'Isn't it just positive thinking? My problems are real.'"},{"h":"Start with the real situation","say":["Losing your job and your girlfriend leaving are real losses. CBT doesn't pretend otherwise.","It looks at what happens after something hard: what you think, feel and do, and how those feed each other."],"hear":"'So you're not saying it's all in my head?'","mark":"Situation identified"},{"h":"Find his thoughts","say":["When you think about the job, what goes through your mind?","And about the future?"],"hear":"'I'm a failure. Nothing ever works out for me. I'll never get another job.'","mark":"Thoughts identified"},{"h":"Link feelings, body and behaviour","say":["When you think that, how do you feel, in your mood and in your body?","And what do you end up doing?"],"hear":"'Gutted and tired. I've stopped applying, I sleep in the day, and I've stopped five-a-side.'","mark":"Vicious cycle identified"},{"h":"Name the thinking errors using his words","say":["'I'm a failure' is labelling: one event becomes who you are.","'Nothing ever works out' is overgeneralising, and 'I'll never get another job' is predicting the future as if it's certain.","CBT doesn't swap these for cheerful thoughts. It tests them against the evidence."],"hear":"'I do that all the time.'","mark":"Thinking errors identified"},{"h":"Explain the practical side","say":["Usually it's weekly sessions, often 12 to 20, with a trained therapist through NHS Talking Therapies.","Early on, we'd get you doing small things again, like one game of five-a-side, because doing less makes mood worse.","There's work between sessions; that's where most of the change happens."],"hear":"'Homework? Really?'"},{"h":"Check risk and mention alternatives","say":["When people feel this low, some of them think life isn't worth living. Has that happened to you?","CBT isn't the only option: counselling, interpersonal therapy or an antidepressant are also possible."],"hear":"'Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to wake up. No plans. My mum would be devastated. I don't want tablets yet.'","mark":"Passive thoughts, no plan identified"},{"h":"Let him choose and close","say":["In your words, how would you explain CBT to a mate?","Would you like me to refer you?","If things get worse while you wait, contact your GP, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"'Alright, I'll give it a go.'","mark":"Shared decision made"}],"table":{"cols":["CBT element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Situation","Lost his job; girlfriend left","Real losses; CBT works with them, not against them"],["Thoughts","'I'm a failure', 'I'll never get another job'","Negative view of self and future"],["Feelings and body","Gutted and tired","Low mood that the thoughts keep feeding"],["Behaviour","Stopped applying, sleeps in the day, no football","Withdrawal deepens low mood; target for behavioural activation"],["Thinking errors","Labelling, overgeneralising, predicting the future","Can be tested against evidence in sessions"]]},"key":"CBT does not say his problems are imagined. It accepts the losses are real and targets the thoughts and withdrawal that keep him stuck. Every illustration should come from his own words.","objmap":[[2,3],[2],[1],[6],[7]]},"c-4-2-2":{"name":"Facing the fear of dogs step by step","why":"Explains why avoidance keeps the phobia going and agrees a first step Amira feels able to take.","frame":["Fear-avoidance cycle","Relaxation","Fear hierarchy (SUDS)","Graded exposure","Practice and pace"],"steps":[{"h":"Reassure and ask what happens","say":["Nobody is going to make you do anything today. We're just going to talk.","Tell me what happens in your body when you see a dog."],"hear":"'My heart races, my hands shake and I feel sick.'"},{"h":"Screen before planning","say":["When did the fear start?","Have you ever fainted at the sight of a dog, blood or needles?","Do you get panic attacks out of the blue, or low mood?"],"hear":"'A dog chased me when I was seven. I've never fainted. No panic out of nowhere, and my mood's fine.'","mark":"Not blood-injury type identified"},{"h":"Explain the fear-avoidance cycle","say":["When you take the long way round, what happens to the fear?","Avoiding brings quick relief, but your brain never learns that the dog wouldn't have hurt you. So the fear grows."],"hear":"'It drops straight away, but it's worse next time.'","mark":"Fear-avoidance cycle identified"},{"h":"Introduce relaxation","say":["First you'd learn a way to calm your body, with slow breathing and relaxing your muscles.","It's hard to be deeply relaxed and very frightened at the same time, so this helps at each step."],"hear":"'So I'd practise that first?'","mark":"Relaxation step agreed"},{"h":"Build the hierarchy together","say":["Let's score some situations from 0, no fear, to 100, the worst fear you can imagine.","A photo of a dog? A video? A dog across the street? A small dog on a lead? Stroking one?"],"hear":"'Photo 30, video 45, across the street 70, small dog on a lead 85, stroking one 95.'","mark":"Fear hierarchy built"},{"h":"Explain graded exposure and her control","say":["We start at the bottom. You stay with each step until the fear falls by about half, and repeat it until it feels easy.","Then you move up, at a pace you agree. You're in charge.","Fear rises, peaks and then settles if you stay with it. It doesn't keep climbing."],"hear":"'What if I panic and can't stop?'"},{"h":"Answer the tablet question","say":["Tablets aren't the main treatment for this.","Sedatives like diazepam can stop your brain learning that you're safe, and they can be habit-forming."],"hear":"'Can't I just have a tablet for it?'"},{"h":"Agree a first step and close","say":["This week, could you look at dog photos for about 20 minutes a day and note your score before and after?","Later on, your friend Sara's calm small dog could be a useful step.","I'll refer you for CBT with a therapist who does this every week."],"hear":"'I can manage photos.'","mark":"First step agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Treatment element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Fear-avoidance cycle","Long routes, avoids parks, turned down a job","Avoidance keeps the phobia going"],["Relaxation","Racing heart, shaking, nausea; no fainting","Relaxation suits her; applied tension not needed"],["Fear hierarchy (SUDS)","Photo 30 up to stroking 95","A ready ladder to work through"],["Graded exposure","Worried she'll panic and not stop","Staying until fear settles is what makes it work"],["Practice and pace","Agrees to daily photos","Regular practice at her pace; no benzodiazepines"]]},"key":"Relaxation supports the work, but exposure is the treatment. Each step must be held until the fear settles and repeated often; leaving at the peak teaches the same lesson as avoidance.","objmap":[[3],[4,5],[4],[2],[4]]},"c-4-2-3":{"name":"ERP for Jake, with his mum: breaking the washing cycle together","why":"Explains OCD and ERP to both, and helps his mother step back from cleaning without blame.","frame":["OCD cycle","Hierarchy","Exposure","Response prevention","Family accommodation"],"steps":[{"h":"Get consent first","say":["Jake, are you happy for your mum to stay while we talk about this?","You can ask her to leave at any point."],"hear":"'Yeah, she can stay.'","mark":"Consent obtained"},{"h":"Map the OCD cycle","say":["Jake, what goes through your mind just before you wash?","How do you feel after washing, and how long does that last?"],"hear":"'That I'll carry germs home and make them ill. The relief lasts minutes, then it's back.'","mark":"OCD cycle identified"},{"h":"Check severity, insight, mood and skin","say":["How many hours a day does washing and cleaning take?","Do you think the fears are more than they should be?","How's your mood been? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"'About four hours. I know it's too much but I can't stop. My mood's been low, but nothing like that.'","mark":"Good insight identified"},{"h":"Build a hierarchy","say":["Let's score some things from 0 to 100 for how anxious they'd make you if you couldn't wash afterwards.","The sofa? A door handle? The bin lid?"],"hear":"'Sofa 40, door handle 60, bin lid 90.'","mark":"Hierarchy built"},{"h":"Explain exposure and response prevention","say":["You'd touch the sofa, then not wash, and stay with the anxiety until it drops on its own.","You'd do that first with a therapist, then practise daily, then move up the list.","Each time, you learn the feared thing doesn't happen and the anxiety passes without washing."],"hear":"'What if I don't wash and someone gets ill?'"},{"h":"Talk about family accommodation kindly","say":["Karen, cleaning for Jake comes from love. Most parents would do the same.","But each time you clean or reassure him, the OCD gets its relief without Jake learning anything new.","We'll plan small steps back together, with Jake's agreement, not stop suddenly."],"hear":"'So I've been making it worse? Should I just stop cleaning for him?'","mark":"Family accommodation identified"},{"h":"Discuss treatment options","say":["With four hours a day and missed college, NICE suggests more intensive CBT with ERP, an SSRI, or both.","An SSRI can help, but it takes weeks to work and often needs a higher dose for OCD.","It's your choice, Jake."],"hear":"'Do I have to take tablets?'"},{"h":"Agree next steps","say":["I'll refer you for CBT with ERP and we'll measure how much OCD affects you, so we can track progress.","See your GP about emollients for your hands.","Karen, there's support for you too, such as OCD-UK and a carer's assessment."],"hear":"'What do we do this week?'","mark":"Next steps agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["ERP element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["OCD cycle","Fears making family ill; washing brings brief relief","Compulsions keep the fear going"],["Hierarchy","Sofa 40, door handle 60, bin lid 90","Start low, at the sofa"],["Exposure","Worried someone will get ill","Facing triggers tests the feared outcome"],["Response prevention","Washes four hours a day; good insight","Resisting washing lets anxiety fall naturally"],["Family accommodation","Mum cleans daily and reassures","Gradual, agreed reduction is part of treatment"]]},"key":"Reducing family accommodation is part of the treatment, not a side issue. Praise his mother's care, then plan gradual steps back that Jake agrees to; stopping suddenly usually adds distress and conflict.","objmap":[[1,2],[2],[5],[6],[3,6]]},"c-4-3-1":{"name":"Is IPT right for Claire? The four areas","why":"Links Claire's depression to her relationships, checks each IPT area in turn and agrees one or two to focus on.","frame":["Grief","Role dispute","Role transition","Interpersonal sensitivity (isolation)"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and set the purpose","say":["Hello Claire, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. Your GP asked whether a talking therapy called IPT might help.","We have about 50 minutes. I'll ask about what's been happening, and we'll decide together at the end."],"hear":"'I'm not really sure what to expect, to be honest.'"},{"h":"Confirm depression and draw the timeline","say":["Tell me in your words how the last six months have been.","When did your mood start to drop, and what was happening in your life at that point?"],"hear":"'Flat, not sleeping, nothing's enjoyable. Mum died last September, and it got worse after clearing her house in May.'","mark":"Onset linked to life events"},{"h":"Area 1: Grief","say":["Tell me about your mum. What has it been like since she died?","When are the hardest moments? Are there things that remind you of her that you avoid?"],"hear":"'Evenings are worst. Her number's still in my phone. I haven't been to the grave and I turned her photo face down.'","mark":"Grief identified"},{"h":"Area 2: Role dispute","say":["How are things with your sister now? What happened between you?","What do you each expect from the other?"],"hear":"'I did all the caring and she barely visited. We fell out clearing the house. I haven't spoken to her since June or answered her birthday card.'","mark":"Role dispute identified"},{"h":"Area 3: Role transition","say":["Have there been other big changes, such as work, moving or relationships?","You were your mum's main carer. What has it been like since that role ended?"],"hear":"'I looked after her through hospital and the hospice. Now evenings feel empty. Work and home with Tom haven't changed.'","mark":"Role transition identified (end of carer role)"},{"h":"Area 4: Interpersonal sensitivity","say":["Who are the important people in your life now?","Is it usually easy or hard for you to make and keep close relationships?"],"hear":"'Tom's been brilliant, and work keeps me going. I've never really struggled with people before this.'"},{"h":"Suitability and safety","say":["Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or thoughts of hurting yourself?","Any voices or feeling people are against you? How much do you drink?","How are you taking the sertraline?"],"hear":"'Sometimes I wouldn't mind not waking up, but no plans; Tom and work keep me going. A glass of wine at weekends. I miss about two doses a week.'","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts, no plan identified"},{"h":"Explain IPT with reasons and details","say":["IPT is a talking therapy for depression that links mood to what's happening in your relationships.","It's usually about 16 weekly sessions with an IPT-trained therapist from the psychological therapies team, alongside your sertraline.","You wouldn't have to make up with your sister. The aim is to work out what you want from that relationship and how to get there."],"hear":"'Will I have to make up with my sister? Do I have to stop the sertraline?'"},{"h":"Agree focus and close","say":["Of what we've talked about, what feels most important to work on?","I'll refer you, start a PHQ-9 so we can track progress, and ask your GP to review the sertraline and how you're taking it.","Let's agree a safety plan for the wait: Tom, your GP, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123."],"hear":"'Mum first, I think, and then my sister. How long is the wait?'","mark":"Focus agreed: grief and role dispute"}],"table":{"cols":["IPT area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Grief","Avoids the grave, photo face down, keeps her number","Main focus; avoidance suggests grief that has become stuck"],["Role dispute","No contact with her sister since June; anger over caring","Second focus; the dispute grew out of the loss"],["Role transition","Carer role ended; evenings feel empty","Present, but best handled within the grief work"],["Interpersonal sensitivity (isolation)","Close to Tom; supported at work","Not a focus; she has good relationships"]]},"key":"Finding several areas does not mean treating them all. Agree one or two tied to the onset with her; here grief comes first and the sister dispute second, because the dispute grew out of the loss.","objmap":[[2],[9],[7],[8],[9]]},"c-4-3-2":{"name":"Late, angry and asking for your number: using transference safely","why":"Owns what really happened, links Leah's feelings to her past gently, holds the boundary and uses supervision.","frame":["Real relationship","Transference","Countertransference","Boundary","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Stay calm and curious","say":["I'm glad you came, Leah.","It sounds like you're really angry with me today. Can you tell me more?"],"hear":"'You're just like all the doctors. You don't really care.'"},{"h":"Check what really happened first","say":["Has something I did made you feel that way?","You're right. I was ten minutes late last week and I didn't explain. I'm sorry."],"hear":"'I nearly left the waiting room.'","mark":"Real grievance identified"},{"h":"Link to the past, gently","say":["You've told me about waiting by the window for your mum.","I wonder if waiting for me last week brought back some of that feeling."],"hear":"'Don't psychoanalyse me. ... Maybe. It's the same feeling.'","mark":"Transference identified"},{"h":"Hold the boundary warmly","say":["I won't give you my mobile number. That's not because I don't care.","I can't always answer, and you deserve help that is there whenever you need it."],"hear":"'If you cared you'd give me your number.'","mark":"Boundary held"},{"h":"Check risk","say":["Weekends sound really hard. When you're alone by the phone, do you have thoughts of self-harm or wishing you weren't here?","When did you last hurt yourself?"],"hear":"'Sometimes I want to disappear, not die. I last cut in the summer, nothing this week.'","mark":"No current self-harm identified"},{"h":"Give crisis routes","say":["Let's write down who you can contact at weekends.","Jade, the crisis team, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"'I'd text Jade first.'"},{"h":"Repair and close the session","say":["Thank you for telling me straight. Saying it here, rather than not coming back, helps our work.","I'll be on time. If anything changes, I'll let you know beforehand.","I talk about all my sessions with my supervisor. It's how I make sure I'm doing this well, not to get you into trouble."],"hear":"'Are you going to tell your supervisor I kicked off?'"},{"h":"Take it to supervision","say":["Dr Fraser, I noticed I felt irritated with her and also a strong pull to rescue her.","I think that may tell us how others react to her. How do I keep steady and avoid drifting on boundaries?"],"hear":"'Good that you noticed both pulls. What did you do with them in the room?'","mark":"Countertransference identified"}],"table":{"cols":["Concept","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Real relationship","Doctor was late last week with no reason","A real grievance; own it before interpreting"],["Transference","'Just like all the doctors'; waiting by the window for mum","Old feelings of neglect brought to the therapist"],["Countertransference","Your irritation and urge to rescue","Information about her world; take it to supervision"],["Boundary","Asks for your mobile 'for emergencies'","Decline warmly and offer reliable crisis routes"],["Risk","Wants to 'disappear'; last cut in summer","No current self-harm; safety plan for weekends"]]},"key":"Before calling it transference, check what actually happened. She was right that you were late; owning that first makes the link to her past feel true, not defensive.","objmap":[[7],[3],[4,7],[5],[8]]},"c-4-3-3":{"name":"Family intervention for Daniel's parents: lowering stress at home","why":"Explains negative symptoms and expressed emotion without blame, and offers family intervention and carer support.","frame":["Critical comments","Emotional over-involvement","Negative symptoms","Early warning signs","Carer strain"],"steps":[{"h":"Confirm consent and set the scene","say":["Thank you for coming. Daniel said he's happy for us to meet, although he didn't want to come himself.","I can share general information today; anything personal I'd check with him first."],"hear":"'We're at the end of our tether, honestly.'","mark":"Consent confirmed"},{"h":"Listen to the mother","say":["Tell me what a typical day at home is like.","Karen, how are you coping yourself?"],"hear":"'He's in bed till two. I check on him every hour at night. I haven't slept properly in months.'","mark":"Emotional over-involvement identified"},{"h":"Listen to the father","say":["Mark, what goes through your mind when you see him in bed?","What do you end up saying to him?"],"hear":"'He's lazy. I tell him to get up most days.'","mark":"Critical comments identified"},{"h":"Explain negative symptoms","say":["Lying in bed is often part of the illness itself: low motivation and energy, called negative symptoms.","Medication can also make people slow or sleepy. It isn't laziness, and pushing harder usually raises his stress."],"hear":"'Is this saying we caused his illness?'"},{"h":"Explain expressed emotion and family intervention","say":["No. Families don't cause schizophrenia. But stress at home, like criticism or a lot of worry, can make relapse more likely.","Family intervention is at least 10 planned sessions over three months to a year, ideally with Daniel there.","It covers information about the illness, communication, problem-solving and a relapse plan."],"hear":"'What happens in these sessions? Do we just sit and argue?'"},{"h":"Identify early warning signs and risk","say":["Before his last relapse, what did you notice first?","Are you worried he might go missing or harm himself?"],"hear":"'He stopped his tablets and said the neighbours were watching him. I worry he'll go missing.'","mark":"Early warning signs identified"},{"h":"Plan how to involve Daniel","say":["We'll ask Daniel. Sessions can start with you, and he can join when he's ready.","His care coordinator can talk it through with him too."],"hear":"'Will Daniel even come?'"},{"h":"Offer carer support and close","say":["You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, to look at your own needs.","I'll also give you details of a carers' group, and the crisis number for nights when you're worried.","I'll refer you for family intervention through the team and we'll meet again soon."],"hear":"'Nobody's offered us anything like that before.'","mark":"Carer's assessment offered"}],"table":{"cols":["Family issue","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Critical comments","'He's lazy'; tells him to get up most days","High expressed emotion; raises relapse risk"],["Emotional over-involvement","Checks every hour at night; no sleep","High expressed emotion and exhaustion"],["Negative symptoms","In bed until 2 pm","Illness and side effects, not laziness"],["Early warning signs","Stopped tablets; neighbours watching","Basis for a shared relapse plan"],["Carer strain","'End of our tether'; no assessment offered","Carer's assessment and support needed"]]},"key":"Expressed emotion is something families can change, not something they are blamed for. Separate it clearly from cause, present it as stress on Daniel, and offer help for the parents' own strain.","objmap":[[1,5],[4],[5],[2,5],[5,8]]},"c-4-3-4":{"name":"Three referrals, three therapies: matching patient to treatment","why":"Gives the team a clear, evidence-based match for each patient with interim safety and referral routes.","frame":["DBT","MBT","Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR","ACT"],"steps":[{"h":"Set out the approach","say":["I'll take each referral in turn: the main problem, the therapy that fits, the evidence, and anything that needs stabilising first.","Then I'll suggest who sends each referral."],"hear":"'Go ahead. We've got about seven minutes for these.'"},{"h":"Woman, 23: DBT","say":["Her main problems are repeated self-harm and emotional instability, so DBT fits best.","It combines weekly individual therapy, a skills group, phone coaching between sessions and a consultation team for the therapists.","The skills cover mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness."],"hear":"'She's had five A&E visits for cutting in three months, and she hates groups.'","mark":"DBT matched"},{"h":"Her wait and an alternative","say":["MBT is an alternative, helping her understand her own and others' minds, if DBT isn't available or she prefers it.","While she waits, one consistent care coordinator, a crisis plan and regular planned contact, not only crisis contact.","Let's ask her which she prefers, and talk the group part through with her."],"hear":"'She might not survive a nine-month wait.'","mark":"MBT considered as alternative"},{"h":"Man, 41: PTSD","say":["Four months after the crash, with flashbacks, nightmares and avoidance, NICE supports trauma-focused CBT, or EMDR for non-combat trauma.","EMDR still involves bringing the memory to mind, but in shorter bursts with eye movements or tapping."],"hear":"'He doesn't want to relive the crash.'","mark":"Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR matched"},{"h":"Stabilise him first","say":["His drinking needs addressing before or alongside trauma work, as it blunts the therapy.","Let's offer advice on alcohol and sleep and recheck risk, though he denies suicidal thoughts."],"hear":"'He drinks four or five pints most nights to sleep.'","mark":"Alcohol use identified"},{"h":"Woman, 50: chronic primary pain","say":["NICE recommends ACT or CBT for chronic primary pain. ACT fits her 'I just have to live with it'.","It helps her live alongside the pain and move towards what matters, like seeing her grandchildren.","We'd frame it as treating real pain, not saying it's in her head, and screen her mood."],"hear":"'She may hear therapy as \"it's all in your head\".'","mark":"ACT matched"},{"h":"Answer the challenge","say":["Putting all three on the general CBT list would miss what each person needs.","Each therapy targets a different problem and has its own evidence."],"hear":"'Why not put them all on the general CBT list?'"},{"h":"Agree referral routes","say":["Jo, could you take the DBT referral and interim plan? Sam, the trauma assessment?","I'll write to the GP and pain service about ACT. Each choice will be checked with the patient."],"hear":"'Who is sending which referral?'","mark":"Referral routes agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Therapy","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["DBT","Woman, 23: repeated cutting, five A&E visits","First choice to reduce self-harm; plan for the wait"],["MBT","Dislikes groups; nine-month DBT wait","Alternative for borderline personality disorder; ask her preference"],["Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR","Man, 41: flashbacks, nightmares, avoidance; heavy drinking","Evidence-based for PTSD; address alcohol alongside"],["ACT","Woman, 50: chronic pain, stopped seeing grandchildren","NICE option for chronic primary pain; values-led"]]},"key":"Match each therapy to the problem the person most needs to change, not to the shortest list, and say what keeps each one safe while they wait.","objmap":[[1,7],[3,8],[5],[8],[1,3]]},"c-4-4-1":{"name":"Gareth: repair the rupture, respond to the slide, end well","why":"Owns the rupture, finds out why he stopped coming, reviews risk and the plan, and prepares a planned ending.","frame":["Rupture and repair","Missed sessions","Deterioration and risk","Treatment plan","Ending and handover"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge without defending","say":["Thank you for telling me. If I looked bored, I'm sorry. That's not how I want you to feel here.","I'm really glad you came back today."],"hear":"'Didn't see much point, to be honest.'","mark":"Rupture acknowledged"},{"h":"Explore the missed sessions","say":["What made it hard to come the last two times?","If you miss a session again, would a text from us be easier than a call?"],"hear":"'I lost my job three weeks ago. I couldn't face anyone. I'd rather you text.'","mark":"Reason for missed sessions identified"},{"h":"Look at the deterioration","say":["Your PHQ-9 is higher today, which fits with everything that's happened.","How are sleep and appetite? How much are you drinking now?"],"hear":"'About four cans most nights since the job went.'","mark":"Deterioration identified"},{"h":"Assess risk","say":["Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or that others would be better off without you?","Any plans? What keeps you going?"],"hear":"'Some nights I think everyone'd be better off without me. No plans. My daughter stays at weekends.'","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts, no plan identified"},{"h":"Deal with practical pressures","say":["Have you been able to claim Universal Credit yet? What about the rent?","Citizens Advice or the Jobcentre can help with the claim and rent this week."],"hear":"'Haven't claimed. Rent's due in two weeks.'","mark":"Practical crisis identified"},{"h":"Review the plan together","say":["Shall we change how we use the time: less paperwork, more on the job loss?","I'll discuss with my supervisor whether a medication review would help, and we'll decide together."],"hear":"'I don't want tablets that make me a zombie.'"},{"h":"Name the ending honestly","say":["There's something I need to tell you. I'm moving to a new post, so we have four sessions left.","Let's plan those together, including a relapse prevention plan you can keep."],"hear":"'Now you're leaving too. What was the point?'"},{"h":"Agree handover and close","say":["I'll talk to my supervisor about who continues with you, so there's no gap.","Here's a safety plan with crisis numbers. I'll text you before next week's session."],"hear":"'Will I get someone else, or is this it?'","mark":"Handover agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Rupture and repair","'You looked bored'","Confrontation rupture; own it to repair"],["Missed sessions","Couldn't face anyone after losing his job","Withdrawal linked to deterioration, not lack of interest"],["Deterioration and risk","Higher PHQ-9; four cans a night; passive thoughts, daughter protective","Review risk and safety plan; address alcohol"],["Treatment plan","Wants more talking, less paperwork","Adapt the plan; consider medication review with supervisor"],["Ending and handover","'You're leaving too'","A planned ending with relapse prevention and handover"]]},"key":"A rupture that is owned and repaired often strengthens the work. Acknowledge your part without over-apologising, then move to what he needs now: risk, practical help and a planned ending.","objmap":[[1,2],[2],[3,4],[7,8],[6,7]]},"c-5-1-1":{"name":"Is it ADHD? Three core features, two settings, no blame","why":"Builds a home-and-school picture of Jayden's attention, activity and impulses, screens the look-alikes and heart history, and gives Mrs Walsh a blame-free plan.","frame":["Inattention","Hyperactivity","Impulsivity","Two or more settings, before age 12","Impairment","Look-alikes and heart screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, check parental responsibility, lift the blame","say":["Hello, Mrs Walsh, I'm Dr Ahmed from the CAMHS team. Thank you for coming in about Jayden.","Before we start, do you and his dad both have parental responsibility?","You've said you feel blamed from two directions. Today is about understanding Jayden, not judging you."],"hear":"\"Yes, we both do. I just want someone to tell me whether this is my fault.\""},{"h":"Inattention and overactivity at home","say":["Walk me through a school morning, from waking up to getting out of the door.","Can he finish something dull, like getting dressed or homework, without you standing over him?","Is he on the go even at mealtimes, in the car, or when he's tired?"],"hear":"\"I tell him five times to put his shoes on and he still wanders off. He can't sit through tea.\"","mark":"Inattention and hyperactivity identified"},{"h":"Impulsivity and danger","say":["Does he act before he thinks, like shouting out, grabbing or running off?","Has that ever put him in danger, near roads or water?","How does he cope with waiting his turn in games?"],"hear":"\"He ran into the road after a ball last month. The football club asked him to leave.\"","mark":"Impulsivity identified, with a safety risk"},{"h":"Every setting, early onset, real impairment","say":["His teacher's form describes much the same. Is it there at clubs and with grandparents too?","When did you first notice it? Was it there at nursery?","What is it costing him: friendships, learning, how he sees himself?"],"hear":"\"Nursery said he never sat for story time. Now he calls himself the bad boy.\"","mark":"Two settings, onset before 12 and impairment confirmed"},{"h":"Look-alikes, heart history and safety","say":["How were the pregnancy and birth, and when did he walk and talk?","Has his hearing ever been tested? Any snoring, or moments when he stares and doesn't respond?","Has anyone in the family had heart problems, fainting, or died suddenly when young?","Has anything frightening happened at home, or has anyone hurt him?"],"hear":"\"Milestones were normal and no heart problems. Hearing was never checked. There's shouting at home, but nobody hits him.\"","mark":"Hearing check needed; no cardiac or safeguarding red flags"},{"h":"Explain the likely diagnosis without blame","say":["This looks very like ADHD: a difference in how the brain manages attention, activity and impulses.","It is mostly down to genes and brain development. It is not caused by your parenting.","To confirm it we need the full picture: rating scales from school, an observation, and time with Jayden himself.","Meanwhile, we'll offer an ADHD parenting programme and, with your consent, ask school for classroom support."],"hear":"\"So it's not me. Honestly, I was exactly like him at school.\"","mark":"Family history noted"},{"h":"What medicine would involve, then close","say":["If it's confirmed and problems continue despite support, medicine such as methylphenidate is an option.","Before starting we measure his height, weight, pulse and blood pressure and plot them on charts.","We start low, review within weeks, then keep checking growth, appetite, sleep, pulse and blood pressure.","What will you tell your husband tonight?"],"hear":"\"That it isn't my fault, there's a proper assessment first, and tablets come with regular checks.\""}],"table":{"cols":["ADHD feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Inattention","Told five times about shoes; loses work at school","Core inattentive feature, seen at home and school"],["Hyperactivity","Can't sit through tea; out of his seat in class","Core hyperactive feature"],["Impulsivity","Ran into the road; shouts out; left football","Core feature with real danger: give road-safety advice"],["Settings and onset","Nursery, school, home and clubs","Pervasive and before 12, as the diagnosis requires"],["Look-alikes","Hearing never tested; no snoring or staring spells","Arrange a hearing test before confirming ADHD"],["Heart screen","No cardiac symptoms or sudden deaths in the family","No routine ECG needed before a stimulant"]]},"key":"One questionnaire or one setting never makes the diagnosis. Confirm the picture across settings, rule out look-alikes such as hearing loss, and remove the blame before you mention medicine.","objmap":[[6],[5],[6],[6],[7]]},"c-5-1-2":{"name":"Could Amara be autistic? A developmental story in order","why":"Takes Mr Okafor through Amara's development, communication, play, routines and senses in order, then explains autism honestly and starts support now.","frame":["Early development and regression","Social communication","Social interaction and play","Routines and repetitive behaviour","Sensory differences","Look-alikes and co-occurring conditions"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, check who is involved, find his fear","say":["Hello, Mr Okafor, I'm Dr Shah from the CAMHS team. Thank you for bringing Amara's story to us.","Do you and her mum both have parental responsibility, and does she know about today?","Before I ask anything, what do you already know about autism, and what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Her brother was just like this and grew out of it. Is she autistic, and will it ruin her life?\""},{"h":"Start at the beginning: milestones and lost skills","say":["Let's go in order: pregnancy, birth, then when she sat up and walked.","When did her first words come, and when did she join two words together?","Has she ever lost skills or words she used to have?"],"hear":"\"She walked at about one. Words came late, nearly two, but she's never lost any.\"","mark":"Language delay; no regression"},{"h":"Social communication","say":["When she wants something out of reach, how does she let you know?","Does she point at things just to share them with you, like a dog in the park?","If you call her name from across the room, what happens?"],"hear":"\"She pulls my hand to it. She doesn't really show me things, and she often ignores her name.\"","mark":"Social communication difference identified"},{"h":"Play, other children and masking","say":["Tell me how she plays with her toys. Does she play pretend, like feeding a doll?","How does she get on with other children at school?","When she gets home, does she seem worn out, as if she's held herself together all day?"],"hear":"\"She lines her animals up by colour and plays alone. School say she's quiet, then she explodes at home.\"","mark":"Social interaction difference; likely masking at school"},{"h":"Routines and senses","say":["What happens when plans change, or you drive a different way?","Are there things she must do the same way every time?","Is she bothered by noise, clothing labels, food textures or bright lights?"],"hear":"\"If we take another route she screams. Hand dryers terrify her, and she only eats plain beige food.\"","mark":"Restricted, repetitive behaviour and sensory differences identified"},{"h":"Look-alikes, health, family and home","say":["Has her hearing been checked recently?","How are her sleep, toileting and attention? Any fits or staring spells?","Is anyone else in the family autistic? And how is everyone at home coping?"],"hear":"\"Hearing was fine at school. She's up most nights. Thinking about it, her brother still struggles with friends.\"","mark":"Sleep problem and family history noted"},{"h":"Explain autism, the next step, and support now","say":["What you describe fits autism: a lifelong difference in how she communicates, relates to people and experiences the world.","It isn't caused by parenting, and many autistic people live full, happy lives with the right understanding.","The next step is an assessment by our autism team, with school's view and time with Amara; I can't promise the answer today.","Support doesn't wait for a label: a visual timetable, warnings before changes, and help with sleep can start now."],"hear":"\"So she might be autistic, there's an assessment, and school can help now. Maybe her brother needs looking at too.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Early development","First words near two; no lost skills","Language delay; no regression, so no urgent neurological review"],["Social communication","Pulls his hand, doesn't show things, ignores her name","Core social communication difference"],["Play and peers","Lines up animals; plays alone","Little pretend or shared play"],["Routines","Screams if the route changes","Insistence on sameness"],["Senses","Hand dryers, beige food only","Sensory differences; watch diet and nutrition"],["Masking and co-occurring","Quiet at school, explodes at home; poor sleep","Likely masking; treat the sleep problem now"]]},"key":"Girls often hold it together at school and fall apart at home, so 'fine at school' does not rule autism out. And support starts now; it does not wait for the diagnosis.","objmap":[[2],[6],[7],[7],[2]]},"c-5-1-3":{"name":"Ethan: new psychosis or longstanding autism?","why":"Uses Ethan's own baseline and short, literal questions to separate autistic traits from new psychotic symptoms, then explains the reasoning to his mother and the nurse.","frame":["Change from his own baseline","Self-talk or voices","Literal belief or delusion","First-rank symptoms","Withdrawal: autism or negative symptoms","Catatonia, drugs and physical causes"],"steps":[{"h":"Fix the baseline with the people who know him","say":["Mrs Clarke, think back to last spring. What did a normal day look like for Ethan?","Has he always talked to himself, or is the way he does it now different?","To the nurse: Is he eating, drinking and sleeping? Any stiffness, freezing or long silences?"],"hear":"Mother: \"He's always repeated lines from films, but now he whispers back angrily, and he's dropped his trains.\"","mark":"Clear change from baseline"},{"h":"Adapt the room and the questions","say":["Ethan, I'm Dr Patel. I'll ask short questions. Take your time, and you can write your answer if that's easier.","Is the light or noise in here all right for you?","What has changed for you since the summer?"],"hear":"\"I don't go downstairs now. It's not safe to eat with them.\""},{"h":"Self-talk or a voice?","say":["When you talk out loud, are you talking to yourself, or answering someone?","Do you hear a voice when nobody is in the room? Where does it come from?","What does it say?"],"hear":"\"A man's voice from the ceiling. He says 'they know, Ethan'. I tell him to stop.\"","mark":"Auditory hallucinations identified, not scripting"},{"h":"Literal meaning or delusion?","say":["You told the nurse the TV talks about you. What exactly does it say?","How do you know it means you and not someone else?","Could there be another reason the newsreader said that?"],"hear":"\"The newsreader said 'boy' and looked right at me. It's definitely me. There's no other reason.\"","mark":"Delusion of reference, held with full conviction"},{"h":"First-rank symptoms","say":["Does anyone put thoughts in your head, or take thoughts out?","Can other people hear or know what you're thinking?","Do you feel something outside you is controlling your body?"],"hear":"\"They take my thoughts through the router. That's why I unplugged it.\"","mark":"Thought withdrawal identified"},{"h":"Catatonia, drugs, body and risk","say":["Have you used cannabis, or vapes with something else in them?","Do you ever get stuck and can't move or speak?","Have you thought about hurting yourself, or anyone you think is against you?","To the nurse: Can we send bloods and a urine drug test, and score him on a catatonia scale today?"],"hear":"\"No drugs. Sometimes I freeze in the doorway for ages. I don't want to hurt anyone.\"","mark":"Possible catatonic features: screen formally"},{"h":"Explain the reasoning to his mother and the nurse","say":["His autism hasn't changed; something new has been added. Answering a voice, the TV belief and thoughts being taken are not autistic traits.","Autistic young people can develop psychosis, and it is treatable. We'll finish the physical checks and catatonia screen before choosing any medicine with you both.","At 16, whether Ethan stays here informally depends on his own capacity and agreement, not on your consent, but we want you fully involved."],"hear":"Mother: \"I didn't want it to be anything worse. But you're right, he's not himself.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Baseline","Always scripted; now whispers back angrily and has dropped his trains","A real change from his own baseline, not autism getting worse"],["Voices","Man's voice from the ceiling that he answers","True auditory hallucination"],["TV belief","Newsreader looked at him; no other explanation possible","Delusion of reference with full conviction"],["Thought interference","Thoughts taken through the router","First-rank symptom: thought withdrawal"],["Catatonia and body","Freezes in doorways; not eating with family","Score catatonia, check food and fluids, physical screen"]]},"key":"The question is not 'autism or psychosis?' but 'what is new compared with his own baseline?' Long-standing traits are not symptoms, and new first-rank features must not be put down to autism.","objmap":[[1],[5],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-5-2-1":{"name":"Chloe's overdose: medical safety, risk, and what her parents need to know","why":"Makes sure Chloe's paracetamol treatment is safe, understands why she took it and how risky she is now, and agrees with her what her mother is told.","frame":["Medical safety first","Confidentiality and its limits","The overdose: plan, intent, concealment","Bullying and online harm","Risk now and protective factors","Gillick and what parents are told"],"steps":[{"h":"Check the medical plan first","say":["To the nurse: What time did she take them, how many, and all at once or over hours?","Has the four-hour level been sent, and has TOXBASE been checked about acetylcysteine?","Chloe, however well you feel, paracetamol can harm the liver without any symptoms, so treatment comes first."],"hear":"Nurse: \"Sixteen tablets at about seven, all at once. The level's due back soon.\"","mark":"Single ingestion with a known time"},{"h":"Explain confidentiality and its limits","say":["Chloe, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the CAMHS doctors. Would you like your friend here, or to talk on your own?","What you tell me stays within the team, unless I'm worried you're at serious risk.","If that happens, I'll tell you first, and we'll work out together how it's shared."],"hear":"\"On my own. Just don't tell Mum about the messages.\""},{"h":"The overdose itself","say":["Can you take me through tonight, from when you got home?","Had you planned it, or was it on the spur of the moment?","What did you hope would happen? Did you tell anyone?"],"hear":"\"I read the group chat, took them from the kitchen drawer, then panicked and messaged my friend. I just wanted it all to stop.\"","mark":"Impulsive act, help sought, wish to escape"},{"h":"The bullying, and anyone harming her","say":["What's been happening with the messages from classmates?","Has anyone asked you for photos, or threatened to share something about you?","Is anyone hurting you, at school, online or at home?"],"hear":"\"They post about me every night, and they've made a fake account of me. Nobody hurts me at home.\"","mark":"Online bullying identified; no abuse disclosed"},{"h":"Risk now and what keeps her going","say":["How do you feel now that you're here?","Have you hurt yourself before, or thought of ending your life?","How have your mood, sleep and enjoyment been? What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Sort of relieved. I've cut a few times. My dog and my friend keep me going.\"","mark":"Previous self-harm; protective factors present"},{"h":"Her fear of her parents, and her competence","say":["What do you think your mum would do if she knew? Is there anything at home that makes it unsafe for her to know?","You understand what's happened and what the treatment is, so your views carry real weight with me.","Your mum needs to know about the tablets to keep you safe. The messages we can decide together."],"hear":"\"She'll just be upset. She can know about the tablets, but let me tell her about the chat.\"","mark":"Fear is embarrassment, not harm at home"},{"h":"Agree the plan and close","say":["You'll stay in tonight for the treatment and the blood tests.","Before you go home we'll make a safety plan with you and your mum: medicines locked away, who to tell, numbers to ring.","With your agreement we'll ask school to act on the bullying, and CAMHS will see you again within days."],"hear":"\"Okay. Will you stay with me when I tell her?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Medical safety","Sixteen tablets at once at about seven; feels fine","Treat by the level and TOXBASE; feeling well is not being safe"],["The act","Impulsive, told a friend, wanted it to stop","Less planning, but real intent to escape; not low risk on that alone"],["Bullying","Nightly posts and a fake account","Main trigger; needs school action and online reporting"],["Risk now","Past cutting, relief, dog and friend","Ongoing risk with protective factors; a written safety plan"],["Parents","Embarrassment, no harm at home","Share safety-critical facts with her involved in how"]]},"key":"Confidentiality for a teenager is not all or nothing. Share what her mother needs to keep her safe, let Chloe shape how and what else is said, and tell her before you do it.","objmap":[[1],[2],[5],[1,2],[7]]},"c-5-2-2":{"name":"Liam's first psychosis: timeline, symptoms, causes, risk, then early help","why":"Builds the timeline of Liam's change, pins down the psychotic symptoms and their likely causes, checks risk, and gives Liam and his father a clear early-intervention plan.","frame":["Timeline and prodrome","Delusions","Hallucinations and thought interference","Drugs, family history and physical causes","Risk to and from others","Early-intervention plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the format and get the timeline","say":["Liam, Mr Hughes, I'm Dr Okoro. I'd like to talk with you both, then with Liam on his own for a bit, then together. Is that all right?","Mr Hughes, when did you first notice Liam changing?","What came first: friends, schoolwork, sleep?"],"hear":"Father: \"Before the summer he stopped seeing mates and his grades dropped. The foil went up six weeks ago.\"","mark":"Prodrome, then a first episode"},{"h":"The belief about the phone","say":["Liam, your dad says you feel watched through your phone. Tell me about that.","Who is watching, and why you?","How sure are you, out of ten?"],"hear":"\"Men from the phone company. They put a camera in it because I know things. Ten out of ten.\"","mark":"Persecutory delusion, full conviction"},{"h":"Voices and thoughts","say":["Do you ever hear voices when nobody is around? What do they say?","Do your thoughts ever feel taken out, put in, or broadcast to others?"],"hear":"\"They whisper through the wall at night that I'm next. Sometimes they read my thoughts.\"","mark":"Hallucinations and thought broadcasting identified"},{"h":"Drugs, mood, body and family","say":["Have you used cannabis, vapes or anything else? How are your sleep, eating and mood?","To father: Any head injury, fits, fevers or recent illness?","To father: Has anyone in the family had psychosis, bipolar disorder or died by suicide?"],"hear":"Liam: \"Weed a few times with some older lads in the spring.\" Father: \"My sister has schizophrenia.\"","mark":"Cannabis use and family history identified"},{"h":"Risk to himself, to other people and from other people","say":["Have you thought about hurting yourself or ending your life?","Have you thought about protecting yourself from these men? Do you keep anything to do that?","Do the older lads ever ask you to carry or hold things for them?"],"hear":"\"I wouldn't hurt myself. I keep a hammer under my bed in case they come.\"","mark":"Risk to others driven by fear; weapon to remove"},{"h":"Explain what it is, and answer the father's questions","say":["What you're describing sounds like psychosis: the brain under strain, getting mixed up about what's real. It's common and treatable.","It's too early to call it schizophrenia. We'll know more over the coming months.","He doesn't need hospital today if we can keep him safe at home with close support."],"hear":"Father: \"So it's not schizophrenia yet, and he can stay at home?\""},{"h":"The plan and tonight's safety","say":["I'm referring Liam to the early intervention in psychosis team today; they aim to start treatment within two weeks.","Before any medicine we'll do bloods, a physical check and an ECG. Talking therapy and family sessions come alongside, and you'll both help decide.","Tonight, the hammer comes out of the bedroom, no cannabis, and here is the crisis number."],"hear":"Liam: \"Fine, if it stops them watching me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Timeline","Lost friends and grades, then foil on the window six weeks ago","Prodrome followed by a first psychotic episode"],["Delusion","Phone company camera, ten out of ten sure","Persecutory delusion with full conviction"],["Voices and thoughts","Whispering through the wall; thoughts read","Hallucinations and thought broadcasting"],["Causes","Cannabis with older lads; aunt with schizophrenia","Raised vulnerability; drug screen and check for exploitation"],["Risk","Hammer under the bed","Fear-driven risk to others; remove it today"]]},"key":"At first contact call it psychosis, not schizophrenia, and treat it as urgent. Refer to early intervention now with the family involved, rather than waiting to see whether it settles.","objmap":[[1],[4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-5-2-3":{"name":"Ruby tonight: mania, online risk and a lawful safe plan","why":"Confirms Ruby's mania and its likely triggers, finds the online risk, works out the legal basis for keeping a 16-year-old safe, and explains the plan to her mother.","frame":["Mood, energy and sleep","Grandiosity and racing thoughts","Online and sexual risk","Antidepressant, drugs and physical causes","Capacity and the law at 16","Tonight's plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Physical picture and immediate risk from the registrar","say":["What are her observations, bloods, urine drug screen and pregnancy test showing?","Any fever, confusion or neurological signs?","Has she tried to leave, and who is with her now?"],"hear":"Registrar: \"Pulse 110, otherwise fine. Drug screen and pregnancy test negative. She's tried the exit twice.\"","mark":"No physical cause found yet; risk of leaving"},{"h":"Mood, sleep and speed of thought","say":["Ruby, I'm Dr Evans from the CAMHS team. How have you been feeling these last few days?","How much have you slept, and do you feel tired?","Are your thoughts going faster than usual?"],"hear":"\"Amazing. I don't need sleep. My brain's going so fast I can't type quick enough.\"","mark":"Elevated mood, reduced need for sleep, racing thoughts"},{"h":"Grandiose ideas","say":["Tell me about the business you want to start.","Do you feel you have special abilities, or a special mission?"],"hear":"\"I'll be a millionaire by Christmas. I've been chosen to change the fashion industry.\"","mark":"Grandiose ideas identified"},{"h":"Online and sexual risk","say":["Who have you been messaging online?","Has anyone asked to meet you, or asked for photos?","Have you spent money or done anything you wouldn't normally do?"],"hear":"\"A guy from an app wants to invest. I'm meeting him tonight. I sent him some pictures.\"","mark":"Online sexual exploitation risk identified"},{"h":"History and the antidepressant from her mother","say":["Mrs Shah, has Ruby had spells like this before, or very low periods?","Which antidepressant does she take, and has the dose changed recently?","Anyone in the family with bipolar disorder? Any drugs or energy drinks?"],"hear":"Mother: \"Fluoxetine since January; the dose went up a month ago. My brother has bipolar.\"","mark":"Possible antidepressant-triggered switch; family history of bipolar"},{"h":"Her capacity to decide to leave","say":["Ruby, the team want you to stay tonight so you can sleep and be safe. What do you think?","What could happen if you left to meet him tonight?","Why might your mum and the nurses be worried?"],"hear":"\"Nothing would happen, he's lovely. I'm leaving whatever you say.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh the risk of leaving: lacks capacity for this decision"},{"h":"Explain the plan to her mother","say":["I think Ruby is having a manic episode, possibly tipped over by the antidepressant, which we'll stop with my consultant's agreement.","Tonight a nurse stays with her one-to-one. At 16, your consent can't be used to keep her here if she refuses; if she tries to go, we'll use the Mental Health Act.","My consultant may start a medicine such as aripiprazole. Valproate isn't safe for a girl her age, so we won't use it.","We'll tell the police and the safeguarding team about the man online, and look for a young people's bed if she needs one."],"hear":"Mother: \"I knew something wasn't right. Will she have to go to a psychiatric unit?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood and sleep","Four nights without sleep; feels amazing","Manic episode"],["Grandiosity","Millionaire by Christmas; chosen for fashion","Grandiose ideas"],["Online risk","Meeting a stranger tonight; sent pictures","Exploitation risk: police and safeguarding referral"],["Triggers","Fluoxetine dose raised; uncle with bipolar","Likely switch: stop the antidepressant; bipolar vulnerability"],["Capacity and law","Can't see any risk in leaving","Use the Mental Health Act if needed, not a parent's consent"]]},"key":"At 16, a parent's agreement cannot hold a refusing girl on the ward, and the antidepressant may be part of the problem. Name the legal basis and review the antidepressant before you leave.","objmap":[[6,7],[4],[1],[6,7],[7]]},"c-5-3-1":{"name":"Lily: anxious school refusal and silence at school","why":"Shows that Lily's absence and silence are anxiety, rules out the look-alikes, and agrees a graded return and small speaking steps with her mother and school.","frame":["Refusal or truancy","What she is avoiding","Selective mutism","Look-alikes: hearing, language, autism, EAL","Mood and safety","Graded return and speaking steps"],"steps":[{"h":"Welcome Lily without pressure to speak","say":["Hello Lily, I'm Dr Rahman. You don't have to talk to me today. You can nod, point or draw if you like.","Mrs Turner, I'll mostly ask you, and Lily can show me if I get anything wrong.","Here are some pens and a feelings chart, Lily, in case you want them."],"hear":"Mother: \"She chats all evening at home, but she won't say a word to anyone outside the family.\""},{"h":"Refusal, not truancy","say":["Talk me through a school morning, from breakfast to the door.","What happens once you agree she can stay at home?","Has she ever missed school without you knowing?"],"hear":"\"Stomach ache from breakfast, crying at the door. Once I say she can stay home, it settles and she's fine with me.\"","mark":"School refusal, not truancy; avoidance brings relief"},{"h":"What she is avoiding","say":["What happened around five weeks ago, just before she stopped going?","Is it leaving you, the classroom, a person, or things like the toilets or lunchtime?","Lily, could you point on this scale to how scary each part of school feels?"],"hear":"\"A new teacher asked her to read aloud and she froze, and the class laughed.\" Lily points to 'reading aloud' and 'lunch hall'.","mark":"Social anxiety trigger identified"},{"h":"Map the speaking pattern","say":["Who does she talk to, and where: grandparents, cousins, in shops?","When did she first stop speaking outside the family, and has it ever been different?","How does she let her teacher know what she needs?"],"hear":"\"She talks to me and her nan, and whispers to her cousin. Not a word at school for three years; she nods or points.\"","mark":"Selective mutism identified: speaks at home, silent at school for years"},{"h":"Rule out the look-alikes","say":["Has her hearing been checked recently?","At home, is her speech clear, and does she understand everything you say?","Any difficulty with friendships, routines or sensory things? Which languages do you speak at home?"],"hear":"\"Hearing was fine last year. She's clear and chatty at home. Just English. She's shy, but she's not rigid about things.\"","mark":"No hearing, language, EAL or autism features"},{"h":"Mood, bullying and the home side","say":["Lily, you can show me thumbs up or down. Have you been feeling sad most days?","Has anyone at school been unkind to you, or hurt you?","Mrs Turner, how are you managing? Is anything at home making it hard to send her?"],"hear":"Lily shows a sideways thumb and shakes her head about being hurt. Mother: \"I've taken time off work. I hate making her go.\"","mark":"No bullying or self-harm; mother's worry keeps the avoidance going"},{"h":"Explain, then agree a plan with school","say":["This is anxiety, not naughtiness. Each day at home brings relief, which teaches her brain that school is dangerous, so going back gets harder.","With school we'll agree a gradual return: a key adult, a quiet base, short mornings first, building up week by week.","Speaking comes in tiny steps, never forced: a trusted adult joins you and Lily, first just being there, then nods, then single words.","With your consent I'll write to school and the attendance officer to explain it's a health need and ask for a support plan, not a fine."],"hear":"\"I thought keeping her home was helping. Yes, please talk to school.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Refusal or truancy","Stomach aches and tears; settles once allowed home","Anxious school refusal; avoidance is being rewarded"],["Trigger","Reading aloud, laughed at; lunch hall","Social anxiety focus: adjust reading and lunchtimes"],["Speaking","Chatty at home; silent at school for three years","Selective mutism: plan graded speaking steps"],["Look-alikes","Normal hearing and speech; English only; not rigid","Anxiety explains it; no other cause found"],["Home","Mother off work; hates making her go","Coach her mother; she is part of the plan"]]},"key":"Never make speaking the price of being at school. Get her back first with no pressure to talk, then build words in tiny steps. Forcing speech and allowing long absence both feed the anxiety.","objmap":[[1,2],[4,6],[5],[6,7],[7]]},"c-5-3-2":{"name":"Jayden's meltdowns: before, during, after, then parent training","why":"Maps what sets Jayden off and what keeps it going, screens for hidden causes and safety at home, and offers Ms Price parent training as help rather than blame.","frame":["Before, during and after","Oppositional or conduct problems","Hidden causes: ADHD, autism, language, learning, hearing","Home pressures and safety","Parent training"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with her, not the behaviour","say":["Ms Price, I'm Dr Lewis. You look worn out. How are you holding up?","Jayden can carry on playing. I'll talk with you, and say hello to him in a minute.","What would you most like to be different?"],"hear":"\"I'm on my knees. He kicked the TA, now he's excluded, and school ring me every day.\""},{"h":"One meltdown, step by step","say":["Take me through the last meltdown. What happened just before?","What did he do, and how long did it last?","How did it end?"],"hear":"\"I told him to switch off the tablet. He threw it and kicked the door. In the end I gave it back to calm him.\"","mark":"Trigger is a demand; giving in rewards the outburst"},{"h":"Pattern, settings and how long","say":["Is it mostly arguing, saying no and temper, or does he also steal, hurt animals or damage things on purpose?","Is it the same at school, at his nan's, with other adults?","When did it start?"],"hear":"\"Mostly temper and refusing. At school it's when he has to sit and write. Since about four.\"","mark":"Oppositional pattern across settings; no cruelty or stealing"},{"h":"Screen for hidden causes","say":["Can he concentrate on things he enjoys, or is he always on the go?","How are his talking, understanding instructions, reading and writing?","Has his hearing been tested? How does he get on with friends and changes in routine?"],"hear":"\"He can't sit still at all. Teachers say he's behind with reading. His hearing's never been checked.\"","mark":"Possible ADHD and learning or language need"},{"h":"Home, discipline and safety","say":["Who lives at home, and how do the adults handle his behaviour?","Lots of families have shouting or fights. Does anyone at home hurt anyone, or frighten Jayden?","How has your own mood been?"],"hear":"\"Me, him, his little sister and my partner. My partner shouts a lot. I've been low for months.\"","mark":"Harsh discipline and low maternal mood: keep safeguarding in mind"},{"h":"Answer the tablet question, explain parent training","say":["I can hear how desperate you are. There's no tablet that fixes behaviour like this, and calming medicines have real side effects, so we don't start there.","What works best is a parenting group: a small group of parents, once a week for about three months.","That's not because you've done something wrong. It gives you tools, like play, praise and calm limits, for a child who's harder than most."],"hear":"\"So it's not my fault? I'll try it if it's not a lecture.\""},{"h":"Agree the plan and leave the door open","say":["I'll refer you to the local group, such as Incredible Years, or one-to-one sessions if a group won't work for you.","With your consent I'll ask school for questionnaires, talk to the SENCO about reading and an ADHD screen, and arrange a hearing test.","Please see your GP about your mood. And is there anything else at home I should know?"],"hear":"\"Yes, speak to school. And... there's something about my partner I haven't said.\"","mark":"Possible domestic abuse: explore before she leaves"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Before, during, after","Told to stop the tablet, throws it, gets it back","Coercive cycle that parent training targets"],["Pattern","Temper and refusing at home and school since four","Oppositional defiant picture, not conduct disorder"],["Hidden causes","Can't sit still, behind with reading, no hearing test","Screen for ADHD, learning needs and hearing"],["Home and safety","Partner shouts a lot; mother low","Harsh parenting and maternal depression; ask directly about abuse"],["Treatment","Asks for a tablet to calm him","Parent training first; no routine medication"]]},"key":"Offer parent training as help with a hard child, not a verdict on her. Parents who feel blamed don't come, and not attending is the commonest reason it fails.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5,7],[6],[6]]},"c-5-3-3":{"name":"Chloe tonight: MEED risk, admission and family-based treatment","why":"Judges Chloe's physical risk on the whole MEED picture, decides she cannot safely go home, and explains admission and family-based treatment to her and her parents.","frame":["Weight and rate of loss","Heart and circulation","Bloods, ECG and fluids","Eating disorder thoughts, mood and self-harm","Where she is safe tonight","Family-based treatment"],"steps":[{"h":"Get the vital signs before the conversation","say":["To the nurse: Can we have lying and standing pulse and blood pressure, temperature and an ECG now?","Chloe, Mr and Mrs Bennett, I'm Dr Clarke. I know you want an answer about the weekend; I'll give you one.","First I need to know how safe Chloe's body is right now."],"hear":"Nurse: \"Pulse 42 lying, 78 standing, and she went dizzy. Temperature 35.6. ECG's being done.\"","mark":"Bradycardia with a large postural rise: high risk"},{"h":"What she is eating, drinking and doing","say":["Chloe, what have you eaten and drunk today and yesterday?","How fast has your weight been dropping lately?","Do you do anything else to control your weight: exercise, being sick, laxatives?"],"hear":"\"An apple and black coffee. I run every night. I don't make myself sick.\"","mark":"Severe restriction with compulsive exercise"},{"h":"The faint and the heart","say":["Tell me about the faint today. Any chest pain, fluttering or breathlessness?","Does it happen when you stand up?"],"hear":"\"I stood up in PE and everything went black. My heart flutters sometimes.\"","mark":"Syncope and palpitations"},{"h":"The thoughts, mood and self-harm","say":["What goes through your mind when you look at a plate of food?","How's your mood been? Have you hurt yourself, or thought of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"I feel disgusting if I eat. I'm low, but I'd never kill myself.\"","mark":"Anorexic thinking; low mood; no suicidal intent"},{"h":"Bloods and the risk picture","say":["We'll check blood count, kidney salts including potassium, phosphate and magnesium, glucose, liver, thyroid and a coeliac screen.","Chloe has several high-risk markers in the MEED guidance: the faint, a pulse in the 40s, the rise on standing, and a weight about 72% of what's expected.","Normal blood results wouldn't make her safe on their own."],"hear":"Mother: \"She seems fine now. Can't we bring her back on Monday?\""},{"h":"Decide on tonight, and feed safely","say":["It isn't safe for her to go home this weekend. Her heart is under strain, and today's faint could be an early warning.","We'll admit her to the children's ward tonight with our eating disorder team: heart monitoring, bloods, and a dietitian starting food.","We won't underfeed her. We'll start properly and check her blood salts closely in the first days, when problems can appear."],"hear":"Chloe: \"I'm not going to hospital, and I'm not eating their food.\"","mark":"Refusal: assess Gillick and involve the consultant"},{"h":"Explain family-based treatment and consent","say":["Chloe, that's the illness talking, and it's frightening. You'll be part of every decision we make.","The best treatment at your age is family-based: for a while your parents take charge of meals, the illness is the enemy, not you, and control comes back to you step by step.","If Chloe refuses treatment she needs, we'll talk with the consultant about the legal options rather than rely on your consent alone."],"hear":"Father: \"We'll do whatever it takes. We just didn't realise how serious it was.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Risk marker","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Weight","About 72% of median BMI; running every night","Amber, close to high risk; weight still falling"],["Heart","Pulse 42; faint at school; fluttering","High risk: ECG and cardiac monitoring"],["Circulation","Pulse 42 to 78 on standing, dizzy","Postural compromise: not safe at home"],["Intake","An apple, black coffee and water","Severe restriction: refeeding risk, so monitor bloods"],["Mind","Disgust at food; low mood; no intent","Anorexic thinking; keep checking mood and safety"]]},"key":"Judge risk on the whole MEED picture, not BMI alone: a faint and a pulse in the low 40s outrank the number on the scales. Then feed properly, because underfeeding is the commoner mistake.","objmap":[[1],[4],[6],[7],[5,6]]},"c-5-4-1":{"name":"Jade's disclosure: listen, don't promise, protect tonight","why":"Receives Jade's disclosure safely, explains why it cannot stay secret, checks her and her sister's safety, and starts the child protection process the same night.","frame":["Listen, don't investigate","No promise of secrecy","Self-harm and safety tonight","Other children in the home","Refer tonight and escalate","Record her own words"],"steps":[{"h":"Receive it calmly","say":["Thank you for telling me. That took real courage.","You're not in trouble, and this is not your fault.","Can you tell me a bit more, in your words, about what you mean?"],"hear":"\"He comes in when Mum's asleep. He touches me. Please, you can't say anything.\"","mark":"Disclosure of sexual abuse by a household member"},{"h":"Be honest about confidentiality","say":["I can't keep this secret, and I won't pretend I can.","My job is to keep you safe, so I have to share it with people whose job is protecting young people.","I'll tell you each step before it happens, and you'll have a say in how."],"hear":"\"He'll kill me. And Mum will hate me.\""},{"h":"Her fear and safety at home","say":["Has he said anything to stop you telling, or hurt you in other ways?","Do you feel safe to go home tonight?"],"hear":"\"He said nobody would believe me. I'm scared to go back while he's there.\"","mark":"Threats and fear of going home: not safe to discharge"},{"h":"Tonight's self-harm and suicide risk","say":["Can I ask about your arms? What did you use, and what was happening for you?","Have you hurt yourself before? Have you thought of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"A razor blade, to make the feelings stop. Sometimes I wish I wasn't here.\"","mark":"Self-harm linked to abuse; passive wish to die"},{"h":"Other children in the home","say":["Who else lives at home with you?","Are there any younger brothers or sisters?"],"hear":"\"My little sister. She's eight. I don't think he goes in her room.\"","mark":"Another child in the home at risk"},{"h":"Act tonight","say":["You'll stay in hospital tonight on the children's ward, where you're safe.","I'm going to phone the out-of-hours children's social care team and the police now, and tell my consultant and the safeguarding lead.","I won't tell your mum yet. Social care will advise on that, and on keeping your sister safe."],"hear":"\"Will you stay with me when the police come?\"","mark":"Referral and safe admission arranged"},{"h":"Record and reassure","say":["I'm going to write down exactly what you told me, in your words, and what I've done about it.","You did the right thing. A nurse will sit with you, and I'll come back after the calls."],"hear":"\"Okay. Just please don't leave me on my own for long.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Disclosure","'He comes in when Mum's asleep. He touches me.'","Child sexual abuse by a household member: child protection now"],["Threats and fear","Nobody would believe her; scared to go home","Not safe to discharge; admit tonight"],["Self-harm","Razor blade; sometimes wishes she wasn't here","Ongoing risk linked to the abuse"],["Sister","Eight years old, at home with him","Must be considered in the strategy discussion"],["Secrecy request","Begs you not to tell","Explain the limits kindly; share and keep her informed"]]},"key":"Listen, don't investigate: open prompts, no leading or detailed questions, no promise of secrecy, and don't tell her mother until social care and police advise. Record her exact words.","objmap":[[1],[7],[3],[6],[6]]},"c-5-4-2":{"name":"Sam and fluoxetine: Gillick, parental responsibility and a split family","why":"Hears Sam alone, tests his competence for this one decision, clarifies who holds parental responsibility, engages his objecting father, and agrees safe prescribing.","frame":["His own view, heard alone","Gillick: understand, retain, weigh, communicate","Who holds parental responsibility","The objecting parent","Safe prescribing and monitoring"],"steps":[{"h":"See Sam on his own first","say":["Sam, thanks for seeing me on your own first. What you say stays with the team unless I'm worried about your safety.","How have the last eight weeks been?","What would you like to happen now?"],"hear":"\"CBT helped a bit, but I'm still flat every day. I want to try the tablets. Dad doesn't get it.\""},{"h":"Check risk before anything else","say":["Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","What has stopped you acting on them?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think everyone would be better off. I haven't done anything. My sister stops me.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts: monitor closely"},{"h":"Test competence for this decision","say":["In your words, what is the medicine and why are we suggesting it?","What could go wrong, and what would you do if your thoughts got darker?","What are the other options if you don't take it?","Why does taking it make sense to you?"],"hear":"\"Fluoxetine might lift my mood in a few weeks. At first it can make you feel worse, so I'd tell you or Mum. I could just keep doing CBT, but that's not working.\"","mark":"Understands, retains, weighs and communicates: Gillick competent for this decision"},{"h":"Clarify parental responsibility","say":["Mrs Fletcher, were you and Sam's dad married when Sam was born, or is he named on the birth certificate?","Are there any court orders about decisions for Sam?"],"hear":"Mother: \"We were married then. There aren't any court orders.\"","mark":"Both parents hold parental responsibility"},{"h":"Talk with the father, not past him","say":["Mr Fletcher, thank you for calling. What worries you most about the medicine?","That's a fair worry. Fluoxetine is the one recommended for young people, it isn't addictive, and we'd see him every week at first.","Sam can lawfully make this decision himself, but he'd like you involved. Would you join the next appointment?"],"hear":"Father: \"I read they make kids suicidal. If he's being watched closely, I'll come and hear about it.\"","mark":"Father's objection is about safety, not control"},{"h":"Safe prescribing plan","say":["We'll carry on therapy alongside, and the team has reviewed this together.","Fluoxetine 10 mg once a day, with weekly contact for the first month.","If you feel worse, restless or more suicidal, tell us straight away. We'll write a safety plan and keep medicines stored safely at home."],"hear":"\"Okay. Mum can see the safety plan.\""},{"h":"Record and escalate","say":["I'll record how I tested your understanding, your consent, and both parents' views, and discuss it with my consultant today.","Once you turn 16 the Mental Capacity Act applies instead, and I'll explain what that changes."],"hear":"\"That's fine. Thanks for actually listening to me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His view","Still flat after CBT; wants to try tablets","His informed wish leads the plan"],["Gillick","Explains benefit, early risk and the alternative","Competent for this decision; record how you judged it"],["Parental responsibility","Married at his birth; no court orders","Both hold it; a competent 15-year-old's consent is enough"],["Father","Fears suicidal side effects","A real risk: answer it with close monitoring"],["Risk","Passive thoughts; sister protective","Weekly contact and a written safety plan"]]},"key":"Gillick is decision-specific: a competent 15-year-old can consent, and one parent cannot veto it. Refusal is different: don't rely on a parent's consent to override a competent young person's no.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5],[6],[3,7]]},"c-5-4-3":{"name":"Amira at 18: a transition, not a transfer","why":"Puts Amira at the centre of a joined-up move to adult care, so her support, methylphenidate and information sharing continue safely past her 18th birthday.","frame":["Her goals at the centre","Named worker and early planning","Adult referrals and a joint meeting","Medication without gaps","Consent and sharing after 18","Risk and practical adult life"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with what she wants","say":["Amira, this is your plan, so I'll ask you first. What matters most to you over the next year?","Mr Hassan, I'll want your view too, in a moment."],"hear":"\"Keeping my medication. I might move away for university next year, and I can't lose my tablets.\""},{"h":"Current picture and transition risks","say":["How are your mood and concentration at the moment?","Any self-harm or suicidal thoughts recently? How much do you drink, and any drugs?"],"hear":"\"Mood's been okay since last winter. I cut once in Year 11, not since. I drink at weekends.\"","mark":"Past self-harm and alcohol: transition risk factors"},{"h":"Named worker and a written plan","say":["Four months is tight, so we start today. I'll be your named worker until adult services take over.","We'll write a transition plan with you: who does what, by when, and a fallback if something stalls."],"hear":"Father: \"Last time we were told to wait and see. We need someone who'll answer the phone.\"","mark":"Named worker agreed"},{"h":"Referrals and a joint meeting","say":["I'll refer you to the adult ADHD service and the community mental health team today, with a full summary.","If they decline, I'll ask for the reasons in writing, discuss it with my consultant, and look at primary care mental health support.","I'd like a joint meeting with the adult team before your birthday, so you meet them while we're still here."],"hear":"\"What if they still say I'm not ill enough?\""},{"h":"Methylphenidate without a gap","say":["Methylphenidate is a controlled drug, so we'll ask your GP to take over prescribing under a shared care agreement.","CAMHS keeps prescribing until that is confirmed in writing. There won't be a gap.","Let's check your pulse, blood pressure and weight today so the handover is complete."],"hear":"\"My GP said they don't do ADHD medicine.\"","mark":"Shared care not yet agreed: confirm before any discharge"},{"h":"Consent and information sharing after 18","say":["At 18 you're an adult. We'll only share with your dad what you agree to, unless there's a serious risk.","Who would you like us to keep informed, and about what?"],"hear":"Amira: \"Dad can know about appointments and medication, not everything I say.\" Father: \"That's fair.\"","mark":"Consent to share recorded"},{"h":"Practical adult life and summary","say":["If you drive, you must tell the DVLA if your condition or medicine affects your driving.","If you move for university, register with a GP there early and ask student services about the Disabled Students' Allowance.","So: named worker, referrals today, a joint meeting, GP shared care before CAMHS stops, and you decide what Dad hears."],"hear":"\"That's the first time I've felt there's actually a plan.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her goals","Keep her tablets; may move for university","Plan around her; arrange a GP there early"],["Risk","Cut once in Year 11; drinks at weekends","Relapse risk around the change: include a crisis plan"],["Adult services","Long ADHD wait; CMHT says 'may not meet threshold'","Refer now, challenge refusals in writing, have a fallback"],["Medication","GP doesn't prescribe ADHD medicine","CAMHS continues until shared care is agreed"],["Consent","Dad may know about appointments and medication only","Record it; it governs sharing from 18"]]},"key":"Transfer is not transition. Discharge only when an adult team or GP has actually accepted her care and prescribing; a referral sent is not care received.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-6-1-1":{"name":"Mr Reid's HCR-20: history, now, and what comes next","why":"Turns twenty rated factors into a formulation, scenarios and a named plan that the team can defend on Friday.","frame":["Historical factors (H1–H10)","Clinical factors (C1–C5)","Risk management factors (R1–R5)","Formulation and scenarios","Management plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Agree the question with Dr Marsh","say":["'Before I rate anything, what decision is this feeding into: leave, a move back to prison, or longer-term planning?'","'This is structured professional judgement. I won't hand the CPA a total; I'll give a formulation and scenarios.'","'Can Dr Kerr co-rate it with me? She's trained in version 3 and free on Thursday morning.'"],"hear":"'Fine, but the tribunal will want a number. Can't we just give them a score?'","mark":"Purpose and co-rater agreed"},{"h":"Collect every source before the interview","say":["'I'll request the prison healthcare record, the probation report, the CPS papers and the list of previous convictions today.'","'I'll ask the nurses for two weeks of observations and any incidents since he arrived.'","'With his consent I'll ring his mother; she's willing to talk by phone.'"],"hear":"'The prison notes mention a fight on the wing last year. Chase those.'","mark":"Collateral sources listed"},{"h":"Historical factors: the offence and the pattern","say":["To Mr Reid: 'Take me back to the day you hurt your neighbour. What did you believe he was doing?'","'Were you on your depot then? Were you using anything?'","'Have there been other times you've hurt someone, including in prison or on a ward?'"],"hear":"'I thought he was poisoning my water. I'd stopped the injection a couple of months before and I was smoking weed every day.'","mark":"Psychosis, non-adherence and cannabis identified as historical drivers"},{"h":"Clinical factors: how he is now","say":["'Do you still think about your neighbour or the water?'","'Have you had any thoughts of hurting anyone since you came here?'","'If the voices or worries came back, how would you know, and what would you do?'"],"hear":"'I've done my time. I'm not a violent person, that was the illness, not me.'","mark":"Partial insight; no current violent ideation reported"},{"h":"Risk management factors: the road ahead","say":["'When you leave here, where would you live and who would you see?'","'What would make it hard to keep taking the depot?'","'What helps you stay well and out of trouble?'"],"hear":"'My mum's always stuck by me. I'd go back to hers if I could.'","mark":"Family support identified as protective"},{"h":"Formulate and write three scenarios","say":["To Dr Marsh: 'In one line: when he stops treatment and uses cannabis, persecutory beliefs return and he attacks the person he thinks is harming him.'","'Repeat scenario: a neighbour or staff member drawn into the delusion. Worst case: a serious injury with a weapon. Best case: early signs caught and the depot continued.'","'Warning signs for each: missed depot, cannabis, poor sleep, talk about the water.'"],"hear":"'Good. That's more useful to the tribunal than any number.'","mark":"Formulation and scenarios agreed"},{"h":"Plan with owners and dates","say":["'Depot continued and level of support reviewed weekly by his named nurse; urine screens after every leave.'","'Psychology to start relapse prevention work; the social worker to confirm the MAPPA level with his offender manager before Friday.'","'We review the whole assessment at Friday's CPA and again after any incident.'"],"hear":"'Put a name and a date against each one and I'm happy.'","mark":"Management plan owned and dated"}],"table":{"cols":["HCR-20 domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Historical","Wounding when off depot, using cannabis daily, believing the neighbour poisoned him; two earlier assaults when unwell","Violence has followed relapse each time: illness and drug use are the core drivers"],["Clinical","Calm on the ward; says 'that was the illness, not me'; no current thoughts of harm","Stable now, but insight is partial and untested outside a secure ward"],["Risk management","Mother supportive; MAPPA level unconfirmed; past non-adherence","Future risk depends on staying on the depot, staying off cannabis and close follow-up"],["Scenarios","Repeat, worst case and best case written, each with warning signs","Gives the team things to watch for, not a number to quote"],["Plan","Depot, screens, psychology, MAPPA check, Friday review","Every risk driver has a matching action, an owner and a date"]]},"key":"Rating all twenty items is the easy part. Excellence is the formulation: one sentence explaining why he was violent, the scenarios that follow from it, and a plan where each action answers a named driver.","objmap":[[2],[3],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-6-1-2":{"name":"Mr Carter on remand: psychosis, risk and the route to hospital","why":"Finds the psychosis behind the assault and food refusal, keeps him safe tonight and starts an urgent transfer.","frame":["Engagement and confidentiality","Psychotic symptoms and their link to violence","Risk to self and physical risk","Substance and organic causes","Transfer under the Mental Health Act and the interim plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Make contact safely at the hatch","say":["'Mr Carter, I'm a doctor from the NHS mental health team. I don't work for the prison.'","'Most of what you tell me stays with the health team. If someone's safety is at risk, I may need to share it, and I'd tell you.'","'Officers are worried you're not eating. Can we talk about that?'"],"hear":"'Who are you? Another one of them?'","mark":"Role and limits of confidentiality explained"},{"h":"Elicit the beliefs and the voices","say":["'Why have you stopped eating? What's happening to the food?'","'Do you hear people talking when nobody's there? What do they say?'","'Who do you think is behind it?'"],"hear":"'They're putting something in the food. The voices say the officers and him in my cell are poisoning me and they'll kill me first.'","mark":"Persecutory delusions and threatening voices identified"},{"h":"Link the symptoms to the assault","say":["'What happened with your cellmate last week?'","'Were the voices telling you to do anything?'","'If someone came into your cell now, what would you do?'"],"hear":"'He was working with them. The voices said he'd strike first, so I hit him. I'll defend myself if anyone comes in.'","mark":"Violence driven by perceived threat identified"},{"h":"Risk to himself and his body","say":["'Have you had thoughts that you'd be better off dead?'","'Have you ever hurt yourself or made plans to?'","'How much have you drunk in the last two days? Would you take sealed water or packet food?'"],"hear":"'Sometimes it'd be easier to be dead. I haven't done anything. I'd drink from a sealed bottle.'","mark":"Passive suicidal ideation; dehydration risk identified"},{"h":"Rule out drugs and physical causes","say":["'Have you used spice or tablets since you came in?'","'Any fits, head injury, fever or confusion?'","'Have you ever been in a psychiatric hospital?'"],"hear":"'Spice twice in my first week. Nothing for ten days. Never been in hospital.'","mark":"Substance trigger possible; first presentation of psychosis"},{"h":"Explain hospital to him","say":["'I think you're unwell, and the fear is part of that. Prison isn't the right place to treat it.'","'I'm going to ask for you to be moved to a secure hospital. Two doctors need to agree, and the Ministry of Justice signs it off.'","'Until then I can offer you a tablet to help, if you're willing. Nobody can force medicine on you here.'"],"hear":"'Will hospital wreck my court case?'","mark":"Transfer explained; treatment cannot be enforced in prison"},{"h":"Agree the plan with Mr Price","say":["'He's psychotic and frightened. Please keep him in a single cell on the healthcare wing with observations at an agreed frequency, and approach with two officers.'","'Open an ACCT for suicide risk, start a food and fluid chart, and offer sealed water and packet food.'","'The prison GP should check his obs, kidney function and CK today. I'm sending the secure unit referral now and booking the second doctor.'"],"hear":"'Right. Single cell, ACCT, fluids chart. Who chases the hospital bed?'","mark":"Interim plan agreed with prison staff"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Psychosis","Food poisoned; voices say officers and cellmate will kill him","Likely psychotic illness needing hospital treatment"],["Violence","Hit the cellmate on the voices' warning; will 'defend himself'","Threat-driven risk to anyone entering his cell"],["Self and body","Easier to be dead; two days without food; little water","ACCT, fluid chart and same-day physical checks"],["Causes","Spice twice early on, none for ten days; no prior admissions","Drug-induced psychosis possible, but symptoms persist: still treat"],["Law","Remand prisoner who will not accept treatment","Section 48/49 transfer; no treatment without consent in prison"]]},"key":"Linking the assault to the voices is not enough. The excellent doctor also secures his physical safety tonight and starts the transfer paperwork today, knowing the prison cannot give treatment against his will.","objmap":[[2,3],[1,5],[7],[7],[7]]},"c-6-1-3":{"name":"Mr Doyle's leave: three kinds of security","why":"Gets the facts, makes a fair decision on today's leave and builds a plan that uses physical, procedural and relational security.","frame":["Facts and warning signs","Physical security","Procedural security","Relational security","Restricted patient duties"],"steps":[{"h":"Get the facts from Ms Adeyemi","say":["'What exactly was passed, and was it found?'","'How late was he on Saturday, and how did he seem when he came back?'","'Was a urine drug screen offered? Has anyone filled in an incident report?'"],"hear":"'Fifty minutes late, giggly with red eyes. He refused the screen. Nobody's done the incident form yet.'","mark":"Possible drug use on leave identified"},{"h":"Look for the pattern","say":["'Is this new for him? What does his relapse signature say?'","'Why has he missed psychology twice?'","'Has anything changed at home? Any visits?'"],"hear":"'His mum visited last week and left upset. We don't know why. He's been quieter since.'","mark":"Disengagement and secrecy match his warning signs"},{"h":"Hear it from Mr Doyle","say":["'Kevin, you were late on Saturday. Can you tell me what happened?'","'Staff saw you give something to another patient. What was it?'","'Is anyone putting pressure on you, here or outside?'"],"hear":"'It was just a biscuit. If I tell you more I'm a grass, and you don't know what happens to grasses.'","mark":"Possible pressure or exploitation identified"},{"h":"Decide on today's leave","say":["To Dr Shah by phone: 'I'd suggest we suspend unescorted ground leave today, pending MDT review this week.'","To Mr Doyle: 'Your leave is paused, not cancelled. It isn't a punishment. Here's exactly what we need to restore it.'","'Would you agree to a room search and a urine test so we can clear this up quickly?'"],"hear":"'So I'm late once and you take my leave? My tribunal's in six weeks.'","mark":"Proportionate leave decision made"},{"h":"Physical and procedural security","say":["'Search his room and the shared areas under policy, with his knowledge.'","'Urine screen on every return from leave for now, and a timed leave log.'","'Ask the other patient's team to review him too; we may have a supply problem on the ward.'"],"hear":"Ms Adeyemi: 'We can do the search this morning and log leave times from today.'","mark":"Physical and procedural actions agreed"},{"h":"Relational security","say":["'Who knows him best? Could his named nurse have a one-to-one today about his mother's visit?'","'Psychology should reach out to him rather than wait for him to turn up.'","'Let's be curious, not cosy: we may have missed this because he's been settled for so long.'"],"hear":"'I've worked with him for years. I don't want to wreck his trust in us.'","mark":"Relational security reinforced"},{"h":"Inform, record and review","say":["'Dr Shah will let the Ministry of Justice casework team know about the late return and the leave change today.'","'Incident report before the end of this shift, and a safeguarding note about possible pressure from other patients.'","'MDT review this week. If screens are clear and he re-engages, we restore leave step by step.'"],"hear":"'That feels fair. I'll tell him the plan myself.'","mark":"Escalation and documentation completed"}],"table":{"cols":["Security type","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Warning signs","Late, red-eyed, refused a screen, missed psychology, passing items","Possible drug use and disengagement: his known relapse pattern"],["Physical","Package not found; other patient refused a search","Search under policy; check for supply on the ward"],["Procedural","No incident report; leave unchanged","Pause leave, test on return, log times, inform the Ministry of Justice"],["Relational","Upset after his mother's visit; fear of being a 'grass'","Talk to him about pressure and home; staff need renewed curiosity"],["Tribunal","Six weeks away","Be open and fair; a clear route back protects both trust and safety"]]},"key":"Pausing leave is easy. Excellence is treating him as possibly exploited, not just as a risk, and giving him a clear, written route back to leave.","objmap":[[1],[4],[6],[1,2],[7]]},"c-6-2-1":{"name":"Mr Bennett's jealousy: belief, behaviour, risk, then Mrs Bennett","why":"Grades the jealousy, measures the danger and protects his wife and children without breaking confidence more than safety demands.","frame":["The belief and its fixity","Checking, following and interrogation","Alcohol and other causes","Violence, weapons and suicide","Partner safety and the children"],"steps":[{"h":"Open on his distress","say":["'Mr Bennett, it sounds like this has been eating away at you. Tell me what's been happening.'","'I'm not here to take sides. My job is your health and making sure everyone at home is safe.'"],"hear":"'I don't know why I'm here. She's the one who should be seeing someone.'","mark":"Engagement established"},{"h":"Test the belief","say":["'What makes you sure she's seeing someone?'","'Might there be another explanation for what you've found?'","'If she swore it wasn't true, would that change your mind at all?'"],"hear":"'The stains on her underwear prove it. There's nothing else it could be.'","mark":"Fixed belief on trivial evidence: delusional jealousy likely"},{"h":"Map what he does about it","say":["'How often do you check her phone or her clothes?'","'Have you followed her, or questioned her for hours?'","'What happens when she denies it?'"],"hear":"'Every night. I follow her to work. I told her one way or another she'll admit it.'","mark":"Checking, following and threats identified"},{"h":"Alcohol and other causes","say":["'How much do you drink in a typical week?'","'Which came first: the drinking or the suspicion?'","'Any memory problems, head injury, or new medicines?'"],"hear":"'About seventy units. I was drinking heavily for two years before any of this.'","mark":"Alcohol identified as likely driver"},{"h":"Ask straight about harm","say":["'Has it ever got physical between you?'","'Do you have any weapons at home?'","'Have you thought about harming her, the man you suspect, or yourself?'"],"hear":"'I pushed her into a door frame three weeks ago. There's a hunting knife in the shed. Sometimes I think it'd be better if neither of us were here.'","mark":"Past violence, weapon access and homicide-suicide thinking identified"},{"h":"See Mrs Bennett alone","say":["'I can't go into what your husband told me, but I want to hear from you. Do you feel safe at home?'","'I'd like to go through some safety questions with you, the DASH checklist. Has he ever choked you or threatened to kill you?'","'How are the children with all this?'"],"hear":"'He's never choked me but I'm frightened of him. Our eldest has started wetting the bed again.'","mark":"High risk to partner; children affected"},{"h":"Explain and act","say":["'Jealousy this strong, especially with heavy drinking, can be part of an illness. It can be treated, but it can also become dangerous.'","'Please plan where you'd go if you needed to leave quickly. The National Domestic Abuse Helpline is a free, 24 hours: 0808 2000 247.'","'Because of the children, I'll be making a safeguarding referral today, and I'll talk to my consultant about a MARAC referral.'"],"hear":"'Is this my fault? Will social services take the children?'","mark":"Safety advice given; safeguarding referral made"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief","Stains 'prove' the affair; no other explanation accepted","Delusional rather than overvalued: treat as a psychotic symptom"],["Behaviour","Nightly checks, following, 'one way or another'","Escalating control and threats"],["Alcohol","Seventy units a week, two years before the jealousy","Probable alcohol-related morbid jealousy"],["Harm","Shoved her, punched a wall, knife in the shed, 'better if neither of us were here'","High risk, including homicide-suicide"],["Family","Wife frightened; 11-year-old bedwetting","Children's safeguarding referral and MARAC consideration"]]},"key":"Respecting his confidentiality never means keeping his wife unsafe. Share only what protects her and the children, give general advice freely, and disclose more when the risk of serious harm justifies it.","objmap":[[2],[5],[4],[7],[7]]},"c-6-2-2":{"name":"Ms Finch's belief: love, pursuit, rival and despair","why":"Understands the erotomanic belief without arguing, measures the pursuit and both kinds of risk, and agrees shared action with the police.","frame":["The erotomanic belief","Pursuit and contact behaviours","Anger at third parties and rejection","Risk to self","Underlying illness and joint management"],"steps":[{"h":"Join her, don't argue","say":["'Ms Finch, I'm one of the hospital doctors. I want to understand what's been going on from your side.'","'You're not in trouble with me. I'm here to check you're OK.'"],"hear":"'I don't need a doctor. I need to get back before he finishes filming.'","mark":"Engagement established"},{"h":"Explore the belief","say":["'How did you first know he felt this way about you?'","'How does he let you know now?'","'What does he want to happen?'"],"hear":"'Eight months ago he smiled at me on air. Blue ties are our sign. We'll marry this summer.'","mark":"Erotomanic delusion identified"},{"h":"Map the pursuit","say":["'How many times have you gone to his home? How many letters or emails?'","'Have you sent him anything else?'","'Has anyone asked you to stop, or warned you?'"],"hear":"'Four times this month, emails every day, a watch and a cake. The police letter was her doing.'","mark":"Persistent, escalating pursuit despite a warning"},{"h":"Ask about anger and the rival","say":["'How do you feel about people who seem to get in the way?'","'Have you ever thought of doing anything to his girlfriend?'","'Has it ever got heated with anyone, like security?'"],"hear":"'She's keeping him from me. I want to have it out with her face to face. I shouted at the security guard.'","mark":"Risk to a named third party identified"},{"h":"Ask about her own safety","say":["'If it turned out he didn't want to marry you, how would that feel?'","'Have you felt like this about anyone before? What happened then?'"],"hear":"'There'd be no point. In 2019 it was my GP, and I took an overdose.'","mark":"High risk to self on rejection"},{"h":"Check for an underlying illness","say":["'How's your sleep and energy? Do you feel unusually happy or speeded up?'","'Do you hear voices or feel controlled by anyone?'","'Any alcohol or drugs?'"],"hear":"'Five hours' sleep, I feel normal. No voices. I don't drink.'","mark":"Delusional disorder relapse likely; mania and schizophrenia less likely"},{"h":"Plan with PC Hill and services","say":["To Ms Finch: 'I think the illness you were treated for in 2019 has come back. I'd like you to stay so we can help.'","To PC Hill: 'She has a fixed delusion about him and anger towards his partner. I'll share what's needed to protect them, and record why.'","'I'm asking for a Mental Health Act assessment today. If she goes home, the community team will see her within 72 hours. You may want to look at a Stalking Protection Order.'"],"hear":"'Are you going to tell the police what I said?'","mark":"Joint plan agreed with police and services"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief","Secret messages, blue ties, marriage this summer","Erotomanic delusion; previous diagnosis of delusional disorder"],["Pursuit","Four visits, daily emails, gifts, over 100 letters, police warning ignored","Persistent intimacy-seeking stalking"],["Third party","Blames the girlfriend; wants to confront her","Real risk to the partner, not only the presenter"],["Self","Overdose after rejection by her GP in 2019; 'no point'","High suicide risk if the belief is challenged or rejected"],["Illness","No mania, voices or drugs","Relapse of delusional disorder: treatable, needs specialist follow-up"]]},"key":"Calm and polite is not safe. The excellent doctor asks about the rival and about rejection, because those, not her love for the presenter, carry the danger to others and to herself.","objmap":[[3,6],[4,5],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-6-2-3":{"name":"Mr Holt's exposures: pattern, escalation, mood and advice","why":"Builds an honest account of three exposures, finds the escalation and the suicide risk, and gives custody clear advice.","frame":["Role and confidentiality","Pattern of each incident","Escalation and other sexual interests","Other causes","Mood and suicide risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Explain who you are","say":["'Mr Holt, I'm a doctor with the liaison and diversion team. I'm not a police officer.'","'I'll write a summary for the custody staff about your health. If you tell me something that suggests someone's at risk, I'd have to share it.'","'Your solicitor can advise you on the legal side.'"],"hear":"'Will you tell the police everything I say?'","mark":"Role and limits of confidentiality explained"},{"h":"Walk through each incident","say":["'You said this is the third time. Tell me about the first one.'","'What builds up beforehand? How do you feel during, and afterwards?'","'Did you touch yourself or approach anyone?'"],"hear":"'The first was a jogger in a park, the second a woman on a platform. It builds when I'm stressed. I feel aroused, then disgusting. I masturbated the second time.'","mark":"Recurrent urge-driven exposure identified"},{"h":"Look for escalation","say":["'Today the girls were about fourteen. Did their age matter to you?'","'Is it happening more often?'","'Any fantasies about touching, following, or children? Any images online?'"],"hear":"'Their age didn't matter. It is getting more often. I've never touched anyone.'","mark":"Escalation in frequency and victim age identified"},{"h":"Rule out other causes","say":["'Any head injury, memory problems, or changes in personality?'","'How much do you drink? Any drugs?'","'Ever felt speeded up, or heard voices?'"],"hear":"'Two pints at weekends. Nothing else.'","mark":"Organic, psychotic and substance causes unlikely"},{"h":"Assess mood and suicide","say":["'How's your mood been lately?'","'Since the arrest, have you thought of ending your life?'","'What stops you?'"],"hear":"'Low for two months. I thought about stepping in front of a lorry. My mum stops me.'","mark":"Suicidal ideation after arrest identified"},{"h":"Advise Sergeant Lewis","say":["'He's fit to be interviewed. He understood the caution and a solicitor is booked.'","'He has suicidal thoughts since arrest: please keep him on close observations and remove ligature risks.'","'If he's released, his mum should know, and he needs his GP and our team's follow-up details.'"],"hear":"Sergeant Lewis: 'Fit, but on close obs. Got it. Anything else?'","mark":"Written advice to custody given"},{"h":"Referral and safeguarding","say":["'The victims were children, so I'll inform the safeguarding lead.'","'I'll refer him to forensic psychology for an assessment of the sexual behaviour, and to the community team for his mood.'","'This goes in the liaison and diversion report for the court.'"],"hear":"Mr Holt: 'Will this go on a register? Will I lose my job?'","mark":"Specialist referral and safeguarding arranged"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Pattern","Urge builds under stress; aroused during, ashamed after","Exhibitionistic disorder: urge-driven, not accidental"],["Escalation","Three incidents in 18 months, now girls of about 14","Rising frequency and younger victims: more concern"],["Other interests","Denies touching, following, interest in children or images","No contact offending reported; record and keep open"],["Other causes","No head injury, psychosis or heavy drinking","Primary paraphilia more likely than an organic cause"],["Mood","Low two months; thought of a lorry since arrest","Suicide risk in custody and on release"]]},"key":"Shame is the main danger tonight, escalation the main danger later. The excellent doctor addresses both: close observation now, and a forensic referral that recognises a pattern moving towards children.","objmap":[[1],[3],[3],[5],[6]]},"c-6-2-4":{"name":"Mr Kerr's disclosure: interest, behaviour, access and himself","why":"Takes a plain, structured history, finds every child he can reach, protects him from suicide and acts on safeguarding the same day.","frame":["Confidentiality first","Nature of the sexual interest","Images and contact behaviour","Access to children","Risk to himself"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the limits before the details","say":["'Thank you for coming. It took courage to tell your GP.'","'What you tell me stays private, unless I think a child or someone else could be at risk. Then I'd have to share it, and I'd tell you first if I safely could.'","'Having thoughts is not a crime. You came for help, and that's what I want to give.'"],"hear":"'If I tell you everything, will you call the police?'","mark":"Confidentiality limits explained before disclosure"},{"h":"Describe the interest plainly","say":["'When did you first notice sexual thoughts about children? How old were you?'","'What age and sex are the children? Are you attracted to adults too?'","'How often do the thoughts come, and how do you feel about them?'"],"hear":"'Since about fourteen. Girls of eight or nine. I've had a relationship with a woman. I hate these thoughts.'","mark":"Paedophilic interest, non-exclusive and unwanted"},{"h":"Ask directly about images and contact","say":["'Have you ever looked at sexual images of children online?'","'Have you ever touched a child, or contacted one online?'","'What makes the urges stronger?'"],"hear":"'Two years ago, for a few months. I deleted them. Never touched anyone. It's worse when I've had a drink, alone at weekends.'","mark":"Past image use; no contact offending; alcohol as trigger"},{"h":"Find every child he can reach","say":["'Do you live with, work with or look after any children?'","'Which children do you see in your family? Are you ever alone with them?'","'Have the thoughts ever been about any child you know?'"],"hear":"'My niece Ellie, she's six. She stays over some Saturdays when my sister works nights. Once or twice I had a thought and left the room.'","mark":"Specific child at risk identified"},{"h":"Assess risk to himself","say":["'You've been low for months. Have you thought of ending your life, especially since telling your GP?'","'Any plans? What keeps you going?'"],"hear":"'Sometimes I think I'd be better off not here. No plan. I wouldn't do it to my mum.'","mark":"Passive suicidal ideation; protective factor noted"},{"h":"Explain the next steps honestly","say":["'Because Ellie stays with you, I have to share this with our safeguarding lead today. Children's social care will need to know so she's protected.'","'For now, please don't be alone with Ellie or any child, and don't drink when she's around.'","'You've done the right thing. This is about keeping her safe and getting you help, not punishment.'"],"hear":"'My sister will find out. She'll never speak to me again.'","mark":"Safeguarding disclosure explained"},{"h":"Agree the plan","say":["'I'll refer you to a specialist forensic service that treats unwanted sexual thoughts, and treat your low mood here.'","'Stop It Now is a confidential helpline: 0808 1000 900. They help people in exactly your position.'","'Here's a written safety plan, the crisis line number, and a review with me next week.'"],"hear":"'Can this be cured?'","mark":"Specialist referral, helpline and safety plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sexual interest","Girls aged 8–9 since age 14; also attracted to women; hates the thoughts","Paedophilic disorder, non-exclusive and ego-dystonic"],["Images and contact","Viewed images two years ago; no contact offences","Past offending matters; no hands-on offending reported"],["Triggers","Alcohol and lonely weekends","Modifiable risks: drinking and isolation"],["Access","Niece Ellie, 6, stays over; sometimes alone with her","Same-day safeguarding action required"],["Self","Passive suicidal thoughts; mother protective","Safety plan, mood treatment and close follow-up"]]},"key":"Help-seeking and distress are protective, but they do not cancel access. The excellent doctor asks about every child by name, and acts on Ellie today while keeping him engaged in treatment.","objmap":[[1],[2],[4],[7],[7]]},"c-6-2-5":{"name":"Mr Price's fire: before, during, after, and why","why":"Analyses the fire-setting step by step, finds the motive and the risk to others, screens for disorder and advises custody clearly.","frame":["Antecedents","The fire itself","Consequences and response","Motive and past fires","Mental disorder screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Explain your role","say":["'Mr Price, I'm a doctor with the liaison and diversion team, not the police.'","'I'm here to check your health and tell the custody staff what you need. If someone is at risk I'd have to share that.'"],"hear":"'Whatever. I just want to go home.'","mark":"Role and confidentiality explained"},{"h":"Antecedents: what led up to it","say":["'Take me through this evening, from the start.'","'What were you drinking? How were you feeling?'","'What was on your mind when you walked to the flats?'"],"hear":"'Cans and vodka at a mate's. Jade posted a photo with her new fella. I walked round there.'","mark":"Trigger: rejection plus intoxication"},{"h":"Behaviour: the fire itself","say":["'What did you use to start it? Did you plan it?'","'Did you know people were in the flats?'","'What did you do once it was burning?'"],"hear":"'My lighter, on the paper in the bin. I knew people were asleep. I watched it a minute and ran. I didn't ring anyone.'","mark":"Fire near an occupied building; no alarm raised"},{"h":"Consequences and motive","say":["'What did you want to happen?'","'How did you feel watching it?'","'How do you feel about it now?'"],"hear":"'I wanted her to feel what I felt. I meant to scare her, not hurt anyone.'","mark":"Revenge motive identified"},{"h":"Past fire-setting","say":["'Have you ever set fires before?'","'What was going on those times?'"],"hear":"'Bins at school when I was fourteen, and one by the shops last year after a row.'","mark":"Repeated fire-setting under emotional stress"},{"h":"Screen for disorder and suicide","say":["'How's your mood been since you split up?'","'Any voices, or feeling people are against you?'","'Did you have extra help at school? How's your reading?'","'Have you thought you'd be better off dead?'"],"hear":"'Low since summer, drinking every night. No voices. I had help at school, I can't read well. Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead.'","mark":"Depression, alcohol misuse, possible learning disability, suicidal ideation"},{"h":"Advise Sergeant Morris","say":["'He's not psychotic, but he's depressed, drinking heavily and may have a learning disability.'","'He's not fit for interview until sober. When he is, he needs an appropriate adult, and the caution explained simply.'","'He's had thoughts of being better off dead, so please keep him on close observations. And there's a risk to Jade: she should be told and safeguarded through your domestic abuse process.'"],"hear":"'So he's not mentally ill, but he's not safe to interview yet?'","mark":"Fitness, appropriate adult, observations and risk to ex-partner advised"}],"table":{"cols":["Stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antecedents","Drunk; ex-partner's new relationship online","Rejection and alcohol set the scene"],["Behaviour","Lit a bin by occupied flats, knowing people slept there","Reckless danger to life"],["Consequences","Watched, ran, raised no alarm; wanted her to suffer","Revenge motive, little concern for others"],["History","Fires at 14 and in 2023 after rows","A repeated way of acting out anger"],["Disorder","Low mood, nightly drinking, reading difficulties, death wishes; no psychosis","Treatable depression and alcohol misuse; possible learning disability; needs an appropriate adult"]]},"key":"'Not psychotic' is not the same as 'no needs'. The excellent doctor names the revenge motive and repeat pattern, protects Jade, and secures an appropriate adult for a vulnerable man.","objmap":[[3,5],[4,5],[6],[7],[1,7]]},"c-6-2-6":{"name":"Mr Walsh's refusal: can he decide about his neck tonight?","why":"Applies the Mental Capacity Act to one specific decision, supports him to make it, and gives the emergency team a clear answer.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate","Impairment of mind or brain"],"steps":[{"h":"Define the decision with Dr Lee","say":["'Which decision exactly: the CT, the collar, or both? How urgent is it?'","'What risks will you tell him about, in plain words?'","'Any signs of head injury or neurological symptoms?'"],"hear":"Dr Lee: 'CT neck tonight and the collar until then. Risk is permanent paralysis. He's drunk and hit his face.'","mark":"Specific decision and urgency clarified"},{"h":"Listen to why he wants to leave","say":["'Mr Walsh, I'm a doctor here to understand what you want. What's the worst thing about staying?'","'Would a nicotine patch help while we talk? Can we close the curtain a bit?'"],"hear":"'I'm desperate for a fag and everyone's staring. I just want to go back to my cell.'","mark":"Reasons for refusal explored; support offered"},{"h":"Understand: explain and check back","say":["'The doctors think the bones in your neck might be broken. If they are and you move, you could be paralysed for life.'","'Can you tell me in your words what could happen?'"],"hear":"'My neck's just sore. Someone said it might be broken.'","mark":"Partial understanding"},{"h":"Retain","say":["'A minute ago I told you about a risk. What was it?'","'Let me say it again, and then I'll ask once more.'"],"hear":"'I don't remember. Something about my neck.'","mark":"Unable to retain information"},{"h":"Use and weigh","say":["'What do you think might happen to you if you go back without the scan?'","'How would being paralysed affect seeing your son on Sundays?'"],"hear":"'Nothing happens to me. I'm fine.'","mark":"Unable to weigh the risk"},{"h":"Communicate and impairment","say":["'You've told me clearly what you want, and I've heard that.'","'Have you been drinking today? Did you black out? Does your head feel fuzzy?'"],"hear":"'Several pints and some vodka. My head's a bit fuzzy, but I don't think I blacked out.'","mark":"Impairment: intoxication and possible head injury"},{"h":"Give Dr Lee the answer","say":["'He lacks capacity for this decision right now: he can't retain or weigh the risk, because of intoxication and possibly head injury.'","'Treat in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act, the least restrictive way: keep explaining, offer nicotine, use the collar, scan soon. Restraint only if needed and proportionate.'","'The Mental Health Act doesn't apply to a physical problem. Reassess capacity as he sobers, and document each part of the test.'"],"hear":"Dr Lee: 'Clear. We'll scan under best interests and recheck when he's sober.'","mark":"Capacity outcome and best interests plan advised"}],"table":{"cols":["Capacity test","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Knows his neck 'might be broken'","Partial: basic facts only"],["Retain","Cannot recall the paralysis risk after a minute","Not met"],["Use and weigh","'Nothing happens to me'","Not met"],["Communicate","Clearly says he wants to leave","Met"],["Impairment","Several pints and vodka; fuzzy head after hitting his face","Explains the inability; may be temporary, so reassess"]]},"key":"An unwise refusal is not proof of incapacity. The excellent doctor shows exactly which ability fails and why, tries to support the decision first, and plans to reassess as he sobers.","objmap":[[1],[7],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-6-3-1":{"name":"Mr Bell's leave: restrictions, readiness, and the route","why":"Explains how leave works under a restriction order, tests whether he is ready, and starts a realistic application.","frame":["What section 41 means","Mental state and early warning signs","Insight and offence work","Substances and medication","The application process"],"steps":[{"h":"Recognise the progress","say":["'Mr Bell, six months of escorted leave without a problem is real progress.'","'I'd like to explain how leave works for you, and look at whether you're ready for the next step.'"],"hear":"'Other patients go out alone. It's not fair that I can't.'","mark":"Progress acknowledged"},{"h":"Explain the restriction order","say":["'Because the court added a restriction order, Section 41, the Ministry of Justice has to agree to any community leave, transfer or discharge.'","'Your doctor can't decide alone. We apply to their casework team, and they decide.'","'The order has no end date, but it doesn't mean you'll never get leave.'"],"hear":"'So it's not up to you at all?'","mark":"Section 41 restrictions explained"},{"h":"Mental state and warning signs","say":["'Any voices or worries that people are watching you lately?'","'What would be your first signs of becoming unwell?'","'What would you do if you noticed them?'"],"hear":"'Nothing for two years. Bad sleep, thinking people on the bus are watching, snapping at staff. I'd tell my named nurse this time.'","mark":"Relapse signature known; plans to report it"},{"h":"Insight and the offence","say":["'What was going on for you at the time of the offence?'","'How do you think about it now?'"],"hear":"'I was off my meds, smoking a lot of cannabis, and thought he was following me. I think about his family every day.'","mark":"Good insight and victim empathy"},{"h":"Substances and medication","say":["'Any cannabis or alcohol since you've had leave?'","'How are you getting on with clozapine? Any side effects?'","'How much do you smoke?'"],"hear":"'All my screens are clean. I ask for my clozapine later because it knocks me out. I drool, and I'm a bit constipated. Ten roll-ups a day.'","mark":"Abstinent; clozapine side effects and timing need review"},{"h":"Set out the process","say":["'I'll update your risk assessment, and the team will agree conditions for unescorted leave.'","'The victim liaison officer and MAPPA will be asked for their views, then we apply to the Ministry of Justice.'","'It usually takes some weeks. We'll start with short trips to set places.'"],"hear":"'Weeks? I thought I'd be going this week.'","mark":"Process and timescale explained"},{"h":"Manage expectations and close","say":["'I know it's frustrating. Your record is strong, and I'm going to back the application.'","'Meanwhile, let's treat the constipation properly and review your clozapine timing. Constipation on clozapine can become serious.'","'Any questions? I'll update you after the next ward round.'"],"hear":"'OK. At least I know what happens next.'","mark":"Hope maintained; plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Restriction order","Feels it's unfair others go out alone","Needs a clear explanation of the Ministry of Justice's role"],["Mental state","No psychosis for two years; knows his early signs","Stable, with a usable relapse plan"],["Insight","Links offence to stopping medicine and cannabis; thinks of the family","Strong insight and remorse"],["Substances and medication","Screens clean; delays clozapine; drooling; constipation","Low drug risk; side effects need action to protect adherence"],["Process","Expects to go this week","Risk update, victim liaison, MAPPA, then application: some weeks"]]},"key":"Readiness is not the same as permission. Show him you support the application, explain the Ministry of Justice's role honestly, and treat his side effects, because missed clozapine is the real threat to his leave.","objmap":[[2],[3],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-6-3-2":{"name":"Mr Grant's fitness to plead: each criterion tested","why":"Tests each Pritchard criterion with a direct question, links any failure to his psychosis, and explains the process and MAPPA to his solicitor.","frame":["Understanding the charge and plea","Challenging a juror","Instructing his lawyer","Following the trial","Giving evidence"],"steps":[{"h":"Explain the purpose","say":["'Mr Grant, I'm a psychiatrist asked by your solicitor to write a report for the court.'","'This isn't confidential like usual care: what you say may go into the report. You can choose what to tell me.'","'I won't be asking whether you did it.'"],"hear":"'The judge and police are all in on it, and now her too.'","mark":"Purpose and limits of confidentiality explained"},{"h":"Mental state","say":["'Do you hear voices when nobody's around?'","'What do you think your solicitor is doing?'","'Have you been treated for this before?'"],"hear":"'A man's voice at night says don't trust her. She signals to the judge with her ring. I was in hospital in 2015, then stopped the tablets.'","mark":"Psychotic relapse identified"},{"h":"Charge and plea","say":["'What are you charged with?'","'What does guilty mean? What happens if you plead guilty?'"],"hear":"'GBH. Guilty means I did it and I'd get sentenced.'","mark":"Understands charge and plea"},{"h":"Challenging a juror","say":["'Before a trial starts, the jury of twelve people is chosen. If you knew one of them, or thought one was against you, what could you do?'","'Could you ask your solicitor to raise it?'"],"hear":"'No point. They'd all be planted.'","mark":"Unable to challenge a juror rationally"},{"h":"Instructing his lawyer","say":["'Would you tell your solicitor your side of what happened?'","'Would you consider her advice?'"],"hear":"'I'm not telling her anything. She's part of it.'","mark":"Unable to instruct legal representatives"},{"h":"Following the trial and giving evidence","say":["'What happens in court, and who does what?'","'If you went in the witness box, what would you say?'"],"hear":"'I'd use it to expose the plot.'","mark":"Unable to give relevant evidence"},{"h":"Explain to Ms Patel","say":["'In my opinion he's not fit to plead: he understands the charge and the plea, but his delusions stop him instructing you, challenging jurors or giving relevant evidence.'","'The judge decides, on evidence from two doctors. If he's unfit, a jury decides only whether he did the act. He may become fit after treatment, so I recommend hospital transfer under Section 48.'","'MAPPA brings police, probation and prison services together to manage people convicted of violent or sexual offences. If he's later given a hospital order, he'd be considered for MAPPA when discharge is planned.'"],"hear":"Ms Patel: 'So you're saying unfit now, but possibly fit later?'","mark":"Opinion, process and MAPPA explained"}],"table":{"cols":["Pritchard criterion","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Charge and plea","Knows GBH; understands guilty and sentence","Met"],["Challenging a juror","'They'd all be planted'","Not met, because of delusions"],["Instructing lawyer","Won't tell or trust his solicitor","Not met, because of delusions"],["Following and giving evidence","Would use the witness box to expose the plot","Not met, because of delusions"],["Mental state","Voices, persecutory beliefs, stopped treatment","Relapsed schizophrenia; treatable, so review after hospital care"]]},"key":"Schizophrenia does not equal unfitness. Excellence is an opinion on every criterion, each tied to a specific symptom, and kept separate from his state of mind at the time of the offence.","objmap":[[1],[3,7],[2],[7],[7]]},"c-6-3-3":{"name":"Mr Reid's threat: who, how, when, and who to tell","why":"Assesses a specific threat in detail, finds the treatable driver, and shares the minimum necessary information to protect the neighbour.","frame":["Target","Intent and plan","Means and access","Drivers: psychosis, depot, cannabis","Protective factors and disclosure"],"steps":[{"h":"Stay safe and hear him out","say":["'I'll sit here by the door. My colleague knows we're talking.'","'Mr Reid, you sound frightened and angry. Tell me what's been happening with your neighbour.'"],"hear":"'Mr Davies at number 14 is poisoning my water through the pipes. I hear him drilling and talking through the wall.'","mark":"Persecutory beliefs about a named person identified"},{"h":"Clarify the threat","say":["'You said you'd stab him. Do you mean that?'","'Have you thought about how and when?'"],"hear":"'Next time I taste it, I'll knock on his door and stab him.'","mark":"Specific plan with a trigger identified"},{"h":"Means and past behaviour","say":["'Do you have a knife or any weapon? Have you got one on you now?'","'Have you ever hurt anyone before?'"],"hear":"'There's a kitchen knife by my front door. Not on me. I hit a man in 2019 when I was ill.'","mark":"Weapon access and past violence identified"},{"h":"Drivers of risk","say":["'Are you still having your depot?'","'Are you using cannabis or anything else?'","'Any voices telling you to do things?'"],"hear":"'I stopped it two months ago. It might be poison too. I smoke weed every day.'","mark":"Relapse after stopping treatment, plus cannabis"},{"h":"Protective factors and self-harm","say":["'What has stopped you so far?'","'Any thoughts of hurting yourself?'"],"hear":"'My mum, and I don't want to go to prison. I don't want to hurt myself.'","mark":"Some protective factors; no self-harm risk"},{"h":"Seek consent, then explain the limit","say":["'I'm worried Mr Davies could be seriously hurt. Can I let the police know, so everyone stays safe?'","'I understand you don't want that. But when someone's life may be at risk, I have to share some information, even without your agreement.'","'I'll only tell them what they need to know to keep him safe.'"],"hear":"'Don't tell anyone. This is between us.'","mark":"Consent sought and refused; disclosure explained"},{"h":"Act and record","say":["To your consultant: 'He has a plan, a knife and a named target. I want an urgent Mental Health Act assessment today.'","To the police call handler: 'I'm a psychiatrist. A patient has made a specific threat to stab his neighbour at number 14 and has a knife at home. He's unwell. I'm sharing this to prevent serious harm.'","To Mr Reid: 'I've told the police about the threat, and nothing else about your health. I've written down why, as GMC guidance asks.'"],"hear":"Police: 'Thank you. We'll send officers to check on the neighbour.'","mark":"Disclosure made and documented"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Target","Mr Davies, number 14","Named, nearby victim: high concern"],["Intent and plan","Will stab him next time he 'tastes it'","Specific plan with a trigger that could occur any day"],["Means","Kitchen knife by his front door; violent in 2019 while unwell","Immediate access; violence has happened before when ill"],["Drivers","Stopped depot two months ago; daily cannabis","Treatable relapse of psychosis"],["Protection","Mother; fear of prison; no self-harm","Some restraint, not enough to rely on"]]},"key":"Disclosure is justified by risk, not by diagnosis. Seek consent first, then share only what protects Mr Davies, tell Mr Reid unless it is unsafe, and treat the relapse driving the threat.","objmap":[[7],[5],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-7-1-1":{"name":"High-risk withdrawal: why Dean stays in tonight","why":"Grades Dean's withdrawal risk fast, starts safe medication and thiamine, and agrees inpatient detox with him.","frame":["Last drink and daily amount","Past fits or DTs","Wernicke's risk and thiamine","Mimics to rule out","Setting: inpatient or community","Driving and children"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and settle him","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Hughes, one of the psychiatry doctors on call. Are you Dean?","You've had a long wait feeling like this. Give me two minutes of answers and I'll chart medicine straight after."],"hear":"\"Fine, but be quick. I'm shaking all over.\""},{"h":"Pin down the drinking","say":["When was your last drink, as close to the hour as you can say?","What do you drink on a normal day, and how much of it?","What happens in the morning before that first drink?"],"hear":"\"About half nine this morning. A litre of vodka, cans on top. I shake and sweat till I've had one.\"","mark":"Dependence confirmed: about 40 units a day, 12 hours since last drink"},{"h":"Ask about complicated withdrawal","say":["Have you ever had a fit when you stopped drinking?","Have you ever seen things that weren't there, or got very muddled?","Do you take diazepam or any other tablets, prescribed or bought?"],"hear":"\"I had a fit in the kitchen last year, second day off it. Street diazepam sometimes, none this week.\"","mark":"High risk: previous withdrawal seizure"},{"h":"Rule out mimics and Wernicke's","say":["I'm going to check your sugar, temperature and pulse, and look at your eyes and how you walk.","Any falls or knocks to the head lately?","How has your eating been?"],"hear":"\"No falls. I've barely eaten for weeks.\"","mark":"Wernicke's risk: poor diet; glucose and NEWS2 checked"},{"h":"Explain treatment and setting","say":["Because stopping gave you a fit last year, doing this at home isn't safe. I'd like you to stay a few nights.","I'm charting chlordiazepoxide now, and the nurses will score your symptoms every hour or two to guide the doses.","You'll also have vitamin B1 through a drip, called Pabrinex, before anything sugary, to protect your memory."],"hear":"\"Can't I just take a few tablets and go home?\"","mark":"Inpatient medically assisted withdrawal agreed"},{"h":"Driving and who is at home","say":["Who lives with you? Are there any children at home?","While you're drinking at this level you mustn't drive, and you'll need to tell the DVLA. I'll write that down for you.","Who is looking after your daughter tonight? We'll let the social worker know, so she gets support."],"hear":"\"My girl's eleven. She's at my sister Karen's tonight. If I can't drive, I lose my job.\"","mark":"Safeguarding and DVLA advice recorded"},{"h":"Close and set triggers","say":["If you feel worse, see insects or get confused, tell the nurses straight away.","Nurses, call me if the score rises, he's confused, or his obs change."],"hear":"\"Okay. Just don't let me have another fit.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Last drink and amount","A litre of vodka daily; last drink 9:30 this morning","Withdrawal is starting and will peak over the next one to three days"],["Past fits or DTs","A fit on day two of stopping alone last year","High risk of another fit: inpatient withdrawal"],["Wernicke's risk","Barely eaten for weeks","Give Pabrinex before glucose"],["Mimics","No head injury; sugar and obs checked","Withdrawal is the likely cause, not something hidden"],["Driving and children","Van driver; daughter of 11 at home","DVLA advice in writing; share with the social worker"]]},"key":"Looking only mildly unwell 12 hours in does not make him low risk. A past withdrawal fit decides the setting: admit and treat now, rather than waiting for symptoms to peak.","objmap":[[3],[5],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-7-1-2":{"name":"Delirium tremens: name it, treat it, move him","why":"Recognises delirium tremens in Tony, gives a clear mental state, treats him under the MCA and gets him to medical care tonight.","frame":["Consciousness and orientation","Perception and beliefs","Autonomic signs","Other causes: head injury, sepsis, low sugar, Wernicke's","Capacity and treatment","Medical transfer"],"steps":[{"h":"Make the room safe","say":["Kim, please stay with him, keep him away from the window and get a full set of obs.","Tony, I'm one of the doctors. You're in hospital and you're safe."],"hear":"\"Get them off me! They're coming out of the socket!\"","mark":"Immediate safety: one-to-one staff, window area cleared"},{"h":"Test orientation and attention","say":["Tony, where are we right now?","What day is it today?","Can you count backwards from 20 for me?"],"hear":"\"Sunday... I'm at the depot. Twenty, nineteen... seventeen... I've lost it.\"","mark":"Disorientated with poor attention: delirium"},{"h":"Explore what he sees and believes","say":["What can you see on the wall?","Who do you think put them there?","Are you trying to get away from someone?"],"hear":"\"Black beetles, on my arms. Men put them there. They want me dead.\"","mark":"Visual and tactile hallucinations with persecutory ideas"},{"h":"Check timing, body and other causes","say":["Kim, when was his last drink, and what are his pulse, temperature and sugar?","Tony, have you fallen or banged your head?"],"hear":"(Kim) \"About 60 hours since his last drink. Pulse 128, temperature 38.1, sugar 5.2. He says no fall.\"","mark":"DTs likely: timing plus autonomic signs; fever needs sepsis ruled out"},{"h":"Assess capacity and treat","say":["Tony, I'd like to give you a tablet to calm the shaking and the fear. Can you tell me what it's for?","He can't hold on to or weigh this, so I'll treat him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act.","Oral lorazepam now if he'll take it, intramuscular if not, and high-dose IV Pabrinex."],"hear":"\"I don't know what it is. I want to go home to my mum.\"","mark":"Lacks capacity: treated under the MCA"},{"h":"Transfer to medical care","say":["I have a 55-year-old man with delirium tremens: last drink 60 hours ago, pulse 128, temperature 38.1, confused and hallucinating.","He needs a medical bed tonight, sepsis screening, and a low threshold for a CT head."],"hear":"(Registrar) \"Send him to A&E now and we'll see him there.\"","mark":"Urgent medical transfer arranged"},{"h":"Give the mental state and document","say":["He's sweating and restless, frightened, speech loud, with persecutory beliefs and visual and tactile hallucinations.","He's disorientated in time and place, with poor attention and no insight.","I'll write up capacity, call his wife and complete a Datix for the window."],"hear":"(Kim) \"I'll ring his wife now.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Consciousness and orientation","Thinks it is Sunday at the depot; loses track counting","Clouded consciousness: delirium, not primary psychosis"],["Perception and beliefs","Beetles on the walls and arms; men want him dead","Typical DTs: vivid visual and tactile hallucinations with fear"],["Autonomic signs","Pulse 128, temperature 38.1, sweating, 60 hours after last drink","Severe withdrawal: a medical emergency"],["Other causes","Sugar 5.2; denies a fall; fever present","Still screen for sepsis and head injury; cover Wernicke's"],["Capacity","Cannot say what the tablet is for","Treat in best interests using the MCA, not the MHA"]]},"key":"Clouded consciousness with a racing pulse and fever in a drinker means delirium, a medical emergency. Treat with benzodiazepine and thiamine and transfer; an antipsychotic alone treats the label, not the illness.","objmap":[[4],[5],[5],[6]]},"c-7-1-3":{"name":"Voices after cutting down: hallucinosis, not DTs","why":"Separates Lee's alcoholic hallucinosis from DTs and primary psychosis, gauges his risk and agrees a safe plan.","frame":["Clear consciousness","Timeline against drinking","Form of the voices","Risk to others and self","Other causes and family history","Plan and the MHA"],"steps":[{"h":"Open","say":["Lee, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. The A&E team say you've had a frightening few days.","Tell me what's been happening."],"hear":"\"There's men outside my flat. They call me a paedophile and say they'll do me tonight.\""},{"h":"Check consciousness","say":["What day is it, and where are we?","Can you list the months backwards from December for me?"],"hear":"\"Wednesday. The hospital. December, November, October, September...\"","mark":"Clear consciousness and orientated: not DTs"},{"h":"Explore the voices and their timing","say":["Do the voices talk to you, or about you?","When you look out, are the men there?","When did they start, compared with when you cut down?","Has this happened before?"],"hear":"\"They talk about me and laugh. Started Sunday; I cut down Friday when the money ran out. Same two years ago, gone in a fortnight.\"","mark":"Third-person voices starting after cutting down: hallucinosis"},{"h":"Ask about risk directly","say":["Have you thought about how you'd protect yourself if they came in?","Do you have anything at home you'd use?","Have you had thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"I keep a hammer by the door. Last night I thought I'd be better off dead, but my sister's kids stop me.\"","mark":"Risk: weapon kept for self-defence; passive suicidal thoughts with reasons to live"},{"h":"Rule out other causes","say":["Any cannabis, speed or other drugs lately?","Has anyone in your family had mental illness or drink problems?"],"hear":"\"Weed months back, nothing now. My dad drank.\""},{"h":"Explain and agree a plan","say":["I think this is a psychosis brought on by heavy drinking and then cutting down. It usually settles within weeks once you're off alcohol.","We'll offer a safe detox with thiamine, and a short course of medicine to quieten the voices and help you sleep.","If the voices made you unsafe and you refused help, the Mental Health Act can still apply, because this is a psychosis."],"hear":"\"So I'm not going mad, then?\"","mark":"Plan: supervised detox, thiamine, short-term antipsychotic, risk plan"},{"h":"Close with safety and review","say":["Would your sister be willing to take the hammer out of the flat?","We'll see you again in a few weeks once you're sober. If the voices carry on, we'll look again at the diagnosis."],"hear":"\"Yeah. She'll take it.\"","mark":"Follow-up booked to review the diagnosis"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Consciousness","Orientated; months backwards intact","Not delirium, so not DTs"],["Timing","Voices began two days after cutting down; same pattern two years ago","Fits alcohol-related hallucinosis"],["Form of voices","Third-person, derogatory, men never there when he looks","Vivid auditory hallucinations in clear consciousness"],["Risk","Hammer by the door; thoughts of being better off dead","Defensive violence and suicide risk need a written plan"],["Course","Previous episode settled in a fortnight","Review in abstinence; persistence points to primary psychosis"]]},"key":"Hallucinosis is defined by clear consciousness: orientation separates it from DTs. What separates it from schizophrenia is the course in abstinence over weeks, not today's picture, so book the review.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[6]]},"c-7-1-4":{"name":"Unsteady and drowsy: cerebellum, liver and the flap","why":"Gives Pat a structured coordination and liver check, recognises hepatic encephalopathy, finds the trigger and escalates.","frame":["Cerebellar signs","Asterixis and conscious level","Signs of chronic liver disease","Wernicke's signs","Triggers","Escalation"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and chaperone","say":["Mrs Hughes, I'd like to check your balance, coordination and some signs of how your liver is working. Is that alright?","Grace will stay with us. Tell me if anything hurts or you need to stop."],"hear":"\"If I have to. I just want to sleep.\""},{"h":"Alertness and orientation","say":["Can you tell me where we are and what day it is?","How long have you felt this muddled?"],"hear":"\"Hospital... Thursday? I came in for my nerves.\"","mark":"Drowsy and disorientated: acute confusion"},{"h":"Cerebellar sequence","say":["Keep your head still and watch my finger with your eyes.","Touch your nose, then my finger, as quickly as you can.","Pat the back of one hand with the other, fast, then swap.","Run your heel down your shin; then, with Grace beside you, walk to the door and back."],"hear":"\"It's like being on a boat. My hand keeps missing.\"","mark":"Cerebellar signs: past-pointing, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, broad gait"},{"h":"Look for the flap and liver signs","say":["Hold your arms out straight and bend your wrists back as if stopping traffic. Hold it there.","I'm going to look at your eyes, hands, chest and tummy."],"hear":"\"My trousers are tight. Grace says my eyes look yellow.\"","mark":"Asterixis, jaundice, ascites, sweet breath: hepatic encephalopathy likely"},{"h":"Find the trigger","say":["When did you last open your bowels? Any black stools or vomiting blood?","Any falls or knocks to the head?","Grace, what sedation has she had this week, and what are her temperature and sugar?"],"hear":"\"Four days, no blood. Slipped yesterday, didn't bang my head.\" (Grace) \"Diazepam all week. Temp 37.6, sugar 4.9.\"","mark":"Triggers: constipation, benzodiazepines, possible infection"},{"h":"Report to the consultant","say":["She has cerebellar signs, likely longstanding from alcohol, but the new drowsiness, confusion and flap point to hepatic encephalopathy.","Likely triggers are four days' constipation and regular diazepam; with ascites and a low-grade temperature, peritonitis must be excluded. Wernicke's needs covering too."],"hear":"(Consultant) \"What do you want to do?\""},{"h":"Escalate and act","say":["Stop the diazepam, give IV Pabrinex, and ask the medical registrar to see her now for transfer.","Bloods: LFTs, clotting, U&Es, FBC, CRP, glucose and cultures, plus anything the medical team want."],"hear":"(Consultant) \"Agreed. Call them now.\"","mark":"Escalated: sedatives stopped, thiamine given, urgent medical review"}],"table":{"cols":["Sign group","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Cerebellar","Past-pointing, slow alternating movements, broad unsteady gait","Alcohol-related cerebellar damage, possibly with Wernicke's"],["Conscious level and flap","Drowsy, disorientated, wrists flap when held back","Acute hepatic encephalopathy, not withdrawal tremor"],["Chronic liver disease","Jaundice, tight swollen abdomen, sweet breath","Decompensated liver disease"],["Triggers","Four days without bowels opening, diazepam all week, temperature 37.6","Treat constipation, stop sedation, screen for infection"]]},"key":"A flapping tremor in a drowsy drinker is asterixis, not withdrawal. More benzodiazepine deepens the encephalopathy: stop sedation, give thiamine, name the trigger and get medical care today.","objmap":[[3],[4],[7],[7]]},"c-7-1-5":{"name":"Which tablet keeps Rachel sober? Choosing between three","why":"Matches a relapse prevention medicine to Rachel's own risks and goal, and ties it to follow-up so it continues after discharge.","frame":["Acamprosate","Naltrexone","Disulfiram","Safety checks: liver, kidneys, pregnancy, opioids","Psychosocial support and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and set the goal","say":["Rachel, you asked whether a tablet could help. Let's work that out together.","What's your goal: to stop completely, or to cut down?"],"hear":"\"Stop completely. I always slip within a month.\"","mark":"Goal: abstinence"},{"h":"Understand her relapses","say":["What usually happens just before you start drinking again?","What happened the last time you slipped?"],"hear":"\"Evenings once my son's in bed, or a bad day at work. One glass becomes a bottle. Last time I hid it and stopped going.\"","mark":"Triggers: evenings and stress; past lapses led to dropping out"},{"h":"Safety checks","say":["Do you take any painkillers, including ones from the chemist with codeine in?","Could you be pregnant at the moment? We'd do a test to be sure.","Any heart problems, or ever been unwell with psychosis?"],"hear":"\"Co-codamol most days for my back. Not pregnant, happy to test. No heart problems.\"","mark":"Daily codeine: naltrexone unsuitable for now"},{"h":"Explain the three medicines","say":["Acamprosate calms the brain's response after alcohol, so cravings are weaker. It's two tablets three times a day.","Naltrexone takes the pleasure out of a drink, but it blocks codeine, so it would clash with your co-codamol.","Disulfiram makes you very sick if you drink, even from mouthwash, and works best when someone sees you take it."],"hear":"\"My partner works away, so nobody could watch me take it.\"","mark":"Disulfiram less suitable: no one to supervise"},{"h":"Decide together","say":["Your kidneys are normal and acamprosate is fine with slightly raised liver tests. Given everything, it seems the best fit. How does that sound?","We start it now, while you've just stopped, and usually continue for up to six months."],"hear":"\"That sounds doable. What if I slip?\"","mark":"Acamprosate chosen"},{"h":"Link support and plan for a lapse","say":["Tablets work best alongside talking support, so I'll refer you to the community alcohol service before you go.","If you slip, keep taking it and tell us. A lapse is something we work through together, not a reason to close your case."],"hear":"\"I'd rather tell someone than hide it this time.\""},{"h":"Close","say":["I'll write to your GP today so the prescription carries on without a gap.","You'll leave with your first alcohol service appointment already booked."],"hear":"\"Okay. Thank you.\"","mark":"GP letter and community follow-up arranged"}],"table":{"cols":["Medicine","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Acamprosate","Normal kidneys, mildly raised LFTs, over 60 kg","Safe and suitable; full dose; start now"],["Naltrexone","Co-codamol most days","Unsuitable while she takes opioids"],["Disulfiram","Partner works away; no one to supervise","Less suitable; needs supervision and full warnings"],["Support and follow-up","Hid her last lapse and stopped attending","Book community follow-up and agree a lapse plan now"]]},"key":"The right tablet comes from her facts: co-codamol rules out naltrexone and no supervisor weakens disulfiram. Any choice fails unless community follow-up is booked before she leaves.","objmap":[[2],[4,5],[2,4],[7],[2]]},"c-7-2-1":{"name":"Heroin withdrawal on a section: confirm, then substitute","why":"Takes Jamie's substance history, confirms dependence objectively and explains safe opioid substitution, overdose and safeguarding steps.","frame":["Substance history: route, amount, other drugs","Objective withdrawal and urine test","Existing prescription","Overdose, naloxone and blood-borne viruses","Methadone or buprenorphine","Safeguarding and the section"],"steps":[{"h":"Engage","say":["Jamie, I can see you're feeling rough, and I want to help with that today.","You're on a section, so leaving isn't an option right now, but I'd rather sort this out with you."],"hear":"\"Just give me methadone or something. I'm rattling.\""},{"h":"Take the substance history","say":["Do you smoke heroin or inject it? How much a day, and how often?","When did you last use?","What else do you take: tablets, crack, alcohol?"],"hear":"\"Forty quid a day, three or four goes. Mostly smoke, sometimes inject. Last about ten last night. Street diazepam, crack at weekends.\"","mark":"Daily heroin with benzodiazepine and crack use"},{"h":"Confirm objectively","say":["Ali, can we do a withdrawal score and a urine drug test now?","Are you with a drug service or picking up from a chemist at the moment?"],"hear":"\"I left the service in the summer. I used the chemist on the High Street.\"","mark":"Withdrawal scored and urine sent; pharmacy and service to confirm no current script"},{"h":"Overdose and blood-borne viruses","say":["Have you ever overdosed?","Have you ever shared needles or other kit? Have you been tested for hepatitis or HIV?"],"hear":"\"Overdosed last year; a mate used the spray on me. Shared a couple of times. Never tested.\"","mark":"Overdose history and BBV risk: offer testing and hepatitis B vaccination"},{"h":"Explain the options","say":["There are two main choices: methadone, a liquid, or buprenorphine, a tablet that dissolves under the tongue or a long-acting injection.","We start low and build up over days, because your tolerance is already dropping in here.","What are you hoping for: to come off, or to be stable on a prescription?"],"hear":"\"I want to be stable on a script, not come off.\"","mark":"Goal: maintenance on opioid substitution"},{"h":"Children and naloxone","say":["Who are the children in your life, and who looks after them?","Before any leave or discharge, you'll have a naloxone kit and training."],"hear":"\"Two girls, four and seven. I have them at weekends. I never use around them.\"","mark":"Safeguarding: children at weekends, consider a referral"},{"h":"Close with a plan","say":["I'll talk to the addiction team today and agree a starting dose; the nurses will give it supervised.","Before you go home, we'll set up the community prescription so there's no gap."],"hear":"\"Fine. As long as I get something today.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Use and route","£40 a day, smoked and sometimes injected; last use 10 pm","Daily dependence; record route for BBV risk"],["Other drugs","Street diazepam, weekend crack","Higher overdose risk with sedatives; plan for it"],["Confirmation","Aching, yawning, runny nose; urine sent","Prescribe only after objective withdrawal and a positive test"],["Overdose and BBV","Overdosed last year; shared needles; never tested","Naloxone kit, BBV testing, hepatitis B vaccination"],["Safeguarding","Daughters of 4 and 7 at weekends","Ask about care and storage; consider a referral"]]},"key":"Never prescribe from the amount he reports: street purity varies and tolerance falls on the ward. Confirm withdrawal objectively, check for an existing script, start low and build up.","objmap":[[2],[3],[7],[4],[1,3]]},"c-7-2-2":{"name":"Two opioid problems on one night: hold one dose, reverse one overdose","why":"Keeps Darren safe by holding unconfirmed methadone, and saves Kelly with ABCDE, naloxone and prolonged monitoring.","frame":["ABCDE and naloxone","Monitoring beyond naloxone","Confirming the methadone dose","Withdrawal relief","Safety steps: search, Datix, naloxone kits"],"steps":[{"h":"Hear Darren's story","say":["Darren, I believe you're struggling, and I'll help you tonight.","Which pharmacy, what dose, and is it supervised?","When was your last dose, and what have you used since?"],"hear":"\"Eighty mil, supervised at Boots on the high street. Missed four days. Bit of heroin Wednesday, couple of cans.\"","mark":"Four missed days: tolerance lost; dose unconfirmed"},{"h":"Hold the dose and treat symptoms","say":["After four days off, your old dose could stop your breathing, so I can't give methadone tonight.","I'll chart medicine now for the cramps, sickness, diarrhoea and sleep, and we'll call your pharmacy first thing.","Using tonight, with lower tolerance, is when people die. Will you give me tonight?"],"hear":"\"Just give me forty, then. Or I'll go out and score.\"","mark":"Methadone held; symptom relief charted"},{"h":"Kelly: call for help and start ABCDE","say":["Grace, put out an emergency call and bring the trolley and naloxone.","Kelly, can you hear me? Open your eyes.","Airway open, high-flow oxygen, and support her breathing with the bag and mask."],"hear":"(Grace) \"Resps 6, sats 84% on air, GCS 8, pupils pinpoint.\"","mark":"Opioid toxicity: emergency call made, ABCDE started"},{"h":"Give naloxone and find out what she took","say":["Give naloxone 400 micrograms IM now, repeat if there's no response, and write down each dose and time.","Kelly, you're in hospital. What did you take, and when?"],"hear":"\"A bit of gear and a couple of pregabalin, about half nine.\"","mark":"Mixed opioid and pregabalin overdose"},{"h":"Explain the need for monitoring","say":["The injection wears off sooner than what you took, so your breathing could drop again.","You need to go to A&E and be watched for several hours.","Did you mean to harm yourself tonight?"],"hear":"\"I didn't want to die, I just wanted to sleep. Okay, I'll go.\"","mark":"Transfer agreed; intent asked"},{"h":"Safety steps on the ward","say":["Grace, was her bag searched when she came back from leave?","Let's search under ward policy, complete a Datix and review her leave conditions."],"hear":"(Grace) \"No, it wasn't searched.\"","mark":"Search, Datix and leave review"},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["Both of them get a naloxone kit and training before any leave or discharge.","Morning plan for Darren: phone Boots and his prescriber, suspend the community script, and do an ECG before restarting."],"hear":"(Grace) \"I'll put it all in the handover.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Safety point","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["ABCDE and naloxone","RR 6, sats 84%, GCS 8, pinpoint pupils","Opioid toxicity: oxygen, airway support, naloxone now"],["Monitoring","Heroin plus pregabalin two hours earlier","Naloxone wears off first; several hours of medical observation"],["Methadone dose","80 mg claimed; four days missed; unconfirmed","Tolerance lost: hold tonight and confirm with the pharmacy"],["Withdrawal","Cramps, sweats, runny nose; threatens to score","Treat symptoms with empathy; explain the overdose risk"],["Ward safety","Bag not searched after leave","Search, Datix, leave review, naloxone kits for both"]]},"key":"Naloxone wakes the patient but leaves before the opioid does, so waking is not recovery. In the same way, four missed days means there is no 'usual dose': hold it, treat symptoms and confirm.","objmap":[[4],[5],[2],[7],[6]]},"c-7-2-3":{"name":"Coming off benzodiazepines at Linda's pace","why":"Assesses Linda's benzodiazepine dependence and agrees a slow, single-prescriber diazepam taper she can trust.","frame":["Dose and supply","Withdrawal risk: fits, alcohol, other sedatives","Anxiety, grief and suicide risk","Switch to diazepam and taper","Driving and a single prescriber"],"steps":[{"h":"Reassure and open","say":["Linda, your GP asked us to help. Nobody is going to stop your tablets suddenly today.","Can you tell me how this started?"],"hear":"\"After Mike died. Diazepam first, then lorazepam for bad nights. I'm scared you'll cut me off.\""},{"h":"Current use and supply","say":["What do you take in a day, and at what times?","Do you get any from anywhere other than your GP?"],"hear":"\"Diazepam five, three times a day, lorazepam one at night. I buy blue ones online when I run short, one extra most days.\"","mark":"Dependence with online supply: true dose and content uncertain"},{"h":"Withdrawal history and other sedatives","say":["What happened when you tried to stop before?","Did you have any fits, confusion or see things that weren't there?","How much do you drink? Any painkillers, pregabalin or gabapentin?"],"hear":"\"Shaking, no sleep, felt unreal. No fits. About six units a week, nothing else.\"","mark":"No past fits and little alcohol: lower seizure risk"},{"h":"Mood, risk and what helps","say":["How's your mood been? Have you ever felt life isn't worth living?","What has helped the anxiety in the past?"],"hear":"\"Low at times, but my daughter and grandson keep me going. Breathing exercises helped a bit.\"","mark":"Anxiety and grief underneath; no current suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Explain the plan","say":["First we change the lorazepam to diazepam, which leaves your body slowly, so the levels stay steadier.","Once you're settled, we reduce by a small amount every two to four weeks, and you help set the pace.","If a step feels too hard, we stay at that dose longer before the next cut.","Online tablets can contain anything, so we'll aim to stop those first, with your GP as the only prescriber."],"hear":"\"How long will all this take?\"","mark":"Patient-led diazepam taper agreed"},{"h":"Driving and safety","say":["You drive to work: if you feel drowsy or slow, don't drive. Untested tablets bought online make that more likely.","If you feel very unwell during a step, ring us rather than buying more."],"hear":"\"I didn't know the online ones were different.\"","mark":"Driving and safety advice given"},{"h":"Support and close","say":["We'll offer psychological help for the anxiety alongside the reduction.","I'll write to your GP with the plan, with the dose changes checked by our pharmacist, and we'll review every few weeks."],"hear":"\"That's less frightening than I expected.\"","mark":"GP letter, pharmacist check and regular reviews"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Dose and supply","Diazepam 15 mg and lorazepam 1 mg daily, plus online diazepam","Dependent; one prescriber; stop the online supply first"],["Withdrawal risk","Shaking and unreality before; no fits; 6 units a week","Lower seizure risk, but a slow taper is still essential"],["Mood and risk","Low at times; no suicidal thoughts; family protective","Treat the anxiety and grief alongside"],["Taper","Fear of being cut off","Switch to diazepam, small cuts every 2–4 weeks at her pace"],["Driving","Drives to work daily","Advise not to drive if impaired; online tablets add risk"]]},"key":"Her tolerance sets the pace, not the calendar. Switch to one long-acting drug, cut in small steps, pause when needed and treat the anxiety beneath it; going too fast is what makes reductions fail.","objmap":[[3],[2],[5],[6]]},"c-7-2-4":{"name":"Not psychosis: GBL withdrawal delirium","why":"Recognises Jamie's GBL withdrawal as a medical emergency and steers the A&E team to benzodiazepine-led medical care, not a psychiatric ward.","frame":["Dosing pattern and timing of last dose","Delirium or primary psychosis","Physical severity and other drugs","Benzodiazepine-led medical care","Capacity, MCA and restraint","Chemsex, sexual health and safeguarding"],"steps":[{"h":"Get the timeline from his friend","say":["Before he goes anywhere, can I hear the timeline from his friend?","Ollie, how often was he using, and when was the last dose?"],"hear":"(Ollie) \"Every hour or two, day and night, for months. Last one about eight last night.\"","mark":"Hourly GBL for months; last dose about 7 hours ago: high-risk withdrawal"},{"h":"Check consciousness","say":["Jamie, I'm one of the doctors. Do you know where you are and what day it is?","What are you hearing right now?"],"hear":"\"I'm at a party... There's men behind the curtain coming to get me.\"","mark":"Disorientated: delirium, not primary psychosis"},{"h":"Obs and other drugs","say":["What are his obs?","Ollie, anything else: crystal meth, alcohol, benzodiazepines?"],"hear":"(Registrar) \"HR 128, BP 165 over 100, temp 38.1, GCS 14.\" (Ollie) \"Meth on Friday, a few drinks.\"","mark":"Autonomic instability: medical emergency"},{"h":"Advise treatment","say":["This is GBL withdrawal delirium, and it can kill. He needs medical care, not a psychiatric ward.","Start benzodiazepine early and in high doses, titrated until he settles, and check TOXBASE; baclofen on specialist advice.","He needs close monitoring, possibly HDU or ITU. An antipsychotic isn't first line."],"hear":"(Registrar) \"So he's ours, then. What dose are we talking?\"","mark":"Medical admission with benzodiazepine-led treatment"},{"h":"Capacity and the right law","say":["He can't weigh information right now, so treat him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act.","The Mental Health Act isn't the route: a physical illness is causing his confusion.","Any restraint should be the least possible, by trained staff, with obs throughout."],"hear":"(Registrar) \"Fine, I'll document capacity.\"","mark":"MCA, not MHA; restraint minimised"},{"h":"Chemsex and sexual health","say":["Ollie, Jamie said I can update you. Does he go to chemsex parties?","We'll offer sexual health follow-up, including HIV and STI testing and PEP if needed, and ask about any exploitation."],"hear":"(Ollie) \"Yeah, sometimes. He goes to the clinic now and then.\"","mark":"Chemsex context: sexual health and safeguarding covered"},{"h":"Plan the next days and close","say":["Once he's settled, reduce the benzodiazepine slowly, guided by symptoms, with the addiction team.","Before discharge, refer him to the specialist service; tolerance falls fast, so going back to old doses can be fatal."],"hear":"(Registrar) \"Okay. I'll call the medics now.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Dosing pattern","Every hour or two for months; last dose 8 pm","Severe dependence; withdrawal starts within hours"],["Consciousness","Thinks he is at a party; does not know the day","Delirium, not primary psychosis"],["Physical signs","HR 128, BP 165/100, temp 38.1, GCS 14","Medical emergency: HDU or ITU level monitoring"],["Treatment","Registrar asking for a psychiatric bed","Early high-dose benzodiazepine; TOXBASE; MCA not MHA"],["Context","Chemsex parties, weekend meth","Sexual health, safeguarding and specialist drug service"]]},"key":"GBL withdrawal starts within hours and looks like psychosis, but it is delirium. Early, generous benzodiazepine under medical care is right; an antipsychotic or a section to a psychiatric ward is the dangerous mistake.","objmap":[[1],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-7-2-5":{"name":"Hot and agitated in the 136 suite; ketamine next door","why":"Treats Connor's stimulant toxicity as a medical emergency within section 136 rules, and gives Chloe a brief, practical ketamine response.","frame":["Observations and temperature","Agitation: benzodiazepines first","Restraint risk","Section 136 time limits and A&E transfer","Ketamine bladder damage","Harm reduction and referral"],"steps":[{"h":"Observations first","say":["Can I have a full set of obs now, including temperature?","Connor, I'm one of the doctors. What have you taken tonight?"],"hear":"\"Couple of lines and a pill at a party. My heart's going mad.\"","mark":"Cocaine and MDMA from about 20:00"},{"h":"Gauge severity","say":["Any chest pain or headache? Do you take antidepressants?","How much water have you drunk?"],"hear":"(Nurse) \"Temp 39, pulse 140, BP 170 over 105, NEWS2 high.\" (Connor) \"No pain. Loads of water.\"","mark":"Stimulant toxicity with hyperthermia; low sodium possible"},{"h":"Restraint and timing","say":["PC Davies, how was he held, and for how long?","Please avoid holding him face down. When did you arrive here?"],"hear":"(PC Davies) \"Cuffed about five minutes in the van, not face down. We got here at 21:50.\"","mark":"Restraint risk noted; arrival 21:50 recorded"},{"h":"Transfer and treatment","say":["He needs A&E now: cooling, IV fluids, ECG, and bloods including CK, sodium and kidney function.","For agitation, benzodiazepine first, by mouth if he'll take it; avoid haloperidol unless advised, and follow TOXBASE.","Section 136 continues in A&E. The 24 hours run from 21:50 and can be extended by 12 if he isn't fit to be assessed."],"hear":"(Sam, AMHP) \"I'll come to A&E once he's medically fit.\"","mark":"Transfer to A&E under s136"},{"h":"Chloe: ask about her bladder","say":["Chloe, you mentioned ketamine and pain passing urine. How often are you going, and have you seen blood?","Do you need more than before to get the same effect?"],"hear":"\"I'm going all the time, saw blood once last week, and get tummy cramps. I need more now.\"","mark":"Likely ketamine bladder damage; tolerance"},{"h":"Chloe: advice and referral","say":["Ketamine can scar the bladder. Cutting down or stopping early gives it the best chance to recover.","I'll ask your GP for a urine test and a urology referral, and refer you to the local drug service.","You've been feeling low: any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"Low, but no thoughts of hurting myself.\"","mark":"Harm reduction, urology and drug service referral"},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["Connor's 136 assessment finishes in A&E once his temperature and CK settle.","Chloe gets a GP letter today and a safety plan for her mood."],"hear":"(Nurse) \"The ambulance is on its way.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Issue","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Temperature and obs","39.0, pulse 140, BP 170/105","Medical emergency: cooling, fluids, CK and ECG"],["Agitation","Restless, clenched jaw after cocaine and MDMA","Benzodiazepines first; avoid haloperidol without advice"],["Restraint","Cuffed five minutes, not prone","Keep holds brief; prone restraint can kill"],["Section 136","Arrived 21:50","24-hour clock keeps running in A&E; 12-hour extension possible"],["Ketamine","Frequent painful urination, blood once, tolerance","Bladder damage: urine test, urology and drug service"]]},"key":"A hot, sweating, agitated man in the 136 suite is a medical emergency before he is a mental health assessment. Take his temperature first; cooling and benzodiazepines save lives, while prone restraint and haloperidol can kill.","objmap":[[2],[4],[3,4],[5]]},"c-7-3-1":{"name":"Treat both together: three dual diagnosis plans","why":"Agrees joint plans for Liam, Karen and Josh that treat mental illness and substance use together without excluding anyone.","frame":["Motivational, non-judgemental approach","Psychosis and cannabis","Depression and alcohol dependence","ADHD and cocaine","Joint working and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Liam: explore the cannabis","say":["Liam, what does skunk do for you?","Have you noticed any link between how much you smoke and the voices?"],"hear":"\"It calms me. But the voices get louder when I smoke more. Both times I came in I'd been smoking loads.\"","mark":"He sees the link between cannabis and relapse"},{"h":"Liam: build motivation","say":["On a scale of nought to ten, how ready are you to cut down?","We'll keep treating your psychosis whatever you decide, and Priya can work with you on the cannabis.","Would you like your mum involved through the early intervention team?"],"hear":"\"Maybe a five. I could try cutting down.\"","mark":"Ambivalent: motivational work with integrated care"},{"h":"Karen: dependence and risk","say":["How do your mornings feel before a drink?","Have you ever had a seizure or seen things when you stopped?","When you've been drinking, do thoughts of not being here come up?"],"hear":"\"Shaky and sweaty; sometimes a drink before work. No fits. When I'm drunk I think I'd be better off not here, but my son keeps me going.\"","mark":"Alcohol dependence; suicidal thoughts when drunk"},{"h":"Karen: safe plan","say":["Please don't stop all at once; your body depends on it now. We'll plan a supervised detox with chlordiazepoxide and thiamine.","Your depression treatment carries on alongside, not after.","At this level of drinking you shouldn't drive, and you'll need to tell the DVLA."],"hear":"\"I need the car for work.\"","mark":"Medically managed withdrawal, thiamine and DVLA advice"},{"h":"Josh: weigh the misuse risk","say":["How often do you use cocaine, and has anyone else ever had your tablets?","Any chest pain, palpitations or fainting?","I'd suggest a long-acting form, such as modified-release methylphenidate or lisdexamfetamine, which is harder to misuse, with small supplies."],"hear":"\"Couple of weekends a month. Never sold them. No chest pain.\"","mark":"ADHD medication restarted in a low-misuse form"},{"h":"Josh: safety","say":["Cocaine with a stimulant strains the heart, so I'll check your pulse and blood pressure before and during treatment.","If your ADHD or the medicine affects your driving, you need to tell the DVLA."],"hear":"\"Fair enough. I'll try to cut down.\"","mark":"Cardiovascular monitoring and driving advice"},{"h":"Joint working and close","say":["Priya, can you pick up all three for joint work with the addiction service?","Nobody gets discharged for using. We'll review each of them within four weeks."],"hear":"(Priya) \"Yes, I'll see them this week.\"","mark":"Joint plan with dual diagnosis worker"}],"table":{"cols":["Pairing","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Psychosis and cannabis","Voices louder with more skunk; both admissions after heavy use","Motivational work, early intervention family input, keep on caseload"],["Depression and alcohol","Morning shakes, drink before work, suicidal thoughts when drunk","Planned detox with thiamine, safety plan, DVLA advice"],["ADHD and cocaine","Cocaine two weekends a month; no diversion","Long-acting stimulant, small supplies, heart checks"],["Joint working","Dual diagnosis worker available","Treat both conditions together, not one after the other"]]},"key":"Substance use is a reason to treat both conditions together, not to wait. 'Come back when you've stopped' shuts out the people at highest risk; match each plan to its own risk.","objmap":[[7],[4],[5],[7]]},"c-8-1-1":{"name":"Why has Ben changed? Pain, bowels, fits, medicines, surroundings","why":"Finds the physical or situational cause of a non-verbal man's new behaviour before anyone reaches for more risperidone.","frame":["Pain","Bowels","Seizures","Medicines","Environment"],"steps":[{"h":"Safety first","say":["Thanks for ringing, Lorraine. Before anything else, is Ben safe right now? Any head wound, vomiting, fever or unusual sleepiness?","Is anyone on your team hurt and needing treatment?"],"hear":"'He's in his room rocking. No blood, no sick. Two of my staff have bruises.'","mark":"No emergency right now"},{"h":"Set the frame","say":["I can hear how hard this has been for your team, and I'm taking it seriously.","When someone can't tell us what's wrong, a sudden change in behaviour is usually how they tell us. Can we go through pain, bowels, fits, medicines and his surroundings before we talk about doses?"],"hear":"'Fine, but we need something today. My staff are getting hurt.'"},{"h":"Pain","say":["Is he hitting one part of his head or body more than the rest?","How is he with food? Chewing on one side, or spitting out hard things?","When did he last see a dentist?"],"hear":"'Always the right side, by his ear. He holds his jaw and spits out toast. Dentist was over a year ago.'","mark":"Possible dental or ear pain identified"},{"h":"Bowels","say":["When did he last open his bowels properly?","Any soiling in his pad, a swollen tummy or being sick?","Is he on a laxative, and is he actually getting it?"],"hear":"'Five days, just smears since. His tummy looks bigger. The lactulose ran out a fortnight ago.'","mark":"Constipation with likely overflow identified"},{"h":"Seizures and medicines","say":["Any fits, staring spells, sudden falls or wet beds?","Has anything in his tablets been started, stopped or changed in the last two months, including anything bought over the counter?"],"hear":"'He wet the bed once last week. No fits we've seen. Just his risperidone and valproate, and the lactulose that ran out.'","mark":"Seizure activity not excluded"},{"h":"Surroundings and ABC charts","say":["What has changed at the house or in his week lately?","What do your ABC charts show about when it happens?"],"hear":"'A loud new lad moved in three weeks ago, his day centre's shut, and it's worst at mealtimes and during personal care.'","mark":"Environmental triggers identified"},{"h":"Explain and agree a plan","say":["Putting it together, the jaw-holding, the food spitting and five days without a proper bowel motion, I think Ben is most likely in pain. More risperidone would quieten the signal and leave the cause.","I'll ask his GP to see him within 48 hours, with adjustments: a quiet slot, a familiar staff member and time to settle. I'll also ask for his lactulose to be reissued today.","Could you start daily bowel, ABC and pain charts, and tell me who on your team will own them?"],"hear":"'I'll put my senior on the charts. But last time he bit the nurse.'","mark":"Shared plan agreed, no dose increase"},{"h":"Safety-net and close","say":["If he vomits, his tummy goes hard and swollen, he becomes drowsy or he has a fit, ring 999.","I'll ring you after the GP visit, and we'll review together in two weeks, including whether he still needs the risperidone at all."],"hear":"'Right. That's clearer than I expected. Thank you.'","mark":"Red flags and review date given"}],"table":{"cols":["Cause area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Pain","Hits right side by his ear, holds his jaw, spits out crunchy food, no dentist for over a year","Dental or ear pain is likely; needs a mouth and ear check with adjustments"],["Bowels","No proper motion for five days, smearing, bigger tummy, lactulose not reordered","Constipation, possibly impaction with overflow; can become dangerous"],["Seizures","One wet bed, no fits seen","Night seizures possible; check valproate level and watch at night"],["Medicines","Risperidone and valproate unchanged; laxative stopped by accident","No new drug to blame, but the missing laxative matters"],["Environment","Loud new housemate, day centre shut, incidents at meals and personal care","Adds stress; meals and personal care also fit with mouth and tummy pain"]]},"key":"The request is for more medicine; the job is to find the message. In a man who cannot speak, new behaviour means pain, constipation, seizures or a drug effect until shown otherwise, and sedation hides all four.","objmap":[[7],[7,8],[7],[7],[8]]},"c-8-1-2":{"name":"Telling Chloe about her tablet: one idea at a time, checked back","why":"Gives Chloe a plan she truly understands and owns, rather than a 'yes' given to please.","frame":["Why we want to change it","How: small, slow steps","What we will check","What if she feels cross","Her choice and her helpers"],"steps":[{"h":"Talk to her first","say":["Hello Chloe, thanks for coming in. Which name should I use for you?","You're not in trouble at all. I'd like to talk about your white tablet. Is it all right if Sam stays with us?"],"hear":"'Chloe's fine. Yeah, Sam can stay.'","mark":"Her agreement to key worker being present"},{"h":"Find her starting point","say":["What do you know about the white tablet you take at night?","How has your body been feeling lately?"],"hear":"'It's for when I got cross. I've got bigger and I'm sleepy in the mornings.'","mark":"Weight gain and sedation noticed by her"},{"h":"Ask about hidden side effects","say":["Some women on this tablet notice changes in their periods. When did you last have one?","Sometimes a bit of milk comes from the breasts. Lots of people find that embarrassing to mention. Has it happened to you?"],"hear":"'No period for about a year. And yes, sometimes milk.'","mark":"Raised prolactin likely"},{"h":"Idea one: why","say":["You've been calm for over three years. That's really good.","Now the tablet may be causing more problems than it solves: the weight, the sleepiness and your periods.","Can you tell me, in your words, why we want to change it?"],"hear":"'Cos I'm not cross now and it's making me big and sleepy.'","mark":"Teach-back: reason understood"},{"h":"Idea two: how","say":["We won't stop it all at once. We take away a tiny bit, wait a few weeks, and see how you are.","Look at this picture: here's the tablet now, and here are the small steps down.","What happens first?"],"hear":"'A little bit less. Then wait.'","mark":"Teach-back: small steps understood"},{"h":"Idea three: if she feels cross","say":["Some people worry they'll get angry again. If you feel cross or upset, tell Sam or ring the number on this sheet.","If that happens we can slow down or go back a step. You won't be in trouble."],"hear":"'So if I get cross I tell Sam, and we go slower?'","mark":"Fear addressed; she can explain what to do"},{"h":"Agree and close","say":["When would you like to start the first small step?","We'll check your weight, blood pressure and some blood tests now and again later on. Sam can help you remember.","Here's an easy read sheet with our number. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"'Next week. Can Sam keep a copy of the sheet too?'","mark":"Start date and sharing agreed with her consent"}],"table":{"cols":["Idea","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Why change it","Calm for three years; bigger, sleepy, no period, milk from breasts","Benefit now unclear and harms are real: reduce under STOMP"],["How","'A little bit less, then wait'","She understands gradual reduction, shown by teach-back, not by 'yes'"],["What we will check","Agrees to weight, BP and bloods","Baseline for weight, glucose, lipids and prolactin to track recovery"],["If she feels cross","Knows to tell Sam and that it can be slowed","Fear eased; she has a plan she can use"],["Her choice and helpers","Chose the start date; happy for Sam to help","Decision made with her, support shared with her consent"]]},"key":"A quick 'yes' is not understanding. Give one idea, then ask her to put it in her own words; only teach-back shows the plan has landed.","objmap":[[4],[7],[1],[6,7],[2,3]]},"c-8-1-3":{"name":"Psychosis or his autism? New versus usual","why":"Separates new psychotic symptoms from Jamie's autistic baseline, rules out mimics, and keeps care as unrestrictive as safety allows.","frame":["His usual self (baseline)","New psychotic features","Mimics: catatonia, cannabis, delirium","Risk","Least restrictive care"],"steps":[{"h":"Calm the space","say":["Jamie, I'm one of the doctors. It's loud here, so let's go somewhere quieter.","I'll speak slowly, one person at a time. If you need a break, hold up your hand and we'll stop."],"hear":"'Too loud. Make it stop.' (settles once in the side room)","mark":"Sensory distress reduced"},{"h":"His usual self","say":["Mrs Walsh, what is Jamie like on a good day?","When did you first notice a change, and what was the first thing?"],"hear":"'He loves his trains and his college. Six weeks ago he stopped going and started talking to himself.'","mark":"Clear change from baseline, with onset date"},{"h":"New psychotic features","say":["Jamie, tell me about the wires in the walls.","Do you hear anyone talking when nobody is in the room? What do they say?","How sure are you that the neighbours put the wires there?"],"hear":"'A man through the wall says I'm stupid. They put wires in to listen. I know they did.'","mark":"Persecutory delusion and voices identified"},{"h":"Rule out mimics","say":["Mrs Walsh, has he had times of freezing, staying stuck in odd positions, or stopping eating and drinking?","Jamie, do you smoke anything or take anything to relax?","I'll also check his temperature, pulse, blood sugar and a urine test."],"hear":"'Weed with a lad from college. Most days.' Mother: 'No freezing, he's drinking, no new tablets.'","mark":"Daily cannabis identified; catatonia unlikely"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Have you thought about doing something about the neighbours?","Is there anything in your room you could hurt someone, or yourself, with?","Have you had thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"'There's my craft knife for models. I don't want to hurt anyone. I don't want to die.'","mark":"Access to a blade and self-neglect; no current intent"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["Mrs Walsh, this looks like a new illness on top of his autism, possibly made worse by the cannabis. It isn't just his autism.","If it can be made safe, I'd like the crisis team to see him at home with an autism-friendly plan, rather than a ward. Could the craft knife be locked away?","I'll alert his learning disability team so a care and treatment review can be arranged quickly."],"hear":"Mother: 'So he's not going to be locked up on a ward?'","mark":"Least restrictive plan offered"},{"h":"Tablets, cannabis and close","say":["Jamie, you don't have to decide about tablets today. We can talk about what might help the voice when you're ready.","Cutting down the cannabis may help the voice too. If things get worse tonight, your mum can ring the crisis line or bring you back."],"hear":"'No tablets today. OK.'","mark":"Safety-net given"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Baseline","Loved trains and college; six weeks of withdrawal and self-talk","A clear break from his usual self, dated by his mother"],["Psychotic features","Wires in the walls; a man's voice calling him stupid for a month","Persecutory delusion and auditory hallucinations: new psychosis"],["Mimics","Daily cannabis; no freezing; drinking; no new drugs","Cannabis may be driving it; catatonia and delirium less likely but obs and tests still needed"],["Risk","Craft knife at home; not eating or washing; no intent","Remove the blade; self-neglect matters; monitor risk to neighbours"],["Least restrictive care","Mother fears a ward; he refuses tablets","Home treatment with adjustments if safe, plus urgent care and treatment review"]]},"key":"Self-talk, rigid ideas and sensory distress can all be autism. What marks psychosis is change from his own baseline: new beliefs, voices and lost interests, dated by the people who know him.","objmap":[[1],[2],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-8-2-1":{"name":"Can Kelly decide about sertraline? The four abilities","why":"Shows lawfully, with her own words as evidence, whether Kelly can make this one decision, then acts on the result.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up","say":["Kelly, today is about one decision: whether to start a tablet for your low mood. There are no wrong answers.","Would you like Jo to come in, or shall we talk on our own first?"],"hear":"'Just us is OK. Jo can come after.'","mark":"Her choice of support recorded"},{"h":"Mood and safety","say":["Tell me how things have been since your dad died.","Sometimes when people feel this sad they think about not being here. Has that happened to you?"],"hear":"'I cry every day. Sometimes I want to be with Dad. I wouldn't do anything. My cat and my sister keep me going.'","mark":"Passive wish to die; no plan; protective factors"},{"h":"Give the information her way","say":["This is sertraline. It can lift mood and help sleep, but it takes a few weeks to work. Some people feel sick or get an upset tummy for the first week or two.","Another choice is talking to someone, like bereavement counselling. Or you could wait and see.","It won't change who you are."],"hear":"'OK. Yes.'"},{"h":"Understand","say":["What is the tablet for? Say it any way you like.","What might happen when you start it?"],"hear":"'It's for the sad. It might make me feel sick at first.'","mark":"Understands: yes"},{"h":"Retain","say":["We've talked about a few other things since. Can you remember the other choices I mentioned?","And what did I say the tablet might do to your tummy?"],"hear":"'Talking to someone, or waiting. And it might make me sick.'","mark":"Retains: yes"},{"h":"Use and weigh","say":["What's good about taking the tablet, for you?","And what's the bad side?","How do you decide between them?"],"hear":"'It might help me sleep. I'm scared it'll change me, but you said it won't. And I can still talk to someone.'","mark":"Weighs: yes"},{"h":"Communicate and conclude","say":["So what would you like to do?","I think you can make this decision yourself, Kelly. I'll write down what you told me."],"hear":"'I'll try the tablet. And I want to talk to someone too.'","mark":"Has capacity for this decision"},{"h":"Act on it and close","say":["We'll start next week. If you feel sick or worse in yourself, tell Jo or ring us.","I'll see you in two weeks and refer you for bereavement support.","If you ever feel close to acting on wanting to be with your dad, ring the crisis line or 999."],"hear":"'Can Jo help me remember the tablet?'","mark":"Plan agreed; safety-net given"}],"table":{"cols":["Ability","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","'It's for the sad. It might make me feel sick at first.'","Grasps purpose and main side effect"],["Retain","Recalled talking help, waiting, and nausea after other topics","Holds the information long enough to decide"],["Use and weigh","Set sleep benefit against fear of change and kept talking help as well","Balances pros and cons in her own terms"],["Communicate","'I'll try the tablet. And I want to talk to someone too.'","Clear, consistent choice: has capacity for this decision"]]},"key":"A string of 'yes' answers proves nothing either way. Capacity is shown by what she can explain in her own words, about this one decision, after you have given every practicable help.","objmap":[[3],[3,4],[1],[1],[6,8]]},"c-8-2-2":{"name":"Can Paul decide about the operation? Fear versus inability","why":"Separates Paul's fear from his ability to decide, judges capacity fairly with Dean's help, and gives the surgeons a lawful next step.","frame":["All practicable help","Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate"],"steps":[{"h":"Pin down the decision","say":["What exactly is the decision: a keyhole gallbladder operation this admission?","What are the main benefits, the risks, the other options, and what happens if he doesn't have it?","Saying no isn't the same as lacking capacity, so let's not discharge him yet."],"hear":"Registrar: 'Lap chole now. Without it, more attacks, maybe pancreatitis. He's said no, so we'll discharge him, yes?'","mark":"Decision defined"},{"h":"Agree roles with Dean","say":["Dean, thank you, you clearly know Paul well. I need to hear his answers in his own words.","Could you sit with him and prompt if he gets stuck, rather than answering for him?"],"hear":"'He never understands these things. But OK, I'll try.'","mark":"Support worker role agreed"},{"h":"Find the fear","say":["Paul, I'm not here to make you do anything. You're not in trouble.","What is scary about hospital?"],"hear":"'Needles hurt. They held me down.'","mark":"Refusal driven by past restraint"},{"h":"Give the information his way","say":["You have stones in your gallbladder. That's what makes your tummy hurt after food.","The doctors can take it out through tiny cuts while you're asleep. You'd usually go home in a day or two.","Tablets help the pain for a while, but the stones stay and the pain comes back."],"hear":"'Tablets instead?'","mark":"Information given simply, with pictures"},{"h":"Understand and retain","say":["What's making your tummy hurt?","What do the doctors want to do?","A few minutes later: what was the operation for?"],"hear":"'Stones. They want to cut. Tablets make it go away.'","mark":"Understands partly; retains basics"},{"h":"Use and weigh, communicate","say":["What might happen if you don't have the operation?","What's good about having it? What's bad?"],"hear":"'Don't know. No hospital.'","mark":"Cannot weigh the cost of refusing today"},{"h":"Conclude, then reassess","say":["Paul, thank you. I'll come back tomorrow with pictures, and Dean can be there.","To the team: today he can't weigh what happens without surgery, even with support. Morphine and fear may be playing a part, so I'll reassess once before a final view."],"hear":"Dean: 'Can't his mum just sign the form?'","mark":"Lacks capacity today; reassess"},{"h":"Advise the team","say":["Dean, no one can sign for another adult unless they hold a lasting power of attorney for health or are a court-appointed deputy.","If he still lacks capacity, the surgeons decide in his best interests at a meeting. His only family can't be consulted and Dean is paid staff, so an IMCA must be instructed for serious treatment like this.","Adjustments: no restraint, numbing cream, music on headphones, a theatre visit, and Dean with him to the anaesthetic room."],"hear":"Registrar: 'So we keep him in and book the meeting.'","mark":"Best interests route and IMCA advised"}],"table":{"cols":["Ability","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["All practicable help","Calmer with time, Dean beside him, pictures; afraid after being held down","Fear is a barrier to address, not proof of incapacity"],["Understand","Knows pain is in his tummy and doctors want to 'cut'","Partial understanding of the problem and the operation"],["Retain","Repeated the key facts a few minutes later","Can hold basic information"],["Use and weigh","Believes tablets end the pain; cannot say what happens without surgery","Cannot weigh the consequence of refusing: lacks capacity today"],["Communicate","'No hospital', said consistently","Can communicate; his wish counts in any best interests decision"]]},"key":"'No' is a decision, not a diagnosis. Find why he refuses and give the information his way first. Fear of needles calls for adjustments; being unable to weigh what refusal means is what points to lacking capacity.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3],[8],[1,2]]},"c-8-2-3":{"name":"'I want her sterilised': hear the cry, protect the daughter","why":"Answers the request honestly, finds the exhaustion and the risk behind it, and builds a plan that keeps Amy's rights at the centre.","frame":["The request and the law","Safeguarding","Amy's own capacity and wishes","Mother's strain","Less restrictive options"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge","say":["Thank you for coming, Mrs Cole. You look worn out.","Take your time. Tell me what's been happening."],"hear":"'I can't cope with a pregnancy on top of everything. I want Amy sterilised.'"},{"h":"Be honest early about the law","say":["I can hear how frightened you are, and I want to help. But I need to be straight with you: I can't arrange sterilisation on your request.","Amy is an adult, and no parent can consent for another adult. If she couldn't decide for herself, a permanent operation like this would need the Court of Protection, and it would look for less restrictive options first."],"hear":"'I'm her mother. Why can't I decide?'","mark":"Legal position stated"},{"h":"Explore the relationship","say":["Tell me about Lee. How old is he, and how did they meet?","Has Amy said or done anything that worried you?"],"hear":"'He's about thirty, a volunteer at the centre. She came home with a love bite and said it was their secret.'","mark":"Possible exploitation identified"},{"h":"Safeguarding","say":["An older volunteer in a position of trust, asking her to keep secrets, worries me too.","I need to raise this as a safeguarding concern with the local authority today, and the day centre must be told.","Has she ever seemed frightened, hurt or been given gifts by him?"],"hear":"'Not that I know of. Will she get into trouble?'","mark":"Safeguarding referral agreed"},{"h":"Amy's voice","say":["What does Amy say about Lee?","Has she ever had relationships and sex education she could follow?","I'd like to see Amy on her own, to hear her views and check what she understands about relationships and sex."],"hear":"'She says she loves him and wants to marry him. Nobody's ever taught her properly.'","mark":"Capacity for sex and for contraception to be assessed separately"},{"h":"The mother herself","say":["You said 'on top of everything'. How are you, in yourself?","How are you sleeping? Has it ever got so bad you thought of hurting yourself, or Amy?"],"hear":"'I cry most days, four hours' sleep. No, I'd never hurt her. Am I a terrible mother for asking?'","mark":"Carer strain and low mood; no risk to self or Amy"},{"h":"Options and close","say":["You're not a terrible mother. You asked because you're scared and doing this alone.","There are safer steps: relationships education adapted for Amy, and, if she chooses, long-acting contraception she understands, through her GP or sexual health clinic.","I'll refer you for a carer's assessment and suggest you see your GP about your mood. We'll meet again after I've seen Amy."],"hear":"'I didn't know I could ask for help for me.'","mark":"Shared plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The request and the law","'I'm her mother. Why can't I decide?'","No parent can consent for an adult; sterilisation without consent needs the Court of Protection"],["Safeguarding","Volunteer about 30, love bite, 'their secret'","Possible grooming by someone in a position of trust: refer today"],["Amy's capacity and wishes","Loves Lee, wants to marry; no adapted sex education","See Amy alone; assess capacity for sex and contraception as separate decisions"],["Mother's strain","Sole carer, cries daily, four hours' sleep, no carer's assessment","The request is partly a cry for help; carer's assessment and GP review"],["Less restrictive options","Asked about an injection without Amy knowing","Education and LARC with Amy's informed consent; nothing covert"]]},"key":"The request is rarely the real question. Behind 'sterilise her' sit exhaustion and fear; behind the boyfriend may sit exploitation. Treat both, and keep Amy's own rights and voice at the centre.","objmap":[[2],[2],[7],[2,7],[7]]},"c-8-3-1":{"name":"Is it dementia? Her best, the decline and what else it could be","why":"Builds the picture of change against Susan's own best, screens for treatable mimics, and gives Margaret an honest answer and a clear plan.","frame":["Her best (baseline)","Pattern of decline","New seizure","Treatable mimics","Planning ahead"],"steps":[{"h":"Include Susan","say":["Hello Susan, hello Margaret. Susan, is it OK if I ask you some questions, and Margaret too?","Margaret, I'll answer your question properly, but I need the full picture first."],"hear":"Susan: 'Yes!' Margaret: 'Fine, but I want it straight.'"},{"h":"Her best","say":["When was Susan at her very best? What could she do then?","Do you have anything from that time, such as a video, a day-centre report or an earlier memory assessment?"],"hear":"'About two years ago she did her own bus pass, made tea, did the shopping. I've a video of her dancing at a wedding.'","mark":"Baseline established, about two years ago"},{"h":"What has changed","say":["What can't she do now that she could then?","Has her personality changed: more irritable, quieter, losing interest?","Is it worse at certain times of day?"],"hear":"'She repeats herself, gets lost, can't do buttons, and she's snappy in the evenings and dozes in the day.'","mark":"Progressive loss of skills identified"},{"h":"The seizure","say":["Tell me about the seizure. What did it look like, and when did it happen?","Any sudden jerks since, especially just after waking?"],"hear":"'She jerked suddenly when she woke, then shook all over. Nothing's been started.'","mark":"Late-onset seizure: red flag"},{"h":"Treatable mimics","say":["When were her thyroid, hearing and eyesight last checked?","Does she snore or seem to stop breathing in her sleep?","Has anything upsetting happened, like losing someone close?"],"hear":"'Not for over two years. She snores loudly. Her best friend moved away in the spring.'","mark":"Thyroid, sensory loss, sleep apnoea and low mood to exclude"},{"h":"Answer straight","say":["Honestly, it might be dementia. People with Down syndrome get Alzheimer's more often and younger, and a first seizure at her age adds to that worry.","But thyroid problems, poor hearing or sight, poor sleep and low mood can look just the same, and they're treatable, so we check those first.","That means blood tests, hearing and eye tests, an epilepsy review, and a memory assessment compared with her own best."],"hear":"'How long has she got? Is there a tablet for it?'"},{"h":"Plan ahead and close","say":["If it is Alzheimer's, there are medicines that help some people for a while; we'll talk about that once we're sure.","While Susan can still have her say, it's worth thinking together about a lasting power of attorney and what she'd want later on.","And Margaret, you're carrying a lot. Let's look at support for you too. I'll see you both when the results are back."],"hear":"'That helps. I've been worrying who'll look after her when I can't.'","mark":"Next steps and carer support agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her best","Bus pass, tea, shopping two years ago; wedding video","A personal baseline to measure decline against"],["Pattern of decline","Repeats, gets lost, loses dressing skills, irritable evenings","Progressive loss of skills and personality change, typical of early Alzheimer's in Down syndrome"],["New seizure","Jerk on waking then generalised shaking","Late-onset seizures strongly suggest Alzheimer's; needs epilepsy review"],["Treatable mimics","No thyroid, hearing or sight check for two years; loud snoring; friend moved away","Hypothyroidism, sensory loss, sleep apnoea and depression must be excluded first"],["Planning ahead","Margaret worried about the future and her own health","Raise LPA and advance care planning while Susan can contribute; carer support"]]},"key":"Judge decline against her own best, not population norms or a standard memory test. And check treatable mimics first: in Down syndrome, thyroid disease, sensory loss and depression often look exactly like dementia.","objmap":[[1,2],[6],[6],[6],[7]]},"c-8-3-2":{"name":"Stiff and seeing children: safety first, then the Lewy body pattern","why":"Spots a dangerous antipsychotic reaction, recognises the core features of dementia with Lewy bodies against his own baseline, and gives Grace a safe plan.","frame":["Antipsychotic sensitivity","Fluctuation","Visual hallucinations","Acting out dreams","Parkinsonism","Delirium mimics"],"steps":[{"h":"Safety first","say":["Grace, thank you for bringing him. Before anything else: is he hot or sweaty, very stiff, drinking and passing urine?","I'd like to check his temperature, pulse and blood pressure now, and feel how stiff his arms are."],"hear":"'Not hot. Very stiff, sleeps in his chair, barely drinking since Tuesday.'","mark":"Likely antipsychotic sensitivity; NMS to exclude"},{"h":"Speak to Mr Shah","say":["Mr Shah, I'm a doctor. How are you feeling today?","You told Grace about children in your room. Can you tell me about them?"],"hear":"'Tired. The children come at night. They stand there.'","mark":"Well-formed visual hallucinations"},{"h":"His best and the good and bad days","say":["Grace, what could he do a year ago?","Does he have good days and bad days? How quickly does it change?","Does he see anything else that isn't there?"],"hear":"'He made his own toast and did jigsaws. Some days he stares and is muddled, next morning he's joking. And he sees a cat on the sofa. There's no cat.'","mark":"Marked fluctuation; recurrent visual hallucinations"},{"h":"Sleep and movement","say":["What's he like at night? Any shouting or thrashing, as if acting out a dream?","Has his walking changed? Any falls or shaking?"],"hear":"'Night staff hear him shouting and thrashing; he fell out of bed in March. Three falls, small shuffling steps, his right hand shakes.'","mark":"REM sleep behaviour disorder and parkinsonism identified"},{"h":"Exclude delirium","say":["Any recent infection, constipation, pain, or new tablets apart from the haloperidol?","When were his eyes last checked?"],"hear":"'Urine was clear last month, bowels normal for him. Glasses not checked for two years.'","mark":"No clear delirium cause; repeat bloods and urine"},{"h":"Explain","say":["I think he may have a type of dementia called dementia with Lewy bodies. It would explain the good and bad days, the visions, the shouting at night and the shuffling.","People with this condition can react badly to medicines like haloperidol. The stiffness and sleepiness are likely that, so we'll stop it today, and I'll ring the GP myself."],"hear":"'Was it my fault for asking for the tablet?'","mark":"Haloperidol stopped"},{"h":"Ease the guilt, manage the visions","say":["No. You saw he was distressed and asked for help. That's good care.","When he sees the children, don't argue. Say you can't see them, check he feels safe, and comfort him. Good lighting at night helps."],"hear":"'OK. We can do that.'"},{"h":"Plan and safety-net","say":["We'll do bloods including CK, and arrange a specialist memory assessment compared with how he used to be.","We'll put an alert on his record and hospital passport: avoid antipsychotics.","If he gets hot, very stiff, more confused or can't drink, call 999."],"hear":"'I'll add the alert to his passport tonight.'","mark":"Alert placed; 999 triggers given"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antipsychotic sensitivity","Stiff, drowsy, barely drinking two days after haloperidol","Severe sensitivity reaction; stop now and check for NMS"],["Fluctuation","Muddled then sharp, changing within an hour","Core feature of DLB; delirium still to be excluded"],["Visual hallucinations","Children at night; a cat on the sofa for months","Recurrent, well-formed: core feature"],["Acting out dreams","Shouting and thrashing in sleep; fell out of bed","REM sleep behaviour disorder: core feature"],["Parkinsonism","Falls, small shuffling steps, resting tremor","Core feature; adds to falls risk"],["Delirium mimics","Urine clear, bowels normal, eyes not checked","No clear trigger; recheck bloods and urine, arrange eye test"]]},"key":"With fluctuation, visions, acting out dreams and parkinsonism, even a small antipsychotic dose can be dangerous. Stop the drug first, then work towards a diagnosis; do not simply swap to another dopamine blocker.","objmap":[[1],[6],[3],[8],[8]]},"c-8-3-3":{"name":"Behaviour and epilepsy: timing, pain, medicines, then the health check","why":"Links Amira's behaviour to her seizures, pain and losses, closes the seizure safety gaps, and gets her annual health check back on track.","frame":["Seizure timing","Pain","Medicines","Environment","Seizure safety","Annual health check"],"steps":[{"h":"Greet both","say":["Hello Amira. Mrs Hassan, how does Amira tell you yes, no, or that something hurts?","Thank you for pushing for this. You know her best."],"hear":"'A few Makaton signs. She hums when she's happy and leads you by the hand. Nobody listens to me.'","mark":"Communication profile noted"},{"h":"Seizure link","say":["How often is she having seizures now, compared with before?","When is the hitting out worst: just before a seizure, straight after, or any time?"],"hear":"'Up from two to five a month, mostly at night. She's worst the morning after.'","mark":"Seizures increased; post-ictal pattern"},{"h":"Pain","say":["Does she hold any part of her face or body?","How is she with food? When did she last see a dentist?","Any period pain, falls or bruises?"],"hear":"'She holds the side of her face and won't eat toast or apples. Dentist three years ago. Cramps with periods, and a bruise on her shin from a fall.'","mark":"Possible dental pain identified"},{"h":"Medicines","say":["What changed with her tablets, and when?","Did anything get better or worse after the lamotrigine went up?"],"hear":"'Lamotrigine went up about four weeks ago. Hard to tell, she was already bad.'","mark":"Dose change to review with the epilepsy nurse"},{"h":"What changed around her","say":["Has anything changed at the home: staff, routine, activities?","What happens at mealtimes?"],"hear":"'Her favourite key worker left in August. She screams at mealtimes.'","mark":"Loss of key worker; mealtime trigger"},{"h":"Seizure safety","say":["Do night staff know her seizure plan? Is her rescue midazolam in date?","Rescue midazolam is only for prolonged seizures, never for upset behaviour."],"hear":"'They don't really know the plan. Someone asked about giving it when she kicks off. Could she die from a seizure at night?'","mark":"Rescue plan gap; misuse of midazolam flagged"},{"h":"Explain risk and plan","say":["There is a small risk of sudden death in epilepsy, higher with night seizures. That's why we act now: an epilepsy nurse review, night-time monitoring and staff training on her plan.","I don't think a calming tablet is the answer. Pain, more seizures and losing her key worker are the likely causes.","I'll ask the GP for a dental check, a physical review and her annual health check with a health action plan."],"hear":"'So the calming tablet's off for now?'","mark":"Annual health check and health action plan requested"},{"h":"Charts and close","say":["Could staff keep a seizure diary and an ABC chart side by side for four weeks?","I'll review with you in four weeks. Here's our number if things get worse before then."],"hear":"'Finally someone's listening.'","mark":"Review booked"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Seizure timing","Five a month, mostly at night; worst the morning after","Post-ictal behaviour; seizure control needs review"],["Pain","Holds her face, refuses hard food, no dentist for three years","Dental pain likely; urgent dental check with adjustments"],["Medicines","Lamotrigine raised four weeks ago","Review with the epilepsy nurse; a sedative would add risk"],["Environment","Favourite key worker left; screams at meals","Loss and mealtime pain both feed the distress"],["Seizure safety","Night staff unsure of plan; midazolam maybe out of date; asked to use it for behaviour","SUDEP risk; fix the rescue plan, train staff, never use midazolam for behaviour"],["Annual health check","None for two years","Book it now with a health action plan"]]},"key":"Ask when the behaviour happens relative to seizures. Distress the morning after a night seizure calls for better seizure control and a pain check, not sedation, and rescue midazolam is never a behaviour drug.","objmap":[[2],[3],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-8-4-1":{"name":"'Put the risperidone up': function first, STOMP, then PBS","why":"Answers the staff's real need without raising the dose, finds what Jake's behaviour is communicating, and starts a safer plan.","frame":["What the behaviour is for","Physical health","Mental health","Risperidone: benefit and harm","Positive behaviour support"],"steps":[{"h":"Greet Jake first","say":["Hello Jake. I'm one of the doctors. How do you like to talk: words, pictures, your iPad?","Dana, Leon, is anyone hurt, and is everyone safe right now?"],"hear":"Leon: 'Two staff hurt this month. We need something now.'","mark":"No immediate danger"},{"h":"Walk through the last incident","say":["Talk me through the last one: when, where, who was there, and what happened just before?","And what happened afterwards?"],"hear":"Dana: 'Tuesday, 10 o'clock in the kitchen. An agency worker told him there was no centre. After, he got his iPad in his room and calmed down.'","mark":"Trigger: loss of routine; ends with escape to calm"},{"h":"What has changed","say":["What's changed in his life this year?","Jake, what would you like to do in the day?"],"hear":"'His day centre shut in the summer and half the team left.' Jake: 'Can I go back to the centre?'","mark":"Loss of day service and familiar staff identified"},{"h":"Physical and mental health","say":["Any pain, constipation, poor sleep or low mood?","When did he last have his weight, blood pressure, bloods and an ECG?"],"hear":"'Sometimes constipated. Sleeps fine, not low. No bloods or ECG that we know of. He's always hungry.'","mark":"Metabolic monitoring overdue"},{"h":"The risperidone story","say":["Why was the risperidone started? Has he ever had psychosis?","Has anyone reviewed whether it still helps?"],"hear":"'Started for behaviour when he moved in. Never psychotic. Never reviewed.'","mark":"Antipsychotic for behaviour without psychosis: STOMP applies"},{"h":"Explain and hold the line","say":["I understand your staff are getting hurt, and that has to change. But more risperidone is unlikely to fix this; it adds weight and can even make people more restless.","The national STOMP programme asks us to use these medicines for behaviour only when other help has failed or the risk is severe, and to review them regularly.","Losing his centre and familiar faces looks like the main trigger. That needs a behaviour plan, not a bigger dose."],"hear":"'So you're doing nothing?'"},{"h":"The plan","say":["Not at all. From today: ABC charts for four weeks, a positive behaviour support referral, and a push for a new day activity.","We'll check his weight, bloods, blood pressure and ECG, and sort his bowels.","Once he's steadier, we'll plan a slow, supported reduction of the risperidone, involving Jake and his mum, and checking whether he can make that decision himself."],"hear":"'When do we talk about the dose, then?'","mark":"PBS referral and monitoring agreed"},{"h":"Close","say":["We'll review in four weeks with the charts. If someone is at serious risk before then, ring us the same day.","Jake, we'll work on getting you back to a centre."],"hear":"'Four weeks, then. OK.'","mark":"Review date set"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What the behaviour is for","Kitchen, morning, told 'no centre'; calms with iPad in his room","Distress at lost routine, with escape to a calm activity"],["Physical health","Sometimes constipated; always hungry; no bloods or ECG","Treat constipation; metabolic checks are overdue"],["Mental health","Sleeps well, not low","No clear depression or anxiety disorder; keep watching"],["Risperidone: benefit and harm","Started for behaviour, never psychotic, never reviewed, 14 kg gain","STOMP case: do not increase; plan slow reduction once settled"],["Positive behaviour support","Day centre closed, many agency staff","PBS assessment, structured days and consistent staff are the real treatment"]]},"key":"Staff injury is real, but the dose is not the lever. Find what the behaviour is doing for him, here loss of routine and familiar people, and treat that; risperidone reduction comes later, slowly, once he is settled.","objmap":[[6,7],[2],[5],[7],[7]]},"c-8-4-2":{"name":"Why does she bite? ABC charts and a first support plan","why":"Teaches Leah's parents to record what happens around the behaviour, finds its likely function, and starts simple proactive and reactive steps.","frame":["Antecedent","Behaviour","Consequence","Function","Proactive strategies","Reactive strategies"],"steps":[{"h":"Safety and settle","say":["Hello Leah. Can I see your hand? Mr and Mrs Carter, is the skin broken or red anywhere?","What would help you most from today?"],"hear":"Paul: 'Why does she do it? And what do we write on these charts?'","mark":"Hand checked; no infection"},{"h":"Explain ABC","say":["Behaviour is usually Leah's way of telling us something. ABC charts help us work out what.","A is what happened just before. B is exactly what she did. C is what happened straight after."],"hear":"'So we write the before, the what and the after.'","mark":"ABC understood"},{"h":"Practise on yesterday","say":["Let's fill one in together for yesterday. What was going on at ten past three?","What exactly did she do? Just what you saw, not why.","And what happened next?"],"hear":"'I was on the phone, telly on, she was pacing. She bit her right hand twice and threw the remote. We took her upstairs with ear defenders and she calmed.'","mark":"First ABC entry completed"},{"h":"Look for the pattern","say":["Is it usually the same time of day?","What did her afternoons look like at school?"],"hear":"'Always about three. At school, afternoons were swimming and the sensory room.'","mark":"Loss of afternoon structure after school"},{"h":"Check for pain","say":["Some people bite their hand when something hurts. How are her periods?","When did she last see a dentist? Does she ever hold her jaw or ear?"],"hear":"'Heavy periods with cramps. Dentist two years ago.'","mark":"Period and dental pain to exclude"},{"h":"Function and first plan","say":["My best guess: the noise is too much and the afternoons are empty, and biting gets her to somewhere quiet.","Proactive: a calm afternoon routine, ear defenders before the noise starts, and a sensory activity around three.","Reactive: if she starts, lower the noise, give her space and offer the ear defenders. Please don't hold her hands; it can hurt her and make it worse."],"hear":"'What if we don't know what came before?'","mark":"Likely function: escape from noise and an empty afternoon"},{"h":"Charts for the next weeks","say":["If you didn't see what came before, write 'not seen'. That still helps.","Keep the charts for two to four weeks, and I'll ask the GP to look at her periods and arrange a dental check."],"hear":"'OK. We can do that.'"},{"h":"The parents","say":["How are you both managing? Do you get any breaks?","I'll refer you for a carer's assessment and short breaks, and ask our behaviour specialist to build a full plan with you."],"hear":"Julie: 'Paul's stopped work. We've had no break since she left school.'","mark":"Carer's assessment and PBS referral made"}],"table":{"cols":["ABC item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antecedent","About 3 pm, TV on, father on the phone, neighbour's drill","Noise and no structured activity set it off"],["Behaviour","Bit right hand twice, threw the remote","Recorded as seen, without guessing why"],["Consequence","Taken upstairs with ear defenders; calmed","The behaviour reliably ends the noise"],["Function","School afternoons were swimming and sensory room","Escape from sensory overload and an empty afternoon; pain still to exclude"],["Proactive and reactive","Parents asked about holding her hands","Prevent with routine and ear defenders; respond calmly; no restraint"]]},"key":"The chart is not a diary of bad days. Record what you saw, not why; the 'why' emerges from the pattern over weeks, and the plan should prevent the trigger, not just respond to the bite.","objmap":[[3,8],[5,7],[5],[8],[1,4]]},"c-g-01":{"name":"Chest pain after panic: clear the heart before you reassure","why":"Finds or excludes a cardiac, PE or aortic cause on evidence, so any later talk of panic is honest.","frame":["Pain history","Hands and pulse","BP and neck","Praecordium","Lung bases and legs"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce, consent, chaperone","say":["Mrs Doyle, I'm Dr Ade, the doctor on call. Could you give me your date of birth and full name?","I'm taking this pain seriously. I'll ask a few quick questions, then check your heart properly. Is that all right?","Jo will stay with us. Can you sit back at about this angle for me?"],"hear":"\"Fourteenth of March, 1965. Please don't just tell me it's nerves again.\"","mark":"Consent given, chaperone present"},{"h":"Focused pain history","say":["Point with one finger to where it hurts. Is it there right now?","Has this tightness ever come on when you were walking or climbing stairs?","Does it spread to your arm, jaw or back? Any sweating, sickness or feeling faint?"],"hear":"\"Like a band round my chest. I've had it on the stairs at home for a month and told no one.\"","mark":"Exertional chest pain identified"},{"h":"Hands and pulse","say":["I'm starting with your hands. I'll press a fingernail for five seconds and see how fast the colour returns.","Now I'm feeling the pulse at both wrists together.","Your hands are warm, which is a good sign."],"hear":"\"Is it racing? It feels like it's racing.\"","mark":"Peripheries warm, CRT under 2 s, pulse 92 and regular"},{"h":"Blood pressure in both arms, face and neck","say":["I'll take the blood pressure in each arm. A big difference between them is something I'd want to know about.","Rest your head back and turn it a little to the left. I'm looking at a vein in your neck.","Look up for me while I check your eyes, then open your mouth."],"hear":"\"Is my blood pressure high again?\"","mark":"BP 138/84 both arms, JVP not raised"},{"h":"Praecordium","say":["I'll look at your chest, then feel for the heartbeat just under your left breast.","Now I'm listening in four places. Just breathe normally.","Roll onto your left side, then sit forward, breathe out and hold it for me."],"hear":"\"What can you hear?\"","mark":"Apex undisplaced, heart sounds I and II, no murmur or rub"},{"h":"Lung bases, ankles and calves","say":["I'll listen at the bottom of your lungs from behind. Deep breaths through your mouth.","Now your ankles and calves. Tell me if anything is tender when I press."],"hear":"\"No, that doesn't hurt.\"","mark":"Chest clear, no oedema, calves soft: DVT not suggested"},{"h":"Tell her the findings and the plan","say":["Your pulse, blood pressure, heart sounds and heart tracing are all normal. That's reassuring, but it isn't the whole answer.","Because the tightness also comes on the stairs, I'm sending a blood test called troponin now and a medical doctor will see you today.","If the pain returns or gets worse, tell the nurse at once. We'll repeat the tracing and call an ambulance if it changes."],"hear":"\"So you're not just saying it's in my head?\"","mark":"ACS not excluded: troponin and same-day medical review"}],"table":{"cols":["Examination stage","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Pain history","Central band at rest today; also on the stairs for a month","Possible new angina; panic cannot be the working diagnosis yet"],["Hands and pulse","Warm, CRT under 2 s, 92 regular","No shock, no AF"],["BP and neck","138/84 in both arms, JVP not raised","Dissection and right heart strain unlikely"],["Praecordium","Apex normal, I + II + 0","No sign of failure, valve disease or pericarditis"],["Lung bases and legs","Clear bases, no oedema, soft calves","No pulmonary oedema; PE less likely but not excluded"]]},"key":"A normal examination and ECG lower the risk; they do not clear the heart. Exertional tightness in a 61-year-old smoker needs troponin and medical review before panic becomes the explanation.","objmap":[[1],[4,6],[7],[7],[2,5]]},"c-g-02":{"name":"Fever on clozapine: the chest and the clozapine dangers","why":"Finds the pneumonia, grades its severity and checks for neutropenia, myocarditis and a rising clozapine level before deciding on tonight's dose.","frame":["Hands and face","Neck and trachea","Expansion and percussion","Auscultation and vocal resonance","Clozapine dangers"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and consent","say":["Mr Mensah, I'm Dr Ade, the ward doctor. Sister Grant says you've got a cough and a temperature.","I'd like to ask a few questions, then examine your chest. You'll need your top off; Sister Grant will stay. Is that OK?"],"hear":"\"Yeah. I feel rough. You're not taking my clozapine off me, are you?\"","mark":"Consent given, chaperone present"},{"h":"Focused history with clozapine in mind","say":["Any sore throat or ulcers in your mouth?","Any pain in your chest? Is it worse when you breathe in? Does your heart feel fast, and can you lie flat?","Do you ever choke on drinks or wake with a wet pillow? And when did you last smoke?"],"hear":"\"Sharp pain low on the right when I breathe in. Pillow's soaked most mornings, I coughed on my tea yesterday. Stopped smoking when I came in.\"","mark":"Aspiration risk and smoking cessation identified"},{"h":"Hands, face and neck","say":["Stretch your hands out in front of you. I'm checking your fingers and nails.","I'll keep my fingers on your pulse for a moment.","Stick out your tongue. Now I'll feel the glands in your neck from behind and check your windpipe."],"hear":"\"My heart's going fast.\"","mark":"HR 108, RR 22 counted, no cyanosis, trachea central"},{"h":"Front of the chest","say":["I'll put my hands on your chest. Take a big breath in.","Now I'm tapping across your chest, side to side.","Say 'ninety-nine' each time I move. Then deep breaths through your mouth while I listen."],"hear":"\"Ninety-nine.\"","mark":"Front of chest: expansion equal, resonant, clear"},{"h":"Back of the chest","say":["Sit forward and hug yourself for me.","Same again: hands, tapping, 'ninety-nine', then deep breaths."],"hear":"\"That's the sore bit, low down on the right.\"","mark":"Right base dull, coarse crackles, increased vocal resonance: consolidation"},{"h":"Severity and clozapine checks","say":["Your temperature, pulse and breathing are all up, so I'm treating this as a chest infection that needs close watching.","Clozapine can lower white cells, inflame the heart muscle, and its level rises with infection or after stopping smoking.","So I'm sending urgent bloods tonight: white cells, CRP, kidney and liver tests, troponin and a clozapine level, plus a heart tracing."],"hear":"\"Is my heart all right?\"","mark":"NEWS2 5, CRB-65 0; neutropenia and myocarditis to exclude"},{"h":"Agree the plan for tonight","say":["I'd like to hold tonight's clozapine until the blood count and troponin are back. It's a pause, not stopping it.","If your white cells are normal, I'll start amoxicillin, which doesn't affect clozapine.","If your oxygen falls, your breathing gets harder or the bloods are abnormal, you'll go to the medical hospital tonight."],"hear":"\"OK, as long as I get it back.\"","mark":"Plan agreed with transfer triggers"}],"table":{"cols":["Examination stage","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Hands and face","HR 108, RR 22, no cyanosis","Systemic response; sats 94% need watching"],["Neck and trachea","Trachea central, no nodes","No collapse or effusion pulling the trachea"],["Expansion and percussion","Right base dull","Consolidation (or small effusion) at the right base"],["Auscultation and vocal resonance","Coarse crackles, increased resonance right base","Right lower lobe pneumonia, likely aspiration with hypersalivation"],["Clozapine dangers","Week 3 of titration, stopped smoking a week ago","Myocarditis window, neutropenia possible, level likely raised"]]},"key":"On clozapine, a fever is never just a chest infection. Check neutrophils, troponin and the level, and remember that stopping smoking alone can raise the level by half.","objmap":[[3,4],[6,7],[6],[7],[7]]},"c-g-03":{"name":"Twelve days without a bowel movement on clozapine: the abdomen","why":"Recognises clozapine-induced bowel obstruction or ileus as a surgical emergency and looks for liver disease from his drinking.","frame":["General inspection","Hands, face and chest","Palpation","Percussion and auscultation","Hernias and rectum"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce, consent, chaperone","say":["Mr Pickering, I'm Dr Ade. The nurse tells me your bowels haven't moved for nearly two weeks and you've been sick.","I'd like to ask a few questions and examine your tummy. Nurse Patel will stay with us. Is that all right?"],"hear":"\"Go on. It's really swollen and sore.\"","mark":"Consent given, chaperone present"},{"h":"Focused history","say":["When did you last pass wind?","What colour was the sick? Does the pain come in waves?","Have you been taking your laxatives? And how much do you drink when you're on leave?"],"hear":"\"No wind since Wednesday. Sick was greenish. I stopped the laxatives, they made me rush. Six cans most days.\"","mark":"Absolute constipation and bilious vomiting identified"},{"h":"General look, hands, face and chest","say":["Lie flat with one pillow. I'm looking at you from the end of the bed first.","Hold your arms out and bend your wrists back for me. Keep them there.","Look up so I can see the whites of your eyes."],"hear":"\"Like this?\"","mark":"Visibly distended, dry; no flap, jaundice or spider naevi"},{"h":"Palpation","say":["Tell me if anything hurts. I'll watch your face as I go.","I'm pressing gently all over first, then a little deeper.","Now breathe in deeply while I feel for your liver and spleen."],"hear":"\"Ow, it's sore all over, worse in the middle.\"","mark":"Distended, diffusely tender, no guarding or rigidity; liver not enlarged"},{"h":"Percussion and listening","say":["I'm going to tap across your tummy now.","I'll listen with the stethoscope for a minute. Try to keep still."],"hear":"\"What can you hear?\"","mark":"Tympanic throughout, high-pitched tinkling bowel sounds: obstruction"},{"h":"Hernias and rectal examination","say":["I need to check your groins for a hernia. Give a cough for me.","A finger examination of your back passage would tell me if the bowel is blocked low down. It's your choice, with Nurse Patel here."],"hear":"\"If it helps. Just be quick.\"","mark":"No hernia; rectum empty"},{"h":"Explain and act","say":["I think your bowel has stopped working and may be blocked. Clozapine slows the gut, and that can be dangerous.","You need to see the surgeons at the main hospital today. No food for now, and no enemas.","I'm holding clozapine and the hyoscine until the surgeons are happy, and we'll restart carefully with the pharmacist."],"hear":"\"Will I get my clozapine back after?\"","mark":"Surgical transfer arranged; clozapine and hyoscine held"}],"table":{"cols":["Examination stage","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["General inspection","Distended, dry, HR 104, T 37.6","Dehydration and a systemic response"],["Hands, face and chest","No flap, jaundice, palmar erythema or spider naevi","No decompensated liver disease today; still check LFT"],["Palpation","Diffuse tenderness, no peritonism","Obstruction or ileus without signs of perforation yet"],["Percussion and auscultation","Tympanic, tinkling sounds","Mechanical-type obstruction picture"],["Hernias and rectum","No hernia, rectum empty","Not low faecal impaction; the problem is higher"]]},"key":"Clozapine constipation can kill. Bilious vomiting and no wind turn 'constipated' into 'obstructed': stop enemas and stimulant laxatives and send him to the surgeons today.","objmap":[[6],[5,7],[7],[7],[5,6]]},"c-g-04":{"name":"A swollen neck on lithium: the thyroid examination","why":"Confirms a goitre, looks for hypothyroid signs and red flags, and plans treatment that keeps her on lithium.","frame":["Inspection and swallow","Palpation from behind","Nodes, percussion and bruit","Hands, pulse and eyes","Reflexes and proximal power"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce, consent, chaperone","say":["Mrs Clarke, I'm Dr Ade. You mentioned your neck looks fuller in photos. I'd like to ask a few questions and examine your neck and thyroid.","Nurse Ahmed will stay with us. Could you loosen your top button?"],"hear":"\"Yes, that's fine. It's been about six months.\"","mark":"Consent given, chaperone present"},{"h":"Brief thyroid history and the lithium record","say":["Any neck pain, change in your voice, or trouble swallowing or breathing?","Do you feel the cold more than others? Any dry skin or constipation?","Your last thyroid test was 14 months ago, which is overdue on lithium, so I'll put that right today."],"hear":"\"No pain, voice is fine. I'm always cold now and my skin's dry.\"","mark":"Hypothyroid symptoms; no compressive symptoms"},{"h":"Inspection, swallow and tongue","say":["Tip your head back slightly. I'm looking at the front of your neck.","Take a sip of water and hold it, then swallow when I say.","Now stick your tongue out for me."],"hear":"\"Like that?\"","mark":"Diffuse swelling rises on swallowing, not on tongue protrusion: goitre, not thyroglossal cyst"},{"h":"Palpation from behind","say":["I'll stand behind you and feel your neck. Tilt your chin down a little and tell me if anything is sore.","Another sip and swallow while my hands are here.","Now I'm feeling the glands along your neck and above your collarbones."],"hear":"\"No, it's not sore.\"","mark":"Smooth, firm diffuse goitre, no nodule; no lymphadenopathy"},{"h":"Percussion and bruit","say":["I'll tap gently on your upper breastbone.","Now I'll listen over each side of your neck. Hold your breath for a few seconds."],"hear":"\"OK.\"","mark":"No retrosternal dullness, no bruit"},{"h":"Hands, pulse, eyes, reflexes and power","say":["Hold your arms out with your fingers spread. I'm looking for a tremor and feeling your pulse.","Follow my finger up and down with your eyes, keeping your head still.","I'll test the reflex at your ankle, then ask you to stand from the chair with your arms folded."],"hear":"\"My hands have always shaken a bit.\"","mark":"Fine tremor, pulse 58, no eye signs, slow-relaxing ankle jerks"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["Your thyroid is evenly enlarged and your pulse and reflexes are slow, which fits an underactive thyroid. Lithium causes this quite often.","The good news is that it's treatable with a thyroid tablet, and you can stay on lithium, which has kept you well.","I'm sending thyroid tests, calcium, kidney function and a lithium level. A scan is only needed if a lump or a warning sign appears."],"hear":"\"So I don't have to stop the lithium?\"","mark":"Lithium continued; TFT, calcium, U&E and level sent"}],"table":{"cols":["Examination stage","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Inspection and swallow","Diffuse swelling moves with swallowing","Thyroid in origin"],["Palpation from behind","Smooth, firm, no nodule","Diffuse goitre, typical of lithium; no lump to refer urgently"],["Nodes, percussion and bruit","None","No red flags; no retrosternal extension"],["Hands, pulse and eyes","Pulse 58, no eye signs","Hypothyroid, not Graves'"],["Reflexes and proximal power","Slow-relaxing ankle jerks","Clinical hypothyroidism adding to her low mood"]]},"key":"Lithium-induced hypothyroidism is a reason to add levothyroxine, not to stop lithium. Check calcium too: lithium also causes hyperparathyroidism.","objmap":[[3,5],[6],[7],[7],[7]]},"c-g-05":{"name":"Sudden visual loss labelled functional: pupils, colour and fundi","why":"Tests each eye for an organic cause before any functional label, then screens mood and risk without letting them drive the diagnosis.","frame":["Acuity","Colour vision","Pupils and RAPD","Fields and eye movements","Fundoscopy"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and listen first","say":["Ms Bennett, I'm Dr Ade from the liaison psychiatry team. I'm here to understand what's happened to your sight, not to assume anything.","Can you tell me how the vision changed, and what you can and can't see now?","Has anything like this, or numbness or weakness anywhere, happened before?"],"hear":"\"It went blurry then dim over a morning. It's worse in the middle. My legs went numb for two weeks a few years ago.\"","mark":"Central visual loss and past neurological episode identified"},{"h":"Acuity and colour, each eye","say":["Cover your right eye and read the lowest line you can on this chart. Now the other eye.","Look at this red cap with your right eye, then your left. Is the red the same?","Does it hurt when you move your eyes?"],"hear":"\"Red looks washed out in the left. It aches when I look sideways.\"","mark":"Left acuity reduced, red desaturation and pain on eye movement"},{"h":"Pupils and swinging light","say":["I'm going to shine a light in each eye. Keep looking at the far wall.","Now I'll swing the light from one eye to the other a few times."],"hear":"\"That's bright.\"","mark":"Left relative afferent pupillary defect present"},{"h":"Fields and eye movements","say":["Sit facing me, about an arm's length away. Cover your right eye and look at my nose.","Say 'now' when you first see my finger. Then tell me if any part of my face is missing.","Follow my finger without moving your head. Tell me if you see double."],"hear":"\"Your nose is a bit grey in the middle.\"","mark":"Left central scotoma; eye movements full, no diplopia"},{"h":"Fundoscopy","say":["I'll dim the lights. Keep your eyes on that spot on the wall even if my head gets in the way.","I'm coming in close now from the side to look at the back of each eye.","For a second, look straight at the light."],"hear":"\"OK, I'm looking at the spot.\"","mark":"Left disc normal, no haemorrhages or emboli: retrobulbar"},{"h":"Mood and risk, separately","say":["You've had a lot happen recently with your job and relationship. How's your mood been?","Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes 'what's the point'. No plans. My sister and my cat keep me going.\"","mark":"Passive thoughts, no plan, clear protective factors"},{"h":"Explain and recommend","say":["I've found real physical signs in your left eye, particularly in how the pupil reacts. This isn't 'all in your head'.","I'll ask the medical team for an eye specialist review today and a neurology opinion with an MRI scan.","I'll stay involved to support you while the tests are done."],"hear":"\"Finally. Thank you for actually checking.\"","mark":"Same-day ophthalmology and neurology recommended; functional label withdrawn"}],"table":{"cols":["Examination stage","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Acuity","Reduced left, normal right","Genuine monocular loss"],["Colour vision","Left red desaturation","Optic nerve involvement"],["Pupils and RAPD","Left RAPD","Objective organic sign; cannot be produced by choice"],["Fields and eye movements","Left central scotoma, pain on movement","Typical of optic neuritis"],["Fundoscopy","No retinal haemorrhage or emboli","Retrobulbar optic neuritis likely; with past leg numbness, think MS"]]},"key":"Stress in her life is not evidence of a functional cause. An RAPD is objective, and a functional diagnosis rests on positive features, never on a missing examination.","objmap":[[2,3],[1,3],[7],[6],[1]]},"c-g-06":{"name":"Tingling and stumbling after nitrous oxide: the limb neurological examination","why":"Maps his signs to dorsal column and peripheral nerve damage from B12 inactivation, so treatment starts today.","frame":["Red flags","Gait and Romberg","Tone and power","Reflexes and plantars","Sensation and coordination"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and screen red flags","say":["Mr Walsh, I'm Dr Ade. The nurse says your feet and fingers have been tingling and you keep stumbling. I'd like to examine your nerves.","Any back pain, numbness between your legs, or change in your bladder or bowels?"],"hear":"\"No back pain, nothing like that. Just the tingling and I keep bumping into walls at night.\"","mark":"Cauda equina and cord compression red flags absent"},{"h":"Non-judgemental drug and diet history","say":["Lots of people use different substances. Have you used anything like laughing gas, nitrous oxide?","How much, how often, and when was the last time? And what do you eat in a normal day?"],"hear":"\"The big canisters, most weekends for a year, then every day. Last time the night before I came in. I mostly eat toast and pizza.\"","mark":"Heavy nitrous oxide use and poor diet identified"},{"h":"Gait and Romberg","say":["Walk to the door and back, then heel to toe as if on a tightrope.","Stand with your feet together, arms by your sides. I'll stand close. Now close your eyes."],"hear":"\"Whoa, I can't stay up with my eyes shut.\"","mark":"Broad-based gait, Romberg positive: sensory ataxia"},{"h":"Inspection, tone and power","say":["Lie on the couch. I'm looking at your legs, then I'll roll and lift them; let them go floppy.","Now push and pull against me: lift your leg, bend your knee, point your foot up and down.","Same for your arms: shoulders, elbows, wrists and grip."],"hear":"\"I feel weaker than I should.\"","mark":"Tone normal, mild distal weakness 4/5, no clonus"},{"h":"Reflexes and plantars","say":["I'll tap your knees and ankles. If I need to, I'll ask you to clench your teeth.","Now I'm running something along the sole of each foot."],"hear":"\"That tickles.\"","mark":"Knee jerks present, ankle jerks absent, plantars upgoing: mixed UMN and LMN"},{"h":"Sensation and coordination","say":["Close your eyes. Tell me when you feel the tuning fork buzzing, and when it stops.","I'm moving your big toe up or down. Tell me which way.","Run this heel down your other shin, then touch my finger and your nose."],"hear":"\"I can't tell which way it's going.\"","mark":"Vibration and joint position lost to the knees; heel-shin clumsy with eyes closed"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["The nerves that carry position and vibration sense are damaged. Nitrous oxide knocks out vitamin B12, which those nerves need.","I'm taking bloods now and starting B12 injections today, without waiting for results. Neurology will see you and arrange an MRI of your spine.","Stopping the gas is the most important step, and we'll get the drug team to help."],"hear":"\"Will it get better?\"","mark":"IM hydroxocobalamin started; urgent neurology referral"}],"table":{"cols":["Examination stage","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Red flags","No back pain, saddle or sphincter change","No emergency cord or cauda equina picture"],["Gait and Romberg","Broad-based, falls with eyes closed","Sensory ataxia from dorsal column loss"],["Tone and power","Normal tone, mild distal weakness","Peripheral neuropathy component"],["Reflexes and plantars","Absent ankle jerks, upgoing plantars","Subacute combined degeneration pattern"],["Sensation and coordination","Vibration and position lost to the knees","Large-fibre dorsal column damage, B12 related"]]},"key":"Absent ankle jerks with upgoing plantars is the signature of subacute combined degeneration. Serum B12 can be normal after nitrous oxide; treat on the clinical picture and check homocysteine or MMA.","objmap":[[1],[3],[],[7],[7]]},"c-g-07":{"name":"Spells of unreality with lip smacking: could it be focal epilepsy?","why":"Builds the episode from start to finish from her and the witness, recognises focal impaired awareness seizures, and makes her safe today.","frame":["Warning and onset","During the episode","Recovery","Cardiac and other causes","Safety and driving"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and the start of a spell","say":["Ms Hassan, I'm Dr Ade. I'd like to understand these spells in detail, with Nurse Okafor's help since she saw one.","What's the very first thing you notice when one starts?"],"hear":"\"A rising feeling in my stomach, and I feel like I've been here before.\"","mark":"Epigastric aura and déjà vu identified"},{"h":"During the spell: the witness","say":["Nurse Okafor, could you describe exactly what you saw, from start to finish?","Were her eyes open? Did she respond when you said her name or touched her arm?","How long did it last, and what was she doing with her mouth and hands?"],"hear":"\"(Nurse) Eyes open, staring, no response to name or touch, lip smacking and picking at her sleeve, about a minute.\"","mark":"Impaired awareness with automatisms identified"},{"h":"After the spell and pattern","say":["What do you remember of the middle of it? How do you feel afterwards?","How often do they happen, and have you ever woken with a sore tongue or found yourself on the floor?"],"hear":"\"I can't remember the middle. I'm muddled for a while. Two a week, more lately. Sore tongue once or twice.\"","mark":"Post-ictal confusion and nocturnal events identified"},{"h":"Background and other causes","say":["Did you have fits as a child, a head injury, or anyone in the family with epilepsy?","Any palpitations or blacking out when you stand? How much do you drink, and any drugs?","I'll do a heart tracing, lying and standing blood pressure, bloods and a nerve examination today."],"hear":"\"A fit with a fever when I was two. A couple of glasses of wine at weekends.\"","mark":"Febrile convulsion in childhood; cardiac screen planned"},{"h":"Driving and safety","say":["Have you had a spell while driving?","Until a specialist has seen you, please don't drive. You must tell the DVLA about these episodes.","Showers rather than baths, and no swimming or cooking alone on the hob for now."],"hear":"\"I had one at the wheel last month and only just pulled over.\"","mark":"Driving risk identified; advice given and recorded"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["The rising feeling, the déjà vu, the staring with lip smacking and the muddled recovery fit focal seizures from the temporal lobe better than panic or dissociation.","I'm referring you to the first seizure clinic for review within two weeks, with an MRI scan and an EEG.","A normal EEG wouldn't rule it out, so the nurses will keep a chart and video a spell if you agree."],"hear":"\"So it might not be anxiety after all?\"","mark":"Focal epilepsy suspected; neurology, MRI and EEG requested"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Warning and onset","Rising epigastric feeling, déjà vu","Typical temporal lobe aura"],["During the episode","Eyes open, unresponsive, lip smacking, picking, a minute","Focal impaired awareness seizure with automatisms"],["Recovery","Muddled ten minutes, no memory of the middle","Post-ictal state; panic and dissociation rarely do this"],["Cardiac and other causes","Febrile convulsion aged two; sore tongue on waking","Risk factor for mesial temporal sclerosis; possible nocturnal seizures"],["Safety and driving","Spell at the wheel last month","Must stop driving and tell the DVLA; written advice now"]]},"key":"Stereotyped spells with automatisms and post-ictal confusion are seizures until proved otherwise. Dissociation is diagnosed on positive features, and a normal EEG never excludes epilepsy.","objmap":[[2],[5],[4],[6],[5]]},"c-g-08":{"name":"BMI 14 on the medical ward: the history and MEED risk","why":"Shows the medical team how dangerous this is, using MEED markers, and leaves a safe refeeding and legal plan.","frame":["Eating and weight","Compensatory behaviours","Cognitions","MEED physical risk","Mood, risk and capacity"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and engage","say":["Ms Turner, I'm Dr Ade from the liaison psychiatry team. The medical team asked me to see you after you fainted.","I'd like to understand what eating has been like for you recently. Can you walk me through a normal day?"],"hear":"\"Black coffee, an apple, maybe some soup. Coursework made me forget to eat.\"","mark":"Severe restriction identified"},{"h":"Weight and compensatory behaviours","say":["What did you weigh before this started, and how quickly has it dropped?","Some people make themselves sick, use laxatives or exercise a lot to control weight. Does any of that apply to you?"],"hear":"\"About 52 kilos in February. I make myself sick after most meals. I walk two hours a day.\"","mark":"Purging and compulsive exercise identified"},{"h":"Cognitions and physical symptoms","say":["How do you feel about your body and your weight now?","Any dizziness, palpitations or chest pain? When was your last period?"],"hear":"\"I see fat on my stomach. I'm terrified of ballooning. No period for six months.\"","mark":"Body image disturbance and amenorrhoea identified"},{"h":"MEED physical risk and SUSS","say":["Your heart rate is 44, your blood pressure is low and your potassium and phosphate are low. Those all put your heart at risk.","Could you sit up from lying without using your hands? Now squat and stand up without your hands?"],"hear":"\"I can't get up without my hands. I'm just tired.\"","mark":"MEED high risk: BMI 14, HR 44, low K and PO4, SUSS failed"},{"h":"Mood and suicide risk","say":["How's your mood been? Have you had thoughts that it would be easier not to be here?","Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to harm yourself?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to wake up. No plans.\"","mark":"Passive death wish, no plan"},{"h":"Capacity for discharge","say":["The doctors think going home tomorrow could stop your heart. What do you understand about that risk?","If you went home, what would you eat tomorrow?"],"hear":"\"I'll just eat more. I'm not that thin.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh the risk: capacity for discharge in doubt"},{"h":"Advise the medical registrar","say":["Dr Rao, she's high risk on MEED and needs to stay. Dietitian-led refeeding, thiamine before feeding, daily phosphate, potassium and magnesium, and cardiac monitoring.","Supervised meals, an hour's observation after them for purging, and the eating disorder service involved today.","If she tries to leave, assess capacity; the MHA can authorise refeeding as treatment for anorexia."],"hear":"\"(Dr Rao) Fine. We'll keep her and start feeding tonight.\"","mark":"Refeeding plan and legal plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["MEED area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Eating and weight","52 kg to 39 kg since February, BMI 14","Rapid loss; very high medical risk"],["Compensatory behaviours","Vomiting after most meals, walking 2 h a day","Explains the low potassium; post-meal supervision needed"],["Cognitions","Fear of 'ballooning', sees fat","Anorexia nervosa, not simple forgetting to eat"],["MEED physical risk","HR 44, BP 86/54, K 2.9, PO4 0.8, SUSS failed","Red risk: inpatient refeeding with close monitoring"],["Mood, risk and capacity","Passive death wish; denies danger","Suicide risk to monitor; capacity to refuse doubtful"]]},"key":"'I just need to eat more' is the illness talking. Low phosphate before feeding starts makes refeeding syndrome likely, so feed promptly but monitor bloods daily rather than starting too low.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[4,7],[7],[7]]},"c-g-09":{"name":"Discharge when he cannot go home: news, needs and safeguarding","why":"Tells him about the court order honestly, finds out what he can and cannot do alone, and builds a discharge plan that also protects his wife.","frame":["The news","Daily living","Alcohol","Options and wishes","Safeguarding"],"steps":[{"h":"Check understanding and give the news","say":["Mr Pike, I'm Dr Ade, and this is Mrs Fry, the ward social worker. You're much better. What do you remember about why you came in?","I have some difficult news. Jean has a court order that means you can't return to the house or contact her."],"hear":"\"A row, and the police came. What do you mean, I can't go home? It's my house.\"","mark":"News given; shock and anger"},{"h":"Allow the reaction","say":["I can see that's a real shock. Take your time.","The order is a legal decision; we can't change it, but we can help you plan what comes next."],"hear":"\"Forty years in that house. She's been seeing someone, you know.\"","mark":"Jealous beliefs about Jean noted"},{"h":"Daily living","say":["Who did the cooking, the money and your tablets at home?","What can you manage on your own: washing, dressing, shopping?"],"hear":"\"Jean did all of it. I've never cooked. I can wash and dress myself.\"","mark":"Needs with cooking, money and medication identified"},{"h":"Alcohol","say":["How much were you drinking before you came in, in a usual day?","Was drink involved on the night the police came?"],"hear":"\"Four or five pints at the club and some whisky at home. I don't remember much of that night.\"","mark":"Harmful drinking and amnesia for the assault identified"},{"h":"Options and wishes","say":["There are some options: a flat with staff on site, a care home for a short time while housing is found, or staying with family if that works for them.","What matters most to you about where you live?"],"hear":"\"I want to be near Paul. A flat with someone on hand would suit me.\"","mark":"Preference for extra care housing near son"},{"h":"Agree the plan and who does what","say":["Mrs Fry will arrange a Care Act needs assessment and a housing referral. Our OT will check cooking, money and tablets.","The alcohol team will see you before discharge, and we'll repeat your memory test once you've been dry a few weeks.","We'll need to share your discharge address with the police and Jean's support worker. That's part of keeping everyone safe."],"hear":"\"I won't go near her. I just want to see Paul.\"","mark":"Plan agreed; safeguarding information to be shared"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The news","Shock; 'it's my house'","Needs time and a clear, repeated statement of the order"],["Daily living","Never cooked; Jean managed money and tablets","OT assessments and a blister pack before discharge"],["Alcohol","Daily pints and whisky, no memory of the night","Alcohol service before discharge; cognitive retest when dry"],["Options and wishes","Extra care flat near Paul","Realistic, supported option; interim placement if needed"],["Safeguarding","Believes Jean unfaithful; violent before","Ongoing risk to Jean: DASH, MARAC if high, police informed"]]},"key":"Discharge planning here is also safeguarding. His belief that Jean is unfaithful plus heavy drinking is a risk pattern, so share the plan with police and her IDVA, not just social care.","objmap":[[2],[6],[5],[6],[6]]},"c-p-01":{"name":"Planning a pregnancy on sertraline: relapse risk against medicine risk","why":"Helps her make an informed choice about sertraline before conception, starts folic acid and leaves a written plan she owns.","frame":["Her worries","Relapse risk","Risks to the baby","Preconception health","Her decision"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with what she has read","say":["Mrs Shah, it's great that you're planning ahead. Before I say anything, what have you read, and what worries you most?","Is Dev involved in this decision with you?"],"hear":"\"That SSRIs cause heart defects and babies are born withdrawing. I want to do everything right.\"","mark":"Fears about malformation and newborn withdrawal identified"},{"h":"Establish her relapse risk","say":["Can you tell me what happened when you stopped sertraline before?","How bad did things get in 2023? Did you have thoughts about ending your life?","Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby?"],"hear":"\"I stopped in 2022 and was ill again within three months. In 2023 I thought Dev would be better off without me. Mum had postnatal depression.\"","mark":"High relapse risk identified"},{"h":"Risks to the baby, in numbers","say":["Any baby has about a 2 to 3 in 100 chance of a birth defect. Most good studies find sertraline adds little or nothing to that.","About 1 in 3 babies exposed late in pregnancy are jittery or feed poorly for a few days. It settles on its own, and the midwives watch for it.","A rare breathing problem in newborns may rise from about 2 to 3 in 1,000. And untreated depression carries its own risks for you and the baby."],"hear":"\"That's much less scary than what I read. Can I breastfeed on it?\"","mark":"Risks understood in absolute terms"},{"h":"Preconception health","say":["Start folic acid 400 micrograms a day now, and keep it up until 12 weeks of pregnancy.","Sertraline is one of the preferred antidepressants for breastfeeding.","The safest choice is no alcohol once you start trying."],"hear":"\"I'm still on the pill. When should I stop it?\"","mark":"Folic acid started; alcohol advice given"},{"h":"Her options and her decision","say":["Your choices are to stay on 100 mg, to try a lower dose with close monitoring, or to stop slowly, which given your history carries a high chance of relapse.","Whatever you decide, if you find you're pregnant, please don't stop sertraline suddenly. Call me first.","Would you like to talk it over with Dev before we write it down?"],"hear":"\"I think I'll stay on it. I'd like to tell Dev tonight.\"","mark":"Informed decision to continue sertraline"},{"h":"Write the plan and close","say":["I'll write the plan and send it to you and your GP: sertraline, folic acid, and early signs of relapse to watch for.","I'll refer you to the perinatal team for preconception advice, and I'll see you again in three months.","If you get pregnant before then, ring the clinic and we'll bring the appointment forward."],"hear":"\"Thank you. I feel I can actually plan now.\"","mark":"Written plan copied to GP; perinatal referral"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her worries","Read that SSRIs cause heart defects and withdrawal","Correct with numbers, not 'completely safe'"],["Relapse risk","Relapsed within three months of stopping; suicidal in 2023","Stopping carries a real risk to her and the pregnancy"],["Risks to the baby","Relaxed once given absolute figures","Small, mostly short-lived risks set against a serious relapse"],["Preconception health","No folic acid; on the pill; two glasses of wine at weekends","Folic acid 400 micrograms now; no alcohol once trying"],["Her decision","Will continue; wants to tell Dev","Record it, copy to GP, perinatal referral"]]},"key":"The question is not 'is sertraline safe?' but 'which risk is smaller for her?'. With a quick, severe relapse after stopping, continuing is often the safer choice for mother and baby.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-p-02":{"name":"Bipolar I at 12 weeks: postpartum risk and the perinatal plan","why":"Gives her an honest postpartum relapse risk, keeps quetiapine going and starts a written perinatal plan everyone will share.","frame":["Current state and adherence","Early warning signs","Postpartum risk","Medication and baby","Perinatal and birth plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Current state and medication","say":["Congratulations, Leah. How are your mood and sleep at the moment?","Are you still taking quetiapine every night? Has sickness made you miss any?"],"hear":"\"I'm well. I missed two nights in early pregnancy with sickness. I wondered if I should stop it for the baby.\"","mark":"Well; adherence mostly good"},{"h":"Early warning signs from 2021","say":["Can you tell me how 2021 started? What did you notice first?","Tom, what would you notice first if Leah were becoming unwell?"],"hear":"\"I was sleeping three hours and buying things. Then I thought I had a mission. (Tom) She stops sleeping and gets snappy.\"","mark":"Relapse signature: sleep loss, spending, irritability"},{"h":"Postpartum risk, honestly","say":["You asked whether you could get ill after the birth. Yes, it's possible: roughly 1 in 5 women with bipolar I have a severe episode after birth.","The risk is highest in the first two weeks. Your aunt's bipolar disorder adds to it.","The good news is that planning and staying on treatment lower that risk."],"hear":"\"Will they take the baby away if I get ill?\"","mark":"Risk understood"},{"h":"Medication and the baby","say":["Stopping quetiapine would raise your chance of relapse a lot. I'd advise staying on 300 mg.","It can raise blood sugar, so you'll have a glucose test at 24 to 28 weeks, and your weight and blood pressure are checked at each visit.","The paediatric team will watch the baby for a few days after birth. Many women breastfeed on quetiapine, and we'll plan that with you."],"hear":"\"OK. As long as I know why.\"","mark":"Quetiapine continued; GTT and newborn observation planned"},{"h":"Answer the fear about the baby","say":["If you did become unwell, the aim is to keep you and your baby together, for example in a mother and baby unit.","Being ill is not a reason on its own to remove a baby. Planning ahead is exactly what keeps you both safe."],"hear":"\"That helps. Tom can take two weeks off, and my mum is twenty minutes away.\"","mark":"Support network identified"},{"h":"The written plan","say":["I'm referring you to the specialist perinatal team today. By 32 weeks we'll have a written plan shared with maternity, your GP and the health visitor.","It will cover your birth wishes, protecting your sleep after the birth, a few days' stay on the postnatal ward, and who to call the same day if warning signs appear.","Tom, if she sleeps less than four hours or seems unusually high or irritable, ring the number on the plan that day."],"hear":"\"(Tom) I can do that.\"","mark":"Perinatal referral; plan by 32 weeks; partner has contacts"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Current state and adherence","Well; missed two doses with sickness","Stable; support adherence through sickness"],["Early warning signs","Short sleep, spending, irritability, then a 'mission'","Specific signs for Tom and the plan"],["Postpartum risk","Aunt with bipolar; first baby","About 1 in 5, highest in the first two weeks"],["Medication and baby","Wondered about stopping quetiapine","Continue; glucose screening and newborn observation"],["Perinatal and birth plan","Tom on leave two weeks; mum nearby","Written plan shared by 32 weeks with same-day contacts"]]},"key":"Don't reassure her that she'll be fine, and don't frighten her. An honest risk figure with a concrete plan for sleep, warning signs and same-day help is what lowers the danger.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[6],[6]]},"c-p-03":{"name":"Severe fear of childbirth after a traumatic first birth","why":"Hears the first birth, screens for PTSD, depression and risk, and makes sure her choice of birth is respected.","frame":["The first birth","Trauma symptoms","Mood and risk","Birth options","Support and therapy"],"steps":[{"h":"Let her tell the first birth","say":["Mrs Doyle, thank you for coming in; I know the hospital is hard for you. Missing appointments doesn't count against you.","Would you tell me about your first birth, in your words and at your own pace?"],"hear":"\"Thirty hours, forceps, a bad tear and I bled a lot. Nobody told me what was happening. I thought I'd die and leave my son.\"","mark":"Traumatic birth with fear of death identified"},{"h":"Trauma symptoms","say":["Does it come back to you, in dreams or flashbacks?","Are there places or things you avoid because they remind you?","Do you feel on edge or jumpy? How are you sleeping?"],"hear":"\"Nightmares a few times a week, the theatre lights. I avoid the swimming pool because it smells like the hospital. Always on edge.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing, avoidance and hyperarousal: PTSD likely"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How's your mood been? Are you still enjoying things?","You said part of you wishes you weren't pregnant. Some women in your position think about ending the pregnancy, harming themselves or not being here. Has that happened for you?"],"hear":"\"Mood's a bit low but I love time with my son. It's fear, not wanting to end it. No thoughts of hurting myself.\"","mark":"No self-harm, suicidal or termination thoughts"},{"h":"Birth options and her right to choose","say":["You're allowed to choose how you give birth. If you still want a caesarean after we've talked and you've had support, you can have one.","A planned caesarean and a supported vaginal birth each have risks and benefits; a consultant obstetrician will go through them with you.","Whatever you choose, the plan will say you're told what's happening and can say stop."],"hear":"\"I thought they'd just refuse me.\"","mark":"Right to a planned caesarean explained"},{"h":"Therapy and the notes","say":["I'd like to refer you to the maternal mental health service for therapy for fear of birth and the trauma symptoms.","Would it help to go through your first birth notes with a midwife at a birth reflections appointment?"],"hear":"\"Nobody has ever explained what happened. Yes.\"","mark":"Therapy and birth reflections agreed"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["I'll arrange a joint appointment with a consultant obstetrician, and we'll rebook your missed checks today.","Would you like Mark to come to the next appointment?"],"hear":"\"Yes. I feel like someone's listening.\"","mark":"Consultant obstetric referral and checks rebooked"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The first birth","Long labour, forceps, tear, bleeding, nobody explained","Loss of control and fear of death: a trauma"],["Trauma symptoms","Nightmares, pool avoidance, on edge","Probable PTSD driving fear of birth"],["Mood and risk","Mildly low; no harm or termination thoughts","Low current risk; review again"],["Birth options","Wants a caesarean, expects refusal","NICE supports a planned caesarean after support if still wanted"],["Support and therapy","Never had notes explained; Mark supportive","Therapy, birth reflections, consultant-led plan"]]},"key":"Therapy is offered to help her choose, not to talk her out of a caesarean. If she still wants one after support, offer it, and build control and consent into every step of the plan.","objmap":[[1,5],[3],[4],[5,6],[6]]},"c-p-04":{"name":"Breastfeeding on lamotrigine and sertraline: her choices and the baby's checks","why":"Corrects wrong advice, gives her real feeding choices, keeps her medicines unchanged and sets out how Noor is watched.","frame":["Repair the advice","The medicines in milk","Feeding choices","Watching Noor","Protecting her sleep"],"steps":[{"h":"Apologise and correct","say":["Mrs Hussain, I'm sorry. You were told you couldn't breastfeed on your tablets, and that wasn't right.","I'm here to give you the facts so you can choose."],"hear":"\"I've been giving her formula because of that. I wanted to breastfeed so much.\"","mark":"Wrong advice acknowledged"},{"h":"What gets into the milk","say":["Both lamotrigine and sertraline are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding.","Sertraline passes into milk in very small amounts. Lamotrigine passes in more, so we keep an eye on Noor.","You haven't harmed her. You've done everything right by taking your tablets."],"hear":"\"So I haven't been poisoning her?\"","mark":"Both medicines compatible with breastfeeding"},{"h":"Her feeding choices","say":["You could breastfeed fully, mix breast and bottle, or bottle feed. All three are good choices, and bonding doesn't depend on which you pick.","What feels right for you?"],"hear":"\"I'd like to breastfeed, maybe with Karim doing a night bottle.\"","mark":"Mixed feeding chosen"},{"h":"Watching Noor","say":["The midwife will check Noor's feeding, alertness and weight over the next days, and the health visitor after that.","Tell someone if she's unusually sleepy, feeding poorly, has pauses in her breathing or gets a rash.","If that happens, we'd check her and might take a blood level, not stop feeding automatically."],"hear":"\"What should I actually look for at home?\"","mark":"Baby warning signs named"},{"h":"Keep her medicines unchanged","say":["Please don't stop lamotrigine. It protects you from becoming unwell, and the weeks after a birth are a high-risk time.","If it's stopped for more than a few days, it has to be restarted at a low dose because of a serious rash risk."],"hear":"\"I'd been thinking of stopping it to feed.\"","mark":"Lamotrigine and sertraline continued"},{"h":"Sleep and close","say":["How much have you slept since the birth? Your hypomania last time started with poor sleep.","If Karim gives one expressed or formula feed at night, you can get a solid block of sleep.","I'll write this down for the midwives and your GP, and let the ward manager know about the advice you were given."],"hear":"\"About three hours. Karim can do a night feed.\"","mark":"Sleep protection planned; plan shared"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Repair the advice","Told she can't breastfeed; tearful and angry","Apologise and correct; raise with the ward manager without blame"],["The medicines in milk","Fears she harmed Noor","Both compatible; lamotrigine needs baby monitoring"],["Feeding choices","Wants to breastfeed with a night bottle","Supported choice"],["Watching Noor","Asks what to look for","Drowsiness, poor feeding, apnoea, rash: midwife and health visitor check"],["Protecting her sleep","Three hours since birth; past hypomania began with poor sleep","Night feed by Karim; sleep is relapse prevention"]]},"key":"Never trade her stability for breastfeeding. Both medicines are compatible; the real risks are stopping lamotrigine and losing sleep.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-p-05":{"name":"Thoughts of harming the baby: OCD-type or psychotic?","why":"Characterises the thoughts, excludes psychosis and intent, and reassures her with reasons rather than repeated comfort.","frame":["Nature of the thoughts","Response and avoidance","Psychosis screen","Mood and self-harm","Risk and plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Ask directly and calmly","say":["Mrs Whitfield, thank you for telling the health visitor. Many new mothers have frightening thoughts like this. Can you tell me what happens?","When did the pictures start, and how often do they come?"],"hear":"\"Pictures of stabbing Leo with a kitchen knife, since he was two weeks old. Many times a day.\"","mark":"Intrusive harm images identified"},{"h":"How she responds","say":["How do you feel when the picture comes?","Have you ever wanted to act on it?","What do you do because of them? Hiding things, avoiding things, checking?"],"hear":"\"I'm horrified. I'd never want to. I've hidden the knives, I won't bath him alone and I keep asking Ben if I'm a bad mother.\"","mark":"Unwanted, resisted thoughts with avoidance and reassurance seeking: OCD-type"},{"h":"Psychosis screen","say":["Do you ever hear voices when no one is around?","Do you ever feel Leo is different, or not really yours, or that something is controlling you?"],"hear":"\"No voices. He's definitely my baby.\"","mark":"No psychotic features"},{"h":"Mood and her own safety","say":["How's your mood been? Do you still enjoy anything?","Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","Has anything like this happened before, worries you couldn't shake?"],"hear":"\"A bit low, but I love our walks. No thoughts like that. I had a spell of checking and washing at university.\"","mark":"Low suicide risk; past OCD symptoms"},{"h":"Explain and reassure safely","say":["These are intrusive thoughts, part of perinatal OCD. The thoughts horrify you precisely because you don't want to act on them. They don't make harm more likely.","If you ask me again, I'll answer once more, but repeated reassurance keeps the cycle going, so we'll work on that in therapy.","Leo will not be taken away because of this, and I don't need to involve social services."],"hear":"\"How do you know I won't do it?\"","mark":"OCD-type thoughts explained; safeguarding decision recorded"},{"h":"Treatment plan","say":["I'd recommend CBT with exposure and response prevention, which works well for this.","Sertraline would also help. It's compatible with breastfeeding. We'd start at 50 mg.","With your agreement I'll update the health visitor and GP. Tell us if the thoughts start to feel like something you want to do, or if Leo starts to seem not yours."],"hear":"\"I'll try the therapy and the tablets.\"","mark":"CBT with ERP and sertraline offered; review booked"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Nature of the thoughts","Sudden images of stabbing Leo since two weeks","Ego-dystonic intrusions"],["Response and avoidance","Horrified; hides knives, avoids bathing, seeks reassurance","OCD pattern; avoidance maintains it"],["Psychosis screen","No voices or beliefs about Leo","Not postpartum psychosis"],["Mood and self-harm","Mildly low; no suicidal thoughts; past OCD","Low risk; treat the OCD"],["Risk and plan","Fears Leo will be removed","No safeguarding referral; reasons recorded; CBT with ERP and SSRI"]]},"key":"What separates OCD-type thoughts from psychotic ones is her relationship to them: unwanted, resisted and horrifying versus believed or acted on. Reassure once with reasons, then name the reassurance trap.","objmap":[[2],[4],[5],[5],[6]]},"c-p-06":{"name":"Birth trauma three months on: reaching a PTSD diagnosis","why":"Covers the trauma at her pace, establishes PTSD, checks mood, risk and bonding, and explains trauma-focused therapy.","frame":["Re-experiencing","Avoidance","Sense of threat","Mood and risk","Bonding"],"steps":[{"h":"The worst moment, at her pace","say":["Ms Bennett, you can tell me as much or as little as you like, and we can pause any time.","What was the hardest moment for you?"],"hear":"\"Waking up alone after the anaesthetic. Nobody told me if Isla was alive.\"","mark":"Index trauma identified"},{"h":"Re-experiencing","say":["Does that moment come back to you, in dreams or as if it's happening again?","Are there things that set it off?"],"hear":"\"Nightmares four times a week. Sirens or the smell of hand gel and I'm back there.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing identified"},{"h":"Avoidance and sense of threat","say":["What do you avoid because of it?","Do you feel on guard, jumpy, or that something bad will happen? How's your sleep?"],"hear":"\"The hospital road, the GP surgery, talking about it. I check Isla all the time and jump at noises.\"","mark":"Avoidance and hyperarousal: PTSD"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How's your mood been? Do you blame yourself?","Have you had thoughts about ending your life, or of harming Isla?"],"hear":"\"Low. I feel my body failed. No thoughts like that.\"","mark":"No suicidal or infant harm thoughts"},{"h":"Bonding, gently","say":["Many mothers after a frightening birth tell me they don't feel connected to their baby at first.","Has anything like that happened for you? There's no wrong answer."],"hear":"\"I don't feel like her mum. She bonded with Jamie first. Will I be reported?\"","mark":"Bonding difficulty disclosed; no safeguarding concern"},{"h":"Name it and explain treatment","say":["What you're describing has a name: post-traumatic stress disorder after birth. It's common and it responds to treatment.","The best treatments are trauma-focused CBT or EMDR. Talking about it in a planned way helps it settle, rather than making it worse.","Feeling distant from Isla is part of this, and it usually improves as the trauma does. You won't be reported for that."],"hear":"\"Will I have to go back into the hospital?\"","mark":"PTSD explained; therapy agreed"},{"h":"Practical next steps","say":["I'll refer you to the maternal mental health service for therapy and a birth reflections appointment.","I'll ask the health visitor to arrange Isla's vaccinations somewhere easier for you, such as a home visit or a different clinic."],"hear":"\"That would really help.\"","mark":"Vaccinations rearranged with health visitor"}],"table":{"cols":["PTSD cluster","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Re-experiencing","Nightmares 4 a week; flashbacks to sirens and hand gel","Core PTSD symptom"],["Avoidance","Avoids hospital road, GP surgery, talking","Causing harm: missed vaccinations"],["Sense of threat","Constant checking, jumpy, 4 to 5 hours' sleep","Hyperarousal, current sense of threat"],["Mood and risk","Low, guilt; no suicidal or harm thoughts","Comorbid low mood; low immediate risk"],["Bonding","Doesn't feel like Isla's mum","Trauma effect; parent-infant support, not safeguarding"]]},"key":"Ask for the worst moment, not every detail. Recounting everything in a first meeting can overwhelm her; trauma-focused therapy does the detailed work safely.","objmap":[[3],[4],[6],[6],[7]]},"c-p-07":{"name":"Postpartum psychosis: helping her husband understand and help","why":"Explains the illness and outlook honestly to Tom, gives the future risk, and agrees how he can help Hannah, Alfie and himself.","frame":["His understanding","The illness","Outlook","Future pregnancy","His role and his health"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and how he is","say":["Mr Lewis, Hannah has agreed that I can speak with you about her care. Before anything else, how are you doing?","What have you understood so far about what's happened?"],"hear":"\"I've barely slept. She stopped sleeping, then talked non-stop, then said Alfie wasn't ours.\"","mark":"Carer exhausted"},{"h":"Explain the illness and lift blame","say":["Hannah has postpartum psychosis. It's a serious illness that comes on suddenly in the days or weeks after birth, linked to big changes in hormones and sleep.","It isn't caused by anything you or she did, and nobody could have predicted it.","The Section lets us treat her quickly and keep her and Alfie safe; it doesn't mean she's a dangerous person."],"hear":"\"Is it my fault for not getting help sooner?\"","mark":"Guilt named and lifted"},{"h":"Outlook","say":["Most women recover fully. The worst usually settles over weeks with treatment.","Afterwards, many feel low or anxious for a while, so recovery can take months. That's part of getting better, not a relapse."],"hear":"\"Will she ever be herself again?\"","mark":"Realistic outlook given"},{"h":"Family history and future pregnancy","say":["Has anyone in either family had mental health problems, especially after having a baby?","If you have another baby, there's about a 1 in 2 chance of it happening again. With a plan and early treatment, that risk falls and episodes are caught early."],"hear":"\"Her mum had a breakdown after Hannah was born. Nobody talks about it.\"","mark":"Family history of postpartum illness identified"},{"h":"How he can help","say":["You can visit outside set times and help with Alfie here on the unit.","Bring photos and familiar things, and keep visits calm and short at first.","Tell us anything she says that worries you."],"hear":"\"I didn't know I could help with Alfie here.\"","mark":"Practical help agreed"},{"h":"Look after him","say":["You're replaying that night and not sleeping. How much are you drinking to sleep?","I'd like you to have a carer's assessment and see your GP, which helps Hannah and Alfie too.","Here is the unit number and details of a peer support group for partners."],"hear":"\"Two or three beers a night. I'll see the GP.\"","mark":"Carer's assessment and GP review offered"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His understanding","Saw sleep loss, pressured talk, 'Alfie isn't ours'","Classic onset; he needs a name for it"],["The illness","Fears it was his fault","Remove blame; explain the Section plainly"],["Outlook","'Will she be herself?'","Good recovery likely; low mood afterwards is common"],["Future pregnancy","Hoped for a second child; maternal history","About 1 in 2 recurrence; plan from early pregnancy"],["His role and his health","Low, not sleeping, drinking to sleep","Visits, help with Alfie; carer's assessment and GP"]]},"key":"Honesty about the 1 in 2 future risk is not cruelty. False reassurance takes away the chance to plan a safer next pregnancy.","objmap":[[2,3],[3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-p-08":{"name":"Severe postnatal depression: agreeing a mother and baby unit admission","why":"Rechecks risk, explains why the MBU is needed now and what it is like, and answers her fears honestly so she can agree.","frame":["Current risk","Why admission now","What the MBU is like","Her fears","Practical plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Recheck risk","say":["Mrs Haddad, how have the last few days been?","You said Lina would be better off with someone else. Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","Any thoughts of harming Lina?"],"hear":"\"Last week I thought about stepping in front of a bus. I stepped back. I'd never hurt Lina.\"","mark":"Recent suicidal impulse; no thoughts of harming baby"},{"h":"Screen for psychosis","say":["Have you heard voices when nobody is there?","Have you felt that Lina is ill or damaged in some way?"],"hear":"\"No. She's perfect. It's me that's wrong.\"","mark":"No psychotic features"},{"h":"Why admission now","say":["You're very unwell with depression. Even with the crisis team twice a day you're getting worse, you're barely sleeping and you've lost weight.","My consultant and I recommend you come into a mother and baby unit today, with Lina."],"hear":"\"A proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital.\"","mark":"Recommendation given"},{"h":"What the MBU is like","say":["Lina stays with you in your room. Nurses help with care, especially at night, so you can sleep.","You can keep breastfeeding, and we'll raise your sertraline in a way that's safe for her.","Karim can visit, usually any time during the day."],"hear":"\"Can Karim come? How far is it?\"","mark":"MBU described concretely"},{"h":"Fears about social services and saying no","say":["Going into an MBU is not a safeguarding step. Social services aren't involved because you're unwell.","If you said no, I'd be very worried, and we might have to consider a Mental Health Act assessment. I'd much rather you came in agreeing."],"hear":"\"What if I say no?\"","mark":"Fears answered honestly"},{"h":"Agree and arrange","say":["Do you agree to go in today? Can I call Karim now?","We'll pack together and the team will arrange transport.","I'll record that you've understood and agreed."],"hear":"\"OK. Please ring Karim.\"","mark":"Informal admission agreed; capacity recorded"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Current risk","Suicidal impulse at a road; passive wish to die; no harm to Lina","High and rising self-harm risk"],["Why admission now","2 hours' sleep, 5 kg loss despite crisis team","Community care has failed; MBU indicated"],["What the MBU is like","Wants Karim to visit; worried about milk","Lina stays, breastfeeding continues, night support"],["Her fears","Social services; 'what if I say no?'","Honest answers build trust and consent"],["Practical plan","Karim unaware how bad it is","Call Karim, transport today, capacity recorded"]]},"key":"Answer 'what if I say no?' honestly. Hiding the Mental Health Act option feels kinder but undermines trust; she agreed because she was told the truth.","objmap":[[1],[3],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-p-09":{"name":"A father's postnatal depression: diagnosis, risk and plan","why":"Takes his low mood seriously, separates illness from baby-related tiredness, and assesses risk to him and Ama.","frame":["Core depression","Sleep and alcohol","Trauma images","Anger and baby safety","Suicide risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Normalise and open","say":["Mr Mensah, you're not wasting my time. About 1 in 10 fathers get depressed after a baby, and it's treatable.","How's your mood been since Ama arrived? Do you still enjoy anything?"],"hear":"\"Low most days for two months. Even football does nothing for me.\"","mark":"Low mood and anhedonia identified"},{"h":"Sleep and drinking","say":["When Ama sleeps, do you sleep? What time do you wake?","How much do you drink in a usual evening?"],"hear":"\"I lie awake when she's asleep and wake at four. Four cans most nights.\"","mark":"Early waking; about 50 units a week"},{"h":"The birth","say":["The birth sounds frightening for both of you.","Does any of it come back to you, as pictures or sounds?"],"hear":"\"The alarm and the theatre. It pops into my head.\"","mark":"Intrusive birth images identified"},{"h":"Anger and the baby","say":["Many tired parents feel close to snapping, and it helps to talk about it.","Has there been a time you felt angry with Ama, or worried you might hurt her?"],"hear":"\"Once at 3 a.m. I shouted at her. I put her in her cot and walked out for five minutes.\"","mark":"Safe action taken; no intent to harm"},{"h":"Suicide risk","say":["Have you had thoughts that the family would be better off without you, or of ending your life?","What stops you?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes. No plans. My faith and Ama stop me.\"","mark":"Passive thoughts with strong protective factors"},{"h":"Explain and agree a plan","say":["You have depression, which can happen to fathers after a difficult birth. Putting Ama down and leaving the room was exactly the right thing to do.","Sertraline helped you at 20. I'd suggest 50 mg, with a review in two weeks, plus a referral to NHS Talking Therapies.","The beer is making sleep and mood worse. Can we aim for no drinking on work nights? With your agreement I'll let the health visitor know how to support you."],"hear":"\"Will my work find out? OK, I'll try the tablets.\"","mark":"Sertraline, therapy, alcohol plan and review agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Core depression","Two months low, no enjoyment, guilt","Moderate depressive episode"],["Sleep and alcohol","Awake while Ama sleeps; wakes 4 a.m.; four cans nightly","Depressive insomnia, not baby waking; drinking worsens it"],["Trauma images","Alarm and theatre intrusions","Trauma symptoms to watch"],["Anger and baby safety","Shouted once, put Ama down safely","Low risk; praise the safe action"],["Suicide risk","Better off without him; faith and Ama protect","Low to moderate; safety plan and review"]]},"key":"Separate sleep loss from the baby from depressive insomnia: lying awake while the baby sleeps and waking at 4 a.m. point to illness, not parenthood.","objmap":[[2],[6],[4],[5],[6]]},"c-p-10":{"name":"Stillbirth six weeks on: grief, depression or trauma?","why":"Separates acute grief from a depressive episode and trauma symptoms, assesses risk, and agrees support without unnecessary medication.","frame":["Grief","Depression features","Trauma symptoms","Risk","Support"],"steps":[{"h":"Use Leo's name; let her tell it","say":["Sophie, I'm so sorry about Leo. Would you tell me about him?","How have the weeks since been?"],"hear":"\"I held him. I have his photos and footprints. I cry every day, in waves. I feel my body failed him.\"","mark":"Grief in waves identified"},{"h":"Separate grief from depression","say":["Are there any moments of relief, or things you can still enjoy, even briefly?","How do you feel about yourself as a person, apart from the guilt about Leo?","How are your sleep and appetite?"],"hear":"\"I laughed on a walk with my sister, then felt guilty. I still feel I'm a good person. I sleep 5 or 6 hours.\"","mark":"Enjoyment and self-worth preserved: no depressive episode"},{"h":"Trauma symptoms","say":["Do pictures from the delivery come back to you, or nightmares?","Do you avoid places or things?"],"hear":"\"The silent delivery room, a few times a week. I avoid the nursery and the hospital road.\"","mark":"Trauma symptoms identified"},{"h":"Risk, sensitively","say":["Some mothers tell me they wish they could be with their baby. Has that happened for you?","Have you thought of ending your life or made any plans?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I wish I could be with Leo. No, I wouldn't end my life.\"","mark":"Wish to be with Leo; no suicidal intent"},{"h":"The anger about care","say":["You told them about reduced movements and were sent home. That must be very hard to live with.","Every stillbirth is reviewed, and you can send in your questions and see the findings. I'll make sure your question is included."],"hear":"\"Will anyone actually listen?\"","mark":"Concern routed to the perinatal mortality review"},{"h":"Explain and agree support","say":["What you're going through is grief, and it's deep because Leo mattered. It isn't depression, and you don't need tablets now.","Some of the flashbacks are trauma. Your bereavement midwife can continue, Sands can help, and counselling is available if you want it.","I'll see you in four weeks. Come back sooner if you stop enjoying anything at all or start thinking of ending your life."],"hear":"\"Everyone expects me to be better by now. It helps to hear it's normal.\"","mark":"Support agreed; four-week review"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Grief","Waves of crying, triggered by prams and the nursery","Normal acute grief at six weeks"],["Depression features","Brief enjoyment, self-worth intact, sleeping 5 to 6 h","No depressive episode now"],["Trauma symptoms","Delivery room images, weekly nightmares, avoidance","Trauma symptoms; rescreen for PTSD at review"],["Risk","Wish to be with Leo; no plans","Passive, not active suicidal thinking"],["Support","Partner struggling; anger about care","Midwife, Sands, PMRT input, review in 4 weeks"]]},"key":"In grief, self-worth and capacity for brief pleasure survive; in depression they go. A wish to be with Leo is not the same as suicidal intent, but ask about both.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3],[4],[6]]},"c-p-11":{"name":"Pre-birth safeguarding: an honest conversation about the conference","why":"Validates her fear, explains the process and your report honestly, finds the domestic abuse, and agrees actions she controls.","frame":["Her fear","The process","Your report","Risks now","Actions she controls"],"steps":[{"h":"Validate first","say":["Chloe, you're frightened your baby will be taken. That makes complete sense, and I'm not here to build a case against you.","Can I explain what's happening, and what I'll write?"],"hear":"\"You're all building a case to take my baby.\"","mark":"Fear validated"},{"h":"Explain the process honestly","say":["Social care do a pre-birth assessment when they worry about a baby's safety. The conference brings everyone together to decide what support is needed.","Outcomes range from no plan, to support as a child in need, to a child protection plan. I can't promise the outcome.","You can read the reports beforehand and bring someone with you, like Jade."],"hear":"\"If I do everything you say, will I keep her?\"","mark":"Process and outcomes explained honestly"},{"h":"What your report will say","say":["I'll write about your strengths: you've been to every midwife appointment and cut right down on cannabis.","I'll also be honest about the concerns: the cocaine, the self-harm and the stress you're under."],"hear":"\"What will you write about me?\"","mark":"Report content shared, strengths first"},{"h":"Risks now: self-harm and substances","say":["When did you last self-harm? What helps when the urge comes?","Do you ever think about ending your life?","When did you last use cocaine?"],"hear":"\"Two months ago. I use ice and ring Jade. Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead, but the baby stops me. Cocaine three weeks ago.\"","mark":"Self-harm and substance risks assessed"},{"h":"Domestic abuse, alone","say":["I ask everyone this, and I'm asking while we're on our own.","Does anyone at home hurt you, frighten you or control what you do?"],"hear":"\"Ryan pushed me into a wall last month. He checks my phone.\"","mark":"Domestic abuse disclosed"},{"h":"Respond and agree actions","say":["Thank you for telling me. You and the baby deserve to be safe. I'll do a risk assessment with you now and connect you with a domestic abuse adviser.","This has to go to the social worker, because it affects your safety and the baby's. I'll tell you what I share.","Things you control: stopping cocaine, the substance misuse midwife, weekly testing, and coming to the conference. That shows them what you're doing."],"hear":"\"I'm scared what he'll do. But OK.\"","mark":"DASH, IDVA referral, information shared, review date"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her fear","'Building a case'","Validate before information"],["The process","Wants a promise","Honest: outcomes uncertain; she can see reports and bring support"],["Your report","Asks what you'll write","Strengths and concerns, shared with her first"],["Risks now","Self-harm two months ago; cocaine three weeks ago","Ongoing risk with coping strategies in place"],["Actions she controls","Ryan pushed her; checks her phone","Domestic abuse: DASH, IDVA, share with social worker"]]},"key":"Refusing to promise she'll keep her baby builds more trust than false comfort. Asking about domestic abuse alone often changes the whole risk picture.","objmap":[[2],[2],[3],[4],[6]]},"c-p-12":{"name":"Panic at 28 weeks with no medicines: a therapy-first plan","why":"Confirms panic disorder, excludes physical causes, checks risk and agrees a therapy-led plan that respects her choice.","frame":["Panic attacks","Worry and avoidance","Physical causes","Mood and risk","Fear of birth"],"steps":[{"h":"Accept her choice first","say":["Ms Chen, I've read that you don't want medication. I respect that, and today is about therapy and support.","Tell me what happens during an attack."],"hear":"\"My heart pounds, I can't breathe, my hands tingle and I'm sure I'll die. It peaks in about ten minutes.\"","mark":"Panic attacks identified; choice respected"},{"h":"Worry and avoidance","say":["What do you worry about between attacks?","What have you stopped doing because of them?"],"hear":"\"That something's wrong with the baby. I check the movements all day. I've stopped the bus and shopping alone.\"","mark":"Health anxiety about baby and agoraphobic avoidance"},{"h":"Exclude physical causes","say":["Are you ever breathless at rest, or do you have chest pain or a swollen leg?","Do palpitations happen outside the attacks? How much coffee do you drink?","I'll check your thyroid, blood count and iron, and listen to your heart."],"hear":"\"Only in the attacks. Four coffees a day.\"","mark":"No red flags; bloods planned; caffeine to cut"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How is your mood?","Any thoughts of self-harm or of harming the baby?"],"hear":"\"Not low. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"No self-harm or infant harm thoughts"},{"h":"Fear of birth","say":["How do you feel when you think about the birth?","Has anything happened to make it feel frightening?"],"hear":"\"Terrified. My friend had a traumatic birth.\"","mark":"Fear of childbirth identified"},{"h":"Explain and agree the plan","say":["Panic is your body's alarm going off when there's no danger. It's frightening but it doesn't harm the baby.","CBT helped you before. I'll refer you to the perinatal priority pathway for high-intensity CBT; NICE says therapy should start within a month of assessment.","Meanwhile: cut coffee to one cup a day, use a breathing app, and I'll ask your midwife about a birth planning appointment. Medicine stays an option only if you ever want it."],"hear":"\"How long will I wait? That sounds manageable.\"","mark":"CBT, self-help, bloods and review agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Panic attacks","Pounding heart, tingling, fear of death, peak 10 minutes","Panic disorder"],["Worry and avoidance","Constant worry about baby; avoids bus and shops","Health anxiety and agoraphobic avoidance"],["Physical causes","No symptoms at rest; four coffees","Bloods for thyroid and anaemia; cut caffeine"],["Mood and risk","Not low; no harm thoughts","Low risk"],["Fear of birth","Terrified after friend's birth","Address in therapy and birth planning"]]},"key":"Accept her no-medicines choice early and explicitly; pushing medication would lose her. Rule out physical causes before calling it panic.","objmap":[[2,5],[3],[4],[6],[6]]},"c-s-01":{"name":"Is this EUPD? Today, the pattern, then what it isn't","why":"Turns a reluctant, repeated attender into a clear, evidenced working diagnosis and a plan she agrees with.","frame":["Emotions and anger","Self-image and emptiness","Relationships and fear of being left","Impulsivity and self-harm","What it isn't: mood episodes and psychosis"],"steps":[{"h":"Earn the conversation","say":["I know you've told your story a lot today, and I'm sorry about that.","This is the fourth time this year, so I'd like to understand what keeps happening and make sure the help fits.","About fifteen minutes, then we plan getting you home. Is that OK?"],"hear":"\"Fine. But I'm not staying in.\""},{"h":"Today's overdose: trigger, intent, rescue","say":["Take me through this morning from the start.","How many tablets, and what did you want to happen?","What made you ring for help? How do you feel about being alive now?"],"hear":"\"Callum accused me of flirting. I took sixteen with wine. Then I thought of my cats and rang 999.\"","mark":"Interpersonal trigger identified"},{"h":"Emotions: speed, anger, emptiness","say":["When your mood changes, how fast is it and how long does it last?","How often do you lose your temper, and what happens?","In between, is there a feeling of being empty inside?"],"hear":"\"It flips in minutes if a mate cancels, then it's gone by tea time. I'm angry a lot. Empty, yeah.\"","mark":"Mood instability identified"},{"h":"Self-image and relationships","say":["How would you describe yourself? Does that change with who you're with?","What are your relationships usually like, from start to end?","How do you feel if someone might leave you?"],"hear":"\"I'm whoever they want. He's perfect, then an idiot. I panic if they might go.\"","mark":"Unstable self-image and fear of being left identified"},{"h":"Impulses, substances and self-harm","say":["Do you act on impulse in ways you regret? Give me an example.","How much do you drink, and what drugs do you use?","Apart from overdoses, do you hurt yourself in other ways? What does it do for you?"],"hear":"\"I blew my whole payment in an afternoon. Wine most nights. I cut my arms weekly; it lets the pressure out.\"","mark":"Impulsivity and repeated self-harm identified"},{"h":"What it isn't: depression, bipolar, psychosis","say":["Have you had two weeks or more of feeling low every day, whatever is happening?","Have you had days of feeling high, full of energy, needing little sleep?","Have you heard voices from outside your head? When you think people are against you, does it pass?"],"hear":"\"My ups last an hour, not days. No voices. When I'm stressed I think people are talking about me, but I know they're not.\"","mark":"Mood and psychotic disorders excluded"},{"h":"Brief risk: now, next time, relationships","say":["Do you have any thoughts about ending your life now?","If things go wrong with Callum again, what do you think you'd do?","Have rows ever got physical, either way?"],"hear":"\"Probably the same thing. My ex and I used to hit each other. Callum just shouts.\"","mark":"Ongoing risk identified"},{"h":"Name it, give hope, agree the plan","say":["What you've described, since your teens, fits a pattern called emotionally unstable personality disorder, sometimes called borderline.","It's not about being bad or mad. Your feelings fire up fast and strong, and therapy like DBT really helps; there's no tablet for it.","Tell me in your words what we've said. Then let's write a crisis plan and get the rest of the paracetamol out of the flat."],"hear":"\"So it's a known thing, and therapy helps. My mate Shannon can take the tablets.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Emotions and anger","Mood flips in minutes after a cancelled plan; frequent rages","Mood instability triggered by people, not a mood episode"],["Self-image and emptiness","'I'm whoever they want'; feels hollow, worthless","Unstable identity and chronic emptiness"],["Relationships and fear of being left","Perfect then an idiot; begging at 3 a.m.","Intense, unstable relationships with frantic efforts to avoid being left"],["Impulsivity and self-harm","Spending sprees, caution, binge drinking, weekly cutting, four overdoses","Impulsivity in several areas and repeated self-harm"],["What it isn't","Highs last an hour; no voices; paranoia only under stress","No depressive or hypomanic episode; no psychosis"]]},"key":"Ask how long the lows and highs last and what sets them off. Minutes to hours, triggered by people, is emotional instability; days to weeks with sleep change is a mood episode.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[2,7],[8]]},"c-s-11":{"name":"Five results, one picture: keep the clozapine, treat the body","why":"Turn a page of abnormal numbers into one clear diagnosis and a plan for each part, without threatening the drug that keeps him well.","frame":["Risk factors","The results as one picture","Smoking and clozapine","Lifestyle","Metformin, statin and blood pressure","Clozapine stays"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with his worry","say":["Hello Mr Hale, I'm Dr Mensah. Your yearly results are back. Before I go through them, what's on your mind?","What did the article say, and what worried you most?"],"hear":"\"That I'll die 20 years early. And I'm scared you'll stop my clozapine.\"","mark":"Fear of losing clozapine identified"},{"h":"Risk factors, one by one","say":["How many cigarettes a day? Are you thinking of stopping?","Has anyone in your family had a stroke, heart attack or diabetes?","The GP mentioned your blood pressure. What happened?"],"hear":"\"Thirty a day; I'm stopping Monday, cold turkey. Dad had a stroke at 70. I said no to the BP pills.\"","mark":"Heavy smoker planning abrupt stop; family history; untreated high BP"},{"h":"Diet, activity, sugar symptoms","say":["What did you eat yesterday?","How much do you move in a day?","Are you very thirsty, or passing a lot of urine?"],"hear":"\"Crisps and microwave meals. I'm hungry all the time and too tired to walk far. Not thirsty.\"","mark":"No symptoms of high sugar; hunger and inactivity identified"},{"h":"Results as one picture","say":["Four things have come back together: your waist, blood pressure, sugar and blood fats. Together we call that metabolic syndrome.","Your sugar is in the 'prediabetes' range, not diabetes yet. That means we can turn it round.","Clozapine is the most likely main cause, along with smoking and sitting a lot."],"hear":"\"So it is the clozapine. Does that mean it has to go?\"","mark":"Metabolic syndrome explained"},{"h":"Why it matters, and clozapine stays","say":["Left alone, this raises the chance of diabetes, heart attack and stroke. It's a big part of that 20-year gap.","Clozapine stays. Switching would risk you becoming unwell again, so it's a last resort only."],"hear":"\"Thank God. I'll do what it takes.\""},{"h":"Smoking and clozapine","say":["Stopping smoking is the single best thing for your heart.","But smoke keeps your clozapine level down. Stop suddenly and it can rise within days.","Let's set the date in two weeks, check your level before, and weekly after."],"hear":"\"I never knew that. Can I still use patches?\"","mark":"Safe quit plan agreed"},{"h":"Lifestyle and medicines","say":["A dietitian and the Healthier You programme work better than going it alone. Which change could you start this week?","Metformin helps the sugar and can ease the hunger. Start with one tablet at breakfast; tummy upset usually settles.","A statin, atorvastatin 20 mg at night, cuts your heart and stroke risk. We'll check your liver and blood fats.","I'll ask the GP to check your blood pressure at home for a week, then talk about tablets."],"hear":"\"I'll try the metformin and the statin. Not sure about BP pills yet.\"","mark":"Metformin and atorvastatin agreed; BP to GP"},{"h":"Safety net and close","say":["If you're very thirsty, passing lots of urine, vomiting or very drowsy, call the GP or 111 that day.","Could you tell me the plan in your words?","I'll write it down for you and your key worker, copy the GP, and see you in four weeks."],"hear":"\"Keep the clozapine, quit in two weeks with a blood test, new tablets, see the dietitian.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Result or risk","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Waist 108 cm, hunger","Always hungry, crisps, little exercise","Central obesity from clozapine: dietitian, Healthier You, walking"],["Glucose 6.4, HbA1c 45","No thirst or frequent urine","Prediabetes: metformin, DPP, DKA warning signs"],["HDL 0.8, triglycerides 2.7","Never told his cholesterol","Atorvastatin 20 mg with LFTs and lipids at 3 months"],["BP 146/92","Turned down GP's tablets","Confirm with home readings; GP to offer treatment"],["Smokes 30 a day","Planned cold-turkey stop","Level before and weekly after; dose review"]]},"key":"Every abnormal result gets its own action, but the drug that keeps him well stays. The excellent doctor also catches that quitting smoking will raise the clozapine level, and plans it.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-12":{"name":"Keep the drug that works: map the gain, then fight the hunger","why":"Show the weight is a side effect, screen what it is doing to his body, and agree a plan that protects both his weight and his recovery.","frame":["Illness and medicines tried","Weight timeline","Health effects and risk factors","Lifestyle and programme","Add-on medicines or a switch","Monitoring"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and validate","say":["Hello Mr Clarke, I'm Dr Patel. You wanted to talk about your weight. Tell me what's been happening.","That sounds really frustrating after everything you've done to get well."],"hear":"\"I'm well now, I'll give it that. But I've put on 12 kilos again.\"","mark":"Concern about weight; wants to stay on olanzapine"},{"h":"Illness and medicines","say":["How have you been in yourself? Any voices or worries about people?","Which other medicines have you tried, and how were they?","What happened last time you stopped olanzapine?"],"hear":"\"Nothing worked like this one. I stopped it two years ago and ended up back in hospital.\"","mark":"Five other drugs less effective; relapse off olanzapine"},{"h":"Weight timeline and habits","say":["When did the weight go on, and how much?","What's your appetite like? What does a day's eating look like?","How much do you sleep and move? What have you tried?"],"hear":"\"Seventy kilos before I ever took it, 88 now. I'm starving all the time and sleep ten hours. I walk three times a week.\"","mark":"Gain tied to olanzapine; appetite and sleep up"},{"h":"Health effects and risk factors","say":["Any breathlessness, chest pain, snoring or sore joints?","Do you smoke or vape? How much do you drink?","Any heart problems or diabetes in your family?"],"hear":"\"Out of breath on stairs. My girlfriend says I snore. I vape. Dad had a heart attack at 65.\"","mark":"Possible sleep apnoea; family history of heart disease"},{"h":"Explain","say":["Weight gain is one of the commonest side effects of olanzapine. It mainly turns your appetite up.","Extra weight raises the chance of diabetes and heart disease, so it's worth acting on now.","I wouldn't switch: the others didn't work, and switching risks another relapse."],"hear":"\"So it's not just me being greedy.\""},{"h":"Lifestyle and programme","say":["Regular meals, no grazing, more protein and high-fibre food to keep you full, and the crisps out of the house.","Groups do better than going it alone. Would you try one, or the NHS app at home?","Which two changes could you start this week?"],"hear":"\"No snacks after eight, and walk every day. I'll try the group.\"","mark":"Lifestyle changes and programme agreed"},{"h":"Add-on medicines","say":["If you're still gaining in three months, metformin helps people lose about 3 kg on average. Your GP would check your kidneys first.","Another option is adding a small dose of aripiprazole, about 2 kg on average; it can cause restlessness."],"hear":"\"I'd rather try metformin if it comes to it.\"","mark":"Metformin preferred as next step"},{"h":"Monitoring and close","say":["We'll weigh you monthly and check sugar and cholesterol today and in three months.","Can you tell me the plan in your words?","I'll write to your GP and send you a copy."],"hear":"\"Keep the olanzapine, two changes, the group, bloods today, metformin if it's still going up.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Medicines tried","Five others less effective; relapsed off olanzapine","Do not switch; work around olanzapine"],["Weight timeline","70 kg before, 88 kg now; worse on restarting","Gain caused by olanzapine, driven by appetite"],["Health effects","Breathless on stairs, snoring, long sleep","Screen for sleep apnoea; GP to consider sleep study"],["Risk factors","Vapes, father heart attack at 65","Higher cardiovascular risk; monitoring matters"],["Lifestyle","Snacks, big portions, walks 3 times a week","Specific changes plus a structured programme"]]},"key":"The weight is real and so is the relapse risk. Excellent care keeps the effective drug, gives specific advice and a structured programme, and offers add-on medicines with honest, modest numbers.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-s-14":{"name":"Hear her first, then four honest answers: what, will he recover, again, who calls","why":"Turn a frightened, angry mother into a partner by apologising, explaining NMS plainly and answering her four questions.","frame":["Her anger and what she knows","What NMS is and why","Treatment and complications","Outlook","Restarting safely","Updates and contact"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle and confirm consent","say":["Mrs Daley, I'm Dr Osei, one of the doctors on Ryan's ward. Shall we sit somewhere private?","Ryan has agreed we can share everything with you."],"hear":"\"Finally. I've been told nothing. He came here to get better and he's in a medical ward.\"","mark":"Consent confirmed"},{"h":"Let her speak; apologise","say":["What have you been told, and what's worrying you most?","I'm sorry you were phoned in the night and given so little. That shouldn't have happened."],"hear":"\"Just 'a side effect'. Is he going to die? Can he take tablets again? Who do I even ring?\"","mark":"Her four worries identified"},{"h":"What NMS is, and why","say":["Ryan has a rare reaction to antipsychotic medicine called neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","The muscles go stiff, the temperature rises, the pulse races, and people become confused. A blood test showed the muscles were under strain.","We can't predict who gets it, but increasing the dose quickly is one known risk, and we're reviewing that."],"hear":"\"So you did put it up too fast.\"","mark":"NMS and contributing factors explained"},{"h":"Treatment and complications","say":["The haloperidol was stopped straight away. He's on a drip, being cooled, and having medicine to relax him.","The team watches his kidneys closely, because muscle strain can affect them; they'll also prevent clots and chest infection.","We checked for other causes too, like infection."],"hear":"\"And it's working?\""},{"h":"Outlook","say":["NMS can be dangerous, which is why he moved quickly.","This morning his temperature is down, his blood test is improving and his kidneys are fine. Most people recover fully within a week or two."],"hear":"\"Thank God. I thought you were going to say the worst.\"","mark":"Honest outlook given"},{"h":"Restarting safely","say":["He'll need medicine for his illness again, but not until he's fully recovered, at least two weeks.","We'd choose a different medicine, start very low and go slowly, and check his temperature, pulse and blood tests closely.","Meanwhile our team visits him daily and can give something calming if he's distressed."],"hear":"\"Can I be part of that decision?\"","mark":"Safe restart plan explained"},{"h":"Updates, check and close","say":["His named nurse will ring you at 4 pm every day, and here's the ward number.","You'll meet his consultant on Monday. If you'd like to raise concerns formally, here's the PALS leaflet.","Can you tell me what you'll say to Ryan's dad tonight?"],"hear":"\"A rare reaction, it's being treated, he should get better, different tablet later, a call at four.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Her question","What you heard","What you answer"],"rows":[["What happened?","'They just said side effect'","NMS: a rare reaction to antipsychotics; stiffness, fever, fast pulse, confusion"],["Was it our fault?","'You put it up too fast'","Quick increase is a known risk; it is being reviewed, and she will hear the outcome"],["Will he recover?","'Is he going to die?'","Serious but responding well; most recover within one to two weeks"],["Can he take tablets again?","'Never again?'","Yes, after recovery: different drug, low and slow, close checks"],["Who updates me?","'Who do I ring?'","Named nurse daily at 4 pm, ward number, consultant on Monday"]]},"key":"Families forgive a rare reaction far more easily than silence. The excellent doctor apologises for the gap, names the risk factors honestly, and leaves her with a name, a time and a plan.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6],[7]]},"c-s-22":{"name":"QTc 520: read it, explain it, find the causes, act today","why":"Confirms the QTc yourself, explains it so she understands the urgency, finds every contributing factor and agrees a safe same-day plan that keeps her treated.","frame":["Read and recalculate","Symptoms","Drugs and doses","Electrolytes and fixed risks","Plan by threshold"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and tell her it's abnormal","say":["Hello Mrs Walsh, I'm Dr Mensah. Has anyone explained why we do heart tracings with your tablets?","I've looked at today's tracing, and I'm afraid it isn't normal. Can I show you?"],"hear":"\"Only that it's routine. Go on, then.\""},{"h":"Read it in order and calculate","say":["Rate 75 and a steady, normal rhythm. Beats normal in size and direction, no sign of damage.","This gap is the QT, about 465 milliseconds. Corrected for your rate, it's 520. Eight months ago it was 446."],"hear":"\"I don't know what any of that means.\"","mark":"QTc 520 ms confirmed by hand"},{"h":"Explain in plain words","say":["After each beat the heart recharges. Yours is taking too long to recharge.","That can trigger a fast, chaotic rhythm that causes fainting, or very rarely stops the heart. Acting today makes that much less likely."],"hear":"\"Is that dangerous? I feel perfectly well.\""},{"h":"Symptoms and heart history","say":["Any racing or fluttering, dizzy spells, blackouts, chest pain or breathlessness?","Any heart problems before? Anyone in the family die suddenly when young?"],"hear":"\"No. Mum and Dad lived to their eighties.\"","mark":"Asymptomatic; no cardiac or family history"},{"h":"Drugs, doses and electrolytes","say":["Anything else from the GP, the chemist or a shop recently?","Could you have taken an extra quetiapine by mistake?","Your potassium and calcium are low, and the water tablet lowers potassium. We'll check magnesium too."],"hear":"\"Antibiotics for my chest. Clarithromycin, I think. Finished Saturday.\"","mark":"Clarithromycin, low K and Ca, indapamide identified"},{"h":"Hear the fear","say":["I can hear how much you don't want the voices back.","I'm not stopping your treatment for good; we're making it safe first."],"hear":"\"Please don't take it off me.\"","mark":"Relapse fear named"},{"h":"Today's plan","say":["Please don't take tonight's quetiapine. Dr Rahman agrees, and missing a few doses is very unlikely to bring the voices back.","The medical team are expecting you at the same-day unit to repeat the tracing, monitor you and top up your potassium.","Could Karen drive you straight there?"],"hear":"\"Can't I just go home? ... All right, if it's important.\"","mark":"Agrees to hold and attend SDEC"},{"h":"Restart and monitoring","say":["When your levels are normal, Dr Rahman will restart quetiapine at 400 and we'll repeat the tracing a week later.","If it's still long, we'd switch to aripiprazole, which barely affects the rhythm. Sue will visit weekly."],"hear":"\"And Sue will keep an eye on the voices?\""},{"h":"Teach-back and safety net","say":["Could you tell me in your words what happens today?","If your heart races or you feel faint or black out, call 999 straight away."],"hear":"\"No quetiapine tonight, hospital now, Dr Rahman this week.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Read and recalculate","QT 465 ms, rate 75, QTc 520 (was 446)","Above 500 ms: same-day action"],["Symptoms","No palpitations, faints or chest pain","Asymptomatic: SDEC, not a blue-light transfer"],["Drugs and doses","Quetiapine 400 to 600 mg; clarithromycin; no extra doses","Dose increase is the main trigger; macrolide added to it"],["Electrolytes and fixed risks","K 3.2, Ca 2.04, indapamide; 74, female","Correct and recheck; ask GP to review the diuretic"],["Plan by threshold","Wants to stay on quetiapine; fears voices","Hold now, restart at 400 mg once QTc < 470, aripiprazole as fallback"]]},"key":"The QTc is rarely one drug's fault. Excellent practice lists every contributor (dose, interacting drugs, potassium, calcium, magnesium, age, sex) and fixes all of them, so the antipsychotic she needs can safely continue.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[7],[8,9]]},"c-s-23":{"name":"ST elevation in clinic: spot it, say it, send her","why":"Turns a routine tracing into a life-saving transfer: recognise the STEMI, confirm symptoms, explain it plainly and get her to a heart attack centre fast with safe first aid.","frame":["Read the ECG","Clinical picture","Explain","Emergency actions","Handover"],"steps":[{"h":"See her now","say":["Hello Mrs Kemp, I'm Dr Patel. I've looked at your tracing.","Before anything else, how are you feeling right now?"],"hear":"\"Bit of indigestion. I took two Rennies.\"","mark":"Chest discomfort today"},{"h":"Focused pain history","say":["Show me where. When did it start and what does it feel like?","Does it spread to your arm, neck or jaw? Any sweating, sickness or breathlessness?","Any heart trouble before?"],"hear":"\"Burning weight in the middle since eight, into my left arm. Clammy.\"","mark":"Ischaemic-sounding pain with radiation and sweating"},{"h":"Name it and call 999","say":["Femi, ST elevation V1 to V4 and lead I, reciprocal depression inferiorly, rate 108: anterior STEMI. Please call 999 and bring the bag and defibrillator.","Tell them: suspected STEMI, 74-year-old woman, pain since 8 am, alert."],"hear":"\"Calling now.\"","mark":"Anterior STEMI recognised; 999 called"},{"h":"Explain in plain words","say":["I'm worried this isn't indigestion. The tracing shows you're having a heart attack right now.","A blood vessel feeding the heart is blocked, so part of the muscle is short of blood.","It's frightening, and I'm staying here with you."],"hear":"\"A heart attack? Am I going to die?\"","mark":"Fear acknowledged"},{"h":"Aspirin and monitoring","say":["Are you allergic to aspirin, or had an ulcer or bleeding?","Please chew this 300 milligram aspirin and stay sitting back.","Femi will check your blood pressure, pulse and oxygen; the defibrillator is just a precaution."],"hear":"\"No allergies.\"","mark":"Aspirin 300 mg given; obs stable"},{"h":"Why not home","say":["You mustn't go home or drive. You need a heart hospital that can open the artery within the next hour or two.","Every minute saved protects more heart muscle."],"hear":"\"Can't I just go home and rest?\"","mark":"Accepts transfer"},{"h":"Her question about tablets","say":["Mainly it's diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol.","Quetiapine can raise blood fats and sugar, so we'll review it later. Keep taking it for now."],"hear":"\"Is it my schizophrenia tablets?\""},{"h":"Teach-back and family","say":["Can you tell me what you understand is happening now?","May I phone Alan and ask him to meet you there?"],"hear":"\"Heart attack, ambulance to the heart hospital, not home.\""},{"h":"Handover","say":["74-year-old, central pain since 8 into the left arm, anterior STEMI, aspirin 300 at 11:52, obs stable.","Diabetes, hypertension, schizophrenia on quetiapine, no allergies. Here's the ECG and medication list."],"hear":"\"Great handover. We'll pre-alert the cath lab.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Read the ECG","ST elevation V1–V4 and I; depression II, III, aVF; rate 108; Q waves V1–V3","Acute anterior STEMI"],["Clinical picture","Burning central pressure since 8 am, left arm, clammy; diabetes","Atypical description, typical heart attack"],["Explain","Thought it was indigestion; scared; wants to go home","Plain words, honest urgency, stay with her"],["Emergency actions","999, aspirin 300 mg, obs, defibrillator, no oxygen at 96%","Safe first aid while the ambulance comes"],["Handover","ECG copy, meds, psychiatric history, husband called","Cath lab pre-alerted; family and GP informed"]]},"key":"The ECG outranks the story. A patient calling it indigestion, with territorial ST elevation and reciprocal change, is having a STEMI. Call 999 first, explain while you wait; never repeat the tracing to be sure.","objmap":[[3],[2],[4,6],[3,5],[8,9]]},"c-s-24":{"name":"Presenting an ECG: one system, every time, then the clozapine lens","why":"Gives a consultant a quick, measured and complete reading, then shows you know which findings change a clozapine plan.","frame":["Details and calibration","Rate and rhythm","Axis and intervals","ST, T and QTc","Clozapine-specific risks"],"steps":[{"h":"Agree the format","say":["Do you want the full system out loud, then what matters for clozapine?","Is there a previous ECG to compare with?"],"hear":"\"Exactly that. Go.\""},{"h":"Details and calibration","say":["This is Kwame Asante, 45, recorded today for his annual check on clozapine 400 mg a day.","Standard calibration: 25 millimetres a second, 10 millimetres per millivolt."],"hear":"\"Why bother with calibration?\"","mark":"Calibration confirmed"},{"h":"Rate and rhythm","say":["14 complexes on the 10-second strip, so 84 a minute.","A P before every QRS, a QRS after every P, constant PR: sinus rhythm."],"hear":"\"And the rhythm?\"","mark":"Sinus rhythm 84"},{"h":"Axis and intervals","say":["Lead I and aVF both positive: normal axis, about plus 45.","PR 120 milliseconds, QRS 80: no block, narrow complexes."],"hear":"\"What would make you worry about the axis?\"","mark":"Normal axis and intervals"},{"h":"ST, T and QTc","say":["ST segments flat against the baseline in every lead; T inverted only in aVR.","QT 340, RR 0.71 seconds: Bazett about 400, Fridericia about 380."],"hear":"\"Is Bazett good enough at 84?\"","mark":"QTc ~400 ms"},{"h":"Summary and comparison","say":["In one line: sinus rhythm 84, normal axis and intervals, no ST or T change, QTc about 400.","Normal, and the same as last year's."],"hear":"\"Good. Now, before clozapine?\""},{"h":"Before clozapine","say":["I look for an existing arrhythmia or block, a long QTc, ischaemia and LVH.","Plus baseline troponin and CRP to compare with later."],"hear":"\"Why does an arrhythmia matter so much?\""},{"h":"Myocarditis, infarct, pericarditis","say":["Myocarditis: mostly first four weeks; fever, fast pulse, chest pain; ECG often non-specific; troponin and CRP decide.","Infarct is territorial with reciprocal change; pericarditis is widespread and saddle-shaped with no reciprocal change."],"hear":"\"I lost a patient to myocarditis. His ECG looked almost normal.\"","mark":"Consultant's experience acknowledged"},{"h":"When to repeat and close","say":["Baseline, after dose rises, yearly, and at once with symptoms or a persistently high pulse.","I'd check our trust protocol for the exact schedule. Could you give me feedback?"],"hear":"\"Solid system. Name the leads for each territory next time.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Step","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Details and calibration","Kwame Asante, 45, annual check, 25 mm/s, 10 mm/mV","Numbers can be trusted"],["Rate and rhythm","84 bpm, sinus","No arrhythmia; clozapine tachycardia not present now"],["Axis and intervals","+45°, PR 120 ms, QRS 80 ms","No block or conduction delay"],["ST, T and QTc","Isoelectric ST, T inverted aVR only, QTc ~400 ms","No ischaemia; QTc safe"],["Clozapine-specific risks","Three years in; no symptoms","Myocarditis unlikely now; watch for cardiomyopathy and metabolic risk"]]},"key":"A normal tracing early in clozapine titration does not exclude myocarditis. The excellent trainee reads the ECG alongside the pulse, temperature, symptoms, troponin and CRP, never alone.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,5],[5],[6],[7,8,9]]},"c-s-25":{"name":"Explaining vascular dementia: evidence, course, and what helps","why":"Moves a doubtful son from 'it's an infection' to understanding the diagnosis, what to expect, what helps and what keeps her safe, one chunk at a time.","frame":["What he knows and fears","The evidence","What vascular dementia is","Course and treatment","Safety and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and his starting point","say":["Your mum has said she's fine with me talking to you. What have you heard so far?","What worries you most about it?"],"hear":"\"Dad said she's got dementia. I think it's an infection.\"","mark":"Doubts diagnosis; infection theory"},{"h":"Dementia as an umbrella","say":["Dementia is an umbrella term for brain changes that steadily affect memory, thinking and daily life.","Alzheimer's is one type. Your mum has a type called vascular dementia. Does that make sense?"],"hear":"\"I thought dementia was Alzheimer's.\""},{"h":"The evidence, including infection","say":["The changes built up over months; infection comes on over hours or days and fluctuates.","Her urine and bloods were all clear, and her mood questionnaire was low.","On the memory test she scored 68 out of 100; a score under 82 usually means a real problem with thinking."],"hear":"\"Okay. And the scan?\"","mark":"Delirium and depression excluded"},{"h":"Vascular dementia explained","say":["The scan shows damage to the tiny blood vessels deep in the brain, plus the old stroke.","Her diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol have all harmed those vessels. Does that fit?"],"hear":"\"It does. I should have nagged her more.\"","mark":"Guilt acknowledged"},{"h":"Course and what slows it","say":["It usually progresses in steps, sometimes after another small stroke; some people stay stable for a long time.","Controlling blood pressure, sugar and cholesterol and taking her blood thinner lower the chance of further steps."],"hear":"\"Can you stop the steps?\""},{"h":"Memory tablets and inheritance","say":["Those tablets help in Alzheimer's, not in vascular dementia alone.","It isn't usually passed down directly; keep your own blood pressure and sugar in check with an NHS Health Check."],"hear":"\"Can she have donepezil? Will I get it?\""},{"h":"Driving and wandering","say":["By law she must tell the DVLA. Because she got lost, our advice is to stop driving until the DVLA decides.","A GPS tracker and the police Herbert Protocol can help if she wanders."],"hear":"\"She'll go mad if you take her car.\"","mark":"Driving risk addressed"},{"h":"Support for her and Ron","say":["We'll offer group cognitive stimulation therapy and a dementia adviser.","Your dad can have a carer's assessment, and now is the time for a Lasting Power of Attorney."],"hear":"\"Dad's eighty. He's struggling already.\""},{"h":"Teach-back and close","say":["What will you tell your dad tonight?","Here's a leaflet and our number. We'll see her again in six weeks, and you're welcome to come."],"hear":"\"Vascular dementia, not infection; blood pressure matters; she stops driving; help for Dad.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What he knows and fears","Thinks it's an infection; shocked; worried about inheriting it","Address his theory with evidence first"],["The evidence","Months of change, normal bloods and urine, HADS low, ACE-III 68","Not delirium or depression; dementia confirmed"],["What vascular dementia is","Small vessel disease and old stroke; diabetes, BP, cholesterol","Vascular cause; risk factors are the treatment target"],["Course and treatment","Wants memory tablets; asks if it gets worse","Stepwise course; no cholinesterase inhibitors; tighten vascular control"],["Safety and support","Still driving; got lost; father struggling","DVLA, Herbert Protocol, CST, dementia adviser, carer's assessment, LPA"]]},"key":"Don't argue with a relative's theory; test it aloud with the evidence. Showing why infection and depression were excluded earns the trust that makes the rest of the explanation land.","objmap":[[1],[2,4],[3],[5,6],[7,8,9]]},"c-s-26":{"name":"Name it, link it, warn about antipsychotics","why":"Gives his daughter a diagnosis she can believe because it explains what she has seen, and makes the biggest drug danger impossible to miss.","frame":["Fluctuating attention","Visual hallucinations","REM sleep behaviour disorder","Parkinsonism and falls","DaT scan","Antipsychotic sensitivity"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up and find her starting point","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Rahman, one of the ward doctors. Your dad agreed yesterday that I could talk to you about his results.","Before I explain, what have you been told, and what's worrying you most?"],"hear":"\"Only that the scan 'supports Lewy body'. I've never heard of it. I just want to know if he can come home.\"","mark":"Starting point and main worry identified"},{"h":"Her story, mapped to the core features","say":["Does he have good and bad days, when he's sharp one hour and muddled or drowsy the next?","What does he see, and how does he react?","Does he shout or lash out in his sleep, as if acting out a dream?"],"hear":"\"Mornings he's himself, by lunch he barely knows me. He sees dogs. Mum used to say he fought in his sleep, years ago.\"","mark":"Fluctuation, hallucinations and REM sleep behaviour disorder identified"},{"h":"Movement, falls and medicines","say":["Has he fallen, or seemed stiffer or slower on his feet?","On the medical ward, did anyone mention how he reacted to a medicine for agitation?"],"hear":"\"Two falls in the summer. They gave him something and he went stiff as a board for two days.\"","mark":"Parkinsonism, falls and antipsychotic sensitivity identified"},{"h":"Warning shot, then name it","say":["I'm afraid it isn't good news. The scan and what you've told me show that your dad has a type of dementia called dementia with Lewy bodies.","Tiny clumps of protein, called Lewy bodies, collect in brain cells and stop them working properly.","That explains the good and bad days, the dogs he sees, the fighting in his sleep and the falls.","The scan looks at dopamine, a chemical messenger in the brain's movement centres. His shows less than normal, which fits."],"hear":"\"So it's like Parkinson's? I thought he was just getting old.\"","mark":"Diagnosis linked to each of his symptoms"},{"h":"The course, honestly","say":["There's no cure. It usually gets worse over the years: thinking, movement and the hallucinations.","The pace varies a lot. It does shorten life, but many people live several years, and good care makes a real difference."],"hear":"(tearful) \"I promised Mum I'd look after him.\""},{"h":"Treatment and the big danger","say":["A tablet called rivastigmine often helps alertness and the hallucinations. We check his pulse and heart tracing first; the main side effects are sickness and a slow pulse.","The most important thing: antipsychotic medicines can make people with Lewy bodies very stiff, drowsy or dangerously ill. That's what happened on the medical ward.","We'll put a warning on his drug chart, his records and his GP file. Please mention it to any doctor who sees him."],"hear":"\"So that's why he went rigid. Nobody told me.\"","mark":"Antipsychotic sensitivity explained and flagged"},{"h":"Home, falls and the grandchildren","say":["Before he comes home, our occupational therapist will visit the house: rugs, glass, lighting.","When he sees the dogs, stay calm, ask what he sees, put on a brighter light and reassure him. Don't argue.","For now, please don't leave the children alone with him when he's frightened or muddled."],"hear":"\"Can he still come home, then?\"","mark":"Falls and home-safety plan agreed"},{"h":"Check understanding and close","say":["It's a lot to take in. What will you tell your sisters tonight?","Let's meet with them next Wednesday. Here's a leaflet, and the carers' support line."],"hear":"\"It's Lewy body dementia, it'll get worse, no antipsychotics, and the OT will check the house.\"","mark":"Teach-back correct"}],"table":{"cols":["Core feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Fluctuating attention","Sharp in the morning, lost by lunch","Typical of DLB; explains 'good and bad days'"],["Visual hallucinations","Dogs and children in the room","Core feature; rivastigmine may help; no antipsychotic"],["REM sleep behaviour disorder","Fighting in his sleep for years","Core feature; often comes years before the dementia"],["Parkinsonism and falls","Two falls, slower, stiff","Falls plan, OT and physio before discharge"],["DaT scan","Reduced uptake in both striata","Indicative biomarker: confirms probable DLB"],["Antipsychotic sensitivity","Rigid for two days after haloperidol","Flag on every record; avoid antipsychotics"]]},"key":"A good explanation names the illness. An excellent one makes her recognise her father in it, feature by feature, and leaves the antipsychotic warning written on every record before she goes home.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-s-27":{"name":"Watch, feel, test, walk, stand: parkinsonism in ten minutes","why":"Finds the motor and autonomic signs that make DLB probable, and turns them into safety actions.","frame":["Rest tremor","Rigidity","Bradykinesia","Posture, gait and postural instability","Postural blood pressure","Link to DLB"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and brief symptoms","say":["I'm Dr Fernandes. I'd like to examine how your muscles and movements work, and check your blood pressure lying down and then standing. Is that all right?","Any shaking, stiffness, or slowness doing buttons or writing?","Do you act out your dreams, or feel dizzy when you stand?"],"hear":"\"My right hand's clumsy. June says I thrash about at night. I go woozy getting up.\"","mark":"Probable RBD and postural symptoms identified"},{"h":"Start the lying BP and watch","say":["Please lie on the couch for five minutes while I take your blood pressure and look at your hands.","Let your hands rest on your tummy; just relax."],"hear":"(lying 148/82; slight pill-rolling of the right thumb and finger, reduced blink, little facial expression)","mark":"Hypomimia and right rest tremor seen"},{"h":"Tone, with activation","say":["I'm going to move your wrist and elbow. Let them go floppy.","Now keep opening and closing your left hand while I move the right."],"hear":"(ratchety, catching resistance at the right wrist, increasing with activation; mild on the left)","mark":"Cogwheel rigidity, right more than left"},{"h":"Bradykinesia and writing","say":["Tap your first finger and thumb together, as big and fast as you can, ten times.","Now open and close your hand. Now write your name and address."],"hear":"(taps start well then shrink and slow; writing gets smaller along the line)","mark":"Bradykinesia with decrement and micrographia"},{"h":"Standing BP","say":["Now stand up slowly. I'll measure your pressure at one minute and three minutes.","Do you feel light-headed?"],"hear":"\"Bit swimmy, doctor.\" (1 min 130/76; 3 min 122/74)","mark":"Orthostatic hypotension: 26 mmHg systolic drop"},{"h":"Posture, gait, turning, pull test","say":["Walk to the door, turn and come back. I'm right beside you.","I'm going to stand behind you and give a quick tug on your shoulders. Take a step if you need to; I'll catch you."],"hear":"(stooped, short steps, reduced right arm swing, turns in five steps; three steps back on the pull)","mark":"Gait disorder and postural instability"},{"h":"Quick screens to rule out mimics","say":["Follow my finger up and down, then side to side.","I'll check the reflexes in your feet."],"hear":"(full vertical gaze; plantars downgoing)","mark":"PSP and vascular signs absent"},{"h":"Explain and link","say":["Thank you. Your muscles are stiffer and slower on the right, your balance is unsteady, and your blood pressure drops when you stand: both help explain the falls.","Along with your memory and what you've been seeing, this points to a condition related to Parkinson's. The consultant and I will talk it all through with you and June."],"hear":"\"So it's not just old age, then?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Sign","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Rest tremor","Intermittent, right hand, settles on movement","Supports parkinsonism; often mild or absent in DLB"],["Rigidity","Cogwheel, right > left, worse with activation","Parkinsonism"],["Bradykinesia","Taps shrink and slow; micrographia","Core of parkinsonism"],["Gait and pull test","Shuffle, poor arm swing, 3 steps back","Postural instability: falls risk"],["Postural BP","148/82 to 122/74 with dizziness","Orthostatic hypotension: autonomic, review drugs"],["Link","Parkinsonism + hallucinations + fluctuation","Probable DLB; no antipsychotics"]]},"key":"Finding signs is good; excellent is proving bradykinesia by decrement, unmasking rigidity with activation, and doing a proper lying and standing BP, then turning those findings into a diagnosis and two safety actions.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[6,7],[5],[8]]},"c-s-28":{"name":"Let him vent, find the cause, honest risks, plan with a stop date","why":"Turns an angry brother into an ally by being honest about the drug and putting the search for causes first.","frame":["Defuse the anger","What BPSD is","Causes: pain, bowels, infection, grief","Risperidone: why, limits, risks","Non-drug plan","Stop date and review"],"steps":[{"h":"Defuse","say":["Mr Fairbairn, I'm Dr Mensah from the older people's mental health team. Please come and sit down so I can hear this properly.","Tell me what you've seen and what worries you most."],"hear":"\"He's half asleep, he's dribbling, he's not eating. You've drugged him so your staff have an easy life.\""},{"h":"Reflect and agree what's true","say":["So you're angry because your brother seems sedated, and nobody asked you. Is that right?","You're right to question it. These medicines have real risks, and they should never be used to keep someone quiet."],"hear":"\"Yes. At last someone's listening.\"","mark":"Anger acknowledged; brother engaged"},{"h":"Explain BPSD and look for causes","say":["In dementia, changes like hitting out or calling at night are common. They're usually a sign that something's wrong that he can't put into words.","Pain, constipation, infection and grief are the commonest. He hits out mostly when he's washed: that makes me wonder about pain.","Does he still talk about his wife?"],"hear":"\"He calls for Margaret every night. He never got over her.\"","mark":"Pain, constipation and grief identified as likely causes"},{"h":"Risperidone honestly","say":["Risperidone was started because he was hurting people. It's the only one licensed for that, and only for up to six weeks.","It can cause sleepiness, stiffness, falls and swallowing problems, and it raises the risk of stroke and death, roughly one extra death per hundred people over three months.","That's why I won't increase it."],"hear":"\"So the paper was right.\"","mark":"Risks stated honestly"},{"h":"The non-drug plan","say":["Today I'll examine him, start regular paracetamol, treat his bowels and check his blood.","Staff will keep a chart of what happens before and after each outburst, so we can spot triggers.","What did Leonard love? Music, places, routines?"],"hear":"\"Big band. Glenn Miller. He danced with Margaret every Saturday.\"","mark":"Personalised plan built with brother"},{"h":"Who decides and the stop date","say":["Do you hold a Lasting Power of Attorney for his health?","If not, it's a best-interests decision, and your views count. I'll write them down.","Let's halve the dose from tomorrow and aim to stop by the end of November, unless he's at serious risk again."],"hear":"\"No LPA. I'm happy with that.\"","mark":"Best-interests consultation; stop date agreed"},{"h":"Check understanding and close","say":["Could you sum up what we've agreed, so we're both clear?","I'll see you here in two weeks. If you're unhappy at any point, the manager or our PALS team will take it seriously."],"hear":"\"No increase, check for pain and bowels, music, halve it, stop by the end of the month.\"","mark":"Teach-back correct"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Pattern","Hits out during washing","Pain or fear during personal care"],["Body","Bowels not open 4 days; knee grimace","Treat constipation and pain first"],["Mood","Calls for his late wife every night","Grief or depression; assess, comfort"],["Drug","Drowsy, dribbling, eating less","Side effects; reduce, don't increase"],["Person","Loves Glenn Miller, danced with his wife","Music during care; photos"],["Decision","No LPA","Best interests, brother consulted"]]},"key":"Good doctors explain the risks. Excellent ones say 'you're right' early, find the cause behind the behaviour, and leave a stop date, not just a dose.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5],[6,7]]},"c-s-29":{"name":"Not dementia, results, the honest odds, what helps","why":"Contains a husband's fear of Alzheimer's with accurate facts, and leaves him with a plan they can act on.","frame":["What he wants to know","What MCI is","What the tests ruled out","Chance of progression","No tablets; what helps","Follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and his agenda","say":["I'm Dr Osei. Margaret signed a form saying I can talk to you. Thank you for coming.","What did she ask you to find out, and what's worrying you most?"],"hear":"\"Whether it's Alzheimer's. Her mum had it. She's terrified.\"","mark":"Fear of Alzheimer's identified"},{"h":"Check her function","say":["How does she manage day to day: money, cooking, appointments, driving?","What changes have you noticed?"],"hear":"\"She does everything. Forgets names and the odd appointment. Never used to.\"","mark":"Function preserved"},{"h":"Lead with the news, then name it","say":["The good news first: this is not dementia, and it is not Alzheimer's.","She has mild cognitive impairment. Her memory is a bit lower than expected for her age, but not enough to affect her daily life.","The difference from dementia is that she's still managing everything."],"hear":"\"So why call it impairment?\""},{"h":"Results: what each one ruled out","say":["The memory test was 81 out of 100, just below the usual range, so the change is real but mild.","Her mood questionnaire was normal, so it isn't depression; the bloods ruled out thyroid and vitamin problems; the scan showed no stroke or tumour."],"hear":"\"That's a relief, I think.\"","mark":"Results explained"},{"h":"The honest odds","say":["Some people with this stay the same for years, some get better, and some develop dementia.","In clinics like ours, about one in ten a year go on to dementia. So most don't in any given year.","Her mum's history raises the chance a little, but it doesn't decide it."],"hear":"(quietly) \"I've been short with her when she forgets.\"","mark":"Progression risk explained in natural frequencies"},{"h":"No tablets; what helps","say":["The dementia tablets don't help at this stage, so we don't use them.","What does help: walking or exercise most days, getting her hearing tested, keeping blood pressure under control, and staying socially busy.","A shared calendar and phone reminders make daily life easier."],"hear":"\"She'd love an excuse to join the walking group.\"","mark":"Risk-reduction steps agreed"},{"h":"Follow-up, her own appointment, teach-back","say":["We'll repeat the memory test in a year, or sooner if anything changes in how she copes.","I'd like to see Margaret myself when she's back, so she hears it from me.","What will you tell her first?"],"hear":"\"It's not Alzheimer's. It's mild. Walk, hearing test, come back in a year.\"","mark":"Teach-back correct; plan to see her"}],"table":{"cols":["Item","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["Function","Runs the house, money, drives","Not dementia"],["ACE-III","81/100, memory and fluency lowest","Real but mild deficit: MCI"],["HADS, bloods, CT","All normal or age-related","Depression and reversible causes unlikely"],["Family history","Mother had Alzheimer's","Modestly raised risk; no gene test"],["Lifestyle","Little exercise; mild hearing loss","Concrete risk-reduction targets"]]},"key":"Good is explaining MCI accurately. Excellent is saying 'not dementia' first, giving the odds as natural frequencies including the people who stay well, and making sure Margaret hears it herself.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-s-30":{"name":"One decision, four abilities, then a plan she will take","why":"Gives a lawful, defensible capacity conclusion and still sends her home as safely as possible.","frame":["The decision and the information","Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate","Safety plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce and name the decision","say":["Good morning, Mrs Ashworth. I'm Dr Nwosu, one of the psychiatry doctors. The team asked me to talk with you about carers when you go home.","I'm not here to make you have them. I want to understand your decision and make sure it's properly recorded."],"hear":"\"Another doctor. I've told everyone, I don't need strangers washing me.\""},{"h":"Give the information","say":["The team suggested carers twice a day, morning and evening, to help with washing, dressing and meals.","That's because you fell on the ward, your walking is slower after being so poorly, and your eyesight isn't at its best yet."],"hear":"\"I know all that. I want my own house.\""},{"h":"Understand","say":["Can you tell me in your words what the carers would do and why they were suggested?","What do you think has changed since you came in?"],"hear":"\"Help me wash and dress, and do meals. Because I fell and I'm a bit wobbly.\"","mark":"Understands the information"},{"h":"Use or weigh","say":["What do you see as the good and bad sides of having carers?","What might happen if you go home without them?","How will you manage washing and meals in the first week? And if you fell at night, what would you do?"],"hear":"\"I could fall again. I'd crawl to the phone. The Patels next door will bring meals. I'd rather risk it than lose my independence.\"","mark":"Weighs risks against her values"},{"h":"Screen falls, mood and isolation","say":["Do you get dizzy when you stand up?","How's your mood been? Do you get lonely?"],"hear":"\"A bit dizzy, yes. Lonely since Arthur died, but not down.\"","mark":"Postural symptoms and isolation identified"},{"h":"Retain and communicate","say":["Just so I know I explained it well, can you remind me what the carers would do and why?","And what's your decision about carers?"],"hear":"\"Wash, dress, meals, twice a day, because of the fall.\"","mark":"Retains and communicates"},{"h":"Tell her the conclusion","say":["You've understood and remembered the information, weighed it up and told me clearly. You have the capacity to make this decision, and it's yours to make.","I'll tell the team and write it in your notes."],"hear":"\"Thank you. Finally someone's listened.\"","mark":"Conclusion communicated"},{"h":"Build the safety plan","say":["Would you accept an OT visit at home, some physio and a pendant alarm?","We'll check your blood pressure lying down and then standing before you go, and the GP will see you in two weeks.","If things get harder, you can ask for carers any time; here's the number."],"hear":"\"The alarm and the OT, yes. Not carers. Not yet.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Part of the test","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Carers would help her wash, dress and cook because of the fall","Understands"],["Retain","Repeated it accurately later","Retains"],["Use or weigh","Named the falls risk, had a plan, valued independence more","Weighs; decision is unwise to some but capacitous"],["Communicate","Clear and consistent","Communicates"],["Impairment","4AT 0, MoCA 27/30","Delirium resolved"],["Risks","Dizzy on standing, cataracts, lonely","Postural BP, eye surgery, befriending"]]},"key":"Concluding capacity is the start, not the end. The excellent doctor turns a respected refusal into a safety plan she accepts, and leaves the door open for carers.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,6],[5],[7],[8]]},"c-s-31":{"name":"Can Liam manage his money? The four abilities, tested on his own figures","why":"Gives a defensible, decision-specific capacity finding and turns it into practical help he accepts.","frame":["Mental state and the decision","Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate","Outcome and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Name the decision and presume capacity","say":["Liam, I'm Dr Mensah. Your housing officer asked whether you can manage your own money. Today is only about that.","The law starts by assuming you can. I'll ask some questions and tell you honestly what I think at the end.","Is that OK with you?"],"hear":"\"Fine. But I'm not ill, if that's what this is about.\"","mark":"Decision named; agrees to go ahead"},{"h":"Check the illness isn't touching his money","say":["How have you been in yourself? Any voices, or feeling watched lately?","Do you feel the housing association is against you in any way?","Are you taking your quetiapine every night?"],"hear":"\"No voices for years. They're not against me, they just want their money.\"","mark":"No psychotic ideas linked to money"},{"h":"Understand: his own figures","say":["What comes in each month, and from where?","What has to go out before you spend on anything else?","How much do you owe, and how did it build up?"],"hear":"\"About £1,140 from Universal Credit. Rent's £520. I owe about £2,600. I kept meaning to pay it.\"","mark":"Understands income, outgoings and arrears"},{"h":"Money skills and other drains","say":["Quick sum: a £4.85 meal deal paid with a £10 note. What's the change?","Has anyone been borrowing money, or is any going on betting, drink or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Five fifteen. A mate borrowed sixty quid once, paid it back. It's mostly just sat there.\"","mark":"Basic skills intact; no exploitation found"},{"h":"Use and weigh, including consequences","say":["If the rent isn't paid, where could that lead?","What's good about handling it all yourself, and what's the downside?","How do your options compare: pay it yourself, rent paid direct, or a support worker?"],"hear":"\"I could lose the flat. Doing it myself I'm independent, but if I forget no one spots a slip.\"","mark":"Weighs consequences, pros and cons"},{"h":"Retain and communicate","say":["Can you tell me back what you owe and what could happen?","So what have you decided?","If I asked you again next week, would you say the same?"],"hear":"\"£2,600, and I could be evicted. I'll keep my own money but have the rent paid straight to them.\"","mark":"Retains; clear, consistent decision"},{"h":"Tell him the outcome","say":["You followed the facts, kept hold of them, balanced both sides and gave me a clear choice. So you can make your own decisions about your money.","The rent slipped because of organisation, not your illness.","Which help you use is your choice."],"hear":"\"Right. So what happens now?\"","mark":"Outcome given plainly"},{"h":"Agree support and consent to share","say":["Shall we ask Universal Credit today to pay your rent direct?","Would you see a debt adviser this week to agree a repayment plan?","Can I tell your housing officer the outcome and this plan, and nothing about your health?"],"hear":"\"Yes to all that. Just don't ring my mum.\"","mark":"Support chosen; consent to share given"}],"table":{"cols":["Capacity ability","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mental state","No voices, no paranoid ideas about the landlord; takes quetiapine","Illness is not distorting this decision"],["Understand","Knows income, rent, bills and the £2,600 arrears","Holds the relevant information"],["Retain","Repeats the arrears and eviction risk near the end","Keeps it long enough to decide"],["Use and weigh","Independence versus nobody catching a missed payment","Balances both sides in his own words"],["Communicate","Keeps control, rent paid direct; same answer when re-asked","Clear, consistent decision: has capacity"]]},"key":"Finding capacity is not the end of the task. A man who can decide but keeps forgetting still faces eviction: the excellent doctor finds capacity, then fixes the rent with help he chooses.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5,6],[7],[8],[8]]},"c-s-32":{"name":"'My insides are rotten': mood, belief, intake, risk, urgency","why":"Confirms severe psychotic depression and turns the intake and suicide risk into a same-day plan.","frame":["Mood and biological symptoms","The belief: delusion or worry","Food, fluid and physical risk","Suicide and self-neglect","Insight and urgency"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle her and start with the loss","say":["Mrs Pryce, I'm Dr Khan from the older people's mental health team. Your GP and Susan are worried about you.","I'm so sorry about Gerald. How have things been since he died?"],"hear":"\"Terrible. Everything's gone grey. Nothing matters now.\"","mark":"Onset after bereavement identified"},{"h":"Core and biological symptoms","say":["Does anything still give you some pleasure, even small things?","How are you sleeping? What time do you wake?","Are mornings or evenings worse? Has your weight changed?"],"hear":"\"I wake at three and lie there. Mornings are the worst. My clothes hang off me.\"","mark":"Severe depressive syndrome identified"},{"h":"The belief: open, then test certainty","say":["Susan said you're worried about your insides. Can you tell me about that?","How do you know they're rotten?","If 100 is completely certain, how certain are you? Could anything else explain it?"],"hear":"\"I can smell it on my breath. I'm dying, doctor. There's no other explanation.\"","mark":"Nihilistic delusion identified: fixed and mood-congruent"},{"h":"Other psychotic features and attention","say":["Do you feel you've done something wrong, or that you deserve this?","Do you hear voices when no one's there?","Can you tell me today's date, then the months in reverse, starting with December?"],"hear":"\"I've let everyone down. No voices. It's November... I think.\"","mark":"Guilt identified; attention reduced"},{"h":"Intake and physical risk","say":["What have you eaten and drunk since Monday?","When did you last pass water? Any dizziness when you stand?","May I check your pulse and blood pressure, lying and standing?"],"hear":"\"Just sips of tea when Susan makes me. I'm scared to eat. I went dizzy getting up.\"","mark":"Dangerously low intake identified"},{"h":"Suicide and self-neglect","say":["When you feel like this, do you think you'd be better off dead?","Have you thought of ending your life, or made plans? Any tablets saved up?","Who is with you during the day?"],"hear":"\"I'd be better off gone. But I wouldn't do anything. Susan comes in the evenings.\"","mark":"Passive death wish; no plan; alone by day"},{"h":"Past episodes, treatment and insight","say":["Have you felt like this before? What helped?","Is the mirtazapine helping?","Do you think you might be ill, rather than dying?"],"hear":"\"Once, in my thirties. Tablets helped. I don't see how I can be helped now.\"","mark":"Previous depression; partial insight"},{"h":"Explain and decide the urgency","say":["I think you have a severe depression, and it's making your body feel this way. It can be treated.","Because you're hardly drinking, you need hospital today, first for fluids and blood tests.","Can I speak to Susan now and arrange it?"],"hear":"\"If Susan thinks so. Will you tell her gently?\"","mark":"Same-day admission agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood and biology","Grey, nothing matters; wakes at 3; mornings worst; weight loss","Severe depressive episode"],["The belief","Insides rotting, smells it, 100% sure, no other explanation","Nihilistic delusion: psychotic depression"],["Intake","Sips of tea for three days; dizzy on standing; dark urine","Dehydration: a medical emergency"],["Suicide","Better off gone; no plan; alone by day","High, mainly passive risk; needs supervision"],["Insight and urgency","Doubts treatment but will go for her daughter","Same-day hospital; MHA only if she refuses"]]},"key":"The delusion makes the diagnosis, but the fluid sets the urgency. Ask about drinks early, check a postural blood pressure, and judge urgency by her physical state, not by the suicide questions alone.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[4,5],[6],[8]]},"c-s-33":{"name":"Space, numbers and movement: a seven-minute parietal run-through","why":"Covers every parietal domain in a fixed order and turns the findings into a side, a cause and practical help.","frame":["Fields and neglect","Gerstmann features","Praxis","Constructional drawing","Cortical sensation","Awareness and recognition"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and the essential checks","say":["I'd like to do some short tests of how your brain handles space, numbers and movement. Would that be alright?","Ola will stay with us. Which hand do you write with?","Glasses on? Any weakness or numbness in your arms or legs?"],"hear":"\"Right hand. Glasses on. My arms are fine, it's my memory.\"","mark":"Right-handed; no weakness, vision corrected"},{"h":"Fields, then visual inattention","say":["Look at my nose. Cover your left eye. Tell me when my finger moves.","Now the other eye.","Keep looking at my nose: which hand is moving now?"],"hear":"\"There... and there. Both of them.\"","mark":"Fields full; no visual inattention"},{"h":"Gerstmann features","say":["Show me your ring finger on your left hand.","Touch your right ear with your left hand.","What's 100 take away 7? Keep going.","Write me a sentence about the weather."],"hear":"\"Ninety-three... eighty... I can't. (Writes 'It is' and stops.)\"","mark":"Dyscalculia and agraphia; fingers and left-right intact"},{"h":"Praxis: mime, sequence, dressing","say":["Show me how you'd wave goodbye, then brush your teeth.","Fold this letter, put it in the envelope and seal it.","This cardigan's inside out. Could you put it right and put it on?"],"hear":"\"(Uses her finger as the toothbrush; seals the letter; gets stuck in the wrong sleeve.)\"","mark":"Ideomotor and dressing apraxia; ideational intact"},{"h":"Constructional drawing","say":["Copy these two overlapping shapes, then this cube.","Draw a clock with all the numbers, hands at ten past five."],"hear":"\"(Pentagons don't overlap; cube is flat; clock complete on both sides, hands wrong.)\"","mark":"Constructional apraxia without left neglect"},{"h":"Cortical sensation, eyes closed","say":["Close your eyes. What's in your hand?","What number am I drawing on your palm? One point or two?","Up or down? Which hand am I touching now?"],"hear":"\"A key. A ten pence. A three. Two points. Up. Both hands.\"","mark":"Cortical sensation intact"},{"h":"Awareness, way-finding and faces","say":["Do you have any problems with your arms or legs?","Do you get lost finding your way around the ward?","Who's in this photo?"],"hear":"\"My memory's the problem. I can't find the toilet without the sign. That's Ryan, my grandson.\"","mark":"Topographical disorientation; no anosognosia or prosopagnosia"},{"h":"Close and localise","say":["Thank you, you've worked hard. Numbers, writing, dressing and drawing are harder for you, and we can help with each.","Dr Shah will go through your scan tomorrow.","Ola, she needs prompts with dressing and help with the menu; I'll ask OT to assess."],"hear":"\"Will I get my numbers back?\"","mark":"Findings localised and turned into a plan"}],"table":{"cols":["Parietal domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Fields and neglect","Full fields; sees both hands together; clock complete on both sides","No neglect, no field cut"],["Gerstmann features","Fails serial 7s and writing; finger naming and left-right intact","Partial Gerstmann: left (dominant) parietal"],["Praxis","Finger as toothbrush; letter sequence intact; stuck in the cardigan","Ideomotor (left) and dressing apraxia (right)"],["Constructional","Pentagons apart, cube flat, clock hands wrong","Constructional apraxia: either side"],["Cortical sensation","Key, coin, number, two points, position all correct","Sensory parietal cortex spared"],["Awareness and recognition","Knows her memory is poor; lost without signs; knows her grandson","Topographical disorientation (right)"]]},"key":"Localising is the point. Checking handedness, fields, strength and comprehension first lets you say 'left', 'right' or 'both', and a vascular patient with mixed signs usually has both.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-s-34":{"name":"After the fit: memory, words and the upper fields","why":"Finds and localises temporal lobe dysfunction after a first seizure without over-reading the postictal state.","frame":["Seizure experiences","Memory","Language and naming","Reading and writing","Visual fields and recognition","Delayed recall and interpretation"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent, chaperone and the basics","say":["I'd like to do some short tests of memory and language after the seizure. Is that alright?","Grace will stay with us. Which hand do you write with? Glasses on?"],"hear":"\"Right-handed. I'm still all over the place, though.\"","mark":"Right-handed; a few hours postictal"},{"h":"Ask about seizure experiences","say":["Before the fit, any odd smells or tastes, a rising feeling in your tummy, or feeling you'd lived a moment before?","Has anyone seen you stare, smack your lips or fiddle with your clothes?"],"hear":"\"A smell like burning rubber, and déjà vu, for months. My sister saw me stare and pick at my sleeve.\"","mark":"Probable focal temporal seizures identified"},{"h":"Memory: register, recent, remote, semantic","say":["Repeat this name and address; I'll ask again later: Harry Barnes, 73 Orchard Street, Kendal.","What did you have for breakfast? Where did you grow up?","Who was the first woman UK Prime Minister? Name as many animals as you can in a minute."],"hear":"\"Registered first time. Breakfast unsure. Preston, St Mary's. Thatcher. Nine animals.\"","mark":"Registration intact; recent memory and fluency reduced"},{"h":"Language: comprehension, repetition, naming","say":["Point to the ceiling, then the door, then touch your nose.","Say after me: 'The red bus stopped by the old church.'","What's this called? And this part? And this?"],"hear":"\"Fine with the commands and the sentence. Watch, strap... I can't get the word for the buckle.\"","mark":"Naming difficulty for low-frequency words"},{"h":"Reading and writing","say":["Please read this card and do what it says.","Write any sentence you like."],"hear":"\"I can't make the words line up. (Writes 'My son is' and stops.)\"","mark":"Alexia and agraphia"},{"h":"Upper fields and faces","say":["Look at my nose, cover one eye, and tell me when my finger moves, top and bottom on each side.","Who is this famous face?"],"hear":"\"I don't see it top right, either eye. That's the King.\"","mark":"Right superior quadrantanopia; faces recognised"},{"h":"Mood and psychosis","say":["Since all this, any voices, or a sense that people are plotting against you?","How's your mood been?"],"hear":"\"No voices. Frightened and a bit low.\"","mark":"No psychotic symptoms"},{"h":"Delayed recall, then explain","say":["Can you tell me the name and address from earlier?","Was it Orchard, Oakwood or Elm Street?","This points to the left side of the brain. We'll repeat these tests in a day or two, and the team will arrange a scan and EEG."],"hear":"\"Harry... Barnes? Maybe Orchard. Can I still drive?\"","mark":"Verbal memory deficit; driving advice given"}],"table":{"cols":["Temporal domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Seizure experiences","Burning smell, déjà vu, staring and sleeve picking","Earlier focal temporal seizures"],["Memory","Registers the address but recalls none freely, two items with cues","Verbal memory deficit: left (dominant) temporal"],["Language and naming","Fluent, follows commands, repeats well; can't name the buckle","Mild anomia from dominant temporal involvement"],["Reading and writing","Can't read the command; stops mid-sentence","Temporoparietal spread, or postictal: repeat"],["Fields and faces","Misses the upper right in both eyes; knows the King","Left Meyer's loop; right temporal spared"]]},"key":"On the day of a seizure, every deficit is provisional. Record a careful baseline, localise it, and book the repeat; the excellent doctor also asks about auras, which turn a 'first seizure' into epilepsy.","objmap":[[2],[3,8],[4,5],[6],[8]]},"c-t-13":{"name":"Frontal lobe at the bedside: fluency, abstraction, sequencing, inhibition, reflexes","why":"Gives a quick, structured picture of executive function that the consultant and neuropsychologist can act on.","frame":["Verbal fluency","Abstraction","Estimates and sequencing","Response inhibition","Primitive reflexes"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: consent and set-up","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Can I check you're Callum Okafor?","Thanks, Callum. I'd like to do some short thinking and movement tasks, some a bit odd, to see how the front of the brain is working since the crash. Is that alright?","Some are meant to be tricky, so don't worry about mistakes. Tell me if you need a break."],"hear":"\"Sure, go for it.\""},{"h":"Brief history","say":["Have you noticed any change in your sense of smell since the crash?","Have your family or friends said you've changed in any way?","Do you find yourself doing things on the spur of the moment, like spending?"],"hear":"\"Can't smell much now. Mum says I make bad jokes. I'm just a laugh, me. Spent a bit on games.\"","mark":"Anosmia and reported disinhibition identified"},{"h":"Verbal fluency","say":["In one minute, give me every word you can think of that starts with F. No names of people or places, and no same word with different endings.","Now, as many animals as you can in one minute."],"hear":"\"Fish, fun, Frank, food... fish... France...\" (7 F words, 12 animals)","mark":"Low letter fluency with rule breaks"},{"h":"Abstract thinking","say":["What does it mean if someone says, 'Don't count your chickens before they hatch'?","In what way are a table and a chair alike?"],"hear":"\"You can't count them till they're out of the egg, can you?\" ... \"They've both got legs.\"","mark":"Concrete thinking identified"},{"h":"Estimates and sequencing","say":["Take a guess: how tall is an average woman in this country?","Watch my hand and don't copy yet: fist, edge, palm. Now you do it, over and over.","Copy this pattern and carry it on to the edge of the page."],"hear":"\"About eight feet?\" Then fist, fist, palm, fist... repeats one shape in the drawing.","mark":"Poor estimate and perseveration identified"},{"h":"Response inhibition","say":["When I tap once, you tap twice. When I tap twice, you tap once.","Now new rule: when I tap once, you tap once. When I tap twice, don't tap at all."],"hear":"Gets the first rule after errors; taps on most no-go trials.","mark":"Impaired inhibition identified"},{"h":"Primitive reflexes","say":["I'd like to check some reflexes by touching your palm and your lips. Is that okay?","Rest your hand open on your knee. Don't hold my fingers.","Close your eyes for me; I'll tap gently above your lip."],"hear":"He grips the doctor's fingers despite being asked not to; no palmomental or pout response.","mark":"Grasp reflex present"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you, Callum, you worked hard. Switching tasks, explaining sayings and stopping yourself were harder for you.","That fits with the front of the brain being affected by the crash.","I'd like to do a scored test next time, refer you for detailed testing and a smell test, and talk to your parents if that's okay."],"hear":"\"Fair enough. Mum'll be pleased someone's listening.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you saw","What it means"],"rows":[["Verbal fluency","7 F words with proper nouns and a repeat; 12 animals","Reduced fluency and poor self-monitoring"],["Abstraction","Literal proverb; 'both have legs'","Concrete thinking"],["Estimates and sequencing","'Eight feet'; perseverates on Luria and pattern","Poor judgement and set-shifting"],["Response inhibition","Taps on no-go trials","Impaired inhibitory control"],["Primitive reflexes","Grasp present; palmomental and pout absent","Frontal release sign"]]},"key":"One failed task means little; a consistent pattern across fluency, abstraction, sequencing and inhibition, with anosmia and disinhibition, is what points to frontal injury.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-14":{"name":"Lower limb neurology with functional signs","why":"Gives a complete, comparable lower limb neurological assessment and finds the positive signs that support functional weakness, without dismissing her.","frame":["Inspection and gait","Tone","Power and functional signs","Reflexes and coordination","Sensation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: consent, chaperone, pain","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working with the medical team. Are you Gillian Mbatha?","I'm sorry the leg feels worse today. That must be frightening. I'd like to check your legs carefully, the nerves, muscles and reflexes. Is that alright?","Is anything painful right now? I'll ask a nurse to stay with us as a chaperone."],"hear":"\"No pain. Just weak. Yes, that's fine.\""},{"h":"Inspection and red flags","say":["When it started, what were you doing? Any fall, back pain or pins and needles?","Any change with your bladder or bowels, or numbness around your bottom?","I'm just looking at both legs for any muscle wasting, twitching or scars."],"hear":"\"I was making tea and it just went. No pain, no tingling, nothing with my bladder.\"","mark":"No red flags; normal inspection"},{"h":"Tone","say":["Let your legs go floppy. I'm going to roll each leg from side to side.","Now I'll lift behind your knee quickly, and then flex your foot up sharply."],"hear":"Normal tone both legs; no clonus.","mark":"Normal tone"},{"h":"Power and Hoover's sign","say":["Lift your leg up off the bed and stop me pushing it down. Now push down into my hand.","Bend and straighten your knee against me; push your foot down and pull it up.","Now push your left heel down into my hand. Keep my hand there, and lift your right leg against my other hand."],"hear":"Left leg very weak with give-way; heel pushes strongly into the hand when the right hip flexes.","mark":"Hoover's sign positive"},{"h":"Reflexes","say":["I'm going to tap your knees and ankles with this hammer. Grip your hands and pull if I ask.","Now I'll run something along the sole of each foot."],"hear":"Knee and ankle jerks normal and equal; both plantars flexor.","mark":"Normal reflexes and plantars"},{"h":"Coordination","say":["Rest your right heel on your left kneecap, then run it down the front of the shin to the ankle.","Now the same with the left heel on the right leg, as best you can."],"hear":"Normal on the right; left limited by weakness, no tremor or overshoot."},{"h":"Sensation and gait","say":["Close your eyes and say yes when you feel this cotton wool. Is this sharp or blunt?","Tell me when the buzzing stops. Is your toe moving up or down?","If you feel able, stand and walk a few steps; I'll stay right beside you."],"hear":"Normal sensation throughout; walks dragging the left leg behind, hip turned out.","mark":"Normal sensation; functional gait pattern"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you, Gillian. Your nerves, reflexes and feeling are working normally, and nothing new has appeared today.","When you lifted your right leg, your left leg pushed down strongly by itself. That tells us the wiring is there; the problem is sending the signal on purpose, and that can improve.","I'll share this with neurology. I'd also like to check your arms, face and leg pulses. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"So it's not a stroke? That's a relief. Will I walk properly again?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Inspection and gait","No wasting or fasciculation; drags left leg, hip turned out","Typical functional gait; no lower motor neurone signs"],["Tone","Normal both legs, no clonus","No upper motor neurone lesion"],["Power and functional signs","Severe left weakness with give-way; Hoover's and hip abductor signs positive","Positive signs of functional weakness"],["Reflexes and coordination","Symmetrical jerks, flexor plantars; no ataxia","No structural pattern"],["Sensation","Normal in all modalities","No sensory level or neuropathy"]]},"key":"Normal tests do not make the diagnosis; positive signs do. Find Hoover's sign, then explain it as working wiring, never as 'nothing wrong'.","objmap":[[1,2],[2,3],[4],[5,6,7],[8]]},"c-t-15":{"name":"Breaking the diagnosis, then the plan: results, Alzheimer's, treatment, home, future","why":"Gives an anxious daughter a clear, kind understanding of the diagnosis and a practical plan she can act on.","frame":["What she knows and fears","Results and diagnosis","Medication","Non-drug help and safety","Planning ahead"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: consent, what she knows","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the older adults team. Are you Claire Oduya?","Thanks for coming, Claire. Your mum has said I can talk to you about her results. How has she been, and what do you know so far?","What's worrying you most?"],"hear":"\"Only that the scan's back. I'm nervous. She's been forgetting things for a year.\""},{"h":"Results in plain words","say":["Your mum's memory test was well below what we'd expect for her age.","Her scan shows shrinkage in the parts of the brain that handle memory and finding her way around.","Her blood tests were normal, and her mood questionnaire doesn't suggest depression."],"hear":"\"Shrinkage? What does that mean?\"","mark":"Results explained"},{"h":"Diagnosis","say":["Putting this together, your mum has Alzheimer's disease. It's the commonest type of dementia, at a moderate stage.","Proteins build up in the brain and damage the cells, so the brain slowly shrinks. It's not her fault and not normal ageing.","It does get worse over time, though the pace varies. Shall I pause there?"],"hear":"\"Please, stop a second. It's a lot.\" ... \"Okay. So it's Alzheimer's.\"","mark":"Diagnosis named and understood"},{"h":"Medication","say":["There's a tablet called donepezil. Alzheimer's lowers a brain chemical used for memory, and donepezil stops it being broken down so quickly.","It doesn't cure it, but can help memory and daily life for a while. Some people get sickness, diarrhoea, odd dreams or a slow pulse, so we check her pulse first.","If it doesn't suit her, or later on, there's another one called memantine."],"hear":"\"My neighbour's on that, I think. So it slows it down?\"","mark":"Medication explained"},{"h":"Non-drug help","say":["Keeping her mind and body active really helps. There's a group called cognitive stimulation therapy, twice a week for seven weeks.","A dementia adviser can help with routines, a calendar clock, labels and photos of family.","There's also support for you: carers' groups and information sessions."],"hear":"\"She'd like a group. She loves company.\""},{"h":"Living alone and risk","say":["Is she safe on her own? Let's think about cooking, medicines, falls, money and getting lost.","An occupational therapist can visit to fit a cooker cut-off, a falls sensor and an alarm; the pharmacy can do a blister pack.","Does she still drive? With these results, it isn't safe, and she must tell the DVLA."],"hear":"\"She burnt a pan last month. And she gave her bank details to a caller. She still drives to the shops.\"","mark":"Home risks identified"},{"h":"Planning ahead","say":["While she can still make decisions, it's worth her making lasting powers of attorney, for health and welfare and for money.","Social services can assess her for help at home as needs grow, and you're able to ask for a carer's assessment.","How much can you realistically do alongside work?"],"hear":"\"I'm ten minutes away, but I work full time. I didn't know about the attorney thing.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["To check I've been clear, what will you tell your mum about today?","Here are leaflets from the Alzheimer's Society and our contact number.","I'll see your mum with you in four weeks to start the plan. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"Alzheimer's, moderate. A tablet to help a bit, a group, the OT visit, the attorney, and she has to stop driving.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard or found","What it means"],"rows":[["What she knows and fears","'Only that the scan's back'; worried about Mum alone","Start from her level; address home safety"],["Results and diagnosis","ACE-III 46; hippocampal and parietal atrophy; normal bloods; low HADS","Moderate Alzheimer's disease; depression unlikely"],["Medication","Neighbour on a memory tablet","Donepezil after pulse check; memantine later"],["Non-drug help and safety","Burnt pan, scam call, still driving","OT, telecare, bank protection, stop driving and tell DVLA"],["Planning ahead","Works full time; no LPA","LPA now; social care and carer's assessments"]]},"key":"Name Alzheimer's clearly, then deliver the rest in small plain chunks with pauses. When she says 'stop', stopping and re-explaining simply is the skill, not a delay.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-16":{"name":"What's changed in Clive, and is Angela safe? Frontal features, then risk","why":"Turns a frightened wife's account into evidence of a frontal-type illness and a safety plan that does not let the diagnosis hide the danger.","frame":["Disinhibition","Loss of empathy and apathy","Eating change and compulsions","Poor judgement and insight","Risk to Angela and others"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle her first","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors here in A&E. Are you Angela Thornbury?","I'm so sorry about what happened this morning. How is your neck now? Are you comfortable to talk?","Take all the time you need. Everything you say helps us keep you safe and understand Clive."],"hear":"\"It hurts. I just don't understand him any more.\""},{"h":"This morning in detail","say":["Can you take me through what happened, from the start?","How long were his hands on your neck? Did you black out, wet yourself or struggle to breathe?","Did he use or threaten anything else? Where is he now?"],"hear":"\"I'd hidden his car keys. He grabbed my throat with both hands. The dog barked and he let go. The police have him.\"","mark":"Strangulation without loss of consciousness; Clive in custody"},{"h":"When did he stop being Clive?","say":["When did you first feel he was different? Sudden or gradual?","Has he said or done things that embarrassed you? Can you give me an example?","How does he react when you're upset?"],"hear":"\"About two years. He says crude things to strangers. When I cry, he just turns the telly up.\"","mark":"Gradual disinhibition and loss of empathy identified"},{"h":"Frontal features","say":["Has his eating changed? Any cravings for sweet things?","Any habits he repeats, or new rituals?","How is he with money, washing and dressing? Does he think anything is wrong?"],"hear":"\"Whole packets of biscuits. Thousands on the internet. He checks the locks again and again. He says I'm the one making a fuss.\"","mark":"Hyperorality, compulsions, financial recklessness and lack of insight identified"},{"h":"Other causes and family history","say":["How's his memory for recent things? Does he get lost or lose words?","Has he seemed low, or heard or believed things that aren't real?","Any falls, head injury, weakness, drink or new tablets? And has anyone in his family changed like this?"],"hear":"\"Memory's fine. No voices. Just his blood pressure tablet. His dad went strange in his fifties; they called it Pick's disease.\"","mark":"Other causes screened; family history of early dementia identified"},{"h":"Her risk","say":["Has he ever hurt or threatened you before today?","Are you frightened of what he might do? Has he ever threatened to kill you?","Are there any weapons at home? Who else could you stay with?"],"hear":"\"Never before. But I'm scared now. There's no gun. My daughter Sophie lives ten minutes away.\"","mark":"High risk on DASH: strangulation and fear"},{"h":"Wider risk","say":["Is he still driving? Any near misses?","Has he behaved sexually or aggressively towards anyone else, or had any police involvement?","Does he look after the grandchildren? Has he ever talked of harming himself?"],"hear":"\"He went through a red light last month. He made a dirty remark to the girl in the bakery. Never in trouble before.\"","mark":"Driving, sexual disinhibition and child safeguarding risks identified"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["What you describe sounds like an illness affecting the front of the brain. It needs proper tests, and I'll make sure he's assessed.","But it doesn't make this morning any less dangerous. I'd like you to stay with Sophie tonight. Is that possible?","I'll tell the police and custody team what you've told me, refer you to a domestic abuse adviser, and ask the doctors to check your neck again before you go. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"Thank you. I'll ring Sophie now.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Disinhibition","Crude remarks to strangers, sexual comment in the bakery","Loss of social restraint: a core frontal sign"],["Loss of empathy and apathy","Turns the telly up when she cries; lost interest in grandchildren","Emotional blunting, not just stress"],["Eating change and compulsions","Packets of biscuits; checks the locks repeatedly","Hyperorality and repetitive behaviour"],["Poor judgement and insight","£4,000 online; red light; 'you're making a fuss'","Executive and insight loss: risk to money and the public"],["Risk to Angela and others","Strangled today, frightened, no previous violence","High risk: she must not be alone with him tonight"]]},"key":"Finding a likely illness is not the end of the risk assessment. Strangulation is high risk whatever the cause, so the excellent doctor makes Angela safe tonight and still starts Clive's diagnostic route.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3,4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-17":{"name":"Found wandering: tonight, the months before, then risk in three parts","why":"Gets a full picture quickly from a worried relative, separates delirium from dementia, and makes tonight safe.","frame":["Tonight: before, during, after","Course: sudden or gradual","Delirium and physical causes","Function and mood","Risk: physical, social, psychiatric"],"steps":[{"h":"Reassure and set the pace","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Leah, Harold's granddaughter?","He's safe and warm on the ward. Thank you for coming in so quickly.","He's happy for us to talk. Could you tell me what you know about tonight?"],"hear":"\"The police found him on the estate in his pyjamas. I don't know much more.\""},{"h":"Tonight in detail","say":["I'll ask lots of short questions; quick answers are fine. What happened before he left, as far as you know?","Was the door open? What was he wearing? How long was he out?","Was he hurt? Has this ever happened before?"],"hear":"\"Dad found the door wide open. He was in pyjamas and slippers. Nobody knows how long. Never before.\"","mark":"First episode of night wandering, poorly clothed, door left open"},{"h":"The months before","say":["What changes have you noticed in him lately?","Can you give me an example, like something left on or forgotten?","Has it crept up gradually, or got suddenly worse?"],"hear":"\"He's scruffy, stained shirts. Last week the gas was on and he swore he'd turned it off. Slowly, but worse since Nan died.\"","mark":"Gradual decline in self-care and memory identified"},{"h":"Delirium and physical causes","say":["Has he been unwell: a cough, fever, water infection, constipation?","Any falls, bangs to the head, weakness or slurred speech?","What tablets does he take, any changes, and how much does he drink?"],"hear":"\"Not that I know. No falls. Blood pressure and cholesterol tablets. He only drinks at Christmas.\"","mark":"No obvious delirium trigger yet; tests needed"},{"h":"Function and mood","say":["How does he manage meals, shopping, bills and tablets? Who did those before?","Does he still drive?","How has he been since your nan died?"],"hear":"\"Nan did all that. The fridge is empty. He drives to the shop; there's a new dent. He's quiet, tearful sometimes.\"","mark":"Functional decline and grief identified"},{"h":"Risk in three parts","say":["Physical: is he eating, washing, taking his tablets properly?","Social: is he alone most of the time? Has anyone taken money or knocked at the door offering work?","Psychiatric: has he ever said he doesn't want to go on?"],"hear":"\"A man offered to do his roof for cash. He's never said anything like that.\"","mark":"Risks: gas, wandering, self-neglect, driving, exploitation"},{"h":"Her feelings","say":["How are you doing with all this?","You've lost your nan too. That's a lot to carry.","What worries you most?"],"hear":"\"(crying) Is this the beginning of the end for him?\"","mark":"Distress contained"},{"h":"Summary and plan","say":["Thank you, that was really helpful. We'll test tonight for infection and other causes of sudden confusion.","It may be memory problems underneath, but we can't say until he's well. He won't go home alone tonight.","We'll involve a social worker and make home safer. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"Thank you. Can I sit with him?\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Tonight","Pyjamas, door open, time unknown, first time","Serious risk: cold, traffic, falls"],["Course","Months of slipping self-care, worse since wife died","Probable underlying cognitive decline, unmasked"],["Delirium and physical","No known illness, falls or new tablets","Delirium still possible: tests tonight"],["Function and mood","Empty fridge, unpaid bill, driving, tearful","Needs care assessment; grief may add to it"],["Risk","Gas left on, cash caller, still driving","Not safe home alone; safeguarding and telecare"]]},"key":"Never call it dementia on the night. Wandering in a man living alone means delirium first and risk second; the excellent doctor gets both done and still leaves the relative feeling held.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3,4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-18":{"name":"The journals: collection, impact, discarding, differentials, then the mental state","why":"Wins a proud, guarded man's trust, establishes hoarding disorder, rules out the look-alikes and makes the home safer.","frame":["Excessive acquiring","Difficulty discarding","Clutter and impact","Insight","Look-alikes: OCD, autism, psychosis, depression, dementia"],"steps":[{"h":"Collegial opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dr Okoro?","Thank you for coming. Your GP mentioned some worries about how things are at home.","I'd like to hear about life at home in your words."],"hear":"\"Ha, my daughter's doing, no doubt. The house is fine.\""},{"h":"The collection","say":["I gather you have a collection. Tell me about it.","How did it start, and what do the journals mean to you?","Roughly how many are there now?"],"hear":"\"My wife's psychiatry journals. I carried on after she died. How many? Enough... over a thousand, if you must know.\"","mark":"Excessive acquiring identified, linked to bereavement"},{"h":"Impact on the home","say":["Where are they kept now? Which rooms can you still use for what they're meant for?","What about the kitchen? Can you cook?","Have you ever had a fire, or tripped over them?"],"hear":"\"Every room, really. Some in the kitchen. A small fire burned twenty. I don't use the oven now. I've tripped once or twice at night.\"","mark":"Clutter preventing room use; fire and falls identified"},{"h":"Discarding and insight","say":["How would you feel if some were thrown away?","Are you still buying more?","Your daughter sees it differently. What do you make of that?"],"hear":"\"I couldn't. They might be worth a fortune. She fusses, but maybe the kitchen is a bit much.\"","mark":"Difficulty discarding; fair insight"},{"h":"Rule out the look-alikes","say":["Do you ever have to check, count or arrange things a set way, or get thoughts you can't push away?","What would happen if someone took the journals? Is there any special meaning or message in them?","How's your mood been, and how is your memory?"],"hear":"\"No checking. No messages, they're just journals. Mood's fine, sleep's fine, memory's good. I still miss her.\"","mark":"OCD, psychosis, depression and cognitive decline screened"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Do you smoke, or use heaters near the piles?","Do you have a working smoke alarm? Can you get to the front and back doors easily?","Any pests or damp? Have you ever thought of harming yourself?"],"hear":"\"Don't smoke. I took the alarm battery out; the toast kept setting it off. The back door's blocked. Never thought of harming myself.\"","mark":"Fire risk high: no alarm, blocked exit"},{"h":"Mental state","say":["(Observe) Well kempt, warm, guarded at first, normal speech, euthymic.","No abnormal beliefs or perceptions; oriented, good recall; insight fair.","Could I check a few memory questions, just for completeness?"],"hear":"\"Fire away. Old habits.\"","mark":"MSE completed"},{"h":"Explain and agree a first step","say":["What you describe has a name: hoarding disorder. It's common and often follows a loss.","Nobody will clear your house without you. The first aim is safety: would you let the fire service visit and fit an alarm?","Then there's a talking therapy that helps you decide what to keep, at your pace. What do you think?"],"hear":"\"The fire people, yes. The rest I'll think about.\"","mark":"First safety step agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Excessive acquiring","Over 1,000 journals, still buying more","Ongoing collecting, not a closed collection"],["Difficulty discarding","'I couldn't' throw any away; thinks they're valuable","Core feature of hoarding disorder"],["Clutter and impact","Every room; kitchen unusable; fire; trips at night","Rooms lost to their purpose; real harm"],["Insight","'Maybe the kitchen is a bit much'","Fair insight: a foothold for change"],["Look-alikes","No checking, no special meanings, mood and memory fine","Not OCD, psychosis, depression or dementia"]]},"key":"Hoarding disorder feels meaningful, not intrusive; that separates it from OCD. And the urgent job is fire safety, not decluttering: agree one safe step he accepts rather than demanding a clear-out.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-t-29":{"name":"Thought and perception, sense by sense, without arguing","why":"Elicits the full psychopathology of late-onset psychosis from a reluctant patient while keeping her engaged.","frame":["Persecutory delusion","Partition and other delusions","Hallucinations in each sense","Thought interference and passivity","Senses and cognition"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Dorothy Pennington?","Thank you for coming anyway, Mrs Pennington. It sounds like things with your neighbours have been very stressful.","Would you pop your hearing aids in so I don't miss anything? Then I'd love to hear your side of it."],"hear":"\"I shouldn't be here. It's them who need looking at.\""},{"h":"The neighbours","say":["What have they been doing, and since when?","When did you first realise? What happened at that moment?","Why do you think they're doing it? How sure are you?"],"hear":"\"A year ago they put a dish up on our wall and I knew right then they wanted my house. I'm certain.\"","mark":"Persecutory delusion; probable delusional perception"},{"h":"Other beliefs","say":["How do they get to you? Through the walls, the pipes, anything else?","Has anything been taken or tampered with: post, food, belongings?","Do you feel special in any way, or that the TV or papers refer to you?"],"hear":"\"They pump a sweet gas through the bedroom wall. They take my post. Nothing on the telly.\"","mark":"Partition delusion; theft and tampering beliefs"},{"h":"Hallucinations","say":["Do you hear them? Where from, and what do they say? Are they speaking to you, or about you?","When is it worst? Is it clear, like we're talking now?","Does food or drink taste odd? Seen anything strange, or felt anything on your skin?"],"hear":"\"At night, through the wall. 'She won't last.' They say my name. My tea tastes metallic. Nothing I've seen.\"","mark":"Third-person auditory, olfactory and gustatory hallucinations"},{"h":"Passivity and thought","say":["Do thoughts ever arrive that feel like someone else's, or get pulled away?","Can other people hear or know your thoughts?","Do you feel anyone controls your movements or feelings?"],"hear":"\"Certainly not. My thoughts are my own.\"","mark":"No thought interference or passivity"},{"h":"Senses and cognition","say":["How's your hearing and eyesight generally? How often do you wear the aids?","I ask everyone a few memory questions. What's the date, and where are we?","Please remember these three words; I'll ask again in a minute: apple, table, penny."],"hear":"\"I don't wear them, they whistle. Monday the something, the clinic. Apple, table, penny.\"","mark":"Hearing impairment; cognition grossly intact"},{"h":"Risk and coping","say":["How have you coped? Have you changed where you sleep or what you eat?","Have you ever thought of getting back at them?","With all this, has it ever felt too much to go on?"],"hear":"\"I sleep in the back room. I only eat things in packets. I shouted at them and called the police. I'd never hurt anyone or myself.\"","mark":"Behavioural response; no intent to harm"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you. You've had a hard year and you're not sleeping in your own bed.","I'd like some blood tests, a brain scan and a hearing check, and to see you again in two weeks.","Would you be happy for your son and daughter to join us next time?"],"hear":"\"If you won't stop them, what are you suggesting I do?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Persecutory delusion","Neighbours want her out; certain; began when they put a dish up","Fixed persecutory belief with probable delusional perception"],["Partition and other delusions","Sweet gas through the bedroom wall; post taken","Partition delusion typical of late-onset psychosis"],["Hallucinations","Voices at night saying 'she won't last'; sweet smell; metallic tea","Third-person auditory, olfactory and gustatory hallucinations"],["Thought interference","None","No first-rank passivity phenomena"],["Senses and cognition","Hearing aids unused; orientated; recall 3/3","Sensory deficit; dementia and delirium less likely"]]},"key":"Go through every sense and ask how it reaches her. Partition delusions and smells or tastes are the hallmarks of late-onset psychosis that a voices-only screen misses.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-31":{"name":"Galantamine in plain words: hope, honesty, safety","why":"Gives a worried son an honest picture of what the drug can do, how to use it safely and what else will help.","frame":["How it works","Realistic benefit","Dosing","Side effects and the heart","Concerns: liver, addiction, cost, supplements","Monitoring and review"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent, his knowledge, his worries","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists here. Your dad has said he's happy for us to talk about his treatment.","What do you already know about galantamine?","What's on your mind most about it?"],"hear":"\"Mainly whether it actually works. And his heart's slow already.\"","mark":"Main concerns identified"},{"h":"Diagnosis recap and how it works","say":["Your dad has Alzheimer's at a mild to moderate stage, so memory and planning are affected, but he still manages a lot himself.","In Alzheimer's, a brain chemical called acetylcholine runs low. It helps nerve cells pass messages for memory.","Galantamine slows the breakdown of acetylcholine, so more is available."],"hear":"\"So it fixes the memory?\""},{"h":"Honest benefit","say":["It doesn't cure the illness or stop it, I'm afraid.","Some people improve a little in memory, daily tasks or mood; others stay steadier for a while; some notice no change.","We'll judge it at about three months. If it's helping and suits him, he stays on it; if not, we stop."],"hear":"\"OK. That's more honest than what I read online.\"","mark":"Realistic expectations agreed"},{"h":"Dosing","say":["It's one long-acting capsule each morning with breakfast; food helps with sickness.","He starts on 8 mg for four weeks, then goes up to 16 mg if he's fine.","If he misses more than a few days, ring us: he'll need to restart on the low dose."],"hear":"\"I can sort his tablets each week.\""},{"h":"Side effects and the heart","say":["The commonest ones are feeling sick, poor appetite, loose stools and dizziness. They often settle in a week or two.","It can slow the heart, and his bisoprolol already does, so we'll do a heart tracing before he starts.","If he faints, falls, his pulse drops below 50, or he gets a blistering rash, stop it and get help that day."],"hear":"\"So you'd check his heart first. Good.\"","mark":"Cardiac risk addressed"},{"h":"His other questions","say":["It isn't known to damage the liver; we'd only adjust the dose if he had serious liver disease.","It isn't addictive, and on the NHS his prescription is free.","Daffodil extracts aren't safe or tested, even though the drug was first found in bulbs."],"hear":"\"That's a relief. Someone at work swore by daffodil tablets.\"","mark":"Concerns answered"},{"h":"Monitoring and the wider plan","say":["His GP will check his pulse and weight; we'll see him at three months, then every six months.","Beyond the tablet: memory groups, a carers' assessment for you, and planning ahead with Lasting Power of Attorney.","He also needs to tell the DVLA about the diagnosis; we can help with a driving assessment."],"hear":"\"I hadn't even thought about the driving.\""},{"h":"Check, summarise, close","say":["Can you tell me the main things you'll keep an eye out for?","Here's a leaflet, the Alzheimer's Society details and our clinic number.","Is there anything we haven't covered?"],"hear":"\"Sickness, dizziness, his pulse, any rash. And ring you if he faints.\"","mark":"Understanding confirmed"}],"table":{"cols":["Topic","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Realistic benefit","\"I've read they don't do much\"","Honest framing plus a three-month review keeps trust"],["Heart","Slow pulse on bisoprolol","ECG first, pulse checks, clear stop rules"],["Side effects","Worried about him feeling ill","Slow start, take with food, most settle in two weeks"],["Other concerns","Liver, addiction, cost, daffodils","Each answered briefly and accurately"],["Wider plan","\"What else can we do?\"","Memory groups, carer support, LPA, DVLA"]]},"key":"Honesty earns more trust than optimism: say plainly it is not a cure, name a review point, and raise the heart risk yourself before the son has to ask.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-t-34":{"name":"Angry relative: listen, agree, explain, plan, route","why":"Turns an angry mother into a partner by meeting her fears with facts, honesty and a shared plan.","frame":["Listen and acknowledge","Clozapine: why and how safe","His results","Care on the ward","Going home safely","Complaint and next steps"],"steps":[{"h":"Let her speak","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Jamal has said we can talk with you about his care.","I can see you're upset, and I want to hear why. Tell me what's worrying you most.","Is there anything else, so I can cover it all?"],"hear":"\"Bone marrow. Nobody rings me. He's filthy. I'm taking him home.\"","mark":"Concerns listed: clozapine, contact, self-care, discharge"},{"h":"Acknowledge and apologise","say":["You've been worried sick and felt shut out. That's understandable.","Nobody rang you, and that shouldn't have happened. I'm sorry.","From this week his named nurse will call you every Friday."],"hear":"\"Well, that's something.\""},{"h":"Clozapine: why and how safe","say":["Jamal's illness didn't respond to two other medicines. Clozapine is the one most likely to help when that happens.","You're right that it can lower white blood cells. It's uncommon, under one in a hundred.","So we check his blood weekly. If the count dropped, we'd stop it at once."],"hear":"\"So you're checking every week?\"","mark":"Monitoring understood"},{"h":"His results","say":["All his white cell results so far have been normal.","His liver tests were high when he arrived, before clozapine, probably from life at the flat. They've been normal since."],"hear":"\"Nobody told me that.\""},{"h":"Slow progress and care","say":["Clozapine works slowly; people often keep improving over six months to a year.","The nurses and occupational therapist work on washing and dressing with him daily.","He's hearing voices less and joining groups now."],"hear":"\"He did seem calmer on Sunday.\""},{"h":"Going home safely","say":["I want him home too. Can I ask about home, so we plan it properly?","Who else lives with you? How is your husband's drinking at the moment?","Did Jamal tell you about his money at his brother's?"],"hear":"\"My husband drinks every night. His brother took his benefits.\"","mark":"Home risks and financial abuse identified"},{"h":"Plan, complaint route, close","say":["Let's meet with his consultant on Monday and plan graded leave: day visits first.","I'll raise a safeguarding concern about his money today.","If you'd still like to complain, here's PALS and the leaflet; it won't affect his care."],"hear":"\"I'll still complain about the phone calls.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Concern","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Clozapine","'It destroys bone marrow'","Partly true; weekly FBC makes it safe to use"],["Results","'What about his blood tests?'","Liver tests normal since admission; all FBCs normal"],["Progress","Still unwashed after three months","Expected slow response; OT and nursing plan"],["Home","Husband drinks; 30 miles; brother took money","Unsafe now; graded leave, safeguarding"],["Communication","Nobody rang her","Apologise; weekly calls; complaints route"]]},"key":"Agree with what is true before correcting what is not: confirming that clozapine can harm white cells, then showing his normal results, calms her faster than any reassurance.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,5],[4],[6],[7]]},"c-t-38":{"name":"Depression in later life: severity, mimics and risk","why":"Reaches a confident diagnosis in a slowed older man while excluding physical, cognitive and psychotic alternatives.","frame":["Depressive symptoms and severity","Physical causes","Cognition","Psychotic features","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Reginald Thompson?","Thank you for having me, Reg. Your GP is worried you've lost weight and felt low.","Would you tell me how things have been for you lately? Are you happy for Pat to stay?"],"hear":"\"Can't say I've been right. She can stay.\""},{"h":"Mood and depression","say":["Is your mood low most of the day, every day? How long has it been like this?","Are mornings or evenings worse? What time do you wake?","What do you still enjoy? How are your energy, appetite and concentration? Do you blame yourself for anything?"],"hear":"\"Since I retired. Mornings are worst; I wake at four with dread. Haven't been to the allotment in months. I'm useless.\"","mark":"Severe depressive symptoms with biological features identified"},{"h":"Physical causes","say":["How much weight have you lost, and over how long?","Any pain, change in your bowels, blood, cough, or passing water more?","What tablets do you take? Has the GP done blood tests? How much alcohol, week to week?"],"hear":"\"About a stone in three months. No pain or bowel trouble. Blood pressure tablet. Two cans most nights.\"","mark":"No physical red flags reported; alcohol quantified"},{"h":"Cognition","say":["Has your memory changed? Do you know today's date?","I'll say three words; please repeat them, and I'll ask again in a minute."],"hear":"\"Wednesday... the 6th? Apple, table, penny. (later) Apple... table... penny.\"","mark":"Effortful but intact cognition: depression rather than dementia"},{"h":"Psychotic features","say":["You said you're worried about money. What comes in each month, and what goes out?","Do you ever feel your body is rotting or something terrible is happening inside you?","Do you hear voices when no one's there, or feel you deserve punishment?"],"hear":"\"The pension's less... but the house is paid off. I know we're alright really, it just goes round my head. No voices.\"","mark":"Money worry, not a delusion of poverty; no psychotic features"},{"h":"Background and stressors","say":["Have you ever felt like this before? Anyone in the family with depression?","What else has changed in the last year or two? Who do you see?"],"hear":"\"Never. Mum had her nerves. My best mate Ken died last year. I don't see anyone now.\""},{"h":"Risk","say":["When things are at their worst, do you feel life isn't worth living? Have you thought of ending it, or how?","What stops you? Are there tablets saved up, or any guns in the house?","Are you eating and drinking enough? Are you still driving?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead. No plan. I wouldn't do that to Pat. I drive to the shops, never after a drink.\"","mark":"Passive death wishes, no plan; driving to address"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Reg, this sounds like depression, and quite a heavy one. It's an illness, and very treatable at any age.","We'll ask your GP to check your physical health, start a medicine to help sleep and appetite, and see you weekly.","Please don't drive for now. Pat, if he stops eating or talks of ending his life, call us that day. Can you both tell me the plan?"],"hear":"(Pat) \"Is there anything that can get him back to himself?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Depressive symptoms and severity","Low every day, worse mornings, 4am waking, no enjoyment, slowed","Severe depressive episode with biological features"],["Physical causes","A stone in 3 months; no red flags; 2 cans a night","Physical work-up still needed; alcohol harmful"],["Cognition","Slow, effortful, 3/3 recall","Depression, not dementia; recheck later"],["Psychotic features","Money worry shifts with facts; no voices","No delusion of poverty; non-psychotic depression"],["Risk","Passive death wish, no plan; drives; no firearms","Significant but not imminent risk; stop driving; weekly contact"]]},"key":"Test the belief, do not label it: a money worry that bends to facts is anxiety; one held with certainty against the facts is a delusion of poverty and changes the diagnosis, the urgency and the treatment.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[7],[6,8]]},"c-t-40":{"name":"Right parietal check: notice, build, feel, then what is spared","why":"Gives the stroke team a test-by-test map of what Harry misses and why, so rehabilitation and safety can be planned.","frame":["Inattention and neglect","Constructional and dressing apraxia","Awareness of weakness","Cortical sensation","Dominant parietal signs","Fields and praxis"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Harold Fenwick?","Thank you, Harry. Your family wondered if the stroke has affected how you think and notice things.","I'd like to do some short tests with paper and objects. Is that alright, and are you comfortable? Which hand do you write with? Glasses on? Is it OK if the nurse stays?"],"hear":"\"Go on then. Right-handed. Glasses are here.\""},{"h":"Neglect and inattention","say":["Look at my nose. Tell me which finger moves: this one... this one... now?","Eyes closed. Say 'left', 'right' or 'both' when I touch your hands.","Mark the middle of this line for me. Now cross out every star on this page."],"hear":"\"Right... left... right.\" (Misses the left when both move or both are touched; marks the line well to the right; leaves the left-hand stars.)","mark":"Left visual and sensory extinction; left neglect"},{"h":"Constructional and dressing","say":["Copy these two overlapping shapes as carefully as you can.","Draw me a clock, numbers included, showing ten past eleven.","Could you put this gown on for me?"],"hear":"(Left side of the pentagons distorted; clock numbers bunched on the right; gown on the right arm, left sleeve hanging.) \"Done.\"","mark":"Constructional and dressing apraxia"},{"h":"Anosognosia","say":["Is anything wrong with your left arm or leg?","Do you think you could walk home today?"],"hear":"\"It's just a bit lazy. I'd be fine at home.\"","mark":"Partial anosognosia"},{"h":"Cortical sensation","say":["First, can you feel me touching each hand? Is your finger going up or down?","Eyes closed: what am I putting in your hand? Now the other hand.","What number am I drawing on your palm? Is this one point or two?"],"hear":"\"Coin. Key.\" (right) \"Don't know... something round?\" (left; cannot read numbers or tell one point from two on the left)","mark":"Left cortical sensory loss with basic touch intact"},{"h":"Dominant parietal signs","say":["Which finger is this? Put your right hand on your left ear.","What's 100 take away 7? And 38 minus 14?","Write a sentence about the weather."],"hear":"\"Index. (correct) 93. 24. 'It's raining again.'\" (written on the right of the page)","mark":"Gerstmann features absent: dominant parietal spared"},{"h":"Visual fields and praxis","say":["Cover one eye. Look at my nose and tell me when you see my finger move, in each corner. Now the other eye.","Show me how you'd brush your teeth. Salute for me.","Take the cap off this pen and write your name. Now repeat after me, and repeat these numbers."],"hear":"(Misses the lower left quarter in both eyes; mimes and uses the pen normally; repeats the sentence; six digits forwards.)","mark":"Left inferior quadrantanopia; praxis intact"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you, Harry, you've worked hard. The stroke has affected the right side of your brain that helps you notice your left and judge space.","Your memory, sums and writing are fine. That's why the left seems to disappear and dressing is tricky.","I'll share this with your therapists so they can train you to scan left. What will you tell your family?"],"hear":"\"So it's the stroke, not my marbles. I've got to keep looking left.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Inattention and neglect","Extinction left (sight and touch); line marked right; left stars missed","Left hemispatial neglect"],["Constructional and dressing apraxia","Pentagons distorted left; clock crowded right; left sleeve missed","Non-dominant parietal damage"],["Awareness of weakness","'Just a bit lazy'; thinks he could walk home","Partial anosognosia: safety risk"],["Cortical sensation","Touch intact; left astereognosis, agraphaesthesia, two-point loss","Right parietal sensory cortex involved"],["Dominant signs and fields","Fingers, left-right, sums, writing normal; left inferior quadrantanopia","Dominant parietal spared; right optic radiation affected"]]},"key":"Test each side alone before both together. A left target missed only on double stimulation is inattention; one missed even alone is a field defect, which he can learn to compensate for.","objmap":[[1],[2,7],[3,4,5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-43":{"name":"Honest, then medical: explaining NMS to a frightened father","why":"Turns an angry relative into a partner by being truthful about the cause, the danger and the plan.","frame":["What we found","What it is","What we are doing now","What could go wrong","What happens to his psychosis"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent, honesty, urgency","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Mr Asante, Kofi's father?","Kofi has agreed I can talk to you. I'll be honest: he's become physically unwell and we're acting quickly.","May I explain what we've found and what we're doing, then answer your questions?"],"hear":"\"Yes. The nurse said he's unwell. What's going on? Go on.\""},{"h":"What we found","say":["This afternoon Kofi developed a very high temperature, 41 degrees, a fast heart rate, and his muscles have become stiff.","His blood tests show signs of muscle breakdown and inflammation, and his liver tests are a little raised."],"hear":"\"He was fine on Monday. What's happened to him?\""},{"h":"Name it: NMS, not an allergy","say":["We think he's had a rare but serious reaction to haloperidol, called neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","It's not an allergy. It's unpredictable, but higher doses, quick increases and injections make it more likely.","I'm very sorry that this has happened to him."],"hear":"\"So the injections did this. I agreed to hospital so he'd get better, not be poisoned.\"","mark":"Cause explained and apology given"},{"h":"What we are doing now","say":["We've stopped the haloperidol and won't give him any similar medicine for now.","He's moving to the medical team right now for fluids into a vein, cooling and close monitoring of his temperature, heart, muscles and kidneys.","They'll also check for infection, and may use medicines to relax his muscles if needed."],"hear":"\"How long will he be there?\""},{"h":"Complications and outlook, honestly","say":["It is serious and can be life-threatening, which is why we're acting fast.","The main risks are to the kidneys, the chest and clots, and the medical team will watch for all of these.","With prompt treatment most people recover fully, usually within one to two weeks."],"hear":"\"Could he die?\"","mark":"Risk and prognosis communicated"},{"h":"His anger and blame","say":["You have every right to be angry and to ask questions.","This will be reported and reviewed, his consultant will meet you, and I'll give you PALS details if you want to raise a formal concern.","Bringing him in was right; this reaction couldn't have been predicted."],"hear":"\"I want someone senior to explain why he got so much.\""},{"h":"Future treatment of his psychosis","say":["He'll still need treatment for his psychosis, but not until at least two weeks after he's fully recovered.","We'd then choose a different medicine at a low dose and increase it slowly, checking temperature and blood tests.","We'll record this reaction clearly so no one gives him haloperidol again."],"hear":"\"So he can still be treated. Will it happen again?\""},{"h":"Summary, contact, close","say":["Can I check what you've taken from this? It's a lot to hear.","Here's the ward number. Please tell staff straight away if you notice he's more confused or stiff.","Would you like to go with him to the medical ward now?"],"hear":"\"A rare reaction, drug stopped, medical ward, fluids, consultant on Monday. Yes, I'll go with him.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Part of the explanation","What he heard","What it means for him"],"rows":[["What we found","Fever 41, fast pulse, stiff muscles, muscle enzyme over 1,000","His son is physically ill, not just 'unsettled'"],["What it is","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome; not an allergy; doses and injections raised the risk","An honest cause, with an apology"],["What we do now","Drug stopped; medical team; fluids, cooling, monitoring, infection checks","Concrete action tonight"],["What could go wrong","Kidneys, chest, clots; can be fatal; most recover in 1-2 weeks","The truth, with realistic hope"],["What happens next","Different drug, low dose, slow increase after 2 weeks; reaction recorded","His psychosis will still be treated, safely"]]},"key":"Being right about NMS is not enough. Excellent practice pairs the diagnosis with an early apology, an honest answer about death, and a clear route to future treatment.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-51":{"name":"Lewy body dementia for the family: hear, check, explain, then the safe options","why":"Meets the grandson's hopes honestly, rules out a treatable cause, and leaves the family with a safe, practical plan.","frame":["Three core features","New causes and delirium","DaT scan and the one-year rule","Rivastigmine, levodopa and antipsychotics","Home, carers and planning"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, consent, his hopes","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso. Gerald has agreed that we can talk openly.","Before I explain, what do you already know?","And what were you hoping we could do today?"],"hear":"\"Something with Lewy in it. I hoped there's a tablet to stop the visions.\"","mark":"Ideas and expectations identified"},{"h":"What Ellis has seen","say":["Tell me what he sees, and how he is with it.","Are there good days and very muddled days?","What happens at night, and has he been cross with anyone?"],"hear":"\"He talks to Gran and his mum, puts milk out for a cat. Some days he's nearly himself. He shouted at Mum, thought she was a burglar.\"","mark":"Hallucinations, fluctuations and night-time disturbance identified"},{"h":"Rule out new causes","say":["Has anything changed recently: a water infection, a cough, constipation or pain?","Any new tablets, from the GP or the hospital?","Was the change sudden, over a few days, or gradual?"],"hear":"\"Bloods were fine two weeks ago. No new tablets. It's crept up over months.\"","mark":"Delirium triggers screened"},{"h":"Explain Lewy body dementia","say":["Small protein deposits, called Lewy bodies, collect inside brain cells and upset the chemicals for alertness, memory and movement.","That's why he has three things: visions, good and bad days, and stiffness with falls.","The scan shows low dopamine activity. Memory went first, stiffness later, so it's this, not Parkinson's disease dementia. It will slowly get worse."],"hear":"\"So it's all one illness. And it won't get better.\"","mark":"Diagnosis and prognosis understood"},{"h":"Medicine options","say":["Rivastigmine is the best medicine for the visions. If his pulse and heart tracing are fine, we raise it to 4.5 mg twice a day today.","Levodopa helps stiffness only, and can make visions worse, so not for now.","You've read right: antipsychotics can be very dangerous in this illness. We avoid them."],"hear":"\"Thank God. I was scared someone would give him one.\""},{"h":"Managing at home","say":["Don't argue with what he sees. If it comforts him, go along gently; if it frightens him, reassure and distract.","Good lighting, fewer shadows, night lights, his glasses on.","I'll ask an occupational therapist and the falls physio to visit."],"hear":"\"We've been telling him Gran's dead. That just upsets him.\""},{"h":"Risk and the carers","say":["Is he still driving, and who gives him his tablets?","How are you and your mum coping?","There's a carers' assessment for your mum, and a power of attorney is worth doing while he can take part."],"hear":"\"He stopped driving. Mum's exhausted. He forgets his tablets.\"","mark":"Carer strain and adherence risk identified"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["What will you tell your mum about today, in your words?","If he's suddenly much more confused or drowsy, ring the GP that day or 111. A fall with a head injury, 999.","The memory nurse will call this week, and I'll see him in four weeks."],"hear":"\"Higher dose, no antipsychotics, lights on, help for Mum. I've got it.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature or option","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Visual hallucinations","Late wife, his mother, cat, fox, children's faces","Core feature; treat if distressing, with rivastigmine first"],["Fluctuations","Some days nearly himself, others lost","Core feature; explains 'good days' to the family"],["Parkinsonism and falls","Stiff, slow, two falls this month","Core feature; falls service, check postural BP"],["New causes","Bloods clear, no new tablets, gradual change","Delirium unlikely now; recheck if sudden change"],["Antipsychotics","Has read they are dangerous","Avoid; severe sensitivity reactions"],["Carers","Mother exhausted, tablets forgotten","Carers' assessment, blister pack or patch, LPA"]]},"key":"The safest treatment for Lewy body visions is usually more cholinesterase inhibitor, not an antipsychotic. Say so plainly, and always exclude delirium before calling a decline 'the dementia'.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-60":{"name":"Not lithium today, but this: gather, explain, plan","why":"Holds a safe line with a pressured colleague while giving the ward a plan that changes things today.","frame":["Her concerns and expectations","Presentation and treatment so far","Physical health and monitoring","Why not lithium today","Today's medication plan","Ward safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge and find her expectation","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on call. Are you Aisha, the nurse in charge?","You're worried about Connor not improving and other patients reacting to him.","Let's go through what's happened so far, then agree a plan together."],"hear":"\"Exactly. And we want him on lithium today.\"","mark":"Expectation identified: lithium today"},{"h":"Current presentation","say":["How is he: mood, speech, sleep, eating and drinking?","Any suspicious ideas or psychotic symptoms?","What's his behaviour like with others?"],"hear":"\"Elated, talking non-stop, two or three hours' sleep, barely eating, thinks we'll experiment on his blood, in everyone's face.\"","mark":"Severe mania with paranoid ideas identified"},{"h":"Treatment so far","say":["What's he on, since when, and is he taking it?","How much PRN has he had?","What did Dr Hollis say exactly?"],"hear":"\"Olanzapine 10 at night for three nights, with persuasion. One lorazepam. Dr Hollis said lithium if olanzapine fails.\"","mark":"Low dose, short trial identified"},{"h":"Physical health","say":["Has he allowed bloods or observations?","How much is he drinking? Any injuries?"],"hear":"\"He refuses bloods and obs. Drinks a bit of juice. No injuries.\"","mark":"No baseline bloods; dehydration risk"},{"h":"Why not lithium today","say":["I agree he needs more help today. But lithium isn't safe without kidney, thyroid and calcium tests first.","We'd need levels within a week; if he's not drinking, it could become toxic without us knowing.","And three days at 10 mg isn't a fair trial of olanzapine; 15 mg is the usual dose for mania."],"hear":"\"But Dr Hollis said lithium.\"","mark":"Reasoned decline given"},{"h":"Agreed medication plan","say":["Olanzapine 15 mg tonight, and up to 20 mg in the week if he needs it.","Lorazepam 1 to 2 mg by mouth as needed, up to 4 mg in 24 hours.","Keep offering bloods with his named nurse; Dr Hollis can start lithium once they're done."],"hear":"\"OK, that's something we can actually do.\""},{"h":"Ward safety","say":["Let's increase observations to within eyesight and nurse him in the quieter lounge.","Keep him away from the patient who nearly hit him, and log it as a safeguarding concern.","Food and fluid chart, and visual observations if he won't allow hands-on."],"hear":"\"I'll need another member of staff for that.\"","mark":"Risk to and from others addressed"},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["I'll document this and email Dr Hollis for Monday.","Call me if there's violence, he stops drinking, or you're worried about him physically.","Can you talk the bleep holder through the staffing? Does this plan work for you?"],"hear":"\"Yes. Thanks for explaining it properly.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Expectation","Lithium today because the consultant said so","Needs a reasoned no and an alternative"],["Presentation","Elated, pressured, 2 to 3 h sleep, little food, suspicious","Severe mania"],["Treatment","Olanzapine 10 mg for 3 nights","Below usual dose; trial too short"],["Physical","No bloods or obs; drinking little","Lithium unsafe; dehydration risk"],["Risk","In others' faces; nearly assaulted","Risk to him and others; observation and environment"]]},"key":"A good no comes with a better yes. Explain exactly why lithium is unsafe today, then give the ward a plan they can use within the hour.","objmap":[[1,3],[2,3,4],[2,7],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-61":{"name":"The low carer: his mood, her care, then both their safety","why":"Finds a treatable depression in an exhausted carer and uncovers the risk to him and to his wife.","frame":["Depressive symptoms","Caring burden","Physical health and substances","Suicide risk","Risk to Margaret"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: privacy and purpose","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the older adults team. Are you Mr Desmond Clarke?","Thank you for letting me visit, Des. Your GP mentioned things have felt heavy lately.","Is there somewhere we can talk privately for a while, and is Margaret settled?"],"hear":"\"She's watching telly, she's fine. We can sit in the kitchen.\""},{"h":"Mood symptoms","say":["How's your mood been, and how long has it been like this?","What have you stopped enjoying? How are your sleep, appetite and weight?","How is your concentration? Do you feel guilty, or that things won't get better?"],"hear":"\"Three months now. I'm up at four every morning. I've lost half a stone and I've let the allotment go.\"","mark":"Depressive episode identified"},{"h":"Caring role","say":["Talk me through a normal day and night with Margaret.","Who helps you? When did you last have a break?","How do you feel about it all, deep down?"],"hear":"\"She's up most nights, wandering. Nobody helps. I feel I'm failing her, and sometimes I'm cross with her, then I hate myself.\"","mark":"Carer strain identified"},{"h":"Physical health","say":["How are your knees and hands? What do you take for the pain?","Any falls? How are your hearing and eyesight?","Have you noticed problems with your memory?"],"hear":"\"The knees are agony. I take ibuprofen and co-codamol. I fell in the garden. I forget things, and I worry I'm going the way she did.\""},{"h":"Psychotic and cognitive screen","say":["When you feel guilty, do you feel you deserve to be punished?","Do you ever feel your body is rotting, or that you have no money at all, even if others disagree?","Any voices when nobody's there, or seeing things others can't?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. It's just guilt.\"","mark":"Psychotic features excluded"},{"h":"Alcohol and medicines","say":["What's your drinking like these days, in a typical week?","Are there any tablets saved up in the house, yours or Margaret's?"],"hear":"\"A couple of whiskies at night to sleep. There's a tin of old co-codamol in the drawer.\""},{"h":"Suicide risk","say":["Have things ever felt so bad that you've thought of ending your life?","Have you thought about how, or done anything to prepare, like writing letters or sorting affairs?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"I've looked at that tin and thought it'd do it. No plan. The grandchildren keep me going.\"","mark":"Suicidal ideation with access to means identified"},{"h":"Risk to Margaret","say":["Some carers think it might be kinder if they both went. Has that crossed your mind?","Have you ever felt like hurting her, or found it hard to keep up with her care?"],"hear":"\"Some nights I wonder if we'd both be better off gone. I'd never hurt her. I did shout at her once, at three in the morning.\"","mark":"Joint-death thoughts and carer frustration identified"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["What you've told me sounds like depression, and it's treatable. You've been carrying far too much alone.","Today I'd like that tin out of the house, to call your daughter with your permission, and to get help in for both of you.","If things get worse before I see you again in two days, ring Samaritans on 116 123 or 111 option 2."],"hear":"\"All right. Karen can have the tablets. I didn't think anyone could help.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Depressive symptoms","Wakes 4 am, half a stone lost, allotment dropped, guilt","Moderate to severe depressive episode"],["Caring burden","Night wandering, no help, frustration then self-blame","Carer strain driving and maintaining the depression"],["Physical health and substances","Uncontrolled arthritis pain, fall, whisky nightly, saved co-codamol","Pain and alcohol worsen mood; means at hand"],["Suicide risk","Thought of the tin, no plan, grandchildren protective","Significant risk needing means removal and urgent follow-up"],["Risk to Margaret","'Both better off gone'; shouted once; no wish to harm","Joint-death thoughts and risk of neglect: support and safeguarding awareness"]]},"key":"Ask the joint-death question. A depressed husband caring alone for a wife with dementia may plan for two; the risk assessment is incomplete until you have asked about Margaret as well as him.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-t-68":{"name":"Results, then the road: explain, then stop the driving safely","why":"Gives a worried son clear answers and a lawful, humane plan to stop his mother driving.","frame":["His concerns","The memory test","The alcohol picture","What it may mean","Driving now","Longer term"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists at the memory clinic. Am I speaking with Stephen Doyle?","Thank you for calling, Stephen. Your mum has agreed I can talk with you. I can hear this has been upsetting.","Let me explain what we've found and why, then we'll plan together."],"hear":"\"She's livid. Says you're taking her car.\""},{"h":"His concerns and observations","say":["What have you been told, and what's worrying you most?","How has she been managing at home?","Any problems with her driving? Where does she drive?"],"hear":"\"Missed bills. Scraped the car twice, got lost driving to mine. I only found out about the whisky from a neighbour.\"","mark":"Driving incidents and self-neglect identified"},{"h":"What we found","say":["The nurse did a memory test called the ACE-III, scored out of 100. Your mum scored 50.","Most people without memory problems score above 82 to 88.","Her weakest areas were memory and judging space and shapes. The nurse also noticed she looked unkempt and smelled of alcohol."],"hear":"\"Fifty. That's half.\""},{"h":"The alcohol picture","say":["She told her GP she gets through half a bottle of whisky or more each day, about 14 units: a week's limit, daily.","One blood test shows heavy drinking, the other that her liver is under strain.","She mustn't stop suddenly; that can cause fits. Her GP will start thiamine, a vitamin that protects the brain."],"hear":"\"I had no idea it was that much.\""},{"h":"What it might mean","say":["The score suggests dementia, probably moderate.","Alcohol can harm memory too, so it may be dementia, alcohol-related damage, or both.","A brain scan, bloods, and a repeat test after she's cut down will tell us more."],"hear":"\"So it might get better if she stops?\"","mark":"Diagnostic uncertainty explained"},{"h":"Driving now","say":["I'm sorry, but on what we have, she isn't safe to drive, and needs to stop now.","By law she must tell the DVLA. We'll ask her once more, together.","If she won't, we tell her we'll inform the DVLA, do it, and write to her. Police and keys are last resorts."],"hear":"\"She'll never forgive me.\"","mark":"Driving stopped; DVLA sequence agreed"},{"h":"Longer term","say":["What does driving mean to her? Let's plan what replaces it: lifts, taxi account, bus pass, community transport.","An alcohol worker can help her cut down safely.","Lasting Power of Attorney while she still can, a social care assessment, and a carer's assessment for you."],"hear":"\"Her freedom, and seeing her friends at church.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: the test suggests dementia, the drinking may add to it, and she must stop driving now.","In your words, how will you put this to her this week?","The consultant will meet you both this week. Call 999 if she's driving and anyone is at risk."],"hear":"\"No driving, you'll tell the DVLA if she won't, and we sort lifts.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His concerns","Missed bills, two scrapes, got lost","Real-world decline, already affecting driving"],["The memory test","ACE-III 50/100, memory and visuospatial","Well below cut-offs: likely moderate dementia"],["The alcohol picture","Half a bottle daily, raised ALT and GGT","Heavy drinking with liver strain; withdrawal risk"],["Driving now","Refused advice twice, still driving","Unsafe: stop now, DVLA, GMC sequence"],["Longer term","Driving means freedom to her","Replace transport, treat alcohol, LPA, carer support"]]},"key":"Do not let the alcohol become a reason to wait: whatever the cause, she is unsafe now. Treat the drinking and retest, but stop the driving today in the right order.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-t-69":{"name":"Both arms, every system: look, tone, power, reflexes, coordination, feeling","why":"Rules a neurological deficit in or out reliably, while showing the patient his symptom is taken seriously.","frame":["Inspection","Tone and power","Reflexes","Coordination","Sensation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","I hear your right arm has felt numb and tingly. I'd like to check the nerves and muscles in both arms. It takes about ten minutes. Is that alright, and would you like a chaperone?","Could you slip down to your vest? Any pain anywhere before I start?"],"hear":"\"That's fine.\""},{"h":"Inspection","say":["I'm just going to look at both arms first.","Hold your arms out in front, palms down, fingers spread."],"hear":"\"Like this?\"","mark":"No wasting, fasciculation, scars or tremor"},{"h":"Tone","say":["Let your arm go floppy; I'll do the moving.","I'll roll the wrist and bend the elbow, then the other side."],"hear":"\"Okay, relaxed.\"","mark":"Tone normal and equal"},{"h":"Power","say":["Arms out like a chicken; don't let me push them down. Now pull me in, push me away.","Cock your wrists back; now bend them down. Make a fist; squeeze my fingers.","Spread your fingers; don't let me close them. Thumb to the ceiling; hold it there."],"hear":"\"Both feel the same strength.\"","mark":"Power 5/5 throughout, symmetrical"},{"h":"Reflexes","say":["Let your arms rest on your lap; I'll tap a few places.","Clench your teeth for me while I tap again.","I'll flick the tip of your middle finger and watch your thumb."],"hear":"\"Funny feeling.\"","mark":"Reflexes normal; Hoffmann's negative"},{"h":"Coordination","say":["Arms out, palms up, eyes closed; hold still.","Touch my finger, then your nose, as quickly as you can.","Pat one hand with the other, flipping it over, fast as you can."],"hear":"\"Both feel fine.\"","mark":"No drift; coordination normal"},{"h":"Sensation","say":["Here's the cotton wool on your chest; eyes closed now, say yes each time you feel it, and if it's the same both sides.","Now sharp or blunt? Now cold? I'll go shoulder, thumb, middle finger, little finger, inner elbow.","Feel the buzz on your thumb joint? Tell me when it stops. Is your thumb moving up or down?"],"hear":"\"Same both sides, all of them.\"","mark":"All modalities intact C5-T1"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you; you can get dressed.","Strength, reflexes, coordination and feeling are all the same on both sides. I haven't found a nerve problem.","We'll check some bloods for causes of tingling, and if anything changes, tell the nurses so we look again."],"hear":"\"That's a relief. But it's still tingling.\""}],"table":{"cols":["System","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Inspection","No wasting, fasciculation or tremor","No lower motor neuron or extrapyramidal sign"],["Tone and power","Normal tone; MRC 5/5 throughout","No upper or lower motor neuron weakness"],["Reflexes","2+ symmetrical; Hoffmann's negative","No root lesion or cord sign"],["Coordination","No drift; finger-nose normal","No cerebellar or subtle pyramidal sign"],["Sensation","All modalities intact C5-T1","No root, nerve or tract deficit found"]]},"key":"A normal examination does not end the story: say it is reassuring, still treat the symptom as real, and screen for treatable causes such as B12 deficiency, diabetes and carpal tunnel.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-t-70":{"name":"Pulse to feet: hunting for vascular causes of memory loss and falls","why":"Finds the treatable vascular signs behind cognitive decline and falls, while teaching what each one means.","frame":["General signs","Pulse and rhythm","Blood pressure","Neck and heart","Lungs, legs and feet"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Desmond Clarke?","Thanks, Des. I'd like to check your heart, pulse and blood vessels, as they can affect memory. A student is with me today. May we go ahead, and would you like a chaperone?","Lie back at this angle and slip your top off; any pain anywhere?"],"hear":"\"That's fine.\""},{"h":"General look","say":["Priya, I'm looking for breathlessness at rest, signs of self-neglect, and insulin injection sites.","These yellow patches round the eyes are xanthelasma; with a grey ring on the iris before 60, they suggest high cholesterol."],"hear":"Student: \"So these are a vascular risk clue?\"","mark":"Xanthelasma noted"},{"h":"Hands and pulse","say":["Nicotine staining tells us about smoking.","I'm feeling the radial pulse for 30 seconds: rate, rhythm, volume. Irregularly irregular would mean atrial fibrillation, a cause of strokes.","Now both wrists together for any delay, which would suggest narrowing above."],"hear":"Student: \"It's regular?\"","mark":"Regular pulse, no delay"},{"h":"Blood pressure","say":["Both arms first: a gap over 15 means narrowing; we use the higher arm.","Des, I'll take it lying, then after a minute standing. Tell me if you feel dizzy.","A fall of 20 or more on standing is postural hypotension, which could explain his falls."],"hear":"\"I'm a bit dizzy.\"","mark":"Hypertension and postural drop found"},{"h":"Neck","say":["Head turned slightly left; I'm looking for the JVP, the pressure in the neck vein.","Now each carotid pulse, one side at a time, never both together.","Hold your breath for me: I'm listening for a bruit, a whoosh from a narrowed artery."],"hear":"Student: \"Why one side at a time?\"","mark":"No carotid bruits"},{"h":"Heart","say":["I'm feeling for the apex beat, then heaves and thrills.","Listening in four areas, timing with the carotid pulse.","A harsh murmur spreading to the neck could be aortic stenosis, a cause of dizziness and falls."],"hear":"Student: \"Nothing extra?\""},{"h":"Lungs and legs","say":["Sit forward: crackles at the bases would suggest heart failure.","Pressing the ankles for swelling.","Now foot pulses, skin, ulcers and sensation: in diabetes, weak pulses mean disease in other arteries too, including the brain."],"hear":"\"My feet always feel cold.\"","mark":"Peripheral arterial disease signs"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you, Des, you can get dressed.","Your blood pressure is high and drops when you stand, which may explain the falls; the arteries in your left foot are weak.","Next: a heart tracing, bloods for cholesterol and sugar, a brain scan, and a review of your tablets."],"hear":"\"So my heart might be behind my memory?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["General signs","Xanthelasma; nicotine staining","High cholesterol and smoking: vascular risk"],["Pulse and rhythm","Regular 82, no delay","No AF on examination; ECG still needed"],["Blood pressure","162/96 lying, 138/88 standing, dizzy","Hypertension and postural hypotension: falls"],["Neck and heart","No bruits, no murmurs, apex normal","No carotid or aortic stenosis signs"],["Lungs, legs and feet","Weak left foot pulses, reduced sensation","Peripheral arterial disease and neuropathy"]]},"key":"The heart sounds rarely make the diagnosis here. The lying and standing BP, pulse rhythm, carotids and feet carry the vascular evidence and explain the falls; say each link out loud.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,6,7],[8]]},"c-u-11":{"name":"The 4 a.m. request to leave: attempt, intent, others, decision","why":"Gets the full picture of a near-lethal attempt fast, then reaches a safe, lawful and honest decision he can understand.","frame":["The attempt","Current intent and plan","Mental state","Risk to and from others","Decision, rights and holding powers","Ward safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (under 1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on call tonight. Are you Gareth Lloyd?","I hear you, Gareth. I'd like to understand why, and talk it through properly before anything is decided.","Can we sit down for about ten minutes? Tell me what's changed since you arrived."],"hear":"\"There's no point me being here. I need to get home.\""},{"h":"Why leave now (1 min)","say":["What's made you want to go right now, in the middle of the night?","What would you do first when you got home?","How would you get there at this hour?"],"hear":"\"I'm going to bring the kids back. I haven't thought about how.\"","mark":"Plan on leaving identified"},{"h":"The attempt (2 min)","say":["Can you take me through the night it happened, from the start?","How long had you been planning it? Did you write anything, or tell anyone?","When the rope broke, what went through your mind? How do you feel about it now?"],"hear":"\"I'd planned it for a week. I left a note for the kids. I'm not glad it broke.\"","mark":"High-intent, near-lethal attempt identified"},{"h":"Current intent (1 min)","say":["Are you still thinking about ending your life tonight?","Have you thought how, or is there anything at home you'd use?","If the children didn't want to come with you, what then?"],"hear":"\"I don't know. It's my last chance. If that fails, what's the point?\"","mark":"Current intent linked to a likely trigger"},{"h":"Mental state (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been over the last couple of months?","Sleep, appetite, enjoying anything, concentrating?","Any drink or drugs? Any voices or feeling that people are against you?"],"hear":"\"Low for two months. Can't enjoy anything. I don't drink. Nothing like voices.\"","mark":"Depressive episode, no psychosis"},{"h":"Risk and protective factors (1 min)","say":["Have you had any thoughts of harming anyone else, including your wife or the children?","Is anyone threatening you or taking advantage of you?","Who's still on your side? What's kept you going until now?"],"hear":"\"God, no, I'd never hurt them. My brother rings sometimes. The kids, I suppose.\"","mark":"Risk to others denied; protective factors named"},{"h":"Plan and his rights (1.5 min)","say":["I don't think it's safe for you to leave tonight: you nearly died a day ago, you might try again, and nothing has been treated yet.","I'd like you to stay voluntarily until the consultant sees you at nine.","To be honest with you: if you tried to leave, a nurse could hold you for up to six hours, and I could hold you for up to 72, so a full assessment can happen."],"hear":"\"So I'm stuck here.\"","mark":"Decision and holding powers explained"},{"h":"Ward safety (1 min)","say":["A nurse will stay with you, within sight, for the rest of tonight.","We'll go through your belongings together and take away anything you could hurt yourself with.","How's your neck? Any change in your voice, or trouble swallowing or breathing?"],"hear":"\"It's sore but my voice is fine. I can swallow okay.\""},{"h":"Summary and close (under 1 min)","say":["So: you'll stay tonight, a nurse is with you, and the consultant sees you at nine.","I'll ring my senior now and write all this down.","What questions do you have for me?"],"hear":"\"Will I get to see my kids?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The attempt","Planned a week, note to children, timed to avoid rescue, rope snapped","High intent, high lethality"],["Now","Not glad he survived; 'last chance' plan to collect the children","Current risk with a trigger likely tonight"],["Mental state","Two months low, anhedonic, hopeless; no psychosis; no alcohol","Untreated depressive episode"],["Others","Denies harming wife or children; no threats from others","Low-certainty risk to children: tell senior"],["Capacity to leave","No transport, no plan, can't weigh outcome","Cannot safely weigh the decision tonight"]]},"key":"Good: you assess risk and say 'stay'. Excellent: you ask about the children, check the neck, explain holding powers honestly without threatening, and ring a senior before using 5(2).","objmap":[[3],[2,4],[5,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-u-13":{"name":"Chest pain on the ward: ECG first, then a full heart check","why":"Gets the dangerous causes ruled in or out fast, with an ordered examination she understands and a clean handover to the medics.","frame":["Immediate safety","Pain history","Peripheral signs","Heart","Lungs and legs","Escalation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (under 1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Brenda Oyelaran?","I'm glad you told the nurses. Is the pain there right now, and are you short of breath at this moment?","I'm going to check your heart properly, starting with your hands and pulse. May I, with a nurse here too?"],"hear":"\"It's eased a bit, but I'm scared. Yes, go ahead.\"","mark":"Pain present now, consent and chaperone"},{"h":"Quick safety check (1.5 min)","say":["Joy, could you do a 12-lead ECG straight after the observations, please?","When did it start, where is it, and what does it feel like? Does it spread anywhere?","Any sweating, sickness, faintness, or pain when you breathe in? Any calf pain?","Any heart problems, blood pressure, diabetes, smoking, or heart disease in the family?"],"hear":"\"Tight, in the middle, during my panic. Blood pressure tablets. My dad had a heart attack at 60.\"","mark":"Pain history and risk factors taken"},{"h":"General inspection (under 1 min)","say":["Could you sit back at about 45 degrees? I'll keep the sheet over you and only uncover what I need.","I'm just looking at your breathing and colour, and around the bed for any sprays or oxygen."],"hear":"\"I don't have any sprays.\""},{"h":"Hands and pulse (1.5 min)","say":["Your hands are warm. I'm pressing a fingernail for a few seconds to see how quickly the colour comes back.","I'm feeling the pulse at both wrists together. Any pain in your shoulders before I lift your arm?","The nurse will check your blood pressure in both arms."],"hear":"\"No pain. Is my pulse okay?\"","mark":"Pulse regular, BP equal in both arms"},{"h":"Face and neck (1 min)","say":["Could you look up for me? I'm checking under your eyelids for paleness.","Turn your head slightly to the left and relax; I'm looking at the veins in your neck.","I'm going to feel the pulse in your neck gently, one side at a time."],"hear":"\"That's fine.\""},{"h":"Praecordium (2 min)","say":["I'm looking at your chest for scars, then feeling where your heartbeat is strongest.","Now I'll listen in four places. Roll onto your left side for me, please.","Last one: lean forward, blow all your breath out and hold."],"hear":"\"Can you hear anything wrong?\"","mark":"Heart sounds normal, no murmurs"},{"h":"Lungs and legs (1 min)","say":["I'm listening at the bottom of your lungs at the back. Deep breaths through your mouth.","I'm pressing gently on your lower back and ankles for any fluid, and checking your calves. Tell me if anything hurts."],"hear":"\"No, nothing hurts there.\"","mark":"Chest clear, no oedema, calves soft"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["Your heart tracing and examination look reassuring, and your oxygen is good.","Because of your age and your dad's history, I'm asking the medical doctor to see you and check a heart blood test.","If the pain comes back or gets worse, press your buzzer at once. Do you have any questions?"],"hear":"\"Thank you for actually checking. I'll press the buzzer.\""}],"table":{"cols":["System","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Pain history","Central tightness during panic, now 2/10; hypertension, ex-smoker, father MI at 60","Cardiac cause possible: needs troponin"],["Observations and ECG","NEWS2 2; sinus 96, no ST changes, QTc 440","No immediate emergency"],["Hands, pulse, BP","Warm, CRT under 2 s, regular, equal BP both arms","No shock or dissection signs"],["Face, neck, heart","No pallor, JVP not raised, normal heart sounds","No failure or valve disease found"],["Lungs and legs","Clear bases, no oedema, soft calves","No heart failure or DVT signs"]]},"key":"A good doctor examines thoroughly. An excellent one gets the ECG running first, checks both arms and the calves, and still escalates a normal-looking 63-year-old with risk factors for troponin.","objmap":[[1,2],[2],[3,4,5,6,7],[8],[8]]},"c-u-14":{"name":"Neck swelling on lithium: symptoms, then front, behind and beyond","why":"Confirms a lithium-related underactive thyroid at the bedside in a set order, rules out red flags and keeps her on lithium.","frame":["Swelling and red flags","Hypothyroid symptoms","Hyperthyroid symptoms and lithium history","Hands, eyes and pulse","Neck: front and behind","Completing signs"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (under 1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Kerry-Ann Mensah?","The nurses told me you've noticed some swelling in your neck. I'd like to ask a few questions about it, then have a look and feel. Is that alright?","Nurse Ade will stay with us. Shall I close the door?"],"hear":"\"Yes please. It's been bothering me.\""},{"h":"Neck swelling story (1 min)","say":["When did you first notice it? Has it grown?","Is it painful? Any trouble swallowing, breathing, or any change in your voice?","Any lumps anywhere else?"],"hear":"\"About a month. It's slowly got bigger. No pain, swallowing's fine.\"","mark":"No red flags"},{"h":"Underactive thyroid symptoms (1.25 min)","say":["How's your energy? Have you put on weight without eating more?","Do you feel the cold more than others? How are your bowels?","Any change in your skin, hair, periods or sex drive? And your mood now?"],"hear":"\"Exhausted. Put on weight. I'm always cold, constipated, my hair's like straw.\"","mark":"Hypothyroid pattern identified"},{"h":"Overactive and background (under 1 min)","say":["Any feeling hot, sweaty, shaky, or a racing heart?","How long on lithium? When was your last lithium and thyroid blood test?","Anyone in your family with thyroid trouble?"],"hear":"\"No. Six years on lithium. Not sure about the thyroid test. Mum's on thyroid tablets.\"","mark":"Lithium exposure and family history"},{"h":"Inspection and swallow (1 min)","say":["Could you sit on this chair and loosen your collar? Is your neck sore anywhere?","Take a sip of water and hold it; now swallow while I watch.","Stick your tongue out for me. Now I'll look at your eyes from the side."],"hear":"\"It moved up when I swallowed, didn't it?\"","mark":"Swelling rises on swallowing"},{"h":"Palpation from behind (2 min)","say":["I'm going to stand behind you and feel your neck gently. Another sip and swallow, please.","Now the glands around your neck and the windpipe in the middle.","I'll tap just below your neck, then listen over the swelling."],"hear":"\"It doesn't hurt.\"","mark":"Smooth, symmetrical, no nodules or nodes"},{"h":"Peripheral signs (1.75 min)","say":["Hold your hands out, palms down. Now follow my finger without moving your head.","I'm checking your pulse, then tapping your ankle reflexes.","Cross your arms and stand up from the chair for me. Can I look at your shins?"],"hear":"\"Is my pulse slow?\"","mark":"Pulse 58, slow-relaxing reflexes"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.75 min)","say":["I think your thyroid has become underactive, and lithium is the likely cause. It's common and very treatable.","I'll send a thyroid test, calcium, kidney function and a lithium level. If it's confirmed, a daily thyroid tablet usually sorts it.","Please keep taking your lithium. Tell us at once about any voice change or trouble swallowing. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"So I can stay on lithium. That's a relief.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Swelling","Painless, one month, no swallowing, breathing or voice problems","No red flags: routine work-up"],["Symptoms","Tired, weight gain, cold, constipated, dry skin, low libido","Hypothyroid pattern"],["Background","Six years of lithium; mother hypothyroid; no recent TFTs","Lithium plus family risk"],["Neck","Smooth symmetrical goitre rising on swallowing, no nodes or bruit","Diffuse goitre"],["Peripheral signs","Pulse 58, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes","Supports hypothyroidism"]]},"key":"Good: you find the goitre. Excellent: you link it to lithium and her depression, check calcium and kidneys too, time the lithium level at 12 hours, and tell her not to stop lithium.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5,6,7],[7],[8]]},"c-u-16":{"name":"A bedside chest check that ends in a safe transfer","why":"Finds the cause of fever and breathlessness quickly, scores how sick she is, and gets her to the right team within the hour.","frame":["NEWS2 and history","Inspection","Hands, face and neck","Front of chest","Back of chest","Plan and Section 17"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, the psychiatry doctor on call tonight. Are you Sinead Fairweather? Can I check your wristband?","I'd like to ask a few questions and listen to your chest. Is that okay, with Nurse Okafor here too?","Can you sit up at an angle for me? Tell me if anything hurts. Nurse, what's her NEWS2 now?"],"hear":"\"Yes. It's six, Doctor.\" \"Okay. I just can't breathe properly.\"","mark":"NEWS2 6: urgent response"},{"h":"Focused questions while you look (1.5 min)","say":["When did the cough start? What colour is the phlegm, and any blood in it?","Does it hurt when you breathe in? Any pain or swelling in your legs?","Any asthma or chest problems before? Do you smoke? Have you choked on drinks since the new tablet?"],"hear":"\"Four days. Green. No pain. I smoke ten a day. Sometimes water goes down the wrong way.\"","mark":"Productive cough, no pleuritic pain, possible swallowing problem"},{"h":"From the end of the bed (1 min)","say":["You're working hard to breathe, I can see. Just breathe normally while I watch for a moment.","That cough sounds rough. Is this the pot you've been using?","Respiratory rate 24, using her neck muscles a little, speaking in full sentences, no oxygen on."],"hear":"\"Yes, that's it. It's all green.\""},{"h":"Hands, pulse, face and neck (1 min)","say":["Can I see your hands? Hold them out straight and cock your wrists back for me.","I'm feeling your pulse and now looking at your lips and tongue. I'll gently check your windpipe and the glands in your neck.","Can you bend and straighten your arm while I hold it? I'm checking your muscles aren't stiff."],"hear":"\"Okay. My arms feel normal.\"","mark":"No clubbing, cyanosis or flap; trachea central; no rigidity"},{"h":"Front of chest (1.5 min)","say":["I'm putting my hands on your chest. Take a deep breath in, and out.","Now I'll tap on your chest, comparing each side. Then I'll listen; breathe in and out through your mouth.","Say 'ninety-nine' each time I move the stethoscope."],"hear":"\"Ninety-nine... sorry, I need a break.\""},{"h":"Back of chest and legs (1.5 min)","say":["Lean forward for me, arms crossed. I'll do the same checks on your back.","Your right side sounds different down at the bottom. That fits an infection there.","Last thing: I'll press on your calves and ankles. Any tenderness?"],"hear":"\"No, they're fine.\"","mark":"Right basal consolidation; calves soft"},{"h":"Explain, plan and close (2 min)","say":["I think you have a chest infection at the bottom of your right lung. You need an X-ray, blood tests and antibiotics tonight, at the main hospital.","A nurse from here goes with you. I'll speak to the medical doctor and to your consultant about leave and about your new tablet.","Nurse, bloods with cultures and a CK, observations every 30 minutes, and 999 if the score reaches 7. Thank you, Sinead. I'll wash my hands now."],"hear":"\"Okay. As long as someone comes with me.\"","mark":"Transfer, Section 17 leave and safety net agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Check","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["NEWS2","RR 24, SpO2 95%, HR 106, T 39.6: score 6","Urgent response: medical review within the hour"],["History","4 days green sputum; chokes on drinks","Chest infection; possible aspiration on olanzapine"],["Hands, neck, tone","No cyanosis, trachea central, no rigidity","No chronic lung disease; NMS unlikely"],["Chest","Right base dull, bronchial breathing, crackles, louder vocal resonance","Right lower lobe consolidation"],["Legs","Calves soft, non-tender","Clot less likely, but keep in mind"]]},"key":"A good doctor finds the crackles. An excellent one scores NEWS2 first, rules out NMS and a clot, and gets a detained patient to the medical team legally, with Section 17 leave, an escort and a 999 trigger.","objmap":[[1],[2],[5,6],[4,6],[7]]},"c-u-17":{"name":"An abdominal check that does not miss clozapine bowel obstruction","why":"Separates simple constipation from obstruction quickly, and gets him safely to the surgeons with a clozapine plan.","frame":["Red-flag history","Inspection and obs","Hands to face","Palpation","Percussion and bowel sounds","Remaining checks and plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Lewis Marangoni? Can I check your wristband?","I need to check your tummy now. Is that okay, with Nurse Patel here?","Before I touch anything: where is the pain, and how bad out of ten?"],"hear":"\"Down here, low. About seven. Please don't stop my discharge.\"","mark":"Pain located: lower abdomen, 7/10"},{"h":"Red-flag questions (1.5 min)","say":["When did you last open your bowels? Are you passing wind?","Have you felt or been sick? What have you eaten today? Any watery leakage or blood?","Which laxatives did you actually take on leave? Any missed clozapine? Any tummy operations?"],"hear":"\"Twelve days. No wind since yesterday. A bit sick. I stopped the sachets at home.\"","mark":"No flatus, nausea, laxatives stopped: obstruction red flags"},{"h":"Inspection and observations (1 min)","say":["Can you lie flat on one pillow, arms by your sides, and uncover your tummy for me?","Your tummy looks swollen. Nurse, what's his NEWS2?","Pulse 104 and temperature 37.6: a score of 1, but that doesn't make this safe."],"hear":"\"It's one, Doctor.\""},{"h":"Hands to face (45 sec)","say":["Let me look at your hands and feel your pulse.","Now your eyes and mouth: look up for me. Stick your tongue out.","Your mouth is quite dry; you're probably a bit dehydrated."],"hear":"\"I've not drunk much today.\"","mark":"Dehydrated; no jaundice or pallor"},{"h":"Palpate: light, then deep (2 min)","say":["I'll start away from the sore area. Tell me if anything hurts; I'll watch your face too.","Now a little deeper in each area. Any pain when I let go?","Lower tummy tender, worst on the left, no guarding or rebound, no lumps."],"hear":"\"Ow, that bit, on the left.\""},{"h":"Percuss and listen (1 min)","say":["I'm going to tap across your tummy. It sounds hollow, like a drum: that's trapped gas.","Now I'll listen for gut noises for a full minute. Lie as still as you can.","Very quiet bowel sounds: your gut has slowed right down."],"hear":"\"Is that bad?\"","mark":"Tympanic, quiet bowel sounds: ileus or obstruction"},{"h":"Remaining checks (45 sec)","say":["I'd like to check the openings for hernias in your groin. Is that okay?","I'd also offer a check inside your back passage, with Nurse Patel here. It's your choice."],"hear":"\"Groin's fine. Not the back passage, though.\"","mark":"Hernias clear; rectal check declined and recorded"},{"h":"Explain, plan and close (1.75 min)","say":["Clozapine can slow the bowel right down, and yours may have stopped. That needs the surgeons at the main hospital today.","Nothing to eat or drink for now. We'll take bloods, hold tonight's clozapine until I've spoken to your consultant and pharmacist, and stop the hyoscine.","Your discharge may move a little, but it's not cancelled. Can you tell me what happens next?"],"hear":"\"Surgeons today, no food, bloods, and the clozapine might pause.\"","mark":"Same-day surgical review and clozapine plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Bowel history","12 days no stool, no wind 1 day, nausea","Obstruction or ileus, not simple constipation"],["Drugs","Clozapine 400 mg, hyoscine, laxatives stopped on leave","Strong gut slowing; preventable"],["Observations","NEWS2 1, pulse 104, dry mouth","Dehydrated; score falsely reassuring"],["Abdomen","Distended, tympanic, tender lower left, quiet sounds","Surgical review today"],["Remaining checks","Hernias clear; rectal check declined","Record; surgeons may repeat the offer"]]},"key":"A good doctor feels the tender, swollen abdomen. An excellent one calls it a possible obstruction despite a NEWS2 of 1, keeps him nil by mouth, sends him to the surgeons today, and plans the clozapine restart.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5,6],[7],[8],[8]]},"c-u-18":{"name":"The daughter's view: timeline, thinking skills, causes, risks","why":"Turns a worried relative's story into the pattern that points to the cause, and makes him safer today.","frame":["Timeline and course","Thinking skills (memory, language, recognition, orientation)","Daily living","Other causes","Risks and carer"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist in the older adults team. Are you Zainab Hussain?","Your dad has agreed we can talk. Anything you share helps us understand what's happening.","Before I ask questions, what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"That he'll get lost and something will happen to him.\""},{"h":"Timeline (1.25 min)","say":["What first made you notice something had changed, and when was that?","Has it crept on gradually, or come in sudden steps?","Does it vary: good days and very muddled days?"],"hear":"\"About a year. Missed appointments first. It's just slowly got worse.\"","mark":"Gradual, steady decline over 12 months"},{"h":"Thinking skills (1.25 min)","say":["Does he forget recent things, like conversations, or repeat himself?","Does he struggle to find words? Does he know who people are?","Has he ever got lost somewhere familiar? Does he know the day and the date?"],"hear":"\"He got lost driving to the mosque he's gone to for 30 years.\"","mark":"Memory, word-finding and orientation affected"},{"h":"Daily living (1 min)","say":["How is he with washing, shaving and dressing?","Who handles the bills and money now? Does he cook or use the cooker?","Has he dropped any hobbies he loved?"],"hear":"\"He used to do all the bills. Now there are red letters. His beard's a mess.\"","mark":"Planning and self-care slipping"},{"h":"Other causes (1.25 min)","say":["Has his mood been low, or has he lost interest in things?","Does he ever see things that aren't there? Any falls, shaking or stiffness?","Has his personality changed: ruder or more impulsive? Does he drink alcohol?"],"hear":"\"No, none of that. He's quieter, but not sad. He's never drunk.\"","mark":"No Lewy body, frontal or depressive features"},{"h":"Medical and family background (1 min)","say":["Has he had a stroke, heart problems, diabetes or a head injury?","What tablets does he take, including anything from the chemist for sleep?","Has anyone in the family had memory problems?"],"hear":"\"Just blood pressure tablets. No one in the family that I know of.\""},{"h":"Risks and carer (1.5 min)","say":["Is he still driving? Any near misses?","Has he left the cooker on, or been caught by phone or doorstep scams?","How is your mum coping with all this?"],"hear":"\"He burnt a pan last month. He paid a man £300 on the phone. Mum's exhausted.\"","mark":"Driving, cooker, scam and carer risks identified"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["What you describe fits a memory illness such as Alzheimer's, but we need bloods, memory tests and a brain scan first.","I'll advise your dad not to drive until he's assessed. I'll also give you information on the Herbert Protocol and a carer's assessment for your mum.","What questions do you have? Can you tell me the next steps as you understand them?"],"hear":"\"Tests, no driving for now, and help for Mum.\"","mark":"Impression and next steps agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Timeline","12 months, gradual, steady","Neurodegenerative, Alzheimer's type most likely"],["Thinking skills","Forgetting, word-finding, got lost","Memory, language and visuospatial skills affected"],["Daily living","Bills missed, self-care slipping","Executive decline; function affected, so not mild cognitive impairment"],["Other causes","No hallucinations, falls, personality change, alcohol or low mood","Lewy body, frontal, alcohol and depression less likely"],["Risks","Driving, burnt pan, £300 scam, tired wife","Act today: driving advice, OT, bank, carer support"]]},"key":"A good collateral history describes the memory loss. An excellent one fixes the course, screens each other cause with a sharp question, and leaves with a named action for driving, money, the cooker and the carer.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-u-19":{"name":"Delirium or dementia? The son's view of before and now","why":"Uses the family's knowledge of baseline and timeline to answer the referral question and find the cause.","frame":["Last seen well and onset","Features of delirium","Physical causes","Baseline function","Grief and background"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the liaison psychiatry team. Are you Rhys, Gordon's son?","I can see how worried you are. Your dad has agreed we can talk, and I'd really value your view of how he was before this."],"hear":"\"Yes. What's going on with him?\""},{"h":"Timeline of change (1.25 min)","say":["When did you last see him completely himself?","How quickly did things change: hours, days or months?","Is he better at some times than others? Is he drowsy?"],"hear":"\"He was fine last week. Now he's asleep one minute, wandering the next.\"","mark":"Acute onset, fluctuating"},{"h":"Features of confusion (1 min)","say":["Can he follow a conversation, or does he drift off mid-sentence?","Does he know where he is? Is he sleeping in the day and awake at night?","Has he seemed to see or hear things that aren't there? Is he eating?"],"hear":"\"He calls me by my brother's name. He picks at things in the air. He didn't touch his lunch.\"","mark":"Inattention, disorientation, perceptual change"},{"h":"Physical causes (1.25 min)","say":["Had he been unwell before this: cough, fever, waterworks?","Any new tablets, or anything from the chemist? How much does he drink?","Any falls or knocks to the head? Any constipation?"],"hear":"\"He had a bad cough, green phlegm. I told him to see the GP.\"","mark":"Likely chest infection"},{"h":"Baseline function (1 min)","say":["Before this, was he driving, shopping and managing his money himself?","Had you noticed any memory problems over the past year?"],"hear":"\"Totally independent. He took my kids to the park last week.\"","mark":"Independent baseline: dementia unlikely"},{"h":"Grief, mood and background (1 min)","say":["How has he been since your mum died?","Has he ever seen a psychiatrist? Anyone in the family with dementia?"],"hear":"\"Quieter. Ready meals, less bothered about his appearance. Never seen anyone.\""},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["Has he tried to leave the ward or pulled at his drip?","At home he lives alone and drives, is that right?"],"hear":"\"He tried to leave last night. And yes, he drives.\"","mark":"Wandering, falls, driving risks"},{"h":"Explain and close (2 min)","say":["This looks like delirium: sudden confusion from a physical illness, probably the chest infection. It's not dementia or a mental illness, and most people get better.","You can help: regular visits, his glasses, family photos, gentle reminders of where he is.","What's your main takeaway from all this? I'll speak to the ward team now."],"hear":"\"So it's the infection, and I can help him get his bearings.\"","mark":"Delirium explained; Rhys has a role"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Onset","Well last week, worse over days","Acute: delirium, not dementia"],["Course","Drowsy, then wandering; worse evenings","Fluctuating consciousness"],["Features","Wrong names, picking at air, day-night reversal","Inattention and perceptual disturbance"],["Cause","Productive cough, green phlegm","Likely chest infection"],["Baseline","Driving, money, minding grandchildren","No prior cognitive decline"]]},"key":"A good doctor names delirium. An excellent one proves it from last-seen-well, fluctuation and baseline, hunts every cause including bowels and bladder, and turns a frightened son into part of the treatment.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5,6],[8]]},"c-u-20":{"name":"Is it mania? Mood, beliefs, behaviour, causes, risk","why":"Establishes a manic episode quickly in a pressured patient, rules in physical causes to test, and maps the risks.","frame":["Mood, sleep and energy","Grandiose beliefs and insight","Behaviour and money","Physical causes","Past and family history","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist with the liaison team. Are you Mrs Hartley?","Thank you for sparing me some time. I gather today has been frustrating.","Could we find somewhere quieter, and you tell me what happened in the department?"],"hear":"\"That doctor had a toxic aura. Nobody here listens.\""},{"h":"Mood, sleep and energy (1.5 min)","say":["How have you been feeling in yourself this past month?","How many hours are you sleeping? Do you feel tired?","Do your thoughts race? Have people said you're quicker to anger?"],"hear":"\"Three hours. I don't need more. My mind is buzzing.\"","mark":"Reduced need for sleep, racing thoughts, irritability"},{"h":"Beliefs and insight (1 min)","say":["Tell me about the auras. What can you see or do?","Do you feel you've been given special powers or a mission?","Any voices talking to you when nobody is around?"],"hear":"\"I can heal by touch. I read people's energy. No voices.\"","mark":"Grandiose delusions, no hallucinations"},{"h":"Behaviour and money (1 min)","say":["Martin mentioned the library. What happened there?","How much have you spent online this month? Has anyone asked for your bank details?"],"hear":"\"About two thousand pounds. One seller wants my bank details to certify me.\"","mark":"Overspending and exploitation risk"},{"h":"Physical causes (1 min)","say":["Any recent illness or new medicines, such as steroid tablets?","Any headaches, falls, bumps to the head, or trouble with memory?","Do you drink alcohol or take anything else?"],"hear":"\"Steroids for my chest a few weeks ago. No headaches. My memory is perfect.\"","mark":"Recent prednisolone; no organic red flags"},{"h":"Past and family history (1 min)","say":["Have you ever had a time like this before, full of energy, spending or travelling?","Any spells of depression? Has anyone in the family had problems with their moods?"],"hear":"\"I went to Spain on a whim once. Depression after my husband died. Father was in hospital for his moods.\"","mark":"Probable past high, past depression, family history"},{"h":"Risk (1.5 min)","say":["How are your lungs? Are you using your inhalers? What would you do with the crystal?","Have you come close to hurting anyone, or felt like harming yourself?","How are things at home with Martin? Are you driving?"],"hear":"\"I'll swallow it. I nearly hit a librarian. I'd never harm myself.\"","mark":"Risks to self, others and from others mapped"},{"h":"Explain and close (1.75 min)","say":["I think your mood and energy are running far too high. Doctors call this mania. It's an illness and it can be treated.","The steroids may have set it off, and I'd like blood tests and a scan to check your body and brain.","What do you make of that? I'll talk to my consultant and, with your agreement, to Martin."],"hear":"\"I don't agree, but I'll have your tests.\"","mark":"Mania explained; tests agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood, sleep, energy","3 h sleep, not tired, racing thoughts, irritable","Manic episode"],["Beliefs","Heals by touch, reads energy; no voices","Grandiose delusions: psychotic features"],["Behaviour and money","£2,000 spent, library confrontations","Disinhibition; financial risk"],["Physical causes","Prednisolone weeks ago; no organic red flags","Possible trigger; bloods and scan"],["Past and family","Spain trip, depression, father with moods","Probable bipolar disorder"],["Risk","Crystal to swallow, inhalers stopped, near-assault, online seller","High: needs senior review and likely admission"]]},"key":"A good doctor spots mania. An excellent one finds the steroid course and the forgotten Spanish trip, tests for physical causes at 72, and asks what she will do because of her beliefs.","objmap":[[2,3],[5],[6],[4,7],[8]]},"c-u-21":{"name":"The persecutory belief and the five senses: a focused mental state in ten minutes","why":"Describes the delusion and every perception precisely, tests the real and organic explanations, and ends with a usable risk level.","frame":["The belief: content, onset, evidence, conviction","Perceptions: smell, taste, voices, vision, touch","Organic clues: seizures, cognition, senses","Mood and self-care","Risk and insight"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and lower the heat (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Cartwright?","I'm sorry, that sounds really upsetting. I'm not here to make things worse.","I'd genuinely like to hear your side. Could we talk for about ten minutes? You choose where we sit."],"hear":"\"Get away from me! ... Fine. Ten minutes, and then I'm going home.\"","mark":"Anger acknowledged, choice offered"},{"h":"Her account (1.5 min)","say":["Would you tell me what's been happening at home?","When did it start?","What did you do about it?"],"hear":"\"About a year. That smell, rotten eggs, it's them next door. I've written to them and the council, and the police just laugh.\"","mark":"Onset and actions recorded"},{"h":"The belief in detail (1.5 min)","say":["What do you think they're doing, and why you?","What made you sure?","From 0 to 100, how certain are you? Could anything else explain the smell?"],"hear":"\"They buried cats in their garden, that's the smell. They took Misty. Now they want me gone because I know. A hundred per cent.\"","mark":"Systematised persecutory delusion, full conviction"},{"h":"Perceptions, gently (1.5 min)","say":["Is the smell constant, or does it come and go? Does anyone else notice it?","Has food tasted odd? Any strange feelings on your skin?","Some people under this kind of strain hear or see things others don't. Has that happened to you? And have you ever had funny turns or blackouts?"],"hear":"\"Food tastes normal. I'm not mad! I don't hear voices. No funny turns.\"","mark":"Probable olfactory hallucination; other senses negative; no seizure history"},{"h":"Mood, self-care and cognition (1.5 min)","say":["How's your mood been in yourself?","How are you sleeping and eating?","Just so I've got it right: what's today's date, and where are we now?"],"hear":"\"My mood's fine, I want justice. I hardly sleep. I only eat packets from the big shop in the next town. It's Tuesday the 3rd, and this is the hospital.\"","mark":"Not depressed; poor sleep and intake; oriented"},{"h":"Risk (1.25 min)","say":["Have you thought of going next door to confront them?","Do you keep anything at home to protect yourself?","Has it ever got so bad you've wanted to harm yourself?"],"hear":"\"Go round there? I'm terrified of them. No weapons. I'd never hurt myself.\"","mark":"Low risk to others; self-neglect risk"},{"h":"Insight (0.75 min)","say":["Do you think any of this could be your nerves or your health?","Would you accept some help with the fear and the sleep?"],"hear":"\"There's nothing wrong with me. They should be in here, not me.\"","mark":"No insight"},{"h":"Close and next steps (1 min)","say":["Thank you. I can see how frightened you've been. Here's what happens next: a few blood tests and a brain scan to check your body, and we'll ask for your house to be checked for gas.","I'll ask the nurses for sealed food. The team will meet you at eleven.","What questions do you have for me?"],"hear":"\"Sealed food? Well, that's something.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief","Neighbours buried cats, took Misty, now poisoning her; 100% sure","Systematised persecutory delusion"],["Smell","Rotten eggs in her house, no one else notices","Probable olfactory hallucination; exclude gas leak"],["Other senses and seizures","No voices, visions, taste or touch; no funny turns","Favours delusional disorder; keep organic screen"],["Mood and self-care","Mood 'fine'; poor sleep; eats only packaged food","Not depressed; self-neglect risk"],["Risk and insight","Too frightened to confront; no weapons; 'nothing wrong with me'","Low risk to others; no insight"]]},"key":"A good doctor finds the delusion. An excellent one rates its conviction, screens every sense, asks about seizures because of the smell, and checks for a real gas leak before calling it a hallucination.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,7],[6,8]]},"c-u-47":{"name":"Can't go home: news, needs, wishes, options","why":"Breaks the news honestly, then turns his real abilities and wishes into a discharge plan he helped shape.","frame":["His understanding","The news and his reaction","Daily living","Money","His wishes","Options with benefits and barriers"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (¾ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Bernard Kowalski?","That's exactly what I'd like to talk about, Bernard. You've made real progress with your mood.","There's some difficult news about home too. Can we talk about what you'll need and plan it together? It'll take about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"What news?\""},{"h":"His understanding (1 min)","say":["Before I explain, can you tell me what you understand about why you came into hospital?","What's happened since you've been here: any tests, any new tablets?","You seem unsure about some of it. Have you noticed your memory isn't what it was?"],"hear":"\"There was some trouble at home... I had a scan, I think. My memory's not great, no.\"","mark":"Possible memory difficulty noted"},{"h":"Breaking the news (1¼ min)","say":["I'm afraid what I have to tell you is difficult.","Halina has got a court order which means you can't go back to the house. Your children support her.","She and Tomasz told us about drinking and about violence over the years. I'm sorry; this must be a shock."],"hear":"\"(silence) Forty-eight years... over one argument?\"","mark":"News given"},{"h":"His reaction (1 min)","say":["What are your thoughts about what I've just said?","How much do you usually drink?","How have things been between you and Halina lately?"],"hear":"\"I drink most days, I lose it sometimes. And she's been seeing someone, I'm sure of it.\"","mark":"Drinking acknowledged; jealous belief noted for team"},{"h":"Daily living (1½ min)","say":["What did Halina do for you at home, and what did you do yourself?","Can you wash and dress yourself? Who sorted your tablets?","Did you ever cook? Could you do your own shopping and washing?"],"hear":"\"I wash and dress myself. She did the cooking, the tablets, everything else.\"","mark":"Needs help with meals, medication, chores"},{"h":"Money (1 min)","say":["Who looked after the bills and the bank?","Do you know roughly what your pension is each month?","Could you pay for things in a shop and keep track?"],"hear":"\"She did all that. I couldn't tell you what comes in.\"","mark":"Needs support with finances"},{"h":"His wishes (1 min)","say":["It sounds like you'll need a fair bit of help each day after you leave.","If home isn't possible, where would you like to be?","What worries you most about leaving?"],"hear":"\"My own place, I suppose, with someone popping in. Not a home. And being on my own.\""},{"h":"Options and reasons (1½ min)","say":["A flat on your own with no help isn't safe yet, because cooking, tablets and money would all fall on you.","One option is sheltered or extra-care housing: your own front door, staff on site, carers for meals and tablets. A barrier is waiting lists, and some providers may hesitate after the assault.","A care home gives the most support and company, but I know you're not keen, and you don't need nursing care.","What do you think?"],"hear":"\"The flat with staff sounds better than a home.\"","mark":"Preference: extra-care or sheltered housing"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min)","say":["So: our social worker will look at what care you need and refer you to housing; the OT will look at cooking and tablets.","We'll speak to your solicitor and the police about your bail address, and the alcohol nurse will see you.","Can you tell me what happens next? We'll meet again at the discharge meeting next week."],"hear":"\"Social worker, the kitchen test, the solicitor. And no going home.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understanding","Vague about tests and recent events","Possible memory problems: test when settled"],["Reaction","Shock, minimising, drinks most days, believes wife unfaithful","Needs time; alcohol support; tell the team about the belief"],["Daily living","Washes and dresses; never cooked or managed tablets","Needs meals, compliance aid, help with chores"],["Money","Wife did bills; does not know his pension","Needs financial support; consider LPA"],["Wishes","Own place with help; fears care homes and loneliness","Extra-care or sheltered housing with a care package"]]},"key":"A good doctor breaks the news. An excellent one builds every option from what he can actually do, gives each a benefit and a barrier, and remembers the bail address and the housing referral.","objmap":[[3,4],[5,6],[7],[8],[9]]},"c-u-51":{"name":"The secluded patient who will not drink: numbers, gaps, a staffed plan","why":"Turns a worried handover into a safe, realistic plan that does not depend on the patient agreeing.","frame":["Why he is secluded","Intake and output","Signs of dehydration","What we do not know","A staffed way in","Triggers for transfer"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Sean, covering seclusion?","Could you give me the background, then what's worrying you about Marcus right now?","Thanks. Let's go through it, then decide the plan together."],"hear":"\"Yes, thanks for coming quickly. He's barely drinking and won't let us near him.\""},{"h":"Admission and seclusion (1½ min)","say":["What brought him in, and what's his legal status now?","When did he stop the clozapine, and what happened on the day he went into seclusion?","When was the last medical review, and has the independent review been done?"],"hear":"\"Section 136, now Section 3. Stopped clozapine a week before. Hit a nurse on day one and took several of us to move him.\"","mark":"Reason for seclusion established"},{"h":"Intake and output (1 min)","say":["Let's put numbers on it. How much has he drunk each day?","When did he last pass urine?","Has he eaten anything at all?"],"hear":"\"Roughly 600 mL on day one, then 300, and only sips from the tap since. Nothing in the toilet for 12 hours.\"","mark":"Poor intake quantified"},{"h":"Symptoms and signs (1 min)","say":["Apart from the headache and dry mouth, has he seemed dizzy, muddled or sleepier?","From the window, how do his lips and eyes look? Is he lying down more?","Have we managed any pulse, blood pressure, bloods or ECG this admission?"],"hear":"\"Lips are cracked, he's lying down a lot. No obs, no bloods, nothing.\"","mark":"Likely dehydration, no physical baseline"},{"h":"Mental state and risk (1 min)","say":["What does he say when he refuses food and drink?","Is he still threatening staff? Has he said what he'd do?","Is there anything he will take from us?"],"hear":"\"He thinks we're after his DNA. Only touches things in sealed packets. Says he'll hit anyone who comes in.\"","mark":"Paranoid basis for refusal"},{"h":"Safe assessment plan (2 min)","say":["This is a medical priority now. First, sealed bottles and packaged food, opened in front of him.","Nobody goes in short-staffed. I'll ask the bleep holder for three more trained staff.","Then a planned check: full observations with NEWS2, glucose, U&E, CK, full blood count and an ECG."],"hear":"\"And if he still won't let us?\""},{"h":"Medication and transfer (1½ min)","say":["Then IM lorazepam, 1 to 2 mg. No IM antipsychotic: he's dry, has no ECG, and the risk of NMS is higher.","After it, observations every 15 minutes for the first hour.","If NEWS2 is 5 or more, he's confused, or the kidneys or CK are off, he goes to A&E: ambulance, two escorts, Section 17 leave, and I ring the medical team first."],"hear":"\"Okay. That feels doable if we get the staff.\"","mark":"Lorazepam chosen; transfer triggers set"},{"h":"Hand over and close (1½ min)","say":["I'll ring Dr Okafor now and the bleep holder straight after.","I'll write this in the seclusion record and be back at half four for the planned check, sooner if he changes.","If he becomes drowsy, collapses or fits, call 999 and bleep me. Anything I've missed?"],"hear":"\"No, that's clear. Thanks.\"","mark":"Senior informed, review time set"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Seclusion","Day 3, Section 3, assault on day 1","Still a risk to others; reviews on schedule"],["Intake and output","600 mL, 300 mL, sips; no urine 12 h","Probable dehydration; possible AKI"],["Symptoms","Headache, dry mouth, cracked lips, lying down","Clinically dry until proved otherwise"],["Physical data","No obs, bloods or ECG","Must be obtained today, by plan"],["Mental state","Fears staff want his DNA; sealed items only","Use sealed fluids; expect refusal of checks"]]},"key":"Good residents spot dehydration. Excellent ones do not depend on his agreement: they plan staffing, choose lorazepam because of NMS risk, and set objective triggers and a safe route for transfer.","objmap":[[2,5],[3,4],[6,7],[7],[8]]},"c-u-55":{"name":"The informant's story: memory, beyond memory, safety, carer","why":"Gets the pattern that points to the type of dementia and the risks that need action today.","frame":["Onset and course","Other cognitive domains","Safety risks","Behaviour, mood and other dementia features","Medical, medicines and family history","Carer strain"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the memory clinic. Are you Mrs Gwen Harper?","Thank you for coming, Mrs Harper. Leonard has agreed for us to talk; he's with my colleague now, so we have about ten minutes.","I understand you're worried about him getting lost. Could you tell me when you first noticed changes?"],"hear":"\"Yes, Leonard's wife. Good, he'd be cross otherwise.\""},{"h":"Memory history (1½ min)","say":["What was the first thing you noticed?","Has it come on gradually, or in sudden drops?","Is it recent things he forgets, or things from years ago too?"],"hear":"\"About a year ago. He asks the same thing over and over and loses his keys daily. He can tell you all about his apprenticeship, though.\"","mark":"Gradual onset, recent memory loss"},{"h":"Other cognitive areas (1 min)","say":["Does he struggle to find words or name things?","How does he get on with gadgets, the bills, recognising people?"],"hear":"\"He calls everything 'the thingy'. Can't work the remote. I took over the bills six months ago. He knows the family.\"","mark":"Language and executive decline; function lost"},{"h":"Wandering and safety (1½ min)","say":["Talk me through the times he went missing. When, and how was he found?","Any problems with cooking or the gas? Is he still driving?","Any odd phone calls, people at the door, or falls?"],"hear":"\"Twice this month he walked towards his old depot, once at ten at night. Neighbours brought him back. He left a pan on the gas. He still drives to the shop and scraped the gatepost. He nearly gave his bank details to a caller.\"","mark":"Wandering, gas, driving and scam risks"},{"h":"Behaviour and mood (1 min)","say":["Has his personality or mood changed?","Has he seen things that aren't there, acted out dreams, shaken or fallen?"],"hear":"\"He's quieter, gave up the allotment, gets snappy if I correct him. Nothing like seeing things.\"","mark":"Apathy; no Lewy or frontal features"},{"h":"Medical, medicines and family history (1 min)","say":["Any strokes, heart problems, blood pressure or diabetes?","What tablets is he on? And how much does he drink?","Anyone in the family with memory problems?"],"hear":"\"Blood pressure tablets, and some for his waterworks. Two pints on a Sunday. His sister had dementia.\"","mark":"Vascular risk; anticholinergic flagged"},{"h":"Daily living and carer (1 min)","say":["Can he wash and dress himself? Any problems with continence?","And how is all this affecting you?"],"hear":"\"He manages if I lay his clothes out. I'm so tired. I'm up half the night listening for the door.\"","mark":"Carer strain"},{"h":"Next steps and close (2½ min)","say":["From what you've described, it sounds like a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's. His tests today, the GP bloods and a brain scan will help us be sure, and we'll see you both in about four weeks for the results.","For now: please fill in a Herbert Protocol form, and I'll refer to social care for a door sensor, a GPS device, a gas valve and a carer's assessment for you.","I'd advise he doesn't drive until we're done, and I'll ask his GP to review the bladder tablet, which can affect memory.","Here's our number. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"So is it dementia? ... Thank you. That's the first time anyone's asked how I am.\"","mark":"Impression and next steps given"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Onset and course","Gradual over a year; recent memory lost, old memories kept","Typical of Alzheimer's disease"],["Other domains","Word-finding, remote, bills handed over","Beyond memory, with lost function: dementia likely"],["Safety","Lost twice at night, pan on gas, driving scrape, near scam","Act today: Herbert Protocol, telecare, no driving"],["Other features","No hallucinations, falls, tremor or disinhibition; HTN; oxybutynin","Lewy and frontotemporal less likely; review anticholinergic"],["Carer","Exhausted, up at night listening for the door","Carer's assessment and support"]]},"key":"A good collateral history describes the memory loss. An excellent one names the pattern, finds the anticholinergic on the list, and acts on today's risks before any diagnosis is made.","objmap":[[2],[3,5],[4,8],[6],[7,8]]},"c-u-64":{"name":"Lithium and a slow thyroid: symptoms, results, look-feel-listen, reflexes","why":"Confirms clinical hypothyroidism in ten minutes and turns it into a clear shared-care plan that keeps lithium.","frame":["Symptoms","Results explained","Hands and pulse","Neck: look, feel, listen","Eyes and reflexes"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (30 s)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pugh?","Thanks for coming, Gareth. Your GP sent us some blood results about your thyroid, so I'd like to ask a few questions and then check your neck. Is that okay?","Would you like a chaperone? We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Sure. Is it the lithium?\""},{"h":"Low thyroid symptoms (1 min)","say":["Have you been more tired, put on weight, or felt the cold more?","Any change in your skin, hair or bowels?","How has your mood and energy been? Any thoughts life isn't worth living?"],"hear":"\"Shattered for months, five kilos on, always cold, dry skin. Flat, but nothing like that.\"","mark":"Hypothyroid symptoms present; no thoughts of self-harm"},{"h":"Other points (30 s)","say":["Any swelling in your neck, trouble swallowing or a hoarse voice?","Any thyroid problems in the family? Any other medicines, like ibuprofen?"],"hear":"\"No. Mum had a thyroid tablet, I think. Ibuprofen sometimes for my knee.\"","mark":"No pressure symptoms; family history; NSAID use"},{"h":"Explain results (1 min)","say":["The thyroid is a small gland at the front of your neck that sets your body's speed. The bloods show it's running slow.","TSH is the brain's signal telling it to work harder. It's high because the gland isn't keeping up.","Lithium commonly does this. It's very treatable with a daily thyroid tablet, and usually you stay on lithium."],"hear":"\"So I don't have to stop it?\"","mark":"Results understood"},{"h":"General inspection (1 min)","say":["Can I look at your hands? Hold them out for me.","I'm feeling your pulse now. And I'll look at your face, hair and skin."],"hear":"\"My hands are always cold now.\"","mark":"Pulse 56 regular; dry skin, mild facial puffiness; no tremor"},{"h":"Neck (2.5 min)","say":["I'll look at your neck from the front. Take a sip of water and hold it, now swallow. Now stick your tongue out.","I'll stand behind you and feel the gland. Tip your chin down a little. Swallow again for me.","I'm feeling the glands in your neck and checking your windpipe is in the middle.","Now I'll listen over the gland with my stethoscope."],"hear":"\"Is there a lump?\"","mark":"Small smooth goitre, rises on swallowing; no nodules, nodes or bruit"},{"h":"Eyes and reflexes (1.5 min)","say":["Look straight ahead. Now follow my finger down slowly, keeping your head still.","Kneel up on this chair, feet dangling off the back. I'll tap your ankle tendons.","And I'll look at the front of your shins."],"hear":"\"Okay.\"","mark":"No eye signs; slow-relaxing ankle reflexes; no pretibial myxoedema"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["Thank you, Gareth. Your pulse is a little slow, the gland is a bit enlarged but smooth, and your reflexes relax slowly. That all fits a slow thyroid.","I'll check your lithium level, kidneys, calcium and thyroid antibodies today, do a heart tracing, and write to your GP about a thyroid tablet.","Swap ibuprofen for paracetamol. If you get sickness, diarrhoea, a shaky hand or feel unsteady or muddled, get help that day.","Can you tell me what you'll do next?"],"hear":"\"Bloods today, GP about the thyroid tablet, stay on lithium, no ibuprofen.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms","Tired, 5 kg gain, cold, dry skin, flat mood","Clinical hypothyroidism"],["Bloods","High TSH, low T3 and T4","Primary hypothyroidism, likely lithium"],["Hands and pulse","Cold dry hands, pulse 56","Slowed metabolism; ECG today"],["Neck","Small smooth goitre, no nodules or nodes","Typical lithium goitre; scan only if it changes"],["Eyes and reflexes","No eye signs; slow-relaxing ankle jerks","Supports hypothyroidism; no Graves' features"]]},"key":"A good assessment examines the neck. An excellent one finishes with slow-relaxing ankle reflexes, tells him lithium continues, and writes a same-day GP plan naming the tests and levothyroxine.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6,7],[8],[8]]},"wc-01":{"name":"Running the clinic alone: triage, lithium gap and the man who was not brought","why":"Keeps every patient on the list safe by closing a monitoring gap, chasing a missed dependent adult and leaving clear letters.","frame":["Triage the list","Lithium monitoring","Interactions and toxicity","Was not brought","Safeguarding","Letters"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with Mr Holt and agree today's priority","say":["Morning, Mr Holt. I'm Dr X, covering for Dr Ellis while she's on leave.","We have half an hour. What's the one thing you most want sorted today?","I can see you need to be back at work by half ten, so I'll keep us on time."],"hear":"\"I'm fine really, I just need my prescription. I've got to get back for 10:30.\"","mark":"Patient priority agreed"},{"h":"Own the missing lithium level","say":["Before we do the prescription, I need to be honest about something. I can't find a lithium level on our system for nine months.","When and where was your last blood test?","That gap is on us, not you, and I'm going to fix it today."],"hear":"\"Before Christmas, at the GP. Nobody ever rang me, so I thought it was fine.\"","mark":"Nine-month monitoring gap identified"},{"h":"Screen for toxicity","say":["Any new shakiness, or a tremor that's got worse?","Any diarrhoea, being sick, feeling unsteady on your feet or muddled?","How much do you drink in a day, water or otherwise?"],"hear":"\"My hands have always shaken a bit. It's no worse. No sickness or anything.\""},{"h":"Hunt for interacting drugs","say":["Have you started anything new, from the GP or from a shop? Painkillers, water tablets, blood pressure tablets?","How often have you been taking those?","Ibuprofen can push lithium up to a harmful level, so let's switch to paracetamol for your back until we see the result."],"hear":"\"Ibuprofen for my back. Most days for about three weeks.\"","mark":"Interacting drug identified: daily ibuprofen"},{"h":"Close the gap with the right bloods","say":["I'd like a lithium level taken 12 hours after your evening dose, so tomorrow morning if you take it at nine tonight.","At the same time we'll check your kidneys, thyroid and calcium.","I'll ring you with the result and agree regular checks from now on, so this can't slip again."],"hear":"\"Tomorrow morning I can do. Will you actually ring me this time?\"","mark":"Bloods booked with a named person to check the result"},{"h":"Phone about Callum, the 10:00 patient","say":["Ms Grant, I'm calling because Callum hasn't arrived for his 10:00 appointment. Did he know about today?","Was someone meant to bring him?","Has anything changed for him lately: eating, sleeping, mood, visitors, money?"],"hear":"\"He wanted to come, but two staff are off. He's been in his room and skipping meals, and there's a new friend who's had money off him.\"","mark":"Was not brought, not a DNA; possible financial exploitation identified"},{"h":"Act on the safeguarding concern and agree the letters","say":["Thank you for telling me. I'm going to ask our duty worker to see Callum today, and I'll raise a safeguarding concern with the local authority.","Please write down what staff saw with the cash and keep it safe.","Mr Holt, you'll get a letter in plain English, with a copy to your GP, saying no ibuprofen and when your bloods are due."],"hear":"\"Okay. I didn't know if we were supposed to report it. I'll get it written down.\"","mark":"Care Act concern raised; letters agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Clinic item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Lithium monitoring","No level for nine months; last bloods 'before Christmas' with no result","A missed safety check: book a 12-hour post-dose level with kidney, thyroid and calcium, and name who checks it"],["Interactions and toxicity","Ibuprofen most days for three weeks; tremor unchanged; no sickness or confusion","Real risk of rising lithium without symptoms yet: stop the NSAID and check the level promptly"],["Was not brought","Callum wanted to come but no staff were free","He depends on others to attend: a missed contact is a welfare signal, not a choice"],["Safeguarding","Withdrawn, missing meals, a new 'friend' taking cash","Possible financial abuse and self-neglect: same-day duty visit and a Care Act section 42 concern"],["Letters","Patient worried nobody follows up","Plain-English letter to him, copied to the GP, with doses and the bloods date spelled out"]]},"key":"Coding a dependent adult as 'did not attend' and discharging him is the error. 'Was not brought' triggers active same-day follow-up and a look for neglect or exploitation.","objmap":[[2,6],[5],[5,7],[1,6],[7]]},"wc-02":{"name":"Care plan review: his goals, the injection, the carer and section 117","why":"Turns a tense review into a shared plan that respects his choices, protects against relapse and supports his mother.","frame":["His goals","Medication and relapse history","Early warning signs and crisis plan","Carer needs","Section 117 aftercare"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with what he wants","say":["Thank you all for coming. Daniel, this meeting is about your plan, so let's start with you.","What would make the next six months go well for you?","You've mentioned stopping the injection and getting a job. Let's take both seriously."],"hear":"\"I want a job, outdoors ideally. And I want off the injection.\"","mark":"Patient goals identified: work and stopping the injection"},{"h":"Explore the injection, not just the decision","say":["What do you like and dislike about the injection?","How are you sleeping, and how is your weight?","If we could cut the side effects, would that change how you feel about it?"],"hear":"\"It knocks me out every morning and I've put on two stone. That's the real problem.\"","mark":"Side effects driving refusal: sedation and weight gain"},{"h":"Recall what happened last time","say":["Can we look back at two years ago, when the tablets stopped? What happened?","How long did it take before things went wrong?","I'm not saying that will happen again, but it helps us plan."],"hear":"\"About three months. I stopped sleeping and thought the neighbours were filming me. I ended up on a section.\"","mark":"Previous relapse after stopping identified"},{"h":"Agree early warning signs, risk and physical health","say":["What are the first signs that you're getting unwell, and who spots them first?","Any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else? Any drugs or alcohol?","You smoke and haven't had a health check this year. Shall we book bloods, weight and blood pressure, and offer stop-smoking help?"],"hear":"\"Mum notices first. I go quiet, stop eating, stay in my room. No thoughts like that. A few beers at weekends.\"","mark":"Relapse signature recorded; no current risk to self or others"},{"h":"Hear from his mother","say":["Mrs Price, you've said you're exhausted. How are you coping, really?","Has anyone offered you a carer's assessment from the council? You're entitled to one.","Daniel, are you happy for your mum and your GP to have a copy of the plan?"],"hear":"\"Nobody's ever asked me that. I'm not sleeping. I'm frightened he'll stop and it'll all start again.\"","mark":"Carer strain identified; carer's assessment offered; consent to share given"},{"h":"Make the medication decision together","say":["You can decide about your medication. It's your choice, and you're not on a community treatment order.","Options are a lower dose, a different injection that's less sedating and kinder to weight, or tablets, with close monitoring.","What feels right to you?"],"hear":"\"If there's one that doesn't knock me out, I'd try that.\"","mark":"Shared decision: switch or reduce rather than stop"},{"h":"Work, aftercare rights and closing","say":["I'll refer you to the employment support worker in our team, who helps people find and keep real jobs.","Because you were on section 3, your aftercare is free under section 117, and it can't be ended without a proper joint decision.","We'll write the crisis plan with names and numbers and review in three months. Does that sound fair?"],"hear":"\"Yeah. That's more than I expected, to be honest.\"","mark":"Plan agreed with review date"}],"table":{"cols":["Plan area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His goals","Wants outdoor work, like the garden centre","Recovery goal: refer to individual placement and support employment"],["Medication and relapse","Morning sedation and two stone gained; relapsed within three months of stopping before","Side effects are the real objection: offer dose change or switch, with his informed choice"],["Early warning signs","Goes quiet, stops eating, stays in his room; mother notices first","Write these into the crisis plan with who to call"],["Carer needs","Mother exhausted, never offered a carer's assessment","Refer for a Care Act carer's assessment and give carer support contacts"],["Section 117 aftercare","Previously detained under section 3","Free aftercare continues until the ICB and local authority jointly decide it is no longer needed"]]},"key":"He has capacity and is not on a CTO, so persuasion is not the goal. Find out why he wants to stop, fix that, and leave him with an honest choice.","objmap":[[1],[6],[4,7],[5],[7]]},"wc-03":{"name":"The home visit: plan safe, look closely, act on what you find","why":"Gets a disengaged man seen safely at home and turns what you see there into a clear risk plan.","frame":["Lone working plan","Mood and suicide risk","Means at home","Self-neglect and fire risk","Others at risk","Next steps"],"steps":[{"h":"Plan before you leave base","say":["Priya, let's go together at 11:30. I'll log our visit, address and return time with the base.","His alert is about verbal aggression when drunk. If it gets heated, we leave.","We'll ask for the dog to be shut in another room before we sit down."],"hear":"\"Fine by me. I'll ring base when we're in and when we're out.\"","mark":"Lone working plan in place"},{"h":"At the door","say":["Hello, Mr Morgan. I'm Dr X from the mental health team, and this is Priya. We were worried because we hadn't heard from you.","Could we come in for a few minutes?","Would you mind putting Bruno in the kitchen while we talk?"],"hear":"\"Who is it? I never asked anyone to come. ... Go on then. He's harmless, but I'll shut him in.\""},{"h":"Mood, grief and drinking","say":["How have things been for you since we last saw you?","I'm sorry about your brother. How has that been?","How much are you drinking in a day now? Do you get shaky in the mornings?"],"hear":"\"Not great. Since our kid died I can't be bothered. Six or eight cans a day. No shakes.\"","mark":"Depressive relapse with bereavement and heavy drinking identified"},{"h":"Ask directly about suicide and means","say":["Some days, does it feel like there's no point going on?","Have you thought about how you might end your life? Have you got anything ready?","Would you be willing to let us take those tablets away with us today?"],"hear":"\"Some days. I've thought about taking all my tablets. There's a bag of them in the drawer. No date, no note.\"","mark":"Stockpiled tablets identified: means present"},{"h":"Look at the home and his self-care","say":["When did you last eat a proper meal? May I look in the fridge?","Are you still taking your sertraline?","I can see burn marks on the chair. Do you smoke in it? How do you heat the place?"],"hear":"\"Once a day if that. I stopped the sertraline a month back. Yeah, I smoke in the chair.\"","mark":"Self-neglect, stopped antidepressant and fire risk identified"},{"h":"Check who else is affected","say":["Does anyone else live here or come to visit, like family or grandchildren?","Is there anyone you'd like us to call?","Would you agree to a fire safety visit from the fire service? It's free."],"hear":"\"My grandson used to come on Saturdays. Not for a month. My daughter, maybe.\""},{"h":"Agree the plan and close","say":["I'm worried about you. I'd like the home treatment team to visit you today or tomorrow, and visit daily for now.","We'll take the bag of tablets, talk about restarting your antidepressant safely, and offer help with the drinking.","If things feel worse before then, here's the crisis number. Can I ring your daughter with you?"],"hear":"\"Alright. I'll give it a go. Bruno needs me, anyway.\"","mark":"Escalated to home treatment team; means removed with consent"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood and suicide risk","Thoughts of taking all his tablets; recent bereavement; heavy drinking; lives alone","High risk despite no date or note: same-day escalation to the home treatment team"],["Means at home","Carrier bag of saved sertraline and paracetamol","Remove with consent today and limit future supplies"],["Self-neglect and fire risk","Eats once a day; smokes in a burnt armchair","Self-neglect and fire danger: fire service home safety visit; consider a Care Act concern if needs persist"],["Others at risk","Grandson, 9, has stopped visiting","No child currently exposed, but record and recheck if visits restart"],["Engagement","Stopped sertraline; 'Bruno needs me'","A protective reason to live and a route back into treatment"]]},"key":"Safety on a home visit is planned before the door opens, not improvised inside. The other half is looking: the fridge, the drawer and the burnt chair tell you more than his answers.","objmap":[[1],[4],[5],[7],[1,5]]},"wc-04":{"name":"Planning Friday's discharge: housing, means, crisis plan and 72-hour follow-up","why":"Makes discharge a planned transition, not an event, so the riskiest days after leaving are covered.","frame":["Readiness and current risk","Housing","Medication and means","Crisis plan","Communication and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Gauge how he feels about Friday","say":["Morning, Liam. The team are thinking about Friday. How do you feel about going?","What has helped most while you've been here?","What still worries you about leaving?"],"hear":"\"I'm ready, honestly. Sleeping and eating again. I just want to get back to work.\""},{"h":"Re-check suicide risk against admission","say":["Do you still have thoughts about ending your life?","How strong are they now compared with when you came in?","What would make them worse again?"],"hear":"\"Once a day maybe, just a flash. Nothing like before. Rows with Sarah and money would set me off.\"","mark":"Reduced but present suicidal ideation; triggers identified"},{"h":"Find out where he will live","say":["Where will you live after Friday?","How long can you stay at Claire's?","With your agreement, I'd like to refer you to the council housing team today. We have a duty to do that."],"hear":"\"Sarah's asked me to leave. Claire said her sofa for a couple of weeks. I'm behind on rent too.\"","mark":"Housing need identified; duty to refer with consent"},{"h":"Medication and means","say":["You're on sertraline 100 mg each morning. Any problems with it?","Are there tablets stored at the flat?","We'll give you a two-week supply rather than a month, and can someone clear out the paracetamol at the flat?"],"hear":"\"No problems. There's paracetamol in the bathroom at the flat. Claire could get rid of it.\"","mark":"Means at the flat identified; limited supply agreed"},{"h":"Write the crisis plan together","say":["If things started going downhill, what would you notice first?","Who would you call, and what helps?","Let's write those down with the crisis line number, so you and Claire both have a copy."],"hear":"\"I'd stop sleeping and start avoiding people. I'd ring Dan or Claire.\""},{"h":"Agree who is told what","say":["Who would you like us to involve in your discharge?","You've said you don't want us to contact Sarah. We'll respect that.","Is it okay to talk to Claire about the plan?"],"hear":"\"Claire and Dan, yes. Not Sarah, please.\"","mark":"Consent to share recorded: Claire and Dan, not Sarah"},{"h":"Book follow-up and decide on Friday","say":["A member of the community team will see you within three days of leaving. Can we fix a time now?","Your GP will get a discharge summary within a day.","Friday works if the housing referral is made and Claire confirms. If that falls through, we'll talk again rather than send you out with nowhere to go."],"hear":"\"That's fair. Saturday afternoon at Claire's works for a visit.\"","mark":"72-hour follow-up booked; discharge conditional on housing"}],"table":{"cols":["Discharge area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Readiness and current risk","Fleeting daily thoughts, no plan; triggers are rows and money","Improved but not risk-free: the first days after discharge need close cover"],["Housing","Asked to move out; sister's sofa for two weeks; rent arrears","At risk of homelessness: duty to refer with consent, and debt advice"],["Medication and means","Sertraline 100 mg; paracetamol at the flat","Limit supply and ask Claire to remove stored tablets"],["Crisis plan","Stops sleeping and avoids people; would ring Dan or Claire","Write these warning signs and contacts into the plan he takes home"],["Communication and follow-up","Wants Claire and Dan involved, not Sarah","Respect his choice; face-to-face review within 72 hours; GP summary within 24 hours"]]},"key":"An improving mood is not a safe discharge on its own. Housing, means and a dated 72-hour contact are what make Friday safe; if they are not in place, the date moves.","objmap":[[3],[5],[7],[3,4],[7]]},"wc-05":{"name":"Getting training back on track: supervision, evidence, ARCP and MRCPsych","why":"Turns a worrying gap into a dated written plan that protects supervision and makes the ARCP evidence complete.","frame":["Weekly supervision","WPBA evidence","ARCP outcomes","Psychotherapy","MRCPsych plan","Wellbeing"],"steps":[{"h":"Name the problem calmly","say":["Thanks for making time, Dr Ward. I'd like to cover three things: supervision, my portfolio for ARCP, and Paper A.","My weekly supervision with Dr Hassan has been cancelled four times this post.","I know he's stretched. I'm not trying to blame anyone, but I need it fixed."],"hear":"\"That's fair, and thank you for raising it. He's been covering two wards since the locum left.\"","mark":"Supervision gap named"},{"h":"Fix a protected time","say":["The standard is an hour of supervision a week. Could we agree a fixed slot that doesn't move?","Could we call Dr Hassan now and put it in both diaries?","If it keeps slipping, who should I go to?"],"hear":"\"Let's ring him. Tuesday at two is free. If it fails again, tell the college tutor first, then the training programme director.\"","mark":"Protected weekly slot agreed; escalation route named"},{"h":"Check the learning agreement and evidence gaps","say":["Does my learning agreement cover the outcomes for this post?","I have three assessments so far. Which ones matter most before your report?","When do you need everything in by?"],"hear":"\"Spread them across the post and link them to the High Level Outcomes. I need it all two weeks before the panel.\""},{"h":"Make a dated assessment plan","say":["Could we do a case-based discussion today on a risk case?","I'll book an observed assessment of a new patient and an ACE in the next two weeks.","For the mini-PAT, I'll nominate ten to twelve people from different roles this week."],"hear":"\"Good. Let's do the CbD now. Get the mini-PAT out today; responses take time.\"","mark":"Dated WPBA plan agreed"},{"h":"Understand the ARCP outcomes","say":["If something is still missing at the panel, what happens?","What's the difference between an Outcome 2 and a 3?"],"hear":"\"An Outcome 5 means incomplete evidence, not a failure. Outcome 2 is no extra time; 3 means extra time is needed.\"","mark":"Outcome 5 understood as missing evidence"},{"h":"Psychotherapy and Paper A","say":["Have I started what I need for psychotherapy, like a Balint or case-based discussion group?","I'd like to sit Paper A. When would be sensible, given the ARCP?","I'll check the current College dates and syllabus changes before I book."],"hear":"\"The psychotherapy tutor starts a Balint group in January. Get your ARCP evidence done first, then focus on Paper A.\""},{"h":"Check wellbeing and write it down","say":["Honestly, this has been stressful. Is there support I should know about?","Could we write today's plan into my portfolio with dates, and review it in four weeks?"],"hear":"\"Of course. Occupational health and the trust wellbeing service are there, and my door's open. Let's write it up now.\"","mark":"Written plan with review date"}],"table":{"cols":["Training item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Weekly supervision","Cancelled four times; Dr Hassan covering two wards","Protected Tuesday 14:00 slot; college tutor then programme director if it fails"],["WPBA evidence","Three done; panel wants them spread and linked to outcomes","CbD today, more observed assessments within two weeks, mini-PAT out now"],["ARCP outcomes","Report due two weeks before the panel","Missing evidence gives Outcome 5, which is fixable; plan to avoid it"],["Psychotherapy","Balint group starts in January","Book a place now so the requirement is underway"],["MRCPsych plan","Paper A after the ARCP evidence is secure","Book once the portfolio is safe, checking current College rules"]]},"key":"Raising the problem is not the skill. Leaving with a fixed slot, named dates and a named person to escalate to is what changes the outcome.","objmap":[[2],[6],[4],[2],[1]]},"wc-06":{"name":"Driving after mania: his duty, your duty, and the GMC steps","why":"Stops unsafe driving now while keeping trust, using the DVLA rules and GMC guidance in the right order.","frame":["Condition and timing","Vehicle and licence group","Medication and alcohol","His legal duty","GMC steps: advise, persuade, tell, disclose"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and get consent for Julie to stay","say":["Thanks for coming, both of you. Steve, are you happy for Julie to hear everything today?","Tell me about the driving. What do you drive, and how much?","How have you been since you left hospital?"],"hear":"\"Yes, she can stay. I drive a Transit for a plumbing firm, all day. I feel fine now.\"","mark":"Daily work driving since discharge identified"},{"h":"Check the vehicle and licence group","say":["What weight is the van, and which licence do you drive it on?","Do you hold a lorry or bus licence?"],"hear":"\"3.5 tonnes, on my normal car licence. No lorry licence.\"","mark":"Group 1 licence confirmed"},{"h":"Ask about medication, sleep and alcohol","say":["Do your tablets make you drowsy or slow?","How much do you drink over a week?","Julie, have you noticed anything about his driving?"],"hear":"\"Groggy till about nine on the quetiapine. Couple of pints on a Friday. (Julie) He drove the kids to school on Monday after barely sleeping.\"","mark":"Morning sedation and driving after poor sleep identified"},{"h":"Explain the rule and his duty","say":["After a manic episode that needed admission, the DVLA says you must stop driving and tell them.","Usually you can drive again once you've been well and stable for about three months, taking treatment, with no side effects that affect driving, and a supportive report.","Telling the DVLA is your legal duty, not mine. Not telling them can mean a fine of up to £1,000."],"hear":"\"Three months? Nobody told me any of this when I left.\"","mark":"DVLA rule and legal duty explained"},{"h":"Hear the worry and solve the practical problem","say":["I can see this hits your work and your money. What worries you most?","Is there other work you could do for now that doesn't involve driving?","I'll write you a letter confirming you're unfit to drive at the moment, for your employer."],"hear":"\"I'll lose the job. Well, the boss did mention warehouse cover.\"","mark":"Alternative work identified"},{"h":"Agree the plan and record it","say":["Will you stop driving from today and tell the DVLA this week?","I'll record today's advice in your notes and in a letter to you and your GP.","If you kept driving, I'd have to tell the DVLA's medical adviser myself. I'd tell you first, and write to you afterwards."],"hear":"\"Alright. I'll do the form online. I don't want you doing it behind my back.\"","mark":"Patient agrees to stop and self-report"},{"h":"Close with follow-up","say":["We'll check at your next appointment that the DVLA has been told.","Julie, if you're worried he's driving, you can tell us.","Any questions before you go?"],"hear":"\"No. Thanks for being straight with me.\"","mark":"Follow-up check agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["DVLA item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Condition and timing","Manic episode needing admission, discharged three weeks ago","Must not drive now; must notify the DVLA; relicensing usually after about three months stable"],["Vehicle and licence group","3.5-tonne van on a car licence","Group 1 rules apply; lorry or bus licences have stricter rules"],["Medication and alcohol","Groggy till 9 am on quetiapine; drove after barely sleeping","Separate impairment risk: do not drive while drowsy, even once relicensed"],["His legal duty","Nobody told him; worried about losing work","Advise clearly, record it, help with alternatives at work"],["GMC steps","Agrees to stop and self-report","Disclosure to the DVLA only if he keeps driving, telling him before and writing after"]]},"key":"The duty to tell the DVLA is his. Yours is to advise, record and persuade, and only disclose if he keeps driving unsafely, telling him before and after.","objmap":[[2],[6],[4],[7],[6]]},"wc-07":{"name":"The 01:00 request for diazepam and pregabalin: verify, observe, decide","why":"Treats real withdrawal and real risk, refuses unsafe supply, and keeps him engaged rather than angry.","frame":["Story and verification","Objective withdrawal","Substance use and overdose risk","Self-harm risk","Decision and alternatives"],"steps":[{"h":"Calm, curious opening","say":["Hi, Ryan, I'm Dr X from the mental health team. Sounds like a rough few days. Tell me what's happened tonight.","I want to understand properly before we decide anything."],"hear":"\"My bag got nicked on the bus Sunday with all my tablets. GP's on holiday. I just need my diazepam and pregabalin.\""},{"h":"Pin down the prescription and check it","say":["Who prescribes them, at what doses, and which pharmacy do you use?","When did you last collect, and when did you last take each one?","I can see your GP record on the Summary Care Record. Is it okay if I look now?"],"hear":"\"Diazepam 10 three times a day, pregabalin 300 twice. The GP stopped the pregabalin, so I get it off a mate.\"","mark":"Pregabalin not prescribed: illicit supply"},{"h":"Ask about everything else he takes","say":["What else do you use: alcohol, heroin, methadone, anything else?","When did you last use, and are you on a script with a drug service?","Have you ever had a fit coming off tablets or drink?"],"hear":"\"I smoke a bit of heroin, last night. No script. Couple of cans today. Never had a fit.\"","mark":"Opioid use without treatment; mixed sedatives identified"},{"h":"Look for objective withdrawal","say":["I'd like to check you over: pulse, sweating, pupils, tremor, yawning, gooseflesh.","I'll use a withdrawal scale so we're going on what we can see, not guesses.","How does your body feel right now?"],"hear":"\"Sick, restless, can't settle.\"","mark":"Withdrawal signs scored on a named scale"},{"h":"Take the self-harm statement seriously","say":["You said you'd cut yourself if you leave with nothing. Tell me more about that.","Have you ever hurt yourself before? Any thoughts about ending your life?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Cut my arms years ago after a break-up. Sometimes think everyone's better off without me. No plan. My daughter Maisie keeps me going.\"","mark":"Suicide risk assessed: passive thoughts, no plan, strong protective tie"},{"h":"Explain the decision kindly and clearly","say":["I can't give you a supply of diazepam or pregabalin tonight. I can't confirm the doses, and with heroin and alcohol on top the overdose risk is real.","What I can do is treat any withdrawal I can see while you're here, and help you feel more comfortable.","I'll also give you a naloxone kit and show you how to use it."],"hear":"\"So I'm getting nothing? That's a joke.\"","mark":"Unsafe supply declined with a reason"},{"h":"Offer the morning plan and close","say":["In the morning we'll speak to your GP and the drug service so you can get proper treatment, including a heroin substitute if you want it.","Report the theft to the police and get a crime number; your GP will ask for it.","If you feel worse tonight or think about hurting yourself, come straight back. You're welcome here."],"hear":"\"Fine. I'll ring the drug service. Can you write that down?\"","mark":"Follow-up with GP and drug service; documented reasoning"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Story and verification","Stolen bag, GP away, pregabalin bought from a friend","Stated doses unconfirmed and partly illicit: no replacement supply out of hours"],["Objective withdrawal","Nausea and restlessness; signs scored on a scale","Treat what you can see; symptoms alone do not justify controlled drugs"],["Substance use and overdose risk","Untreated heroin use, alcohol, pregabalin","High overdose risk from mixed depressants: naloxone and urgent drug service link"],["Self-harm risk","Past cutting; passive thoughts; no plan; daughter protective","Real distress, currently lower risk: safety plan and easy route back"],["Decision and alternatives","Angry at refusal","Kind, clear reason plus a concrete offer keeps him engaged"]]},"key":"Refusing the request is not refusing the patient. Assess withdrawal objectively and risk fully, treat what you can confirm, and offer a real next step.","objmap":[[4],[5],[6],[2],[6,7]]},"wc-08":{"name":"Nowhere to sleep tonight: mental state first, then the housing route","why":"Separates mental illness from crisis, keeps him safe tonight and uses housing law rather than a bed by default.","frame":["Mental state when sober","Suicide risk","Alcohol and physical health","Housing duty","Safeguarding (adults and children)"],"steps":[{"h":"Respectful opening once sober","say":["Hi, Mr Walsh, I'm Dr X from the mental health team. How are you feeling now?","Tell me what happened yesterday.","Where did you sleep last night?"],"hear":"\"Headache. Cold. Bit embarrassed. Bailiffs changed the locks. I've got one bag. Slept in the park.\"","mark":"Eviction yesterday; rough sleeping identified"},{"h":"Look for depression beneath the crisis","say":["Before all this, how had your mood been?","How's your sleep, appetite and enjoyment of things?","Have you felt like this before, even when you had a home?"],"hear":"\"Low for months since I lost the job. Can't sleep. Nothing's any fun. At night I think, what's the point of me.\"","mark":"Depressive symptoms predating eviction"},{"h":"Explore what 'end it' means","say":["You told the nurse you'd end it if you were sent back out. What did you have in mind?","Have you ever tried to end your life, or made a plan?","Do you feel that way now, sober?"],"hear":"\"I thought once about walking in front of a lorry. Nothing since. Never tried. I just can't face the street.\"","mark":"Suicidal ideation without plan or past attempts"},{"h":"Alcohol, withdrawal and physical risk","say":["How much do you drink on a usual day? Any shakes in the morning?","When was your last drink? Ever had a fit coming off it?","When did you last eat? Did you fall or hit your head?"],"hear":"\"Three litres of white cider. Shakes till I drink. Last one about eight. No fits. Haven't eaten in two days. Slipped, didn't bang my head.\"","mark":"Alcohol dependence; withdrawal and Wernicke risk"},{"h":"Ask who else is affected","say":["Is anyone else affected by this, like children or anyone who depends on you?","Where is Jordan tonight, and who's looking after him?","Is anyone hurting you or taking your money?"],"hear":"\"My son, Jordan. He's 14. He's with me most of the time. He's at a mate's tonight.\"","mark":"Child affected: safeguarding concern for Jordan"},{"h":"Make the housing referral","say":["I'd like to refer you to the council housing team. It needs your agreement, and you choose which council.","Tonight I'll ring their out-of-hours line. Because of your mental health, they may have to find you somewhere tonight.","I'll also call children's out-of-hours social care so Jordan is safe and you both get help."],"hear":"\"Yes, refer me. Don't take Jordan off me, though.\"","mark":"Duty to refer made with consent; out-of-hours housing contacted"},{"h":"Plan for tonight and close","say":["We'll treat the withdrawal here and give you a vitamin injection, as you've not been eating.","You don't need a psychiatric bed, but you do need a roof and follow-up. I'll ask the community team to see you this week.","Here's a safety plan with numbers. If you feel worse, come back."],"hear":"\"Okay. Better than the park.\"","mark":"Safety plan and follow-up documented"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mental state when sober","Months of low mood, poor sleep, no enjoyment","A depressive episode needing treatment, not just a social crisis"],["Suicide risk","One past thought of a lorry; no plan; no attempts","Moderate, situational risk: safety plan, shelter and follow-up rather than admission"],["Alcohol and physical health","Three litres of cider daily, morning shakes, not eating","Treat withdrawal and give parenteral thiamine before he leaves"],["Housing duty","Evicted, rough sleeping, 2 degrees outside","Duty to refer with consent; out-of-hours council may owe interim accommodation"],["Safeguarding (adults and children)","Son, 14, mostly lives with him","Same-night contact with children's out-of-hours social care"]]},"key":"'Social admission' is the wrong question. Assess him properly once sober, then use the housing duty and safeguarding routes tonight; a bed only if his mental state needs one.","objmap":[[1,3],[4],[6],[5],[7]]},"wc-09":{"name":"Catatonia on the ward: recognise it, rule out the dangerous causes, start lorazepam","why":"Spots a treatable, life-threatening state that is easily mistaken for stubbornness, and starts treatment the same day.","frame":["Catatonic signs (Bush-Francis)","Malignant features and NMS","Medical causes","Lorazepam challenge","Physical care","Law and ECT"],"steps":[{"h":"Reframe 'difficult' as a medical concern","say":["Thanks, Grace. A pulse of 112 and no fluids for two days worries me. I don't think this is behaviour.","When did Leah last eat, drink and pass urine?","Let's see her together now."],"hear":"\"Not since Friday lunchtime. Hardly any urine today.\"","mark":"Dehydration risk identified"},{"h":"Look for catatonic signs and score them","say":["Leah, I'm Dr X. If you can hear me, squeeze my hand.","Grace, has she held odd positions or pushed against you when you move her?","Any grimacing, or copying your words or movements?"],"hear":"\"She held her arm up for ages this morning. If we guide her to the bed she pushes the other way. Some grimacing, no echoing.\"","mark":"Catatonia identified: mutism, posturing, negativism, rigidity"},{"h":"Check for malignant features and NMS","say":["What are her latest temperature and blood pressure?","When was olanzapine started, and any recent dose increase?","Has she stopped a benzodiazepine or clozapine recently?"],"hear":"\"37.6, BP 145 over 90. Olanzapine 10 mg at night, started five days ago. Nothing stopped.\"","mark":"Possible early malignant features with recent antipsychotic"},{"h":"Rule out medical causes and order tests","say":["Any infection, seizures, head injury or drug use before admission?","Let's send CK, full blood count, kidney and liver function, glucose and a urine drug screen, and do an ECG.","I'll check her over for infection and rigidity myself."],"hear":"\"None that we know of. I'll get the bloods kit.\"","mark":"Investigations for NMS and organic causes sent"},{"h":"Hold olanzapine and give lorazepam","say":["Please hold the olanzapine until the CK is back and the consultant has reviewed.","Let's give lorazepam 2 mg IM now, or IV if we can get a line, and rescore her in 20 to 30 minutes.","Under section 2 we can give this without consent as treatment for her mental disorder."],"hear":"\"Right. I'll draw it up and set a timer.\"","mark":"Antipsychotic held; lorazepam challenge given"},{"h":"Reassess and protect her body","say":["How has her score changed? Is she moving or speaking more?","She needs fluids now, a VTE risk assessment, pressure area checks and a fluid chart.","If she can't drink, I'll ask the medical team about IV fluids today."],"hear":"\"(Leah, after lorazepam) I couldn't move. I was scared.\"","mark":"Positive lorazepam response; fluids and VTE plan in place"},{"h":"Escalate and plan ahead","say":["I'm calling the consultant now, and the medical registrar about the bloods and fluids.","We'll continue regular lorazepam and recheck observations closely.","If she doesn't respond, or there are malignant features, we'll discuss ECT early. That needs a SOAD under section 58A if she lacks capacity, or section 62 if it's urgent."],"hear":"\"I'll handover to nights with all of that written down.\"","mark":"Consultant and medical team informed; ECT pathway named"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Catatonic signs (Bush-Francis)","Mute, posturing, negativism, rigidity, grimacing","Catatonia, not defiance: score it and treat it"],["Malignant features and NMS","Pulse 112, BP 145/90, temp 37.6, olanzapine five days ago","Possible early malignant catatonia or NMS: hold antipsychotic, check CK urgently"],["Medical causes","No known infection, seizure or drug use","Still screen bloods, urine drugs and ECG; do not assume a psychiatric cause"],["Lorazepam challenge","Moved and spoke after lorazepam","A response supports the diagnosis and guides regular dosing"],["Physical care","Two days without fluids; very little urine","Fluids, VTE prophylaxis assessment and pressure care today"]]},"key":"Antipsychotics can tip catatonia into a malignant state. Hold them, give lorazepam, watch the vital signs and CK, and raise ECT early rather than as a last resort.","objmap":[[6],[3],[5],[6],[7]]},"wc-10":{"name":"An allegation of sexual assault on the ward: safety, belief, evidence, reporting","why":"Protects the woman and others, keeps evidence intact and starts the police and safeguarding response without retraumatising her.","frame":["Safety and separation","Believe and support","Preserve evidence","Police and SARC","Safeguarding and reporting","Consent and capacity"],"steps":[{"h":"Make everyone safe","say":["Priya, thank you. Keep Kerry in the lounge with Ellie, and keep him on the men's corridor with a staff member nearby, not questioning him.","Please lock Kerry's room and don't let anyone clean it.","Write down exactly what she said, the time and who was there."],"hear":"\"We didn't note the exact time or who was there. I'll write it up now.\"","mark":"Patients separated; room secured"},{"h":"Meet Kerry and believe her","say":["Kerry, I'm Dr X. I'm sorry this happened. You did the right thing telling us.","Are you hurt anywhere, or in any pain?","Would you like Ellie to stay with you?"],"hear":"\"I don't want to talk about it again. I just feel shaky. Yes, Ellie stays.\"","mark":"Supportive response; no detailed interview"},{"h":"Explain evidence gently","say":["I know you want a shower, and that's your choice. Can I explain one thing first?","A wash or change of clothes can remove evidence the police may need. If you'd rather shower, we'll keep your clothes in a paper bag.","There's no rush to decide everything tonight."],"hear":"\"I feel disgusting. But I'll wait a bit if it helps.\"","mark":"Evidence preservation explained; her choice respected"},{"h":"Offer the police and SARC","say":["Would you like us to call the police? We can do that now.","There's also a sexual assault referral centre where a specialist can check you over and keep evidence, even if you don't decide about the police yet.","Is there anyone you'd like us to contact?"],"hear":"\"Call the police. Don't tell my mum yet.\"","mark":"Police called with consent; SARC offered"},{"h":"Check capacity and confidentiality","say":["Can you tell me back what the police and the centre would involve?","I won't tell your mum without your agreement.","If you changed your mind about the police, we might still need to share some information if others were at risk. I'd tell you if that happened."],"hear":"\"Okay. I understand. I just want him away from me.\"","mark":"Capacity to decide about reporting assessed"},{"h":"Act on the alleged perpetrator and ward safety","say":["He stays on enhanced observations away from the women's area, and we'll consider moving him to another ward.","Nobody questions him about it; that's for the police.","Let's check whether any other women need support or have concerns."],"hear":"\"He's asking why someone's sitting with him. I'll tell him it's a staff decision tonight.\"","mark":"Ongoing risk managed without contaminating evidence"},{"h":"Report and escalate","say":["I'll ring the on-call consultant and the duty manager now.","We'll raise an adult safeguarding concern and complete an incident report tonight.","I'll document everything, including what Kerry said in her own words."],"hear":"\"I'll get the safeguarding form open.\"","mark":"Safeguarding concern and incident report made"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Safety and separation","She is in the lounge with Ellie; he is in his room","Keep apart with staff; enhanced observation and possible move for him"],["Believe and support","'I don't want to talk about it again'","Listen, don't interview; detail is for trained police officers"],["Preserve evidence","Room not cleaned; same clothes; wants a shower","Lock the room; explain evidence before washing; bag clothes if she showers"],["Police and SARC","Wants police; does not want mother told","Call police now; offer SARC; keep her confidence about family"],["Safeguarding and reporting","Exact time and witnesses not recorded","Write a contemporaneous verbatim note; safeguarding concern and incident report the same night"]]},"key":"Your job tonight is safety, belief and evidence, not investigation. Listen without probing for detail, and leave questioning both patients to the police.","objmap":[[1,6],[1,5],[3],[4,5],[7]]},"wc-11":{"name":"Found hanging: resuscitate first, then lead the aftermath","why":"Gives him the best chance of survival and then protects the scene, the other patients and the staff.","frame":["Call for help","CPR and defibrillation","Hanging-specific care","Handover","Scene and evidence","Aftermath and reporting"],"steps":[{"h":"Recognise arrest and start CPR","say":["Ben, he's not breathing normally. Those gasps are a sign of cardiac arrest. Start chest compressions now.","Has 999 been called? Ring now and say cardiac arrest after a hanging.","Sam, bring the AED, oxygen and bag-valve-mask from the clinic room."],"hear":"\"999 hasn't been called. I'm calling now.\"","mark":"Cardiac arrest recognised; 999 called"},{"h":"Allocate roles","say":["Priya, you're scribe. Write the time found, 03:08, and the time we started.","Someone meet the paramedics at the door and keep the route clear.","Swap compressors every two minutes."],"hear":"\"Got it. Found 03:08, cut down 03:09.\"","mark":"Leadership and roles allocated"},{"h":"Defibrillate and manage the airway","say":["Pads on now, follow the AED, and keep compressions going while it charges.","Open the airway with a jaw thrust, ventilate 30 to 2 with the bag and oxygen.","Remove anything still round his neck, cutting away from the knot."],"hear":"\"Analysing... no shock advised. Continuing CPR.\"","mark":"High-quality CPR with AED"},{"h":"Gather the key facts while CPR continues","say":["When was he last seen alive?","Was he fully suspended or partly? Did he drop?","Any drugs taken or medical history?"],"hear":"\"Last seen in bed at 02:55. Partly suspended from the door. No known drugs.\""},{"h":"Hand over to paramedics","say":["This is Jamie, 29, detained under section 3. Found hanging at 03:08, last seen at 02:55.","CPR since 03:10, AED attached, no shocks so far. His drug chart and section papers are here.","Two staff will go with him to A&E as he's still detained."],"hear":"\"Thanks, we'll take it from here.\"","mark":"Clear handover including MHA status"},{"h":"Secure the scene and care for others","say":["Please close off the room. Keep the belt and knot as they are, and don't clean anything.","Who's with the other patients? Mark was close to him; can someone sit with him now?","Let's do a brief check-in with staff before anyone goes home."],"hear":"\"Mark's very upset. I'll stay with him.\"","mark":"Scene preserved; patients and staff supported"},{"h":"Report and inform","say":["I'll call the on-call consultant and the manager now, and complete an incident report.","The consultant or a senior should speak to his family, openly, as soon as possible.","If he dies, it's a death in detention: the coroner and CQC must be told."],"hear":"\"I'll get the family details from the front sheet.\"","mark":"Incident reported; family contact planned"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Call for help","Agonal gasps; 999 not yet called","Gasping is not normal breathing: start CPR and call 999 immediately"],["CPR and defibrillation","AED in the clinic room","Compressions first, AED as soon as it arrives, minimal pauses"],["Hanging-specific care","Partly suspended, no drop","Spinal injury is unlikely without a drop; never delay CPR for neck concerns"],["Handover","Detained under section 3","He stays detained: escort with papers to A&E"],["Scene and evidence","Knot intact; nobody noting times","Preserve ligature and room; a scribe records every time"],["Aftermath and reporting","Another patient very upset","Support patients and staff; incident report; coroner and CQC if he dies"]]},"key":"Gasping is cardiac arrest, not breathing. Compressions and the AED come before anything else; the scene, the paperwork and the debrief follow once paramedics take over.","objmap":[[1,2],[2],[5],[6],[6]]},"wc-12":{"name":"An expected-looking death that must be treated as unexpected","why":"Confirms death properly, triggers the legal reporting a detained patient's death requires, and tells the family well.","frame":["No CPR: signs of established death","Confirm death","Report: coroner and police","Notify: consultant, MHA office, CQC","Tell the family","Support the ward"],"steps":[{"h":"Agree no CPR and get the facts","say":["Jo, cold and stiff means he's been dead some time. You were right not to start CPR.","When was he last seen, and how did he look?","Is there a DNACPR form or advance decision?"],"hear":"\"Last seen settled at five on hourly checks. Found at quarter past six. No DNACPR.\"","mark":"Rigor and cold body: CPR not appropriate"},{"h":"Confirm death correctly","say":["I'll now confirm death. I'll observe for five minutes for any pulse, heart sounds or breathing.","Then I'll check his pupils, corneal reflexes and response to pain.","I'll record the time I confirm death and who was present."],"hear":"\"I'll stay with you.\"","mark":"Death confirmed after five minutes' observation"},{"h":"Leave everything in place","say":["Please leave him, the bed and his things exactly as they are. Nothing removed or cleaned.","Close the door, and keep his notes, drug chart and blood results together.","Any recent changes: falls, fever, constipation, breathlessness, his clozapine level?"],"hear":"\"He's been on clozapine 400 mg. I'll pull the bowel chart and last levels.\"","mark":"Scene and records preserved"},{"h":"Report the death","say":["He was detained under section 3, so this is a death in state detention. I'll notify the coroner and follow our policy on informing the police.","I won't write a death certificate; that's the coroner's decision.","I'll ring the on-call consultant now, and the MHA office will notify the CQC."],"hear":"\"The consultant's on her way in.\"","mark":"Coroner notified; CQC notification via MHA office"},{"h":"Phone his son with a warning shot","say":["Mr Lewis, I'm Dr X, a doctor on your father's ward. Is now a good time to talk? Are you somewhere you can sit down?","I'm afraid I have some very bad news.","I'm so sorry. Your father died early this morning."],"hear":"\"What? He was fine yesterday. I spoke to him at teatime. He was meant to be coming home.\"","mark":"Death communicated clearly"},{"h":"Answer honestly and offer support","say":["We don't know yet why he died. Because he was in hospital under the Mental Health Act, the coroner will look into it.","You'll be able to see him, though the coroner may have some say over timing.","Is there anyone with you? Would you like a priest or our chaplain?"],"hear":"\"My wife's here. Yes, Dad was Catholic. He'd want a priest. I'll ring my sister.\"","mark":"Family support and faith needs met"},{"h":"Support the ward and document","say":["Let's tell the other patients simply and kindly, and have staff available to talk.","We'll do a short debrief this morning, and I'll complete the incident report.","I'll write a clear note of times, findings and who I told."],"hear":"\"Thanks. One of the patients is already asking.\"","mark":"Ward supported; incident reported"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["No CPR","Cold and stiff; last seen 05:00","Established death: resuscitation would not help"],["Confirm death","No DNACPR; no clear cause","Five minutes' observation, then pupils, corneal reflex and pain response, with times recorded"],["Report: coroner and police","Section 3 patient on clozapine with COPD","Death in detention: coroner must be notified; no certificate from the ward doctor"],["Notify: consultant, MHA office, CQC","Consultant on her way","MHA office notifies the CQC without delay"],["Tell the family","Son shocked; family Catholic","Warning shot, plain words, offer priest and viewing information"],["Support the ward","Patients waking and asking","Simple explanation, staff available, debrief and incident report"]]},"key":"A death in detention goes to the coroner however natural it looks. Do not certify, do not tidy up, and tell the family in plain words that he has died.","objmap":[[2],[4],[4],[7],[3]]},"wc-13":{"name":"Threats against a named man and a nurse: assess, contain, decide on disclosure","why":"Separates angry words from real intent, keeps the ward safe tonight and protects the named person lawfully.","frame":["Intent, plan, means and victim","History of violence","Drivers: alcohol, psychosis, mood","Risk to himself","Disclosure in the public interest","Ward and staff safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Safe, calm opening","say":["Raj, I'll see him in the quiet room near the door, with you there, and the response team aware.","Liam, I'm Dr X, the doctor on call. I hear you're very angry tonight. Can you tell me what's going on?","I'm not here to tell you off. I want to understand."],"hear":"\"Everyone's watching me like I'm an animal. Sophie left me for Darren and I lost my flat.\"","mark":"Relationship breakdown and loss identified"},{"h":"Explore the threat itself","say":["You said something about Darren. What did you mean by 'sort him out'?","Have you thought about actually hurting him? How?","Do you know where he lives or works?"],"hear":"\"He's got what's coming. I know he's on Ferndale Road. I'd wait outside his work the first night I'm out. A beating, or worse, I don't know.\"","mark":"Named victim, known location and plan identified"},{"h":"Ask about means and past violence","say":["Do you have a knife or any weapon, here or anywhere you could get to?","Have you ever hurt anyone before? Have the police been involved?","Has there ever been violence between you and Sophie?"],"hear":"\"Mate's got a bat in his car. I glassed a bloke in 2019, got a community order. Police came once when me and Sophie rowed. I pushed a door, not her.\"","mark":"Access to weapon and history of serious violence identified"},{"h":"Look for drivers","say":["When did you last have a drink? Any shakes now?","Do you ever hear voices, or feel sure Sophie was unfaithful before she left?","How is your mood? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"Five days. Shakes went after two days. No voices. I just feel everyone's better off without me. No plan. My mum keeps me going.\"","mark":"No psychosis or withdrawal; passive suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Ask what stops him and address the nurse","say":["What stops you from acting on it?","You shouted at Amy earlier that she'd get what's coming. What did you mean?","I need to be honest: if I think someone is at serious risk, I may have to tell the police."],"hear":"\"I didn't mean Amy, I was just wound up. Darren's different. You can't tell anyone, that's private.\"","mark":"Threat to staff likely expressive; threat to Darren persists"},{"h":"Contain risk on the ward","say":["Raj, let's increase his observations tonight and keep him away from Amy.","If he tries to leave and the risk is still high, we'd consider the holding powers under section 5.","I'm phoning Dr Khan now to agree the plan."],"hear":"\"(Dr Khan) Agree. Increase obs and assess for the Mental Health Act if he tries to leave.\"","mark":"Consultant involved; observation increased"},{"h":"Disclose the minimum and document","say":["Liam, the risk to Darren is serious enough that I'm going to tell the police. I'll only share what they need: the threat, his name and that you're on the ward.","We'll also think about whether Sophie needs to be warned through the police.","Amy, are you okay? We'll complete an incident report and support you whether or not you report it."],"hear":"\"You're grassing me up? Fine. I'm not going anywhere tonight anyway.\"","mark":"Public interest disclosure made; reasons recorded"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Intent, plan, means and victim","Named victim, known address, plan to wait at his work, access to a bat","Specific and credible threat: high concern"],["History of violence","Glassing in 2019; police called during a row with Sophie","Past violence is the strongest predictor; possible domestic abuse history"],["Drivers: alcohol, psychosis, mood","Sober five days; no psychosis; low mood after the break-up","Risk driven by anger and loss, not delusion; treat depression and alcohol"],["Risk to himself","Passive thoughts; mum protective","Assess suicide risk alongside violence risk"],["Disclosure in the public interest","Wants it kept private","Real risk of serious harm justifies minimum disclosure to police, told first if safe"]]},"key":"Confidentiality is not absolute, and disclosure is not a reflex either. Assess intent, plan and means, involve the consultant, then share only what police need, telling him first if safe.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[7],[6],[5,7]]},"wc-14":{"name":"Refusing treatment for sepsis: the right law and a decision-specific capacity test","why":"Uses the Mental Capacity Act, not the Mental Health Act, so a detained man gets urgent physical treatment lawfully.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate","Best interests and the law"],"steps":[{"h":"Recognise the urgency","say":["Grace, a temperature of 38.9, heart rate 118 and a hot leg is early sepsis. This is time-critical.","Can you repeat his obs every 30 minutes while I talk to him?","Mr Clarke, I'm Dr X. Your leg looks very sore. How does it feel?"],"hear":"\"Throbbing. Hot. I feel shivery and sick. I'm not going anywhere. They'll poison me there.\"","mark":"Sepsis risk recognised"},{"h":"Choose the right framework","say":["Section 3 lets us treat his mental disorder, but not an infection in his leg.","So the question is his capacity to refuse treatment for the leg, under the Mental Capacity Act.","I'll start by assuming he has capacity."],"hear":"\"(Grace) So we can't just give it under his section?\"","mark":"MCA identified as the correct framework"},{"h":"Understand","say":["The doctors think there's an infection in your leg that's getting into your blood. What do you understand about that?","What could happen if it isn't treated?"],"hear":"\"There's an infection, it could go in my blood and they want a drip.\"","mark":"Understands the information"},{"h":"Retain and communicate","say":["Can you tell me back, in your words, what the doctor said?","Let me ask again in a few minutes to check it's stayed with you."],"hear":"\"Infection in my leg, might spread to my blood, needs a drip in hospital.\"","mark":"Retains and communicates the information"},{"h":"Use or weigh","say":["How did you weigh up going to hospital against staying here?","What worries you about going?","Would you take antibiotic tablets here instead?"],"hear":"\"The leg will settle on its own. The drip is the danger, they'll put chemicals in it. I'll take tablets from Grace, not from them.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh: decision driven by persecutory belief; lacks capacity"},{"h":"Best interests: ask, check and consult","say":["Do you have an advance decision or anyone with power of attorney for health?","Mr Winston Clarke, what would your brother want if he were well?","(To medical registrar) He lacks capacity for this decision. Is oral treatment safe, or does he need IV now?"],"hear":"\"(Winston) No attorney. He had his appendix out years ago, complaining all the way. When he's well he always wants to get better.\"","mark":"No ADRT or LPA; past wishes favour treatment"},{"h":"Act, transfer and document","say":["Mr Clarke, we're going to take you to A&E for the drip. Grace will come with you.","We'll use the least restrictive approach possible; restraint only if it's needed and proportionate to prevent serious harm.","He remains detained, so he'll be escorted with his papers. I'll document the capacity assessment and best-interests reasons in full."],"hear":"\"If Grace comes, I'll go. But I'm not having anything from them.\"","mark":"Best-interests transfer under MCA; detailed record"}],"table":{"cols":["Capacity item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Knows the infection could reach his blood","Passes this part"],["Retain","Repeats it back correctly","Passes this part"],["Use or weigh","Believes the drip is poison and the leg will settle","Cannot weigh, because of his psychosis: lacks capacity for this decision"],["Communicate","Clear, consistent answers","Passes this part"],["Best interests and the law","No ADRT or LPA; brother says he would want treatment","MCA best interests, proportionate restraint if needed, escorted transfer as a detained patient"]]},"key":"Detention under section 3 does not cover cellulitis. Capacity, decided under the MCA for this decision now, is what lets you treat; if he had capacity, his refusal would stand.","objmap":[[6],[],[6],[7],[5,6]]},"wc-15":{"name":"An autistic 13-year-old in crisis in A&E: calm first, then a lawful plan","why":"Settles a distressed child without sedation or detention by default, finds the cause and protects everyone at home.","frame":["Safety and low stimulus","Mother's knowledge","Physical causes","Risk and safeguarding","Law for under-16s","Plan with CAMHS"],"steps":[{"h":"Lower the temperature","say":["Let's move Jayden to the quietest room we have, lights down, fewer people, nobody touching him unless it's unsafe.","I'm not going to sedate or section him as a first step. Let's try calming him first.","Mrs Walsh, I'm Dr X from the mental health team. Thank you for staying with him."],"hear":"\"Kerry. I'm so sorry about the nurse. I don't know what to do any more.\"","mark":"Low-stimulus environment arranged"},{"h":"Use what his mother knows","say":["What usually helps him when he's this upset?","Can we get his headphones on and keep words short?","(To Jayden) I'm here to help. Do you want to sit here or there?"],"hear":"\"Headphones, low light, no touching, not many words. That's what works.\"","mark":"Mother's calming plan used"},{"h":"Find out what changed","say":["What happened tonight?","What's been different lately: school, sleep, eating?","Does anything seem to hurt him?"],"hear":"\"New school six weeks ago when his unit closed. Up at 3am for two weeks. Chewing on one side, off his food, won't let me look in his mouth.\"","mark":"Possible dental pain; school change and sleep loss identified"},{"h":"Rule out physical causes","say":["Pain often comes out as behaviour in autistic children. I'd like the paediatric team to check his mouth and look for anything else, when he's settled.","Is he constipated, or has he been unwell otherwise?","Does he hurt himself when frustrated?"],"hear":"\"He bangs his head when he's frustrated. Never talked about dying.\"","mark":"Physical review requested; self-injury without suicidal intent"},{"h":"Check parental responsibility and safety at home","say":["Who has parental responsibility for Jayden?","Are there other children at home? Are you safe?","Is a social worker or anyone else involved?"],"hear":"\"Just me. His dad's not on the birth certificate. Lily and the little one are at my mum's. He pushed Lily last week. I'm bruised. Nobody's involved, we're on a waiting list.\"","mark":"Sole parental responsibility; siblings and mother at risk"},{"h":"Explain the legal position","say":["At 13, the Mental Capacity Act doesn't apply. We'd ask whether he's Gillick competent, and if not, you can consent for most decisions.","There's nothing tonight to suggest he needs detention under the Mental Health Act.","If restraint were ever needed, it would be the least restrictive option by trained staff, as a last resort."],"hear":"\"I don't want him sectioned. I just need help.\""},{"h":"Agree the plan","say":["I'll speak to the CAMHS on-call team now and ask for urgent follow-up.","With your agreement, I'll make a referral to children's social care for support for the whole family. It's about help, not blame.","Let's agree a safety plan for tonight: where he sleeps, what calms him, and who you can call."],"hear":"\"Okay. If it means we get help, yes.\"","mark":"CAMHS involved; safeguarding referral; safety plan"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Safety and low stimulus","Shouting, hitting walls, kicked a nurse","Reduce noise, people and touch before any medication"],["Mother's knowledge","Headphones, low light, no touching, few words","Use her plan; she is your best resource"],["Physical causes","Chewing on one side, off food, poor sleep","Possible dental pain: paediatric and dental review"],["Risk and safeguarding","Mother bruised; siblings 6 and 9; pushed Lily","Children's social care referral for support and safety"],["Law for under-16s","Mother alone has parental responsibility","Gillick competence and parental consent apply, not the MCA; no indication for detention"]]},"key":"Distressed behaviour in an autistic child is communication until proven otherwise. Calm the environment and look for pain before any talk of sedation or detention.","objmap":[[1],[2],[4],[5],[7]]},"wc-16":{"name":"Side-effect clinic: name each problem, test, then switch together","why":"Keeps a well woman on treatment she can live with by tackling each side effect and the risks she cannot see.","frame":["Sedation","Weight and metabolic risk","Prolactin, periods and sex","Constipation","Relapse risk and the switch"],"steps":[{"h":"Validate and prioritise","say":["I can see how much this is getting to you. Thank you for telling me rather than just stopping.","Which side effect bothers you most?"],"hear":"\"The weight. I can't fit my clothes. And I'm too tired to work.\"","mark":"Side effects threaten adherence"},{"h":"Sedation","say":["What time do you take your olanzapine, and when are you sleepiest?","How many hours do you sleep?"],"hear":"\"Ten at night. I sleep twelve hours and feel drugged till lunch.\"","mark":"Excessive sedation identified"},{"h":"Weight and metabolic risk","say":["Has your appetite changed?","Are you thirsty or passing more urine than usual?","We'll check your weight, waist, blood pressure, HbA1c and lipids today."],"hear":"\"Starving, especially late at night. No, not thirsty.\"","mark":"Weight gain with appetite increase; diabetes symptoms absent"},{"h":"Periods, prolactin and sex","say":["You've had no periods for three months. Could you be pregnant at the moment?","Any milk from your breasts?","Has your sex drive changed, and is that something you'd like to talk about?"],"hear":"\"I have a boyfriend. Condoms, mostly. No milk. My sex drive's gone and we're rowing.\"","mark":"Pregnancy must be excluded; possible raised prolactin"},{"h":"Constipation","say":["How often do you open your bowels? Any pain, bloating, vomiting or blood?","Olanzapine can slow the gut. Let's start a laxative today, and come back quickly if you get severe pain or vomiting."],"hear":"\"Every four days. Bloated, no bad pain.\"","mark":"Constipation without red flags"},{"h":"Weigh stopping against switching","say":["What happened last time you stopped your medication?","If we can reduce these side effects, would you be willing to carry on with treatment?","One option is a slow switch to aripiprazole, which is usually kinder to weight, energy and periods."],"hear":"\"I ended up in hospital for six weeks thinking the neighbours were filming me. If it's better, I'll try.\"","mark":"Shared decision to switch, not stop"},{"h":"Make it safe and close","say":["First a pregnancy test today, and bloods including prolactin.","If your periods come back, fertility can too, so let's talk about reliable contraception.","We'll cross-taper over a few weeks, I'll offer healthy lifestyle support, write to your GP, and see you again soon."],"hear":"\"Thank you. I didn't think anyone would listen.\"","mark":"Pregnancy test, bloods and GP letter arranged"}],"table":{"cols":["Side effect","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sedation","Sleeps 12 hours, drugged till lunch","Disabling sedation: a reason to switch, not just reduce"],["Weight and metabolic risk","Nearly two stone gained; ravenous at night","Metabolic monitoring now and lifestyle support; switch to a lower-risk drug"],["Prolactin, periods and sex","No periods for three months; low libido; condoms 'mostly'","Exclude pregnancy first; check prolactin; discuss contraception"],["Constipation","Bowels every four days, bloated","Treat now; olanzapine constipation can become serious"],["Relapse risk and the switch","Admitted six weeks after stopping before","Stopping is high risk: cross-taper to aripiprazole instead"]]},"key":"Check for pregnancy before you blame prolactin. And remember that switching to a prolactin-sparing drug can restore fertility, so contraception is part of the plan.","objmap":[[4,6],[5],[3],[7],[2]]},"wc-17":{"name":"Preparing for ECT: capacity, section 58A and a safe first treatment","why":"Gets a woman at physical risk to effective treatment through the correct legal route, with her family involved.","frame":["Physical risk","Capacity for ECT","Advance decision, LPA or deputy","Section 58A route","Anaesthetic and physical workup","Family involvement"],"steps":[{"h":"Gentle opening and physical urgency","say":["Mrs Lewis, I'm Dr X. I'd like to talk with you about a treatment that might help.","How much have you eaten and drunk today?"],"hear":"\"If you like. It makes no difference. A sip of tea. Dead people don't need food.\"","mark":"Poor food and fluid intake: physical risk identified"},{"h":"Explain ECT simply","say":["It's called ECT. You'd have a short general anaesthetic and a muscle relaxant.","A small electric current causes a brief, controlled seizure. It's usually given twice a week.","The common side effects are headache, muscle aches, confusion just after, and some memory problems."],"hear":"\"Electric shocks, like in the films.\""},{"h":"Test understanding and retention","say":["Can you tell me in your words what ECT involves?","What might the side effects be?"],"hear":"\"An anaesthetic, a current, a fit. My memory might be affected.\"","mark":"Understands and retains"},{"h":"Test weighing","say":["What might happen if you have it, and if you don't?","How do you weigh that up?"],"hear":"\"You can't treat a dead body. There's nothing to weigh.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh because of nihilistic delusion: lacks capacity for ECT"},{"h":"Check for refusals and talk to Sarah","say":["Have you ever written down treatments you wouldn't want?","Sarah, does anyone hold a lasting power of attorney for health or a deputyship?","What do you think your mum would want if she were well?"],"hear":"\"(Sarah) Nothing like that. I just help with bills. When she's well she'd want to get better. She loves her grandchildren.\"","mark":"No ADRT, LPA or deputy; past wishes favour treatment"},{"h":"Follow the section 58A route","say":["As she's detained and lacks capacity, we need a SOAD to certify ECT on form T6 before treatment.","If she stops drinking and her life is at risk before the SOAD visits, section 62 allows urgent ECT.","We'll reassess her capacity before each treatment."],"hear":"\"(Sarah) So someone independent checks it? That's reassuring.\"","mark":"SOAD requested; section 62 noted for urgency"},{"h":"Physical workup and close","say":["We'll do bloods, an ECG and an anaesthetic review. She's had an anaesthetic before for her hip without problems.","She has an upper denture; it comes out before treatment, and she'll need to be nil by mouth beforehand.","We'll watch food, fluids and memory closely, and keep you updated, Sarah."],"hear":"\"(Sarah) Thank you. What worries her most is memory.\"","mark":"Workup arranged; family involved; monitoring planned"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Physical risk","A sip of tea today; 'dead people don't need food'","Life-threatening if prolonged: ECT is urgent; consider section 62"],["Capacity for ECT","Repeats the facts but 'you can't treat a dead body'","Understands and retains but cannot weigh: lacks capacity for ECT"],["Advance decision, LPA or deputy","None","No valid refusal blocks treatment"],["Section 58A route","Detained under section 3","SOAD certificate on T6 needed; reassess capacity before each session"],["Anaesthetic and physical workup","Previous uneventful anaesthetic; upper denture","Bloods, ECG, anaesthetic review, remove denture, fasting"]]},"key":"Capacity is about weighing, not repeating facts. If she regains capacity during the course and refuses, the T6 no longer covers treatment and ECT must stop.","objmap":[[4,6],[2,3],[6],[7],[6]]},"wc-18":{"name":"After an overdose, with an interpreter: risk, trauma and the right support","why":"Assesses risk and trauma symptoms safely across a language barrier and links him to services he is entitled to.","frame":["Interpreter and confidentiality","Overdose and suicide risk","Trauma symptoms","Living situation and safety","Entitlement and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up with a professional interpreter","say":["Hana, thank you. Please translate everything exactly, including anything that sounds odd. Mark, could you wait outside for the first part?","Yousif, I'm a doctor in mental health. What you tell me is private, but if someone is at risk we may need to share some information.","Is it okay to talk with Hana's help?"],"hear":"\"Yes. It's fine.\"","mark":"Professional interpreter used; confidentiality explained"},{"h":"Understand the overdose","say":["Can you talk me through what happened before you took the tablets?","How many did you take, and when?","What did you do afterwards?"],"hear":"\"The Home Office letter came. I didn't sleep for three nights. I bought two boxes and took them all. Then I was frightened and called Mark.\"","mark":"Paracetamol overdose after a trigger; sought help"},{"h":"Assess suicide risk now","say":["Did you want to die?","Do you feel that way now?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Then, yes. Now I regret it. My faith forbids it. My mother in Sudan keeps me going. Sometimes I think about death.\"","mark":"Regret, no current plan, strong protective ties"},{"h":"Ask about trauma symptoms without the story","say":["You don't need to tell me what happened to you unless you want to.","How are you sleeping? Do you have bad dreams or memories that come back suddenly?","What happens in your body when they come?"],"hear":"\"Nightmares every night. Loud noises bring it back, my heart races, I can't breathe. I sit with my back to the wall.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing, avoidance and hyperarousal: likely PTSD"},{"h":"Safety and daily life","say":["Where are you living? Do you feel safe there?","Do you have food, money and someone to talk to?","What worries you most about the interview next week?"],"hear":"\"A hotel room with three men. One shouts at night. I have a solicitor. I'm scared they won't believe me.\"","mark":"Unsafe-feeling housing and interview anxiety identified"},{"h":"Explain help and entitlement","say":["The medical team will finish treating the overdose first.","While your asylum claim is open, NHS care is free for you. Being registered with a GP is free too.","There are talking treatments for these nightmares and memories, and we'll refer you to a trauma service."],"hear":"\"I didn't know I could see a doctor for this.\""},{"h":"Agree the plan and close","say":["With your agreement, I can write a letter about your health for your solicitor.","We'll ask your housing provider about a quieter room, and Mark can help with that.","Here's a safety plan with numbers, in Arabic. If the thoughts come back, call them or come here."],"hear":"\"Yes. Thank you. Please send the letter.\"","mark":"Trauma referral, practical support and follow-up agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Interpreter and confidentiality","Little English; support worker present","Professional interpreter, private space, explain limits of confidentiality"],["Overdose and suicide risk","Two boxes of paracetamol; now regrets; faith and mother protective","Medical treatment first; lower current risk but needs a safety plan and follow-up"],["Trauma symptoms","Nightmares, flashbacks, hypervigilance","Probable PTSD: refer for trauma-focused therapy"],["Living situation and safety","Shared hotel room; shouting at night","Ask housing provider for a move, supported by medical evidence"],["Entitlement and support","Open asylum claim; has a solicitor","Exempt from NHS charges; GP registration; letter to solicitor with consent"]]},"key":"Trauma-informed means you assess symptoms and risk without making him tell the story. Never use the support worker as interpreter, and never share with the Home Office without consent.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[6],[7]]}};
